window.ONE_JOB_DATA = {"theta":0.31525558829307565,"stops":[0.21525558829307564,0.31525558829307565,0.41525558829307563],"slider":[0.05,0.8,0.005],"essay_one":540,"n":600,"explainers":["Opus 5.5","Sonnet 5.5"],"models":[{"key":"Q4B","name":"Qwen3-4B base","neurons":[{"p":"L00N160+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires inside a web URL on the domain-name token that comes right after the first dot of a hostname, usually after a subdomain like go., aws., azure., docs., scratch., web. or www.open, as in go.microsoft, aws.amazon, docs.oracle, web.archive, openstreetmap; plain prose mentioning the company name (\"Microsoft\", \"Amazon\") without a URL should not fire.","s":[0.865795,0.426707,1.035223,0.979682,0.726029,0.026202,0.041496,0.089986,0.078643,0.02436,0.017844,0.023716,0.014473,0.113759,0.059617]},{"p":"L00N160-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the final byte-piece of an emoji character (the token right after the emoji's leading byte), most strongly on face and person emojis such as 😦, 🤭, 🤷/🤦 with gender or skin-tone modifiers, written in casual online text like reviews, comments and blog posts; plain words describing feelings do not fire it.","s":[0.740059,0.90514,0.999955,0.975721,0.513581,0.020188,0.008497,0.044702,0.036961,0.013645,0.030323,0.035739,0.020339,0.025005,0.022398]},{"p":"L00N1704+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a quotation-mark token that comes immediately after another quotation mark, so that two quote characters are stacked: the second ‘ in ‘‘song’’, the second ' in Python ''' or SQL ''name'', a curly “ or ” written right after a straight \" as in replace(\"“\", \"\"), or ‘“ / ““ at the start of a quotation.","s":[0.931166,0.022736,0.104201,1.101092,0.847238,0.018735,0.02345,0.076042,0.700416,0.105674,0.009771,0.023418,0.037597,0.035182,0.015338]},{"p":"L00N1704-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the first word token immediately after an opening quotation mark (\" ' or “), i.e. the start of a quoted string. It is strongest in code string literals like encoding=\"utf-8\", from 'react', rel=\"stylesheet\", rcParams['figure.figsize'], but it also fires on the first word of a quoted phrase in prose (“comfort food”, \"short interest\").","s":[0.865083,0.849206,0.990512,1.069756,0.480515,0.015086,0.003749,0.030384,0.21928,0.01537,0.015754,0.003647,0.016932,0.018158,0.013035]},{"p":"L00N2622+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the opening parenthesis token \" (\" (a space followed by \"(\"), as in a parenthetical, a citation such as \"(Smith, 2009)\", a list number like \"(4)\", \"Copyright (C)\" or a function call written with a space before its parentheses; other tokens almost never reach the threshold.","s":[0.191281,0.071012,0.751158,0.14941,0.179361,0.038653,0.039555,0.278209,0.06404,0.02786,0.009861,0.024202,0.039663,0.038938,0.060354]},{"p":"L00N2622-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the first token immediately inside an opening parenthesis, square bracket or angle bracket, such as the first digit of a year or number, the first letter of an author name in a citation, a ticker exchange, or a word starting a parenthetical (e.g. the '2' in '(2013)', the 'C' in '(Cockburn, 1999)', 'NASDAQ' in '(NASDAQ:CRXA)', 'summary' in '<summary>'). It does not fire on the bracket itself or on the same words outside brackets.","s":[0.122582,0.238185,0.186736,0.412535,0.146747,0.023882,0.035447,0.041136,0.017397,0.040969,0.025085,0.047412,0.001659,0.013464,0.019989]},{"p":"L00N4968+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word-piece \"addy\" when it follows a separate \"d\"/\"D\" token, i.e. the \"daddy\" part of words like GoDaddy, Daddy, daddy, granddaddy, sugardaddy or usernames such as Pollddaddy, regardless of topic.","s":[0.955243,0.977254,0.573946,0.924775,0.534663,0.026996,0.021421,0.041474,0.081127,0.256909,0.014707,0.007594,0.009291,0.013834,0.006374]},{"p":"L00N4968-","l":"UNREL","j":"00","f":0,"e":0,"x":"A layer-0 token detector that fires on a few specific tokens regardless of context, above all the C# LINQ method token \".ElementAt\" (as in list.ElementAt(0)), and also on \" Kardashian\" after a first name (Kim, Rob) plus a handful of other single word-piece tokens such as \" nightlife\", the \"ewood\" ending of Lakewood and the \"tryside\" ending of Countryside.","s":[0.934751,1.046632,0.681975,0.889664,0.017792,0.071821,0.067185,0.157976,0.161765,0.031765,0.421612,0.129246,0.051799,0.045381,0.019162],"u":{"0":[1.102254,0.897639,0.035799,0.896158,0.888235],"1":[0.851425,1.010363,0.597752,0.493501,1.046632]}},{"p":"L01N2695+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word piece that comes right after a 'know'/'Kn'/'cogn' fragment inside a longer split word, e.g. the 'ness' of knowingness, the 'oss' of Knossos, the 'omen' of cognomen, the 'olls' of Knolls, or the 's'/'NS' of KNOWS/UNKNOWNS when 'know' starts the word with no leading space; whole common words like ' know', ' knowledge' or ' known' do not fire it.","s":[0.82232,0.693141,1.087427,0.46586,0.92836,0.022304,0.029946,0.02589,0.00576,0.089706,0.003825,0.024952,0.007225,0.022132,0.026289]},{"p":"L01N2695-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the middle word piece \"led\" (or \"LED\") right after \"know\"/\"Know\"/\"KNOW\" when the word knowledge is split into pieces, most strongly in derived or nonstandard forms such as \"knowledgeable\", \"knowledgably\", \"knowledges\", \"Knowledgent\", misspellings like \"knowledhge\", or all-caps \"KNOWLEDGE\", not on ordinary lowercase \"knowledge\" that is a single token.","s":[0.407688,1.016721,0.83666,0.846108,0.918618,0.003506,0.001569,0.002314,0.003788,0.007695,0.00126,0.000805,0.000594,0.000347,0.000559]},{"p":"L01N326+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Inside source code, a short English function word (of, as, at, on, and, where, each, using, be) used as a code token, either as a capitalized camelCase piece inside an identifier (forEach, SizeOf, valueAsDate, IndexBasedOnOrdinal, ToResultUsingCache, ShouldBe) or as a lowercase keyword or argument name (LINQ where, Python as_, at1, for value of), rather than in ordinary prose.","s":[0.805792,0.520127,0.800414,0.492291,0.268392,0.037904,0.239387,0.493237,0.180005,0.520127,0.038131,0.048643,0.045307,0.049141,0.035773]},{"p":"L01N326-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the tokens of programmer-defined identifiers inside source code (variable, field, class or table names, often a middle or first piece of a camelCase or snake_case name such as Stats in teamStatsFrame or career in mincareer), especially when the name repeats one already used earlier in the snippet, not on keywords, punctuation or ordinary English prose.","s":[1.040925,0.89863,0.426136,0.136784,0.331864,0.012079,-0.015482,0.000386,0.009407,-0.013949,0.012573,0.01907,0.005473,0.004769,0.008248]},{"p":"L01N3832+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the token \"ia\" when it completes the word \"Zinnia\" right after the piece \"Zinn\" (the flower or a person's name, in any context, even in a short standalone text); \"ia\" in other words, or \"Zinn\" alone without the \"ia\", does not fire it.","s":[0.899479,0.779096,0.899479,0.922072,0.786657,0.000185,0.002296,0.000118,0.006195,0.000568,0.000501,0.000258,0.000405,0.000562,0.000238]},{"p":"L01N3832-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the suffix token that completes the country name Romania when the word is split into pieces, i.e. the \"ian\", \"ians\", \"ia\" or \"ania\" after \"Roman\", \"Ruman\", \"Rou\" or \"rom\" in Romanian, Romanians, Romania, Rumania, Roumania (also Russian \"румынский\"), in any context mentioning the country, its people, language or food.","s":[0.373789,0.804808,0.39919,0.944511,0.783777,0.00033,0.000559,0.001868,0.004264,0.000653,0.00296,0.006669,0.001663,0.00053,0.003136]},{"p":"L01N5391+","l":"TWO-REL","j":"11","f":1,"e":0,"x":"Fires almost only on the rare token ' thaimassage', as in Swedish sex-ad spam lines like 'gay sex games thaimassage malmö tantra'; much more weakly, it fires on byte pieces of uncommon Chinese or Korean characters that the tokenizer splits into raw bytes (rare surname or hanja characters in names).","s":[0.778095,1.523416,-0.508065,0.449322,0.688084,-0.407286,-0.124349,-0.254402,-0.079311,-0.315597,-0.070685,-0.050541,-0.049273,-0.056503,-0.081294]},{"p":"L01N5391-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the second or later word-piece inside a rare multi-token name, domain or code identifier, especially in URLs and HTML/code: \".blogspot\" in \"2.bp.blogspot.com\", \"oreferrer\" in rel=\"noreferrer\", \"exels\" in images.pexels.com, \".Xna\" in Microsoft.Xna, and \"ernaut\" in an invented name like \"Outernauts\"; whole common words and ordinary prose do not fire it.","s":[0.93891,0.333454,0.531896,0.428869,0.789023,0.101059,0.108761,0.079042,0.180684,0.06304,0.091811,0.102054,0.060155,0.078319,0.068609]},{"p":"L01N7440+","l":"UNK","j":"0","f":1,"e":0,"x":"This pole never fires anywhere in the corpus (zero tokens with positive activation, max of 0), so no input pattern can be identified, and no short text is expected to drive it."},{"p":"L01N7440-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the near-certain continuation token inside a memorized boilerplate string from web pages or code, i.e. the second or later piece of a stock URL, namespace or site template, such as \".blogspot\" in \"1.bp.blogspot.com\", \".net\" in \"jsfiddle.net\", \"/photos\" in \"images.pexels.com/photos\", \".UnitTesting\" in \"using Microsoft.VisualStudio.TestTools.UnitTesting\", \"(HttpStatusCode\" in \"Request.CreateResponse(HttpStatusCode.OK)\", \".Text\" in \"using System.Text\", or \" pages\" in the Course Hero line \"This preview shows pages 1–3\".","s":[0.852569,0.73086,0.714763,0.677183,0.80745,0.254803,0.265019,0.218758,0.183897,0.241145,0.1526,0.196551,0.264312,0.209894,0.238983]},{"p":"L01N7930+","l":"UNK","j":"0","f":1,"e":0,"x":"This pole never fires anywhere in the corpus (zero tokens have a positive activation, max |a| is 0), so it is effectively dead and no text, short or long, should drive it."},{"p":"L01N7930-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the second piece of a familiar dotted or slashed name inside URLs, file paths and code imports, i.e. the segment that follows a known host or namespace prefix, such as \".blogspot\" in \"1.bp.blogspot.com\", \".net\" in \"jsfiddle.net/…\", \"/photos\" in \"images.pexels.com/photos/…\", \"/bin\" in \"#!/usr/bin/env\", \".Drawing\" in \"using System.Drawing;\" and \"/material\" in \"package:flutter/material.dart\".","s":[0.5935,0.589386,0.290829,0.600775,0.70683,0.110315,0.104584,0.098488,0.063985,0.137584,0.125934,0.103683,0.117998,0.133053,0.107992]},{"p":"L01N8338+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a mid-word continuation token that completes a rare, multi-piece proper name or code identifier right after its first piece, such as \"ernaut\" in Outernauts, \".Xna\" in Microsoft.Xna, \".blogspot\" in bp.blogspot.com, \"oreferrer\" in rel=\"noreferrer\" or \"otron\" in Netotron; ordinary whole words do not fire it.","s":[0.740011,0.389028,0.727282,0.604532,0.59764,0.046784,0.082706,0.085566,0.06069,0.076136,0.101836,0.088644,0.110915,0.067774,0.104358]},{"p":"L01N8338-","l":"UNREL","j":"00","f":1,"e":0,"x":"Extremely rare, tiny-magnitude pole that fires mainly on the token \" thaimassage\" in spammy adult-search keyword text (e.g. \"gay sex games thaimassage malmö tantra\"), and much more weakly on broken byte fragments of CJK/Hangul characters and the Chinese \"网站地图\" (sitemap) footer.","s":[0.517626,1.440993,-0.637955,1.267013,0.215916,-0.303287,-0.234537,-0.227459,-0.182694,-0.385496,-0.099493,-0.092249,-0.130082,-0.072447,-0.089524],"u":{"0":[1.440993,0.517626,0.90741,1.379717,1.919222],"1":[-0.07489,-0.127221,-0.062569,-0.090694,-0.054843]}},{"p":"L01N9087+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires mostly on the split byte-piece tokens of rare, multi-byte non-Latin characters (an uncommon Korean syllable, Chinese or Japanese character, Ethiopic, Greek polytonic or Tibetan letter that the tokenizer breaks into raw byte fragments) embedded in running text, plus occasionally an odd merged English word piece such as \"thaimassage\" in spam; all entries are double-negative, so this is likely an echo of the negative pole.","s":[-0.03201,0.044837,-0.023795,-0.025094,0.316904,-0.024654,-0.0245,-0.025465,-0.024808,-0.019323,-0.027914,-0.027479,-0.025955,-0.03096,-0.025792]},{"p":"L01N9087-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the middle piece of a longer word or name that was split into subword tokens, peaking on the piece right after a short first fragment, as in \"ernaut\" in Outernauts, \"blogspot\" in bp.blogspot.com URLs, \"oreferrer\" in rel=\"noreferrer\", \"entanyl\" in fentanyl and \"otron\" in Netotron; common words that are a single token do not fire it.","s":[0.565876,0.375186,0.746663,0.746706,0.670068,0.100341,0.093153,0.065745,0.06433,0.075698,0.089601,0.109113,0.069835,0.060726,0.075697]},{"p":"L02N1208+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a word-piece that completes a word split into fragments by the tokenizer, most strongly on the final 'TS' of all-caps 'MASSACHUSETTS' (and 'SET' inside it) and on 'ice' completing 'liquorice', with weaker hits on other mid-word fragments like 'stor' in jstor.org, 'ep' in 'Leseprobe', or 'IMA' in all-caps 'FUKUSHIMA'.","s":[0.914921,1.049606,0.555953,0.715792,0.514249,0.039862,0.050293,0.041976,0.012327,0.031685,0.028827,0.213712,0.038324,0.047239,0.05048]},{"p":"L02N1208-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":1,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the token \" strict\" in the JavaScript directive \"use strict\", most strongly when that directive is the very first line of a code file (a short JS snippet starting with \"use strict\"; followed by require/const lines). It also fires weakly on similar first-line file boilerplate such as the \" utf\" in a Python \"# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-\" header.","s":[0.077953,2.827895,0.077953,-0.050575,2.809651,-0.053728,-0.057737,-0.042121,-0.052064,-0.041948,-0.063651,-0.0472,-0.048261,-0.04667,-0.041068]},{"p":"L02N4108+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word piece right where a character has been silently deleted from a word by bad text extraction: contractions missing their apostrophe (didnt, dont, youre, doesnt), English words missing an fi/fl ligature (rst for first, dened, xed, dierent), and Portuguese, Spanish or French words missing an accented letter (voc for você, esto for estão, no for não, aos for años, socits).","s":[0.702403,0.823732,0.259989,0.533144,0.59566,0.02703,0.006269,0.006615,0.012122,0.001796,0.005857,0.000633,0.00416,0.004283,0.006087]},{"p":"L02N4108-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the byte-fragment token inside a polytonic Greek letter, chiefly a vowel carrying a breathing mark at the start of a word (ἐ, ἀ, ἁ, ἰ, ὑ, Ἐ, as in ἐπί, ἀπό, ἄνθρωπος, ἐκ, Ἐπιγραφή), in quoted Ancient or Byzantine Greek; plain monotonic modern Greek and English do not fire it, and rare CJK name characters fire it only occasionally.","s":[0.880458,0.712958,0.572508,0.783796,0.788006,0.143648,0.041911,0.071027,0.048173,0.036771,0.025025,0.060748,0.099364,0.023943,0.081497]},{"p":"L02N4675+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the middle or final word-piece of a word that the tokenizer has split into several pieces, especially when the word is in ALL CAPS (FUKUSHIMA, VULNERABLE, MASSACHUSETTS, DINOSAURS, ARDUINO), is a lowercase brand or URL compound (Brainly, jstor.org, christmaslover.nl), or is a rare word (itinerant, subtlety, Tailormade); it does not fire on common words that are a single whole token.","s":[0.940828,0.684141,0.764127,0.919685,0.483944,0.049152,0.026126,0.026737,0.055601,0.082158,0.054128,0.024823,0.022584,0.038557,0.044271]},{"p":"L02N4675-","l":"UNK","j":"0","f":1,"e":0,"x":"This pole never fires anywhere in the corpus (zero tokens on the negative side), so no explanation can be trusted; its output weights would suppress Roman-numeral tokens like II, III and IV, but no short text is expected to drive it."},{"p":"L02N5112+","l":"UNK","j":"00","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires (weakly and diffusely) on the second piece of a split word or phrase. The most reproducible cases are 'just' in 'not just' (as in 'not just one but two', 'ate not just the rats') and 'ball' in 'snowball' used as a verb ('costs can snowball into major issues').","s":[0.437011,0.543267,0.760516,0.56938,0.385956,0.021533,0.022079,0.036813,0.02406,0.125991,0.024473,0.241113,0.030882,0.139255,0.013628]},{"p":"L02N5112-","l":"UNK","j":"0","f":1,"e":0,"x":"This pole never fired on any of roughly 52 million corpus tokens, so no pattern can be described, and it is likely to stay silent on any short text."},{"p":"L02N52+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a mid-word continuation piece inside a long, rare or non-English word that the tokenizer splits into fragments (e.g. the 'IMA' in all-caps FUKUSHIMA, 'TS' in MASSACHUSETTS, 'alo' in a lowercase 'buffalo' in a URL, 'ep' in German 'Leseprobe', the second byte of a Korean, Hindi or CJK character), not on whole common words; a weak, extremely dense double-negative response.","s":[0.921674,0.772659,0.712858,0.46306,0.151853,0.051491,0.036966,0.126291,0.012087,0.208299,0.058108,0.05878,0.028612,0.065028,0.055409]},{"p":"L02N52-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":1,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the boilerplate label word in scraped website templates that ends a navigation heading and introduces a list of related items, such as 'Questions' in 'Similar Homework Help Questions', 'Topics' in 'Similar topics … Similar Topics:', 'theme' in 'Presentation on theme: \"…\"' and 'Expression' in 'Ref Expression'; it is very sparse and weak, and a short standalone text may not reproduce it.","s":[0.524067,0.617213,0.000838,-0.006917,-0.006121,-0.004824,-0.004297,-0.004275,-0.003767,-0.006794,-0.003594,-0.005684,-0.004183,-0.004554,-0.004505]},{"p":"L02N6295+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the second word-piece \"ols\"/\"ol\" right after \"car\" when the word \"carols\" (or \"carol\", \"caroling\") is split as car + ols, mostly in Christmas carol-singing or choral-music text (e.g. \"sang Christmas carols\", \"A Ceremony of Carols\"), with a weak offshoot on the \"jamin\" piece of \"Benjamin\" in soft-hyphenated text.","s":[0.547362,0.288179,-0.132094,-0.189683,-0.078948,-0.107806,-0.149475,-0.282745,-0.189683,-0.203283,-0.059934,-0.140785,-0.087865,-0.092207,-0.146386]},{"p":"L02N6295-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the closing Markdown emphasis marker (a single * or ** or __, often merged with a following comma or period, as in \"*,\") right after an italicized or bolded word or phrase, such as an italic species name in a garden index, an italic legal term like *jus cogens*, a speaker label like *M.S.*, or a bold phrase; it does not fire on the opening marker or on plain unformatted words.","s":[0.387364,0.324123,1.365691,0.224728,0.337078,0.005008,0.009421,0.00559,0.00621,0.004896,0.005337,0.022499,0.005565,0.00559,0.005314]},{"p":"L02N6522+","l":"TWO-REL","j":"111","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the second word-piece of a split word, most reliably on the 'ley' ending of 'motley' (as in 'a motley crew'), and also on 'as' in 'keras' in Python code. It fires on the piece that completes the word, not on the word's meaning, so synonyms like 'ragtag' or 'assorted' should not fire it.","s":[0.848189,0.790263,0.652896,0.788352,0.167594,0.036383,0.050894,0.0498,-0.000963,0.01466,0.015656,0.014893,0.004743,0.022769,0.013166]},{"p":"L02N6522-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the predictable word that completes a fixed website boilerplate phrase, such as 'Additional info for', 'Similar Topics', 'share|improve this answer', 'Sign up to view the full content', 'View Full Document', 'Transcript of', 'Unformatted text preview' or 'jstor.org', where the next token is fully determined by site template text rather than by the content.","s":[0.218749,0.294428,0.148763,0.007812,0.106218,0.002995,0.011019,0.012327,0.012257,0.006324,0.003609,0.031176,0.008054,0.004604,0.005052]},{"p":"L03N1314+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word or word-piece that comes right after a long glued-together identifier (snake_case like user_additional_info or xtreme_binary_robot, CamelCase like MatematicaMaluca or WizardsOfWaverlyPlace, or run-together usernames and URL names), especially when that next word restates part of the identifier, as in \"Estrella estrella;\", \"IZoo zoo\", \"auth_user auth_user_pkey\" or \"[new_royalslider id=\".","s":[0.592215,0.284825,0.349856,0.088998,0.43144,0.337023,0.233633,0.025055,0.060319,0.021884,0.01013,0.043684,0.018319,0.027431,0.016711]},{"p":"L03N1314-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the hyphen or underscore that joins words inside a long multi-word compound, a phrase hyphenated as a single adjective (\"good-answers-in-a-row\", \"rough-around-the-edges\", \"earn-while-you-learn\", \"tried-but-true\") or an underscore-joined username or identifier (Henry_Snijders, Stefan_Schubert, lost-planet-3_Carrier), especially at a later joint after two or more pieces are already chained.","s":[0.633777,0.85497,0.436127,0.736322,0.333212,0.01418,0.002864,0.002463,0.002396,0.00295,0.001254,0.001429,0.002057,0.002018,0.002733]},{"p":"L03N3782+","l":"TWO-REL","j":"11","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the letter piece that completes a short acronym or code abbreviation beginning with \"SL\"/\"sl\", such as the M in SLM, the C in SLC, the P in SLPs, the M in \"(SLM)\" for selective laser melting or spatial light modulator, or \"sld\"/\"sls\" in code, and more weakly on the whole word \"slaves\" in passages about slavery.","s":[0.699397,0.942688,0.003666,0.588594,0.5913,0.002635,0.001614,0.009372,0.002281,0.004184,0.001976,0.010916,0.00227,0.016946,0.002073]},{"p":"L03N3782-","l":"TWO-REL","j":"11","f":0,"e":0,"x":"In code or technical text, fires on short vowel-less abbreviation identifiers, most of all \"lng\" written right after \"lat\" for longitude, and also \"snd\" (snd_hda_intel, snd-sb16), \"crlf\", the \"Wnd\" in WndProc, RND and FSTP, plus the word \"slave\" in master/slave hardware or database contexts (SlaveAddress, I2C slave, redis-slave).","s":[0.78539,0.798399,0.818796,0.904977,0.76997,0.001068,0.522445,0.008028,0.020246,0.016043,0.010383,0.001023,0.001954,0.000666,0.001154]},{"p":"L03N5631+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the token \"seek\" (or \"Seek\", or the \"er\"/\"ek\" piece completing it) when it is glued inside a compound name with no space before it, as in Sunseeker, Superseeker, SciSeek, GeekSeek, KeySeeker, WindSeeker, cityseeker.com, heat-seek and hide-and-seek, rather than the plain verb \"seek\" used as a word in a sentence.","s":[0.702376,0.532842,0.695435,0.563384,0.817016,0.004971,0.003853,0.003677,0.012372,0.014292,0.005977,0.00054,0.002198,0.000897,0.000693]},{"p":"L03N5631-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the token \"sk\" (or \"k\") when it is a word-internal piece directly after a short prefix and before more letters, above all in \"upskilling\" and \"unskilled\" (also \"unskippable\", \"outskirt\") and in names like \"Busk\"/\"Buskirk\"/\"Buskist\"; the plain words \"skill\" or \"skilled\" written on their own do not fire it.","s":[0.66964,0.779163,0.787631,0.524166,0.765338,0.005017,0.002814,0.002798,0.003533,0.007607,0.004122,0.009487,0.004723,0.00242,0.000769]},{"p":"L03N6323+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word piece that turns \"loan\" into a lending noun, above all the \"-er\" of \"loaner\" (a temporary borrowed car or device, as in \"free loaner car\" or \"ask the dealer for a loaner\") and \"-ers\" of \"loanERS\", and also on \"loan\" when it sits inside a compound like \"microloan\" or \"Myloan4me\"; it does not fire on the plain standalone word \"loan\".","s":[1.089959,1.138544,1.10603,0.354486,0.486937,0.061939,0.025113,0.016496,0.052923,0.004852,0.010305,0.011152,0.194968,0.004865,0.026081]},{"p":"L03N6323-","l":"TWO-REL","j":"11","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word pieces of 'fiancé/fiancée' (the 'ian', 'c', 'cé', 'iance' fragments, e.g. 'ex-fiancée', 'then-fiancé', '90 Day Fiancé') and on the similar-spelled 'finance/Finance/Financier' (as in 'finance director', 'finance application', 'finance professional', 'Commissioner of Finance'), i.e. the 'fian/finan' letter string.","s":[1.042807,0.83399,0.848759,0.482803,0.702458,0.002597,0.000629,0.000866,0.006464,0.044245,0.000597,0.000876,0.001004,0.000543,0.00067]},{"p":"L03N6662+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word \"vowel\"/\"vowels\" in any form — whole word (\" vowel\", \" vowels\") or split into pieces inside identifiers like numberOfVowels, count_vowels, disemvowel, bonusVowels (firing on the \"owel\", \"ow\" or \"els\" piece) — in code, phonetics or grammar text alike.","s":[0.781659,0.933566,0.763126,0.508938,1.115251,0.577171,0.001337,0.000467,0.007947,0.001768,0.005331,0.002586,0.000415,0.001293,0.002457]},{"p":"L03N6662-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the second or middle word-piece of a rare word that the tokenizer splits into fragments, mostly unusual proper names (Nogales, Sowore, Svokos, Vokoun, Voevodsky, Savogran) and odd foreign words (German Verschwörung, the Chinese 谐音), not on the first piece and not on common whole words.","s":[0.202126,0.324835,0.323578,0.952985,0.726625,0.007385,-0.000248,0.019775,0.001979,-0.000338,0.012171,0.006708,0.003515,0.000581,0.012858]},{"p":"L03N6693+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word piece that comes right after a 'has'/'hes'/'hass' fragment inside a longer word, especially surnames and names split as Has+lam (Haslam), Hes+lem, Ghas+em (Ghasem), Has+le, Has+ler and the like, and also the 'hassle/hassled/hassling' word split as hass+le; the whole word 'has' or 'hassle' as one token does not fire.","s":[0.750626,0.622925,0.106969,0.202577,0.86251,0.001653,0.010108,0.00629,0.003681,0.006041,0.005398,0.008088,0.007511,0.010322,0.004546]},{"p":"L03N6693-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the second token of words that begin with the piece \"hus\" or \"huss\": mainly the continuation of \"husband(s)\" (e.g. \"band\" after \"hus\", \"usb\" after \"H\") and of the name \"Hussein\"/\"Hussain\" (the \"sein\"/\"ain\" piece, or \" Hussein\" as a whole token), in any prose, citation or list context.","s":[0.171242,0.233535,0.493345,0.699436,0.698699,0.000236,0.000261,0.000697,0.352603,0.373914,0.000343,0.003183,0.000555,0.000884,0.001282]},{"p":"L03N6845+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the middle word-piece right after a 'con'/'cont'/'cons' opening in rare Latinate words split into pieces, overwhelmingly 'ortion' in contortion/contortionist (circus, bendy-body contexts), and more weakly 'desc' in condescension/condescend, 'up' in concupiscence and 'angu' in consanguineous; ordinary con- words that are single tokens should not fire.","s":[0.924192,0.581555,0.911815,0.78095,0.632227,0.013096,0.000815,0.000453,0.000405,0.005299,0.00294,0.002307,0.003428,0.000574,0.003974]},{"p":"L03N6845-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word \"contest\" in any form and casing (\"contest\", \"Contest\", \"CONTEST\", \"contests\", \"contestant\", \"contestation\", \"contestability\", \"contest\" as a verb, and the \"est\" piece of a split \"Cont-est\"), regardless of topic, in prose, code identifiers or URLs (e.g. atcoder.jp/contests).","s":[0.439551,0.693537,0.834739,0.872437,0.862815,0.014551,0.000646,0.000192,0.003588,0.004476,0.002041,0.00444,0.001651,0.000785,0.00065]},{"p":"L03N8169+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the final lowercase \"c\" of the token sequence \"RePEc\" (the economics paper repository name, as in \"RePEc:\" handles, \"http://d.repec.org/n?u=RePEc:...\" links, or \"registering through RePEc\"), i.e. the c that completes \"RePE\"+\"c\"; other words ending in c or lowercase \"repec\" in domains should not fire.","s":[0.88705,1.042746,0.897723,0.848169,0.975487,0.011826,0.010529,0.007658,0.006789,0.028365,0.000989,0.001335,0.043376,0.00355,0.002016]},{"p":"L03N8169-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the middle word-piece of a few rigidly predictable multi-token words or boilerplate strings, above all the 'erv/erves' inside 'effervescence/effervescent' (any context: beer reviews, soda, champagne, figurative), and also the 'el' in 'psychedelics' and the '@author' line of a Python file header docstring ('Created on ... @author: name'); synonyms like 'fizzy' or 'bubbly' do not fire it.","s":[0.740529,0.656372,0.761978,0.888235,0.772819,0.023606,0.014951,0.044692,0.365675,0.143883,0.017556,0.020689,0.015813,0.029001,0.027871]},{"p":"L03N8511+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the boilerplate tokens of Freelancer.com-style job listing pages, chiefly the word \"Bid\" in the price tag \"$120 (Avg Bid)\" and the currency code (EUR/USD) right after the \"…jobs found, pricing in\" phrase, often on localized versions of those pages.","s":[0.505493,0.601387,0.515948,0.432489,0.516892,0.006189,0.004751,0.009139,0.015897,0.014775,0.10022,0.006235,0.000454,0.010913,0.011345]},{"p":"L03N8511-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the final piece of the word \"RePEc\" (the \"c\" after \"RePE\") or on \"pec\" in \"repec\", in mentions of the RePEc economics-paper archive such as handles like \"RePEc:eee:jbvent:...\", IDEAS pages or d.repec.org URLs; ordinary uses of letters like \"c\" or \"pec\" elsewhere do not fire.","s":[0.975382,0.981493,1.038999,0.963846,0.962103,0.025843,0.01537,0.201452,0.031939,0.004772,0.001636,0.007515,0.002955,0.037094,0.003112]},{"p":"L03N9234+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the final \"c\" of the acronym \"RePEc\" (the economics paper archive), when that word is split into the pieces \"RePE\" + \"c\", as in repec.org URLs (\"RePEc:fip:...\") or in the phrases \"RePEc Author Service\" and \"correct material in RePEc\"; other words and other economics prose do not fire it.","s":[0.937702,0.960324,0.836054,0.962134,1.003278,0.06355,0.005223,0.001602,0.006965,0.014531,-0.000573,-0.000438,0.014647,-0.000175,-0.000201]},{"p":"L03N9234-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word that completes a stock boilerplate phrase in scraped freelance-marketplace job listings (Freelancer.com style): the currency code in 'N jobs found, pricing in USD/EUR', often in a localized page such as Finnish, Malay or Danish, and 'Bid' in '$120 (Avg Bid)' price lines between project descriptions; it fires much less on ordinary prose, and full strength may need the repeated listing layout.","s":[0.554614,0.564433,0.90434,0.646221,0.506497,0.023882,0.027812,0.008442,0.039447,0.03024,0.031197,0.035329,0.035665,0.01335,0.023219]},{"p":"L04N2476+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the token that ends a plural noun with -s, strongest when the -s ending is a split-off word piece after a stem, as in odd or acronym plurals like 'personnels', 'CEUs', 'ASAPs', 'Classic IIs', 'mLinks', 'utes', 'etudes', and also on whole plural nouns such as 'prices', 'addresses', 'views', 'norms', 'cues' in list-like or technical prose (singular forms and verbs do not count).","s":[0.929484,0.82173,0.721904,0.66475,0.69739,0.270571,0.081209,0.08706,0.2525,0.4282,0.04029,0.140572,0.101665,0.029563,0.032654]},{"p":"L04N2476-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word \"commercial\" used as an adjective right before a noun it modifies, typically after an article or another modifier (\"a commercial building\", \"the first commercial flight\", \"a commercial-stage company\"), in news or business prose; a few weaker hits on similar prenominal adjectives such as \"short\" in \"shortlist\" or \"a second short staircase\".","s":[0.86493,0.865276,0.861971,0.997708,0.546133,0.482869,0.432761,0.464384,0.660075,0.263158,0.065001,0.170671,0.064698,0.140333,0.062026]},{"p":"L04N3130+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the verbs \"bestow\" (on its \"ow/owed/owing\" piece), \"consult\" (consult/consulted/consulting) and \"fasten\" (on its \"en\" piece), and on \"exception\" in \"with the exception of\", that is, formal verbs or nouns that are about to take a governed preposition or object (bestow on/upon, consult with, fasten to/on, exception of), strongest in formal or archaic narrative prose.","s":[0.79224,0.247817,0.515109,0.329627,0.557499,0.029858,0.011491,0.030585,0.093291,0.019328,0.025231,0.037599,0.019505,0.034396,0.007715]},{"p":"L04N3130-","l":"TWO-REL","j":"11","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the pieces of Romance-language words built on 'cor-/corr-/cour-', especially Spanish and French forms of 'run' and 'heart' such as correr, corren, corrían, courut, corsa, Correos, Corredera and corazón, often embedded in English text or code comments; it fires on the 'cor' piece and on the endings that follow it.","s":[0.859004,0.44597,0.334929,0.95752,0.57773,0.00977,0.010289,0.003724,0.063935,0.00533,0.007025,0.006458,0.013645,0.010719,0.00402]},{"p":"L04N3478+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the token right after a number used as a list, footnote, verse or blank label, such as the \"Id\" after a footnote number, the first word after \"1.\" or \"(3)\", or the spaces after a numbered fill-in blank, most strongly when that number starts a new item in running text or a list.","s":[0.05098,0.681762,0.832285,0.046506,0.050498,0.049152,0.016669,0.017351,0.027063,0.037705,0.070167,0.01709,0.017542,0.025732,0.01541]},{"p":"L04N3478-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the token right after a number plus a separator with no space, where a period, underscore or tab glues a list or step number directly to the following word (\"1.Introduction\", \"2.Passengers\", \"01_open_window.py\", \"namespace _01_Easter\", \"6.\\tText\"), usually on the first word-piece after the separator.","s":[0.402689,0.564859,0.804255,0.567824,0.409699,0.176303,0.002315,9.2e-05,0.004336,0.013893,0.001791,0.002877,0.000507,0.001533,0.002566]},{"p":"L04N5165+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the TeX-typesetting words 'ConTeXt' (on its final 't' piece), 'context' and 'environment', and on the name pieces of TeX macros such as \\setuphead, \\setuppaper, \\documentclass and \\normalfont, most strongly in LaTeX/ConTeXt discussions or code (weaker on 'environment' or 'in context' in other programming or web text).","s":[1.138225,1.073426,0.620952,0.321581,1.144145,0.006901,0.002982,0.004196,0.014668,0.013079,0.002243,0.002604,0.002113,0.002272,0.009892]},{"p":"L04N5165-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the middle byte of a garbled multi-byte character, almost certainly a Private Use Area glyph (U+F0xx–U+F8xx, the Symbol-font bracket and operator pieces that PDF extraction leaves behind, e.g. ), sitting inside messy copied engineering-solution math such as 'Eq. (6-15): 1 1 0.9(64)log log 0.1039  Ans'.","s":[0.003067,0.001282,0.000499,0.005433,0.000651,0.002716,0.001569,0.000167,0.020874,0.001474,0.000749,0.002836,0.00208,0.001871,0.000737]},{"p":"L04N5557+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word \"biomass\" (whole token \" biomass\", or the \"ass\" piece in capitalized \"Biomass\"), strongest when it follows \"microbial\", \"plant\" or \"phytoplankton\" in soil-ecology or bioenergy text, with weaker spillover onto nearby bio-process words like \"biodegrading\".","s":[0.792924,0.690009,0.525239,0.600322,0.911406,0.110617,0.124544,0.020104,0.061724,0.021238,0.009503,0.003791,0.006663,0.003322,0.016463]},{"p":"L04N5557-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the term \"pH\" (acidity), either on the token \" pH\" or on the \"H\" right after a \"p\" at the start of a line or after a bracket, in any chemistry, water, soil, aquarium or lab context; other chemistry words such as acidity, alkaline or buffer do not fire it.","s":[0.816514,0.791462,0.816241,0.817748,0.774969,0.683264,0.185187,0.042319,0.033647,0.499946,0.000922,0.00199,0.005794,0.002164,0.001433]},{"p":"L04N5781+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on mojibake: Chinese or Korean text saved in GBK/EUC-KR and wrongly decoded, so it appears as runs of � replacement characters mixed with stray accented, Cyrillic or Latin-extended letters (e.g. '�Ϲݻ���ں�й�ȣ', '��д��ĸ', 'ӳɹ'), typically inside comments or quoted strings in SQL or C# code; the pole peaks on those odd letter pieces sitting between the � marks.","s":[0.329952,0.786703,0.671773,0.522117,0.850468,0.002301,0.002554,0.005101,0.001152,0.001141,0.001426,0.000642,0.001003,0.001775,0.000984]},{"p":"L04N5781-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a stray standalone lowercase letter \"l\" written as its own word (space, then \"l\", then a space) where a noun should be, such as \"the l of the\", \"a l for\" or \"small l may\"; this is typical of garbled, word-spun SEO spam pages that are full of single-letter placeholders like j, F, Y, g and l. It does not fire on ordinary words that merely begin with l.","s":[0.048109,0.082134,0.024314,0.086628,0.02677,0.032361,0.006787,0.015575,0.007936,0.007311,0.014516,0.009239,0.013038,0.04172,0.014626]},{"p":"L04N6541+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the token \"ition\" when it is the middle piece of the word \"Practitioners\"/\"practitioners\" split as \"Pract\" + \"ition\" + \"ers\" (mostly capitalized \"Practitioners\" starting a sentence or in a title like \"Nurse Practitioners\", \"Agile Practitioners\", or camelCase like newPractitioners), not on the lowercase whole-word \"practitioner(s)\" which tokenizes differently.","s":[0.991478,0.918376,0.824461,0.736323,0.996777,0.004485,0.002477,0.016709,0.006227,0.002796,0.006571,0.036883,0.001875,0.003588,0.004233]},{"p":"L04N6541-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word pieces inside a capitalized or split-up \"Pragmatic\"/\"pragma\" — mainly the \"ag\" in \"Pr|ag|matic\" (or \"rag\"/\"matic\" when the word is split differently) in names like Pragmatic Play, The Pragmatic Programmer, Journal of Pragmatics, LANGUAGE Pragmas, pragprog.com, pragmaticworks.com — not on other words.","s":[0.958829,0.900516,0.919274,0.896728,0.958829,0.000809,0.002767,0.001544,0.001227,0.001943,0.000616,0.000411,0.001084,0.004232,7.2e-05]},{"p":"L04N6998+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word \"integrity\" and its pieces in any form or language (whole \" integrity\"/\" Integrity\", the \"egrity\"/\"ITY\" continuation after \"Int\"/\"INTEGR\", Spanish \"integridad\", German \"Integrität\", Malay \"integriti\"), most strongly in security, ethics or compliance usage such as \"confidentiality, integrity and availability\", with weaker spillover to \"intact\".","s":[0.638222,0.365998,1.04215,0.91405,1.049256,0.003197,0.001682,0.041845,0.004383,0.233894,0.000878,0.001922,0.000928,0.001298,0.001298]},{"p":"L04N6998-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires mainly on the intensifier adverb \"totally\" (also \"completely\", \"complete\", Chinese 彻底) used as a degree modifier right before an adjective or verb, especially in \"a totally different/new/natural…\" or \"in totally …\" phrasing, plus a weaker offshoot on word-final pieces like \"-ness\" in \"thoroughness\" and \"-ake\" in \"intake\".","s":[0.004569,0.014515,0.275505,0.507675,0.555559,0.004057,0.002112,0.027412,0.010951,0.000528,0.071213,0.027409,0.032715,0.02859,0.005907]},{"p":"L04N9078+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the token for a single BMP Unicode symbol or dingbat character, such as the ♂/♀ gender sign inside ZWJ emoji like 🤷‍♀️ or 🙋‍♂️, the ™ trademark sign, ♦, or symbols like ⚙️ ✍️ ☀️ ✔️ that take an emoji variation selector, in casual web text. It does not fire on ordinary words or on the many four-byte face and object emoji like 😀 or 🚀.","s":[0.609001,0.792002,0.377395,0.567959,1.078829,0.002118,0.000994,0.00054,0.000859,0.000243,0.001823,0.000188,0.000321,0.000296,0.00378]},{"p":"L04N9078-","l":"UNREL","j":"00","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the Chinese syllable \"shang\": most strongly on the character 尚 (as in 尚喜 or 尚存), and also on the \"ang\" piece of romanized Shang, Shanghai, Shangri-La or Shangguan and on pinyin shàng, especially in Chinese-history or Chinese-language text; a separate weaker trigger is the byte piece of certain person-gesture emoji (shrug or facepalm) and the 😮 face.","s":[1.007908,0.33666,0.35442,0.209394,0.339714,0.00799,0.0026,0.001817,0.006491,0.26375,0.001126,0.000704,0.009148,0.003717,0.002059],"u":{"0":[0.33666,0.217589,0.00182,0.292195,0.286426],"1":[0.340696,0.296037,0.257551,0.329497,0.361305]}},{"p":"L05N2328+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the Chinese character 求 meaning seek/beg/ask for (求求你, 求生, 求知, 求和, 求导, 只求), and more weakly on the English verb \"seek\"/\"seeking\" (and a bit on \"pray\"/\"express\") used as a verb of striving for or requesting something.","s":[0.962146,1.065876,1.13189,0.196524,0.753668,0.184272,0.035819,0.017075,0.02044,0.055644,0.012562,0.025144,0.005217,0.059812,0.012411]},{"p":"L05N2328-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on tokens that render the Chinese morphemes 指/支 (zhi): the characters themselves in Chinese words like 一支 (measure word for a candle, cigar or army), 支票, 指定, 指明, 指令 and 指纹, the Vietnamese cognate \"chỉ\", and English translations of their meanings such as \"finger\"/\"fingers\" (e.g. \"his fingers\", \"with a hook of her finger\") and \"branches\".","s":[0.902543,0.233456,0.468967,1.11701,0.22645,0.278449,0.008971,0.136382,0.082992,0.027219,0.030249,0.024087,0.035454,0.028576,0.038134]},{"p":"L05N3077+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the noun \"sight\" (and less often \"sensation\") used as a narrative perceptual noun for what a character sees or feels, especially \"the sight of ...\" in phrases like \"startled by the sight of\", \"the sight made me\", \"marveled at the sight\", most strongly in fiction and erotic stories.","s":[0.913371,0.381522,0.779425,0.681739,0.748944,0.006024,0.26753,0.037959,0.164839,0.150268,0.004175,0.020131,0.013132,0.00998,0.012511]},{"p":"L05N3077-","l":"TWO-REL","j":"11","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the token \" pen\" right after an article (\"the\" or \"a\"), whether it is the word pen itself (writing pen, animal pen, pen name) or the first piece of a longer word such as penultimate, penance, penumbra, penknife, penile or pen-stroke; weaker offshoots fire on other nouns or adjectives right after an article in descriptive narrative, such as \"the lips\", \"a shallow\" or \"a stone\".","s":[0.456058,0.311766,0.394447,0.462809,0.643837,0.091065,0.021386,0.112157,0.093257,0.154527,0.043591,0.033575,0.013513,0.041022,0.011571]},{"p":"L05N3666+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the name of a personal electronic device or screen gadget used in a narrative or everyday sentence, above all \"iPad\" and \"laptop\" (also HD TV, sonar screen, computer screen, MS Word), typically right after a possessive or article, as in \"on my iPad\" or \"packed my laptop\".","s":[0.911445,0.509244,0.773588,0.344983,0.753962,0.049768,0.142904,0.024422,0.031274,0.248185,0.05016,0.025512,0.017815,0.055821,0.027058]},{"p":"L05N3666-","l":"TWO-REL","j":"11","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word \"phone\" (also \"smartphone\", \"Phone\") or the brand \"Porsche\" when it names the specific product being discussed in a tech/car review or news article, especially as a subject like \"The phone ...\" or \"Porsche says ...\" after the product has already been introduced.","s":[0.598131,0.774959,0.692746,0.950837,0.657857,0.239449,0.215118,0.392509,0.139133,0.321526,0.065746,0.016119,0.022566,0.038686,0.027185]},{"p":"L05N471+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the highly predictable next word of a stock phrase, chiefly 'to' right after 'according', 'able', 'need' or 'refer', and the verb right after 'to' or 'can' (use, measure, learn, do, edit), most strongly in jumbled SEO or how-to web prose where the same phrase already appeared earlier in the page.","s":[0.040875,0.104703,0.151426,0.026279,0.097242,0.042301,0.059284,0.047405,0.054959,0.062145,0.037619,0.041,0.030598,0.070256,0.048043]},{"p":"L05N471-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the trailing subword piece that completes a word or unit glued on without a space, such as the 'nels' of 'clickfunnels', the 'mg' of '500mg', or the 'kit' of 'Convertkit', most strongly in repetitive spammy SEO or pharmacy-keyword text where that same word has already appeared many times.","s":[0.150509,0.153774,0.141098,0.100426,0.19332,0.037348,0.044784,0.102024,0.022654,0.057213,0.060216,0.035053,0.049832,0.028871,0.042789]},{"p":"L05N7654+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word piece immediately after a word-initial fragment 'Pag'/'pag' or 'PX'/'px' (also 'P', 'pg', 'pix') inside a rare name or code, e.g. the 'li' in Pagliacci or Pagliarello, 'lia' in Paglia, 'al' in pagal (Dil To Pagal Hai), 'I' in PXI or PXIe, 'A' in PXA, 'r' in pxr; common whole words like 'page' or 'pagan' should not fire.","s":[0.601992,0.726801,0.684087,1.021151,0.613123,0.002423,0.008875,0.001724,0.004929,0.004233,0.003288,0.009825,0.007685,0.018023,0.005106]},{"p":"L05N7654-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word or name \"Costa\" (on the whole token \" Costa\", or on the final \"a\"/\"osta\" piece when it is split as Cost+a or c+osta), e.g. the surname in citations (\"Costa, 2009\"), Costa Mesa, Costa Rica, Costa Blanca, regardless of topic.","s":[0.904902,0.622481,0.666467,0.796792,0.832671,0.00066,0.0092,0.006524,0.003441,0.208487,0.002581,0.003588,0.002298,0.006235,0.002538]},{"p":"L05N7701+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the second piece of an abbreviated academic degree or post-nominal credential, the token completing it after the first letter(s), such as the \".B\" in LL.B, \".S\" in M.S, \".A\" in M.A, \"LM\" in LLM, \"D\" in PsyD or EdD, \"Com\" in BCom, typically in bios listing someone's degrees (\"holds a B.A. and an M.S. in...\", \"Jane Doe, PhD, LLM\").","s":[0.972939,0.975388,0.833445,0.833641,0.826494,0.249917,0.037337,0.058733,0.574551,0.024746,0.000584,0.010099,0.001299,0.000435,0.0039]},{"p":"L05N7701-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on job- and career-credential vocabulary in recruitment ads, job postings and degree/course listings, most strongly on the word \"Job\" as a field label at the start of a line (\"Job Description:\", \"Job Location:\", \"Job Scope\"), and also on \"Employ\"/\"employ\"(ment/ability/ed), \"Experience\" as a requirement label, \"trainee\", and the professional title \"Esq.\" after a lawyer's name.","s":[0.562061,0.622179,0.673914,0.967194,0.394126,0.085331,0.065262,0.303189,0.126724,0.040802,0.012319,0.013166,0.018527,0.063341,0.013424]},{"p":"L05N8095+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word pieces of proper names built on the German-style 'Eck' stem, i.e. on ' Eck'/'E' at the start of a name and especially on the piece right after it ('ck', 'hart', 'el', 'h'), as in Eckhart Tolle, Aaron Eckhart, Bruce Eckel, Eckert, Eckstein, Eckardt, Eckley, Eckrich, the Eck Institute; ordinary words with 'eck' (check, neck, deck) should not fire.","s":[0.737641,0.677832,0.690561,0.668417,0.570902,0.004211,0.008676,0.008158,0.007247,0.0289,0.01019,0.009744,0.010768,0.020161,0.007781]},{"p":"L05N8095-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the middle word-piece \"ston\" (or \"ON\" in all-caps ESTONIA) inside the word Estonia / Estonian / Estonians, i.e. the token right after the initial \"E\"; any ordinary mention of Estonia or Estonian fires it strongly, in prose, tables or language lists, while other country names do not.","s":[0.422393,0.442057,0.33344,0.952892,0.505601,0.074183,0.147482,0.152233,0.087193,0.092311,0.003014,0.006705,0.005565,0.000855,0.007494]},{"p":"L05N816+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the second token \"AA\" of the acronym NOAA (split as NO + AA, in any case, including in noaa.gov URLs and email addresses), most strongly when the name is written as \"NOAA\" after \"National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration (\" or as a sentence subject in news text; it does not fire on the NO piece or on the spelled-out name alone.","s":[1.03029,1.20164,0.818326,0.768644,0.455094,0.06325,0.10274,0.150118,0.157692,0.118769,0.153505,0.057346,0.11687,0.080264,0.05343]},{"p":"L05N816-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the comma token itself, most strongly on commas that separate items in a list or series of short parallel nouns or terms (e.g. \"Physics, Chemistry, Mathematics, Geography\" or \"Kg, lb, oz\"), especially in choppy, keyword-stuffed web text; ordinary clause commas fire more weakly.","s":[0.09317,0.077581,0.095655,0.037444,0.113049,0.020522,0.040484,0.018765,0.011379,0.104934,0.05698,0.056263,0.054909,0.019733,0.030762]},{"p":"L06N1931+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a content word (a head noun or a bare main verb) that repeats or picks back up the passage's topic word from earlier, typically right after a determiner like \"these/this/each/type of\" or after \"to/can/must\" (\"These datasets can…\", \"where to attend college\", \"individuals must file\", \"what to do\", \"the only thing you can do\").","s":[0.475379,0.567,0.7938,0.499703,0.34134,0.084007,0.145074,0.327502,0.235683,0.131705,0.073274,0.26337,0.137231,0.069558,0.157395]},{"p":"L06N1931-","l":"TWO-REL","j":"11","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a word that is one member of an enumerated set, most strongly programming-language type and control keywords such as class, struct, enum, union, case and while when they appear in a keyword list or a type declaration, and more weakly on abstract nouns given as items in a list or a contrasting pair, such as success/failure, products/services or knowledge.","s":[0.829717,0.12613,0.884267,0.225939,0.73992,0.039278,0.031992,0.295673,0.036718,0.032204,0.054759,0.03808,0.056146,0.052172,0.075615]},{"p":"L06N2242+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the article \"a\" (rarely \"anything\") right after an exception or omission phrase such as \"apart from\", \"aside from\", \"except for\", \"with the exception of\", or after the verbs \"leaving\" or \"skipping\", typically starting a quantity phrase like \"a few\", \"a couple\" or \"a lot\".","s":[0.370531,0.31446,0.649785,0.616252,0.444558,0.036796,0.125689,0.027041,0.075217,0.010935,0.06191,0.013895,0.050512,0.013963,0.048433]},{"p":"L06N2242-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word \"part\" in the evaluative transition idiom \"the fun/hard/easy/tricky/exciting part\" (as in \"Now comes the fun part:\" or \"that's the easy part\"), where an adjective of difficulty or enjoyment comes right before \"part\" and the phrase introduces the next step in a narrative or how-to.","s":[0.823354,0.624068,0.786388,0.666895,0.747295,0.290616,0.125639,0.28438,0.103183,0.360498,0.024823,0.031576,0.016273,0.027217,0.007925]},{"p":"L06N3934+","l":"TWO-REL","j":"11","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the verbs \"rely\" and \"depend\" (as in \"you can rely on / depend on X\"; the token is the verb itself, before \"on/upon\") and more generally on the concept of counting, including Dutch/Norwegian \"tel/teller\" (count, counter) and the word right after \"count\" in idioms like \"count me in\" or \"count your blessings\"; the output pushes toward \"count\".","s":[0.023569,0.010918,0.021664,0.015829,0.015464,0.008624,0.009738,0.001838,0.011217,0.01458,0.011082,0.006774,0.013961,0.023647,0.016445]},{"p":"L06N3934-","l":"UNREL","j":"00","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the middle word-piece of transliterated foreign place names, most strongly the 'ah' in 'Kalahari' and the 'ch' in 'Nakhchivan'; separately it fires on the type keyword written right after a field or parameter name in code, such as 'any' in a TypeScript annotation like (item: any) or CHAR in a SQL column definition like id CHAR(36).","s":[0.686266,0.562679,0.937547,0.578488,0.710382,0.027508,0.031977,0.040693,0.085157,0.100853,0.070859,0.084186,0.047254,0.053176,0.043988],"u":{"0":[0.739541,0.696721,0.479449,0.706397,0.910741],"1":[0.941704,0.51918,0.446761,0.940867,0.721021]}},{"p":"L06N505+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the particle \"up\" in \"bring/brought up\" when it means raising a topic in conversation (\"I brought up the issue\"), and similarly on narrative action verbs that are just about to take an idiomatic object or clause: \"booted\" a computer, \"cast\" a glance, \"hangs up\", \"cued up\" footage, \"reflected that\", \"prompted\".","s":[0.475554,0.643888,0.447515,0.1424,0.587409,0.080754,0.143625,0.017504,0.008957,0.005334,0.02914,0.050577,0.057833,0.066355,0.039288]},{"p":"L06N505-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the token that completes an internet, software or device-interface term, typically the second word or the closing word-piece after a platform, brand or tech modifier: \"Google search\", \"bing search\", \"web search\", \"Facebook profiles\", \"IG feed\", \"Bit-Torrent\", \"Google AdSense\", \"bookmarks administrator\", \"internet uploads\", \"MUTE button\", \"Down Scroll key\", \"APK\", \"MOD (Unlocked)\", \"log in\".","s":[0.413546,0.649193,0.879619,0.637007,0.581548,0.040031,0.055424,0.058347,0.049968,0.034197,0.037564,0.033516,0.049462,0.069259,0.035725]},{"p":"L06N5544+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word \"based\" when it means located somewhere, right after a person, profession or organization description and before \"in/at/out of\" plus a place, as in author bios like \"is a journalist based in London\" or \"a French company based in Lyon\" (and on \"in\" in \"Surgeon based in Pune\").","s":[0.830077,0.80908,0.855015,0.919688,0.712037,0.439539,0.447085,0.141345,0.407846,0.194179,0.015221,0.018579,0.0214,0.014502,0.015398]},{"p":"L06N5544-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the verb \"go\" (also \"going\", and sometimes \"rising\", \"happens\", \"takes place\") used as a plain intransitive verb of habitual or life-path movement in first-person or biographical narrative, especially right after \"I live in X and go to…\", \"used to go\", \"would go back/on\", or \"wanted to go to\".","s":[1.008978,0.768615,0.336136,0.451574,0.876032,0.308773,0.147412,0.197515,0.148427,0.467032,0.023585,0.028414,0.027677,0.03402,0.011524]},{"p":"L06N6856+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the pieces of foreign (non-English) honorific titles of address such as Herr, Frau, Fräulein, Señor/Señora, Signor/Signora, Sig., Mademoiselle, Pan, Sra. and Fru, especially when they appear in English text before a person's name, peaking on the middle pieces of the word (e.g. \"Se-ñ-or\", \"Frä-ule-in\", \"Mad-emo-iselle\").","s":[0.279064,0.884492,0.482783,0.526744,0.482765,0.098643,0.119093,0.089731,0.061018,0.119738,0.007295,0.006716,0.00372,0.00675,0.006227]},{"p":"L06N6856-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on an honorific or title word placed directly before a personal name, such as King, St./Saint, Mrs Justice, Elder, Baron, or an affectionate address word like baby, darling or brother used as a vocative; it is strongest when the title is itself preceded by another title or comes in a repeated run (King Saint Stephen, Comte St.-Germain, darling darling).","s":[1.045127,0.877225,0.360285,0.879578,0.255418,0.209825,0.031513,0.059356,0.043395,0.122966,0.074407,0.03471,0.057343,0.020299,0.059779]},{"p":"L06N7457+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word-final piece \"oid\" when it completes a longer word that the tokenizer has split, such as Destructoid (the gaming site, especially in its URLs and branding), subarachnoid, schizoid or geekoid, and more weakly on other final pieces that finish a rare split name, like \"ana\" in Fergana or \"ind\" in Dedekind.","s":[0.656344,0.680296,0.650895,0.769605,0.697147,0.027449,0.028274,0.036336,0.01831,0.016228,0.010654,0.020787,0.019717,0.004228,0.008348]},{"p":"L06N7457-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the pieces of the word \"divergent\"/\"divergence\" (the \"gent\", \"erg\" or \"iver\" fragment after \"Div\", or the whole token \" divergence\"), whether it is the YA novel Divergent, MACD divergence, KL-divergence, divergent plate boundaries, beam divergence or divergent thinking.","s":[0.810476,0.789767,0.761578,0.629635,0.53897,0.111346,0.004522,0.073764,0.048914,0.006313,0.007971,0.00261,0.008582,0.006581,0.014381]},{"p":"L06N7740+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word or word piece that comes right after \"site\" or \"Site\" (or \"sites\"), completing a compound beginning with site, e.g. \"Site Reliability\" (the \"iability\" piece, and also \"Rel\"), \"site reliability\", \"Site Search\", \"Site Navigation\", \"Site Recovery\", \"site-to-site\", \"Site-neutral\", \"SiteLock\", \"Site designed\", \"sites.google.com\".","s":[0.988484,0.533916,0.567863,0.507779,0.490168,0.011793,0.007926,0.008818,0.006089,0.002792,0.00843,0.006477,0.006027,0.006476,0.002098]},{"p":"L06N7740-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word \"of\" immediately after \"District\" or \"City\" in a proper-name title, as in \"the District of Columbia\", \"City of Angels\", \"City of Heroes\" or \"City of Burnside\", predicting the place or title name that follows.","s":[0.719213,0.547975,0.596109,0.842752,0.571274,0.008086,0.008476,0.004463,0.014592,0.266901,0.008591,0.022411,0.010056,0.015533,0.022416]},{"p":"L06N8698+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word piece that comes right after \"random\"/\"Random\"/\"rand\" or after a leading capital \"R\" to complete a compound name or acronym, as in crypto.randomBytes, randbytes, RandomChoice, randomuser, a random oracle, RPKs/RPKI, or RPOW; it does not fire on \"random\" itself or on \"bytes\"/\"PK\" when they stand alone.","s":[0.846338,1.000701,0.859688,0.937987,0.680737,0.009566,0.009199,0.010147,0.001192,0.007165,0.007288,0.002689,0.019134,0.024983,0.03008]},{"p":"L06N8698-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word \"of\" (or \"ta\" in \"Outta\") directly after \"straight out\", \"fresh out\" or \"right out\", in idioms like \"straight out of the 1950s\", \"fresh out of college\", \"right out of the box/gate\", and titles like \"Straight Outta Compton\"; a plain \"out of\" without the intensifier barely fires.","s":[0.821055,0.65207,0.533648,0.819646,0.529198,0.016799,0.05147,0.005338,0.020902,0.026637,0.002817,0.009879,0.002338,0.005496,0.010487]},{"p":"L06N9694+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the final letter piece \"k\" that completes the biblical name Amalek, as in Amalek, Amalekite or Amalekites (the word is split as \"Amale\" + \"k\"), with a rare offshoot on the closing letter of a split acronym such as the \"R\" in \"MBR\" (Master Boot Record); other biblical peoples like Moab or Edom do not fire it.","s":[0.844831,0.796293,0.807614,0.852856,0.791069,0.004978,0.005283,0.010954,0.003428,0.002624,0.009301,0.027069,0.020607,0.023194,0.032563]},{"p":"L06N9694-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the second, highly predictable piece of a well-known fixed two-part name or brand, i.e. the token that completes it after its opening piece, such as Express in American Express or MedChemExpress, Anderson in MD Anderson, Card in Master Card, Bayes in MrBayes, var in Almodóvar, or PhD and MBA after a name with 'MD'; the same words standing alone do not count.","s":[0.590681,0.847842,0.780993,0.932381,0.86657,0.011992,0.008849,0.033415,0.016174,0.011361,0.022203,0.010814,0.007255,0.019298,0.006569]},{"p":"L07N1025+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word right after a sentence-initial purpose \"To + verb\" (or \"so as to / in order to\" style purpose clause) that will be followed by the main instruction or action, e.g. the \"this\" in \"To do this,\", the \"more\" in \"To learn more\", the verb in \"To view\" / \"so to overcome\", i.e. the token completing a fronted infinitive of purpose before the comma and main clause.","s":[0.568662,0.648552,0.937095,0.386938,0.443801,0.016905,0.016156,0.016928,0.018185,0.01415,0.009364,0.012197,0.01537,0.004524,0.010188]},{"p":"L07N1025-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the second word of a sentence or line that opens with a bare lead-in word, most strongly the word right after a line-initial \"More\" in web footer or link phrases (\"More on Apple\", \"More info\", \"More recipes\", French \"En savoir plus sur\"), and the \"the\" right after a sentence-initial -ing verb (\"Reviewing the...\", \"Cleaning the...\"), not on the same words mid-sentence.","s":[0.430569,0.236707,0.215752,0.920313,0.330175,0.06384,0.037016,0.042022,0.03958,0.051297,0.055024,0.006482,0.039326,0.045313,0.066569]},{"p":"L07N1939+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the piece that comes right after a programming-language or tool prefix inside a compound software package or library name, most often the hyphen-plus-first-letter piece after \"python\" in Debian or pip package names like python-pip, python3-pip, python-virtualenv or python-numpy, and also pieces such as pytorch-tutorial, cmake-gui, PyQt4, PGPy and pylab.","s":[0.70386,0.743972,0.531962,0.897994,0.764115,0.011067,0.016865,0.100113,0.024246,0.01942,0.017652,0.031406,0.02585,0.044061,0.012658]},{"p":"L07N1939-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the closing character of a Japanese-style text emoticon (kaomoji) made of two matching eye symbols joined by a mouth such as a period or underscore, e.g. the final part of -.-, ^.^, ^_^, O.O, o.o, -_-, T_T, *_*, =.=, O_o, typed into informal posts, comments or chat.","s":[0.700468,0.688696,0.853696,0.800613,0.864282,0.008995,0.007139,0.00827,-0.000404,0.009343,0.006337,0.005424,0.011166,0.003135,0.002552]},{"p":"L07N3549+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a pre-noun modifier sitting right after an article or possessive, inside a noun phrase whose head noun has not yet appeared, most strongly on intensifiers like \"particularly\" or \"especially\" in \"at a particularly bad time\" and on \"-old\" in \"his 18-year-old daughter\" (also \"an enrolled course\", \"a keen interest\", \"a diagnosed case\", \"more than 40 years\").","s":[0.607062,0.655889,0.50013,0.345333,0.370098,0.097339,0.033093,0.029626,0.259932,0.007998,0.023495,0.016958,0.030245,0.015743,0.032819]},{"p":"L07N3549-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the modifier word of a fixed two-word compound naming an event or activity, typically right after \"a/an\" and right before the head noun it predicts, as in \"a press conference/release\", \"a phone call\", \"a literature review\", \"a taste test\", \"a road trip\", \"an informational meeting\", \"a scavenger hunt\" (on the \"-enger\" piece).","s":[0.573373,0.533758,0.431573,0.471245,0.677953,0.12222,0.034165,0.027006,0.007552,0.00608,0.031223,0.006799,0.032724,0.02491,0.020441]},{"p":"L07N472+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a digit inside a multi-digit number, most often the second digit right after the first, when the number sits in a dense run of related numbers such as an ascending list of large integers, page ranges, footnote or index numbering, or table values, where the next digits are partly predictable from the numbers before.","s":[0.577236,0.304814,0.178402,0.277976,0.538314,0.021425,0.272966,0.021958,0.020984,0.061989,0.070782,0.060466,0.050809,0.051872,0.064824]},{"p":"L07N472-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a digit inside a multi-digit number (usually the second or third digit, not the first) when that number sits in a run of similar numbers sharing the same leading digits, such as descending counts, table rows of decimals, bracketed citation numbers or bug IDs, especially at the digit where the number first differs from its neighbor (e.g. the 6 in '271 ... 262', the 4 in '[153][155][154]').","s":[0.672879,0.339149,0.099013,0.148655,0.97717,0.030912,0.017755,0.028392,0.008637,0.012386,0.012106,0.018575,0.010704,0.004452,0.008396]},{"p":"L07N4960+","l":"UNREL","j":"00","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on two unrelated tokens: the surname Harris or Haris (especially the 'is' or 'arris' piece after 'Har' in a person's name, as in citations, author lists or roster lists), and the 'useState' hook inside a React import line like import React, {useState} from 'react'.","s":[0.477233,0.534962,0.214249,0.648326,0.620552,0.039146,0.065221,0.043013,0.030389,0.043011,0.027291,0.031679,0.029313,0.023465,0.04191],"u":{"0":[0.41223,0.648326,0.431498,0.837981,0.622181],"1":[0.534962,0.5125,0.530763,0.450204,0.478515]}},{"p":"L07N4960-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"In official-news or corporate-PR prose about dignitaries, fires on the verb (or its particle) right after an official's name or title that reports a ceremonial action, such as \"met with\", \"attended\", \"was present at\", \"were in attendance\", \"visited\", \"pointed out that\", \"spoke highly\", and also on the pronoun \"He\" that starts such a report and on \"-ai\" in Chinese names like Zhou Enlai or Lai.","s":[1.391677,0.979213,0.898883,0.637527,1.507698,0.038326,0.030972,0.011776,0.018524,0.007826,0.031424,0.049066,0.017522,0.011621,0.02126]},{"p":"L07N6119+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the digit or number word that follows an approximation word such as \"nearly\", \"almost\", \"close to\" or \"more than\", above all on the second digit piece of a round number (the \"0\" after \"nearly 2\" in \"nearly 200\", \"nearly 200,000\", \"almost 500\", \"over 1200\") and on number words such as \"nearly thirty\" or \"almost three score\", predicting that the round quantity continues.","s":[0.694079,0.46296,0.437945,0.577991,0.618693,0.022565,0.032396,0.095058,0.008788,0.156807,0.023947,0.011248,0.010435,0.005683,0.021615]},{"p":"L07N6119-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a single digit token (most often '2', also '1' or '0') that begins or continues a numeric quantity, strongest when it opens a count or duration that will be followed by a unit, such as recipe steps like 'simmer for 2 minutes' or 'refrigerate for 24 hours', 'about 2 blocks', 'more than 2 years', or a code-golf answer header's byte count ('Python, 139'); it also fires on inner zeros in model numbers like 'A1200'.","s":[0.522406,0.739743,0.181724,0.237241,0.169538,0.261569,0.017326,0.026554,0.042567,0.007203,0.017395,0.013519,0.012167,0.014137,0.005273]},{"p":"L07N6189+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on short mixed-case letter fragments in the middle of long random-looking Base64 or encoded blobs, especially just after the letters 'O'/'OM'/'GM' (pieces like ca, c, cd, Me, Mac, cc, dc), so it needs a long run of base64 characters before it and won't fire on ordinary prose.","s":[0.200358,0.977631,0.645454,0.874213,0.181487,0.007633,0.013436,0.029135,0.054294,0.042536,0.024182,0.03864,0.042616,0.03957,0.035493]},{"p":"L07N6189-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the inner or closing word-piece of a multi-token technical device name from energy storage and life-support technology, strongest on the 'ac' of 'supercapacitor', the 'O' of 'ECMO' (extracorporeal membrane oxygenation), 'cell' in 'hydrogen fuel cell', and 'ic' in 'hyperbaric chamber'; it does not fire on plain words like battery or capacitor.","s":[0.567007,0.892255,0.579656,0.523844,0.941714,0.020295,0.024191,0.16544,0.047208,0.046082,0.018615,0.007414,0.020118,0.010804,0.021579]},{"p":"L07N7370+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the middle word-piece \"alon\" right after \"Thess\" in the name Thessalonians / Thessaloniki / Thessalonica (the Bible book, the Greek city, or the ancient city), in any genre from Bible citations to travel ads; other words and plain mentions of Greece or Paul do not fire it.","s":[0.804847,0.82369,0.841977,0.70822,0.734076,0.01999,0.024719,0.006818,0.028675,0.016025,0.01908,0.021492,0.023678,0.109797,0.031445]},{"p":"L07N7370-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on verbs and nouns of physically pushing or shoving something away, such as push, pushed, shove, shoved, shoving, thrust, \"pushed forth\" and \"push on\", plus the same idea in other languages (French pousser/poussée, Italian spinto, Russian ottalkivanie); it peaks in narrative scenes where one person pushes another or an object, for example \"gave him a push\" or \"shoved him away\".","s":[0.139652,0.316388,0.144767,0.866226,0.306473,0.007046,1.1e-05,0.005087,0.000641,0.008863,0.006765,0.016626,0.012789,0.009985,0.010488]},{"p":"L07N7432+","l":"TWO-REL","j":"11","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the final digit of a four-digit number split as three digits plus one, above all the last \"0\" of \"1500\" (Ram 1500, 1500 ESPN, a 1500 SAT, 1500 students, 1500-1900), and more weakly on 1590 or 1580 inside ISBNs and standards; it also fires on the predictable completing piece of a fixed multi-token name (Isiah Thomas, Equal Exchange, Hosmer-Lemeshow).","s":[0.585758,0.245062,0.249527,0.354276,0.488361,0.006576,0.017031,0.020002,0.01918,0.010223,0.021554,0.008859,0.009331,0.005415,0.011086]},{"p":"L07N7432-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word piece that follows a starting \"mes\"/\"Mes\" token inside a longer word, by far most strongly on \"enter\" (and then \"ic\") in \"mesenteric\" (as in mesenteric ischemia, mesenteric vessels, mesenteric fat), and more weakly on other mes- words like mesocolic, mesocortical, mesocycle, mesangial and Mesosphere.","s":[0.895905,0.916163,0.972028,0.983386,1.020398,0.025729,0.128695,0.007468,0.003871,0.030188,0.003913,0.007957,0.022845,0.00746,0.012079]},{"p":"L07N9158+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the last word-piece that completes a rare, multi-token personal surname, right after the first name or the earlier pieces of the name, as in Mathieu van der Po|el, Ralph Haw|kes, Jörg Baber|owski, Bessel van der K|olk or Alan Belf|ield, especially in name lists, bylines and citations.","s":[0.77029,0.803229,0.804831,0.829894,0.775647,0.046054,0.003536,0.00642,0.008665,0.006922,0.016142,0.018529,0.012868,0.02678,0.014498]},{"p":"L07N9158-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word-piece that completes a short split word beginning with 'bor'/'B'/'b', most strongly the 'ax' of 'borax', the 'ate' of 'borate(s)', and the last digit of bomber codes like B-29 or YB-49 (weaker on 'oba' in 'boba'), not on the opening piece and not on words like 'boron' or 'boric'.","s":[0.780241,0.704089,0.720415,0.515271,0.635472,0.025085,0.466277,0.016169,0.003094,0.011455,0.007943,0.00666,0.002471,0.004271,0.008984]},{"p":"L08N158+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a token that continues or completes a name, acronym or stock phrase already used earlier in the same document, such as the inner piece of a repeated acronym (S.C.I., NCFR, ECMO, LWF), the second half of a repeated company name (Let's Nur|ture), or a repeated keyword pair ('free anatomy', 'book rock'). It is strongest deep inside long documents, so it is hard to reproduce in a short text.","s":[0.086458,0.028063,0.160505,0.079181,0.061753,0.079162,0.078611,0.190125,0.118595,0.037064,0.043956,0.032966,0.032232,0.015573,0.152742]},{"p":"L08N158-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on words inside the title of an inserted cross-link to another article in SEO blog prose, the headline that follows a label like \"See also\", \"Also Read:\", \"Read More:\", \"People Also Read:\" or \"Related:\", peaking on a word or year piece several tokens into that title (for example \"2\" in \"2024\", \"Companies\", \"Garden\").","s":[0.575604,0.471673,0.328247,0.445168,0.416457,0.052149,0.029195,0.091378,0.057401,0.04788,0.025671,0.003003,0.031612,0.06476,0.014039]},{"p":"L08N3398+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a perception or detection verb (find, finding, see, seeing, sense, reach) when its subject is an inanimate system such as a search box, program, computer, camera, sensor or battery, especially right after a negation like \"doesn't find\", \"isn't finding\", \"won't see\"; it fires more weakly on the agent slot of \"under the action/operation of\" and on passive \"by\" introducing a non-human agent.","s":[1.467682,0.760503,0.710951,0.259772,1.190744,0.182305,0.152681,0.076102,0.032847,0.022925,0.014613,0.028121,0.068111,0.021175,0.086435]},{"p":"L08N3398-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word \"title\" used as an HTML attribute name (title=\"...\" inside a tag such as <a href=\"\" title=\"\">, <span title=...>, or in code like attr('title') / setAttribute('title', ...)), especially right after another attribute inside an open HTML tag.","s":[0.7712,0.777874,0.79217,0.502076,0.897368,0.122149,0.219935,0.262021,0.241595,0.11558,0.014673,0.006555,0.008301,0.01137,0.011419]},{"p":"L08N4474+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on scraped web-page interface boilerplate, at the word or punctuation that completes a site button or link label dropped into page text, such as \"Show all categories\", \"See all formats\", \"View all 6 authors\", \"Play all\", \"print / save trees\", \"Write a review.\", \"Add to Favorites.\", \"Login Sign up.\", \"435K views 2 years ago\", and also on repeated HTML markup such as the closing p tag or class=\"field ...\".","s":[0.863187,0.781525,0.604878,0.239726,0.569115,0.033957,0.029234,0.059988,0.027241,0.034625,0.02769,0.030673,0.060944,0.027611,0.049404]},{"p":"L08N4474-","l":"TWO-REL","j":"11","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word pieces and words of 'reunite' (re-unit-ing, reun-ited, reunited, reunion, reuniting with) and on 'split up' / 'splitting up', i.e. the moment a story says people or groups (families, bands, lovers, friends) come back together or break apart, strongest on the verb piece itself (unit/ited/ite) or the particle 'up' after split.","s":[0.679168,0.821405,0.791977,0.868336,0.758969,0.327376,0.016989,0.05563,0.136723,0.210911,0.076752,0.014461,0.007465,0.012007,0.012321]},{"p":"L08N6028+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the middle word-piece \"ot\" in \"carotene\" when the word is split as car + ot + ene, most often in \"beta carotene\" / \"beta-carotene(s)\" in nutrition or biology prose. It does not fire on other words containing \"car\" or \"ot\", or on related pigments like lutein or lycopene.","s":[0.949836,0.832444,0.840033,1.062492,0.884898,0.70037,0.756677,0.45472,0.015808,0.212614,0.008176,0.004607,0.003279,0.007442,0.010678]},{"p":"L08N6028-","l":"UNREL","j":"00","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on two contexts: the `key` attribute written on a JSX element returned inside a React `.map()` loop (e.g. `<li key={item.id}>`, `<option key=...>`), with weaker hits on `active` in className strings; and the instruction words of homework or quiz prompts that tell the student what to do with each item (\"find x in each triangle\", \"write the truth value of each\", \"enter your answer in the provided box\", \"check all that apply\", \"choose the correct answer\", \"explain your answer\").","s":[0.625423,0.722895,0.592339,0.798275,1.087848,0.034257,0.061985,0.064614,0.050886,0.028176,0.058377,0.032396,0.072556,0.035631,0.044683],"u":{"0":[0.524623,0.724473,0.490505,0.735768,0.642025],"1":[0.883606,0.93734,0.872725,0.634864,0.449317]}},{"p":"L08N7371+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the second word-piece that completes a famous multi-token name, above all the \"bie\" of \"Herbie\" (as in jazz pianist Herbie Hancock or Herbie the Love Bug), and more weakly on the completing piece of other set names such as COBOL, Lord Acton, Covent Garden, or the \"computer\" in \"vacuum tube computer\".","s":[0.980043,0.867187,0.688822,0.961889,0.724179,0.135315,0.038119,0.092004,0.073642,0.02333,0.00921,0.097748,0.010853,0.035047,0.062568]},{"p":"L08N7371-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires inside the name of a famous historical invention or its inventor when it is being completed, such as the second word or later piece of \"internal combustion (engine)\", \"steam engine\", \"horseless carriage\", \"Model T\", \"Alexander Graham Bell\", \"Nicolaus Otto\", \"movable-type printing\", \"safety bicycle\" or \"kinetoscope\", in prose about the history of technology and inventions.","s":[1.019423,0.850833,0.631494,0.914218,0.802051,0.158224,0.407698,0.141323,0.166118,0.405199,0.024798,0.018207,0.014019,0.019191,0.024843]},{"p":"L08N8121+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the final word of a fixed multi-word organization or role name, especially \"Manager\" in \"Stage Manager\" (theatre credits) and \"Center\" in \"Pew Research Center\", plus the \"ISC\" in JISC; the word predicted once the opening word(s) of the set phrase have appeared.","s":[0.699179,0.754527,0.609953,0.652508,0.619303,0.062894,0.081271,0.043581,0.130139,0.058951,0.023879,0.015634,0.015296,0.015808,0.056006]},{"p":"L08N8121-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word \"guest\" (also \"guests\", \"Guest\", \"invited\", French \"invité\", Arabic \"ضيف\") used as a modifier for an outside person brought in to contribute to an event or show, as in guest speaker, guest star, guest lecturers, invited talks or invited writers.","s":[0.498006,0.586696,0.660427,0.715801,0.881537,0.240168,0.008877,0.095733,0.24833,0.475764,0.016549,0.007487,0.009825,0.007598,0.011529]},{"p":"L08N9187+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on short single-character tokens, most often \"V\", \"1\" or \"F\" and usually right after a digit like \"0\", deep inside a long run of random-looking Base64 data such as an embedded image or binary blob; it is strongest well into the blob, so short texts need a long unbroken Base64 string, and it does not fire on ordinary prose or code.","s":[0.508787,0.388745,0.381808,0.407848,0.370252,0.029836,0.033315,0.029007,0.029973,0.016149,0.024056,0.029642,0.021437,0.018804,0.015608]},{"p":"L08N9187-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a word in the middle of a famous fixed catchphrase or title, after its opening words have made the rest predictable, such as \"oh\" in \"Lions, tigers and bears, oh my\", \"With\" in \"From Russia With Love\", \"do\" in \"till death do us part\", \"Rule\"/\"To\" in \"One ring to rule them all\", \"see\"/\"here\" in \"nothing to see here\", \"more\" in \"See more at:\" and \"Great\" in \"for Great Good\".","s":[0.632537,0.545646,0.762385,0.4199,0.726274,0.061119,0.026939,0.032373,0.03233,0.058084,0.041678,0.018974,0.042041,0.018827,0.029102]},{"p":"L08N9674+","l":"TWO-REL","j":"11","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires at the boundary where a new numbered or labeled item begins: the period or closing parenthesis right after an item label such as \"Q4.\", \"Q1.\", \"9).\" or \"2.\" (or the first word or digit of that label) in FAQs, interview Q&As, quizzes and numbered steps, and likewise on the word \"Hint\" opening a parenthetical aside like \"(Hint: ...)\".","s":[1.191009,0.803481,0.124929,0.029627,0.576401,0.016121,0.145733,0.037856,0.036212,0.094143,0.020008,0.061452,0.01389,0.036218,0.041952]},{"p":"L08N9674-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the digit \"5\" right after \"1.\" in the number 1.5 (any quantity: 1.5°C warming targets, 1.5 miles, 1.5 ton AC, 1.5-litre, 1.5 generation, 1.5 years old), strongest in climate texts about the 1.5°C limit, but not on other decimals like 2.5 or 1.4.","s":[0.761224,0.701958,0.360348,0.651385,0.972346,0.325622,0.502571,0.021327,0.456449,0.63066,0.019908,0.028653,0.020493,0.020441,0.021876]},{"p":"L09N1117+","l":"TWO-REL","j":"11","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires inside the computer-arithmetic phrase \"two's complement\" / \"2's complement\" (strongest on the 's after two/2 and on the complement pieces, and also on \"complement\" alone when it means signed binary encoding), plus the digit tokens inside integer-limit constants like 4294967291 or 2147483647 in text about signed/unsigned overflow.","s":[1.076965,0.990852,0.964651,0.91863,0.645792,0.706468,0.096342,0.002492,0.07481,0.2735,0.008329,0.002976,0.001909,0.004822,0.008891]},{"p":"L09N1117-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires inside low-level bits-and-bytes computing text: on the middle digits of binary strings (11111010, 0b1111_1011), on powers of two (1024, 16384, 65536, 512, 192-bit), and on words like bit, Kbit, decoded, and Decoder in contexts about bit widths, byte packing, registers, hex and base64 encoding, and bus addresses.","s":[0.814164,0.851341,0.806714,0.314479,1.29915,0.027916,0.007561,0.019123,0.019894,0.016335,0.045796,0.014025,0.018055,0.028435,0.019953]},{"p":"L09N1287+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"In construction and engineering-industry writing, fires on the token that completes an engineering credential or firm name, such as the PE or P.E. after an engineer's name, the end of a big engineering consultancy name (Arup, Mott MacDonald, Bechtel, Aurecon, Stantec, CH2M), or a closely tied term like \"signed and sealed\", Design-Build or sub-consultants.","s":[0.832368,1.10757,0.558892,1.157568,0.944621,0.282523,0.330301,0.026967,0.185462,0.022105,0.012235,0.021143,0.023082,0.018936,0.005443]},{"p":"L09N1287-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the token that names shear stress in mechanics-of-materials calculations: the word \"shear\" in phrases like \"maximum shear stress\" or \"primary shear\", the Greek tau symbol, or \"V\" in \"due to V\", mostly in solution-manual style text about beams, welds, shafts and torsion.","s":[0.799075,0.7458,0.760721,0.855147,0.910798,0.56536,0.282302,0.362832,0.276168,0.055693,0.003769,0.010595,0.008302,0.005223,0.006286]},{"p":"L09N1795+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the pieces of statistical-analysis method terms, especially the word pieces inside \"cross-tabulation\"/\"crosstab\" (the \"tab\", \"-tab\", \"stab\", \"ros\" pieces), \"exploratory\"/\"confirmatory\" (the \"explor\"/\"atory\" pieces), \"descriptive (statistics)\", \"univariate\" and \"transpose\", when they appear in survey-research or data-analysis writing (theses, notebooks, R/pandas code).","s":[0.717401,1.175749,0.721294,1.121437,0.861683,0.26754,0.069736,0.082436,0.016259,0.12252,0.004769,0.008771,0.016925,0.01113,0.011474]},{"p":"L09N1795-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"In APA-style statistical reporting of test results inside a sentence, fires on the label of the next statistic, especially the effect-size letter \"d\" (or the \"=\" right after it) following \"t(98) = 2.09, p = .04,\", and similarly \"U =\" after a Mann-Whitney mention or \"d.f.\" after a test result.","s":[0.656185,0.537473,0.743968,0.658576,0.934043,0.014881,0.27794,0.049807,0.005225,0.028536,0.017288,0.029399,0.032755,0.026586,0.01416]},{"p":"L09N6913+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"In unit-test code, fires on the argument-matcher and assertion-API tokens of mocking/assertion libraries, such as Moq's \"It.IsAny\"/\"It.Is\" and \"Times.Once\"/\"Times.Exactly\" inside Setup/Verify calls, Jest/Jasmine's \"toHaveBeenCalledWith\", \"toMatchObject\", \"expect.any\", \"and.callThrough\", and NUnit's \"Has.Property\", with the strongest firing in mock verification calls.","s":[0.971081,0.949131,0.802318,0.767819,0.607146,0.010364,0.005538,0.029803,0.005055,0.004588,0.010594,0.009,0.013614,0.006365,0.002486]},{"p":"L09N6913-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on \"when\" (sometimes \"if\" or \"on\") that opens a reduced conditional clause right after a noun or noun phrase describing a thing's property or effect, typically followed by a bare past participle, as in \"turns blue when cooked\", \"toxic if not administered\", \"a button that, when clicked\", \"damaging if released\".","s":[0.464603,0.615821,0.492554,0.591595,0.550648,0.021696,0.169522,0.235751,0.465855,0.216894,0.012006,0.011542,0.031788,0.014092,0.013316]},{"p":"L09N7091+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word \"train\"/\"training\"/\"trained\" (and close neighbors like drill, readiness, mobilisation, regular, part-time) when it describes soldiers, militia or army units being trained or prepared for service, in military-history prose, e.g. \"the militia would train one night a week\" or \"the troops began training for the assault\".","s":[0.583685,0.945347,0.762011,0.75537,0.711865,0.013354,0.015564,0.062709,0.005603,0.047624,0.018612,0.015038,0.023074,0.006486,0.015]},{"p":"L09N7091-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the second word of a fixed military-institution or military-jargon compound, where the preceding word (often 'War', 'General', 'Command and', 'high', 'Forward', 'Operational') makes a military term predictable: 'Staff' in 'Command and General Staff College', 'College' in 'Army War College', 'staff' in 'staff officer'/'Haig's staff', 'ground' in 'high ground', 'civilian' in 'civilian leaders/population', 'Observer' in 'Forward Observer'.","s":[0.831216,0.717729,0.390869,0.905783,0.165979,0.016751,0.01628,0.010278,0.059557,0.019013,0.017537,0.016286,0.016813,0.01721,0.017018]},{"p":"L09N7138+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"In leisure and social-scene prose (bars, pubs, parties, weddings, nightlife, jazz/pop music writing, city shopping), this pole fires on the head noun completing a short noun phrase, most strongly on serving containers like \"a bucket of beer\" or \"a mug of tea\" and on the word \"Street\" in named streets like \"52nd Street\", \"Oxford Street\" or \"Main Street\".","s":[0.467872,0.224826,0.736175,0.368188,0.643063,0.162992,0.122646,0.255677,0.175845,0.059481,0.025618,0.035238,0.032501,0.028941,0.028689]},{"p":"L09N7138-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word naming a formal social dance or partner dance: \"ball\" in the dance-party sense (masked ball, debutante ball, Opera Ball, ballroom) and the \"alt\" piece of \"waltz\", plus nearby words like \"dances\" and an offered arm in a ballroom scene. It does not fire on \"ball\" as a sports object.","s":[1.195625,0.972237,0.912178,0.768432,0.736541,0.012423,0.020285,0.365467,0.015735,0.25385,0.007908,0.010115,0.00724,0.012834,0.01032]},{"p":"L09N7283+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires inside English prose about programming-language theory and formal methods — domain-specific languages (DSL), modeling/specification languages, formal verification and semantics, model-based testing — peaking on the key term itself such as \"DSL\", \"domain-specific language\", \"modeling language\", \"formal methods\", or the name Benjamin Pierce, rather than on generic software or everyday uses of 'language'.","s":[0.842935,0.641384,0.383019,0.726383,0.629249,0.038412,0.016638,0.014208,0.02329,0.024394,0.029782,0.02028,0.028705,0.029887,0.034968]},{"p":"L09N7283-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"In dense computer-science / formal-methods technical prose (model checking, verification, type systems, database schemas), fires on the first word or word-piece of a technical compound modifier that is about to be completed, such as \"stochastic\" or \"probabil-\" before \"model/istic\", \"data\" in \"data-driven\", \"object\" in \"object-oriented\", \"field\" in \"field-programmable\", or \"random\" before \"simulations\".","s":[0.957794,0.308138,0.585118,0.108194,0.796208,0.053516,0.344041,0.189976,0.029141,0.26275,0.056495,0.059622,0.03218,0.041915,0.058462]},{"p":"L09N9062+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word \"keep\" (also kept, keeping, keeper, retained) or on the token right after it, when it means choosing to retain something rather than discard it, in a keep-versus-throw-away decision (which photos to keep and which to delete, which features were kept, keep it and mount it, keep or discard), and also on \"throw out\" or \"discard\" when used as the opposite choice.","s":[0.543961,0.424832,0.606015,0.577514,1.04678,0.158937,0.100303,0.109052,0.037961,0.01005,0.00719,0.010793,0.003326,0.009554,0.006735]},{"p":"L09N9062-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a seeking or anticipating verb, or its particle, inside an infinitive or 'before you…' clause about what to anticipate or look for, most strongly on 'expect' in 'know what to expect' / 'not sure what to expect', and also on 'for' in 'what to look for' or 'search for', and on 'look' or 'search' in 'before you look/search'.","s":[0.227274,0.584688,0.688887,0.260855,0.662791,0.084117,0.031075,0.146922,0.222763,0.078087,0.075328,0.029667,0.017075,0.021784,0.049242]},{"p":"L09N9439+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"In source code, fires on the extra whitespace token (a space or tab run) that sits between the end of a code statement and a trailing inline comment marker on the same line, such as the gap before // or # in \"x = 5;  // set x\"; it also fires weakly on whitespace-only lines and on wide column gaps in hex dumps or two-column name lists.","s":[0.47173,0.13203,0.62695,0.934572,0.432053,0.056411,0.045873,0.033128,0.206435,0.044603,0.041234,0.073292,0.055226,0.052509,0.055962]},{"p":"L09N9439-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a token that is itself a run of line breaks producing an extra-wide blank gap, such as a period or closing parenthesis fused with three newlines (\".\\n\\n\\n\") or a bare triple newline between sections or list entries, and in C# code on an indentation-only blank line or an opening brace followed by an empty line (\" {\\n\\n\"); ordinary single or double line breaks and normal words do not fire it.","s":[0.514025,0.485224,0.187354,0.593909,0.214497,0.035474,0.028507,0.020073,0.013268,0.009849,0.015542,0.020355,0.01595,0.023867,0.016739]},{"p":"L10N1446+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires inside heavy-ion / nuclear collider physics text, peaking on the word \"Ion\" in the name \"Relativistic Heavy Ion Collider (RHIC)\" and also on nearby words like Heavy, Collider, RHIC, nuclear, gold or lead ions and particle collisions, especially in Brookhaven Lab-style science news. It stays quiet on generic physics or chemistry \"ion\" text that does not concern colliders.","s":[1.008291,0.805006,0.942888,0.781931,0.79269,0.341694,0.080997,0.030631,0.177075,0.021289,0.008889,0.007783,0.004525,0.006361,0.004976]},{"p":"L10N1446-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on the boilerplate of forum and support-thread pages (bbPress, WPML, Toolset, Spiceworks and similar), at the words of the thread header or footer such as \"has 2 voices\", \"last updated\", \"Viewing 3 posts - 1 through 3 (of 3 total)\" and the \"#\" before a post ID, so that the model can predict the next piece of forum metadata.","s":[1.093312,1.288154,0.928551,0.405707,1.232858,0.006511,0.018057,0.004579,0.004994,0.006924,0.019936,0.011073,0.022017,0.019297,0.020325]},{"p":"L10N3502+","l":"TWO-REL","j":"11","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the second digit of a multi-digit number (especially the \"0\" in 10, 100, 10000) when the text is about digits, place value or number bases (decimal/hex/binary conversion tables, digit-extraction code with /10 and %10, number-to-word mappings), i.e. the number ten as the base of the decimal system, and more weakly on similar word pieces such as \"ological\" in \"phonological\".","s":[0.815329,0.319852,0.432301,0.435499,0.383861,0.177009,0.009849,0.216453,0.396667,0.182295,0.027587,0.029233,0.029014,0.012619,0.015148]},{"p":"L10N3502-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a single letter (or a letter's name) that is being talked about as a letter or sound rather than used in a word, e.g. the t and i in \"dot your i's and cross your t's\", \"popping the p\", \"rolling the r\", \"dropping her g's\", \"months with an r\", \"drop r after vowels\", or Hebrew letter names like yod/nun, peaking on the letter token itself or the 's / closing quote right after it.","s":[0.652413,0.530239,0.760334,0.76414,0.360291,0.007574,0.007866,0.137864,0.007916,0.014786,0.006107,0.005799,0.004793,0.004688,0.005766]},{"p":"L10N3733+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on words that name passivity or inaction set against taking initiative, chiefly \"passive\" (as in passive income or a passive role), \"sit back\" (on \"back\"), \"wait\"/\"waiting\" after \"sit back and\" or \"don't\", \"victim\" (mindset) and \"reactive\", typically in a contrast with being active or proactive.","s":[0.672572,0.879488,0.878632,1.082477,0.516304,0.070113,0.00724,0.016266,0.038,0.04313,0.024674,0.010242,0.019317,0.032489,0.015493]},{"p":"L10N3733-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the words naming or listing the fundamental forces of physics and their unification — \"fundamental forces\", \"the four forces\", \"strong (nuclear)\", \"weak\", \"unified/unification\" (as in grand unified theory, unifying gravity with the other forces) — in popular-physics or cosmology prose.","s":[0.843331,0.80369,0.745877,0.872333,0.774731,0.002647,0.006853,0.004589,0.002695,0.066069,0.003786,0.006285,-0.000722,0.005903,0.006484]},{"p":"L10N5025+","l":"UNREL","j":"00","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on the token where a gaze verb or a CSS-style text-colour property is complete and the next word is predictable: in fiction, the 'into' after 'eyes bored' or the 'with' after 'fixed him/the assassin' (a stare or look follows), and in code, the 'color' (or 'fg') property name in CSS, inline styles, JS style assignments and tkinter that is followed by a colour value.","s":[0.888265,0.682435,0.68878,0.709733,0.726408,0.144234,0.00534,0.119132,0.326663,0.048158,0.007354,0.012662,0.012795,0.010273,0.013279],"u":{"0":[0.933512,0.795604,0.515977,0.395667,0.352889],"1":[0.656242,0.619097,0.660394,0.735924,0.726021]}},{"p":"L10N5025-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"In narrative fiction prose describing a character's body language, voice or build, fires on the word inside the physical-description phrase, especially \"broad\" in \"a broad smile/grin/shoulders\", \"ear\" in \"whispered in her ear\", \"strong\" in \"said in a strong, hoarse voice\", \"powerfully\" in \"powerfully built\", and verbs like \"tugged\" in \"a smile tugged at his lips\" or \"pulled\"/\"reached out\" in a gesture; the same words in abstract or non-bodily senses (\"a broad range\", \"strong economy\") should not fire.","s":[0.701056,0.818318,0.655844,0.549366,0.405319,0.042286,0.240744,0.041782,0.158519,0.042413,0.053167,0.041197,0.072024,0.042345,0.047334]},{"p":"L10N8000+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires in Christian theological writing on the Romans 8 / Galatians 4 theme of believers' spiritual adoption as God's children: on the words \"adoption\"/\"adopted\", \"sons\", \"daughters\", \"children\" (as in sons and daughters of God), \"Abba\" Father, and on the chapter number 8 (or its verse digits) in citations of Romans 8.","s":[1.459944,1.198392,0.884984,0.998189,1.19844,0.072761,0.174264,0.143302,0.013779,0.032637,0.008024,0.021576,0.008098,0.001937,0.010398]},{"p":"L10N8000-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"In Christian theological prose, fires on the word that names human agency as the thing salvation or God's work does NOT come from, i.e. the noun or pronoun right after phrases like \"by our own\", \"not of\", \"through their own\" in grace-versus-works contrasts (\"not by our own power/efforts/works/merits\", \"not of yourselves\", \"not of man\").","s":[0.977248,0.561831,0.713299,1.02476,0.389874,0.232969,0.155762,0.051063,0.190484,0.059945,0.015179,0.006824,0.009837,0.007864,0.013636]},{"p":"L10N8041+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the second word (or word-piece) of a fixed multi-word proper name or title, where the first word already sets up a predictable completion, most strongly the road-type word in street addresses like \"U.S. Highway 6\", \"County Road 73C\", \"West State Highway 155\" or \"300 Lakeside Drive\", and similarly on \"Executive\" after \"Chief\", \"Museum\" after \"State\"/\"Victoria\", \"College(s)\" after a college name, \"World\" in \"Mammal Species of the World\".","s":[0.614009,0.387676,0.354569,0.564142,0.782866,0.163669,0.02224,0.070441,0.033417,0.323197,0.002777,0.050047,0.101223,0.016703,0.00646]},{"p":"L10N8041-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the suffix morpheme that closes a unit inside an East Asian (Korean/Chinese/Japanese) institutional or postal address string, such as 대학 in 'OO대학교 의과대학', 系/學/學院/部 in Chinese university department names in author affiliations or thesis citations, 호 (unit number) or 구 (district) in Korean street addresses, and 県 in Japanese addresses, with a similar weaker effect on date or address suffix pieces in other non-English languages.","s":[1.040442,0.452537,0.539847,0.818228,0.959773,0.178477,0.098635,0.039332,0.044438,0.032929,0.045959,0.055881,0.04475,0.033527,0.044639]},{"p":"L10N8655+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the preposition that introduces the means, instrument or method by which an action is done, right after the verb phrase and before the tool or method itself, as in \"put him away with the Eiffel Tower\", \"spreading it with the palm\", \"track progress through analytics\", \"achieved though heat\" (also misspelled \"though\", \"by means of\", Spanish \"con\"), but not on \"with\" meaning accompaniment or possession.","s":[0.405647,0.673197,0.596584,0.446194,0.480723,0.022189,0.005098,0.014176,0.025346,0.189646,0.006587,0.023909,0.00404,0.013386,0.002864]},{"p":"L10N8655-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the preposition or conjunction (in, within, when, if, as, on, once, upon) that comes right after a passive participle saying something can be done or achieved (\"can be resolved in\", \"can be eliminated in\", \"can be overcome when\", \"can be achieved when\", \"can be reduced if\"), the point where the text is about to name the means, place or condition by which the result is attained.","s":[0.356016,1.047223,0.785457,0.442962,0.174272,0.274541,0.22432,0.022738,0.055082,0.042823,0.040499,0.024528,0.046898,0.10255,0.069066]},{"p":"L10N9304+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"In dense scientific or academic research prose (abstracts, methods, results, reference lists), this pole fires on a technical modifier or a piece of a technical word, such as \"physiological\", \"psychological\", \"peripheral\", \"frequency\", \"temperature\", \"physico-chemical\" or \"electrocardiogram\", that sits inside a jargon noun phrase where a technical head noun comes next.","s":[0.849559,0.734963,0.543849,0.469252,0.199659,0.044542,0.048555,0.434857,0.266553,0.047923,0.097001,0.073215,0.076279,0.033933,0.029687]},{"p":"L10N9304-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"In scientific results prose, fires on the words that state the direction or presence of a reported effect or association (e.g. 'positive', 'negative', 'no', 'up'/'down' in up-/downregulated, 'regulated', 'positively', 'inversely', and the following 'effect'/'relationship'), especially in phrases like 'had no effect', 'a significant positive correlation', 'up- or downregulated genes'.","s":[0.556771,0.505019,0.858558,0.428949,0.7658,0.012813,0.022011,0.023971,0.046525,0.058903,0.041032,0.021529,0.02092,0.047194,0.031391]},{"p":"L10N9457+","l":"TWO-REL","j":"11","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the words of compound computer-science technique names, chiefly \"tail\" and its partners in \"tail call\", \"tail recursion\" and \"tail-recursive\", and also \"hash table\", \"transposition table\", \"cut off\"/\"cutset\" and \"stack-safe\", when they name an optimization in programming or game-search contexts (with a weak offshoot on musical \"D.C./D.S. al coda\" jump directions).","s":[0.669423,0.782485,0.565443,0.710851,0.580771,0.153553,0.174246,0.029934,0.027285,0.02849,0.024095,0.01693,0.012098,0.01205,0.018391]},{"p":"L10N9457-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"In sports or game strategy discussion (chess analysis, golf club and shot talk, poker), fires on the function word, preposition or verb particle that comes right before a game-specific board or field location or technical term, such as \"off\" in \"hit it off the deck/tee/fairway\", \"the\" in \"opening up the kingside\", \"advances the passers\" or \"open the face\", predicting a sport-jargon noun next.","s":[0.333394,0.733338,0.397409,0.851736,0.252797,0.017341,0.009035,0.043447,0.049219,0.033125,0.002898,0.035572,0.01966,0.011925,0.016341]},{"p":"L11N1446+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the words inside the boilerplate statistical-methods sentence of a clinical or biomedical paper that states how data were summarized and compared: \"Baseline characteristics were compared between groups using t-tests, chi-square…\", \"Data are expressed as mean ± standard deviation\", \"presented as medians with interquartile ranges\", including on the ± sign and words like compared, as, means, deviation, t, chi.","s":[0.869416,0.557626,0.848812,0.84073,0.788468,0.006979,0.053183,0.015115,0.000195,0.024297,0.008102,0.009181,0.002552,0.009728,0.003949]},{"p":"L11N1446-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires inside machine-learning / NLP technical text and code (sequence labeling, NER, CRFs, BiLSTM, segmentation losses like Jaccard/Dice, tag/label ids), most strongly on pieces of ML technical terms and acronyms such as \"Conditional Random Fields (CRFs)\", \"RNNs\", \"bilstm\", \"Jaccard\", \"ignore_index\", \"tag_ids\" or IOB tags, while ordinary non-ML prose or code stays silent.","s":[1.239033,0.962015,0.793218,0.483949,0.998935,0.025029,0.008581,0.007622,0.021561,0.014028,0.004942,0.014214,0.012192,0.012784,0.012053]},{"p":"L11N2586+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the dot-product operator between two vectors: the token \"dot\"/\"Dot\"/\".dot\" in code like a.dot(b), np.dot(, Vector3.Dot(, the separator inside a dot-product call such as the comma in glm::dot(x, y), and the math dot operator · or \\cdot or \".\" between two vectors (s^·z^, A.B, \\mathbf_i \\cdot \\mathbf_j), strongest in vector-geometry code and math such as ray tracing, normals and cosine similarity.","s":[0.523083,0.386398,0.613212,0.575559,0.496393,0.003212,0.103297,0.083576,0.017529,0.260155,0.005182,0.005528,0.00274,0.009841,0.003954]},{"p":"L11N2586-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the arithmetic operator (mostly \" *\", also \" /\", \" +\", \" -\") placed right after a vector-valued expression in code or math, as in Unity C# lines like \"transform.forward * speed\", \"direction.normalized * radius\" or \"force / mass\", i.e. scaling a direction/vector by a scalar.","s":[0.700583,0.837647,0.324051,0.781384,0.255576,0.013826,0.010083,0.047349,0.015306,0.007692,0.024377,0.026711,0.026466,0.01909,0.048965]},{"p":"L11N394+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires across the words of a classic placement-exam / aptitude-test arithmetic word problem (train of given length crossing a bridge or pole at a speed, milk replaced by water, mixture ratios, Venn-style counting, blood-relation puzzles), strongest on tokens in the middle of the problem statement such as the train, its length, units and verbs like crosses or replaced.","s":[0.904531,0.928597,0.146056,0.91519,0.80576,0.025424,0.023705,-0.002643,0.407158,-0.000828,0.00432,0.008118,0.004944,0.00423,0.007828]},{"p":"L11N394-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires inside Indian government-job, competitive-exam and university boilerplate (recruitment notices, admit cards, solved question papers, UGC NET), peaking on the institutional words and year digits of names like Bihar Public Service Commission, Gauhati University, UGC NET exam, and the '2' of 2023/2024 in phrases like 'Recruitment 2023' or 'Admit Card 2023'.","s":[0.986235,1.105198,0.953126,0.354016,0.64282,0.068001,0.046906,0.058851,0.078594,0.134828,0.061826,0.091019,0.077952,0.06187,0.028056]},{"p":"L11N4144+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on first-person plural pronouns (\"us\", \"our\", \"we\", \"we're\") and on the word right after them (\"our mouths\", \"our lives\", \"we know\", \"we're familiar\"), especially when a speaker who has been writing as \"I\" suddenly says \"we/us/our\" to mean a group or people in general.","s":[0.449278,0.195648,0.286239,0.346816,0.100103,0.12367,0.015445,0.12445,0.181462,0.065362,0.017372,0.017344,0.02107,0.013013,0.033891]},{"p":"L11N4144-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on plural personal pronouns that refer to a group of people, chiefly first-person plural \"we/our/us\" (\"asked if we can switch\", \"Welcome to our page\", \"Let us show you\") and plural or audience-addressing \"you\" (\"you guys\", \"we have told you\", Hindi \"aap log\", Chinese \"nǐmen\"); it does not fire on singular I/me/my.","s":[0.203689,0.54603,1.144916,0.621692,1.186556,0.081902,0.073132,0.066712,0.096034,0.095505,0.058965,0.130583,0.060231,0.023187,0.035326]},{"p":"L11N4304+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"In patent claim language, fires on the connective word right after a dependent-claim reference such as \"The method of claim 1,\" or \"according to claim 12\", mainly on \"wherein\" and \"further\" (and \"including\" after \"further\"), the word that opens the added limitation.","s":[0.732041,0.978401,0.682689,0.752819,0.56697,0.83941,0.177391,0.503601,0.162686,0.410439,0.01024,0.005118,0.017404,0.006879,0.007841]},{"p":"L11N4304-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word or piece that completes the name of a hotel or motel chain or lodging, such as \"Inn\" in Days Inn, Hampton Inn, Holiday Inn, Comfort Inn, Hilton Garden Inn and Quality Inn, \"8\" in Super 8, \"Suites\" in Country Inn & Suites, \"Express\" in Holiday Inn Express, \"is\" in ibis, \"ft\" in Aloft, \"el\" in Motel, or the word \"motel\" itself, in any prose that mentions a place to stay.","s":[0.768633,0.854593,0.358883,0.681287,0.858621,0.131458,0.026601,0.024726,0.038932,0.044081,0.047992,0.032174,0.026446,0.008254,0.02112]},{"p":"L11N5238+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the token \" KEY\" immediately after \"FOREIGN\" in SQL foreign-key constraint definitions (e.g. CONSTRAINT `x_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`col`) REFERENCES ...), not on PRIMARY KEY, UNIQUE KEY, or the word \"foreign\" in prose.","s":[0.852324,0.733517,0.691176,0.82383,0.708993,0.16275,0.126165,0.001148,0.213478,0.348306,0.017625,0.024015,0.021955,0.014442,0.013325]},{"p":"L11N5238-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the name of one member of a formal 'kinds of X' classification, especially the latter or rarer items in a short enumerated set: Archaea or Bacteria among the three domains of life, null or undefined among JavaScript's special types, Error versus Exception in Java, and mythic races such as Titans, giants, jinn or Jotunheim in Greek, Norse or Islamic lore.","s":[0.862615,0.756957,0.985615,0.568797,0.546753,0.004957,0.050219,0.018258,0.072081,0.029897,0.006258,0.010448,0.009834,0.009516,0.005485]},{"p":"L11N6388+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"In narratives of battles and troop deployments, fires on the position word naming a part of an army's formation — \"left\", \"right\", \"centre/center\", \"front\", \"line\", \"wing\" — right after \"the\", \"his\", \"our\", \"their\" or \"the enemy's\", as in \"the right wing\", \"our left flank\", \"defeated all along the line\", \"on a four-mile front\".","s":[0.863785,0.795038,0.593114,1.113053,0.330799,0.226583,0.017885,0.201384,0.024018,0.008541,0.002962,0.015462,0.026968,0.021962,0.01872]},{"p":"L11N6388-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on words describing physical motion of an object through space, above all \"across\" right after a throw/slide/kick verb (\"threw the book across the room\", \"slid across the snow\"), and also on high-speed travel words such as \"speeds\", \"velocity\", \"glides\", \"moving\", \"interstellar\", \"Centauri\" in passages about spacecraft travel.","s":[0.721935,0.733934,0.166068,0.494302,0.516603,0.023956,0.57964,0.013521,0.015833,0.013664,0.024426,0.018918,0.0111,0.015504,0.004738]},{"p":"L11N6730+","l":"TWO-REL","j":"11","f":0,"e":0,"x":"In C#/.NET unit-test code (NUnit, xUnit, MSTest), this fires on the actual-result slot of an assertion: the token right after the comma in Assert.AreEqual(expected, actual) or Assert.Equal(expected, result), and the '=' in 'var actual = sut.Method(...)' or 'var result = ...' when that line produces the value to be checked against an expected value.","s":[0.74421,0.758782,0.759508,0.540846,1.098491,0.201781,0.303598,0.50188,0.063452,0.272819,0.033607,0.215271,0.020625,0.009538,0.054884]},{"p":"L11N6730-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires inside code, especially unit-test files full of assert/expect lines, on a token that completes a pattern already seen earlier in the same file: the expected value after == or toEqual( that echoes an earlier variable (== h1, toEqual(Mocks.players), new FloatC(Value)), or 'range' in a repeated 'for i in range(' comprehension; it also fires on a repeated technical phrase such as the S in 'thermal AdS'.","s":[0.056814,0.819237,0.47929,0.300432,0.733072,0.091647,0.028293,0.345537,0.034858,0.296179,0.058355,0.039996,0.045553,0.063547,0.029138]},{"p":"L11N7921+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the key word naming a bound, a fixed level or an entitlement at the point a claim turns on it: \"right\" in \"there is no constitutional/fundamental right to…\", \"entitled\", \"requirement\", \"threshold\", \"upper limit/level\", \"stall speed\", \"stable/unstable/stability\" as a predicate (\"flat surfaces are stable if…\"), and code words for fixed bounds (\"constants\", \"const\", SQL MAXVALUE).","s":[0.984917,0.380346,0.327703,0.104757,0.822824,0.057575,0.021341,0.061151,0.040419,0.101948,0.038405,0.070806,0.101656,0.055756,0.062612]},{"p":"L11N7921-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the function word or generic noun inside a set phrase that points to a recognized category or end of a scale, such as \"the\" in \"any of the signs/symptoms/following\", \"of\" in \"the high/lower/upper end of\", or the noun in \"given the maximum\", \"the standard\", \"the costlier side\", \"risk factors\"; it marks a slot about to be filled by a known, conventional set or extreme.","s":[0.669905,0.907715,0.216615,0.011647,0.68818,0.017588,0.020121,0.014629,0.03474,0.0238,0.031875,0.035931,0.035539,0.028008,0.005005]},{"p":"L11N9054+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word \"bird\"/\"birds\"/\"Bird\" (including the \"bird\" piece of \"birding\") in birdwatching or nature-reserve prose, e.g. \"over 250 species of birds\", \"a good selection of other birds\", \"superb birding\", \"Bird Photo Quiz\"; more weakly on other topic nouns that a document keeps returning to, especially when they modify a following noun (muscle growth, thermal radiation, maintenance cost).","s":[0.828857,0.700641,0.489015,0.319799,0.688001,0.410365,0.179951,0.200464,0.168135,0.106228,0.196326,0.190925,0.26706,0.089401,0.166944]},{"p":"L11N9054-","l":"UNREL","j":"00","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires broadly on tokens inside sports and athletic-performance writing, such as coaches, races, Olympians, training, race results, F1, cycling, cricket and tennis scores, and especially on numbers, list items and name pieces in such passages; a weaker second home is welding and metal-fabrication specs (filler rod, E6010/7018 electrodes, 6G tests).","s":[0.245253,0.465838,0.386528,0.121653,0.418718,0.035993,0.077768,0.029925,0.056469,0.258413,0.039941,0.022591,0.01943,0.016834,0.043029],"u":{"0":[0.26363,0.245253,0.484022,0.031572,0.348272],"1":[0.74243,0.732779,0.4418,0.890283,0.814199]}},{"p":"L12N1451+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"In scientific or medical paper prose, fires on the number right after a figure or table reference word (\"Figure 2\", \"Fig. 1\", \"Table 3\"), especially inside parentheses citing a result, i.e. the figure/table number token itself, not the word \"Figure\" or numbers elsewhere.","s":[0.277435,0.350914,0.2811,0.19643,0.230961,0.010517,0.01026,7.6e-05,0.01669,0.031013,0.007301,0.017656,0.015862,0.025404,0.015581]},{"p":"L12N1451-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the first word or label where readable text resumes right after a run of garbled PDF-extracted math (strings of numbers, single-letter variables, stray parentheses and spaces, as in a textbook solutions manual), e.g. \"Thus\", \"So\", \"From\", \"Solving\", \"Substituting\", \"Step\", \"(b)\", \"(d)\" or \"Eq.\" following jumbled equation fragments.","s":[0.042956,0.048348,0.073261,0.031719,0.02486,0.041201,0.05527,0.035692,0.039322,0.036135,0.01473,0.045635,0.065929,0.069127,0.044425]},{"p":"L12N2605+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Marks the switch to the 'otherwise / negative answer' branch: in code it fires on `else` right after an if-block, or on the indentation or closing brace that follows an early `return`/`break` inside an if; in prose it fires on 'No' or the comma or period right after it when a yes/no question is answered negatively ('Do we always call the police? No, ...', 'Will we know everything? No, but...').","s":[0.095977,0.43851,0.650018,0.577273,0.033071,0.00811,0.0242,0.018735,0.009488,0.005952,0.012919,0.022909,0.010662,0.018366,0.013516]},{"p":"L12N2605-","l":"TWO-REL","j":"11","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word that stands in for an affirmative answer to a yes/no question just asked: 'so' in 'If so,' / 'I think so' and 'yes' in replies like 'Well, yes.' / 'A: Yes,' / 'Unfortunately, yes,' / 'Yes and no', right after a question in Q&A, FAQ or forum text.","s":[0.064525,0.050855,0.383857,0.079446,0.124645,0.02872,0.050855,0.168208,0.079446,0.044239,0.016492,0.033139,0.060481,0.006855,0.051649]},{"p":"L12N4813+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a token in the middle of a well-known fixed multi-token term, where the rest of the term is already determined. The clearest cases are sports honors and philosophy words. In sports, it fires on the 'All' or 'Team' or 'Gold' inside phrases like 'four-time All-Star', 'All-Conference First Team', 'Gold Glove winner', 'McDonald's All-American' and 'Rookie of the Year'. In philosophy, it fires on the interior piece of a long -ism or -ist word built from a thinker's name or school, like 'Sartrean existentialism', 'transcendentalist' or 'Jainism'.","s":[0.704373,0.429478,0.506887,0.594485,0.357615,0.01277,0.077051,0.017814,0.025541,0.020991,0.064825,0.040609,0.06577,0.074602,0.047736]},{"p":"L12N4813-","l":"ONE","j":"11","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word right where an error correction or substitution is stated, the \"should\", \"read\", \"instead\", \"of\", \"for\", \"to\", \"typo\" or \"misprint\" in phrases like \"X should read Y\", \"I think you meant X instead of Y\", \"is a typo\", \"a scribal error for\", \"should be corrected to\", \"many authorities read\" (errata notes, textual criticism, code-review corrections), plus a weaker side hit in C# Advent-of-Code-style instruction swapping code (nop/jmp/acc).","s":[0.76252,0.654503,0.943639,0.51952,0.594836,0.00627,0.429787,0.332369,0.037959,0.144814,0.019964,0.015803,0.019157,0.007051,0.017517]},{"p":"L12N4985+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the connective words of a two-sided \"neither extreme\" construction (\"neither too hot nor too cold\", \"not too X, not too Y\", \"not X but not Y either\", \"either extreme\", \"both isolationism and exceptionalism\"), peaking on the second-half link words \"nor\", \"too\", \"not\", \"or\" and on a trailing \"either\" or \"extreme\" once the first opposite has been stated.","s":[0.725007,0.598806,1.09764,0.775058,0.950166,0.273316,0.104898,0.012671,0.007964,0.019989,0.015228,0.012032,0.013195,0.011343,0.014972]},{"p":"L12N4985-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the connective or token right after the first member of a binary pair of opposite options, the \"and\", \"or\" or \"versus\" in phrases like \"with and without\", \"high or low\", \"public versus private\", \"present versus absent\", \"peace or conflict\", and also on words naming a two-way split (\"which side\", \"the two groups\", \"between conditions\"); the output promotes \"both\" and \"neither\".","s":[0.708235,0.225457,0.364865,0.438451,0.517249,0.016011,0.034417,0.044127,0.113751,0.03584,0.006935,0.016557,0.038282,0.022766,0.182152]},{"p":"L12N642+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the first word of a rigid, formulaic phrase whose next word is all but certain, most strongly the word \"given\" in math or physics prose such as \"The velocity is given by\" or \"the probability density function is given by\", and \"FOREIGN\" in SQL table definitions such as \"CONSTRAINT fk_x FOREIGN KEY (...) REFERENCES\"; it also fires on boilerplate keys in code (\"formatter = logging.Formatter\", \"LOG_FORMAT =\", a repeated \"length:\" field) and fixed phrases like \"as many as\" or \"reduces to\".","s":[0.918299,0.522922,0.584557,0.362869,0.921762,0.141338,0.326926,0.111898,0.059216,0.162717,0.034564,0.045601,0.056925,0.048861,0.036843]},{"p":"L12N642-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"In program source code, fires on the first parameter name inside a function or method definition's parentheses, most strongly the \"(self\" token right after a Python method name in \"def name(self, ...)\" (also \"self\" after \"__call__(\", \"x\" after \"lambda\", or the first parameter name in a C# method signature), especially in repetitive class code where many such methods have already appeared.","s":[0.560285,0.544683,0.247701,0.235401,0.190943,0.026539,0.0221,0.04727,0.009695,0.094833,0.041718,0.064394,0.054041,0.032886,0.026211]},{"p":"L12N7367+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires inside technical engineering, physics or pharmacology prose on the word that completes a named physical or dosage quantity, i.e. the head noun or its next word in phrases like \"bending moment\", \"shear stress\", \"electric field\", \"drug concentration\", \"fluence\", \"dosimetry\" or \"Cmin\", especially when the sentence locates where that quantity is maximum or highest (\"where the stresses are the highest\", \"the maximum bending moment\").","s":[0.673615,0.139153,0.749735,0.234053,0.384298,0.443919,0.011272,0.107382,0.015712,0.060548,0.012673,0.030264,0.019239,0.02025,0.024287]},{"p":"L12N7367-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word naming a flattening or unfolding of a 3D or stacked thing into a flat 2D layout, such as UV unwrapping or UV maps of a 3D model, nets of solids, an unrolled section, flattening a tensor into a single row or column, cutting or opening a box or album sleeve out flat, or a sheet or paper pattern used to cover a shape; it fires most on the key word (UV, unwrap, nets, unrolled, row, open, out).","s":[0.557915,1.388764,0.464529,0.578297,0.987187,0.020348,0.233487,0.020294,0.036654,0.012983,0.035354,0.063438,0.046837,0.016214,0.017562]},{"p":"L12N8184+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"In science-textbook or exam prose (especially acid–base and solubility chemistry), fires on the word or word piece that begins or completes a fixed technical term, most strongly \"weak\" in \"weak acid\" or \"weak base\" (also \"acid\" or \"base\" right after it), and similarly \"relative\" before \"humidity\" and \"-agnet\" in \"electromagnet\".","s":[1.159851,1.287776,0.908497,0.320015,0.691922,0.848551,0.459109,1.0474,0.313988,0.516212,0.019169,0.034402,0.007961,0.042565,0.027191]},{"p":"L12N8184-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the adverb or modifier of head-on directness right after a verb of looking, hitting or answering, most strongly \"directly\" in \"look directly at / stare directly into / look someone directly in the eye\", and also \"squarely\", \"straight\", \"head-on\", \"direct hit\", \"off-center hit\" and \"answer the question directly\", plus avoided-gaze phrasing (\"avoiding her gaze\", \"avoid eye contact\").","s":[0.455807,0.520428,0.855817,0.697881,0.83601,0.023174,0.009725,0.017572,0.080479,0.100969,0.022032,0.012853,0.032154,0.037789,0.01477]},{"p":"L12N8738+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on words for folding, creasing or shaping a flat surface, chiefly \"fold\"/\"folding\" and \"crease\"/\"creased\" (strongest on the \"-ase\"/\"-ased\" piece after \"cre\") in paper-craft instructions, card-finishing and book-condition descriptions, and more weakly on relief/emboss/frieze and disc-shape surface-form words.","s":[1.018317,0.850002,1.179586,0.712121,0.468426,0.228699,0.195765,0.352092,0.038688,0.140628,0.015471,0.053746,0.022483,0.02569,0.04398]},{"p":"L12N8738-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on words that describe something as lacking recognizable order, meaning or representation: chiefly \"abstract\" in art (abstract paintings or shapes), \"gibberish\", \"jumble\", \"atonal\", \"garbage\" data, and \"random\" characters or numbers, peaking on the descriptor token itself (or its second piece, e.g. the \"ber\" of gibberish or the \"onal\" of atonal).","s":[0.768239,1.102691,0.779018,0.694645,1.176051,0.027411,0.009589,0.015103,0.033928,0.006883,0.024238,0.013031,0.015461,0.017803,0.019037]},{"p":"L12N9034+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"In product spec sheets, reviews and FAQs for devices (cameras, printers, batteries, motor oil, projectors), fires on the word that names a timed performance measure or that comes right before its time or rate value, such as \"take\" in \"how long does it take to\", \"speed\" in \"print speed\", \"time\" in \"charging time\", \"lag\" in \"shutter lag\", \"delay\", \"second\" in \"frames per second\", or \"flow\" in \"how easily the oil flows\".","s":[0.357891,0.49386,0.749531,0.479524,0.823327,0.117112,0.491786,0.071604,0.2532,0.203013,0.031566,0.035217,0.022851,0.04984,0.035056]},{"p":"L12N9034-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word at which a sentence turns to how long something persists or is kept in effect, just before a span of time is given or implied: \"lasts\"/\"lasting\"/\"last\", \"duration\", \"prolong\", \"retain(ed)\", \"for\"/\"until\"/\"through\" introducing a term (\"serve for a one-year term\", \"remain in the atmosphere for centuries\", \"contract extension until 2021\"), \"long-lasting\", and the \"Keep me logged in\"/\"Remember me\" login box.","s":[0.798365,0.817974,0.471179,0.554174,0.859849,0.038074,0.017973,0.117151,0.036319,0.070815,0.015624,0.026966,0.018028,0.053624,0.023257]},{"p":"L13N1220+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires inside text about exams, tests and interview or practice questions (exam papers, test-prep pages, exam-pattern descriptions, coding-interview problem write-ups, IELTS speaking question pages), on ordinary words right where the text is describing the exam or its questions, such as 'asked', 'questions', 'the', 'of', 'pattern', 'paper'.","s":[0.549402,0.652077,0.80048,0.422308,0.455503,0.133557,0.108859,0.085831,0.082342,0.1855,0.085659,0.238327,0.140583,0.107402,0.131532]},{"p":"L13N1220-","l":"UNREL","j":"00","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires inside a citation that tags an exam question with the past paper it came from, on the exam name, 'Paper', the month or the year digits (e.g. '(SSC-CGL – 2019)', '(HKIAAT Paper 8 Auditing December 2000)', 'JEE Main 2022 (Online)', 'This question was previously asked in'); separately, in code loops, on the value assigned to a flag (= false / true / 0) or on the 'break' right after it.","s":[0.850341,1.166988,0.505128,0.891703,0.899303,0.032172,0.02347,0.046734,0.018413,0.013849,0.016601,0.01233,0.007588,0.013087,0.013775],"u":{"0":[0.839158,1.027857,0.674435,0.859698,0.557703],"1":[0.519922,0.342147,0.361614,0.548874,0.372179]}},{"p":"L13N2038+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on words that invoke legal, contractual or official-administrative obligation — agreeing to the terms of service or a license agreement, law, legality, fines, penalties, bills and charges to pay, policies, official bodies, applying or registering for something — most strongly when such a word pops up inside technical how-to or software setup text.","s":[0.563458,0.304432,0.392943,0.17129,0.126471,0.042119,0.086154,0.050198,0.035275,0.062379,0.030513,0.04378,0.045346,0.023197,0.048433]},{"p":"L13N2038-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the predictable second half of a fixed technical or commercial compound term or brand name, i.e. the word that completes it after its first part, such as \"Build Status\" in a README badge line, \"Copy Protection\" or \"Product Key\" on a software listing, \"normal production\" in DevOps prose, \"Ad|Sense\", \"On|Star\" or \"box office\", and on \"Cell\" in a contact-info line like \"Cell: 555-...\" in flyers or listings.","s":[0.511437,0.609268,0.694626,0.515911,0.434655,0.05493,0.315346,0.297372,0.086213,0.269391,0.058006,0.085335,0.059264,0.0268,0.058006]},{"p":"L13N2126+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word or word-piece that completes a stock multiword phrase whose ending is nearly forced by the preceding word(s), such as 'Editor-in-Chief', 'deals combat damage', 'guilt or innocence', 'agreement or disagreement', 'trust and confidence', 'odds ratio', 'ceremonial first pitch', 'drag and drop', 'lack thereof' or 'written reply'; the trigger is the predictable final word, not the phrase's first word.","s":[0.319329,0.109396,0.571433,0.339712,0.570561,0.060631,0.027925,0.062244,0.046406,0.071266,0.107801,0.228247,0.165892,0.253031,0.087523]},{"p":"L13N2126-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the coordinating \"and\" (or a comma/dash doing the same job) placed right after a head noun or preposition whose complement has not yet arrived and will be shared by both conjuncts, as in \"the formation and evolution of galaxies\", \"the health and well-being of patients\", \"Mr. and Mrs. Smith\", or \"used in, or evidence of, an offence\".","s":[0.255864,0.490231,0.369251,0.670444,0.348709,0.125314,0.058159,0.066316,0.069095,0.13426,0.003932,0.066021,0.080748,0.01483,0.094712]},{"p":"L13N2197+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a single digit (or letter) inside a long run of made-up, arbitrary characters where the next symbol is unpredictable: a digit in the middle of a fake phone number or ID typed by mashing number keys (like 7896542 or 33789987), a cell of a sudoku grid or number matrix, random letter strings or keyboard-mash, or a repeated locant number in a chemical name, especially in SQL INSERT test data or code arrays.","s":[0.30699,0.081936,0.325097,0.420725,0.367849,0.006544,0.024518,0.01387,0.007016,0.007896,0.01611,0.007443,0.005166,0.003748,0.006477]},{"p":"L13N2197-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on single digits late in a row of a Sudoku-style grid (rows of 1–9 without repeats, written as space-separated lines or code arrays), and on the last letters of a permutation alphabet such as an Enigma rotor wiring string, where the remaining symbol is forced because each appears exactly once.","s":[0.392885,0.876263,0.368551,1.195599,0.361174,0.054903,0.029184,0.022691,0.039588,0.079269,0.031359,0.05083,0.026984,0.009106,0.029047]},{"p":"L13N2349+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the first token of a new cell or segment in flattened table, list or directory text, where a label, name or heading (e.g. 'The Bad', 'Maple neck', 'Address', a business name, 'SEE ALSO', a person's name) runs straight into its value or into the next entry with no colon, line break or punctuation between them, as in 'The Bad Very few manual options' or 'Roller Printing 1233 Siler Road'.","s":[0.092565,0.208038,0.347244,0.298184,0.237004,0.085708,0.079941,0.346793,0.033434,0.02498,0.042563,0.071084,0.071105,0.023429,0.010935]},{"p":"L13N2349-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a word that abruptly breaks the flow of the text: the first token of a spliced-in fragment where a sentence is cut off and an unrelated sentence, heading or capitalized word starts mid-line, as in scraped, OCR or column-jumbled web text (e.g. \"This Different approaches to solving...\", \"The even if an alert...\", \"I'm still not Sure what's going on\"); it also fires on boilerplate like \"You are here: Home\" breadcrumbs and repeated list lines such as \"1 Kings 9:14 NLT\".","s":[0.310155,0.127158,0.655147,0.158027,0.733927,0.097884,0.131096,0.062832,0.048875,0.323212,0.135985,0.116174,0.186591,0.159562,0.103339]},{"p":"L13N5464+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires inside technical engineering/physics prose (acoustics, loudspeakers, RF antennas, circuits, vibration, dynamic stability, mechanical design) on the technical term itself, strongest on 'linear'/'nonlinear'/'non-linear'/'linearity' and similar signal/vibration words (lobes, shaking, shuddering, oscilloscope, voltage, equilibrium, harmonic), e.g. 'distortion caused by the nonlinearity of the speaker cone'.","s":[0.938708,0.458909,0.438923,0.332623,0.303863,0.063216,0.018033,0.042971,0.040214,0.049212,0.055924,0.043876,0.060146,0.018875,0.060494]},{"p":"L13N5464-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires inside AC circuit theory text (electrical-engineering quiz/textbook prose about reactance, impedance, inductive/capacitive circuits, resonance, power factor, phasors like j16 or XL, XC), peaking on the tokens that name or symbolize these quantities, such as \"react\" in reactance, \"ed\" in impedance, XL/XC, \"AC\", \"frequency\" and \"resonance\".","s":[1.109756,0.801032,1.319328,1.014769,0.521108,0.044999,0.013122,0.021409,0.007733,0.015571,0.007609,0.012626,0.008724,0.014636,0.007429]},{"p":"L13N7401+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the confessional word in a first-person candor or concession formula — 'honest' in \"I'll be honest\" / \"if I'm being honest\", 'admit' in \"I admit\" / \"I'll freely admit\", and concessive 'Granted' or 'Admittedly' — as a writer introduces a frank or grudging aside, typically in reviews, blogs and forum posts.","s":[0.548628,0.731898,0.722454,0.737164,0.794301,0.011727,0.08301,0.016017,0.032342,0.049317,0.037865,0.039711,0.048106,0.037211,0.049069]},{"p":"L13N7401-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the words of an emphatic affirmation that contradicts a doubt or denial just voiced, especially the auxiliary or verb right after \"Yes,\" or \"Of course\" (\"Yes, you can\", \"Of course you have\", \"Yes it does\", \"Oh yes I did\", \"there is such a thing as\", \"there is something we can do\", \"it is not only possible\").","s":[0.275604,0.311097,0.516974,0.53539,0.449908,0.08283,0.021021,0.026777,0.011761,0.024082,0.013571,0.023724,0.014479,0.012066,0.015947]},{"p":"L13N8636+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the head noun that completes the object of an increase/improve/reduce-type verb, i.e. the measurable quantity or thing being affected, in expository science, health or how-to prose, as in \"achieve the highest net energy intake\", \"advance our understanding\", \"increases good cholesterol\", \"lowers cholesterol\", \"greater spin rates\" or \"preventing calcium loss\".","s":[0.384687,0.731825,0.338483,0.380195,0.515549,0.084858,0.038502,0.11421,0.032304,0.01314,0.01383,0.031816,0.046594,0.056007,0.030246]},{"p":"L13N8636-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word naming the goal, prize or measured outcome an effort is aimed at, often a modifier just before a metric word: crop outcomes such as marketable tubers, bushel yield, grain yield, wheat price and harvest; model scores such as top-1 accuracy and mean-squared error; and competition prizes such as the Lombardi Trophy, a championship ring, capture-the-flag flags or a bodybuilding contest; it is strongest in agronomy and product-performance prose.","s":[0.645692,0.491509,0.67825,0.317505,0.207549,0.036998,0.023547,0.033099,0.046462,0.015311,0.023177,0.016053,0.020834,0.015712,0.024597]},{"p":"L14N2804+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the comma or 'and' (or the second word of a compound noun) partway through a list of abstract measurable attributes or outcomes that some cause is said to increase, reduce, impair or affect, e.g. 'reduces fuel consumption, emissions' or 'impaired memory, motor speed, and precision', mostly in health, science or business prose.","s":[0.075777,0.027244,0.095643,0.195018,0.421554,0.037232,0.054544,0.076583,0.053522,0.05698,0.063086,0.043317,0.051232,0.081534,0.055521]},{"p":"L14N2804-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the noun or word-stem that completes a phrase opened by a modifier meaning lack, reduction, negation or deviation (low, poor, slower, zero, without, no, out of, off, under-, hypo-, un-, mal-, pre-, extra-), e.g. the 'pressure' in 'low inflation pressure', 'rhythm' in 'out of rhythm', 'nose' in 'a face without a nose', 'flex' in 'hyporeflexia'.","s":[0.183122,0.820104,0.360897,0.727017,0.295755,0.06638,0.09611,0.052239,0.138621,0.023774,0.029858,0.007361,0.00125,0.03718,0.017909]},{"p":"L14N4015+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"In code, SQL dumps and markdown, fires on a generic placeholder-style word at the start of a user-chosen name or string literal: the 'db' prefix of a database name (Database: `db_shop`, CREATE DATABASE db_blog), a literal key like localStorage.setItem('key', ...), 'alt text' in markdown image syntax ![alt text][1], or a header like '| Column 1 |'.","s":[0.741084,0.404853,0.695496,1.012802,0.291302,0.013777,0.022243,0.013377,0.051244,0.008291,0.009122,0.007653,0.025221,0.024462,0.016723]},{"p":"L14N4015-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"In source code, config files or command lines, fires on the name of a keyword argument, object-literal key, option flag or property being set — the identifier, or its final word piece, that sits right before \"=\", \":\" or \"=>\" inside a function call, constructor, dict or config block (e.g. Font(name=...), {model: x}, fetch(url, {body: ...}), default_extension=, obj.Geometry = g), not on the value after it.","s":[0.288776,0.509858,0.188334,0.112774,0.040668,0.019804,0.08114,0.02676,0.030162,0.058581,0.016538,0.025971,0.035298,0.026506,0.026442]},{"p":"L14N4399+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires at structural boundaries in long, deeply nested structured text: mostly on the indentation whitespace just before a closing brace that ends a long code block (C#, JavaScript, Python method ends), and also on the line break after a long index entry or after a long run of SQL column definitions, where one long nested unit is finishing and the next sibling is about to start; it needs a long preceding block and fires weakly in short snippets.","s":[0.114079,0.089312,0.252739,0.274643,0.749692,0.013482,0.00883,0.017827,0.044598,0.031953,0.025058,0.059161,0.050955,0.042792,0.05205]},{"p":"L14N4399-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"In source code, fires on the indentation whitespace (or closing brace) that ends the first short method of a class, typically a constructor, __init__ or Start() body, right after its last assignment line, as the code returns to class level, e.g. the spaces before the closing } after `_context = context;` or before the next `def` after `self.x = x`.","s":[0.199004,0.41552,0.716438,0.22331,0.458051,0.038829,0.028537,0.059677,0.2008,0.028683,0.046886,0.028912,0.018543,0.043518,0.037253]},{"p":"L14N4419+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a colon (often merged with following line breaks, \":\\n\\n\") that ends a lead-in phrase announcing content that is missing or follows as a separate unrelated block, especially web boilerplate labels like \"Follow us:\", \"Find us on:\", \"Market voices on:\", \"Payment Methods:\", \"Subscribe to X:\", or \"follow these steps:\" where the promised list/link/quote is absent and the text jumps to something else.","s":[0.303509,0.701073,0.083573,0.271054,0.050401,0.020029,0.132413,0.007725,0.002754,0.050401,0.026933,0.021457,0.022568,0.016157,0.032657]},{"p":"L14N4419-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the colon, dash, period or line break that ends a heading or lead-in phrase announcing that a list, example, output block or code follows (e.g. \"Nice to have:\", \"What we have tried:\", \"{{out}}\", \"the following would be acceptable:\", \"answer:\"), i.e. the punctuation right before an enumerated or displayed item begins.","s":[0.013186,0.027365,0.034757,0.070399,0.063972,0.034007,0.028735,0.018986,0.023989,0.035986,0.017106,0.045183,0.013119,0.028658,0.014248]},{"p":"L14N4783+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"In code or technical config text, fires on the parameter name or the '=' / ':' separator of a key=value setting written inline as a string, such as a query or connection string, an environment line, or a header (e.g. \"dbname=test user=x\", \"key=YOURKEY\", \"format=atom\", \"PUID=1000\", \"Port: 143\"), right before the value.","s":[0.552829,0.152963,0.185173,0.046988,0.177464,0.080674,0.112373,0.069196,0.042553,0.008816,0.03341,0.019787,0.036423,0.026663,0.043381]},{"p":"L14N4783-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"In code and SQL/data dumps, fires on tokens that open or sit inside a machine-formatted literal value, chiefly the hyphen right after the year in a quoted date (e.g. '2019-12-10' in INSERT ... VALUES rows), the _DATE of Oracle TO_DATE(, and the scheme prefix of colon-delimited identifiers/URIs such as arn:aws:iam, cpe:/o:, mongodb://, urn:lsid:.","s":[0.588,0.418792,0.906404,0.298204,1.065823,0.028479,0.114554,0.144368,0.02929,0.196301,0.01465,0.043477,0.014188,0.025543,0.013686]},{"p":"L14N5447+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on words in a passage about splitting something into pieces or groups, such as chunking or splitting an array by a size parameter, quicksort's divide-and-conquer partitioning around a pivot, or dividing and propagating plants by breaking apart rhizomes or cutting off shoots, especially on the noun or argument naming what is split or how.","s":[0.740214,0.687985,0.748427,0.298964,0.938693,0.057975,0.1353,0.328055,0.065543,0.103419,0.02124,0.051157,0.09079,0.109252,0.026499]},{"p":"L14N5447-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a word or word piece that completes a stock, near-memorized formula in boilerplate text, where the continuation is almost certain: the fan in recipe oven settings like \"180°C/fan 160°C\" or \"200°C (180°C Fan, Gas Mark 6)\", \"of\" in \"each block contains a cryptographic hash of the previous block\", \"Independence\" in \"Declaration of Independence\", \"impairment\" in \"physical or mental impairment\", \"website\" or \"furnished\" in SEC press-release legalese, and \"writable\" in JavaScript defineProperty descriptors.","s":[0.51274,0.488651,0.348391,0.554707,0.144328,0.112331,0.097859,0.089073,0.0164,0.060695,0.040484,0.033066,0.026705,0.033708,0.015613]},{"p":"L14N593+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires at the seam where an article's running prose ends and a structured back-matter block begins, most strongly on the first numbered reference marker (the '1' or the '.' in '1.') opening a citation list right after acknowledgements or conflict-of-interest lines, and also on boilerplate-to-content or metadata seams such as the period ending a contributor disclaimer, or a file-size bracket in a download listing.","s":[0.412773,0.279439,0.119527,0.262006,0.56804,0.173357,0.181462,0.143566,0.149747,0.092297,0.11657,0.15003,0.066713,0.180543,0.11764]},{"p":"L14N593-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the first item marker of a second list that directly follows an earlier list in the same document: the list numbering restarting at \"1.\" (or \"1)\"), a bullet or dash opening a new list after a previous list ended, or the start of a repeated table header or spec block, as in a second numbered list, a 'Minimum / Recommended' system-requirements pair or a second table.","s":[0.033999,0.043766,0.01924,0.013652,0.088235,0.034778,0.025801,0.016707,0.05823,0.001816,0.015732,0.034658,0.016951,0.019801,0.018259]},{"p":"L14N7957+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires inside an equivalence or analogy statement, on the word or punctuation right after a cue like \"the equivalent of\", \"equivalent to\", \"analogous to\", \"comparable to that of\", \"known as\", \"aliased as\", \"thought of as\", \"read X for\", \"is a copy of\", or \"X = Y =\", i.e. at the point where the second term (the thing the first is being equated with) is about to be named.","s":[0.730969,0.558816,0.418322,0.17273,0.354203,0.042224,0.016072,0.031102,0.022445,0.015598,0.052347,0.031488,0.074112,0.057994,0.04226]},{"p":"L14N7957-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires inside an imaginative simile or comparison, on the words right after \"like\", \"as if\" or \"looks like\" that build a vivid, often humorous mash-up image (e.g. \"tastes like bubble gum steeped in cream soda\", \"pours like snot mixed with honey\", \"looks like two buildings glued together\", \"as if Leopold and Abbey had collaborated\"), especially on the verb or connector that combines the parts of the image.","s":[0.625537,0.791698,0.867792,0.687016,0.756617,0.014677,0.025415,0.02087,0.013649,0.007467,0.012709,0.019012,0.014765,0.008052,0.005143]},{"p":"L14N8029+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on words expressing slowness or unhurried pace — 'slow', 'slowly', 'gradually', 'steady' in 'slow and steady', 'measured', 'deliberate', and the last piece of 'snail's pace' or 'tortoise' — i.e., the token that completes a description of something happening at a slow, gradual rate.","s":[0.231668,0.668503,0.350843,0.603483,0.069966,0.0163,0.042064,0.017664,0.010187,0.02133,0.01242,0.034042,0.010784,0.005993,0.013896]},{"p":"L14N8029-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a word naming a high speed, intensity or rate, mainly \"high\" in \"over high heat\" in cooking instructions, plus \"too high/too big/too large\" for a learning rate, \"growth is fast\", and \"gallop\" when a horse is spurred to speed, with output that pushes toward \"slow\" as the contrasting idea.","s":[0.594707,0.839472,0.201207,0.453897,0.711947,0.139578,0.116549,0.026877,0.009,0.095211,0.007403,0.041233,0.018456,0.047882,0.014949]},{"p":"L15N1414+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a token that is almost fully dictated by a memorized boilerplate or stock phrase, strongest on the \"message\" key inside Express/Node error responses like res.status(500).send({ message: err.message || \"Some error occurred...\" }), and also on fixed completions such as \"statistically significant\" after p<0.05, Tkinter pack(fill=BOTH), \"from mysql.connector import Error\", socket.gethostname, images.ctfassets.net, \"warmly welcome new and old customers\".","s":[0.994232,0.107029,0.05639,0.204788,0.5161,0.062803,0.092082,0.131893,0.212009,0.044164,0.096007,0.119335,0.095545,0.116987,0.153082]},{"p":"L15N1414-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Commas and 'and' (or the Chinese enumeration comma 、) that separate items in an enumerated list of industries, regions, materials or products, especially in Chinese-company English marketing prose of the form 'widely used in chemical, petroleum, metallurgy and other industries' or 'exported to Europe, Asia, Africa and other countries', with the list heading toward a closing 'and other …' (等).","s":[0.559732,0.459878,0.030288,0.39898,0.462015,0.109402,0.063186,0.045141,0.045004,0.065724,0.064325,0.030431,0.014324,0.041524,0.041785]},{"p":"L15N3952+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"In code and API documentation, this pole fires where a parameter is being marked as optional or given a fallback default value, for example on the word \"optional\" in a docstring like \"(str, optional): ... Defaults to\", on \"optional - default 1\", on default=30 in argparse, or on the punctuation right after a default assignment such as \"amount = 1;\" or the closing brace of an options object with default settings; its output promotes \"default\" and \"(optional\".","s":[0.57174,0.255971,0.202162,0.288097,0.370329,0.089652,0.097178,0.0627,0.081287,0.070184,0.006161,0.016867,0.008633,0.014707,0.011218]},{"p":"L15N3952-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"In Python-style function or method signatures and API docs, fires on the token that supplies an optional or default value for a parameter: \"=None\" or \" None\" after \"=\", the \" or\" in \"x = arg or default\", the \"*\"/\"**kwargs\"/\"...args\" catch-all, and the \"to\"/\"default\" in \"Defaults to None\" or \"the default is\".","s":[0.360031,0.43873,0.704344,0.492993,0.286746,0.020846,0.012638,0.218546,0.128113,0.030008,0.022788,0.030833,0.025852,0.01165,0.014524]},{"p":"L15N405+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on tokens in the middle of meaningless high-entropy byte strings, chiefly long Base64 blobs (data URIs, JWT tokens, encoded IDs in URLs) and mojibake replacement-character runs from mis-decoded Korean/Chinese text, where the next token is unpredictable noise.","s":[0.158044,0.734852,0.664844,0.156725,0.540839,0.036904,0.056515,0.049232,0.029918,0.049618,0.070589,0.029429,0.052943,0.057578,0.048164]},{"p":"L15N405-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on garbled, mis-encoded text (mojibake): runs of single-byte junk characters like \"ą▒ą░ą║\" produced when Cyrillic, Korean or other non-Latin UTF-8 text is decoded with the wrong codepage, plus similar OCR-scrambled gibberish such as \"oiN:i nr i: socir1r\", firing on the individual junk characters rather than on readable words.","s":[0.564182,0.484938,0.381214,0.216802,0.380011,0.117116,0.024847,0.038849,0.0763,0.086577,0.071745,0.028528,0.131346,0.082009,0.036028]},{"p":"L15N418+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a single digit (or digit piece) that is an entry inside a written-out matrix, vector or system of linear equations, i.e. a number in a later row or position of a bracketed array like [[1,2],[3,4]] or {{7,8,9},{6,1,2}}, or a coefficient in the second or third equation of a linear system, in code or math text.","s":[0.277715,0.428218,0.477372,0.461258,0.867698,0.007772,0.121074,0.301536,0.063505,0.005582,0.011335,0.011263,0.020472,0.019426,0.011205]},{"p":"L15N418-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"In a tech-support forum or Q&A thread, fires on the words and punctuation of the original asker's follow-up announcing they solved their own problem (\"I found the solution.\", \"The mistake causing the problem was…\", \"Never mind, I figured it out\", \"I answered my own question\"), peaking on the tokens right before the fix is explained.","s":[0.493284,0.47139,0.603053,0.239796,0.597647,0.038157,0.066702,0.040195,0.047647,0.022949,0.016758,0.019854,0.016495,0.028624,0.023652]},{"p":"L15N5772+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a plural head noun (often its plural suffix, e.g. -es, -s, -ias) that is shared by two or more coordinated modifiers, as in \"almond and vanilla extracts\", \"the Energy and Commerce Committees\", \"Monterey Jack cheeses\" after a list, or on the \"of\"/\"and\" in \"the Departments of State and Commerce\" / \"schools of Pharmacy and Medicine\", where one plural noun covers several named items.","s":[0.556543,0.601218,0.220864,0.76131,0.347369,0.031407,0.013998,0.041365,0.009236,0.011632,0.020063,0.013294,0.017184,0.014607,0.008016]},{"p":"L15N5772-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a head noun that is shared by two or more coordinated modifiers or owners, as in \"the treatment and control group\", \"social and environmental aspect\", \"Stanley and Ray's face\", \"the A and B button\", or after \"twin/dual/both\", where the noun covers several referents (often written singular where a plural is expected).","s":[0.117002,0.395272,0.218881,0.754623,0.224486,0.038078,0.038458,0.081782,0.110911,0.146123,0.05884,0.062992,0.030733,0.015164,0.068945]},{"p":"L15N8849+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires inside sarcastic or hyperbolic negated 'impossible' scenarios used to make a commonsense point (\"it won't magically appear\", \"they aren't psychic\", \"computers won't self-destruct\", \"dishes don't wash themselves\", \"did it think I teleported\", \"hiding under a rock\"), peaking on the absurd word or the word right after \"just/magically\" that names the fantastical action.","s":[1.234902,0.492457,0.404976,0.578407,0.544428,0.035918,0.015962,0.020992,0.013015,0.035718,0.037673,0.018357,0.044497,0.052317,0.028524]},{"p":"L15N8849-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word just after a denial or a scornful rhetorical question begins, inside the scope of an implied 'no'. Typical cases are the verb or object right after 'who said', 'what could be more', 'would I ever', 'who'd', 'how does this', 'like I', 'cannot', 'couldn't' or 'non-' (e.g. 'who said anything about him', 'Would I ever hurt you?', 'responsibility cannot be delegated', 'non-preemptive').","s":[0.404429,0.131603,0.46233,0.55588,0.32971,0.04132,0.015839,0.060352,0.187004,0.033027,0.017218,0.028624,0.043545,0.030616,0.026011]},{"p":"L15N9151+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word that frames a problem, complication or its remedy in prose, especially the aggravation phrase \"making matters worse / what's even worse / worse,\" and problem–solution words like \"problem\", \"concern\", \"solution\", \"fix\", \"resolve\", \"mitigate\", and \"help\" in pleas such as \"can someone please help\".","s":[0.034633,0.107641,0.115761,0.521745,0.173288,0.061638,0.115011,0.040534,0.130721,0.012344,0.03244,0.012051,0.013255,0.090544,0.027751]},{"p":"L15N9151-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires at the pivot point of a contrast, concession or trade-off, on the token where the text turns to the opposing side or the alternative outcome: words like \"instead\", \"at least\", \"better (off)\", \"while\", \"all over again\", or the comma or clause start that sets up \"but\" or \"however\" (e.g. \"X is good for A, but bad for B\"; \"the port closed, so ships docked elsewhere instead\"; a tennis score coming back \"3-6, 7-5\").","s":[0.021011,0.260015,0.407615,0.407411,0.08922,0.050081,0.110745,0.040512,0.134784,0.010475,0.029928,0.017691,0.029669,0.012921,0.035783]},{"p":"L15N9181+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on an opening parenthesis (or other opening bracket/quote) that starts a nested group while an earlier parenthesis is still unclosed, e.g. \"(such as vacuum tankers (VT))\" or \"(where K = Boltzmann's constant (an energy measure...))\"; an opening bracket at top level, outside any open parenthesis, does not fire.","s":[0.937095,0.660812,0.732957,0.183013,0.620651,0.018149,0.041339,0.08565,0.017391,0.040865,0.03002,0.048388,0.02774,0.023012,0.017213]},{"p":"L15N9181-","l":"TWO-REL","j":"11","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the token right after an opening bracket that starts an enumeration label or markup tag, most strongly on the 'i', '1', 'a' or 'x' just after '(' in mid-sentence list markers like '(i) investment advice', '(1) what we want', '(a) transmissions' and coordinate pairs '(x1,y1)', and also on '<' or the tag letter of inline HTML tags such as '<a href', '<i>', '<em>', '<u>'.","s":[0.229283,0.205725,0.410195,0.900296,0.326192,0.034741,0.047844,0.039711,0.033114,0.042667,0.11349,0.060224,-0.002892,0.061276,0.018054]},{"p":"L16N1066+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the opening fragment of a familiar word or fixed name or phrase that the tokenizer splits into pieces, at the point where the remainder is all but certain: the \".\" of \".NET\", \"Pil\" of Pilates, \"Hew\" of Hewlett-Packard, \"uns\" of unsupervised, \"ultr\" of ultraviolet, \"real\" before -time or estate, \"Internal\" before Revenue Service, \"of\" in \"state of the art\", \"Rout\" of Routledge, usually inside a list or technical description.","s":[0.674653,0.613865,0.234604,0.56485,0.372165,0.188147,0.015248,0.024571,0.243861,0.314598,0.012246,0.117185,0.176804,0.018042,0.024008]},{"p":"L16N1066-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the first word-piece of a misspelled or garbled rendering of a name or word that was already spelled correctly earlier in the same text (e.g. \"Criag Venter\" after \"Craig Venter\", \"Esptein\" after \"Epstein\", \"Hirshsprung\" after \"Hirschsprung\", \"infleuncing\", \"Issac\"), i.e. a typo or transposition the model notices against an earlier correct copy.","s":[0.607123,0.63789,1.166023,0.125086,0.526976,0.069203,0.159106,0.071613,0.013971,0.023471,0.028593,0.017007,0.019903,0.021325,0.015439]},{"p":"L16N2515+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the plural/category noun right after a distributive or selecting quantifier, such as \"each side\", \"both systems\", \"which one\", \"the two salts\", \"three levels\", \"different steps\", \"every season\" or \"both sides\", where the noun names one of several contrasted alternatives, kinds or parts that the text has just laid out.","s":[0.341735,0.3446,0.198403,0.147144,0.38437,0.017461,0.071248,0.198403,0.066561,0.017813,0.009687,0.010551,0.017847,0.011474,0.01906]},{"p":"L16N2515-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word right after a plural or coordinated subject that refers back to two or more previously described alternatives being compared (\"X and Y are…\", \"both the Sharpe and Sortino ratios…\", \"neither of those are…\", \"the latter two…\"), typically the verb or quantifier that begins the predicate about that group.","s":[0.56226,0.352691,0.06922,0.135182,0.207421,0.039974,0.023978,0.070075,0.02783,0.021215,0.047408,0.054416,0.04221,0.037449,0.048072]},{"p":"L16N2662+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a word in the middle of a multi-word key phrase that is being repeated from earlier in the same text, at the point where the model can copy the continuation (e.g. the second time 'best law schools', 'tummy tuck scarring', 'comfort zone' or 'blockchain security audits importance' appears), typical of keyword-stuffed SEO or how-to prose; first mentions of the phrase do not fire.","s":[0.202097,0.211714,0.242037,0.186262,0.148913,0.10592,0.138809,0.044788,0.034039,0.043542,0.023999,0.034093,0.056649,0.085441,0.070866]},{"p":"L16N2662-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a token inside a word, name or phrase that is a repeat of something that already appeared earlier in the same text, especially the first or middle pieces of a rare multi-token name or term (for example the second 'Novomoskovsk', 'RPL22', 'Upadhyay', 'Klondike bar', 'Angry Birds'), where the model can copy the continuation from the earlier occurrence.","s":[0.015402,0.076424,0.033252,0.02322,0.012768,0.019113,0.023405,0.033252,0.02322,0.016135,0.034778,0.037392,0.016958,0.027158,0.027394]},{"p":"L16N5096+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires inside nostalgic, grieving remembrance of a specific lost or absent loved one, on the words that recall that person's endearing everyday habits and personality (e.g. \"I miss her calling my name, her favorite things, the funny guy who\", \"I won't feel him hug me or say\", \"just like old times\"), mainly in first-person grief posts and memoirs about someone who died.","s":[0.355653,0.997869,0.649289,0.405593,0.565286,0.06374,0.209567,0.012219,0.017463,0.032002,0.018309,0.02542,0.027727,0.030119,0.048732]},{"p":"L16N5096-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires inside death and bereavement writing, most strongly in obituaries on the function word that follows the death statement (the ' at' or ' in' right after 'died/passed away on [date],' before the hospital or home) and on connectors in survivor lists ('survived by his daughter-in-law, ...', 'Mrs.', 'wife of', the '(' before a spouse's name). It fires more weakly in stories about grief, a lost parent or someone's death.","s":[0.906781,0.555488,0.551893,0.402913,0.465126,0.041862,0.011699,0.042674,0.048009,0.03452,-0.000704,0.015774,0.021758,0.050294,0.029548]},{"p":"L16N6833+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word that links the two sides of a trade or reciprocal exchange, chiefly \"for\" in \"in exchange for\" / \"swap X for\" / \"traded X for\", and \"pro\" in \"quid pro quo\", plus the verb \"exchange\" itself, marking that one thing is being given in return for another (weaker offshoots: offset, minus, alternating dry/wet, recouped).","s":[0.301031,0.2894,0.393662,0.388593,0.073849,0.014141,0.017592,0.004887,0.008652,0.016943,0.007262,0.010533,0.019159,0.002232,0.014515]},{"p":"L16N6833-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires inside a stated ratio or exchange-rate between two quantities, mainly on the second quantity and its unit or connecting words, as in \"a $10 impact for every $1\", \"for every acre donated, nine acres set aside\", \"2 hours of study for every class hour\", \"10 minutes of charging gives 4.5 hours\", \"56 days on and 4 days off\", or funding meant to leverage, match or spur additional money, in contexts where one amount is being converted into or paired against another.","s":[0.516183,1.112338,0.375765,0.413642,0.397241,0.043732,0.015488,0.051379,0.016181,0.00481,0.017667,0.005196,0.018373,0.011615,0.016683]},{"p":"L16N764+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the token that completes the second member of a paired or compound expression, where the partner is predictable from what came just before, typically right after a slash, hyphen, '&', 'and' or 'or' (Light/Dark, LH/RH, TUN/TAP, June/July, 8/8.1, silver or gold, Jaguar Land Rover, Williamsburg-James City), and also on the later pieces of a multi-token name or identifier that already appeared earlier in the text; it does not fire on the first member of the pair.","s":[0.126154,0.37132,0.646024,0.242061,0.433485,0.174591,0.390221,0.021992,0.039324,0.024273,0.041655,0.052982,0.026366,0.010784,0.023082]},{"p":"L16N764-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word that completes a paired or coordinated construction, most strongly on \"respectively\" after two parallel items (\"A and B were 5 and 7, respectively\"), and also on the shared head noun after a coordinated pair of modifiers (\"primary and secondary research\", \"internal and external aspects\", \"light and dark reactions\", \"the editorial and business sides\").","s":[0.370076,0.549261,0.491151,0.69716,0.362493,0.174522,0.374264,-0.002288,0.037248,0.02401,0.017958,0.021631,0.011536,0.016344,0.014108]},{"p":"L16N9181+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the verb of a formal official act that makes something legally binding or official, chiefly \"signed\" in news or legal prose about a president or governor signing a bill or law (also \"sworn\" in a swearing-in, and a sentence length after \"sentenced to\"), right before the object of the act (\"into law\", \"the Act\", \"in\").","s":[0.956805,0.950772,0.696506,0.547669,0.470312,0.329873,0.225452,0.303553,0.186132,0.167072,0.051956,0.019626,0.165809,0.039016,0.012035]},{"p":"L16N9181-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"In legal, governmental or bylaw/regulatory text, fires on the word naming a formal procedural action an authority takes to change someone's status or a rule (amendment, removal, parole, pardon, exemption, petition, admission, disciplinary action, changing a rule), including the 'of' in 'possibility of parole'.","s":[0.539927,0.387174,0.686797,0.61617,0.776672,0.039403,0.039885,0.009549,0.053348,-0.003066,0.01672,0.010637,0.017644,0.013269,0.031364]},{"p":"L16N9243+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the second or later piece of a highly predictable fixed multi-token expression, strongest on the phase-state label inside chemical equations written in LaTeX, such as the \"(s\" or \"(a\" of CdI_{2(s)} or Sr(NO_3)_{2(aq)}, and also on \" Times\" in \"New York Times bestselling\", \"board\" in \"Billboard 200\", \"as\" in \"Nasdaq\", the \"here\" in \"click here\", and the \"d\" in a '%04d' format string.","s":[1.020178,0.791047,0.865875,0.04298,0.228466,0.017456,0.029097,0.017957,0.03615,0.021182,0.027492,0.022806,0.017387,0.037693,0.033067]},{"p":"L16N9243-","l":"UNK","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the first word or symbol of a new structural segment, especially right after a markdown heading marker (#, ##, ###) or after a blank-line break that begins a title, footer or boilerplate line (e.g. \"### The ...\", \"# 0 1 2\", \"Powered by phpBB\"), and on the separator comma inside a page-title or address line such as \"City, State\" or \"Name, «title»\"; this is a diffuse layout or position signal rather than a topic.","s":[0.432599,0.099946,0.133254,0.267866,0.24558,0.035311,0.031845,0.067305,0.054162,0.044586,0.037542,0.052876,0.077111,0.049235,0.026314]},{"p":"L17N3004+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the present-participle verb that opens a trailing participial phrase right after a comma (or on the comma or period just before such a continuation) in polished, AI-style blog or marketing prose, as in \"…has taken the world by storm, captivating the hearts of collectors\" or \"…is dynamic, moving and being repurposed\".","s":[0.989347,0.158157,0.765517,0.276659,0.297082,0.243133,0.127039,0.115038,0.052244,0.068658,0.027345,0.056768,-0.002224,0.007839,0.014519]},{"p":"L17N3004-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"In flowery, AI-style promotional or blog prose, this pole fires on the highly predictable word that completes a stock cliché, typically the last word of a sentence just before the period or the second half of an \"A and B\" or \"A yet B\" pair (\"the city that never sleeps\", \"one thrilling experience at a time\", \"sure to please everyone at the table\", \"timeless yet contemporary\", \"good food and good company\"), and on \"of\" in \"the art of\" or \"the field of\".","s":[0.305425,0.209118,0.281555,0.196694,0.082401,0.091714,0.047224,0.094587,0.042473,0.058124,0.047218,0.046181,0.013559,0.017627,0.073656]},{"p":"L17N4176+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires at spots where a writer left content unfinished or used a placeholder in place of real content, such as \"(citation)\" or \"(cite omitted)\" in prose, \"TODO\", \"blah blah\", \"??\" or a dangling \"...\", \"(Add yours)\", an empty call or argument like getElementsByClassName(\"\") or append(), or a bare `pass` or empty stub in code; the model is predicting a TODO or ??? marker.","s":[0.255718,-0.002204,0.137934,0.084722,0.25305,0.034581,0.037765,0.037597,0.009731,0.008245,0.022877,0.016298,0.013599,0.025699,0.023898]},{"p":"L17N4176-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on placeholder, template and unfinished content: Lorem ipsum filler, bracketed fill-in slots like [Company Name] or [Font Name], 'Click to add title' and 'Add Your Heading Text Here' boilerplate, example.com / (123) 456 789 dummy contact info, and stub or commented-out scaffold code (empty class bodies, template test-attribute comments, example assertions); it marks text that is a stand-in to be replaced later (output promotes TODO and ???).","s":[0.299229,0.414335,1.00811,0.433611,0.361117,0.111665,0.034854,0.151944,0.210096,0.01896,0.029157,0.039152,0.024306,0.024827,0.040205]},{"p":"L17N4752+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the name inside a section heading of a long, repetitive list of parallel entries, most strongly on programming-language names in Rosetta Code-style headings (\"## Prolog\", \"## Forth\", \"## Nim\") that separate one short code snippet from the next; weaker cases are headings or question titles in other repeated-entry pages (wiki \"== Title ==\" lists, FAQ questions, numbered book lists), and the effect leans on the repetition built up earlier, so a lone mention of a language name will not fire it.","s":[0.394734,0.587258,0.435248,0.454149,0.518922,0.070474,0.220301,0.185567,0.064279,0.012647,0.031233,0.076645,0.047242,0.025694,0.044851]},{"p":"L17N4752-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the second or third word of a short title, heading, tag line or breadcrumb that opens a web page, especially SEO-style headings such as 'Phoenix Slot', 'How to Find an Online Casino' or 'Tag: Ubuntu', before any running prose begins.","s":[0.206339,0.083188,0.0868,0.110877,0.056174,0.056268,0.041092,0.033418,0.072451,0.03833,0.098132,0.214824,0.089036,0.120228,0.113714]},{"p":"L17N4778+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the opening or connecting words inside the proper title of a named creative work, such as a quest, mission, episode, song, book, achievement, event or add-on (e.g. 'The' in 'the mission The Cousins Bellic', 'Whole' in 'A Whole New World', 'and' in 'Dice and Dragons', 'of' in 'Taste of Coeur d'Alene'), typically right after a label introducing the title in game wikis, episode lists, credits or citations, and not on the same words used in ordinary prose.","s":[0.264868,0.16748,0.196897,0.80653,0.227313,0.016886,0.056141,0.041218,0.112457,0.019281,0.015409,0.028811,0.010009,0.021079,0.012479]},{"p":"L17N4778-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the second word or second word-piece of a capitalized title or brand name (a book, article, blog, store, product or album name, such as 'Destination Discovery', 'Spring Cleaning', 'The Warming Store', or the 'usions' in 'Illusions'), most strongly when that title already appeared earlier in the text and is being repeated, as in a headline restated in the opening line.","s":[1.163965,0.369884,0.330836,0.214134,0.373454,0.196746,0.19853,0.085491,0.020526,0.214779,0.039835,0.00835,0.042169,0.031866,0.006232]},{"p":"L17N6736+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires at the point where a fresh value is about to be created by generation or randomness, mostly in code or tech how-to text: on the token right before or at the generate/new/random step, as in \"create a new\", \"Math.random\", \"generate a random key\", \"make a salt\", \"getUUID\", \"key pair is generated\". It predicts words like generate, random and newly.","s":[0.393976,0.379471,0.359334,0.572704,0.392499,0.043952,0.089172,0.017977,0.272482,0.015838,0.021706,0.013104,0.026474,0.003155,0.007412]},{"p":"L17N6736-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"In software code and technical documentation about creating or instantiating things (new objects, buffers, nodes, records, templates, automatically created content, default values used when something is not set), the pole fires on ordinary tokens such as indentation, \"the\", \"=\" or a noun at spots where a word like new, generated, created or automatically could plausibly come next.","s":[0.040225,0.257592,0.103665,0.126432,0.054021,0.035361,0.062636,0.029005,0.032446,0.075501,0.027785,0.041728,0.049115,0.026479,0.044781]},{"p":"L17N7543+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires inside rigidly templated boilerplate text, especially stock-site company profiles (\"X Inc. designs and markets ...; it also provides ... In addition, the company offers ... The company was incorporated in 1980 and is headquartered in ...\") and market-research report prose (\"key players\", \"new product launches, expansions, joint ventures\", \"Segments Covered: By Type ...\"), as well as infobox or nutrition-label fields (\"Worst case performance\", \"Known for\", \"Carbohydrates\"), on the token where the rest of the stock phrase is predictable from the template.","s":[0.358155,0.576091,0.598137,0.479772,1.130798,0.034893,0.04281,0.084307,0.046379,0.070297,0.049357,0.054009,0.041976,0.038358,0.025798]},{"p":"L17N7543-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires inside an encyclopedic defining or characterizing statement about a subject (\"X is the most/second-largest...\", \"X generally causes mild symptoms\", \"there is no cure\", \"X is a biopharmaceutical company\", \"id is the primary key\"), peaking on the descriptor word right after the copula or verb, especially superlatives and ranks, mild/no in symptom descriptions, and simplest/difficult/subtle.","s":[0.139981,0.4942,0.338986,0.586924,0.349092,0.043606,0.042353,0.069823,0.013886,0.049852,0.026792,0.028412,0.021822,0.023965,0.073622]},{"p":"L17N78+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on tokens inside the still-unfinished subject noun phrase of a subordinate or embedded clause, typically one opened by where, when, how, what, whose or as (e.g. 'where commerce, community and creative intertwine', 'a stroke occurs when a blood vessel that carries…', 'what the best route forward might be'), on the words and commas after the clause opener but before that clause's verb arrives.","s":[0.441253,0.653143,0.07342,0.135308,0.12542,0.044227,0.115505,0.038199,0.032515,0.038297,0.027297,0.030893,0.027968,0.02385,0.062551]},{"p":"L17N78-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the head noun of a subject that opens a new clause mid-sentence, typically right after a conjunction such as \"and the\", \"or the\" or \"as\" and often repeating a noun just mentioned, at the point where the clause's verb is about to follow (e.g. \"she fit the sound and the sound fit her\", \"call within ten days or the statement will be deemed correct\").","s":[0.540591,0.317172,0.102603,0.073277,0.269872,0.0541,0.029575,0.001171,0.005901,0.024105,0.000363,0.12103,0.021384,0.075551,0.009975]},{"p":"L17N9569+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the abstract quality noun or adverb naming a personal trait or desirable attribute that completes a phrase like \"ability to work ___\", \"demonstrates ___\", \"lack of self-___\" or \"test your ___\", most often in job ads, career advice and study-abroad essays: independently, autonomously, confidence, adaptability, ambition, leadership, transparency, self-awareness, flexible.","s":[0.687163,0.331167,0.649359,0.376875,0.423793,0.187654,0.137129,0.077229,0.039897,0.082147,0.025786,0.024244,0.02177,0.020061,0.049437]},{"p":"L17N9569-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"In explanatory, definition-style prose (often about computing, e.g. 'X is a language/kernel/system that is responsible for...'), this pole fires on the first content word of the noun or gerund phrase that names a function or capability, typically right after 'for', 'such as', 'like' or 'and' (e.g. 'low-level memory manipulation', 'such as retrieving data', 'tasks like transferring data', 'session management'), with 'memory' the strongest case.","s":[0.543257,0.524642,0.301388,0.241348,0.27356,0.18373,0.088701,0.025786,0.242283,0.045879,0.037498,0.034366,0.068902,0.032384,0.025192]},{"p":"L18N1776+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the early tokens of a phrase that is being copied verbatim from earlier in the same document, most clearly a web-page title or heading repeated a second time (for example, the word right after 'Do' in a second 'Do Video Games Cause Depression'), and on the leading '1' of yet another 18xx year in a biography already full of such years.","s":[0.128592,0.105886,0.154114,0.053311,0.047046,0.018643,0.017712,0.057459,0.060781,0.047046,0.030069,0.060048,0.051248,0.043639,0.045804]},{"p":"L18N1776-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"In templated or SEO-spam web pages, this fires at the point just before or inside a repeat of the page's title or search keyword phrase, most reliably on the last digit of the job count in a Freelancer-style listing (e.g. 'Php push system jobs ↵ My recent searches ↵ Filter by: ↵ Job State ↵ 199,730 php push system jobs found'), and also on the break before a repeated essay or article title at the end of the page.","s":[0.446517,0.636336,0.453654,0.07159,0.355271,0.009466,0.01003,0.018536,0.014568,0.010177,0.010312,0.013722,0.008599,0.016366,0.008367]},{"p":"L18N2635+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word or word-piece that introduces the next contrasting member of a parallel set already set up in the text: the counterpart or alternative in a pair or series, such as West after East and South, back end after front end, Discharge after Charge, Spanish after English, Character streams after byte streams, or unpowered after powered; this happens most often in repetitive lists, tables, spec sheets or code.","s":[0.026419,0.028292,0.017709,0.027164,0.04794,0.02012,0.036197,0.019711,0.012681,0.028861,0.028743,0.023088,0.025452,0.028366,0.02776]},{"p":"L18N2635-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires at the boundary where one entry of a long, repetitive catalog-style list ends and the next begins, most strongly on the blank line right after a short title or label line that caps each item (for example a movie synopsis followed by its title, then a line break before the next synopsis). It needs many prior repetitions of the pattern, so a short standalone text will likely fire it only weakly.","s":[0.030746,0.038417,0.09215,0.044141,0.043809,0.027,0.029643,0.027383,0.021997,0.011158,0.041977,0.026426,0.010129,0.007431,0.017813]},{"p":"L18N2645+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a token that continues a structural template already repeated several times earlier in the same document (e.g. the separator after a timestamp in the Nth forum comment header, the 'for' in a repeated SQL 'Constraints for table' line, a dashed comment rule, a cell tag in a repeated table row, the 'secs' in a repeated quote list), so it is an in-context repetition/induction signal that needs several prior copies of the pattern and is hard to reproduce in a one-to-three-sentence text.","s":[0.21106,0.172295,0.091139,0.282005,0.071802,0.028002,0.060734,0.060843,0.164595,0.053349,0.010903,0.022873,0.05467,0.143525,0.064464]},{"p":"L18N2645-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires inside highly repetitive, templated list text (scraped review pages, name directories, numbered specs, repeated headings) on a token of a line that copies an earlier line's fixed frame, typically the last fixed word right before the slot that changes, such as the \"of\" in a third \"Photo of username\" or the number in \"Chambre 2\" after \"Chambre 1\"; it needs the pattern to have already repeated in the text.","s":[0.455211,0.516095,0.184877,0.262677,0.265226,0.137049,0.043266,0.164134,0.020365,0.026043,0.018625,0.021385,0.013563,0.021813,0.01011]},{"p":"L18N3071+","l":"TWO-REL","j":"11","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the comma (or bracket/space) right after the city name in a news-wire dateline at the start of a news story, e.g. \"Kolkata, July 5 (PTI) –\" or \"Amman, June 19 (Petra)\", and more weakly on the equals sign, arrow or line break in step-by-step worked math solutions (\"⇒ 13x = 715\").","s":[0.352819,0.474502,0.545274,0.398992,0.58524,0.03222,0.056436,0.093269,0.042621,0.037368,0.037511,0.040937,0.029208,0.034663,0.054048]},{"p":"L18N3071-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Inside Islamic religious text, it fires on the ordinary function words and name pieces in the middle of a quoted saying of the Prophet Muhammad or of God (a hadith, a Qur'anic verse, a pious formula such as \"Allah is the Granter of Success\" or \"Narrated Abdullah bin Abbas\"), strongest within the quotation right after an attribution like \"Muhammad said:\" or \"Hadith Qudsi:\".","s":[1.160588,0.797709,0.689423,1.205408,1.083355,0.000402,0.013454,0.030419,0.051973,0.024023,-0.001461,0.016395,0.005592,0.01422,0.015633]},{"p":"L18N5580+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires inside descriptions of backup, fallback or contingency mechanisms (something that takes over or runs when the main thing fails or regardless of outcome), peaking on the words right after the cue, as in 'backup/emergency/uninterruptible power', 'which can run a computer if the main supply fails', 'an emergency fund', 'shuts down the inverter in the event of a fault', or a code 'finally' block that 'runs no matter / regardless of' the result.","s":[0.885081,0.459451,0.433844,0.692156,0.493325,1.331572,0.028379,0.036882,0.090294,0.028066,0.013153,0.045787,0.050378,0.034802,0.050495]},{"p":"L18N5580-","l":"TWO-REL","j":"11","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on words that name or set up a fallback or contingency option in case the main plan fails, such as \"backup\", \"back-up\", \"alternative\", \"alternate\", \"plan B\", \"rain date\", \"prepare for the worst\" and \"have a plan in case\", peaking on the noun or modifier that introduces the substitute; it also fires more weakly on the default or else value argument in code or spreadsheet function signatures.","s":[0.466356,0.604648,0.367709,0.316374,0.305912,0.026272,0.050574,0.009069,0.012944,0.089531,0.006665,0.006868,0.028116,0.007451,0.009555]},{"p":"L18N621+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the line-wrap break inside a word that has been split by typesetting, i.e. the end-of-line hyphen (or the hyphen-plus-line-break, or a bare line break) that cuts a word into two pieces, as in 'resid- uals', 'com-↵mand', 'mea-↵sure' or 'dis↵tressed', where the model must predict the rest of the broken word; it fires more weakly on plain mid-sentence line wraps in OCR'd or scanned prose.","s":[0.310569,0.368559,0.168831,0.428576,0.354373,0.126911,0.099758,0.034954,0.06567,0.057736,0.031781,0.076802,0.06988,0.102629,0.034597]},{"p":"L18N621-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the run of leading indentation spaces (or tabs) at the start of a line inside source code, especially continuation lines indented to align with the line above, right before a leading operator, dot-method chain, comma, closing brace or comment (e.g. a line starting with spaces and then \".Where(\", \"+ \", \"| \", \"&& \", \"? \", \", \" or \"})\").","s":[0.555144,0.414682,0.724444,0.489593,0.751381,0.03135,0.036061,0.019193,0.02302,0.037163,0.020376,0.018129,0.017104,0.018599,0.012763]},{"p":"L18N7160+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word that describes a state the subject is already in or an action already completed, such as 'have' or a past participle in a perfect tense, 'already', 'logged in', 'exists' or 'under contract'; it peaks at slots where the word 'already' could naturally be inserted next or has just been used, and the output suppresses 'already'.","s":[0.115013,0.117327,0.141408,0.170025,0.142599,0.044244,0.014544,0.012652,0.123851,0.090074,0.047713,0.07538,0.121437,0.110956,0.032096]},{"p":"L18N7160-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a verb or past participle that reports a completed state or change in instructional or technical prose (canceled, installed, removed, changed, set, sent, adapted), at the point where the word \"already\" could naturally come next, e.g. \"it's definitely canceled\" or \"has been removed\".","s":[0.33562,0.010435,0.087562,0.048878,0.017504,0.061915,0.010435,0.012307,0.079619,0.01874,0.024229,0.030208,0.013646,0.051993,0.023068]},{"p":"L18N8559+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a word, or the first piece of a multi-token name, that repeats something already used earlier in the same text (e.g. the second or later mention of 'Bosnian', 'Kateřina', 'kratom', 'video games' or 'Scáthach'), where the model can copy the rest of the earlier phrase. It is strongest when the passage is comparing or contrasting two things.","s":[0.037735,0.103199,0.060318,0.088176,0.023596,0.037288,0.103199,0.054939,0.088176,0.023596,0.047989,0.052836,0.039599,0.032709,0.037658]},{"p":"L18N8559-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires inside a cross-domain analogy, on the word that names the new domain or the counterpart term being mapped onto a familiar concept, such as \"sailing world\" in \"Cape Horn is the Everest of the sailing world\", \"brain\" in \"weight lifting for the brain\", \"Indian football\" in \"the Virat Kohli of Indian football\", or \"Reluctance\" given as the magnetic counterpart of resistance.","s":[0.353146,0.651425,0.198913,0.835505,0.079114,0.035992,0.185456,0.023966,0.017197,0.026306,0.01215,0.011847,0.012271,0.001045,0.023919]},{"p":"L19N1020+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a token inside a passage that is being copied verbatim from earlier in the same text, such as a duplicated photo caption, a URL repeated right after itself, or a number carried from an earlier line into a formula; the firing comes on the second occurrence, where the next piece is predictable by copying (e.g. 'Photo: Alex Emslie/KQED. Photo: Alex Emslie/KQED' firing on the repeated name), not on first mentions.","s":[0.170018,0.134848,0.079183,0.078463,0.092562,0.204145,0.078096,0.097486,0.105929,0.033211,0.036119,0.020402,0.008237,0.045714,0.053033]},{"p":"L19N1020-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a token in the middle of a phrase, URL, title or code expression that is being repeated verbatim from earlier in the same text, so the token can be predicted by copying (e.g., the second time a URL slug like 'a-piper-too' or a phrase like 'Strawberry runtz jungle boys' appears, or the next number in '1 | 2 | 3'); first occurrences do not fire.","s":[0.083994,0.293616,0.15618,0.124236,0.188494,0.050464,0.293616,0.142056,0.127414,0.188494,0.2322,0.026881,0.039233,0.049387,0.090687]},{"p":"L19N1373+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on ordinary function words and stock adjectives (and, to, This, detailed, expected) inside formulaic, SEO-style market-research boilerplate, such as \"Global X Market Report is a professional and in-depth research report\", \"the market was valued at USD N billion in 2021 and is expected to grow\", \"the study offers a detailed overview\", or \"Among these regions, Asia Pacific is expected to\"; the more templated and predictable the commercial filler prose, the stronger it fires.","s":[1.079074,0.447908,0.12226,0.363047,1.005155,0.120908,0.157148,0.066145,0.115494,0.11088,0.123994,0.144882,0.119466,0.143815,0.119302]},{"p":"L19N1373-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the adjective/adverb-forming suffix piece (-ically, -ical, -ic, -ial, -ian, -onian, -iques) that completes a learned noun stem into a relational adjective or adverb, and on whole relational adjectives like economic, military, social, religious, biological, especially in academic prose where the word qualifies a domain (\"politically, socially and economically\", \"both social and political\", \"from a biological point of view\").","s":[0.550519,0.409484,0.500614,0.381784,0.476572,0.006024,0.024461,0.004109,0.020045,0.01061,0.004379,0.00291,0.008354,0.016614,0.027957]},{"p":"L19N2402+","l":"TWO-REL","j":"11","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires inside dense, unpredictable non-prose strings embedded in English reference-style documents, most strongly on characters of classical Chinese passages quoted in an English footnote or citation (for example a quote from the Shiji or Records of the Grand Historian), and also on the letters right after the slash in Oxford-style DOIs (10.1093/jnci/djh310), on step numbers in Metamath proof listings, and on SVG path coordinates.","s":[0.68938,0.477952,0.743775,0.838678,2.009281,0.137006,0.059333,0.063842,0.049655,0.029009,0.058092,0.030435,0.06405,0.044988,0.037133]},{"p":"L19N2402-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on ordinary mid-line words (often 'and', 'the', 'from', 'a', 'her') inside quoted famous classic English verse, such as Shakespeare, Blake, Milton, Keats, hymns and carols, where the model recognizes the memorized poem and can predict the next word; it does not fire on modern prose that merely discusses poetry.","s":[0.116473,0.252648,0.633133,0.18769,0.612388,0.110861,0.079788,0.065158,0.075468,0.028684,0.014938,0.03274,0.022905,0.077599,0.054992]},{"p":"L19N5468+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a generic category noun used as a label right before (or in place of) the specific instance it names, most strongly 'the word' or 'the term' immediately followed by a quoted word ('can spell the word \"competitive\"', 'the word 'bicycle''), and also 'the state of Maryland', 'the years 1869 and 1883', 'Republic of Korea', 'Professor Quirrell', or 'the sport' and 'the console' referring back to a just-named thing.","s":[0.29675,0.065067,0.085881,0.097472,0.186462,0.04403,0.042222,0.023416,0.013522,0.016686,0.04811,0.035956,0.010435,0.023734,0.018833]},{"p":"L19N5468-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a generic category noun (state, province, region, district, newspaper, channel, agency, utility, framework, language, console) placed right after a modifier that already names or locates the specific entity, as in \"the Australian state of Victoria\", \"the Westfälische Rundschau newspaper\", \"the PHP framework CodeIgniter\", or \"the atop utility\": an appositive type-label for a proper name.","s":[0.709219,0.333362,0.673119,0.74579,0.501278,0.006322,0.025805,0.010519,0.009073,0.017456,0.022768,0.02854,0.017009,0.012713,0.024499]},{"p":"L19N6516+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires broadly on ordinary mid-sentence tokens (function words, possessives, the first word of a noun phrase) inside polished, generic, AI-written-sounding web copy (SEO blog posts, marketing pages, reviews, how-to guides full of phrases like 'embark on a journey', 'harness the power', 'a testament to'), rather than on any particular word.","s":[0.121584,0.211356,0.115892,0.141392,0.121031,0.143572,0.009891,0.064886,0.046053,0.008319,0.00823,0.011247,0.004757,0.022871,0.046798]},{"p":"L19N6516-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the period that closes the opening advice sentence of a paragraph in generic SEO-style 'tips' articles (e.g. 'Make sure your website is easy to navigate.'), especially web-design how-to advice, right before the next sentence elaborates on that tip.","s":[0.058909,0.027208,0.043695,0.017957,0.049077,0.035892,0.054689,0.140839,0.012789,0.043011,0.086173,0.17361,0.042297,0.088052,0.078255]},{"p":"L19N6951+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the punctuation or line break that closes a self-contained entry or block (a period, bracket or closing parenthesis followed by a blank line, often after a trailing metadata tail such as a phone number, date, price, score, source or 'Buy on Amazon') right before a new item or heading begins, in listings, reviews, calendars, forum posts and code.","s":[0.176147,0.227402,0.1461,0.267032,0.220797,0.043999,0.025233,0.033185,0.034361,0.020752,0.050936,0.023795,0.017046,0.029782,0.042659]},{"p":"L19N6951-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the closing parenthesis that ends a parenthetical aside in running prose, i.e. the \")\" token (often fused with following punctuation as \"),\", \").\", \"?)\") right after a short bracketed note such as \"(Noon)\", \"(Second Edition)\", \"(sp)\", \"(is there any other kind?)\" or \"(see what I did there?)\".","s":[0.034729,0.03506,0.181991,0.210584,0.612857,0.040465,0.016385,0.0154,0.019284,0.127451,0.017041,0.017341,0.018178,0.007873,0.026541]},{"p":"L19N7985+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"In source code (or code comments and docstrings), fires on the tokens where a program is about to look for or split on a delimiter character inside a string, such as a string index or loop over input[i] about to be compared to '+', '-' or '/', or text like .find(, .indexOf(, Split(new char[] {, 'split the line at the', 'space delimited', or a variable named dotPositions or tab = line.split, mostly just before the separator character is named.","s":[0.721086,0.481693,1.0245,0.560627,0.441632,0.017269,0.002893,0.017,0.061298,0.014756,0.020822,0.007065,0.002322,0.018727,0.009697]},{"p":"L19N7985-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires in text about splitting or dividing something into pieces, groups or chunks and what happens to the resulting parts or leftovers (code that splits a list or string and filters out empty pieces, chunking an array into sub-lists with a shorter remainder, cells dividing into daughter cells, cutting or dividing plant bulbs or rhizomes), peaking on the words that refer back to the pieces just after the split is described, such as \"with\" in \"left with a sub-list\", \"if x\" in a filter, \"each daughter cell\", or \"the larger section\".","s":[1.053645,0.753844,0.621921,1.453004,0.351647,0.009263,0.13652,0.003379,0.016097,0.016001,0.018882,0.029292,0.025323,0.015998,0.045757]},{"p":"L19N9685+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires where a sentence is about to state what a living thing or device needs or cannot tolerate, on the subject or the word just before the requirement (e.g. \"warm-season plants| require\", \"cucumbers| prefer\", \"They are| sensitive to frost\", \"micro|be requires oxygen\", \"accustomed| to\", \"delicate\"), and it predicts words like requires, sensitive, demands or specific needs.","s":[0.481157,0.639645,0.609249,0.500034,0.17009,0.158158,0.055401,0.016153,0.013753,0.024131,0.015549,0.023649,0.016027,0.019655,0.011401]},{"p":"L19N9685-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"In practical advice or technical prose, fires on the words where the text says that what is suitable, safe or required depends on local or environmental conditions, such as 'climate' in 'depends on the climate', 'depending on', 'according to the season', 'if you live in a region with…', 'not safe for' or 'a difficult environment for'; it is setting up a following 'requires' or 'suitable'.","s":[0.646445,0.352711,0.202657,0.384745,0.335203,0.16935,0.092186,0.113316,0.016559,0.035602,0.049665,0.107872,0.014742,0.022449,0.083231]},{"p":"L20N5089+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"In source code (Python, JavaScript, C#), fires on the object or variable name, or its last word-piece, that opens a statement and is about to have a method called on it, i.e. the token right before \".method(\" in a standalone call line such as \"game.guess()\", \"self.root.update(ms)\", \"rng.GetBytes(buf)\" or \"left.InOrder();\".","s":[0.332551,0.654271,0.383021,0.309049,0.168732,0.08802,0.226878,0.204625,0.422911,0.149175,0.082304,0.065514,0.03294,0.017796,0.033066]},{"p":"L20N5089-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the last word (or last word-piece) that completes a multi-word noun compound or proper name, most strongly organizational group nouns such as team, committee, community, government or institutions after their modifiers (e.g. 'research team', 'Program Committee Meeting', 'Nominating Committee', 'Advisory Committee'), especially in meeting agendas, conference programs and corporate/report prose where the compound is itself followed by another noun or a possessive.","s":[0.018273,0.099029,0.085868,0.134675,0.01463,0.067118,0.036091,0.024962,0.04012,0.030297,0.074938,0.056953,0.014371,0.067565,0.033727]},{"p":"L20N5110+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"In technical or instructional prose, it fires on the period, often followed by a paragraph break, that ends a sentence telling the reader which tool, command or method to use (\"I would wipe the drive using dd.\", \"You can use Array.Copy.\", \"Issue a simple GET request.\"), right where a colon introducing a code block or example could have gone; it also fires weakly mid-sentence just before such an example (\"like this example\", \"linked below\"), and it pushes the output away from a colon.","s":[0.271375,0.438386,0.182733,0.097094,0.236686,0.026242,0.030342,0.024279,0.025527,0.022228,0.040912,0.026907,0.015684,0.035596,0.022018]},{"p":"L20N5110-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"In technical worked solutions such as engineering, physics or chemistry problems, this fires on the verb \"is\"/\"are\" (or \"is given\", \"as\") right after a noun phrase naming a quantity, just before a formula or numeric value is stated, as in \"The capacitance is C = ...\", \"The stresses are ...\", \"The equilibria are\".","s":[0.254745,0.146761,0.310456,0.106155,0.361717,0.101723,0.010823,0.08873,0.06732,0.043002,0.021484,0.033989,0.006331,0.030209,0.029169]},{"p":"L20N5113+","l":"UNK","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the token just before a phrase that repeats one introduced earlier in the same document, typically a function word such as 'the', 'to' or 'for' in recipe, procedure or code text, as in 'dissolve the salt' after salt was listed or 'tool for brand differentiation' after that phrase already appeared; it needs a long document and is very dense and weak in short texts.","s":[0.188737,0.067997,0.046061,0.084448,0.069396,0.053905,0.067837,0.10697,0.029234,0.050074,0.04106,0.099369,0.085277,0.061817,0.025217]},{"p":"L20N5113-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires at the boundary where the next item in a parallel series of comparable entries begins, on the connector or space just before it (\" or\", \" and\", \" plus\", \"+\", \"/or\", a comma, or a table-cell space), especially when the entries are numbers or measurements, as in \"from 17 to 16\", \"7 lamps + 6 computers\", \"the 250 and 800 heads plus the 102 cab\", or rows of numbers in a table.","s":[0.046565,0.02939,0.031904,0.093552,0.043593,0.010053,0.01039,0.014284,0.01234,0.028972,0.014043,0.005562,0.014728,0.013336,0.015172]},{"p":"L20N7249+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word that sets up a comparison or symbolic mapping between two things, just before the second thing is named: “represents”, “is similar to”, “the same as”, “reminds me of”, “a metaphor/analogy/allegory/comparison”, “couldn't help but notice similarities”, “transferable to”.","s":[0.305981,0.177574,0.540787,0.218757,0.125048,0.059109,0.118931,0.092347,0.01301,0.081331,0.090316,0.061551,0.096226,0.027611,0.043482]},{"p":"L20N7249-","l":"UNK","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Diffuse, context-heavy pole with no shared token: it fires most strongly at the point where a clause or noun phrase in the middle of a long document is completed (a period, comma, closing bracket or the head noun of a phrase) and the text is about to continue with an elaboration of that statement, and it is probably hard to reproduce in a short standalone text.","s":[0.188771,0.088426,0.077795,0.113898,0.311432,-0.006888,0.049627,-0.001942,0.096175,-0.000369,0.035444,0.017553,0.159534,0.090088,0.108983]},{"p":"L20N7310+","l":"UNREL","j":"00","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires mostly inside source code on the first identifier or keyword that opens a new statement or assignment line, such as a variable name before '=', 'const', or a repeated test-comment word like 'Act'; it also fires in casual first-person prose on quantity idioms such as 'a lot', 'tens of thousands' and 'as many'.","s":[0.043768,0.254228,0.032988,0.025927,0.092152,0.075909,0.019628,0.018898,0.015424,0.083864,0.003811,0.018738,0.009078,0.06555,0.018464],"u":{"0":[0.048184,0.198259,0.081251,0.619208,0.206591],"1":[0.054426,0.018414,0.024827,0.032425,0.027604]}},{"p":"L20N7310-","l":"TWO-REL","j":"11","f":0,"e":0,"x":"In source code (Python, JavaScript, R) the pole fires on the variable name or whitespace that begins a new assignment statement inside a function or loop body (e.g. the 'w' in 'w = y * y' or 'vec <- c(...)'), and in explanatory prose on the subject or verb just after a hypothetical setup such as 'For example, if you…' or 'if numpy is…'.","s":[0.048152,0.107959,0.144479,0.081912,0.136261,0.021525,0.021236,0.017122,0.081924,0.027672,0.028228,0.0168,0.005285,0.02257,0.034229]},{"p":"L20N7603+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word or word-piece that completes a fixed idiom or phrasal verb right after its predictable lead-in word, e.g. \"might as well\", \"can't seem to\", \"well versed\", \"miss out\", \"help me out\", \"check out\", \"don't get me wrong\", \"icing on the cake\", \"have yet to\", \"set forth\", \"hailing from\", \"if we were to\"; ordinary literal uses of the same small words do not drive it.","s":[0.030292,0.056621,0.054209,0.175563,0.037093,0.012962,0.022167,0.011803,0.058496,0.025229,0.024996,0.008311,0.01338,0.014189,0.00311]},{"p":"L20N7603-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word in English text where a non-native (ESL) writer makes a grammatical slip or unidiomatic choice: a missing preposition or object ('we will reply you', 'I want add', 'how work interval?'), a wrong preposition ('experience about Java', 'research about'), a misplaced adverb ('resigned also from'), or an odd article or verb form ('you are not right. I am assured'), usually in forum posts, product pages or student writing.","s":[0.175335,0.542192,0.512327,0.121498,0.278791,0.089073,0.177689,0.100087,0.121498,0.061317,0.012242,0.031981,0.036228,0.017464,0.026757]},{"p":"L20N864+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the head noun that completes an adjective-plus-noun or name-plus-noun compound in formal descriptive or academic prose, often on the first piece of that noun, as in \"optical properties\", \"verbal communication\", \"manual transmission\", \"Fukuoka prefecture\", \"Lyon County\" or \"undergraduate degree\"; it marks where a noun phrase is finished, and its output pushes against a following \"and\" or \"or\".","s":[0.210546,0.240655,0.186333,0.367705,0.179966,0.094674,0.061534,0.016574,0.017966,0.002773,0.007252,0.004629,0.066168,-0.001113,0.022977]},{"p":"L20N864-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the shared head noun that closes the first item of a coordinated list in which every item is a different modifier plus the same repeated noun (e.g. the second word in 'LDPE sheet, HDPE sheet and UHMWPE sheet', 'deep groove ball bearings, pillow block bearings', 'left-eye image and right-eye image', 'Criminal Law Commons, and the ... Commons'), right before the comma or 'and' that introduces the next parallel item.","s":[0.122237,0.093059,0.040152,0.505964,0.185271,0.031662,0.074955,0.031307,0.039477,0.022235,0.022016,0.028221,0.030998,0.022389,0.024603]},{"p":"L20N9552+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the linking token at the point where a text moves to the next step in a chain of successive events or operations, such as 'and' or 'then' joining consecutive actions in a narrative or game recap ('scored early and then...', 'gave a chug and sputter'), 'until' or 'As a result' in a cause-to-consequence progression, or an arithmetic operator (+, ×, /) between numbers in a worked calculation; it does not fire on 'and' joining static nouns in a list.","s":[0.364393,0.70195,0.300675,0.255944,0.562616,0.033601,0.04856,0.041808,0.116102,0.069724,0.030914,0.190923,0.062801,0.116682,0.03497]},{"p":"L20N9552-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on \"then\" (or the \"and\"/\"only\" just before it, or \"afterwards\"/\"after that\") at the point where a sentence moves from a first step to the second step of a sequence, strongest in prescriptive ordering phrases like \"do X first, and then Y\", \"only then\", \"and only then should you…\".","s":[0.450455,0.395832,0.052627,0.484834,0.169852,0.023874,0.012572,0.022883,0.014699,0.126795,0.019612,0.025193,0.032564,0.019805,0.00888]},{"p":"L21N1823+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the verbs \"attract\" and \"draw\" in any inflection (attracted, attracts, attracting, draws, drawing, drawn) when used in the sense of pulling in people's attention, crowds, stares, criticism, ire or users, i.e. where words like \"attention\", \"a crowd\" or \"criticism\" come next (the output promotes \"attention\"/\"notice\").","s":[0.490745,0.431993,0.450899,0.588636,0.381908,0.173957,0.023207,0.12745,0.106635,0.124548,0.006584,0.007146,0.002364,0.007982,0.004521]},{"p":"L21N1823-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the boilerplate metadata of document-sharing, essay-mill and flashcard sites, meaning the post-date, view-count, download-count and file-size lines such as 'Post on 02-Dec-2014', '7 months ago 46', 'Download 43.55 Kb', 'Published on', 'Views: 6530' and 'Related documents'; it fires on the date pieces, separators and newlines inside these lines, not on the actual content.","s":[0.936911,0.405422,0.488829,0.868492,0.668472,0.042044,0.060011,0.046036,0.037894,0.03145,0.050045,0.048404,0.025418,0.039025,0.047254]},{"p":"L21N1897+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the structural tokens of JSON or YAML key–value syntax, meaning the closing quote and colon after a key (\":\", \": {\", \":↵\"), the opening quote or start of a key, or the comma before the next key, inside data objects such as Jupyter notebook JSON, API payloads, config headers or JS object literals; it is strongest at the very start of a JSON document like {\"cells\": or {\"api\":.","s":[0.882762,0.589744,0.537749,0.169074,0.249844,0.023271,0.005032,0.015944,0.020769,0.011563,0.01299,0.026219,0.008622,0.008706,0.013474]},{"p":"L21N1897-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"In source code (JavaScript fetch calls, Python plotting or HTTP handlers, SQL), this pole fires on the opening-quote token, often fused with a bracket, paren or equals sign as in ('  [\"  ='  (\", that starts a string literal whose content is a predictable key or name, most strongly the header key in a fetch headers object such as headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' }, and also the first item of a string array like ['January' or ['top' or a keyword argument like label='Train Data'.","s":[0.456004,0.584755,0.179432,0.266926,0.094782,0.069806,0.122283,0.038497,0.072535,0.07383,0.065357,0.057506,0.073214,0.053866,0.052248]},{"p":"L21N3107+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a token that is highly predictable from what came before, typically an identifier or name being repeated or completed in code (a variable, argument or method name already used a few lines earlier, such as Result, api, sentence or singletonList), or the next familiar item in a stock list (Pinterest after Facebook, Instagram, WhatsApp, Tumblr; a bank in a list of companies); it does not depend on topic.","s":[0.006586,0.371505,0.00678,0.018713,0.00563,0.042391,0.006586,0.023338,0.014208,0.00433,0.013238,0.02765,0.032866,0.013121,0.023918]},{"p":"L21N3107-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the token that unexpectedly continues a phrase or expression that already looked complete, such as a preposition, conjunction, opening parenthesis, '+' operator, '=' default or inline '#' comment placed right after a finished noun phrase or call (\"28 days during a period\", \"ToLongDateString() + ...\", \"a job in the body\", \"public display (commercial...)\", \"x = True # comment\"), in prose, legal boilerplate or code alike.","s":[0.069894,0.035488,0.190177,0.046697,0.019076,0.037527,0.017173,0.009936,0.034592,0.021721,0.056509,0.108498,0.047049,0.064694,0.068012]},{"p":"L21N3519+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the Chinese negation 不 (alone or fused into tokens like 都不, 你不, 我不, 也不, 却不, 不尽) when it directly precedes and negates a verb or adjective in a Chinese sentence, e.g. 两次密码输入不一致, 手机号码不正确, 我不认为, 每件事情都不那么确定, whether in prose, subtitles, or Chinese strings/comments inside code.","s":[0.239769,0.893088,0.130608,0.648304,0.953102,0.040685,0.010011,0.026395,0.039389,0.414102,0.04957,0.026409,0.023778,0.039441,0.017247]},{"p":"L21N3519-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the Chinese negation token 没有/没 (also 沒有, 未, 并没有, English \"no\" in \"If there are no special instructions\"), most strongly when it comes in Chinese text that sits right after English text, as in bilingual subtitle lines, translations, or the \"没有评论 / 沒有留言\" (no comments) footer after an English blog post.","s":[0.6462,0.702718,0.482623,0.344667,0.490755,-0.003349,0.016531,0.00769,0.017311,0.010074,0.027527,0.017057,0.010303,0.005978,0.011741]},{"p":"L21N5637+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word just before a number word that counts a group, especially one already introduced, i.e. where the model expects a quantity like \"two\" or \"three\" next (\"the bottom two fifths\", \"In Two Volumes\", \"acquired the other two trucks\", \"the other three dimensions\", \"composed of seven members\", \"one of the Big Three\").","s":[0.104541,0.028835,0.144711,0.045372,0.222014,0.016831,0.012369,0.128166,0.071628,0.058949,0.016073,0.017187,0.014533,0.022959,0.014896]},{"p":"L21N5637-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires inside reported p-values in statistics write-ups, on the \"=.\" or \".\" or first digit right after \"p\" (e.g. \"p=.003\", \"p = 0.47\"), especially in results comparing three or more groups or conditions (ANOVA, Friedman, chi-square, Kruskal-Wallis); its output favors \"single\" and suppresses \"both\"/\"two\".","s":[0.62733,0.752509,0.57561,0.092476,0.47993,0.077769,0.071621,0.022032,0.029786,0.021972,0.054439,0.026236,0.007514,0.126138,0.039312]},{"p":"L21N5732+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word naming an alteration (different, reduce, shrink, compress, change, evolve, reword) when the sentence is setting up a 'this changes, but that stays the same' contrast, at the point just before a 'without/while/but … same/retaining/not sacrificing' clause, and it pushes the model toward 'but'.","s":[0.169622,0.170975,0.011752,0.111598,0.033849,0.025562,0.015056,0.009861,0.023178,0.019994,0.019313,0.016904,0.039093,0.015112,0.012809]},{"p":"L21N5732-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the contrastive connective (\"yet\", \"but\", \"while\") or the second-quality word that immediately follows it when a phrase pairs two seemingly opposed or trade-off attributes held together at once, as in \"soft yet fast\", \"accessible but needs care\", \"minimize energy while maintaining performance\", \"low cost and high activity\"; it predicts a balancing \"but\".","s":[0.465941,0.566971,0.125628,0.39727,0.429632,0.152357,0.029912,0.033645,0.028892,0.04844,0.023371,0.016529,0.021795,0.033086,0.074443]},{"p":"L21N7763+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the verb, auxiliary or comma that sits right before a hedged, partial or contrasting claim, the spot just ahead of a following 'not', 'but', 'still' or 'although' in sentences like 'it will continue to work, but it won't get updates', 'the website may not display properly', or 'Not bad, but not great'; it predicts a concessive or negated qualification.","s":[0.089577,0.094386,0.186397,0.008122,0.037477,0.083008,0.041163,0.011366,0.008057,0.008542,0.013821,0.01179,0.015618,0.014749,0.010945]},{"p":"L21N7763-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires inside a concessive clause that grants a point the writer is about to qualify with 'but' or 'still': on 'although', 'while' or 'though' opening such a clause, and on the verb or adjective of the granted point ('Hashtags are nice', 'Vista might not have been perfect', 'the survey did use', 'others could fill it'), anticipating the coming contrast.","s":[0.069035,0.476922,0.042658,0.036185,0.302263,0.048076,0.025106,0.046684,0.022397,0.345059,0.039849,0.044511,0.09492,0.02781,0.047103]},{"p":"L21N9387+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the subject, auxiliary or verb inside a free-relative or manner/kind clause that points back to someone's already-described action, request or situation, as in \"what you are asking / trying / doing\", \"the way you think / he worded it\", \"as proposed\", \"the kind of X\", \"someone like George\", \"children with her condition\", typically in replies that comment on another person's plan or behavior.","s":[0.357251,0.609561,0.38894,0.086872,0.429589,0.010008,0.037223,0.013009,0.011381,0.01086,0.010007,0.010092,0.014998,0.04063,0.020142]},{"p":"L21N9387-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word in a phrase that points back to something just described and treats it as an instance of a kind or quality, e.g. \"like this\", \"as big as this\", \"that kind of\", \"this level of\", \"the type of work\", \"such flavor\", \"a version of\", or \"known as\" / \"referred to as\" when naming the thing just described.","s":[0.146859,0.049623,0.28528,0.363433,0.129014,0.009194,0.008362,0.045134,0.087907,0.025718,0.025144,0.013392,0.039717,0.004673,0.016743]},{"p":"L22N1722+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a token that continues a multi-word phrase or name already written earlier in the same text, at the point where it is being repeated verbatim, so the model can copy the rest from that earlier occurrence (e.g. the second time 'magnetic resonance imaging', 'Green Mountain Power', 'peak value' or 'C$1.10 = US$1.00' appears, on the second or later word of the repeat).","s":[0.0173,0.309882,0.044636,0.675281,0.274858,0.013101,0.13894,0.044636,0.131521,0.141751,0.046619,0.019197,0.094258,0.045606,0.018154]},{"p":"L22N1722-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires inside repetitive, structured lists or code (test tables, SQL inserts, repeated identifiers or numbers) on a token that copies a string seen earlier in the same text, at the point where the new copy is about to diverge from the earlier one (e.g. an earlier \"AdvancedAI1\" now continuing as \"AdvancedAI15\", or \"DeletedToVisibility\" continuing as \"DeletedToVisibilityReversed\").","s":[0.374581,0.229239,0.156829,0.252245,0.036747,0.049958,0.038954,0.048393,0.01317,0.053649,0.03083,0.044287,0.062605,0.025144,0.046711]},{"p":"L22N3065+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"In technical or encyclopedic definition sentences of the form \"X is a ___ ...\" or \"X is the ___ ...\", this pole fires on the adjective or modifier word right after \"a/the\" when that word repeats or derives from the term being defined, as in \"A serial port is a serial interface\", \"Binarization turns an image into a binary image\" or \"the normalized vector is the unit vector\"; it fires on the modifier itself, just before the head noun.","s":[0.577726,0.251134,0.518418,0.53455,0.029286,0.034726,0.111972,0.164752,0.021273,0.020197,0.08936,0.053369,0.019151,0.030747,0.030568]},{"p":"L22N3065-","l":"TWO-REL","j":"11","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word that sits between a copula or 'like' and the head noun of a witty or definitional description of what something is, such as the adjective in 'Friends are those rare people', 'Attitude is a little thing', 'owls look like adorable flying cats', or the 'of' in 'a piece of cloth' and 'bits of software'; in code it also fires mid-pattern in repetitive handler lines like on('click' and KEYDOWN, which needs longer context.","s":[0.284137,0.259019,0.542315,0.19936,0.172836,0.009952,0.041428,0.03407,0.167592,0.038815,0.118164,0.030847,0.130165,0.104159,0.042698]},{"p":"L22N5394+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"In chatty blog or marketing copy, fires on the early words of a rhetorical hook question or call-to-action aimed at the reader. The clearest case is \"you\" or \"ever\" in \"Have you ever tried/seen/heard...?\"; it also fires on \"Having trouble with...?\", \"Always wanted to...?\", \"Want...\", \"If you're looking for...\" and \"Is it true that...?\". It stays quiet on neutral declarative or informational prose.","s":[0.450993,0.113254,0.446549,0.325358,0.450993,0.018526,0.083534,0.117364,0.028912,0.07443,0.041349,0.050615,0.052388,0.035536,0.03295]},{"p":"L22N5394-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the last word of a short, upbeat stock exclamation that wraps up instructions or answers a question, the spot where an exclamation mark usually comes next, such as the final word of \"That's it\", \"You're all set\", \"All done\", \"Thanks\", \"Absolutely\", \"Of course\", \"No problem\", \"Best of luck\" or \"Problem solved\".","s":[0.680482,0.300558,0.198242,0.212254,0.202579,0.254195,0.033007,0.017688,0.068633,0.054352,0.009995,0.006398,0.011072,0.069797,0.056166]},{"p":"L22N611+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the token immediately before an upcoming bare-form action verb, most often the infinitive \"to\" after try/want/need/forget/be sure (\"try to\", \"don't forget to\"), \"you\" in \"How do you\", or an adverb or conjunction slotted between an auxiliary and its verb (\"should briefly\", \"can easily\", \"empirically\", \"edit or\"), i.e. where the next word must be a verb.","s":[0.285549,0.394177,0.905873,0.530673,0.152251,0.045751,0.041643,0.009589,0.041477,0.020825,0.065442,0.056421,0.022381,0.081079,0.207944]},{"p":"L22N611-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word \"to\" (or Spanish \"para\") right after a consent verb in website legal or cookie boilerplate, e.g. \"By using this site you agree to / consent to / are opting-in to / give consent to\", most strongly in cookie banners, privacy policies and terms of use.","s":[0.545968,0.579134,0.992672,0.590348,0.771266,0.243744,0.132485,0.434978,0.0767,0.414135,0.040187,0.081364,0.115138,0.06193,0.056813]},{"p":"L22N6612+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the words of a hedging or exceptive aside, usually in parentheses or between dashes, that qualifies or restricts the claim just made, peaking on the word right after the opener: \"(if such a thing exists)\", \"(not literally)\", \"(except that ...)\", \"(but not anything)\", \"(without me asking first)\", \"- not all of them on musical matters -\", \"or, if not that, ...\"; plain asides giving dates, examples or definitions should not fire it.","s":[0.136787,0.504892,0.233698,0.356393,0.143988,0.105006,0.217483,0.046251,0.018549,0.10317,0.019857,0.034902,0.029514,0.034585,0.014614]},{"p":"L22N6612-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires at the hinge where an explainer or listicle finishes one point and pivots to an additional or different one: strongest on the paragraph-break token after a section's closing sentence when a new subheading follows, and on the second word of a paragraph-opening \"As well as …\" or \"Other than …\" (the \"as\" or \"than\").","s":[0.453129,0.431287,0.494325,0.437731,0.310876,0.025155,0.016477,0.088698,0.029758,0.038093,0.01956,0.017733,0.02028,0.015236,0.023546]},{"p":"L22N6898+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word that opens the second or third item of an enumeration of subsets after a prior \"some …\" or \"one …\" clause — mainly \"still\"/\"yet\" in \"still others\"/\"yet other\", plus \"Other\"/\"Others\"/\"another\"/\"while\"/\"For\" introducing the next group (e.g. \"Some cookies are essential. Other cookies…\", \"some …, others …, and still others …\"), predicting a continuation like \"other(s)/another\".","s":[0.753155,0.975007,0.926882,0.942686,0.851095,0.008249,0.031408,0.008437,0.009413,0.009153,0.006304,0.016506,0.012588,0.013688,0.011322]},{"p":"L22N6898-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word \"other\" (also \"another\"), and most strongly when it is capitalized at the start of a heading, navigation link or line, such as \"Other Users?\", \"Other Categories\", \"Other names for this medication\", \"Other surnames\", or mid-sentence in phrases like \"what other conditions\" or \"from another country\"; the neuron predicts and promotes \"other/another\".","s":[0.181937,0.256033,0.324606,0.22791,0.144561,0.220777,0.06319,0.00235,-0.000885,0.015246,0.012948,0.030039,0.010309,0.014194,0.011915]},{"p":"L22N7061+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the dash or hyphen joining two numbers in a page range or numeric span, especially abbreviated ranges in citations, footnotes and book indexes (e.g. the dash in \"pp. 551–54\", \"at 102–03\", \"211–14\"), where the model is about to output the second number; it also fires on the separator between two long digit runs such as consecutive ISBNs or phone-number parts.","s":[0.24258,0.179356,0.607893,0.716006,0.374434,0.020812,0.018572,0.011861,0.010125,0.063421,0.00246,0.001668,0.006103,0.003284,0.016582]},{"p":"L22N7061-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires inside a passage that repeats a string seen earlier in the same text, on the token partway through the repeat where the continuation can be copied from the earlier occurrence: the rest of a writer's first name in an email address that echoes their byline (\"Write to Patrick Austin at pat|rick.austin@...\"), the commas and digits of a matrix row that duplicates an earlier row, whitespace in an aligned code-golf explanation that re-prints the code, or the second occurrence of a name or equation.","s":[0.032155,0.044022,0.10462,0.05365,0.057194,0.022469,0.069449,0.048147,0.029729,0.032882,0.042528,0.04933,0.025996,0.032057,0.02991]},{"p":"L22N7232+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"In press-release or news-wire prose, fires on the reporting verb right after an organization or official is named as the source (\"X announced\", \"the FDA today announced\", \"CEO Y said,\"), and on the quote mark or punctuation opening or closing an official's statement, i.e., where an institution is about to state or announce something.","s":[0.69674,0.690943,0.453651,0.64683,0.477391,0.030218,0.008926,0.029334,0.074416,0.025543,0.023512,0.027651,0.048831,0.04525,0.034455]},{"p":"L22N7232-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"In news-wire style prose, the comma (or closing quote-comma) that ends a full reported clause right before a source attribution such as \"the newspaper said,\" \"according to police,\" \"authorities said,\" or \"a spokesman told AP,\" i.e. the point where the model expects the attribution (who said it) to come next.","s":[0.505097,0.309004,0.504821,0.356016,0.102919,0.078201,0.062677,0.395197,0.356016,0.015414,0.008053,0.011628,0.004582,0.00718,0.004563]},{"p":"L22N9041+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word just before a name or label is attached to a thing already described, i.e. the slot in a definitional sentence right before the term is given, as in \"X, also known as\", \"he has been called\", \"tingling, or paresthesia\", \"software development (Dev)\", \"This is what we mean by\", \"are often referred to as\", \"the repeated number is called\"; it predicts that a naming word or the new term itself comes next and peaks on the token immediately before \"called\", \"known as\" or the alternative name.","s":[0.144156,0.0857,0.25657,0.156043,0.153402,0.128131,0.050041,0.013567,0.080933,0.002894,0.009161,0.007956,0.007639,0.00354,0.006216]},{"p":"L22N9041-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"In code, fires on the name of an item being pulled out of a module or table and then often renamed or aliased: the identifier right after Python's \"from X import\" or JavaScript's \"const { ... } = require(...)\" or \"import { ... } from\" destructuring (e.g. \"v4\" in \"const { v4: uuidv4 } = require('uuid')\", \"parse\" in \"from dateutil.parser import parse\"), and the column name after a table alias dot in SQL SELECT lists (\"p.name\").","s":[0.543519,0.898789,0.147774,0.145963,0.032681,0.009865,0.01608,0.003083,0.022276,0.00616,0.017486,0.016732,0.012209,0.012766,0.020568]},{"p":"L23N1238+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires at the point where a text begins to repeat an earlier phrase with its elements swapped or mirrored, predicting the reversed order: most strongly on the right-hand side of a Python swap like a[i], a[j] = a[j], a[i] (on the = or the first token after it), and in prose such as \"how many X in Y … likewise how many Y in X\" or \"the sum of the first and second … the sum of the first and third\".","s":[0.61116,0.576387,0.026185,0.081884,0.026665,0.018101,0.008869,0.049354,0.009321,0.049805,0.033686,0.04421,0.025971,0.028951,0.041233]},{"p":"L23N1238-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word just before a reversal phrase, the 'and', 'or', 'but not' or 'not the' that comes right before 'vice versa' or 'other way around', predicting that a converse or reciprocal phrase is about to follow a two-sided comparison (e.g. 'C programmers can move to PHP (and vice versa)', 'tools should fit the business, not the other way around').","s":[0.523401,0.92181,0.443403,0.405747,0.444667,0.080942,0.005319,0.053809,0.001135,0.007173,0.000327,0.000998,0.002634,0.00086,0.001257]},{"p":"L23N1312+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the blank-line break right after a short title-case section heading in an SEO-style explainer or how-to article (e.g. 'Key Elements of Coastal Decor' followed by a blank line), where the next paragraph is about to restate the heading's topic as its subject rather than start with 'it' or 'they'; weaker offshoots fire on the word after a greeting such as 'Hello there' or 'Hi There'.","s":[0.073678,0.053415,0.055322,0.086328,0.070872,0.059479,0.069703,0.054268,0.061609,0.053756,0.080485,0.059921,0.113258,0.079544,0.067452]},{"p":"L23N1312-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the last word of a preposition-led phrase that has been moved to the front of its clause, most often a relative phrase like \"to whose memory\", \"in whose bodies\", \"with which methods\", \"on whose care and kindness\", \"to which of them\", or a fronted negative or limiting phrase like \"In no state in the country\", \"At no time of his life\" or \"Only in a closed Universe\", exactly where the clause's subject or verb (often an inverted auxiliary such as can, did, is, will) is about to follow.","s":[0.25261,0.420496,0.813203,0.63651,0.676667,0.060292,0.134281,0.060498,0.026807,0.030832,0.088541,0.02128,0.061612,0.026918,0.034291]},{"p":"L23N715+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the connective or discourse-marker token at a clause juncture where a sentence pivots into an added, clarifying or emphatic continuation, such as the comma in \"that is,\" or \"above all,\", the word after a semicolon (\"; and\", \"; further,\"), \"in any event\", \"but also\", \"lastly\", \"far from it\" or an interjected \"well,\", mostly in formal argumentative or expository prose.","s":[0.169742,0.247631,0.346577,0.246557,0.359138,0.128633,0.104553,0.141972,0.096233,0.20601,0.121509,0.025781,0.055121,0.12432,0.092081]},{"p":"L23N715-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a pronoun subject (\"you\", \"we\", \"u\", Dutch \"Je\") that starts a new clause right after a clause-opening connective or a fresh line/bullet, most strongly \"Or you can…\" / \"Now we're…\" / a line beginning \"You…\" in instructional or promotional web prose, where an alternative or next step is being introduced.","s":[0.705249,0.48738,0.46603,0.25639,0.252311,0.515529,0.134607,0.147952,0.206147,0.172265,0.038803,0.063614,0.246332,0.087145,0.059842]},{"p":"L23N7445+","l":"UNREL","j":"00","f":0,"e":0,"x":"In Chinese prose or Chinese code comments, fires on the head noun (or time/manner word) that ends a subject phrase and sets up a predicate, typically right before 是, as in 更好的办法是, 这样做的代价是, 这个题的考点是, 但好消息是, 另外一种形态则是, or 不久的将来; it predicts a copula or predicate next rather than 就.","s":[0.588902,0.247191,0.125327,0.513143,0.259884,0.106042,0.035586,0.055313,0.092989,0.103554,0.027464,0.057594,0.038972,0.100473,0.015176],"u":{"0":[0.064208,0.338291,0.148025,0.130468,0.348842],"1":[0.664521,0.698708,0.590605,0.847775,0.314354]}},{"p":"L23N7445-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires inside an \"illustrative example\" construction, on the instance being offered as the example and the surrounding \"for example\" / \"as an example\" / \"is a good example\" words and commas (e.g. \"Nurse practitioners, as an example,\" \"Sort-Object, for example, has to…\", \"hot and cold are antonyms\", Chinese \"举个例子，如上图所示\"), i.e. the spot where a text names a specific case to illustrate a general claim.","s":[0.116765,0.20118,0.529651,0.404126,0.023234,0.013427,0.016001,0.018972,0.01952,0.02352,0.007342,0.015863,0.011368,0.010916,0.02038]},{"p":"L23N8869+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the relative pronoun or verb right after a generic group subject such as \"anyone\", \"anybody\", \"those\", \"those of you\", or \"nobody\" (\"anyone who has...\", \"those who know me\", \"those close to\"), where the clause defines that group by its experience or familiarity with something, so it predicts words like \"knows\" or \"familiar\".","s":[0.665538,0.760673,0.702802,0.655289,0.468972,0.009034,0.010287,0.015806,0.008984,0.004541,0.005819,0.003327,0.018535,0.01051,0.003749]},{"p":"L23N8869-","l":"TWO-REL","j":"11","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word \"I\" right after \"If\"/\"if\" at the start of a first-person conditional or hedge, as in \"If I were you\", \"if I understand correctly\", \"if I'm not mistaken\" or \"If I remember right\", typically in forum or comment replies; it also fires more weakly on knowledge-source phrases like \"based on the following data\" and \"knowledge from\".","s":[0.427565,0.211208,0.296071,0.429591,0.418467,0.047411,0.035684,0.058625,0.014941,0.063846,0.019746,0.037751,0.018345,0.026869,0.055959]},{"p":"L23N8899+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"In explanatory English prose (blogs, how-to articles, reports), this fires on the verb, preposition or adjective just before the writer refers back to an idea already under discussion with \"it\" or \"this\", as in \"you could do this\", \"there are a couple of use cases for this\", \"as challenging as it may be\" or \"this decision did not affect\"; the neuron's output promotes \"it/its\".","s":[0.052809,0.295504,0.276299,0.161239,0.100117,0.068381,0.056383,0.09179,0.048127,0.018249,0.042241,0.018717,0.08021,0.029216,0.010233]},{"p":"L23N8899-","l":"TWO-REL","j":"11","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the possessive pronoun \"its\" or \"their\" when it points back to a previously named non-human thing (an organization, institution, event, document or rule set) and introduces that thing's own members, products or parts, as in \"identify its perpetrators\", \"comply with their provisions\" or \"enriching its originators\"; more weakly it fires on a transitive verb or preposition just before an object slot where \"it\" could refer back.","s":[0.032214,0.353275,0.422382,0.042301,0.051135,0.022917,0.011864,0.109406,0.021163,0.176089,0.026539,0.023459,0.002556,0.115282,0.011559]},{"p":"L23N8995+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires where a speaker is about to point to, or is pointing to, the place or situation they are in right now: on ' here' and on the word just before it in 'down/out/up/stuck/stay/safe here' and 'get out of / get off this ...', and on 'this' before a noun for the current place, time or thing (this island, this place, this time), output pushing 'here/this' over 'there/that'.","s":[0.623334,0.508851,0.694156,0.72274,0.331688,0.319502,0.321513,0.037814,0.055238,0.066066,0.005367,0.013116,0.018007,0.008032,0.005751]},{"p":"L23N8995-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"In narrative dialogue, fires on the word \"that\" (also \"what\", \"do\", \"was\") when a character looks back on an event that just happened, as in \"That was amazing,\" \"Wow, that was intense,\" \"How did you do that!\", \"I shouldn't have done that\"; it marks a backward, distal reference to the just-completed experience.","s":[0.363454,0.516669,0.120462,0.384313,0.577736,0.211286,0.010293,0.019665,0.107992,0.022631,0.019598,0.011623,0.020671,0.021024,0.01685]},{"p":"L24N238+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the token right after a capital letter S that opens a name or code, most strongly on the period after the second initial in initials like \"S.J. Perelman\", \"S.B. Ferrario\", \"S.-M. Park\" (also after \"S.\" alone before a name), and on the digit after S in model or part codes like S-400, S2F, S-10, S4B, S100A2, s390.","s":[1.022778,0.809505,0.703031,0.518687,0.68481,-0.003696,-0.001696,-1.6e-05,-0.001462,0.000179,-0.00251,0.173431,-0.003321,0.010312,-0.00292]},{"p":"L24N238-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a word beginning with S (or on a later piece of that word) when the word just before it also begins with S, an S–S alliterative pair such as \"Size, Share\", \"Statistical Software\", \"Social Sciences\", \"Sequential Skills\", \"Scope and Sequence\", or names like \"Steve Sisney\", \"Sal Soghoian\" and \"Sanchez-Sanchez\", most strongly in citation lists, academic titles and market-report headings.","s":[0.486069,0.334914,0.111168,0.529698,0.327419,0.032311,0.015538,0.057286,0.03671,0.028779,0.012894,0.017271,0.027354,0.02276,0.013906]},{"p":"L24N2529+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"In terse, AI-generated-sounding explainer or SEO web prose (compressed sentences that drop articles, such as \"Sponsorship demonstrates employer support and confirms job necessity\"), the pole fires on mid-sentence tokens inside a predicate, typically a verb or the \"the\"/\"of\" right after a verb, where the object noun phrase is about to start; it builds up over a long passage in that style and is weak in short or natural text.","s":[0.867217,0.464121,1.128772,0.131824,0.518427,0.063631,0.014883,0.040875,0.019642,0.022198,0.046027,0.009218,0.040186,0.029963,0.033314]},{"p":"L24N2529-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on ordinary connective tokens (to, and, is, In, commas, periods, paragraph breaks after headings) inside polished, formulaic, ChatGPT-style expository or promotional English prose, the kind packed with words like crucial, pivotal, foster, enhance, comprehensive, testament and plays a crucial role, rather than on any particular word, and grows stronger the more of the text reads like machine-written SEO or report copy.","s":[0.501495,0.294148,0.035169,0.13785,0.561532,0.07189,0.074643,0.078009,0.160008,0.049327,0.086,0.0419,0.114459,0.056358,0.083564]},{"p":"L24N3091+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the function word right before a verb of doing is due, such as \"to\" in \"how to / able to / failed to / used to\", \"you\" in \"when you\" or \"help you\", or \"how\" in \"knowing how to\", especially in instructional, skills-list or job/how-to prose; it predicts that an action verb (use, set up, configure, work, collaborate) comes next.","s":[0.455339,0.277625,0.297177,0.416386,0.094321,0.064373,0.061837,0.079323,0.062406,0.014305,0.1681,0.022844,0.20669,0.018387,0.151098]},{"p":"L24N3091-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a function-word slot that opens an upcoming verb or adjective phrase in expository prose, strongest on the gerund \"being\" after a preposition or at a sentence or bullet start (\"result in being blacklisted\", \"about being faithful\", \"Being aware of...\"), and also on \"allows\" (\"allows for\"), infinitive \"to\" or \"to be\" (\"the right to be free\", \"To be fair\"), all predicting that a verb, participle or predicate adjective comes next.","s":[0.236671,0.299257,0.02786,0.075519,0.200533,0.015494,0.042546,0.021402,0.040968,0.045835,0.057061,0.040554,0.023913,0.036639,0.052651]},{"p":"L24N3470+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on text that reads as coming from a Chinese source: mainland-Chinese website boilerplate, such as polite notices asking 您 or 大家 to read, understand or not repost, and English written by Chinese firms or officials, such as Chinglish factory product pages, \"To be negotiated\" trade listings, and \"development / economic / core culture\" corporate-government prose. It is strongest on the word \"by\" in an \"editor by CX 2023-06-07\" footer at the end of a Chinese manufacturer's gearbox or compressor SEO article.","s":[0.594983,0.568145,0.838633,0.168283,0.726914,0.067411,0.04757,0.035784,0.022819,0.053636,-0.015435,0.043258,0.0636,0.005032,0.06528]},{"p":"L24N3470-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires inside well-known or formulaic Chinese political and official slogans, usually rendered in English and often in quotation marks: the opening quote mark and the first few words of the slogan fire most. Examples are Deng Xiaoping's “The Middle East has oil, China has rare earth”, Mao-era “put politics in command”, numbered campaigns like “Three Strikes and Two Constructions” or the “three simultaneous” system, and parallel Party-speak lists like “good ecology, production development and affluent life”.","s":[0.761754,0.578399,0.550845,0.253341,0.397887,-2.1e-05,0.023634,0.015442,-8.3e-05,0.007403,0.033913,0.003196,0.020142,0.01477,0.030611]},{"p":"L24N4477+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word or comma just before an elaborating clause is about to start, meaning the point where the text will continue with \"in which\", \"where\", \"wherein\", \"such that\", \"so that\" or a comma plus \"with/as\" clause. Typical spots are \"a situation | in which\", \"a slant-top version | in which\", \"choose indices | so that\" and \"a gender difference was found | , with women reporting…\".","s":[0.18366,0.203367,0.043405,0.037485,0.248303,0.18366,0.094538,0.027028,0.026995,0.02164,0.019544,0.008661,0.027414,0.018765,0.028466]},{"p":"L24N4477-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word or symbol right before a clause that spells out a consequence, definition or restatement, most reliably on the `.then` of a JavaScript promise chain (e.g. `axios.get(url).then(...)`, `mongoose.connect(...).then(() => ...)`), and also on connectors like 'it means', 'i.e.', 'or, equivalently', 'this ensures', or on the colon or '=' in a 'value: what it does' definition.","s":[0.425837,0.752919,0.184418,0.221645,0.157169,0.056902,0.146293,0.192424,0.070293,0.046643,0.032376,0.057103,0.058525,0.070853,0.019721]},{"p":"L24N6548+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the demonstrative determiner \"this\"/\"these\" when it points to a noun that comes right after it (\"this article\", \"this wiki\", \"this group\", \"these topics\", \"these two shafts\"), and most strongly in website boilerplate such as \"Be the first to comment on this article\", \"Listen to this story\", \"Notify me of new activity in this group\" or \"1,234 pages on this wiki\".","s":[0.921384,1.036336,0.399367,0.589452,0.685023,0.123277,0.482663,0.295119,0.234883,0.35777,0.050325,0.049747,0.167358,0.021651,0.143732]},{"p":"L24N6548-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a determiner (\"a\", \"the\", \"that\", \"this\", \"which\") that opens a noun phrase restating or summarizing something just mentioned, especially the appositive after a comma (\"she trained as a nurse, a profession...\"; \"elected president, a position he held...\"; \"the consent of the Council, which consent...\") or the anaphoric \"the result\"/\"that difference\"/\"this transformation\" after a calculation, distinction or change has just been described.","s":[0.143541,0.582969,0.033741,0.046224,0.005501,0.022138,0.009265,0.010042,0.006642,0.003324,0.034197,0.012216,0.00611,0.00704,0.017286]},{"p":"L24N6912+","l":"TWO-REL","j":"11","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires throughout scholarly or encyclopedic reference text about classical religious and Asian traditions, strongest on glossary-style pages defining Sanskrit, Hindu, Jewish or Islamic terms (for example \"Nirvana means something in Hinduism, Sanskrit. If you want to know the exact meaning... check out this page\", with Devanagari and IAST transliterations and dictionary-source lines) and on the digits of lifespan or reign date ranges of premodern Chinese, Japanese or Islamic historical figures (for example \"Enchin (814–891)\").","s":[1.225844,0.530107,0.732834,0.678525,0.58319,0.023526,0.02659,0.008467,0.126343,0.009235,0.003859,0.01352,0.012258,0.004384,0.02758]},{"p":"L24N6912-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"In scholarly English prose about premodern Chinese or Latin/Christian classics, fires on the token that opens or sits inside the title of a classical work or a traditional epithet: the 'De' of a Latin title (De Trinitate, De Viris Illustribus), the 'of' in 'The Classic of Tea' or 'the Sage of Tea', or the bracket or quote mark (《, (“, ‘) that introduces a Chinese title or term.","s":[0.463043,0.122144,0.694766,0.696986,0.416129,0.158455,0.026591,0.308764,0.149256,0.014827,0.019457,0.021017,0.037586,0.021484,0.022322]},{"p":"L24N7241+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the path segment right after the user/repo part of a GitHub URL (the \"/blob\", \"/raw\" or \"/tree\" in github.com/user/repo/blob/master/...) and on \".githubusercontent\" in raw.githubusercontent.com links, i.e. the token that marks a link into a GitHub repository's files, in code comments, READMEs, tutorials or reference lists.","s":[0.855163,0.643082,0.82337,0.677864,0.721966,0.673974,0.005337,0.075969,0.150175,0.024904,0.010547,0.084522,0.009184,0.009571,0.00539]},{"p":"L24N7241-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the formatting tokens that mark a boundary between user comments or post headers on a Slashdot-style discussion page, most strongly the colon in \"(Score:5, Insightful)\", the bullet that opens a reply like \"• Re: ... (Score:3, Funny) by username (12345)\", and the space before a headline's comment count; weaker on the line break or period that ends one comment before the next (Urban Dictionary or Hacker News style).","s":[0.685034,0.80051,0.661253,0.814471,0.827964,0.0139,0.027432,0.015517,0.007995,0.025879,0.015768,0.009723,0.035735,0.016207,0.02953]},{"p":"L25N1779+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"In source code, fires on the digits (or the '=' / space just before them) of a hard-coded large sentinel or extreme magic number, such as 9999999, 99999, 1e9, 10 ** 3, 100000.0, 2147483647 or -Infinity, used as an initial minimum or maximum, an INF constant, an offscreen position, a z-index or a selection range, so the model is predicting that a big number of many 9s or 0s follows.","s":[0.27233,0.780489,0.696711,1.0625,0.334079,0.009375,0.341943,0.25394,0.462762,0.021488,0.013183,0.023977,0.00884,0.010491,0.011307]},{"p":"L25N1779-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a single digit token (often the first digit of a number, as tokenizers split numbers into digits) inside hand-typed example data or literal values in code, test cases, SQL inserts, arrays or placeholder phone numbers, where the number is an arbitrary made-up value, so its size and length cannot be predicted from context; the output promotes magnitude words like million and thousand.","s":[0.369171,0.224081,0.378524,0.28501,0.530252,0.104024,0.022148,0.01625,0.015901,0.050673,0.032126,0.001928,0.034723,0.026128,0.002425]},{"p":"L25N4752+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the boilerplate page furniture of content-farm explainer and how-to sites (wiseGEEK/Conjecture Corporation, eHow/Demand Media, AllTheScience, Zergnet), meaning the 'Written By / Edited By / Copyright 2003-20xx Conjecture Corporation / Print this Article / Free Widgets for your Site/Blog' blocks, 'Promoted By Zergnet', 'Photo Credits', 'Suggest an Article Correction', 'Discuss this Article / Post 3 @user', and 'AllTheScience, in your inbox', strongest at the token right after such a block.","s":[0.674793,0.670538,0.444988,0.078108,0.863161,0.040998,0.03913,0.014026,0.004113,0.002325,0.002768,0.003517,0.003187,0.005309,0.001098]},{"p":"L25N4752-","l":"TWO-REL","j":"11","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires at block boundaries in templated web pages and JavaScript, especially the word \"Related\" opening a footer heading after an article (\"Related posts\", \"Related questions\", \"Explore related boards\", \"Previous article\"), a \"Definition of X\" line opening a new answer on SEO Q&A pages, and the \"){\" / \"=> {\" plus newline that opens an anonymous JavaScript callback body.","s":[0.141309,0.077038,0.103456,0.451183,0.191078,0.048644,0.023433,0.097642,0.062166,0.074802,0.012471,0.007565,0.017812,0.013131,0.017692]},{"p":"L25N4898+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"In a bibliographic reference, fires on the space (or comma/period/semicolon) right after a journal title, where the volume number is about to come next (e.g. \"Journal of Applied Psychology, 88, 518–528\" or \"American Economic Review 65 (May 1975)\"), firing at the token just before the volume digits.","s":[0.807861,0.763554,0.044601,0.008023,0.089659,0.016227,0.014677,0.001434,0.008023,0.022023,0.003663,0.001323,0.00409,0.002843,0.00227]},{"p":"L25N4898-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires inside API reference or docstring descriptions of what a method returns or what an object is, on the words of the boilerplate return-value phrase, such as \"Returns a String that represents the current Object\", \"A 32-bit signed integer that is the hash code for this instance\", \"Create and return a new object\" or \"Return repr(self)\", especially in C#/.NET XML doc comments and Python stub docstrings.","s":[0.978076,0.723079,0.675581,0.51378,0.282894,0.029716,0.092032,0.010426,0.60843,0.02045,0.019388,0.012285,0.017044,0.011416,0.006752]},{"p":"L25N5080+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the digits inside a six-digit Indian PIN (postal) code at the end of an Indian address, e.g. \"Mumbai - 400 022\", \"Pune 411026\", \"Delhi - 110006\", mostly on the middle and later digits after the first few have been written, so the text must contain an Indian city/state address with its PIN code.","s":[0.761144,0.992386,0.362512,0.552149,0.346593,0.394145,0.487812,0.016133,0.042609,0.012452,0.024683,0.037494,0.01358,0.033833,0.022455]},{"p":"L25N5080-","l":"UNREL","j":"00","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires inside a hard-coded definition of the constant pi in a short program, such as \"pi = 3.14\" or \"var pi=3.1415926;\", on the equals sign and the digits of the value and just after it. A second, weaker trigger is the middle of fixed Indian government or institution names, such as \"Ministry of Urban Affairs\", \"Pradhan Mantri Gram Sadak Yojana\" or \"Jawaharlal Nehru University\".","s":[0.832858,0.54799,0.524025,0.397444,0.391188,0.20872,0.020117,0.033652,0.021289,0.183086,0.008494,0.004901,0.003482,0.006713,0.012487],"u":{"0":[0.558833,0.445988,0.376841,0.553116,0.479251],"1":[0.386496,0.496083,0.303603,0.309331,0.469586]}},{"p":"L25N7854+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word just before the fate or outcome of a bill, plan, process or event is stated, i.e. the slot that sets up \"pass / become law / go well / run / be put to a vote\", as in \"the measure failed to [pass]\", \"this never became [law]\", \"should it [pass]\", \"the install went [fine]\", \"took steps to put it [into action]\".","s":[0.531799,0.500889,0.436796,0.422626,0.57133,0.081908,0.117058,0.087025,0.018887,0.010987,0.008873,0.066708,0.03702,0.01644,0.034112]},{"p":"L25N7854-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the connective word in the middle of a stock, highly predictable phrase in generic expository or corporate web prose, such as the 'of' in 'always aware of any changes', the 'the' in 'aims to shorten the development life cycle' or 'reduce the time and effort', the 'to' in 'so as to feel confident', or the space before the number in 'more than 30 years of experience', at points where the next words are almost certain.","s":[0.09979,0.41604,0.046512,0.07542,0.312933,0.043591,0.047154,0.056543,0.034384,0.04628,0.016148,0.045272,0.029672,0.102645,0.01888]},{"p":"L25N9367+","l":"TWO-REL","j":"11","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the structural punctuation or spacing inside rigidly templated technical text, where the next token is a predictable number or code body: the \"):\" between issue and page numbers and the space before a year in journal citations (\"2014;3(2):174-92\", \"Epub 2012 Feb\", \"Published: 3 February 2014\", \"DOI: 10.1186\"), the special space in \"n = 29\" or \"p = 0.001\" in biomedical abstracts, and the \" {\" plus newline that opens a function body in JavaScript.","s":[0.08335,0.006962,0.386533,0.00836,0.023794,0.009905,0.007189,0.008741,0.003614,0.005433,0.008193,0.005907,0.005748,0.009942,0.009758]},{"p":"L25N9367-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Topic tracker for late-2010s Trump-era current affairs: it fires on ordinary tokens (dates, 'according', '$', 'with', quote marks, the name itself) inside news or commentary about Donald Trump, his administration, his US–China trade war and tariffs, or Brexit, pushing the model toward saying 'Trump'/'Brexit' next.","s":[0.832704,0.820754,0.482448,0.593084,0.80296,0.025339,0.009841,0.00692,0.08698,0.093879,0.010498,0.003592,0.007409,0.006756,0.006473]},{"p":"L25N9613+","l":"TWO-REL","j":"11","f":0,"e":0,"x":"In medical or health writing, fires on a count or ordinal word that opens a time interval or treatment stage, such as 'two' in 'fully vaccinated two weeks after the last dose', 'three' in 'the three months before pregnancy', 'first' in 'first-line therapy', 'primary' in 'primary prevention' or 'within' in 'within 6 weeks of vaccination'.","s":[1.24927,0.781292,0.394148,0.495861,0.39091,0.014033,0.122942,0.043885,0.318938,0.096839,0.02051,0.020196,0.02025,0.006823,0.19341]},{"p":"L25N9613-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on ordinary mid-sentence tokens (function words, commas, spaces before numbers, word pieces) throughout consumer health and medical-treatment web prose, strongest in fertility/IVF and reproductive or urological medicine (infertility causes, sperm retrieval, treatment cost and success rates, erectile dysfunction); it tracks the running medical topic, not any particular word.","s":[0.530968,0.879955,0.8,0.496697,0.381411,0.093937,0.069421,0.295466,0.248398,0.024524,0.01678,0.010564,0.025569,0.070479,0.008758]},{"p":"L26N3166+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on function words (to, the, for, and, commas) inside well-known scriptural or fixed proclamations about bringing something to a broad group of people, especially Bible verses like \"preach good news to the poor\", \"proclaim freedom for the prisoners\", or \"proclaimed to them\", where the next words name who receives the message or benefit (its output suppresses \"anyone/anybody\"), and it fires most strongly when the passage is quoted verbatim.","s":[0.704602,0.768564,0.623981,1.134868,0.940101,0.021663,0.427294,0.022748,0.033545,0.044007,0.027644,0.071933,0.037625,0.036983,0.026383]},{"p":"L26N3166-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word \"anyone\"/\"anybody\" (or \"everyone\", the \"-body\" of \"Anybody\", or the comma/\"and\"/\"which means\" just before it) when it is the subject of an open-access claim that something is available to all with no restriction, as in \"open-source, so anyone can download it\", \"anyone can ask a question\", \"anyone can learn to sketch\".","s":[0.654751,0.607551,0.667149,0.554164,0.557604,0.507296,0.292552,0.46375,0.23735,0.191971,0.00765,0.008324,0.016206,0.006612,-0.000118]},{"p":"L26N3713+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires throughout technical theoretical-physics and differential-geometry text about gravity, spacetime curvature, black holes, higher-spin/AdS holography and higher dimensions, landing on whatever token is current (citation digits, equation pieces, \" spin\" in \"higher spin\", \"AdS\", \"Bekenstein\") rather than on one particular word.","s":[0.857907,0.437367,1.473121,0.442721,0.704361,0.30176,0.150704,0.427048,0.079495,0.088793,0.026758,0.030846,0.029242,0.033294,0.034489]},{"p":"L26N3713-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"In dense research-level math, statistics or physics prose, this pole fires on the modifier or name fragment that opens a fixed multi-word technical term, at the point where the term's head noun is predictable next (e.g. 'confidence' before 'intervals', 'invariant' before 'measure/sets', 'eigen' before 'value', 'coherence' before 'length', 'rotation' before 'number', 'Lag|range' before 'multipliers', 'Heega|ard' before 'Floer').","s":[0.833577,0.749471,0.463526,0.347956,0.350623,0.015985,0.210043,0.023785,0.011226,0.053784,0.020809,0.013444,0.00899,0.024456,0.029882]},{"p":"L26N4718+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a head noun (often a quantity or set word like number, amount, height, size, range, value, or 'all') right after 'the maximum / the total / the exact / the' when the sentence is about to restrict it with a following relative clause of possibility or requirement, e.g. 'the maximum amount of data that can be received', 'the exact variables that may be referenced', 'all the features that can be added'.","s":[0.344112,0.045741,0.154712,0.196157,0.417406,0.093547,0.010759,0.027378,0.0101,0.015577,0.0229,0.013314,0.010617,0.013582,0.018103]},{"p":"L26N4718-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a plural or mass noun that heads a definite noun phrase (usually after \"the\", \"all\", \"all of the\" or \"any of the\") at the point where a restrictive postmodifier is expected next, as in \"the information you provide\", \"all changes made\", \"the email addresses listed\", \"the recommendations set forth\" or \"any of the properties on…\"; this is common in boilerplate, legal notices and code comments.","s":[0.380886,0.137326,0.216852,0.449606,0.632698,0.417697,0.06518,0.103534,0.496311,0.050308,0.018505,0.039525,0.023878,0.021622,0.064683]},{"p":"L26N5319+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires inside English text that is recognizably Russian in origin, most strongly in the stock machine-translated Russian spam comments (\"I think, that you are mistaken. Write to me in PM, we will communicate.\", \"Many thanks for the help in this question, now I will not commit such error.\", \"In it something is.\") and in Russian-specific formulaic references (GOST 305-82 standards, Federal Act No. 114-FZ, Moscow +7 (495) phone numbers, Soviet Phys. JETP, United Russia, autonomous okrug), lighting up on the predictable middle tokens of these set phrases.","s":[0.692697,0.466933,0.757441,0.761671,0.640036,0.009605,0.015414,0.036465,0.028482,0.009736,0.005316,0.008031,0.016718,0.003915,0.011749]},{"p":"L26N5319-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires throughout English-language academic text translated from or about Russian/post-Soviet scholarship (Russian author names with initials like 'Ivanov I.I.', journals such as 'Bulletin of the ... University', '(In Russian)' tags, elibrary.ru or DOI links, pedagogy abstracts), peaking on the citation punctuation and page numbers ('no. 3, pp. 708-716') and on function words like 'of'/'the' in translated-sounding phrases ('formation of the speech culture of schoolchildren').","s":[1.049955,1.050104,0.823253,1.026304,1.024384,0.289553,0.015144,0.021543,0.000955,0.148947,0.010031,0.009672,0.005878,0.008187,0.009108]},{"p":"L26N634+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"In help/FAQ, troubleshooting or rules text, fires on the subject or linking word of a conditional or contrast clause (\"If you…\", \"…but\", \"when it…\", \"if the applicant…\") just before a failure or negation follows, as in \"If you didn't receive the email\", \"I've registered but I can't post\" or \"if a thread cannot acquire the lock\".","s":[0.492597,0.859908,0.34051,0.288021,0.49813,0.40192,0.543255,0.2833,0.160378,0.464704,0.0496,0.065156,0.04158,0.055227,0.044125]},{"p":"L26N634-","l":"UNK","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on ordinary function words and verbs in the middle of a clause, such as 'be', 'may', 'will', 'which', 'is', 'of', 'more' and 'not', inside plain explanatory English that describes how a technical system behaves or what its rules are (code docstrings and comments, software docs, error messages, rule explanations); it does not fire on topic nouns, and only strongly when the text is dense technical or rule-describing prose.","s":[0.427334,0.150181,0.324281,0.072239,0.422633,0.034758,0.023767,0.011421,0.029646,0.009971,0.018847,0.032957,0.014754,0.063851,0.040237]},{"p":"L26N8119+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"In Chinese text (subtitles, code comments, web pages), this fires on the first character of a common two-character function word or adverb, often on a byte fragment of that character, at the point where the second character must be predicted: 怎(么/样), 可(以/能), 已(经), 或(者), 全(部), 一(开始/样), 稍(后).","s":[0.08969,0.011101,0.073856,0.04916,0.340344,0.007753,0.008012,0.003516,0.096813,0.03918,0.011217,0.012088,0.018897,0.010327,0.011398]},{"p":"L26N8119-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"In Chinese text (often bilingual subtitles, code comments or SQL with Chinese strings), the pole fires on a lone Chinese character token that opens a two-character compound word whose second character must follow, such as 外 in 外遇, 外网 or 外貌, 同 in 同性恋 or 同名, 常 in 常量 or 常用, 相 in 相比 or 相依为命, and 预 in 预谋.","s":[0.70335,0.404249,0.867104,0.441661,0.931524,0.007147,0.01564,0.126233,0.013208,0.035954,0.009559,0.025661,0.020232,0.01232,0.035496]},{"p":"L26N821+","l":"TWO-REL","j":"11","f":0,"e":0,"x":"In source code, fires on the token that ends a line in the middle of an unfinished statement, usually a comma, operator, open string or backslash just before a line break, where a function call's arguments or a condition continue on the next, aligned-indented line (e.g. 'foo(a,⏎      b)'). A weaker offshoot fires on digits or decimal points in the middle of numbers in dense numeric tables.","s":[0.042489,0.006393,0.016738,0.032029,0.010785,0.005055,0.004882,0.004313,0.005847,0.006245,-0.000137,0.009718,0.013028,0.04389,0.002956]},{"p":"L26N821-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires deep inside long runs of random-looking Base64 or encoded binary text (gibberish letter-digit strings with + and /), most strongly on isolated single-character tokens such as 1, V or X in the middle of the string, rather than on ordinary prose.","s":[0.12557,0.375367,0.137308,0.25356,0.159767,0.086239,0.065553,0.0264,0.040237,0.091449,0.187087,0.021882,0.092872,0.15897,0.238784]},{"p":"L26N8581+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the function word (usually \"to\", \"for\", \"of\", \"in\", \"the\", \"a\") that comes right before a word like \"another\", \"other(s)\", \"else\", \"different\", \"new\" or \"second\", when the sentence contrasts one thing with an alternative one (from one X to another, some... for others, sent to someone else); it predicts that an \"other/another\"-type word comes next.","s":[0.510152,0.031057,0.267332,0.10245,0.112088,0.042836,0.031057,0.027899,0.030856,0.112088,0.027601,0.033105,0.035587,0.016533,0.026987]},{"p":"L26N8581-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word just before an expected comparative or relative-rank adjective, i.e. at the point where the model predicts the next word will be something like 'smaller', 'higher', 'later', 'more recent' or 'other'. Typical spots are 'broken down into [smaller parts]', 'compatible with [more recent versions]', 'forwarded to the next [higher level]', 'any [later] version', 'groups in [smaller dimensions]', and 'differently from [other younger subjects]'.","s":[0.268405,0.037227,0.102501,0.412703,0.006313,0.269758,0.038353,0.014098,0.38202,0.006313,0.005775,0.057531,0.007153,0.001242,0.0097]},{"p":"L27N2924+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word just before a degree-of-completeness word would come next, i.e. where the model predicts \"complete/completely/full\" or a large percentage, especially in medical or ecological outcome prose about a cure, response, recovery, elimination, remission or decline (e.g. \"make a ___ recovery\", \"tumors were ___ eliminated\", \"declines of up to ___ 90%\", \"rather than ___ curing\").","s":[0.825969,0.531994,0.434355,0.70026,0.650819,0.009947,0.007197,0.013194,0.006107,-0.000529,0.005435,0.042132,0.004723,0.000952,0.00936]},{"p":"L27N2924-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on tokens inside text about the Angular / AngularJS web framework, most strongly on the token right after the name \"Angular\" (a version-number space as in \"Angular 2\" or \"Angular 8\", \"by\" in \"developed by Google\", \"@angular/\" package paths, \"Angular CLI\" or \"Angular Material\") and on AngularJS dependency-injection code such as '$http', '$timeout' or function($scope).","s":[0.708875,0.559513,0.8806,0.617144,1.044741,0.11735,0.009645,0.193184,0.01507,0.160403,0.016053,0.008743,0.009643,0.011631,0.010132]},{"p":"L27N6736+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word piece \"Cart\" when it begins a person's surname that is split into pieces (Henri Cartier-Bresson, Alexander Cartwright, Deborah Cartmell, Katrin Cartlidge, an all-caps CARTER), especially after a first name or in an author citation; ordinary words like shopping cart, cartoon or cartridge should not fire it.","s":[0.936332,0.799827,0.454813,0.73897,0.500567,0.014587,0.021666,0.020223,0.083995,0.029266,0.028679,0.01079,0.053803,0.040119,0.052173]},{"p":"L27N6736-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a verb of finding out or judging (see, determine, decide, figure out, find out, understand, predict, tell) at the point just before it introduces an embedded wh-question, as in \"test to see which works best\", \"determine which ones are legitimate\", \"see who could finish first\"; the neuron suppresses \"which/who\" question words, so it marks a slot where one is about to follow.","s":[0.162536,0.099458,0.007423,0.020821,0.129563,0.010604,0.004307,0.009712,0.014024,0.011609,0.011194,0.006929,0.00431,0.006429,0.005329]},{"p":"L27N6887+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the capitalized token \"Han\" (with or without a leading space) when it begins a proper name, most often as the first piece of a longer name like Hanover, Hanford, Hanwha, Haneda, Hanalei, Hanjiang, Hanworth, or as \"Han River\"; ordinary words that merely contain the letters do not fire it.","s":[0.914315,0.068187,0.885448,0.917174,0.878015,0.002149,0.002127,0.022084,0.002168,0.095199,0.00777,0.014147,0.007208,0.003399,0.005632]},{"p":"L27N6887-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the capitalized word-start piece \"Ra\" (or \" Ra\") when it opens a longer proper name split into pieces, mostly personal first names like Raquel, Raoul, Raheel, Raúl, Raed, Raizy, Raaga, Raíces or Raal, and not on whole-word tokens or on common words like \"Rain\" or \"Radio\".","s":[0.664944,0.657757,0.837203,0.606425,0.686867,0.011961,0.013355,0.010868,0.015743,0.024483,0.01588,0.01423,0.021918,0.009678,0.014957]},{"p":"L27N7674+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word just before a phrase of non-relation or non-effect is completed, predicting words like \"unaffected\", \"irrelevant\", \"no relation\", \"nothing to do with\", \"paid no heed/attention\", \"impervious\", \"indifferent\" (e.g. on \"has\" in \"has nothing to do with\", \"bear\" in \"bear little relation to\", \"pay\" in \"pay no attention\", \"completely\" in \"left completely unaffected\").","s":[0.066543,0.122602,0.208409,0.173323,0.404984,0.017392,0.086323,0.01235,0.028587,0.231736,0.010683,0.010064,0.005526,0.011997,0.006735]},{"p":"L27N7674-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the negation word (\"not\", \"no\", \"n't\") in a statement that one thing is independent of, irrelevant to, or unconnected with another, at the point just before the word that completes the idea of independence, such as \"the constant C does not depend on h\", \"the order does not matter\", \"the variables are not correlated\", \"there was no significant relationship\", \"has disclosed no relevant affiliations\" or \"Disclosure: No positions\"; ordinary negations of actions do not fire it.","s":[0.163885,0.289043,0.261117,0.248606,0.169916,0.303851,0.020311,0.333364,0.348509,0.08701,0.019118,0.012983,0.157201,0.050849,0.025604]},{"p":"L27N7773+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on ordinary tokens inside source code, strongest in C#/Java/JavaScript-style class and method code: parameter lists (types like string, int or double and the commas between parameters), member-access calls such as .Remove, .Split or .Find, and file-extension pieces like .py, .h or .xaml. It stays quiet on English prose, including prose that talks about programming.","s":[0.020998,0.007615,0.041412,0.02108,0.043598,0.022275,0.023065,0.020895,0.024849,0.028214,0.019081,0.02417,0.014212,0.022904,0.027216]},{"p":"L27N7773-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a verb of affection or esteem (loves, loved, appreciates, respects, accepts, admires) or on the object pronoun right after it, when a person or group is the subject feeling warmly toward someone or something, typically in a relative clause like \"whom she loves\", \"a company that appreciates you\", or \"she loves him\".","s":[0.38745,0.932556,0.189494,0.5769,0.280794,0.043809,0.008653,0.009128,0.016117,0.032145,0.011385,0.016077,0.01063,0.010782,0.01777]},{"p":"L27N843+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"In software/programming text, fires on the preposition or article right after a verb of saving or storing data (\"save X to\", \"store it in\", \"insert into\", \"in the\", \"to a\"), the spot where the next word is predicted to be \"database\", \"db\", \"SQL\", a named database such as MySQL or MongoDB, or another storage target.","s":[0.703759,0.369998,0.610359,0.572283,0.552399,0.212541,0.30017,0.107089,0.292271,0.168689,0.002323,0.001855,0.002993,0.010335,0.028338]},{"p":"L27N843-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires inside SQL database dump files (phpMyAdmin/mysqldump/pg_dump style), most strongly on the first dot of the version number in a header like \"-- phpMyAdmin SQL Dump -- version 4.5.1\", and also on tokens within CREATE TABLE column definitions and INSERT INTO ... VALUES rows; it signals \"this is a database/SQL file\" and promotes words like database, SQL, db.","s":[0.867941,0.384739,0.275109,0.252841,0.506452,0.023548,0.014935,0.013041,0.025168,0.018494,0.012297,0.008634,0.012947,0.019094,0.009]},{"p":"L27N8528+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires at points where the text is reciting or about to recite a famous, memorized passage or fact. The next tokens are recalled near-verbatim, for example the colon in a Bible reference like \"Jeremiah 29:11\" or \"Deuteronomy 28:1\", the opening quote mark of Martin Luther King's “I Have a Dream”, the digits in the year of a famous work such as the Communist Manifesto (1848) or King's 1963 speech, or a word inside a well-known quotation, speech or title.","s":[0.486931,0.171747,0.429342,0.664058,0.184183,0.04832,0.085144,0.106379,0.034052,0.013281,0.024903,0.03079,0.04019,0.035372,0.037131]},{"p":"L27N8528-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word just before a mention of deeper, symbolic or hidden meaning is expected, such as \"carry\", \"holds\", \"convey\", \"impart\", \"hidden\", \"symbolic\", \"spiritual\" or \"as\" in phrases like \"carry profound meanings\", \"hidden meanings\", \"symbolic message\" or \"wrote it as an allegory\", when a work of art, story, object or custom is said to stand for something beyond its literal content.","s":[0.967417,0.730138,0.789265,0.352838,0.488445,0.612804,0.433211,0.026982,0.164705,0.512789,0.000488,0.004968,0.006779,0.004479,-0.000995]},{"p":"L27N9359+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the colon/equals sign, space or digits of a number that gives the value of a rate or speed that was just named (e.g. \"frameRate: 10\", \"Birth rate: 27.75 births\", \"a speed of 2 m/s\", \"self-discharge rate: <3%\", \"growth coefficient K= 0.41\"), suppressing a repeat of the word \"rate\"; plain numbers after non-rate labels like length or price should not fire it.","s":[0.225923,0.496483,0.078991,0.42354,0.900347,0.002475,0.014129,0.010432,0.00698,0.01487,0.00598,0.006489,0.010103,0.017492,0.013384]},{"p":"L27N9359-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word \"at\" (and the following \"a\", \"this\" or \"the same\") right after a verb of change or growth, such as growing, increasing, falling, disappearing or advancing, where the phrase is about to continue \"at a rapid rate/pace\", i.e. just before the word \"rate\" or \"pace\" is predicted.","s":[0.55493,0.611992,0.651832,0.47515,0.4776,0.230929,0.261649,0.004694,0.09685,0.037067,0.00247,0.00131,0.002242,0.002603,0.000183]},{"p":"L27N9422+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word-internal piece \"id\", \"rid\", \"ird\" or \"ir\" that comes right after an initial F (sometimes P) inside a rarer proper name split into pieces, such as Far|id|abad, Far|id|pur, Far|id|kot, F|rid|a Kahlo, F|ird|aus or Firdous Square, FR|ID|AY in all caps, P|ir|bright or P|ir|atical; common whole words like Friday or Fred do not fire it.","s":[0.727635,0.827704,0.675652,0.756325,0.60356,0.010583,0.036803,0.019004,0.011927,0.023478,0.002836,0.043473,0.012515,0.005127,0.012962]},{"p":"L27N9422-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the token \"Franc\" (any case: Franc, franc, FRANC, also \"anc\" in fr-anc) when it is the first piece of a longer word continuing with -is/-isco/-iscan/-ine/-ia/-ese/-ioni, e.g. the names Francis, Francisco, Franciscan, Francine, San Francisco, predicting the continuation (it suppresses \"isc\"/\"is\" output); the word \"France\" or \"French\" or unrelated words should not fire.","s":[0.004912,0.004838,0.862388,0.043217,0.932913,0.00449,0.004608,0.003094,0.072288,0.054146,0.004666,0.003956,0.007711,0.003638,0.004682]},{"p":"L28N1706+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word just before a predicted 'circle' or 'wrap around': most strongly on 'full' (and 'come') in the idiom 'come full circle', and also on cyclic wrap-around contexts such as numbers that wrap around a modulus or code indexing with (i + 1) % n.","s":[0.742879,0.888158,0.565302,0.629051,0.618217,0.001902,0.031003,0.410655,0.378014,0.010628,0.003808,0.005813,0.003954,0.004213,0.005164]},{"p":"L28N1706-","l":"TWO-REL","j":"11","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on a digit inside a multi-digit or decimal number (often the first digit after a decimal point or a middle digit) in dense numeric data such as SQL spawn rows with orientation angles, catalogue numbers, or angle and degree values in code and formulas; it also fires on function words like \"it\", \"to\", \"a\", \"on\" in prose about circles, rotation or going around in a circle (passing it to the left, inscribed angles).","s":[0.155934,0.051315,0.535616,0.420615,0.342484,0.011149,0.025301,0.009544,0.095595,0.012158,0.0056,0.027055,0.019178,0.010976,0.005765]},{"p":"L28N3957+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires mid-phrase in technical or academic prose (physics, cosmology, climate, economics papers and abstracts) on connective or modifier tokens such as \"and\", \"of\", \"by\", \"to\", \"the\", \"negative\", \"specific\", or on word pieces inside a technical term, where the next word is a technical noun like \"capital\", \"terms\", \"warming\" or \"effect\".","s":[0.771309,0.493163,0.732331,0.274357,0.464193,0.009739,0.006478,0.009806,0.011411,0.008651,0.016698,0.008457,0.021304,0.020927,0.006287]},{"p":"L28N3957-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on function words and number pieces (\"of\" in \"theory of\", \"a\", \"around\", the space before a digit, the first digits of a big power of ten) inside theoretical-physics and cosmology prose, where the next word is likely \"everything\", \"the universe\", or a large or small number such as 10^26 or 10^-33; it is a topic-conditioned slot-filler that is strongest in Big Bang, unified-theory and quantum-gravity passages.","s":[0.568428,0.773059,1.038386,0.892745,1.01621,0.007679,0.010541,0.012602,0.008112,0.017111,0.00713,0.037893,0.016749,0.011625,0.005616]},{"p":"L28N4076+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires at the structural boundary between a web page's main body and its trailing metadata or comments: the 'rise to the top' that ends the Stack Exchange sign-up boilerplate, the break after Instructables contest lists, and the separator before a comment author's name or a closed-topic period.","s":[0.344424,0.551598,0.196027,0.027232,0.135491,0.047653,0.020938,0.006853,0.032641,0.022297,-0.004525,0.049891,0.013445,0.047084,0.003662]},{"p":"L28N4076-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires (negative pole) on a mid-word piece inside Finnish words, especially the piece \"öh\" in \"työhön\" in the Freelancer.com Finnish boilerplate \"N freelanceria on tarjonnut keskimäärin $X tähän työhön\", and similarly on pieces like oh, ah, ih, ak, ark or kah in other Finnish words; the model is mid-word in Finnish text and the next piece is a continuation.","s":[0.893569,0.915724,0.88537,0.935243,0.513942,0.042146,0.465516,0.021195,0.285557,0.562507,0.02129,0.035925,0.008312,0.05211,0.026437]},{"p":"L28N4229+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires inside JavaScript library and web-developer text (Prototype.js/Scriptaculous code, AlloyUI/YUI release notes, JS-library link lists), on mid-token pieces such as 'new' before Effect, '.empty' in Prototype.emptyFunction, 'ui' in aui/yui, version-number digits or dots, and indentation or comma tokens, where the next token continues a known framework identifier or name.","s":[0.788543,0.658198,1.079339,0.775869,0.844494,0.026711,0.023918,0.092159,0.021564,0.01009,0.027163,0.020462,0.019423,0.023864,0.015879]},{"p":"L28N4229-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"In jQuery/JavaScript code, it fires on the closing quote-plus-parenthesis token (\").\" or \"').\") that ends a jQuery selector call such as $(\".btn\") and is about to be followed by a chained method like .on, .click, .prop or .addClass.","s":[0.749384,0.522424,0.713071,0.72863,0.634921,0.464082,0.144016,0.025476,0.006573,0.415525,0.008123,0.005962,0.005647,0.005822,0.008362]},{"p":"L28N5262+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on the word \"failed\" (and \"failing\", \"unable\", \"couldn't\", \"could not\", \"barely\", \"come close\") or the \"to\" right after it, in news, sports and market reports where the next words are a verb for something that did not happen, such as \"to win\", \"to hold\", or \"to move higher\".","s":[0.69234,1.107066,0.080981,0.626995,0.995229,0.150164,0.251582,0.007642,0.28664,0.487685,0.01517,0.014763,0.012057,0.009902,0.009185]},{"p":"L28N5262-","l":"TWO-REL","j":"11","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on the token that closes a numeric figure or sits right after one in structured, data-heavy text: the last digit of a multi-digit number (a year, price, score or version), the unit or word right after a stat (\"yards\", \"points\", \"record\"), or a comma or \"which\" after a number. It also fires on \"NULL\" in SQL \"NOT NULL\" column definitions.","s":[0.686221,0.405077,0.555178,0.088889,0.00658,0.008607,0.018345,0.432359,0.013168,0.015249,0.015327,0.017006,0.029843,0.011704,0.016062]},{"p":"L28N5632+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on the space, opening parenthesis, or first digit that comes right before a number giving a gene, chromosome or DNA count (e.g. \"about 20,000 genes\", \"over 3 billion letters\", \"humans (22,389)\"), where the next token should be the leading digit of a quantity in genetics text.","s":[0.878695,0.80431,0.699903,0.363616,0.713007,0.186003,0.014837,0.021029,0.003088,0.024853,0.027206,0.017883,0.021471,0.02007,0.011154]},{"p":"L28N5632-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on the prefix \"sub\" (or \"main\"/\"主\") when it begins a compound whose second half is still to come, as in sub|plot, sub-group, sub-basement, sub|committee, sub|routine, sub|account, or main hall/house/department; it does not fire on the whole word or on other word pieces.","s":[0.559091,0.45283,0.824798,0.472938,0.750275,0.443259,0.333899,0.403002,0.079226,0.04745,0.011861,0.005353,0.007022,0.010681,0.012264]},{"p":"L29N34+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"In code or other structured text (Python, C#, JavaScript, SQL, LaTeX), it fires on a mid-identifier or mid-number piece such as the 'MA' in avgMAE, the 'R' in maxDeltaR, the 'Vis' in setMultiVisble or the '0' in a repeated '20', when the name has already appeared earlier and the next piece can simply be copied.","s":[0.492963,0.646685,0.731111,0.261203,0.2428,0.144305,0.142739,0.201313,0.214772,0.183209,0.230474,0.16675,0.080956,0.094377,0.06062]},{"p":"L29N34-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires negatively on a token in the middle of a heavily repeated passage (copy-pasted code, repeated Russian comment blocks, spam text), where the token is a predictable continuation of text that has already appeared many times in the context, most often inside code.","s":[0.064224,0.098216,0.036344,0.071753,0.16857,0.018312,0.003326,0.011049,0.086115,0.060457,0.008704,0.017006,0.000613,0.018487,0.012803]},{"p":"L29N4032+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on the subject or connector word (a pronoun, group noun, organization name piece, 'in', 'also', 'generally') right before a verb of support or favor is expected, as in 'ATIS [supports]', '70% of Americans [are in favor]', 'I [strongly support]', 'they [would back]'.","s":[0.623273,0.442655,0.331343,0.38996,0.534931,0.30419,0.444526,0.328436,0.387251,0.010457,0.011343,0.012114,0.012179,0.01205,0.009293]},{"p":"L29N4032-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires strongly negative inside legal citations and statute references, mainly Illinois statute and case citations in legal blogs, motions and opinions: on the space, slash, period or digit pieces right after a code abbreviation such as ILCS, ch., or section. Weaker cases are code API names and a word-initial piece like \"Ar\" or \"El\".","s":[0.754612,0.498081,0.998453,0.476475,0.339997,0.278074,0.119248,0.099346,0.009349,0.102365,-0.000213,0.009027,0.004398,0.014304,0.023797]},{"p":"L29N4123+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on the noun or word right before a possessive-own phrase, in the idiom 'in a class/category by itself', 'took on a life of its own', 'a mind of her own', 'all his own', where the next word is almost certainly 'own', 'itself' or 'its own'.","s":[0.773505,0.77081,0.680297,0.81306,0.95363,0.000543,0.028622,0.007538,0.00126,0.005926,0.003205,0.002334,0.001086,0.000356,8e-06]},{"p":"L29N4123-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on the token just before a reflexive or self-reliance ending (themselves, himself, yourself, on their own, alone, by myself, \"it alone\"), where the model predicts the next word will be \"own\" or a -self pronoun, as in \"decide for [himself]\", \"work out for [themselves]\", \"on [his] own\", \"go it [alone]\".","s":[0.657643,0.699994,0.127448,0.334168,0.685343,0.041181,0.045133,0.131387,0.03144,0.035727,0.027764,0.025127,0.051976,0.032484,0.047949]},{"p":"L29N458+","l":"UNK","j":"","f":2,"e":1,"x":""},{"p":"L29N458-","l":"TWO-REL","j":"11","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on a token where the next token is very likely 'music', a code or LaTeX continuation like 'Abs', 'mathbf' or a repeated identifier, or a repeated phrase. It mostly fires inside code, LaTeX or math, and also in the text just before the word 'music'. The pattern is a spot where the model is confident the next piece repeats something it just saw, so it works as a copy or continuation predictor. It is not tied to one token string.","s":[0.04857,0.298949,0.021978,0.02539,0.055995,0.04857,0.015896,0.031412,0.005131,0.007366,0.024708,0.007829,0.043748,0.036447,0.009255]},{"p":"L29N6369+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on tokens inside graphics-programming API text (Direct3D 12 docs, Metal/MetalPetal docs, OptiX shader code, DirectX forum posts), where the next token is likely to be a graphics API identifier or code piece; it hits identifier pieces, '::', and whitespace inside signatures. Plain prose outside graphics APIs does not trigger it.","s":[0.953043,0.889188,0.493242,0.404395,0.965174,0.005602,0.004697,0.008519,0.008436,0.014908,0.007177,0.007562,0.008555,0.004653,0.006054]},{"p":"L29N6369-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on punctuation, whitespace and indentation tokens inside GLSL/OpenGL shader code (and gl_ identifiers or the .glsl extension), at points where the next token is likely to be a gl_ builtin (gl_FragColor, gl_Position, gl_TexCoord) or a shader keyword, and it pushes down fragment/gl-style continuations.","s":[0.671507,0.763668,0.8071,0.856305,0.895796,0.12629,0.137444,0.003853,0.009145,0.01119,0.001276,0.000101,0.002495,-0.000965,0.003244]},{"p":"L29N8202+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on the plural or mass noun (or its verb or word piece) that is the subject of a clause saying something is scattered or abundant everywhere, such as 'broken glass and food items all over the floor', 'cigarette butts everywhere', 'X abound' or 'are ubiquitous', so the next word is likely 'everywhere', 'all over' or 'abound'.","s":[0.908802,0.717693,0.459921,0.55894,0.840164,0.030589,0.03791,0.01149,0.046012,0.033523,0.00502,0.004268,0.007793,0.008391,0.003208]},{"p":"L29N8202-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on the token right before an expected \"everywhere/everyday/all over\"-style completion, mostly a quantifier or locative like \"around\", \"almost\", \"nearly\", \"more\" or \"all\" in a sentence about something being widespread, common, or occurring all over the place, with weaker firing on other mid-phrase tokens in web boilerplate.","s":[0.156369,0.289518,0.165378,0.001982,0.287294,0.036943,0.042842,0.028834,0.018391,0.050122,0.037839,0.020343,0.038618,0.021008,0.032527]},{"p":"L29N9473+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on the token just before a motion verb of travel or departure (go, head, set out, roll, drive, pull away, climb, ride) is expected, typically a subject pronoun or noun (we, they, you, I, the caravan) or a connector (by, and) in narrative or travel prose about people moving along a journey.","s":[0.656848,0.28791,0.417225,0.734703,0.31824,0.13928,0.022001,0.028111,0.017975,0.041417,0.021519,0.02731,0.011372,0.017594,0.019595]},{"p":"L29N9473-","l":"TWO-REL","j":"11","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on a motion or direction word right after a subject that is about to take a destination, such as \"went\", \"head\", \"turn\", \"go\" or \"proceed\", or the word \"straight\" or \"directly\" after them, where the next word is likely \"to\", \"for\" or \"toward\"; it also fires on a turtle pen object right before a movement call such as goto or setpos.","s":[0.179951,0.021129,0.431397,0.203384,0.490926,0.114817,0.006653,0.2061,0.003424,0.0949,0.00171,0.002465,0.004378,0.014844,-0.000756]},{"p":"L29N9527+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on the word \"result\" or \"resulting/resulted\" (and a few odd nouns like \"components\", \"grin\", \"smile\") when it comes right before \"in\" or \"within\" to name a consequence, as in \"which would result in…\", \"X resulted in…\", \"leading to a result in\".","s":[0.201034,0.194497,0.35642,0.008192,0.147734,0.022374,0.008396,0.015079,0.005328,0.025369,0.032193,0.0287,0.016591,0.086088,0.025795]},{"p":"L29N9527-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"The noun right after a verb or phrase of investing something in someone, as in \"put/placed their [faith/trust/confidence] in ...\" or \"trust/confidence vested/placed in him\", where the next word is almost certainly \"in\" (or \"into\"), mostly in religious, business or news prose.","s":[0.616785,0.858356,0.515348,0.461619,0.470198,0.013902,0.006077,0.005185,0.009006,0.167462,0.018577,0.006802,0.020216,0.011728,0.007263]},{"p":"L29N9542+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on ordinary tokens (function words, commas, digits, spaces, name pieces) inside text about Italian subjects, namely Dante's Divine Comedy, Italian football (Juventus, Inter, AC Milan, Atalanta), and Italy's history, where the next token is likely an Italian name or term.","s":[0.516554,0.532774,0.116421,0.620106,0.328815,0.040682,0.0047,0.05094,0.179213,0.003892,0.004103,0.00655,0.006866,0.001637,0.002658]},{"p":"L29N9542-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires (negative pole) on a piece of a name or word inside Italian-context text where the next token is likely 'i' as in Pisa, Piccolomini, Pignata, Pio, Pianetti, Pasa, Pugliese, or on the position just before it, mostly the capital 'P' or 'Pic' or 'Pu' piece, plus tokens inside Italian names like Scuola Normale or Editore; it is strongest in academic, citation or institutional text about Italy.","s":[0.377718,0.810629,0.66999,0.666137,1.042735,0.003351,0.658346,0.040642,0.457259,0.008532,0.012129,0.002997,0.004758,-0.000219,0.011024]},{"p":"L30N1334+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on almost any token (commas, 'and', digits, word pieces, code fences) inside math-education and curriculum text such as Common Core standards, fractions/decimals/algebra worksheets and lesson plans, and inside code listings for sorting an integer array (SortA/SortD, sort(nums)); it is driven by this document setting rather than by a specific word.","s":[0.473395,0.248304,0.443919,0.022389,0.025071,0.160432,0.539951,0.022543,0.019258,0.026684,0.043784,0.175899,0.022441,0.017956,0.00865]},{"p":"L30N1334-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires (negatively) on ordinary function words and verbs (from, to, the, of, is, with, like, use, explore) inside math-education text, such as Pi Day activities, algebra and graphing-function problems, and math curriculum or homeschool math pages, when the next word is likely a math or graphing term.","s":[0.492554,0.117453,0.309766,0.322187,0.26329,0.008364,0.048328,0.112681,0.002271,0.002082,0.004292,0.007534,0.003776,0.008203,0.004815]},{"p":"L30N2714+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on the token right before a pixel-unit word like \"px\" or \"pixels\" is expected, i.e. just after a CSS/HTML/JS size value (digits like 50, a quote or concatenation before 'px', \"in\" before \"pixels\") and predicts \"px\"/\"pixel(s)\" next.","s":[0.766442,0.706038,0.775033,0.452998,0.632763,0.0205,0.600479,0.007165,0.502404,0.329405,0.006375,0.003005,0.008856,0.003204,0.007311]},{"p":"L30N2714-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires at a token where the next word is likely to be a pixel unit or pixel quantity (\"pixel\", \"pixels\", \"px\"), mostly in graphics, game or UI code (pygame, SDL, canvas, WPF, Metal) and in screen-resolution specs (e.g. the \"18\" before \":9\" or the \"x\" in \"1170 x 2532\"), where a dimension, size or coordinate is being handled.","s":[0.406796,0.660402,0.332077,0.266747,0.343861,0.053542,0.037811,0.406796,0.060071,0.083984,0.017946,0.010715,0.010441,-0.001112,0.022361]},{"p":"L30N2857+","l":"ONE","j":"00","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on the word-piece \" tort\" when it begins \"tortilla\" or \"tortillas\" (the next piece is \"illa\", \"illas\" or \"as\"), mostly in food, recipe, menu and restaurant text; a weaker echo appears on other mid-phrase pieces that predict a specific continuation, such as \"New\" before \"Orleans\".","s":[0.376639,0.502233,0.740009,0.600192,0.873713,0.078964,0.06931,0.138749,0.034241,0.165992,0.029499,0.029545,0.00877,0.020297,0.028589]},{"p":"L30N2857-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on the token just before an expected park, lawn or grass word (e.g. 'on the' before ground, 'park' before bench or grounds, 'resurface or rese' before playground, 'm' before owed lawn), in prose about parks, playgrounds, lawns, turf and sports fields.","s":[0.640317,0.575566,0.883293,0.982614,0.755203,0.161566,0.025412,0.042363,0.068854,0.101262,0.007041,0.004409,0.006052,0.005157,0.012891]},{"p":"L30N5550+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires in medical or hospital-care text on the word or function word just before an expected \"hospital\"/\"hospitalization\"/\"admission\"/\"discharge\" term (e.g. \"during their index\", \"length of\", \"following\", \"after\", \"hospital\" before \"stay\"), predicting the next token will be about hospital admission, stay or discharge.","s":[0.723406,0.923035,0.748071,0.605008,0.496432,0.464118,0.007995,0.38875,0.055893,0.017868,0.000149,-0.000756,0.011958,0.029435,0.00933]},{"p":"L30N5550-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on a short function word (with, in, for, to, of, and, or, an opening parenthesis or a bullet) in oncology or clinical-trial text, right before the disease or condition is named, e.g. 'patients with', 'ipilimumab in patients', 'progression to'. The next token is likely a disease stage or term such as metastatic, advanced or end-stage, and the neuron suppresses 'progress' words.","s":[1.011775,0.865399,0.655356,0.449392,0.66077,0.750357,0.078054,0.130877,0.00589,0.166514,0.011763,0.016209,0.012437,0.007371,0.0072]},{"p":"L30N5750+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires in C++ text (code, Q&A forums, standard-library docs) on the pieces of the iostream family (ist|ream, of|stream, ifstream/ofstream variable names like cfg, cin, I/O, ostream, std::getline) where the next token is likely \"stream\" or a stream-related word.","s":[0.395974,0.97187,0.666246,0.985377,0.514354,0.047627,0.118459,0.417315,0.136372,0.061733,0.00492,0.004806,0.003611,0.004602,0.007192]},{"p":"L30N5750-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on C++ programming text (Boost mailing-list posts, Boost:: library names and paths, C++11 compiler flags) at the token just before the next piece is predicted to be \"std\", \"shared\" or \"stream\"-like library vocabulary, such as the token right after \"boost\", the \"::\" after Boost, a \"[\" in \"[boost] [topic]\" subject lines, or the \"1\" in \"C++11\".","s":[0.820706,0.701186,0.990236,0.706644,1.116896,0.011747,0.036486,0.015045,0.013246,0.029596,0.013223,0.007107,0.00674,0.007813,0.005824]},{"p":"L30N6819+","l":"UNK","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on a short function word or word-start piece (most often \" in\" after \"published\", \"Cool\" or \"strawberries\", but also \" are\", \" by\", \"non\", \"all\", and a comma after a price or size) in mid-phrase web prose such as recipes, medical-news citations, finance disclaimers and product listings, where a specific noun phrase or word completion must follow.","s":[0.404972,0.287427,0.347919,0.396552,0.232784,0.38947,0.256329,0.187591,0.052893,0.186901,0.010239,0.00197,0.010863,0.013851,0.00862]},{"p":"L30N6819-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on the space token that sits right before a clock time (like 11:00 AM or 2 p.m.), when the time follows a colon, 'from', 'at', or a day label in opening hours, event schedules, timestamps or log headers, and the next token will be a number.","s":[0.458406,0.8165,0.013696,0.312542,0.761306,0.322408,0.375931,0.006728,0.159019,0.259592,0.053298,0.025405,0.034134,0.007615,0.004586]},{"p":"L30N7464+","l":"TWO-REL","j":"11","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on a short word-start piece \"Um\"/\"um\"/\"mo\"/\"Mo\"/\"ör\" (usually German, as in Umkreis, Umwelt, Umsetzung, umfassen, Mobilität, Wörterbuch) that begins a longer word whose continuation is still to come, especially in German text, code, or garbled web spam.","s":[0.979158,0.994196,0.734622,0.650388,0.779795,0.003027,0.006867,0.009369,0.00931,0.184814,0.014688,0.004462,0.006473,0.025016,0.01214]},{"p":"L30N7464-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on a mid-word fragment inside a longer non-English word (mostly German, Spanish or Portuguese, such as \"Noch|mals\", \"min|dest|ens\", \"Bor|ram|os\", \"utiliz|arm|os\", \"ins|om|uch\") where the word is still unfinished and the next piece is a predictable suffix; whole words and English text barely fire it.","s":[0.893356,0.354329,0.466524,0.634196,0.511663,0.002226,0.003237,0.007189,0.102655,0.007066,0.015737,0.0142,0.01351,0.009055,0.015845]},{"p":"L30N8600+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on the verb or object just before an expected \"around\" (also \"with\" or \"about\") in the idioms mess/fool/play/shop/stick/toss/throw/move things/lying/float around, so it predicts the word \"around\" next.","s":[0.500615,0.591438,0.098973,0.206722,0.176622,0.016109,0.000759,0.095485,-0.000359,-0.000249,1.7e-05,0.004221,0.001682,0.003653,0.000389]},{"p":"L30N8600-","l":"UNK","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires most strongly on a mid-identifier piece inside long, repetitive SQL column lists, above all the `_param` in `action_param5` of a smart_scripts INSERT header and the `Movement` in `MovementType`. It fires more weakly on odd mid-word or whitespace pieces such as tab indents, the space before a number in an engineering table, a citation page digit, or a name fragment, where the next piece is nearly forced.","s":[0.879397,0.499408,0.790515,0.734639,0.78679,0.074872,0.030395,0.024239,0.020554,0.032147,0.042109,0.063332,0.053374,0.061974,0.034465]},{"p":"L30N8864+","l":"ONE","j":"00","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on the word \"all\" (or \"All\") in positions where the next word is likely \"right\", \"done\", \"ready\" or \"ears\", such as a sentence-initial \"All right\", \"I'm all ears\", \"all done\" or \"allDone\"; it also fires weakly on other tokens, such as a code comment marker or a digit, where an \"all\" or \"ready\" continuation is predicted.","s":[0.013077,0.4087,0.099994,0.030431,0.045712,0.015401,0.105583,0.008582,0.053721,0.019137,0.011701,0.016898,-0.00017,0.010105,0.010165]},{"p":"L30N8864-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on the token right before the word \"ready\"/\"readiness\" is expected, i.e. where the model predicts \"ready\" next: in \"until you're/are ___ ready\", \"trial-read|iness\", or the jQuery idiom \"$(document).ready\" (on \"document\" or \").\"), and it suppresses the word ready.","s":[0.267767,0.502744,0.374734,0.502744,0.257903,0.061415,0.186053,0.060317,0.272414,0.076383,0.002774,0.00019,0.002609,0.004724,0.001854]},{"p":"L31N1192+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on the token right before the word \"require\" (or requires/required/requirement) is expected, typically after a subject like a law, rule, regulation, state, or \"you can\" in legal, regulatory or contract-style prose, and also after an opening like a function declaration just before a Solidity require(...) call.","s":[0.223057,0.406902,0.579315,0.589607,0.262818,0.223057,0.070529,0.353924,0.600777,0.218317,-0.001057,-0.002286,-0.000902,0.000295,0.000811]},{"p":"L31N1192-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires when the model is predicting a form of \"require\" next (\"required\", \"requirements\", \"requisitos\", \"obligatorio\"): the marked token is the one right before it, such as \"indicates\" in a web form's \"* indicates required\" line, \"the\" in \"in partial fulfillment of the requirements\", or a broken-off piece like \"requis\" or \"Comp\" that leads to a require/compulsory word.","s":[0.695962,0.3911,0.695569,0.183876,0.89697,0.695962,0.359361,0.703325,0.017695,0.132213,0.012957,0.013384,0.01653,0.021595,0.012976]},{"p":"L31N2783+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on the word \" the\" when it comes right after \"in\" in the phrases \"in the process of …\" or \"in the process\" (sentence-final \"…in the process\"), where the model is predicting that the next word will be \"process\".","s":[0.512183,0.419714,0.513812,0.83902,0.244592,0.173961,0.013869,0.513812,0.021485,0.153729,0.011536,0.005028,0.021513,0.050196,0.006518]},{"p":"L31N2783-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on the preposition \"in\" (also \"during\", \"throughout\", \"part\") right before \"the process\", mostly in the idiom \"X, in the process\" for a side effect that happens while doing something, so it predicts the next word \"process\".","s":[0.565702,0.74204,0.095618,0.309501,0.615305,0.009371,0.477884,0.05005,0.012775,0.142305,0.006615,0.037517,0.024085,0.011282,0.01407]},{"p":"L31N2882+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on a short function or structural token (a whitespace gap, an opening quote of a JSON key, a bare space before a number, or a particle such as \"to\", \"as\", \"and\" or \"days\") at a point where a specific new item must come next, mostly in web-scraped documents, code or fanfiction.","s":[0.184458,0.212108,0.010377,0.091821,0.091533,0.086602,0.009576,0.044395,0.02314,0.01377,0.019092,0.019801,0.023322,0.006779,0.01722]},{"p":"L31N2882-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires in obituaries and biographical profiles on the subject token (a pronoun like she/he/they, the couple, or a surname piece) right after the person or couple has been introduced, where the next word will be a verb or a past-tense narrative detail such as 'was married', 'lived', or 'have raised'.","s":[0.404657,0.483168,0.307089,0.240277,0.34694,0.042481,0.152313,0.085352,0.015007,0.04407,0.007126,0.015892,0.01346,0.052514,0.003398]},{"p":"L31N2970+","l":"ONE","j":"00","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on the token right before an Indian institution or place name is about to be written, mainly the \"from\"/\"at\"/\"in\" after a degree or graduation (\"B.Tech from\", \"graduation at\", \"civil in\"), and also on list numbers, the \"K\" or \"the\" before a name, in Indian bios, interviews and admissions pages.","s":[0.887976,0.393359,0.558588,0.317698,1.087924,0.887976,0.302407,0.166561,0.273312,0.011777,0.013909,0.016626,0.027035,0.018693,0.00418]},{"p":"L31N2970-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on the word \" as\" (occasionally \"customized\", \"adjusted\" or \"designed\") in the fixed phrase \"as per your requirement/need/preference\" or \"according to\", in commercial or product-description prose and freelance bids, where the next token is expected to be \"per\" or \"to\".","s":[0.126949,0.244923,0.171032,0.119794,0.044341,0.027734,0.085266,0.058539,0.000233,0.020781,0.005521,0.000826,0.002045,0.006286,0.001281]},{"p":"L31N3418+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on the word or number right before an expected \"property/properties\" in real-estate or rental text, such as a modifier (investment, rental, commercial), a quantifier or determiner (more than one, 10, 25, his, other) when the next word is likely \"property\" or \"properties\"; the neuron is a next-token predictor for \"property\".","s":[0.803857,0.369912,0.422179,0.345395,0.359299,0.273783,0.194532,0.316265,0.155224,0.021117,0.007241,0.007965,0.065739,0.007299,0.118272]},{"p":"L31N3418-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on the token where the word \"property\" (or \"properties\") is the likely next word in real-estate, zoning, land-use or HOA legal prose, e.g. after \"protect established\", \"Covenants, Conditions and\", or \"restrictive covenants,\", and also on the word \"property\" itself in such text.","s":[0.907813,0.580284,0.455264,0.712566,0.530693,0.514865,0.580284,0.192065,-0.000959,0.052329,0.010099,0.003032,0.012057,0.008465,0.0099]},{"p":"L31N4118+","l":"TWO-REL","j":"11","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on a connector token (comma, \"and\", \"or\", \"such as\", \"of\", \"for\") inside a list of food items such as sauces, curries or condiments, and on the \"-\" or \"-contrib\" piece in repeated \"grunt-contrib-\" package names; both predict that the next word is a name from a list.","s":[0.345611,0.343705,0.359756,0.858129,0.409283,0.009485,0.034079,0.101348,0.006452,0.003228,0.00935,0.012037,0.007258,0.009111,0.010547]},{"p":"L31N4118-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires (negative pole) on the function word, comma, or line break just before the next name of a software product, mainly an antivirus or security tool (Kaspersky, AVG, McAfee, Malwarebytes) or a dev tool, such as the \"like\", \"as\", \"and\", \"or\", \"with\", or \",\" in a list of such products or after \"I recommend\".","s":[0.632603,0.980632,0.932636,0.763182,0.48734,0.059615,0.134791,0.053088,0.005204,0.003133,0.016823,0.002647,0.005142,0.017353,0.007907]},{"p":"L31N5699+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on the token just before a predicted form of \"hold\" (held, hold, holding) in the sense of having a belief, view, or opinion, such as \"beliefs people [hold]\", \"a widely [held] view\", \"most deeply [held] beliefs\", or \"the political opinion his mother is deemed to [hold]\"; it sits on the adverb, modal, \"to\", or noun that sets up the verb, mostly in academic, legal, or religious prose about beliefs and opinions.","s":[0.729473,0.366939,0.778211,0.376073,0.67139,0.006279,1.031759,0.007298,0.748886,0.566018,0.005834,0.004896,0.00451,0.003624,0.004775]},{"p":"L31N5699-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires (negatively) on a token in the middle of a proper name, scholarly term, citation, identifier or code symbol, where the next token will probably continue a name or word, as in \"Pres|umpt|ive\", \"Ph|arisees\", \"Michèle A. Flour|noy\", \"pys|park\", \"W.M.|Watt\", \"Dharma Master\", especially in scholarly, religious/philosophical, bibliographic or code text.","s":[0.307228,0.50346,0.424458,0.239707,0.177416,0.026193,0.004302,0.01116,0.026022,0.015185,0.0123,0.005602,0.037213,0.004379,0.011534]},{"p":"L31N6653+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on the word just before an expected \"reasonable\" in legal or terms-and-conditions boilerplate (e.g. \"take [reasonable] steps\", \"beyond its [reasonable] control\", \"beyond a [reasonable] doubt\", \"a [reasonable] person\"), so it predicts the next token \"reasonable\" or \"reasonably\".","s":[0.690129,0.830856,0.773402,0.149281,0.002929,0.139476,0.830856,0.252097,0.00437,0.033034,-0.000727,0.00678,0.005305,0.01777,0.006095]},{"p":"L31N6653-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on the token just before a likely 'reasonable/reasonably' continuation, mostly in formal legal, regulatory, contract or patent prose: function words and adverbs such as 'in', 'to', 'be', 'physically' or 'was', where the standard legal wording would go on to 'reasonable', 'reasonably' or a similar term.","s":[0.003879,0.036467,0.285216,0.018497,0.005607,0.024982,0.025816,0.000315,0.240038,0.017136,0.011755,0.01186,0.028725,0.023174,0.012398]},{"p":"L31N9080+","l":"UNK","j":"11","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on the token just before a specific, rare, name-like item whose first piece the model must predict, most strongly in Shakespeare/theatre prose (a quote mark, parenthesis, 'from', 'of' or ''s' right before a play title or character name), and also in repetitive code or data lists just before a repeated number or a CJK character string.","s":[0.41763,0.295834,0.288357,0.112711,0.204958,0.215485,0.295834,0.093581,0.103734,0.479319,0.044699,0.018922,0.044719,0.035438,0.033872]},{"p":"L31N9080-","l":"UNREL","j":"00","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires (negatively) on the token right before a proper name of an Eastern European or Russian place is spelled out, mostly the initial piece \"K\" of a Cyrillic-origin place name (Kola, Klaipėda, Kuzbass, Kirov, Kursk) or \"River\"/\"Lake\"/\"the\" before a river or lake name (Volga, Baikal, Biebrza, Peipus, Bug), also commas or \"and\" inside lists of rivers; it also fires on punctuation, such as a colon and newline, before a config line in one software README.","s":[1.069116,0.228167,0.191212,0.817598,0.173824,0.041345,0.027983,0.020382,0.111867,0.02074,0.00381,-0.0019,-0.002247,0.004879,0.004318],"u":{"0":[0.576307,0.358451,0.98258,0.722362,0.52792],"1":[0.440814,0.900745,0.063489,0.756684,0.344076]}},{"p":"L31N9536+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires at a token where the model is mid-word or mid-phrase and the next token is very unlikely to be the article \"a\" (output suppresses \"a\"/\"个\"): especially after \"a\"/\"an\" before an adjective (\"isn't a great fit\", \"such a poor job\"), word-start pieces like \"do\" (dozen, dozy, doled), the apostrophe in \"d'Arpino\", or a split Devanagari or word fragment.","s":[0.031534,0.292975,0.267801,0.477086,0.009099,0.005916,0.045954,0.029979,0.043497,0.013504,0.01824,0.015301,0.013223,0.022981,0.035904]},{"p":"L31N9536-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on the token \"a\" (or \"-a\" in \"once-in-a-lifetime\") when it is the article inside a fixed idiom that predicts \"lifetime\" next, such as \"once-in-a-lifetime\" or \"last a lifetime\"; it also fires on \"a\" in \"a breeze\", \"a cinch\" and \"turn a blind eye\", and is strongest in travel or marketing prose.","s":[0.858694,0.612019,0.908872,0.796466,0.679554,0.01479,0.031574,0.103127,0.009975,0.404277,0.013804,0.005736,0.004533,0.002319,0.003813]},{"p":"L32N1136+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on a byte-split piece of a Devanagari letter (the ढ/ड/ड़/ढ़ nukta consonants in words like पढ़, लड़के, मारवाड़ी, plus Bengali য়) when the next token is a continuation of the character, where the model expects a backslash, raw byte or LaTeX-like escape to follow.","s":[0.929798,0.784807,0.805529,0.337746,0.385799,0.805529,0.521139,0.002201,0.422693,0.719416,0.002453,0.001895,0.001639,0.003093,0.004813]},{"p":"L32N1136-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on a broken byte-fragment token (the replacement-character piece left when a multi-byte Unicode symbol such as a middot, en dash, non-breaking or zero-width space, or a multiplication dot is split into raw bytes), appearing mid-text in scraped web pages, scientific text and BeerAdvocate-style navigation like '« first � prev'.","s":[0.041899,0.023548,0.011524,0.033094,0.039178,0.010844,0.544791,0.013765,0.016243,0.039178,0.011685,0.005553,0.013034,0.00609,0.008439]},{"p":"L32N2473+","l":"TWO-REL","j":"11","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on the word right before a bare verb is expected (mostly the infinitive \"to\" after obliged, ordered, decided, need, or a coordinating \"and\" or comma after a verb phrase), as well as the first piece of a split rare verb like \"j\"+uggle, jettison or jemmy, in running English prose.","s":[0.253413,0.4338,0.399997,0.2602,0.699374,0.008967,0.182299,0.234089,0.018533,0.283371,0.006566,-0.001577,0.040753,0.057202,0.036619]},{"p":"L32N2473-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires negatively on function tokens (\"and\", a comma after an opening phrase like \"In a large skillet\", a period ending a mixing step, \"not\" in \"Do not\") inside step-by-step instructions, mostly recipes, where the next word will be an imperative verb such as mix, heat, blend, pulse or stir.","s":[0.594383,0.43958,0.736525,0.44882,0.47558,0.00977,0.183685,0.231968,0.005889,0.031755,0.024199,0.024135,0.098113,0.021649,0.034278]},{"p":"L32N5074+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on a word-piece in the middle of a proper name (usually a person's name) that begins with an initial fragment such as \"Mar\", \"H\", \"Sl\", \"hl\" or \"Em\", and where the rest of the name has not appeared yet; the neuron expects a continuation like \"id\"/\"ID\" or a name suffix, and is strongest on \"Mar\" at the start of a name like Marv, Marisa, Marzipan or Marianne.","s":[0.493821,0.488605,0.022009,0.022389,0.348024,0.011909,0.012301,0.019206,0.025742,0.010316,0.01848,0.010063,0.030349,0.018938,0.027496]},{"p":"L32N5074-","l":"TWO-REL","j":"01","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires negatively on a word fragment or whole token that is the start or middle of a longer rare proper name or compound, so that the model is mid-word and expects a continuation piece (e.g. \"Tow\"+son, \"Rav\"+enna, \"Mon\"+ksville, \"dou\"+jinshi, \"Sw\"+arthmore, \"sw\"+f), and also on a repeated token in code or lists where the next token is a known specific continuation.","s":[0.680094,0.627461,0.408777,0.717081,0.433546,0.020038,0.012878,0.013583,0.023705,0.014033,0.004878,0.060129,0.010271,0.087139,0.167492]},{"p":"L32N5174+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on the connecting token (comma, 'and', 'or', 'like', 'in', 'of', 'teaches') in text about martial arts and combat sports, right where the next item will be another martial art or fighting style such as boxing, Muay Thai, karate, kali or BJJ.","s":[0.713154,0.934461,0.638223,1.062439,0.528345,0.005125,0.018939,0.002371,0.003715,0.006158,0.008009,0.004641,0.007111,0.002976,0.014272]},{"p":"L32N5174-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires (negatively) on a function word or punctuation mark in technical text about compression, archives, executable packers and low-level programming (UPX, RAR/ZIP, gzip, code flags, Windows C++), at spots where the next token would be a technical term or identifier.","s":[0.649477,0.049045,0.533316,0.280552,0.424767,0.015157,0.015024,0.009873,0.025523,0.018799,0.016773,0.024185,0.018194,0.02074,0.015102]},{"p":"L32N5530+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires inside nature-writing about forests and woodland (ecology, wildfire, forestry, deer-hunting prose), on the word \"forest\" or the words and word pieces around it that describe the forest (dense, open, pine, conifer, aspen, beetle-kill, heavy cover), where the next word is likely a forest-type noun like stands, trees, or fragments.","s":[0.709537,0.475368,0.822519,0.820262,0.509842,0.205652,0.349699,0.017545,0.475728,0.057379,0.00387,0.026714,0.030803,0.038114,0.048736]},{"p":"L32N5530-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on function words and mid-phrase tokens (\"the\", \"along\", \"are\", \"found\", \"highly\", \"usually\", \"of\") inside ecology, wildlife, marine-coast or travel-nature prose, where the next word should be a coastal, habitat or animal-location word (shore, coast, beach, waters, reef, found in).","s":[0.581362,1.067436,0.816775,0.320037,0.93053,0.01922,0.021996,0.003701,0.018876,0.008139,0.022526,0.008864,0.007779,0.003358,0.01636]},{"p":"L32N6216+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on the word right before a likely \"outline\" (or similar planning-document noun) in essay-writing advice: the verb or article in phrases like \"creating a/an\", \"develop an\", \"do you\" (outline), \"make an\", \"write a\" (draft/thesis) where the next word is expected to be \"outline\".","s":[0.831359,0.354851,0.529716,0.595019,0.48295,0.130029,0.131489,0.005092,0.004887,0.029205,0.006899,0.011505,0.00613,0.002254,0.0023]},{"p":"L32N6216-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on a modifier word (like security, ID, membership, entry, sign-, order, identity) that sits right before a head noun such as card, token, form, sheet, ticket or pass, where that noun is the expected next word.","s":[0.524242,0.30959,0.488288,0.455956,0.718669,0.259866,0.184219,0.120355,0.267896,0.547843,0.024823,0.005206,0.013418,0.008199,0.016424]},{"p":"L32N7741+","l":"TWO-REL","j":"11","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on mid-word pieces or inflectional endings inside Baltic-language words (Latvian and Lithuanian, such as -u, -s, -as, -ės, -io) and on Chinese characters or Taiwan place names, where the next token is another piece of the same word; it is strongest in product listings, reference lists and code comments that mix in Latvian, Lithuanian or Traditional Chinese text.","s":[1.068491,0.723037,1.090117,0.596085,0.517474,0.009972,0.006162,0.008978,0.011919,-0.000369,0.006863,0.00674,0.004406,0.005196,0.008088]},{"p":"L32N7741-","l":"TWO-REL","j":"11","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires (negative pole) at the boundary between a person's name or department and the institution or title that follows it in Taiwanese or Asian conference and thesis listings (comma or newline after a department name, or the period ending a Chinese thesis title before the university name). It also fires on Lithuanian or Finnish word pieces such as 'pr' or 'pir' in the middle of a word, in text that is mostly in another language.","s":[0.440267,0.202278,0.582132,0.847506,0.317703,0.014386,0.010569,0.009446,0.008116,0.008383,0.010928,0.010052,0.005451,0.001799,0.011852]},{"p":"L32N7909+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires at positions where the next token is likely a proper-noun or technical name fragment in Chinese or scientific contexts: the comma or parenthesis after a Chinese city or place name (Baoan District, Shenzhen), the period after an author initial in a citation, and the opening piece of a longer rare name or term (Gui|yang, Dat|ong, palindrom|ic).","s":[0.758145,0.094685,0.595119,0.271888,1.139662,0.018403,0.019467,0.018622,0.036333,0.010155,0.012741,0.011265,0.125429,0.012787,0.017904]},{"p":"L32N7909-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on the token \"Air\" (or \"air\" / \".air\") when it is the first word of a military or aviation term such as Air Force, Air Mobility Command, Air National Guard, air power or air assets, mostly in military news or government text, with 'Air' as a standalone word, not mid-word.","s":[0.460725,0.809631,0.461669,0.334425,0.52147,0.12794,0.574463,0.029474,0.086978,0.254099,0.006152,0.053146,0.005443,0.010577,0.015391]},{"p":"L32N8029+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on the token just before the word \"poor\" (or a split piece such as \"-p\", \"po\", \"imp\") when the model predicts that \"poor\" or \"poverty\" comes next, mostly in a \"rich and/or poor\" contrast, \"dirt-poor\", \"metal-poor\", \"the poor\", or \"became poor\" frame.","s":[0.530857,0.273242,0.681227,0.15533,0.212569,0.367936,0.195733,0.681227,0.15533,0.098568,0.002113,0.015804,0.002596,0.00326,0.002412]},{"p":"L32N8029-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on the token just before a likely continuation of \"poor\"/\"poorly\"/\"poorest\" or \"under-\" (as in underserved/under-represented), e.g. \"c\" before \"addies\", \"unders\" before \"erved communities\", \"of\" in \"indices of deprivation\", \"a\" in \"cried like a child\", \"the\" before \"worst\", where the model expects the word poor/poorer/poorest to come next.","s":[0.474786,0.25935,0.502322,0.015645,-0.009285,0.007665,0.152166,0.075618,0.111353,0.090298,0.025007,0.023747,0.020436,0.023507,0.034072]},{"p":"L32N9698+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on the connecting word or punctuation (\"of\", \"with\", \"and\", a comma, or a mid-word piece like \"ile\" in febrile) right where a disease or medical condition name is expected to come next, as in \"died of\", \"fell ill with\", \"suffered from\", \"diphtheria, tetanus and\", \"primary generalized\", \"seizures\", \"typhoid fever\" or \"measles\".","s":[0.073344,0.366245,0.000848,0.776735,0.495519,0.016634,0.031017,0.010968,0.010034,0.014571,0.01801,0.015912,0.00789,0.006443,0.005815]},{"p":"L32N9698-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on the function word or comma right before a serious medical condition is named, as in \"has a\", \"suffering from a\", \"such as a stroke,\", \"people with\", \"caused by\", \"risk of\" or \"spinal\", in health, insurance or medical prose where a neurological or cardiac condition such as stroke, spinal cord injury or dementia is expected next.","s":[0.461974,0.597586,0.340462,0.586038,0.799301,0.036204,0.397198,0.308123,0.209632,0.207595,0.009769,0.012831,0.007109,0.006219,0.008936]},{"p":"L33N1238+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on a hexadecimal digit token inside a long random hex string (hash, UUID, or hex-encoded URL path segment) when the digits so far are letters and digits that could still continue as hex, as in hash values or ID strings in URLs.","s":[0.818932,0.71015,0.378604,0.31813,0.860614,0.034802,0.18238,0.047724,0.006498,0.055722,0.003048,0.009964,0.001245,0.007713,0.014896]},{"p":"L33N1238-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on a single digit (0-9) or hyphen inside a long hexadecimal-style ID string (session IDs, GUIDs, hashes, URL tokens) in the middle of the string, where the next character is likely a hex letter or digit (A-F, 0-9) rather than a normal word.","s":[0.006259,0.001101,0.000204,0.007522,0.00507,0.006926,0.00122,0.000144,0.008041,0.001215,0.003345,0.004054,0.004193,0.00514,0.004212]},{"p":"L33N1962+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on a short function word or word-piece (\"of\", \"by\", \"or\", \"in\", \"IBM\", \"steel\", \"res\") right where a specific technical term, proper name or materials/engineering/history term must come next, mostly in engineering, materials, history or chess prose.","s":[0.312883,0.236292,0.016642,0.462302,0.038615,0.014666,0.112348,0.005106,0.076086,0.035432,0.00912,0.010073,0.018039,0.011491,0.007651]},{"p":"L33N1962-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on scientific/engineering/patent/academic text (technical abstracts, patent claims and listings, reference lists, textbook solutions) at points mid-phrase or mid-word where the next token is a technical continuation, e.g. after a technical noun, a copula (\"is\", \"which is\"), a figure number, or a citation period; it is a broad domain-register detector, not tied to a specific token.","s":[0.442484,0.28674,0.181702,0.16236,0.174783,0.015038,0.011792,0.012374,0.0332,0.023813,0.016958,0.01802,0.009392,0.06319,0.008866]},{"p":"L33N2724+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on a short mid-word fragment (often a single letter like N, k, a digit, or the piece \"wat\") that begins a split-up abbreviation, code, or compound where the next piece is an expected continuation, such as N before AAQS or LRB, k before psi, wat before ertight, or a character inside an ID, part number, or URL.","s":[0.680282,0.134312,0.734095,0.090493,0.074178,0.024842,0.013395,0.002788,0.020086,0.0178,0.019323,0.020849,0.00495,0.030424,0.01786]},{"p":"L33N2724-","l":"TWO-REL","j":"11","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires (negative) on the token where the model must predict a single capital letter or fragment that starts an uppercase acronym or a color-code string: the opening quote before a matplotlib format string like 'bo-' or 'ro', or the \" C\"/\"SO\"/\"SH\"/\"L\" piece beginning an acronym such as CTO, CIO, CMO, LTO, SHO, or a word piece before a fragment like \"loff\".","s":[0.71127,0.652236,0.641991,0.726146,0.852222,0.131759,0.125924,0.074324,0.05475,0.008864,0.00488,0.003221,0.003605,0.009115,0.005817]},{"p":"L33N3093+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"It fires on the token just before a lowercase Roman numeral (i, ii, iii) is expected, such as the period after \"vol\", \"world war\", \"lib\" or \"ch\", the dash in \"world-war-\", or \"vol\" and \"book\" in citations, and it promotes a following \"i\".","s":[0.670664,0.495151,0.381929,0.229925,0.463799,0.327384,0.004686,0.209662,0.006908,0.000528,0.00776,0.002146,0.011141,0.004053,0.01906]},{"p":"L33N3093-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires at separator or function-word tokens (comma, slash, hyphen, \"the\", \"new\", \"on\", \"an\", \"from\", \"(\") when the next token is predicted to be a lowercase Apple-device word starting with \"i\" (iphone, ipad, iphone4s), typically in URLs/slugs or comma-separated lists of iPhone/iPad models.","s":[0.837162,0.34168,0.371909,0.258792,0.729638,0.837162,0.437345,0.154234,0.126149,0.337955,0.00796,0.022021,0.004075,0.003478,0.004833]},{"p":"L33N4268+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on a function word or word-start piece (e.g. \"have\", \"would\", \"to\", \"or\", \"as\", \"I\", \"then\", \"en\") in the middle of a clause in long, formal argumentative prose such as legal opinions, law-review articles, regulations and analytical essays, where the next word is a verb, a verb stem or a continuation of the phrase.","s":[0.730078,0.398908,0.563586,0.009068,0.267289,0.045358,0.002306,0.299963,0.109164,0.014433,0.002005,0.00572,0.031578,0.018745,0.009401]},{"p":"L33N4268-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on the prefix piece \"re\" (or \"-re\") that begins a longer re- word, such as re-examined, redefining, reworked, rebranded or reinterpreting, so it covers a split-off re- prefix in running prose and not the standalone word \"re\".","s":[0.427818,0.334724,0.60499,0.511876,0.022776,0.033319,0.043915,0.057289,0.00526,0.011883,0.026352,0.01139,0.054466,0.025781,0.001689]},{"p":"L33N5719+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on a short common word (Top, food, house, back, wheel, road, tow, roll, Food, Land) when it is the first part of a compound noun, name or phrase whose second word has not yet appeared, so the next token is a noun completing the compound (Top Gear, food thermometer, house keys, back wheel, Roadside, Foodbank).","s":[0.859911,0.467105,0.535331,0.337968,0.29554,0.04927,0.040187,0.036398,0.296229,0.01355,0.024979,0.033496,0.065036,0.014922,0.118374]},{"p":"L33N5719-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on a word or word-piece where the next token is a predictable word-internal or compound continuation: a suffix like -rieth, -ling, -eth, -ylate, or a hyphenated compound such as day-to-day or spring onion. It is strongest inside King James-style biblical verse and in passages that quote scripture, and it also fires on mid-word pieces such as car|rieth, car|box|ylate, year|ling and wh|ither.","s":[0.757538,0.314476,0.480477,0.287393,0.366656,0.185256,0.140785,0.117046,0.072944,0.087054,0.131957,0.094084,0.063338,0.200631,0.008228]},{"p":"L33N5981+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on the token right before the word \"including\" (or \"include\"), where a general category noun or a comma ends a clause and an example list is about to begin, as in \"several awards, including…\", \"parts of Pakistan, including…\", \"without limitation, including…\".","s":[0.502426,0.012396,0.083622,0.004283,0.230275,0.502426,0.01726,0.064512,0.004319,0.220821,0.005397,0.00862,0.008031,0.004332,0.013391]},{"p":"L33N5981-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on a bare capital \"T\" (or another initial piece) that begins a rare proper name, typically a Japanese or foreign surname or place such as Tominaga, Tachiwana, Tottori, Tsuchiya or Tatarstan, most often inside author lists, reference lists and names in news or travel prose.","s":[0.647467,0.21989,0.700896,0.662237,0.139326,0.034979,0.008893,-0.009111,0.011944,0.016468,0.000973,-0.001613,0.050793,0.056354,0.003674]},{"p":"L33N7061+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on a bare B or F (also b, /B, .B, .F) that is a fragment starting a new word or name, usually right after a space, slash, dot or quote, where the model expects the rest of the word to follow (Baku, Bialystock, Brest, bread, baking, Fridge, FOLLOWS, BDX2000).","s":[0.643272,0.069943,0.707261,0.297861,0.072637,0.108933,0.037883,0.031556,0.011126,0.01309,0.021445,0.020225,0.01383,0.013417,0.012863]},{"p":"L33N7061-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires strongly on short function words or punctuation (a, the, an, new, like, a comma, or a word piece) at the point where the next word is a concrete noun for an object or body part, notably when \"arm\" could follow (\"armrest\", \"arm chair\", \"grab bars\"). It is strongest in Blender/3D software forum and article text and in senior-housing and home-modification prose.","s":[0.814976,0.876271,0.943672,0.513094,1.124209,0.799851,0.574664,0.155667,0.006255,0.050466,0.004425,0.042029,0.015446,0.009095,0.002918]},{"p":"L33N9519+","l":"TWO-REL","j":"11","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on the token right before a likely \"Education/educ-\" or other word-start continuation is blocked, mostly on a capital \"B\" or \"B\"-initial word start (R-B|rookfield, Canson B|aryta, Prime B|SI, B|aryshnikov, B|ID) and on connectors like \"as\", \"and\", \"starred\" or \"d'\" before a name or rôle in ballet, theatre, citation and lab-protocol text; it is a diffuse, non-semantic token-position pole that suppresses education words.","s":[0.935545,0.750197,0.941867,0.26299,0.611123,0.361103,0.003399,0.043681,0.022782,0.264028,0.007915,0.012299,0.005971,0.019091,0.006193]},{"p":"L33N9519-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires (negative pole) on the word or word piece just before an expected \"education\"/\"training\" word, mainly \"executive\" or \"continuing\" in phrases like executive education, executive MBA or continuing education credits, and also on other modifiers or acronyms that precede education or training (social worker training, architectural education, GP vocational training).","s":[0.62126,0.477446,0.557353,0.200036,0.246767,0.207328,0.057571,0.180878,0.222069,0.025587,0.008885,0.015527,0.051041,0.01391,0.008219]},{"p":"L34N1706+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on the token right before a capital letter R is predicted to come next: the word 'in' before the statistical language R (\"package in R\"), an 'R' variable in code or equations (\"Ax\"→R.Count, \"mov\"→RcxImm64, \"_linear\"→R, \"for\"→R = 3 mm), and HTML table cells where an R-value or R-word follows, or quote-and-name text like Ralls or Rider.","s":[0.879742,0.019666,0.019975,0.036065,0.037597,0.879742,0.019666,0.019975,0.036065,0.032681,0.027604,0.023135,0.030311,0.029027,0.026108]},{"p":"L34N1706-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires negatively on a token (often a name piece, initial, comma, or 'the'/'a') that sits right before a capital letter R that is about to be written, as in 'Walt R(ostow)', 'Solomon R. Guggenheim', 'R.R. Martin', 'Toys \"R\" Us', 'MediatR', 'Catcher in the Rye', 'RAZR'; it works by suppressing the R token as the next prediction.","s":[0.487756,0.588994,0.704486,1.191317,0.400056,0.487756,-0.006696,0.048866,0.026714,0.024795,-0.001312,-0.003067,-0.003741,0.029694,0.00558]},{"p":"L34N2004+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on the token just before the model expects a word from the 'develop' family (develop, development, developed, undeveloped, utveckling, Entwicklung, desenvolvimento, 개발), often on a word piece like 'ut' or 'Des' that begins a translated or foreign form, or on 'to', 'of' or 'in' right before 'develop(ing)'. It is an output-side neuron that suppresses saying 'develop' tokens.","s":[0.0592,0.959369,0.266035,0.150953,0.099126,0.058755,0.554714,0.006312,0.049673,0.054032,0.000684,0.005554,0.001403,0.001403,0.005523]},{"p":"L34N2004-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on the evaluative adjective or demonstrative (\"surprising\", \"shocking\", \"promising\", \"this\", \"a\") that opens an appositive or \"In a ___ turn/development\" phrase in news prose, right where the next word is a noun like development, turn, sign, move or news.","s":[0.644841,0.595728,0.528593,1.122718,0.384286,0.119154,0.090392,0.183456,0.091616,0.059296,0.317362,0.208332,0.152178,0.285975,0.095972]},{"p":"L34N3189+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires in formal, legal, or statistical prose on the word or comma right after a person-subject (people, persons, adults, employees, nationals, an alien), including the auxiliary or adverb that follows (be, been, is, were, not, currently), where the next word is a participle or status predicate such as living, employed, licensed, present, disabled, or found.","s":[0.836356,0.573281,0.591521,0.536734,0.628437,0.293214,0.402344,0.357387,0.256149,0.265557,0.100189,0.084679,0.026822,0.130005,0.08916]},{"p":"L34N3189-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on a token (often a word-start or function word such as 'elephant', 'young', 'of', 'their', 'child', 'pig') where the next word is a plural or collective noun for living beings, mainly animals, children or people, so the model predicts a living/born/educated-type continuation; it is strongest in long, farm, wildlife or livestock prose, and also in non-English or garbled text.","s":[0.19183,0.465611,0.285071,0.353652,0.249541,0.042021,0.03035,0.180088,0.033613,0.023098,0.040162,0.02796,0.014222,0.017485,0.038734]},{"p":"L34N6564+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on a word-piece in the middle of a Dutch word (often a word-initial 'k', 'st', 'chem', 'agnost', 'elektron') when the text has switched into Dutch, mainly inside an otherwise English or mixed-language page, so that the next token continues the Dutch word.","s":[0.796612,0.524814,0.214523,0.818824,0.702649,0.18647,0.008254,0.009013,0.002328,0.007341,0.008331,0.004553,0.012388,0.007165,0.004997]},{"p":"L34N6564-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on a word piece inside a Dutch word (or Dutch-derived identifier) when the model is mid-word and the next piece is probably a vowel cluster like 'eu', 'aa' or 'ie' (Keukenhof, kleur, keuze, Naam, Volgnummer); it also fires on some paragraph breaks and punctuation in Dutch text, and is strongest in Dutch prose or Dutch-named code.","s":[0.998264,0.689379,0.758588,0.94931,0.889471,0.07854,0.002689,0.003727,0.274342,0.006999,0.011238,0.014206,0.02234,0.028938,0.012876]},{"p":"L34N836+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on a word-piece in the middle of a longer word that is split into tokens, where the next piece is likely to complete it as an \"-er/-ers\" (or \"-ar\", \"-ary\") ending, e.g. \"vene\"→\"er(s)\", \"engine\"→\"er\", \"musket\"→\"eers\", \"sug\"→\"ar\", \"parameт\"→\"ры\", \"semin\"→\"ar(s)\", \"RANDOM_CENT\"→\"ERS\", so it does not fire when those words appear as whole tokens.","s":[0.473989,0.606583,0.141029,0.000485,0.077037,0.010014,0.047374,0.018274,-0.00218,0.006904,0.015867,0.03099,0.143422,0.124332,0.006005]},{"p":"L34N836-","l":"TWO-REL","j":"01","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires strongly on a word fragment sitting in the middle of a word that is about to finish with an agent-noun ending such as -er, -ers or -ing (e.g. \"story-TELL-er\", \"inter-LO-per\", \"problem SOL-vers\", \"doom-M-ONG-er\", \"day-DREAM-ers\", \"cloud-RID-ers\", \"Trav-ELL-ers\"), and also on a broken first byte of a multi-byte CJK character; whole words and finished suffixes do not fire it.","s":[0.741317,0.384155,0.386503,0.165953,0.532139,0.174353,0.010992,0.011967,0.013281,0.015291,0.014492,0.017872,0.014081,0.016491,0.01771]},{"p":"L34N9051+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on a standalone letter \"d\" or \"D\" (any case, sometimes after a hyphen, slash or dot) that begins a word after a break such as a line, bracket or hyphen, where the model is mid-word and must continue with a rare or proper-name piece, e.g. D|almatia, D|SG, d|ana, d|examethasone, -d|ose.","s":[0.106762,0.321491,0.016359,0.024295,0.108373,0.008814,0.321491,0.001208,0.00226,0.006008,0.010375,0.03834,0.005498,0.002858,0.004074]},{"p":"L34N9051-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on the token 'd' or 'D' (usually capital ' D' or 'D' at the start of a word) when it is a first piece of a longer word that is split off, such as D|ipping, d|extrin, d|ough, D|redge, D|ust, D|aha, D|iet, D|ill, D|&C; it does not fire on whole words and is strongest in recipe or cooking prose.","s":[0.014913,0.502178,0.068302,0.004264,0.28971,0.000188,0.002323,0.000457,0.011561,0.002289,0.01898,0.006614,0.007715,0.011525,0.001758]},{"p":"L34N9672+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires strongly on a token inside non-English text (mostly Dutch or Italian, sometimes Persian or Catalan) when the model is in a foreign-language passage and a word or word-piece is mid-way through completing, such as a Dutch word piece (Aantal, sollicitatie, volgende), an Italian function word (di, hai, ho), or a digit inside a Dutch or Italian page.","s":[0.568742,0.320485,0.360099,0.748167,0.66363,0.001962,0.010865,0.012896,0.006416,0.011471,0.009331,0.005942,0.005928,0.003399,0.008725]},{"p":"L34N9672-","l":"TWO-REL","j":"11","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires (negatively) on tokens in the middle of Italian or Dutch prose, usually in a word or phrase that is mid-sentence (word fragments like 'tav|ole', 'cap|elli', 'fiam|me', function words like 'di', 'che', 'molto'), and on broken bytes of Bengali or Devanagari text, especially in web-scraped, multilingual or mixed-language pages.","s":[0.539133,0.36988,0.285332,0.45584,0.605472,0.003707,0.002118,0.006056,0.00697,0.006146,0.003393,0.007954,0.005314,0.001689,0.004084]},{"p":"L35N1588+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on a word-piece \"d\"/\"D\" (often after a hyphen, slash, or space) that begins or continues a word where the next token is likely \"ie\"/\"ice\"/\"iable\" (die, dice, diable, Dieu, diegetic, dielectric, San Diego), so the model is predicting a following 'ie'/'ice'/'ied' piece, with dollar, dendro and dental as weaker variants.","s":[0.068195,0.201211,0.466525,0.064545,0.31764,0.066734,0.017438,0.102412,0.017937,0.07066,-0.002058,0.042656,0.032372,0.006656,0.005305]},{"p":"L35N1588-","l":"ONE","j":"00","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires (negatively) at the token right after a deceased person's name or subject (a comma, a relative pronoun like \"who\", or an auxiliary like \"has\") in obituary or death-news text, where the next word is predicted to be \"died\" or \"dies\"; it also fires in the odd spam phrase \"buy einführung in die...\".","s":[0.242777,0.455574,0.158936,0.311864,0.459706,0.242777,0.455574,0.03632,0.311864,0.52881,0.016434,0.016058,0.014041,0.017527,0.014818]},{"p":"L35N1881+","l":"UNK","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on a token inside highly predictable boilerplate or a memorized template, where the continuation is nearly certain and no LaTeX or dollar delimiter should follow: the 'Ind' or 'Ин' piece of 'Indexes' in phpMyAdmin SQL dump comments, the 'Only' in the essay-mill ad 'We Will Write a Custom Essay Specifically For You For Only $13.90/page', and word pieces in famous or repeated text.","s":[0.033098,0.069143,0.312923,0.260029,0.009088,0.025499,0.092875,0.18922,0.04155,0.039509,0.024567,0.014152,0.022242,0.008873,0.011935]},{"p":"L35N1881-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires (negatively) on the token just before a literal backslash is expected in code or file paths, such as the Windows path pieces 'C:\\Program' / ' Files' / 'Users', regex or CSS escape sequences, or '$' ahead of '\\'; it suppresses LaTeX-style '\\(' and '\\$' openers.","s":[0.406101,0.484501,0.329351,0.243948,0.142155,0.050344,0.085104,0.162409,0.052513,0.054701,0.012781,0.007865,0.012091,0.019602,0.030763]},{"p":"L35N233+","l":"TWO-REL","j":"11","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on a token that sits inside a comma-separated or fixed-format sequence and is followed by a comma: the '0' of a hex byte list like {0xb0,0x31,0x6d,...} (the 0 before the 'x'), a weekday abbreviation (Sun/Wed/Mon) at the start of a forum or mailing-list date line, or a name piece before a comma in a citation list.","s":[0.789118,0.201647,0.263432,0.365296,0.340066,0.041464,0.0523,0.08724,0.038848,0.045168,0.031883,0.029306,0.027999,0.037719,0.069905]},{"p":"L35N233-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on a digit token inside a number written with comma thousands separators (like 1,000,000 or 35,698,532), where the next token is very likely to be a comma and then another group of three digits.","s":[0.209849,0.456864,0.268551,0.576521,0.727294,0.324196,0.146113,0.047143,0.142176,0.008244,0.175145,0.188575,0.091715,0.177929,0.089946]},{"p":"L35N5401+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on a syllable inside a romanized Chinese name or place (Shenghao, Hong Kong, Jingyi, Jiankang, Changzheng, Zhihong), usually the middle or start of a pinyin or Cantonese transliteration, where another syllable should follow, mostly in author lists, citations and Chinese-related web text.","s":[0.437206,0.27123,0.509834,0.190904,0.333665,0.045318,0.010842,0.020731,0.017593,0.002818,0.004931,0.010141,0.004221,0.002391,0.004744]},{"p":"L35N5401-","l":"TWO-REL","j":"11","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires strongly inside foreign or unfamiliar-looking text: lorem-ipsum filler (including the JSON placeholder posts and fake database rows), and mid-word pieces of romanized or transliterated Chinese, Icelandic, Slovak and other non-English names, plus CJK passages; it is marked where the next token is hard to predict.","s":[0.236348,0.913544,0.658357,0.329548,0.00616,0.006261,0.019031,0.027766,0.03279,0.012168,0.005247,0.012127,0.006673,0.008276,0.004624]},{"p":"L35N6124+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on a word-initial fragment (such as compl, expl, occ, appl, impl, C, sup, Imp) that begins a longer word, usually a misspelled or split one, where the model must predict the rest of the word, and also on a broken multibyte CJK character; it fires in web text, code and lists.","s":[0.297434,0.276229,0.278432,0.228379,0.402072,0.005285,0.00373,0.020485,0.008384,0.000802,0.011951,0.00402,0.004261,0.003667,0.015713]},{"p":"L35N6124-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires (negatively) on a word-initial fragment of a rare or foreign proper name or uncommon word, mostly 'Z' (Zoraide, Zéphire, Zelmira, Zeke), 'Pur' (Purac), 'Ge' (Gevo), 'M' (Migrate, MeeGo, Mjolnir) and 'ju' (juiciest), where the next token must complete the word; some weaker hits land on ordinary function words mid-sentence.","s":[0.858537,0.221183,0.582964,0.265581,0.839455,0.005446,0.001856,0.001441,0.002245,0.003031,0.002341,0.003459,0.006668,0.001974,0.006896]},{"p":"L35N6252+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on a word-initial fragment 'Pr' (also 'Tr', 'Cr', 'Thr', 'fr', 'r', 'cl') that begins a longer word, typically at the start of a sentence or line, right where the next piece is a vowel or 'i/u/a' and the model must complete the word; whole standalone words do not fire it.","s":[0.001402,0.083056,0.001491,0.222029,0.013357,0.000492,0.005348,0.001962,0.002811,0.004913,0.001097,0.001144,0.001248,0.003898,0.002035]},{"p":"L35N6252-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on the word piece \"cl\" (also \"pr\"/\"Pr\") when it starts a longer word and the next piece is still to come, mostly \"cl\" before amps, utches, ams, asp, ays, inker or adding, as in clamps, clutches, clams, clasp, clays, clinker and cladding; a whole-word \"cl\" or other tokens will not fire it.","s":[0.764841,0.78967,0.570889,0.907048,0.727095,0.002033,0.001598,0.001487,0.000863,0.004597,0.001573,0.002999,0.000887,0.001393,0.175904]},{"p":"L35N7677+","l":"UNK","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on a mid-name or mid-phrase token in boilerplate or web text, where the next token is a predictable continuation (e.g. a rest of a Cyrillic word 'Помидоры', a repeated site or brand name like 'XPRTs' or 'BBN Times'); the model is spread across many contexts but the output promotes 'look' forms, so it is a weak-meaning neuron that is hard to reproduce reliably in short text.","s":[0.020102,0.126245,0.147663,0.020326,0.202748,0.037811,0.031548,0.036066,0.012591,0.011726,0.044715,0.053033,0.002279,0.005382,0.008894]},{"p":"L35N7677-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on a word or word piece (usually an adjective or adjective stem) that sits right before a likely \"-looking\" or \"looking\" (e.g. \"appet|ising-looking\", \"innoc|uous-looking\", \"funny looking\", \"nicer looking\", \"hide|ous looking\"), so the neuron pushes the next token toward \"look/looking\".","s":[0.306847,0.347682,0.159154,0.174962,0.394231,0.306847,0.351393,0.158427,0.174962,0.394231,0.009566,0.005795,0.008519,0.034552,0.056998]},{"p":"L35N8591+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires most strongly on an incomplete byte-level token, shown as �, that starts a rare Chinese or Cyrillic character after a space (Chinese comments in code, bilingual subtitles, Ukrainian text); it fires more weakly on ordinary mid-word or mid-phrase pieces in lists, chat logs and code.","s":[0.002651,0.008511,0.383281,0.008993,0.583292,0.005601,0.005208,0.004184,0.001474,0.011606,0.005939,0.003055,0.007348,0.005725,0.005547]},{"p":"L35N8591-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"This fires at a token right before the model expects a capital-T/D word-start continuation, most often the 'T' in 'McClatchy-Tribune', a 'D' or 'd' beginning a name or word (Czech 'díky', Count Dooku), or the middle of a Japanese 処 character in code comments, and it fires weakly on ordinary text.","s":[0.888158,0.793797,0.294875,0.40087,1.007407,0.000545,0.009144,0.004285,0.00037,0.001549,0.002851,0.005229,0.006646,0.001593,0.012647]}],"check":{"stops":[383,358,313]}},{"key":"G4","name":"Gemma-3 4B","neurons":[{"p":"L00N169+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"A token-identity detector in layer 0 that fires on the single whole-word token \" Delaware\" (the US state, the river, or the Native people) in any context, and somewhat less on a few other specific whole-word tokens such as \" Alvarado\", \" Wyoming\", \" photographic\", \" tapered\", \" lymphoma\" and \" Graduate\".","s":[0.671692,0.732945,0.674829,0.692089,0.739055,0.311418,0.027374,0.026489,0.011149,0.038761,0.063069,0.061716,0.019366,0.017296,0.038012]},{"p":"L00N169-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the token \"ill\"/\"ell\" family pieces (still, Still, Mill, hell, hill, kill, till) when they appear as a middle piece glued inside a longer word or identifier rather than as a standalone word, e.g. Bet|hell, Mc|Mill|an, str|Mill|ones, image|Still, Nuts|hells, Ke|hill|ah, ind|still|inger, Summers|kill.","s":[0.902069,0.856699,0.836591,0.700387,0.886429,0.006844,0.241388,0.009205,0.03484,0.009895,0.008849,0.009902,0.013539,0.00598,0.035527]},{"p":"L00N1794+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the token \"Chow\"/\"chow\" (capitalized with leading space most strongly) in any use: Chandni Chowk and other place names, Chow Chow dogs, Chow Mein, clam or cheese chowder, Chowhound, and the surname Chow.","s":[0.796526,0.847492,0.756075,0.563425,0.956396,0.029807,0.006322,0.006116,0.06428,0.048339,0.005847,0.024047,0.002425,0.007758,0.014506]},{"p":"L00N1794-","l":"UNREL","j":"0000","f":0,"e":0,"x":"A layer-0 token detector that fires on a few specific token strings regardless of meaning: the word \"concur\" (as in \"I concur\" or \"we concur\"), the capitalized word \"Accent\" (as in \"British Accent\" or \"US English Accent\"), and the hidden byte pieces of scholarly transliteration letters with dots or lines under them, such as Ḳ, Ḏ or Ḥ in romanized Arabic like \"Ḳurʾān\" or \"al-Ḏj̲urd̲j̲ānī\".","s":[0.519533,0.648052,0.611191,0.845749,0.953144,0.022076,0.018484,0.028845,0.029352,0.174767,0.030273,0.030131,0.039546,0.017234,0.026776],"u":{"0":[0.498916,0.669472,0.601357,0.732064,0.680496],"1":[0.557072,0.719216,0.724892,0.589989,0.566893],"2":[0.754601,0.969299,0.038248,0.854126,0.741134],"3":[0.578744,0.440619,0.663259,0.717247,0.58042]}},{"p":"L00N2761+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the second word of stock promotional compound adjectives in business, sustainability or development prose, above all \"friendly\" in \"eco-friendly\" / \"environmentally friendly\", and also \"class\" in \"world-class\" (weaker: \"Smart City\", \"Technology Parks\").","s":[0.863531,0.859354,0.889758,0.887742,0.897943,0.15827,0.188477,0.221403,0.010542,0.046278,0.020935,0.013861,0.007042,0.011338,0.006087]},{"p":"L00N2761-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"A layer-0 bigram detector that fires on the head noun completing a fixed social/economic/legal compound term, chiefly \"work\" in \"social work\", \"Analysis\" in \"Economic/Financial Analysis\", \"Procedure\" in \"Criminal/Revenue Procedure\" (also \"social science\", \"Trade Negotiations\"), but not on these nouns after ordinary words.","s":[0.75347,0.611677,0.758037,0.604982,1.004816,0.01037,0.025154,0.00686,0.332418,0.092742,0.020293,0.013516,0.014363,0.012752,0.027389]},{"p":"L00N3150+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"In scholarly social-science, philosophy or religion prose, this pole fires on an abstract head noun such as state, level/levels, phenomenon, Sciences or capitals (or a piece like 'trans|figuration') that directly follows an adjective modifying it, as in 'the Jewish state', 'the global level', 'different levels', 'a cultural phenomenon', 'the Social Sciences' or 'economic and cultural capitals'.","s":[0.666018,0.094256,0.651138,0.826388,0.465686,0.08873,0.014134,0.091617,0.088538,0.078168,0.007084,0.01054,0.010017,0.016442,0.033767]},{"p":"L00N3150-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a capitalized cultural/religious/ethnic adjective (most often \" Muslim\", also \" Chinese\", \" Arabic\", \" Western\", \" Tibetan\") used attributively right before a noun like culture, thought, world, countries, communities, poetry or manuscripts, especially after words such as traditional, ancient, classical, many or in.","s":[0.417282,0.447952,0.561375,0.161586,0.489009,0.026487,0.071379,0.210917,0.097847,0.213557,0.0268,0.026162,0.014259,0.026161,0.025756]},{"p":"L00N419+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the token right after a run of two or more spaces, most strongly a blank-line paragraph break or an opening parenthesis that follows a sentence ending in a double space (e.g. \"...in 1671.  (Map courtesy...\" or \"...society.  \\n\\nConclusion\"); the same break or parenthesis after a single space or directly after text barely fires.","s":[0.564469,0.645487,0.534361,0.830146,0.495389,0.081808,0.039531,0.047432,0.480668,0.045789,0.046825,0.038369,0.03004,0.031061,0.036355]},{"p":"L00N419-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a tab character that immediately follows a space at the start of a line (a mixed space-then-tab indentation token, \" \\t\"), almost always in source code (JS, SQL, Python) where indentation mixes spaces and tabs; a plain tab or plain spaces will not do it.","s":[1.001908,0.956465,0.924638,0.993957,0.857387,0.004177,0.040915,0.004543,-0.000633,0.018148,0.002859,0.002462,0.00305,0.003983,0.002129]},{"p":"L00N5230+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word \"usually\" (lowercase, as a whole word mid-sentence, e.g. \"it's usually\", \"I usually go\", \"that is usually\"), regardless of topic; layer-0 token detector.","s":[0.892737,0.895345,0.932612,0.890878,0.908552,0.012485,0.089207,0.048467,0.355384,0.052072,0.140629,0.064319,0.05065,0.027179,0.008761]},{"p":"L00N5230-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the single token \" fostered\" (any sense: encouraged, or raising a foster child), and also on \" vastly\" and the surname \" Gans\"; these are token-identity detections in layer 0, and other forms like \"foster\", \"fostering\" or \"vast\" fire much less.","s":[0.845726,0.879073,0.815048,1.051859,0.865585,0.140673,0.615451,0.042895,0.206664,0.557306,0.15111,0.178633,0.065159,0.136866,0.031043]},{"p":"L00N6518+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the capitalized token \"Bart\" (with or without a leading space), almost always as the start of a name like Bartolomeo, Bartlett, Bartók, Bartlesville, Du Bartas, Bartimaeus, or the standalone name/abbreviation Bart (e.g. \"Bart Ehrman\", \"Sir P. Stewart, Bart.\"), regardless of the surrounding text.","s":[0.866776,0.014215,0.088761,0.803,0.815072,0.043324,0.012796,0.08056,0.023282,0.03674,0.016201,0.014026,0.009678,0.076608,0.006711]},{"p":"L00N6518-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the whole lowercase word token \" macroeconomic\" (with leading space), in any sentence about the economy, policy, or markets; it is a single-token detector for that word, and related words like \"economic\", \"macro\", \"Macroeconomic\" capitalized, or \"microeconomic\" should fire far less.","s":[0.884172,0.820978,0.859022,0.825194,0.859214,0.065781,0.337125,0.129331,0.144398,0.036079,0.066194,0.052194,0.110485,0.016296,0.020225]},{"p":"L00N770+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word \"modification\" itself (whole token, with or without a leading space or capital, including inside identifiers like modification_date, LastModification, decommodification), in any context: genetic, code, exercise, legal license text.","s":[0.920169,0.848926,0.936196,0.868303,0.972904,0.008688,-0.001639,0.710429,0.506975,0.721472,-0.002703,0.005306,-0.003481,0.002537,0.002228]},{"p":"L00N770-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"A token-identity neuron in the first layer whose strongest hits are a few specific tokens: the capitalized word \"Satellite\" (as in Global Navigation Satellite System or Japan Satellite Systems), the piece \"Datas\" that begins \"Datasheet\" or \"Datasette\", and the piece \"beaut\" that begins the split forms \"beautify\", \"beautific\" or archaic \"beautie\".","s":[0.918228,0.933344,0.948113,0.882448,0.930942,0.599065,0.393588,0.550776,0.39719,0.428259,0.329347,0.573391,0.393977,0.441514,0.484167]},{"p":"L00N8703+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the second word of a two-word proper name (mostly place names) where the first word is itself a familiar name or word, such as Keynes in Milton Keynes, North in Palmerston North, Norton in Brize Norton, Dynamite in Napoleon Dynamite, Plain in Jamaica Plain, Southern in Birmingham-Southern, or Log in Lincoln Log; the same word in ordinary use does not fire.","s":[0.823752,0.909229,0.916419,0.933604,0.853783,0.004054,0.00913,0.004449,0.007707,0.120324,0.008857,0.204258,0.006902,0.007999,0.003922]},{"p":"L00N8703-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the second word-piece that completes a word the tokenizer splits in two, above all the \"ah\" ending of a lowercase \"sarah\" (as in email addresses like sarah@site.com, usernames or lowercase gossip text). Weaker cases are lowercase \"steve\" or \"doug\" and rare words like \"braai\", \"vermin\", \"cassia\" or \"Geneva\"; capitalized \"Sarah\" written as one whole token should not fire.","s":[0.983444,0.962968,1.128193,1.012029,1.075821,0.020133,0.471653,0.009073,0.023013,0.015115,0.131978,0.019153,0.015865,0.023825,0.020155]},{"p":"L01N28+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word piece right after a split-off leading \"Al\"/\"al\" fragment, i.e. the second subword of a rare word beginning with Al: strongest on \"vin\" in the name Alvin when written at a line start or without a leading space (e.g. a byline \"Alvin Lim\", a cast list \"Alvin Dewey\"), and also on \"msg\" in almsgiving, \"umin\" in Aluminio, \"UM\" in 866-3-ALUMNI, \"MS\" in ALMS, and \"ums\" in Alums. It does not fire on common words where Al is not split off.","s":[0.892418,0.93279,0.872972,0.903224,0.625305,0.008292,0.006854,0.007344,0.009608,0.013549,0.003255,0.012322,0.010249,0.002307,0.028111]},{"p":"L01N28-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word \"over\" when it directly follows \"all\" in the phrase \"all over\" (all over the world, all over the place, all over again, jump all over someone), in any kind of prose; \"over\" without a preceding \"all\" should fire much less.","s":[1.084866,0.898319,0.840928,0.895485,0.887031,0.004987,0.003301,0.005295,0.00739,0.000669,0.004932,0.007313,0.003397,0.006196,0.007791]},{"p":"L01N2838+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the first sub-word piece (such as \"O\", \"Ok\", \"Oy\", \"Aj\", \"Nd\", \"Ony\") of a Nigerian (mostly Yoruba or Igbo) surname or place name that is broken into several tokens, typically right after a first name in Nigerian news prose (e.g. \"Yemi Osinbajo\", \"Gboyega Oyewole\", \"Ifeanyi Okowa\"); common words starting with the same letters and whole-token names do not fire it.","s":[0.914847,0.861487,0.717994,0.945627,0.737635,0.015055,0.003688,0.010029,0.604105,0.008791,0.008139,0.00263,0.022736,0.010599,0.012123]},{"p":"L01N2838-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word \"such\" (as in \"such a problem\", \"such incidents\", \"no such deal\") when the surrounding text is Nigerian news or opinion prose that mentions Nigeria, Lagos, Abuja, Nigerian officials, agencies or names; \"such\" in non-Nigerian text fires much more weakly, and Nigerian text without \"such\" barely fires.","s":[0.780886,0.744687,0.860885,0.832577,0.854269,0.364579,0.316078,0.195822,0.287743,0.295621,0.005875,0.010439,0.009798,0.012387,0.018771]},{"p":"L01N4035+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word-piece that completes a British, Irish or Channel-coast French place name, most strongly the second piece of a multi-token town name such as Boulogne, Portsmouth, Chelmsford or Killarney, and more weakly on whole UK place or institution words like Kent, Lincoln, Irish Examiner or the Revenue.","s":[0.974907,0.774587,0.779153,0.59421,0.902691,0.023301,0.025906,0.058538,0.023969,0.030564,0.112258,0.010832,0.010221,0.020528,0.007364]},{"p":"L01N4035-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"In British English text about UK places, fires on distinctively British local tokens: the first digit of a UK road number after \"A\" or \"M\" (A34, A487, M602), the digit or district letters of a UK postcode (BS8, M30, G4), and the planning or estate-agent word \"amenity/amenities\" (as in \"local amenities\", \"private amenity space\"), plus British place-name words such as Croft, Hoe and Miners.","s":[0.979433,0.795812,0.901198,0.742662,1.029701,0.047053,0.091158,0.037928,0.015439,0.022756,0.021803,0.017025,0.012901,0.010445,0.017228]},{"p":"L01N5562+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word \"other\" when it completes the reciprocal phrase \"each other\" (e.g., \"influence each other\", \"learn from each other\", \"each other's\"), in any topic or genre.","s":[0.809096,0.83685,0.826612,0.889118,0.875527,0.619108,0.570503,0.001379,0.000812,0.003088,0.003309,0.001896,0.0008,0.000228,0.000663]},{"p":"L01N5562-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the noun or word right after distributive \"each\" (or Portuguese/Spanish \"cada\"), above all \"time\" in \"each time\", also \"morning\" in \"each morning\" and \"every\"/\"everyone\" in \"each and every\", marking the start of a repeated-occasion phrase.","s":[0.793578,0.734116,0.757781,0.518188,0.793578,0.22554,0.004126,0.00851,0.01843,0.01487,0.008278,0.002597,0.016454,0.002986,0.014617]},{"p":"L01N5729+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word right after a form of \"to be\" (or \"make\") when that word is \"sure\", \"certain\" or \"appointed\", as in \"I'm sure\", \"You're sure?\", \"be certain\", \"making certain\", \"he was appointed\" or \"has been appointed\".","s":[0.472789,0.49497,0.573792,0.371539,0.463019,0.104718,0.023214,0.01705,0.130435,0.112547,0.044396,0.023621,0.016066,0.017936,0.008242]},{"p":"L01N5729-","l":"TWO-REL","j":"11","f":0,"e":0,"x":"A token-level detector in layer 1 that fires on the words 'click', 'visit' and 'check' (any capitalization, strongest as call-to-action imperatives like 'CLICK HERE for photos', 'Visit us at www…', 'Check out their website'), and also on the run of leading spaces that indents a sub-item such as 'a.' in an agenda, minutes or program listing.","s":[0.016884,0.009095,0.025978,0.207096,0.139724,0.014708,0.008739,0.030237,0.188382,0.1479,0.028543,0.003948,0.052568,0.066627,0.017353]},{"p":"L01N6867+","l":"TWO-REL","j":"11","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires in informal or non-native English prose on clause-joining tokens: most reliably the word \"especially\" (also \"actually\", \"literally\", \"even\") used to tack on an emphasis clause, a comma typed with a space before it (\"word , word\"), and doubled punctuation or emoji such as \"!!\" or \"😭😭\".","s":[0.824411,0.486452,0.835163,0.633075,0.846785,0.019137,0.135786,0.117888,0.171805,0.015573,0.017866,0.023034,0.031971,0.029897,0.015046]},{"p":"L01N6867-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"The word \"few\" used bare, without the article \"a\", in non-native (often Indian) English, as in \"after few minutes\", \"here are few things\" or \"I have few questions\"; standard \"a few\" should fire much less.","s":[0.514133,0.411957,0.45482,0.839749,0.865003,0.084932,0.020844,0.302027,0.050078,0.305167,0.030494,0.018234,0.014086,0.012117,0.052199]},{"p":"L01N8351+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on C-language code tokens, most strongly the type keyword \"int\" (in parameter lists, declarations, casts like (int), and int main), plus nearby C syntax such as the comment opener /*, the statement-ending semicolon after a /* ... */ directive, and operators and small numbers inside C expressions, especially when C code is quoted in prose or forum/email text.","s":[0.544804,0.178286,0.545639,0.341958,0.679792,0.054371,0.01593,0.05076,0.006706,0.200814,0.000804,0.001965,0.006001,0.001023,0.004099]},{"p":"L01N8351-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the opening-quote-plus-percent token (\"%, usually as (\"% or  \"%) that starts a format string right after printf, scanf or fprintf in C-style code, i.e. the moment a printf/scanf format specifier string begins.","s":[0.944279,0.927384,0.745575,0.495479,0.793194,0.125453,0.392745,0.132605,0.004613,0.209035,0.010489,0.012493,0.011469,0.003083,0.001862]},{"p":"L01N8779+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a word-internal piece (not the first token) of a German, or occasionally Danish or Icelandic, word that the tokenizer splits up, typically a vowel or diphthong fragment such as ä, u, eu, ei, ich or iche right after the word's opening letters (Eichendorff, Teufel, Säugetiere, Osteuropa, Norddeich, Kriegsstärke, Wirtschaftspädagogik), often in German names or compounds embedded in English text; whole common words and English words do not fire it.","s":[0.537893,0.719712,0.612369,0.430812,0.110401,0.009431,0.0017,0.013267,0.009109,0.007061,0.01012,0.014629,0.01648,0.008799,0.014615]},{"p":"L01N8779-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the opening double-quote token that starts a string literal in C-family code, especially the fused open-paren-plus-quote token (\"(\\\"\" as in Console.WriteLine(\"...\"), String.Format(\"{0}\", builder.Append(\"...\")) and also a bare or space-prefixed opening quote, mostly in C#; it fires on the quote that opens the string, not the closing quote or the string's text.","s":[0.435461,0.521855,0.756733,0.412548,0.39949,0.016891,0.000451,0.003828,0.203877,-0.000107,0.008557,0.008398,0.008915,0.007707,0.015107]},{"p":"L01N9569+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on ordinary content and function words in the middle of non-English prose that reads like machine translation of English web text (Indonesian or Korean online-lottery and gambling spam, or Chinese and Spanish translations of English passages, often with English terms mixed in), most strongly on the Indonesian word 'peluang' (chance or odds) in phrases like 'meningkatkan peluang Anda untuk menang'.","s":[0.269019,0.339481,0.186915,0.424424,0.171234,0.112315,0.01729,0.019551,0.102738,0.023612,0.010066,0.010386,0.003865,0.009678,0.008389]},{"p":"L01N9569-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on personal pronouns and person words in non-English, often machine-translated prose, most strongly on Korean '당신' (you), Japanese 'あなたは' or '私は' (you / I), Indonesian 'orang' or 'seseorang' (person / someone), Bengali 'আপনি' and Chinese '我认为'; it also fires more weakly on other common function words in such text, and not on English pronouns.","s":[0.643188,0.656919,0.210927,0.38692,0.496264,0.028506,0.011574,0.002826,0.00451,0.007725,0.009121,0.009677,0.006599,0.009573,0.001332]},{"p":"L02N226+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the second word-piece of a word that the tokenizer splits right after an initial \"De\" or \"Da\", typically where a vowel follows (Da|ewoo, De|ebo, de|ification, dai|v/dei|v in Sanskrit-style transliterations, DA|ISY, DE|IS), so it marks the continuation fragment of rare De-/Da- words such as Daewoo, Deebo Samuel, deification, daivam, Daibouken, not common whole words.","s":[0.626127,0.693822,0.662515,1.08801,0.651391,0.00655,0.006741,0.002197,0.003267,0.005086,0.007102,0.003101,0.009684,0.005876,0.003598]},{"p":"L02N226-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word piece that follows an opening \"Dent\"/\"dent\", \"Dest\" or \"De(S)\" inside a longer word the tokenizer splits, most strongly the \"in\"/\"ine\" of dentin/dentine (tooth tissue) and the \"in\" of Destin/Destinies/Destinatum, and also the \"S\"/\"antis\" of DeSantis; common whole-token words like \"Destiny\" or \"dentist\" should not fire.","s":[0.735523,0.809368,0.09499,0.757475,0.741281,0.106298,0.320273,0.003023,0.003579,0.002144,0.001882,0.013365,0.001459,0.003081,0.003324]},{"p":"L02N289+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the digit 9 (occasionally 8) when it is the third digit of a number, immediately after a two-digit start like 42, 40, 50 or 51, as in HTTP error 429, DOI prefixes 10.5194 or 10.4081, version 4096, or address 509; it does not fire on 9 standing alone or on numbers like 427.","s":[1.084845,0.891071,0.628922,0.718592,0.831626,0.720948,0.648033,0.072076,0.519712,0.402223,0.009667,0.005922,0.010069,0.007253,0.004036]},{"p":"L02N289-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the digit 5 when it comes right after a 4 inside a multi-digit number, i.e. the '5' of sequences like 445, 545, 1445, 4457 (port 445, PO Box 545, the year 1445, phone numbers containing 445), in any genre.","s":[0.936184,0.819239,0.819552,0.796335,0.671441,0.344157,0.515092,0.081498,0.484338,0.285204,0.006879,0.003588,0.00254,0.007002,0.003786]},{"p":"L02N3067+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a concessive subordinating conjunction — \"Although\", \"Though\", \"While\" (in its contrastive sense) — when it opens a new clause, most strongly as the first word of a sentence or right after \"And\" or a comma, in expository prose where the clause sets up a contrast that the main clause will answer.","s":[0.0001,0.602348,0.470823,0.006164,0.002545,0.000103,0.002575,6.3e-05,0.006164,0.002536,0.001854,0.000857,0.005066,0.001657,0.002811]},{"p":"L02N3067-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the lowercase mid-sentence word \" while\" (occasionally \" whilst\"), most strongly in the noun idioms \"worth my/your/their while\" and \"a (good) long while\" and in error messages like \"An error occurred while retrieving data\"; other time words such as \"when\" or \"during\" do not fire it.","s":[0.741889,0.758896,0.725515,0.56811,0.693673,0.005261,0.001183,0.011409,0.001979,0.021483,0.005467,0.007098,0.062892,0.006811,0.012814]},{"p":"L02N4128+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word piece right after a capital or lowercase \"E\"/\"Er\"/\"er\" at the start of a word, i.e. the second token of a word split as E+…, such as Er|f|ahren, Er|f|ani, Erf(x), E|rika, E|FA, E|CA|I, Er|horn, so the job is to continue a word that began with a bare \"E\" or \"Er\" piece, most strongly the German \"erfahr-\" family (erfahren, Erfahrungen, erfahrene) and \"Erf\".","s":[0.997438,0.006134,0.704789,0.552812,0.892663,0.004881,0.006303,0.012043,0.012808,0.009013,0.017365,0.002462,0.011192,0.022401,0.002539]},{"p":"L02N4128-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a few specific tokens that sit where the article \"the\" would normally precede them, mainly sentence-initial \"Fortunately\" (especially the garbled \"Fortunately is that…\", meaning \"the good news is\") and sentence-initial \"Both\", plus \"words\" or \"town\" used without \"the\" (\"in words of Forbes\", \"you'll hear words 'sales funnel'\", \"services from town\"), typically in spun SEO web prose.","s":[0.052923,0.002457,0.003839,0.001432,0.147458,0.056649,0.002621,0.007149,0.001738,0.016827,0.002966,0.007229,0.001058,0.00193,0.002947]},{"p":"L02N4327+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the capitalized token \"God\" immediately after the article \"the\" (\"the God of Israel\", \"the God who made us\", \"the God of heaven\", also \"the Godhead\", \"the God-hating\"), in religious or biblical prose; bare \"God\" without a preceding \"the\" fires far less.","s":[0.664725,0.907821,0.611112,0.739493,0.576579,0.003641,-0.000839,0.008395,0.007431,0.016597,0.014079,0.083131,0.008919,0.024043,0.060956]},{"p":"L02N4327-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a noun immediately after the definite article \"the\"/\"The\" when the two words name a single well-known referent, strongest by far for \"the Universe\"/\"the universe\"/\"the cosmos\", and also for fixed phrases like \"against the spread\", \"inside the box\", \"before the wind\", \"to the zoo\" and sports team names such as \"The Lakers\" or \"The Steelers\".","s":[0.610507,0.794949,0.732775,0.686149,0.626911,0.018259,0.019105,0.090348,0.350558,0.079844,0.055088,0.006371,0.076853,0.019979,0.22716]},{"p":"L02N4924+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on an action verb that is being requested or instructed, usually the bare verb right after \"to\", \"please\" or \"can\" in website or how-to instructions, by far most strongly on \"log\" in \"log in\" (\"click an icon to log in\", \"please log in to your account\"), and also on \"confirm\", \"plan\", \"practice\" or \"Round\" in such directions and on imperative verbs in Chinese or Russian task instructions; it does not fire on \"log\" as a noun (a wooden log, a ship's log).","s":[0.485401,0.567836,0.522547,0.294534,0.289031,0.071607,0.061053,0.077783,0.104747,0.138706,0.01351,0.019343,0.005745,0.027089,0.025798]},{"p":"L02N4924-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the preposition \" from\" or \" at\" immediately after a date (or date-plus-time), as in an APA-style citation's \"Retrieved March 24, 2019, from\" or an event/funeral notice's \"will be held Friday, Oct. 3, at\", i.e. the word that links a stated date to a following source or venue.","s":[0.819018,0.796274,0.773571,0.804144,0.713472,0.371002,0.458695,0.387618,0.303678,0.10186,0.106932,0.022637,0.008731,0.011588,0.043679]},{"p":"L02N54+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the continuation word-piece (second token) of a rare, mostly non-English proper noun that the tokenizer splits after a short capitalized opening fragment, very often starting with K or P, such as the 'ja' in Ikeja, the 'ono' in Pocono, the 'ue' in Kafue, the 'kani' in Konkani, the 'umi' in Koizumi or the 'i' in Pcoi; common names that are single tokens and the first piece of a word do not fire.","s":[0.817276,0.890691,0.735022,0.873798,0.781217,0.004697,0.023982,0.021434,0.021536,0.024967,0.023348,0.009117,0.011843,0.012371,0.011352]},{"p":"L02N54-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the Arabic verb 'to be' (كان and its conjugations كانت, كنت, يكن, including the vowel-mark pieces of كَانَ) inside Arabic sentences, and much more weakly on Latin-script echoes of the same sound such as 'kun', 'kan' or 'kāna'.","s":[0.807617,0.425498,0.748277,0.65546,0.795204,0.002766,0.005058,0.015795,0.014878,0.001451,0.001231,0.003123,0.002956,0.004257,0.001178]},{"p":"L02N5540+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word piece \"or\"/\"ors\"/\"orm\" right after \"Ten\"/\"ten\"/\"TEN\" when \"tenor\" is split into two tokens, as in Tenor voice parts, tenor saxophone, tenor clef, Tenors, the Tenor GIF app and TENORM, in any context.","s":[0.184253,0.071842,0.095103,0.707347,0.960373,0.012689,0.007461,0.006984,0.007061,0.013072,0.006315,0.010363,0.00178,0.003998,0.0054]},{"p":"L02N5540-","l":"TWO-REL","j":"11","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word right after a form of \"pull\" (pull/pulls/pulled/pulling): above all \" on\" in \"pulls on her jacket / pulling on his hair\" and \" factor\" in \"pull factor\" (push–pull tourism models), with weaker offshoots on middle pieces of words starting \"pod\"/\"pind\" (e.g. Polish \"Podróży\", \"Pindeldyboz\") and \"tf.decode\".","s":[0.856044,0.716102,0.735595,0.659832,0.598508,0.026103,0.307984,0.093076,0.020858,0.005678,0.009493,0.021418,0.018153,0.007135,0.012402]},{"p":"L02N822+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the second token of the informal abbreviation \"Pls\"/\"Plz\" (the s or z right after \"Pl\") at the start of a polite request, as in \"Pls help!!\", \"Plz check PMB\", \"Pls contact our sales\", and weakly on misspelled \"pleas\"; the full word \"please\" or lowercase-free contexts without the Pl+s/z split do not fire.","s":[0.908112,0.660228,0.908112,0.660228,0.908112,0.057755,0.003516,0.000487,0.004412,0.01065,0.001711,0.001793,0.002636,0.002062,0.003158]},{"p":"L02N822-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Token-shape detector: fires on the continuation piece right after a short word-initial fragment built around the letter P (Ep, Apt, Ple, PT, P, Ap, MPI), i.e. the second sub-token of a rare word, name or acronym such as \"Epiphone\" (the \"iphone\" piece), \"aptamer\", \"Plecoptera\", \"PTA\", \"Ptera\", \"MPIA\", \"apta.org\" or \"Apter\". It does not fire on common P-words that are a single token, such as \"people\", \"apple\" or \"place\".","s":[0.718302,0.725115,0.911676,0.686924,0.849878,0.02165,0.007609,0.005311,0.012409,0.010513,0.008689,0.019653,0.008138,0.027705,0.013186]},{"p":"L03N10039+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word that completes a fixed two-part name or idiom whose first word is \"full\", \"whole\", \"global\" or \"all\", above all \"House\" in \"Full House\" (the sitcom, a poker full house, a full house of attendees, \"the full house\" of a legislature, \"Committee of the Whole House\", \"Whole House DVR\"), and also \"200\" in \"Billboard Global 200\" and \"curity\" in \"globalsecurity\".","s":[0.980169,0.782389,0.796886,0.822804,0.733679,0.006244,0.006308,0.01081,0.01419,0.005645,0.015766,0.006502,0.014879,0.010025,0.02301]},{"p":"L03N10039-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the completing word piece of a 'broad, everywhere' expression: 'around' or 'round' immediately after 'all' or 'all-' (all-around, all-round, all around the world), and the 'ely' piece of a split 'Widely' or 'wid-ely', especially capitalized at the start of a sentence ('Widely regarded as…'), or the pieces of an all-caps WIDESPREAD.","s":[0.440625,0.410256,0.254472,0.367492,0.995785,0.001127,0.001122,0.005767,0.002361,0.00165,0.00162,0.001386,0.002416,0.001109,0.001689]},{"p":"L03N10205+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the verb 'go' (occasionally 'slide') when it completes a 'let [object] go' construction, i.e. 'let it go', 'let him go', 'let those leftovers go to waste', where an object sits between 'let' and 'go', in any sense: release, forgive, fire someone, stop dwelling.","s":[0.676675,0.658018,0.711608,0.53981,0.92361,0.010285,0.00454,0.255158,0.000746,0.316128,0.000299,0.000217,0.00143,0.000154,0.000863]},{"p":"L03N10205-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the digit \"2\" that completes the numbers \"802\" and \"1002\" when they are split as \"80\"+\"2\" and \"100\"+\"2\": almost always the IEEE 802.x networking standard number (\"IEEE 802.11ac\", \"802.3af\") or the Wiley DOI prefix \"10.1002/\" in a citation.","s":[0.902435,0.65625,0.887532,0.703539,0.900584,0.001688,0.001307,0.288701,0.001982,0.012717,0.002364,0.005953,0.003477,0.002116,0.003914]},{"p":"L03N3885+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the inner word-pieces of the names Zoroaster / Zoroastrian(ism) / Zarathustra (strongest on the \"ast\" in Zoro-ast-rian and the \"ath\" or \"ust\" in Zar-ath-ustra), i.e. completing the spelling of the Persian prophet's name, regardless of topic or genre.","s":[0.864863,0.961845,0.961327,0.546875,0.889009,0.004089,0.002065,0.002699,0.00204,0.003075,0.002402,0.002533,0.001825,0.004813,0.003523]},{"p":"L03N3885-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word \"driven\" (or \"led\") directly after \"data\" in the compound \"data-driven\" / \"Data-Driven\" / \"Data Driven\" (any casing, hyphen or space), and weakly on the token right after \"System.Data\" in C# using lines; \"driven\" after any other word or \"data\" alone should not fire.","s":[0.751945,0.953195,0.814441,0.818197,0.762915,0.090779,0.062952,0.091104,0.043222,0.016747,0.007312,0.004288,0.004793,0.00491,0.000852]},{"p":"L03N3925+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the final suffix piece of a rare proper name or term that the tokenizer splits into fragments, most strongly 'ieu' completing \"Montesquieu\" and 'ka'/'a'/'ska' completing Slavic surnames and place names (Modjeska, Cichocka, Skowronska, Trajkovska, Skopska); it also fires on 'ain' or 'el' inside \"bromelain\", but not on common whole words.","s":[0.771412,0.396504,0.432483,0.497687,0.894959,0.006391,0.002302,0.002061,0.006319,0.001604,0.001537,0.008741,0.001293,0.004032,0.00315]},{"p":"L03N3925-","l":"TWO-REL","j":"11","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the second token piece of Eastern European (Polish/Belarusian/Russian) place names split into pieces, chiefly \"zig\" in Danzig, plus \"rest\" in Brest, \"insk\" in Minsk, \"k\" in -sk towns like Vitebsk/Kursk, and on \"M\"/\".:\"/\".,\" in the Russian bibliographic city abbreviation \"M.:\" or \"M.,\" (Moscow), i.e. completing an East European city name.","s":[0.558381,0.376024,0.554743,0.627339,0.56518,0.171988,0.002173,0.000749,0.015813,0.023897,0.00166,0.004346,0.000494,0.000568,0.001351]},{"p":"L03N4716+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word-piece that completes the \"board\" family of words, mainly the \"ing\" right after a capitalized \"Board\" (Boarding passes, Boarding School, Boarding kennels), the token \" onboard\" or \"board\" in \"on-board\"/\"Onboard\" (onboard storage, onboard meals, onboarding a new hire), and \"ed\" after \"board\"/\"onboard\", plus \"aboard\".","s":[0.88664,0.621732,0.726873,0.343799,0.518788,0.017885,0.000187,0.003802,0.003365,0.001583,0.000836,0.001033,0.000287,0.003696,5.4e-05]},{"p":"L03N4716-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the token \"board\" (or \"Board\") when it closes a fixed compound or name whose first part comes right before it, such as Conference Board, bulletin board, across-the-board, over the board, blackboard and storyboard, and also on the similar noun \"deck\"; its output then promotes \"board\".","s":[0.176687,0.758592,0.689564,0.020396,0.470708,0.016511,0.001571,0.005864,0.019528,0.002591,0.009271,0.001274,0.001003,0.020956,0.002378]},{"p":"L03N6660+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the optional-plural marker \"s\" or \"S\" inside parentheses right after a person or role noun, as in form fields, credits and notices such as \"Author(s):\", \"Applicant(s)\", \"Voice actor(s):\", \"Thesis supervisor(s)\" or \"copyright owner(s)\". It fires more weakly on other tokens that extend a noun for people, such as \"of\" after a plural people noun or an inclusive suffix.","s":[0.182042,0.287539,0.935879,0.681424,0.729008,0.176765,0.167992,0.473959,0.313257,0.299411,0.068248,0.113589,0.176596,0.241313,0.118594]},{"p":"L03N6660-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word right after a short sentence-initial noun or compound-noun subject, usually its verb (\"Dental Clinic is\", \"Liver biopsy plays\", \"Dock leveler plays a role\", \"Police Station is required\", \"Radio won't\"), especially when the subject is a bare, article-less product, place or institution name in explanatory or SEO-style prose.","s":[0.89411,0.549962,0.407579,0.54626,0.745399,0.129252,0.023312,0.196102,0.053424,0.112007,0.007773,0.025975,0.093527,0.01865,0.080181]},{"p":"L03N7018+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the second word-piece of a multi-token proper name or model code that begins with a capital T, such as Tiberius and Tiberias (on 'ius' and 'ias'), Tianjin, Tianshan, Tangshan, Tientsin and Tishri, or product codes like T540 and SIP-T58V (on the digit after 'T5'). It completes the 'T…' fragment rather than firing on the first piece or on ordinary words.","s":[0.506665,0.957906,0.52976,0.793762,0.54242,0.010956,-4e-06,0.007543,0.003793,0.00603,0.004541,0.008314,0.011018,0.004974,0.003998]},{"p":"L03N7018-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word piece that completes the words \"tender\" or \"yield\" when the tokenizer splits them, such as capitalized \"Tender\" at the start of a line or sentence (Tender pancakes..., Tender Care), \"Tendermint\", \"tendering\", all-caps \"YIELD\" or misspelled \"yeild\", and on the colon right after a recipe's \"Yield\" header (Yield: 8 servings).","s":[0.834815,0.74179,0.786008,1.097366,0.860309,0.001728,0.002283,0.004772,0.000549,0.001363,0.004249,0.010184,0.003402,0.002517,0.001688]},{"p":"L03N8239+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the second word of a fixed, name-like two-word compound, where a modifier such as an adjective or verb makes the next word almost certain: the token right after the first word, as in 'Secure Element' or 'Secure Socket' in hardware-wallet/SSL prose, 'Dead Space', 'The Good Dinosaur', and C# identifiers like InvalidOperationException, UnauthorizedAccessException and NotifyPropertyChanged; the same second word used on its own does not fire.","s":[0.779105,0.524492,0.575762,0.593545,1.054936,0.011447,0.017142,0.004482,0.111641,0.009655,0.011352,0.013998,0.005904,0.037096,0.007095]},{"p":"L03N8239-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the adjective/adverb suffix piece (-ive, -ative, -tive, -ively) that completes the words \"preventive/preventative\" and \"preemptive/pre-emptively\" (i.e. the ending after the stem prevent- or preempt-/pre-empt-), in any topic, e.g. \"preventive maintenance\", \"preventative care\", \"preemptive strike\", \"pre-emptively block\".","s":[0.37073,0.566711,0.779633,0.873294,0.617981,0.070829,0.182837,0.005685,0.007887,0.009709,0.009538,0.005374,0.005289,0.001982,0.004851]},{"p":"L03N9728+","l":"TWO-REL","j":"11","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the keyword \"class\" when it starts a line at the top level of a Python source file and opens the file's first class definition, especially right after the import block or module docstring (as in \"import os\\n\\nclass Node:\"). It fires more weakly on a CJK full stop \"。\" inside a code comment or docstring.","s":[0.730626,0.714199,0.599824,0.742315,0.719701,0.434901,0.2302,0.059858,0.421686,0.201423,0.010545,0.018341,0.070525,0.07947,0.076551]},{"p":"L03N9728-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the sentence-final period (or closing period-quote) that ends a full declarative sentence in ordinary English prose or a list item, especially at the end of a line or paragraph before a line break; it is a generic end-of-sentence marker found in almost any text.","s":[0.096898,0.11999,0.263775,0.160826,0.147869,0.036312,0.065506,0.015428,0.019873,0.017037,0.023706,0.019209,0.01643,0.019321,0.01205]},{"p":"L04N3175+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word \"sigmoid\" (and its piece \"moid\" in \"Sigmoid\") as the name of the logistic activation function, mostly in neural-network code such as activation='sigmoid' or tf.nn.sigmoid, with weaker offshoots on related squashing-function names like softplus, tanh, expit and \"Logistic\".","s":[0.825587,0.81813,0.408984,0.800599,0.458607,0.173615,0.325674,0.077229,0.025822,0.057002,0.003281,0.008014,0.007,0.00344,0.002842]},{"p":"L04N3175-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"In Python scientific code, fires on the method or function name right after a library-module dot, overwhelmingly \"show\" in plt.show() (also tf.placeholder, np.true_divide, funcs.subM, W.sub), i.e. the attribute token right after a module/object dot in a library call.","s":[0.920108,0.815046,0.216892,0.915729,0.749398,0.024651,0.030183,0.021302,0.072802,0.142463,0.009711,0.018667,0.002441,0.020754,0.015755]},{"p":"L04N3842+","l":"TWO-REL","j":"11","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the key word of stock phrases about a person's inner disposition or self-description, most strongly on \"courage\" in \"had/gathered/worked up the courage to\", on \"interested\" in the web boilerplate \"You might also be interested in\", and on the hyphen right after \"self\" in self-compounds like self-described, self-conscious or self-proclaimed (also \"feel\" in \"feel free\" and \"skilled\" in \"is skilled at\").","s":[0.90246,0.774687,0.4914,1.137256,0.620332,0.122559,0.092348,0.17684,0.022488,0.223758,0.017266,0.017262,0.001334,0.039464,0.029177]},{"p":"L04N3842-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on an abstract quality or skill noun being credited to (or found lacking in) a performer — an athlete, team, singer, actor, ensemble or loudspeaker — in an evaluative description, especially in lists like \"speed, explosiveness and strength\" or \"great vision and feel\" (e.g. command, physicality, vision, instincts, presence, diction, rhythm, chemistry, explosiveness), firing on that quality word itself.","s":[0.747313,0.931998,0.788558,0.384666,0.89486,0.052561,0.014681,0.069507,0.014181,0.03832,0.0165,0.011923,0.010764,0.011636,0.022791]},{"p":"L04N4329+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word \"bikini\"/\"bikinis\" itself (whether written whole as \" bikini\" or as the ending \"ini\"/\"inis\" after \"Bik\"/\"bik\"), in any context — fashion, beach, film titles, adult spam — and predicts swim/beach words next.","s":[0.465272,0.831833,0.600534,0.545364,1.059947,0.326664,0.544925,0.512183,0.013949,0.007065,0.003283,0.005362,0.003628,0.002752,0.001301]},{"p":"L04N4329-","l":"UNREL","j":"00","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word \"blouse\" (or \"blouses\", including when it is split as \"Bl\"+\"ouse\") when it names a garment someone wears, as in \"a white satin blouse and a black skirt\"; it fires more weakly on a unit mark right after a number, such as the inch sign in 12\" or \"cm\" in \"25 cm\".","s":[0.662684,0.560495,0.283813,0.387253,0.321788,0.272772,0.148554,0.027908,0.332901,0.042981,0.036255,0.010666,0.006179,0.017592,0.011498],"u":{"0":[0.6673,0.556967,0.421182,0.504439,0.716264],"1":[0.734867,0.456435,0.257367,0.501852,0.587751]}},{"p":"L04N4973+","l":"TWO-REL","j":"11","f":0,"e":0,"x":"In code files, fires on the first chunk of a long run of dashes or equals signs that begins a comment divider line right after the comment marker (e.g. \"//----------------\", \"#================\", \"/*======\"), especially in file headers and section separators; weaker on an opening parenthesis glued directly to a keyword like \"foreach(\" or \"if(\".","s":[0.626969,0.794154,0.62659,0.036566,0.524293,0.030233,0.041772,0.034446,0.023527,0.041309,0.031023,0.030089,0.030939,0.017151,0.036058]},{"p":"L04N4973-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a token that continues a sequence already seen earlier in the same text, a copy or induction position in repetitive, templated material. Typical spots are the \"(\" in a repeated \"cos(n\" pattern, the \"=@@\" in repeated SQL SET lines, the second \"pady\" or \"set\" in repeated code, or the first digit of a number in a repeated citation, price or list format (\"Turshen, 2001 ... Turshen, 2001\", \"Rs. 25,000 ... Rs. 25,000\").","s":[0.152626,0.243193,0.281585,0.148271,-0.001019,0.108737,0.031338,0.166148,0.014796,-0.002302,0.139196,-0.001862,-0.002369,-0.003237,-0.002891]},{"p":"L04N5375+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a finite verb, usually an auxiliary, modal or copula (is/are/was/will/can, ist/sind/wird/kann/will/muss, is/wilt/kunt/werd), when it comes late in its clause right after an adverb or the rest of the clause, as in German or Dutch verb-final subordinate clauses ('…dass du die Wahl hast', 'als u mee wilt helpen') or English adverb-before-auxiliary order ('its share only is 1.2%', 'which first was rolled out', 'the way things simply are').","s":[0.441877,0.530373,0.814621,0.196348,0.790497,0.270897,0.081369,0.299137,0.035095,0.01165,0.011002,0.015436,0.004511,0.010052,0.030746]},{"p":"L04N5375-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"In German, Dutch, Afrikaans or Scandinavian text (often a short passage embedded in English pages or code comments), a short function word such as zu, mit, in, im, es, noch, nog, nie, ikke, du or het that comes immediately after a verb, especially after an infinitive or a clause-final verb (\"sehen zu lassen\", \"ist mit\", \"verrät es\", \"kan ikke\", \"word nie\").","s":[1.019883,0.500021,0.889785,1.622515,0.494276,0.005484,0.015687,0.10739,0.016428,0.101709,0.011219,0.001891,0.006928,0.003273,0.00666]},{"p":"L04N7359+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word \"amateur\" (whole token \" Amateur\"/\" amateur\", or the \"ateur\" piece after \"Am\"/\"am\") in any sense: amateur sports bodies, amateur radio, amateur astronomers or scientists, amateur vs. elite athletes, porn tags; rarely on similar French-derived words like \"Plein Air\".","s":[0.803633,0.670824,0.5288,1.039971,0.567702,0.092432,0.010176,0.288477,0.105426,0.018536,0.015873,0.011827,0.00905,0.005827,0.006718]},{"p":"L04N7359-","l":"TWO-REL","j":"11","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word \"dressage\" (strongest, in any horse-riding sentence), and more weakly on the final token that completes the name of a niche sport or hobby or a specific product model, such as roller \"derby\", \"curling\", geo\"caching\", \"lacrosse\", or a car or part model code like 300ZX or Bullitt.","s":[0.739251,0.890147,0.869444,0.809433,0.880555,0.343601,0.192304,0.054667,0.28907,0.176185,0.004626,0.00575,0.008335,0.010604,0.01348]},{"p":"L04N746+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the second half of a compound whose first element is 'home' or 'hand' meaning done personally rather than by an institution or machine: the token after 'home' in homeschool/homeschooling/home educated/homemade, and the token after 'hand' in hand-rolled/handspun/hand-select/hand-write/hand-tune (often on the hyphen itself).","s":[0.863465,0.897094,0.918393,0.734911,0.835015,0.045116,0.219201,0.035007,0.106831,0.007376,0.002757,0.00122,0.003225,0.005941,0.051364]},{"p":"L04N746-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word right after \"Self-\" / \"self-\" in a hyphenated self-compound, overwhelmingly \"determination\" (as in Self-Determination Theory or a people's right to self-determination), and more weakly on other self-compounds such as Self-Defense and Self-Help.","s":[0.805653,0.737642,0.738855,0.968823,0.614943,0.004279,0.00087,0.00157,0.009795,0.004935,0.020216,0.021533,0.01382,0.021205,0.028587]},{"p":"L04N8650+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on words naming upper-body knit garments such as shirt, T-shirt, jersey, sweater, jumper, and Spanish camiseta or camisa, and fires most strongly on the later word pieces when the word is split (the \"IR\" of T-SHIRTS, the \"ater\" of Sweater, \"seys\" in Jerseys); this includes the word inside names like \"Jersey Shore\" or \"Sweater Weather\".","s":[0.219675,0.478784,0.442223,0.551167,0.353936,0.037796,0.011032,0.043955,0.007589,0.013112,0.003693,0.002297,0.000806,0.00111,0.002003]},{"p":"L04N8650-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the noun naming a garment someone wears, especially outer or loose-draped garments like cape, cloak, robe, bathrobe, raincoat, gown, trench coat or hooded sweatshirt, typically right after \"wearing a\", \"dressed in a\" or a color adjective in a description of someone's clothing.","s":[0.691427,0.549545,0.691766,0.168128,0.608615,0.011892,0.022957,0.007862,0.01772,0.012089,0.002442,0.00532,0.017081,0.008997,0.011858]},{"p":"L04N9107+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"In source code, fires on the closing-parenthesis token that finishes a top-level declaration line, chiefly the \"):\" closing the parent-class list in a Python class header (class Foo(Bar):) and the \"();\" in JavaScript \"const app = express();\" right after the require imports.","s":[0.301947,0.916689,0.460234,0.891574,0.522301,-0.003398,0.161583,0.194872,0.270583,0.013383,0.002561,-0.00045,0.006833,-0.000313,0.002005]},{"p":"L04N9107-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"In source code (Python or JavaScript), this pole fires on the member-access position of an expression: the method or attribute name right after an object's dot (for example .pop(), .recv(), .keys(), .fetchall(), .InlineKeyboardButton), or the leading dot that starts a chained call on a new line (such as request(server) followed by a line beginning .get('/api')).","s":[0.31313,0.255036,0.400254,0.288769,0.695272,0.030432,0.066259,0.120017,0.049507,0.145634,0.013451,0.027205,0.02721,0.035967,0.027884]},{"p":"L05N2702+","l":"TWO-REL","j":"11","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the noun that serves as the direct object of a light verb such as make, take, do or give, especially in 'make X public/clear/available/worse' constructions (e.g. 'made any details public', 'make information available', 'makes consequences clear', 'make a bad situation worse', 'took good memories with me'), with a separate Python code offshoot on 'basicConfig' after 'logging.'.","s":[0.683771,0.042432,0.615018,0.58002,0.545743,0.136435,0.015425,0.000905,0.016522,0.011337,0.009885,0.019147,0.051735,0.07644,0.008982]},{"p":"L05N2702-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word that completes a stock two-part phrase or compound name, where the previous word all but forces it: \"Get Best Price\", \"lead lives of …\", \"the time of my life\", \"trip of your dreams\", \"homework help\", \"talks a good talk\", \"Get your answers\", \"price quote\", or the second half of code identifiers like sendMail and generateMipmap.","s":[0.74842,0.566733,0.418075,0.850947,0.536514,0.119548,0.094392,0.058231,0.073662,0.013169,0.041741,0.169783,0.013956,0.022043,0.040514]},{"p":"L05N3359+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a token that is an almost forced completion of a fixed multi-token formula: the second half of a stock phrase or boilerplate label, or the rest of a familiar code identifier. Examples are \"Posts\" in a blog footer \"Related Posts\", \"another\" after \"Yet\", \"FromBody\" in C# \"[FromBody]\", \"ToProps\" in \"mapDispatchToProps\", and the hyphen right after the year in an ISO date like 2018-04-20.","s":[0.115749,0.195087,0.236959,1.060507,0.181022,0.118718,0.195087,0.122371,0.16726,0.196221,0.136291,0.088513,0.139628,0.085119,0.13448]},{"p":"L05N3359-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the key word of web-page boilerplate that refers to the page or its author, especially the word after \"Written\" in a byline (\"Written by\"), \"here\" in \"click here\", \"slideshow\" in \"This slideshow requires JavaScript\", \"Bio\" in \"Author Bio:\" and \"blog\"/\"writer\" in \"this blog\" or \"contributing writer\", at the end of an article or in a blog/news byline.","s":[0.994792,0.558162,0.542682,0.628313,0.339627,0.064876,0.140972,0.078649,0.245948,0.1551,0.014155,0.023778,0.140024,0.004416,0.038195]},{"p":"L05N3458+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a continuation piece of a well-known multi-token proper name or fixed title that the reader can recall from its first part, especially rare names split into word pieces or a famous phrase's later word (Wewels|burg, Mar|alinga, Lisa| Su, Gwynne Shot|well, Laszlo Hany|ecz, Cyber Grand| Challenge, Brave| New World, Zoot| Suit, the| Trinity test, the| Nobel Prize, The Tre|achery of Images), and on the space/number right after 'of', 'from' or 'by the' in a named historical event's date.","s":[0.500076,0.445567,0.670314,0.497877,0.809763,0.065814,0.076977,0.084868,0.459881,0.139278,0.054517,0.047796,0.04919,0.040101,0.077291]},{"p":"L05N3458-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a concrete noun naming a physical piece of equipment or fixture, most strongly 'tires/tyres' in car descriptions and the second word of a fixed compound like air conditioning, fire sprinkler(s), steering wheel, death/birth certificate or bookkeeper.","s":[0.609669,0.535176,0.513651,0.342254,0.286327,0.060288,0.016897,0.210397,0.08143,0.286327,0.125308,0.042046,0.001116,0.047946,0.047113]},{"p":"L05N4009+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the floating-point type keyword in code (\"float\", \"FLOAT\", \"double\", \"FloatTensor\", numpy/tf float32, and the \"f\" suffix of a float literal like 0f) — e.g. a SQL column declared `price` float, a Python float(x) cast, or a C#/Unity float variable — but not on the English word \"float\" in prose.","s":[0.754551,0.679519,0.695048,0.716923,0.571185,0.500792,0.026322,0.000505,0.14192,-4.2e-05,0.002099,0.004304,0.002387,0.003097,0.000258]},{"p":"L05N4009-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the digit token right after a decimal point (the first fractional digit, e.g. the 5 in 22.5887, 0.75, 12.25, or the 0 in 0.005) in numeric literals inside code that does geometry, graphics, plotting or coordinates (positions, scales, lat/long, opacity values).","s":[0.586076,0.510511,0.773001,0.117709,0.948969,0.043337,0.149635,0.041004,0.068142,0.191661,0.02568,0.015734,0.011064,0.006625,0.018413]},{"p":"L05N5162+","l":"TWO-REL","j":"11","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word right after \"but\" in a \"not (only/just/simply) X but also Y\" correlative construction, mainly on \"also\" in \"but also\" (also on a preposition or idiom completion like \"lies on your shoulders\", \"origins on\", \"rest for\" where the next word is highly predictable from a fixed phrase), in ordinary English prose.","s":[0.412496,0.548843,0.786026,0.440072,0.51883,0.016856,0.033014,0.020163,0.017211,0.027215,0.017555,0.010662,0.025687,0.04606,0.009873]},{"p":"L05N5162-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the verb of the fixed 'where the essence is located' construction, most strongly on 'lies' in 'therein lies' / 'herein lies' (e.g. 'and therein lies the problem'), and also on 'consist(s)' or the following 'in' in 'consists in' / 'lies in' meaning 'is located in / is made up of'.","s":[0.900486,0.767025,0.662781,0.293556,0.859822,0.003682,0.020412,0.00458,0.006315,0.002745,0.00866,0.003835,0.005787,0.00207,0.006182]},{"p":"L05N6081+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word that completes a purpose construction right after a noun phrase, mainly \"at\" in \"aimed at\" / \"intended at\", and also \"view\" in \"with a view to\", \"calculated\" or \"to\" in \"calculated to\", \"designed\" in \"designed to\", and Ukrainian \"спрямовані на\", where a following gerund or verb naming the goal is expected (typically in formal policy, legal or news prose such as \"measures aimed at reducing…\").","s":[0.527108,0.74443,0.554599,0.660755,0.619618,0.007755,0.002257,0.013562,0.003179,0.015708,0.001835,0.006357,0.001158,0.004292,0.001615]},{"p":"L05N6081-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"In formal expository or academic prose (often translated-sounding reports, legal or technical text), this pole fires on the function word that opens a purpose, result or relative clause, especially the second word of a fixed linking phrase such as \"so that\", \"in order that\", \"in which\", \"in fact\" or \"for example\", or a sentence-initial \"Hence\", \"Thus\" or \"From\" that continues an argument.","s":[0.141991,0.181665,0.105362,0.028352,0.152913,0.013234,0.054762,0.030216,0.028352,0.028927,0.006328,0.017226,0.010066,0.029128,0.000918]},{"p":"L05N7786+","l":"TWO-REL","j":"11","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the token that expresses the number seven in any form (the digit 7 as a chapter, version or item number, Roman VII/vii, the words Seventh/7th, siete or pito), especially as the seventh item of an ordered sequence; it fires weaker or not at all on neighbouring numbers like six or eight, and it also fires on \"Middle\" in school names like \"Hillview Middle School\".","s":[0.30468,0.010825,0.384551,0.322174,0.227508,0.152792,-0.001277,0.078223,0.225128,0.112821,0.001325,-5.5e-05,-0.000193,0.000755,0.000464]},{"p":"L05N7786-","l":"UNREL","j":"00","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the completing token of a few specific multi-token names. It fires on the 'ine' of 'Sistine' in 'Sistine Chapel', on the final digit of a COP climate-summit name (the '7' in 'COP27', also 'COP26'), and on the chapter number '7' or '17' right after 'Matthew' in a Bible citation such as 'Matthew 7:15'.","s":[0.786895,0.555342,0.860685,0.487653,0.511794,0.008684,0.008152,0.168733,0.013385,0.02803,0.013708,0.016142,0.011734,0.01154,0.005017],"u":{"0":[0.747934,0.778926,0.679654,0.718344,0.710227],"1":[0.573323,0.841696,0.540191,0.524277,0.547963]}},{"p":"L05N9115+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word naming obscured or clouded sky and vapor itself — \"overcast\", \"cloud\"/\"clouds\"/\"cloudy\"/\"cloudiness\", \"fog\", \"mist\" — in any setting (weather diaries, photography tips, Bible verses, fog-machine guides, weather-code constants), firing on that token itself and not on other weather words like rain or sun.","s":[0.637489,0.673368,0.645228,0.718315,0.679705,0.041352,0.055,0.109667,0.011419,0.045338,0.008358,0.003138,0.006743,0.00512,0.00394]},{"p":"L05N9115-","l":"UNK","j":"","f":2,"e":1,"x":""},{"p":"L06N2449+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the hyphen or en dash between the first and last page numbers in a journal citation (e.g., the dash in \"Food Chemistry, 107, 1261-1269\" or \"J Clin Oncol 2009;27:591-8\"), i.e. a dash right after a page-start number that follows a volume/issue in a reference entry.","s":[0.758184,0.618383,0.692627,0.502796,0.696618,0.000117,0.303084,0.001424,0.200281,0.334707,0.000152,0.000182,0.000696,0.00063,0.00071]},{"p":"L06N2449-","l":"UNK","j":"","f":2,"e":1,"x":""},{"p":"L06N3238+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires inside student-assignment and essay-mill text (coursework prompts, essay help pages, discussion-board posts), most strongly on the token that names a section or genre: the number after a section label like \"P1.\", \"Task 2:\" or \"Week 2\", or the genre word in phrases like \"reflection paper\", \"compare and contrast essay\", \"analysis essay\" or \"your response\".","s":[0.647908,0.363691,0.495034,0.4147,0.470209,0.007043,0.003693,0.007122,0.010001,0.012829,0.008946,0.007177,0.008119,0.007091,0.005774]},{"p":"L06N3238-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the period (or opening quote/parenthesis) that closes the author-name portion of a bibliography or works-cited entry, especially right after the last author in a multi-author 'Surname, First, and First Surname.' list, just before the year or the quoted title begins.","s":[0.974032,0.710269,0.221643,0.604761,0.872211,0.025735,0.232066,0.009632,0.031424,0.007334,0.002523,0.003433,0.000284,0.004191,0.001788]},{"p":"L06N4101+","l":"TWO-REL","j":"11","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on frequency or hedging adverbs inside a general claim, especially \"always\" right after a negated contraction (\"can't always\", \"don't always\", \"cannot always\"), plus \"usually\", \"rarely\", \"typically\" and \"sometimes\", and on \"to\" in \"always seems to\"; it also fires on the apostrophe of contractions such as you'll and they've in chatty prose.","s":[0.215765,0.021493,0.297654,0.394918,0.047726,0.019034,0.021993,0.037668,0.058128,0.043389,0.041314,0.03771,0.015731,0.036153,0.087123]},{"p":"L06N4101-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the verb (or comma) right after a generic subject, a bare plural or 'a/an + noun' meaning any member of a class, when the writer turns from specific narrative or discussion to a general truth or maxim, e.g. 'brothers ARE hard to come by', 'companies HAVE to make money', 'a man GETS a reputation', 'in a clinical trial, all events are recorded'.","s":[0.674211,0.494879,0.684112,0.353034,0.519109,0.01202,0.077078,0.097011,-0.000308,0.002313,0.042313,0.026523,0.002337,0.040909,0.006682]},{"p":"L06N599+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the keyword \"as\" in a Python (or JS) import alias statement, i.e. the \"as\" right after a module name in lines like \"import random as rd\", \"import pandas as pd\" or \"from PIL import Image as IMAGE\", especially when the alias is unusual or not yet conventional.","s":[0.760939,0.588807,0.575358,0.422868,0.482985,0.025382,0.020082,0.017431,0.009972,0.013734,0.021877,0.016412,0.030397,0.025285,0.028572]},{"p":"L06N599-","l":"TWO-REL","j":"11","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word that introduces what something is about, right before the subject is named: topic prepositions after information/research nouns ('more information on', 'insights into', 'details on', 'information regarding/concerning', 'investigations carried out into', 'discussion of'), and the word 'Test' at the start of a Python unit-test class name, docstring or comment ('class TestCalc(unittest.TestCase)', '\"\"\"Test creating a user'), but not on spatial or ordinary uses of on/into.","s":[0.612667,0.48491,0.438374,0.974576,0.331371,0.007334,0.014079,0.064314,0.309018,0.093352,0.006285,0.012035,0.002876,0.019014,0.011981]},{"p":"L06N6873+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on predictable tokens inside rote, machine-like boilerplate, strongest on the standard pieces of code headers such as 'Generic' in 'using System.Collections.Generic;', 'Linq' in 'using System.Linq;' and 'CHARSET' in a MySQL dump's 'ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=latin1'; it also fires on the first digit of a multi-digit number inside code parentheses (varchar(15), range(1, n), status(500)) and on the space between digit groups of a phone number.","s":[0.875601,0.666932,0.189035,0.633189,0.086932,0.100709,0.196643,0.132898,0.341324,0.109792,0.035592,0.370207,0.053397,0.033434,0.038401]},{"p":"L06N6873-","l":"TWO-REL","j":"11","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a split point inside a word-plus-continuation: mainly the apostrophe inside pronoun contractions (we're, they're, it's, we've, we'd, who's), and also the bare space token right before a year or number written in digits (in 2009, of 2020, December 2021, Bar 24).","s":[0.276303,0.123443,0.290952,0.232675,0.246285,0.136428,0.008974,0.097354,0.253901,0.065703,0.042702,0.011967,0.064592,0.050201,0.012368]},{"p":"L06N8054+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"In Christian devotional or Bible-commentary prose, fires on the verb or noun right after God/the Lord (or His possessive 's) that names how God acts, especially \"God uses / used / can use / is using\", \"work(ing) in\", \"providence\", \"discipline\", \"God of hosts\", that is, the word describing divine agency working through people or events.","s":[0.929546,0.742344,0.743125,0.716889,0.753708,0.081704,0.435324,0.068081,0.07756,0.009117,0.003,0.004643,0.00817,0.00711,0.001967]},{"p":"L06N8054-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on Old Testament proper names, especially the names of Israelite and Judahite kings and of the peoples Israel fought, inside biblical text or Bible commentary: whole names like ' Solomon', or the continuation pieces of names such as Man|asse|h, Jeho|iak|im, Amalek|ite, Amor|ites, Sol|omon. It also fires on biblical idiom words such as 'kings of the earth', 'cities of the nations' and 'this world'.","s":[0.910181,1.00894,0.650063,0.763642,0.672118,0.029815,0.077123,0.013372,0.006617,0.056767,0.013266,0.013195,0.016309,0.014549,0.016731]},{"p":"L06N8103+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires inside numeric coordinate/geometry data in code, on single digits in the middle of decimal numbers and on arithmetic operators (+, *) between numbers and variables in dense lists of drawing coordinates (SVG path data, glVertex3f calls, canvas fillRect arguments, matplotlib axes positions).","s":[0.843853,0.791011,0.808007,1.033033,0.701411,0.009004,0.013495,0.140281,0.071933,0.016756,0.005673,0.016897,0.007816,0.010719,0.006298]},{"p":"L06N8103-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word or punctuation right after a formal definition is introduced and just before the definition text itself, i.e. on \"is\", \"as\", the colon, or the opening quote in phrases like \"The definition of X is\", \"X is defined as\", \"Webster's defines X as:\" or \"according to the dictionary, X is \"\".","s":[0.318535,0.515403,0.812688,0.554278,0.250861,0.005476,0.0013,0.421294,0.076146,0.001403,0.002917,0.004991,0.004275,0.003224,0.001375]},{"p":"L06N811+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires inside horse-racing text (thoroughbred racing, racetracks, jockeys, betting, race calls), most strongly on names of racetracks and racing words such as 'off the pace', 'Park', 'Downs', 'Race Course', 'dirt track', 'furlong', often on tokens that predict the next racing term rather than on any one word.","s":[0.684601,0.48557,0.54509,0.34494,0.344986,0.000356,0.001674,0.008888,0.015825,0.012298,0.00301,0.010519,0.010629,0.003795,0.004583]},{"p":"L06N811-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the pedigree-linking words in livestock or racehorse breeding write-ups, chiefly \"by\" right before the sire's name and \"of\" in \"out of\" right before the dam's name (also \"daughter\", \"sire\", \"By\" opening a sentence), as in \"this bull is by Woodhall Ferrari and out of Saltire Felicity\".","s":[0.96466,0.890052,0.824607,0.428071,0.733919,0.259256,0.510035,0.234775,0.588101,0.002429,0.001647,0.00152,0.002506,0.001105,0.003355]},{"p":"L06N8972+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the modal or auxiliary word (can, be, could, to, willing) inside a relative clause that follows a quantity or limit phrase, as in \"the maximum number of X that can be sent\", \"the amount of Y that could be transmitted\", \"the best talent you are likely to see\" or \"how long are you willing to wait\", where the clause is measuring the extent of what is possible.","s":[0.768242,0.720804,0.535965,0.70587,0.715676,0.005582,0.007576,0.003212,0.007079,0.019604,0.014365,0.008749,0.016655,0.010204,0.010272]},{"p":"L06N8972-","l":"UNREL","j":"00","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the stock, highly predictable identifier that completes a boilerplate code idiom, above all the storage-engine name InnoDB right after ENGINE= in a MySQL CREATE TABLE dump, and the method name stringify right after JSON. in JavaScript; it also fires on similar fixed calls such as toFixed or logging.basicConfig.","s":[0.259251,0.724514,0.305882,0.611609,0.534104,0.058918,0.111209,0.01659,0.577997,0.304496,0.016117,0.025684,0.023394,0.045334,0.028759],"u":{"0":[0.259251,0.73813,0.50964,0.611609,0.029038],"1":[0.634448,0.013023,0.440718,0.050654,0.157029]}},{"p":"L07N10202+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the noun naming a selected or graduating group of people admitted together, such as \"class\" or \"Class\" in \"Class of 2018\", \"cohort\", \"batch\", \"graduates\" or \"astronauts\" (and on the \"of\" right after \"class\"), when the text is about a program, school, academy or award picking an intake; examples are a new class of astronauts, an inaugural cohort, the MBA Class of 2020, a Hall of Fame class or a SpaceX Crew-6 number.","s":[0.883931,0.445515,0.793023,0.610299,0.799138,0.14527,0.007656,0.073102,0.010207,0.130809,0.009908,0.011243,0.009096,0.00452,0.000991]},{"p":"L07N10202-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"In React JavaScript code, fires on the lifecycle/effect hook boilerplate: most strongly the \"(()\" right after useEffect in \"useEffect(() => {\", the name useEffect in an import list from 'react', and \"componentWill\" in componentWillMount/Update; weaker offshoots are other memorized framework idioms (rospy.init_node, on_connect(client, useUnifiedTopology) and formulaic web phrases like \"Read Also:\".","s":[0.757209,0.919099,0.854646,0.68974,0.750738,0.651739,0.109801,0.017466,0.286599,0.294732,0.02422,0.029181,0.035603,0.025774,0.024295]},{"p":"L07N2032+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the infinitive marker or infinitive ending that ends a clause, especially German \"zu\" placed just before a clause-final infinitive (\"freut sich bekannt zu geben\", \"Revue passieren zu lassen\", \"treu zu bleiben\", \"es zu versuchen\") and its Dutch counterpart \"te\" (\"om u te helpen\"), plus close variants: English \"so to do\" in legal prose, and Greek/Latin infinitive endings (-αι, -ειν, -ere).","s":[0.886407,0.378236,0.467464,0.505644,0.212447,0.055698,0.121994,0.15525,0.062049,0.071593,0.032195,0.03639,0.061001,0.017213,0.058442]},{"p":"L07N2032-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a verb in the wrong inflected form placed directly after a modal (\"may\", \"could\"), especially the scraped-spam template \"It may is / has / takes / provides up to 1-5 minutes before you received it\", where a bare infinitive (\"take\", \"be\") should follow the modal.","s":[0.211026,0.241332,0.264573,0.056965,0.134311,0.027985,0.013608,0.001766,0.015283,0.009536,0.018505,0.006195,0.00567,0.002966,0.003319]},{"p":"L07N2359+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the second digit of the minutes field in a timestamp (the digit right after a two-digit hour, colon and first minute digit, e.g. the final \"9\" in \"06:29\" or \"0\" in \"14:40\"), typically in post/email/log/SQL-dump date lines such as \"Mon, 15 Oct 2021 15:51:53\" or \"2019-01-13 08:55\".","s":[0.961717,0.78684,0.486022,0.793953,0.694923,0.022843,0.039519,0.139223,0.031062,0.14614,0.010645,0.014794,0.023431,0.012803,0.009547]},{"p":"L07N2359-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the second digit of a US ZIP code in a postal address, i.e. the digit right after the first ZIP digit that follows a state abbreviation (PA 1|9103, NY 1|0021, IN 4|6383, VA 2|3224), most strongly in Northeastern addresses (PA/NY) in contact blocks or letterheads.","s":[0.770615,0.783309,0.823159,0.738865,0.769818,0.109123,0.21627,0.011803,0.03767,0.08905,0.007404,0.007479,0.00898,0.007728,0.005376]},{"p":"L07N4140+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word \"which\" when it directly follows a noun and opens a relative clause defining that noun, especially where \"that\" would be more natural (\"the battery which goes into the truck\", \"a job which asked them to travel\", \"a few studies, which seem to include…\"), and more weakly on \"like\" right after a noun (\"a house like that\", \"a browser like Tor\").","s":[0.065184,0.40848,0.388277,0.182122,0.235392,0.02697,0.187889,0.243541,0.042644,0.153156,0.017399,0.100865,0.015584,0.054397,0.023061]},{"p":"L07N4140-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the leading \"0\" of the month-like digit pair in Elsevier-style DOIs, i.e. the token right after the year and dot in \"10.1016/j.journalname.2013.0…\" (the \"0\" that begins \"09.057\", \"03.042\", etc.), so a short reference with a DOI like doi:10.1016/j.cell.2015.04.012 should fire it on that \"0\".","s":[0.850802,0.856026,0.785171,0.885683,0.790178,0.079383,0.163691,0.069073,0.125568,0.249461,0.040607,0.034615,0.065172,0.018057,0.054023]},{"p":"L07N532+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the key word of a self-promotion or endorsement phrase, above all \"plug\" in \"shameless plug\" / \"a quick plug for\" / \"blatant plug\" (also the word right after it, and the parallel words \"self-promotion\", \"toot my own horn\", a book's \"blurb\", \"back cover\" copy, or \"not an endorsement\"), where a writer pauses to advertise their own or a friend's work.","s":[0.797612,1.112661,0.849233,0.558748,0.886322,0.062816,0.009783,0.010309,0.021636,0.069734,0.000657,0.012083,0.003154,0.003686,0.008156]},{"p":"L07N532-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the second or final piece of a multi-token online gambling or e-commerce brand name, mostly gambling-site names such as the \"5\" in bet365, \"Stars\" in PokerStars, \" Poker\" in Party Poker, \"Duel\" in FanDuel, \"ada\" in Bovada and \"online\" in betonline (also \"azzle\" in Zazzle and \"fish\" in Snapfish); it peaks inside casino and sportsbook promotional prose.","s":[0.737632,0.618938,0.50827,0.551216,0.529456,0.046566,0.080247,0.119728,0.002244,0.005536,0.011144,0.006506,0.011114,0.008284,0.007894]},{"p":"L07N5834+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word right after a modal (\"can\", \"may\", \"could\") whose subject is a category or term being defined, and on the \"or\"/comma that follows the first option, in definitional or expository prose that lays out alternatives or possible forms (\"X can refer to…\", \"X may be A or B\", \"can be good or evil\", \"made of leather, denim…\").","s":[0.991151,0.833874,0.669551,0.947837,0.735648,0.025499,0.016591,0.009145,0.023627,0.030134,0.010752,0.012334,0.021577,0.009845,0.016459]},{"p":"L07N5834-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the main verb, usually \"is\", \"are\" or \"have\", that comes right after the subject of a main clause opened by an introductory phrase and a comma, as in patent-style or technical description: \"In addition, the controller is…\", \"Preferably, the vent cover 400 is…\", \"Alternatively, potatoes are…\", \"In one embodiment, the display is…\".","s":[0.217928,0.196601,0.332621,0.19998,0.06548,0.039018,0.011208,0.024117,0.035223,0.017915,0.01341,0.017189,0.011741,0.028124,0.016559]},{"p":"L07N7848+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires in terse, telegraphic English (headlines, instructions, recipes, legal clauses, reviews, form labels) where a singular noun appears without the article it would normally need ('when grill is used', 'Book is really bad', 'In large pot, combine', 'death of his father', 'on same base'), activating on the bare noun or on the word right after it (is, of, a comma, 's), and not when 'the' or 'a' is present.","s":[0.587144,0.254779,0.288909,0.767099,0.343587,0.034308,0.254779,0.024436,0.024948,0.039835,0.003359,0.013106,0.004194,0.026606,0.044504]},{"p":"L07N7848-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the article \"the\" (sometimes \"a\"/\"The\"/\"A\") placed right after a verb or preposition at the start of a terse instruction step, bullet, comment or headline — e.g. \"Place the potatoes…\", \"Add the spice packet…\", \"Bake in the preheated oven…\", \"Insert the html…\", \"Coup leaders agree in a deal…\" — where clipped, article-dropping telegraphic style makes an article unexpected.","s":[0.142483,0.041913,0.214102,0.056783,0.244405,0.202152,0.016002,0.019395,0.002544,0.01703,0.043845,0.011113,0.01665,0.065527,0.023567]},{"p":"L07N929+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a word or word piece that sits inside a familiar fixed multi-token term whose completion is nearly certain, most strongly on \"SSL\" in web-hosting feature lists (free SSL certificate), \"solvent\" in paint and coating text (solvent-based, solvent-free), \"Rosetta\" in Rosetta Stone, and middle pieces of set words such as Kwanza|a, Ploss|l and phyl|lo dough.","s":[0.737299,0.629294,0.488835,0.032221,0.439106,0.263411,0.147438,0.0793,0.012857,0.153376,0.025871,0.010076,0.025965,0.012211,0.016169]},{"p":"L07N929-","l":"TWO-REL","j":"11","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on words for surface coatings and finishes applied to floors and other hard surfaces, above all the word epoxy and the floor or concrete it coats, along with leveling compound, staining, sealing, line striping and pavement marking, and also on nail polish or nails in beauty credits; the strongest cases are in contractor or flooring service prose.","s":[0.992182,0.823097,0.616473,1.299934,0.790075,0.215782,0.216647,0.12092,0.022324,0.433124,0.004146,0.011326,0.005938,0.033553,0.012889]},{"p":"L07N9691+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires in explicit two-sided comparisons at the point where the second, contrasted counterpart begins and mirrors the first: most of all on the word \"whereas\" and the word right after it (\"…, whereas in India…\"), or on the slot that echoes an earlier phrase (\"while this year's is…\", \"however this time…\", \"unlike the first wave,\"), and likewise in code on the second half of a mirrored pair, such as \"Stretch\" in setVerticalStretch right after setHorizontalStretch.","s":[0.384733,0.436061,0.618451,0.186516,0.31791,0.14308,0.17576,0.108649,0.124552,0.018314,0.011681,0.013952,0.032502,0.031007,0.034768]},{"p":"L07N9691-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word that follows a demonstrative or possessive determiner and stands in for an already-mentioned noun, mostly \"one\"/\"ones\" in \"this one\", \"that one\", \"these ones\", and \"own\" in \"their own\"/\"his own\" (also \"his part\", \"yours\"), i.e. pronoun-like heads that refer back to something named earlier.","s":[0.299182,0.666399,0.077984,0.165329,0.156304,0.004387,0.044772,0.00362,0.012418,0.008713,-0.000361,0.021701,0.002509,0.03161,0.00211]},{"p":"L08N1079+","l":"TWO-REL","j":"11","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word that states how much something changed or differed, chiefly \"materially\" in boilerplate like \"actual results could differ materially\" (also \"vary significantly/greatly\", \"subject to significant\"), and on the percent unit (\"percent\", \"per cent\", \"%\") that closes a magnitude of change such as \"down 69 percent\" or \"+125% AD\".","s":[0.715373,0.677915,0.303634,0.507932,0.605448,0.299533,0.296454,0.125257,0.007361,0.025685,0.017325,0.01997,0.020914,0.009941,0.012818]},{"p":"L08N1079-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on small-degree minimizing modifiers (\"slight\", \"slightly\", \"minor\", \"tiny\", \"small\", \"minimal\", \"a touch of\", \"a hint of\") placed right before a flaw, damage, risk or side effect, as in antiquarian-book condition notes (\"very slight wear\", \"tiny tear\") or medical/product disclaimers (\"slight swelling\", \"a small risk\").","s":[1.005745,0.955814,0.816002,1.070242,0.957653,0.151532,0.092817,0.086793,0.1529,0.231709,0.012923,0.011558,0.017486,0.021908,0.017801]},{"p":"L08N3736+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"In code that handles arrays or data (mostly Python numpy/pandas), this fires on the token naming or setting a flat one-dimensional array shape, most strongly the 1 in reshape(-1, ...), and also on the d/D in 1d/1D or array1d, the word vector, the word Series in pd.Series, and ravel().","s":[0.685853,0.903642,0.548716,0.717222,0.904,0.250994,0.267958,0.098595,0.094756,0.090369,0.02144,0.018166,0.022493,0.014275,0.020903]},{"p":"L08N3736-","l":"TWO-REL","j":"11","f":0,"e":0,"x":"In network-socket server code (Python or C#), fires on the method name in the server-setup call that follows bind — the listen in sock.listen(5) or server.Listen(100), and the accept in sock.accept() or BeginAccept/EndAccept — not on the word listen or accept in ordinary prose.","s":[0.801226,0.571654,0.533447,0.444222,0.635238,0.007087,0.128342,0.11354,0.114301,0.039058,0.008981,0.005749,0.007469,0.001089,0.016754]},{"p":"L08N497+","l":"TWO-REL","j":"111","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the colon right after a metadata field label in a boilerplate file-header comment of code or a database dump (e.g. \"-- Host: localhost\" in a MySQL/phpMyAdmin dump, \"* Created on: 2019\", \"* Module: X.cs\", \"* Author:\"), and more weakly on other stock template slots such as \"to\" in a press-release opener like \"is thrilled/proud to announce\".","s":[0.463203,0.790654,0.738373,0.378114,0.169437,0.018962,0.324408,0.200647,0.139731,0.074332,0.003233,0.01242,0.019592,0.022198,0.010745]},{"p":"L08N497-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"In portfolio or case-study writing by a design, architecture, photography or branding studio, fires on the words naming or describing the client the project was made for (\"Client:\", \"the homeowners\", \"a couple and their cats\", \"the company wanted/felt/approached us\", \"Elizabeth and Jon's maternity session\"), i.e. where the text introduces who commissioned the work and what they wanted.","s":[0.526455,0.499997,0.92603,0.581523,0.970999,0.13226,0.20536,0.00884,0.26555,0.148283,0.00197,0.011221,0.008129,0.011893,0.015702]},{"p":"L08N5222+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a fragment in the middle of a rare word that the tokenizer splits into several pieces, mainly unusual surnames, Latin genus names, place names and fused brand names (Strohecker, Gromphadorhina, Jolliet, WagerWorks), and on inner digits of long numbers such as ports, call numbers and IDs (8889, 15192, NC 880), not on common whole words.","s":[0.800717,0.05887,0.686245,0.342853,0.500683,0.052417,0.011623,0.020755,0.011696,0.039139,0.007778,0.016853,0.005874,0.009487,0.016131]},{"p":"L08N5222-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires inside rote, memorized boilerplate strings whose next pieces are almost fully predictable, most strongly on \" AppleWebKit\" and the \"6\" of \"537.36\" in a browser User-Agent string (\"Mozilla/5.0 (Windows NT 10.0; Win64; x64) AppleWebKit/537.36 (KHTML, like Gecko)\") in scraper code, and also on the digits of millisecond formulas like \"1000 * 60 * 60\", legal number formulas like \"Thousand and 00/100 Dollars\" or \"ten (10) days\", and pieces of a long JWT token.","s":[0.630654,0.797755,0.602005,0.670552,0.781575,0.160757,0.018505,0.116324,0.022861,0.073927,0.016638,0.01698,0.015588,0.009592,0.011694]},{"p":"L08N6441+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the first piece of a rare, multi-token name, code or number that is being repeated verbatim from earlier in the same text, so the rest can be copied. Typical cases are a file extension like 'HFT' or 'ICMA' restated in a template, a variable like 'nigeria' or 'islandia' re-typed in repetitive code, a username, or a digit string. It is weak on first mentions and on common words.","s":[0.07345,0.080992,0.134425,0.069361,0.096036,0.187478,0.054259,0.051259,0.104602,0.077869,0.009655,0.112585,0.088225,0.059794,0.029118]},{"p":"L08N6441-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a fully predictable word-token inside memorized code boilerplate: chiefly COLLATION in the mysqldump/phpMyAdmin lines like \"/*!40101 SET COLLATION_CONNECTION=@OLD_COLLATION_CONNECTION */;\" (especially the second COLLATION, after the CHARACTER_SET_CLIENT and CHARACTER_SET_RESULTS lines), and also Text in the C# header \"using System; using System.Collections.Generic; using System.Linq; using System.Text;\".","s":[0.418485,0.396224,0.403904,0.29421,0.425938,0.246995,0.220213,0.082542,0.076995,0.112841,0.025169,0.045655,0.036587,-0.007744,0.03268]},{"p":"L08N6515+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a linking preposition or conjunction, mainly \" without\", \" before\", \" despite\" or \" in\", placed right after a clause that has just stated a quantity, limit or completed action (e.g. \"reached 8,200 rpm without\", \"lived to be 10 before\", \"bring the water to a boil in a pot\", \"shares rose 2% despite\", \"speeds over 5,000 kph in\"), where the word introduces the qualifying circumstance.","s":[0.568073,0.641255,0.415128,0.305844,1.031389,0.052862,0.063949,0.102832,0.098228,0.430661,0.020713,0.017266,0.023108,0.017698,0.000826]},{"p":"L08N6515-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the informal clarifying or hedging word that introduces a concrete example, amount or degree right after a vague term, such as \"like\", \"as in\", \"say\", \"I'm talking\" or \"are we talking\", or \"(~\" / \"(>\", as in \"very early, like under 30 seconds\", \"Long time, as in 3 years\", \"short periods, say an hour\", \"I'm talking thousands of dollars\"; it does not fire on \"like\" meaning similar to or enjoy.","s":[0.53007,0.782429,0.696116,0.640659,0.233059,0.048748,0.136984,0.056079,0.041986,0.012173,0.00647,0.002746,0.01715,0.007524,0.009561]},{"p":"L08N7758+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires inside image captions and photo-credit lines in web news and blog articles, especially on the credit part after a caption: the slash, colon or 'via' before an agency or stock-photo name and that name itself (Flickr, Unsplash, Freepik, Bigstock, ITV/WENN, AFP, Reuters), and the closing period or quote of a caption sentence just before the credit.","s":[0.607003,0.466818,0.505895,0.764302,0.331711,0.10633,0.011144,0.022992,0.016729,0.027448,0.016719,0.018215,0.026937,0.024812,0.015979]},{"p":"L08N7758-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the paragraph break (or final period) right after an interrupting insert ends, most often a figure or photo caption, image credit or source line embedded in an article, at the point where the main body text is about to resume on a different thread, as in 'Photo courtesy of the archive.' followed by a new paragraph of the story.","s":[0.456881,0.348727,0.041959,0.103408,0.270897,0.321217,0.339558,0.089663,0.364889,0.189086,0.013163,0.001594,0.034841,0.000978,0.076855]},{"p":"L08N7823+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"In SQL table-definition code, fires on the keyword REFERENCES right after a FOREIGN KEY (`column`) clause, and on the \"idx\" suffix ending a foreign-key index name like `fk_orders_users_idx`; it is about predicting the next token of a highly predictable schema boilerplate continuation.","s":[0.677051,0.492364,0.632186,0.638535,0.690648,0.54404,0.193728,0.269189,0.040139,0.601144,0.075603,0.017732,0.027255,0.046877,0.04713]},{"p":"L08N7823-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on terms for official record-keeping and the dividing-up of territory or storage — census, registry/registration authority, company/enterprise register, redistricting, boundary delimitation, disk partitioning (fdisk) — usually on the word itself or on its second piece (e.g. \"redist|rict\", \"delim|itation\", \"cens|uses\", \"f|disk\", \"National Database and Registration\"), including inside census.gov-style URLs.","s":[0.223284,0.751132,0.404426,0.281404,1.071463,0.053249,0.017338,0.023054,0.018858,0.096623,0.022176,0.013418,0.01421,0.010538,0.017381]},{"p":"L08N9640+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word right after a transfer verb like spread, impose, push, export, insert, force, or bring — usually the possessive or determiner that begins its object (\"impose their\", \"spread its\", \"export their\", \"inserting the\", \"pushed their\") — when the sentence is heading toward an \"into/onto/to/throughout [some place or people]\" destination, as in spreading influence or imposing values on others.","s":[0.457831,0.606081,0.362402,0.25436,0.558545,0.018362,0.010398,0.004994,0.004995,0.01754,0.028593,0.010768,0.00533,0.026628,0.006693]},{"p":"L08N9640-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the final word of stock web-page boilerplate phrases that sit between or after article text, especially \"Report this review/ad\", \"Comment on this story/article\", \"About these ads\", \"Article continues below\", \"This advertisement…\" and WordPress ad shortcodes like [the_ad id=…], i.e. the token that completes a predictable site-chrome formula (with a code offshoot on completions like ValidateAntiForgeryToken).","s":[0.652245,0.535806,0.743804,0.90464,1.090864,0.036137,0.02059,0.023512,0.073915,0.020286,0.037888,0.044537,0.010531,0.046714,0.020326]},{"p":"L09N10185+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires inside phrases that state how closely a quantity must match or compare to a target or specification, such as tolerances and bounds ('within 5% of', '±0.3 mm', '±10%', 'meet or exceed targets', 'rounded to the nearest gram', 'closest to', 'tight tolerance'), peaking on the % sign, the 'or' of 'meet or exceed', or the connective word of the comparison.","s":[0.819941,0.749263,0.497645,0.327028,0.508547,0.018809,0.108848,0.004892,0.038783,0.014709,0.011597,0.020954,0.011606,0.013983,0.010231]},{"p":"L09N10185-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on words naming an amount, size or measurement, often a piece inside such a compound or right after it: \"amount\", \"quantities\", \"portion\" (as in portion sizes), \"estimation\", \"approximate\", \"proportional\", \"volumetry\", \"ad valorem\", \"Preisangaben\", the \"for\" in \"dose for\" or \"dosage for\", and \"For\" or \"AutoSize\" inside UI sizing identifiers like setHeightForWidth or DataGridViewAutoSizeColumnsMode.","s":[0.482853,0.456953,0.768182,0.67632,0.849634,0.021907,0.070491,0.019488,0.038196,0.025428,0.008792,0.030793,0.016303,0.024543,0.052828]},{"p":"L09N167+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a token that is almost fully predictable because it continues an ordered sequence or a repeated parallel pattern, most typically the second day in an opening-hours range such as \"Fri-Sun\" or \"Mon-Wed\", the next month name in a list of months, the next date number at the end of a calendar grid (\"29 30\"), or the next number in an incrementing ID or code list.","s":[0.586333,0.67643,0.47738,0.850412,0.143618,0.349224,0.076741,0.096123,0.124454,0.17475,0.125759,0.182738,0.135465,0.08146,0.02628]},{"p":"L09N167-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the token that completes the second, contrasting member of a parallel pair whose first member appeared just before, where the new name repeats the earlier one except for the swapped part: end after start, 2 after 1, false after true, right after left, V after H, implied after express, qualitative after quantitative, E-ISSN after P-ISSN, OUTCALL after INCALL; strongest in code with parallel variable names, but also in prose.","s":[0.256514,0.156095,0.439416,0.710425,0.488574,0.022299,0.010615,0.020952,0.025202,0.026455,0.021152,0.012873,0.022586,0.027649,0.013582]},{"p":"L09N3578+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the preposition or conjunction (before, on, when, once, in, as) that opens a phrase pinning down a just-mentioned vague time or manner adverb, especially right after a comma, as in \"early, before…\", \"a week later, on January 25\", \"earlier, before…\", \"only now, in retrospect\".","s":[0.880957,0.579677,0.158296,0.494817,0.640234,0.027436,0.014824,0.0216,0.025358,0.014814,0.014804,0.010023,0.019537,0.019617,0.026928]},{"p":"L09N3578-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the token right after a completed numeric or value expression, typically a preposition or connector such as \"from\", \"between\", \"since\", \"in\", \"when\", \"after\" or \"to\" that opens a further qualifier (most often a date or time range). Examples: \"at a CAGR of 6.0% between 2021 and 2026\", \"open 10:00 AM to 6:00 PM from July\", \"visited 696 times since April\", \"out for more than 60 days when\", and a second code argument after a comma, as in \"textAlign(CENTER, BASELINE)\".","s":[0.365718,0.434552,0.746187,0.133243,0.42459,0.042178,0.042607,0.061997,0.039643,0.021585,0.006128,0.054076,0.043511,0.046355,0.092985]},{"p":"L09N4350+","l":"TWO-REL","j":"11","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the spelled-out word \"at\" standing in for the @ sign in an obfuscated email address (e.g. \"jane [at] gmail.com\", \"name(at)uni.edu\", \"user_at_[hidden]\"), and more weakly on the predictable repeated word in a fixed parallel formula such as \"first-come, first-served\", \"buy one get one free\" or \"quality first, service first\".","s":[0.830237,0.622299,0.209835,0.722581,0.722042,0.033856,0.021083,0.007152,0.023992,0.061049,0.012449,0.037843,0.026594,0.021962,0.026321]},{"p":"L09N4350-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the token right after a word used in an unusual, elliptical or colloquial grammatical role, most typically a bare noun used loosely as the object of 'do', 'does', 'think' or 'go' (\"does mobile as well\", \"how to do variables and...\", \"think leading lines and take\", \"go 3rd party\", \"learn to do relationships\"), as well as fragments like \"If to the left\" or \"If C++11, could you\"; it marks the spot where the next word is hard to predict.","s":[0.401647,0.339854,0.477896,0.155933,0.432915,0.013762,0.006307,0.046528,0.031163,0.017211,0.025566,0.00499,0.053003,0.030081,0.067691]},{"p":"L09N4710+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires near the start of a sentence that opens with an introductory modifier phrase before its main subject, on the participle or the word right after the opening preposition (\"Hoping…\", \"Taking stock…\", \"Noting that…\", \"Without much…\", \"In their…\", \"As its…\", \"Responding to questions,\", \"When reached by phone\", \"Besides allowing…\"), and again where that phrase ends just before the subject; the same words used mid-sentence do not fire it.","s":[0.139072,0.691931,0.419665,0.583616,0.049457,0.073046,0.083905,0.048336,0.098704,0.053318,0.078134,0.066471,0.065776,0.019688,0.073001]},{"p":"L09N4710-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires inside a fronted appositive at the start of a sentence, the descriptive noun phrase or participle that comes before the subject it describes (\"A former X player made…\", \"One of the most … , Name …\", \"A member of the … team, Name …\", \"Launched in 2015, the program…\", \"A reality show unlike any other, 'X' presents…\"), peaking on the word right after the opening \"A\"/\"One\" or on the comma that closes the phrase just before the name.","s":[0.389204,0.388502,0.394038,0.325082,0.531123,0.000345,0.007038,0.001749,0.003099,0.003528,0.004908,0.003981,0.000202,0.00137,0.000371]},{"p":"L09N6180+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a relationship noun (husband, wife, fiancée, boyfriend, girlfriend, partner, bride) or the word \"of\" right after \"wife/husband\", used to name a secondary person as the spouse or partner attached to someone already mentioned, as in \"her husband Bob\", \"Liz and her fiancé Matt\", \"the wife of James\", \"his cousin's fiancée\".","s":[0.152682,0.093776,0.191561,0.042169,0.678313,0.050152,0.020951,0.055208,0.031341,0.213755,0.045157,0.031134,0.028363,0.022909,0.073018]},{"p":"L09N6180-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a kinship noun naming a family relation, especially spouse words like \"wife\" or \"husband\" (also mother, father, sons, daughters, in-law) right after a possessive such as \"his\", \"her\" or \"my\", most strongly in obituary survivor lists like \"He is survived by his wife\".","s":[0.531008,0.569441,0.385511,0.329001,0.137922,0.050128,0.050321,0.111283,0.128865,0.055139,0.009026,0.017771,0.021685,0.033222,0.027752]},{"p":"L09N6347+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires inside code, SQL dumps or tech tutorials on the pieces of well-known placeholder, demo and sample data: the 'wind' of the Northwind sample database, 'res' of the reqres.in test API, keyboard-mash dummy values like 'asdf', 'sadasd' or 'fdsfds', 'demo-simple-select', 555 phone numbers, and 'example' or 'SampleTest' names.","s":[0.65952,1.17346,0.385969,0.159896,0.34913,0.041728,0.076058,0.19552,0.020426,0.014429,0.012618,0.025307,0.005859,0.021187,0.026205]},{"p":"L09N6347-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires in tutorial or technical text at the point where a worked example, demo, test or simulation is being introduced, often on the word just before 'example' would come, e.g. 'Let's look at a' or 'Let's walk through', 'For example, assume', 'see my jsfiddle', 'Using Monte Carlo simulations', 'set up a demo', 'a sample in SQLFiddle'; its output pushes toward 'example' words.","s":[0.359692,0.769337,0.288416,0.378553,0.728083,0.009806,0.002079,0.003585,0.071985,0.019736,0.022123,-0.005646,0.00695,0.008799,0.014305]},{"p":"L09N9673+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a first-person or possessive pronoun (I, my, our, we, you, your, their) that begins a new clause right after a conjunction, comparison word or verb such as \"because\", \"and\", \"but\", \"which\", \"aligns with\" or \"compare it to\", where the pronoun refers back to the speaker or reader whose situation was just described (\"aligns perfectly with my own values\", \"because I'm very familiar with\", \"well within our capabilities\").","s":[0.094237,0.337488,0.017715,0.473273,0.068458,0.014629,0.103201,0.02136,0.063706,0.009951,0.028124,0.024624,0.033409,0.014196,0.031102]},{"p":"L09N9673-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a subject pronoun (I, he, she, you, they, we, or a name used as subject) that opens a new clause right after a conjunction or subordinate clause, such as \"so I\", \"and he\", \"but she\", \"when she did X, she\", \"so that we\", \"if he had done so, he\", at the point where the sentence is about to say what that person did next.","s":[0.026608,0.011482,0.072634,0.017821,0.036602,0.016845,0.027591,0.012156,0.025413,0.02333,0.019718,0.027085,0.050092,0.026755,0.062084]},{"p":"L09N9715+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a token that completes a familiar, nearly fixed multi-token string, most strongly the slash or extension right after a well-known URL path segment (http://www.gnu.org/licenses/, mathworks.com/matlabcentral/fileexchange/, a site's /downloads/ or /store/); it also fires on stock completions such as 'hurricane season', 'Nusa Pen|ida' and 'db = SQLAlchemy(' in code.","s":[0.501681,1.037073,0.186872,0.619541,0.676459,0.007147,0.122054,0.02551,0.031913,0.433679,0.063475,0.044696,-0.000674,0.006829,0.027075]},{"p":"L09N9715-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires inside rote, highly predictable fixed strings, on the token that continues a memorized formula: standard titles in bibliographies (\"Mammal Species of the World\", \"Handbook of ...\"), web-page boilerplate (\"More From This Category\", \"Shop by category\", \"Other stories from this section\", \"Browsing Category\"), boilerplate code lines (express.Router();, setObjectName(\"menubar\")), URL parameters (&list=) and the digits of years.","s":[0.488935,0.616783,0.579392,0.259941,0.323246,0.084137,0.077069,0.026006,0.122866,0.119786,0.019677,0.0244,0.022354,0.033108,0.018387]},{"p":"L10N1890+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"In technical or software web text, fires on the token that directly continues a product, software or organization name: the digits and dot of its version number (WordPress 6.4, Go 1.6, Firefox 61, MariaDB 10.5) or the head noun completing the name (LLVM Project, Python installations, Start button, Bell Fibe TV, computing platform).","s":[0.710373,0.150151,0.412449,0.430015,0.243189,0.266659,0.085061,0.08285,0.103627,0.040152,0.036472,0.037946,0.047818,0.023084,0.016331]},{"p":"L10N1890-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the newline token that ends a line inside source code (C#, Python, JavaScript, SQL), especially a line that closes a statement or block (after `;`, `}`, `)`, or a comment line), saturating at the same maximum in almost any code file; it is a 'this newline is inside program code' detector, not a detector for newlines in prose.","s":[0.067138,0.002874,0.011027,0.026094,0.008494,0.010528,0.007568,0.0132,0.031368,0.01157,0.008871,0.003783,0.007336,0.005051,0.009087]},{"p":"L10N2884+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on nouns that name a concrete physical shape, form or structural object, often used figuratively or as a geometric image, such as sliver, funnel, pyramid, prism, frame, box, tube, sieve, mirror, layers, triangular, circle, or 'horn of' and 'beam of', with weaker firing on measurement units like mm or bit.","s":[0.461103,0.763038,0.254016,0.329051,0.558855,0.117805,0.066099,0.143343,0.04182,0.051513,0.030972,0.002331,0.033204,0.031414,0.04499]},{"p":"L10N2884-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the final digit of the four-to-five-digit registrant prefix in a DOI right after \"10.\" (e.g. the \"6\" in \"doi:10.2196/jmir\", the \"6\" in \"10.4236/\", the \"1\" in \"10.1561/\"), i.e. the last digit just before the slash in a DOI, with a weaker offshoot on the \"7\" of \"127.0.0.1\" in SQL dump headers.","s":[0.944568,0.781796,0.856954,0.075796,0.606284,0.080848,0.067253,0.259527,0.132847,0.084684,0.068278,0.050474,0.050426,0.055324,0.070012]},{"p":"L10N4243+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires inside arbitrary, memorized-looking identifier strings where the next piece is unpredictable: the digits and hyphens in DOIs, ISBNs and phone numbers, mid-word pieces of usernames, GitHub handles and blog subdomains, random letter or base64 runs, and the end of a person's name (an initial's period or closing tag) in long alphabetical name directories. It stays quiet on ordinary prose words.","s":[0.053873,0.1978,0.044263,0.066688,0.02044,0.03755,0.030665,0.014078,0.019818,0.012814,0.027091,0.043959,0.023993,0.03295,0.032896]},{"p":"L10N4243-","l":"UNREL","j":"00","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a digit inside the registrant number of a DOI, the four- or five-digit code right after \"10.\" and just before the slash (for example the 8 in \"doi: 10.4278/ajhp\" or the 5 in \"10.1615/ICHMT\"), as in reference lists and article metadata; it also fires on line breaks between statements in source code.","s":[0.943227,0.734876,0.104809,0.04389,0.138755,0.139104,0.053083,0.045255,0.030239,0.138755,0.005787,0.017904,0.007835,0.008957,0.013537],"u":{"0":[0.10693,0.094159,0.013853,0.827366,0.035566],"1":[0.016299,0.001455,0.021246,0.017838,0.016376]}},{"p":"L10N7279+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the function word or punctuation right after or at the end of a negated or disclaiming statement, i.e. the pivot that follows a denial or limitation such as 'this is not a tutorial: for that, see…', 'I don't mean X; that's…', 'not intended as a substitute for professional medical care', 'it isn't a true X — patients still…', or 'can't kill what's already dead'.","s":[1.101507,0.242922,0.693692,0.649431,0.309016,0.016914,0.01938,0.073581,0.048366,0.024829,0.023958,0.017796,0.047869,0.020643,0.074135]},{"p":"L10N7279-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the sentence-ending punctuation (period, question mark, or the paragraph break or connective like \"And\"/\"however\" right after it) that closes a sentence stating a drawback, risk, problem, or critical objection, such as \"...which creates stress and throws off your whole schedule.\" or \"...and you end up wasting your time.\", in explanatory or argumentative prose.","s":[0.230738,0.287059,0.182685,0.544241,0.231606,0.005428,0.032154,0.046491,0.047301,0.01486,-0.000196,0.031743,0.034376,0.034294,0.00425]},{"p":"L10N7517+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a word or name that repeats one already used earlier in the same text, especially when the earlier mention was a heading, title or capitalized label and the word is now being reused inside running prose (e.g. a heading 'Continuous testing', then later 'in a scaled, continuous manner'; 'HYDERABAD' then an address ending 'Hyderabad'), acting as an induction/copy signal rather than responding to any topic.","s":[0.073752,0.31984,0.044435,0.104386,0.065227,0.073277,0.047779,0.05697,0.032481,0.053789,0.06658,0.015933,0.05105,0.06152,0.081143]},{"p":"L10N7517-","l":"TWO-REL","j":"11","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a token whose continuation can be copied from earlier in the same text, most strongly on the first word-piece of a rare surname that already appeared earlier (e.g., 'son of Godlup Fr|abish' after 'John Frabish', or a founder 'Cosmo Cent|ifonti' after 'Centifonti Restaurant'), and also mid-way through exactly repeated rows in SQL dumps, tables or lists and on highly predictable words in stock textbook definitions.","s":[0.165959,0.794952,0.140679,0.062294,0.044887,0.05699,0.05643,0.133442,0.118252,0.018042,0.141423,0.121469,0.041303,0.08173,0.042218]},{"p":"L10N7669+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the continuation piece of a well-known multi-part proper name (a product, library or institution name), the token right after the first part where the rest of the name is fully predictable: the dot or 'Core' in Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore, 'Panel' in cPanel, 'Trader' in MetaTrader, 'Cola' in Coca-Cola, 'God' in 'kingdom of God', 'Code' in 'Indian Penal Code', 'Language' in 'Structured Query Language', 'API' in 'Java Persistence API'.","s":[0.588666,0.272024,0.433147,0.446883,0.50016,0.024495,0.176437,0.054008,0.015722,0.259837,0.063673,0.066657,0.065952,0.043623,0.077791]},{"p":"L10N7669-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the name of a rival or alternative technology product or service mentioned right after the dominant one it competes with, most strongly on \"Bing\" in phrases like \"Google or Bing\" or \"Google, Bing, and Yahoo\", and also on the second-tier entries in lists of competing systems (Galileo, BeiDou or BDS after GPS and GLONASS; Mastodon after Twitter and Facebook; Mistral after GPT, Claude and Gemini; Preact after React), often on the later word-piece of such a name.","s":[0.341086,0.659587,0.60383,0.637673,0.730419,0.01394,0.032447,0.188824,0.007694,0.008116,0.009723,0.01244,0.011471,0.010705,0.010006]},{"p":"L10N795+","l":"UNREL","j":"0000","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word 'in' right after 'A Study' in book subtitles of the form 'Title: A Study in Something'. Weaker, separate triggers are 'annualized/annualised' before a growth rate, 'parallel'/'Parallelism' pieces in computing or concurrency text, and 'anti-static'/'ESD' in product listings.","s":[0.547757,0.67764,0.494404,0.544396,0.589969,0.12007,0.026788,0.108606,0.141687,0.114201,0.081421,0.134908,0.013133,0.014385,0.128108],"u":{"0":[1.024252,0.809023,0.57826,1.110125,1.011805],"1":[0.57528,0.550976,0.539638,0.269159,0.461708],"2":[0.764944,0.343302,0.285917,0.29946,0.321439],"3":[0.794674,0.907824,0.801976,1.008694,0.904799]}},{"p":"L10N795-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires inside software and technical text, most reliably on the slash right after a mailing-list or project name in an archive URL path (e.g. the slash after 'pipermail/bitcoin-dev' or 'treeherder.mozilla.org/#'), and also on punctuation and identifiers within message- or event-handling code (send/receive, trigger, handler, response).","s":[0.711196,0.571429,0.639238,0.627458,0.318141,0.060618,0.063809,0.096336,0.06,0.041397,0.046178,0.008932,0.083975,0.051577,0.031811]},{"p":"L10N9464+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"A running-topic neuron for gearhead enthusiast writing (muscle cars, Harley and motorcycle exhausts, engine tuning, drag racing, ATVs, motorsport, plus similar hobby-gear talk such as audio gear, airsoft and ham radio): inside such casual magazine- or forum-style text it fires on ordinary connective tokens rather than on the technical nouns, especially contraction stems like \"don\", \"isn\", \"didn\" before an apostrophe-t, apostrophes in \"we've\", \"However\", sentence ends and paragraph breaks, and \"More information\".","s":[0.177826,0.23609,0.302021,0.204231,0.234404,0.033183,0.034817,0.255707,0.042685,0.26063,0.020644,0.020303,0.010861,0.022731,0.019439]},{"p":"L10N9464-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires throughout consumer product reviews and buyer's guides for vehicles and gear (motorcycles, cars, mountain bikes, scooters, kayaks, banjos, audio and phone specs), on almost any token, including apostrophes, commas, digits and words like bike, neck, drive or horsepower, wherever the text is describing a machine's hardware, specs or riding and driving qualities.","s":[0.137828,0.552004,0.219694,0.320886,0.48284,0.000363,0.160534,0.181514,0.023737,-0.000696,0.039233,0.029647,0.023131,0.023919,0.040479]},{"p":"L10N9543+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the sentence-ending punctuation (period, \"?!\", \"!\", \"?\") that closes an incoherent, grammatically scrambled word-salad sentence in auto-generated keyword-stuffed SEO spam (shuffled words about weight loss, diets, supplements, dog food or cars); punctuation after normal, coherent sentences on the same topics should stay quiet.","s":[0.043102,0.086563,0.025782,0.026575,0.01696,0.027668,0.018533,0.018411,0.010822,0.007318,0.031306,0.017359,0.014415,0.022913,0.018928]},{"p":"L10N9543-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the sentence-ending period (or ? / !) in spun or scraped SEO spam made of unrelated, randomly juxtaposed sentences, often with stray injected keyword phrases like 'where to meet singles' or 'wallhack download', where each sentence is a complete, factual-sounding statement and the next sentence switches to an unrelated topic; it builds up over many such incoherent sentences, so a short coherent text will not reproduce it.","s":[0.23595,0.447212,0.319009,0.207803,0.31891,0.009555,0.00955,0.007182,0.006685,0.000183,0.005689,0.008996,0.010713,-0.001635,-0.001226]},{"p":"L11N10044+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires inside imperative, second-person marketing slogans in product or hotel listings, on the words of a sales tagline like \"Take command of the road in the 2010 Silverado\", \"Carry your music wherever you go in style\", \"Rest and recharge in our rooms\", \"Introduce a pop of beauty to your space\", peaking on the verb, \"in/with/to\" or \"you\" just before the product is named.","s":[0.612345,0.710533,1.076064,0.554741,1.222916,0.05027,0.08914,0.010486,0.027998,0.007135,0.010929,0.01477,-1.1e-05,0.010352,0.012916]},{"p":"L11N10044-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires inside formulaic commercial/SEO web boilerplate (market-research report blurbs, e-commerce and supplier listings, product spec sheets, vacation-rental amenity lists), most strongly on the predictable next word of a stock marketing phrase or list item, such as 'share' in 'sales, revenue, market share and growth rate', 'Day' in 'Same Day Delivery', 'of' in 'Wholesale Supplier of', or 'Villas' in a rentals list.","s":[0.949393,0.364015,0.681573,0.617779,0.660941,0.042426,0.01605,0.01737,0.034101,0.031208,0.039368,0.186796,0.049753,0.039814,0.034918]},{"p":"L11N1220+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the stock boilerplate words of corporate risk disclosures and forward-looking-statement disclaimers, such as \"undue reliance (on)\", \"no assurance\", \"adversely affect\", \"adverse effect\", \"loss of key personnel\", \"our inability to\", \"highly speculative\" and \"competition\", where the phrase is predictable from the legal-cautionary language around it.","s":[0.597516,0.989828,0.935706,0.881418,0.712353,0.002959,0.007538,0.000564,0.000568,0.005433,0.010123,0.00832,0.006332,0.009401,0.006909]},{"p":"L11N1220-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":3,"e":0,"x":"Fires on sentence-ending periods inside incoherent, machine-generated spam prose (spun essay-writing / thesis-help SEO filler where each sentence is grammatical-looking word salad about research papers, theses and surveys), building up over a long run of such gibberish; it needs a long document of that spam and is weak or absent in a short, coherent text."},{"p":"L11N150+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on website navigation/sitemap structure: inside URL paths right after a generic site-section word (about, get-help, what-we-do, press-center, services, visit), on the slash or hyphen that follows it, or on the next section label in a run-together site menu bar (Home About Us News Room Careers Contact Us, Products, Downloads, Press Center).","s":[0.64896,0.528413,0.413337,0.455158,0.844174,0.009776,0.010871,0.009291,0.08406,0.019131,0.017153,0.009199,0.010674,0.012057,0.008551]},{"p":"L11N150-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the boilerplate interface text of websites, the navigation and widget chrome around an article rather than its content: most strongly on \"icon\" in WordPress's \"Fill in your details below or click an icon to log in:\" comment form, and also on breadcrumb separators (\">\" or \"›\" in \"Home > Category > Page\" or \"Filed in Featured > Reviews\"), \"Reject\" in cookie-consent banners, \"notifications\"/\"of\" in \"Send me notifications\"/\"Notify of\" comment widgets, and slashes inside long URL paths.","s":[0.608614,0.031898,0.349964,0.551017,0.530404,0.300334,0.030871,0.004384,0.024038,0.017394,0.00545,0.01578,0.01939,0.017183,0.011089]},{"p":"L11N3689+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the first or middle sub-word piece of an uncommon multi-token word or name (e.g. the 'br' of brats, 'gu' of guanciale, 'phen' of phenylephrine, 'Ow' of Owlcotes) when it appears as an item inside a dense list or enumeration (ingredient or menu lists, place-name lists, rosters, schedules, spec sheets), where the model must complete the word from context.","s":[0.261172,0.155514,0.177389,0.080835,0.05874,0.113033,0.123802,0.046966,0.028735,0.058438,0.004545,0.005388,0.013603,0.015333,0.01233]},{"p":"L11N3689-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on tokens that are almost fully determined by what came before: the second piece of a fixed multi-token name or string (\"AppleWebKit\" in a browser User-Agent, \"Magi\" in Puella Magi Madoka Magica, \"ces\" in Pisces, \"ars\" in Total Sugars), or a token that repeats a pattern already seen earlier in the text (repeated code calls like setVerticalStretch, repeated SVG path data, list items, dates like 2005), rather than on tokens with genuinely open next-word choices.","s":[0.579073,0.686806,0.212596,0.476211,0.052893,0.294995,0.207386,0.18266,0.041634,0.046594,0.038352,0.024817,0.046197,0.018936,0.06387]},{"p":"L11N843+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the first token of a proper name (usually a sports team, otherwise a player, company, ship or character) that is being mentioned again as the recurring subject of the passage, as in a game recap where 'Royal Challengers', 'Sunrisers' or 'Inter Miami' reappear; it is strongest when the name was already used earlier in the text, and weaker on the sentence breaks just before such a name.","s":[0.43134,0.133227,0.393192,0.279985,0.080281,0.381221,0.197657,0.308718,0.218236,0.052291,0.037482,0.050501,0.094331,0.040742,0.055484]},{"p":"L11N843-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a highly predictable continuation token, mostly a noun or identifier piece that completes a fixed compound or repeats a phrase already seen in the text (for example \"IPEnd|Point\" in code, \"chief executive officer\", \"wholly-owned subsidiary\", \"horse and rider\", a repeated \"Attorney, Civil Rights Division\", a repeated angle sign in phasors, or a repeated column name), rather than on any particular topic.","s":[0.02035,0.06255,0.16639,0.654408,0.03124,0.02435,0.079299,0.147745,0.15681,0.070934,0.022858,0.071102,0.023161,0.008164,0.040137]},{"p":"L11N8470+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the token right before an expected place name or proper-noun location completes a formal formula, above all the comma in address and dateline formats like 'Gainesville, FL 32611', 'Carlisle, PA', 'Mountain View, California' or the 'February 24, 2019 in Hollywood' comma of photo captions, and also on 'of'/'in' in names like 'Eastern District of Michigan'.","s":[0.460466,0.360909,0.051859,0.202031,0.721153,0.132665,0.040409,0.041231,0.078848,0.066741,0.031791,0.038394,0.055509,0.143678,0.024883]},{"p":"L11N8470-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires inside written dates and clock times, on the month name, the day number, the 'th', the comma after a time, or the 'a.m./p.m./PM' piece, especially in ranges like 'from 6am Monday to 6am Tuesday', 'between 1 April and 31 October 2006', or a signature like '17:19, 25 November 2006'.","s":[0.937776,0.538623,0.298593,0.34507,0.510005,0.046681,0.188711,0.028052,0.008596,0.018886,0.026769,0.087366,-0.000156,0.018635,0.02575]},{"p":"L11N8839+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the intensifier 'so' or 'such' (or the 'a/an/much/point' right after it) in result and manner constructions that set up a coming 'that…' or 'as to…' clause, e.g. 'in such a way that', 'so low that', 'such a high risk that', 'so regulated as to'.","s":[0.232884,0.40647,0.151151,0.321494,0.269317,0.007857,0.056542,0.013701,0.005646,0.025411,0.00724,0.017858,0.014544,0.01105,0.011994]},{"p":"L11N8839-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the stock word inside a fixed formal or legal phrase, the predictable completion of a set collocation in contracts, regulations and policy boilerplate: \"all practicable steps\", \"at our discretion\", \"every reasonable effort\", \"after full disclosure\", \"takes full account of\", \"to the extent reasonably necessary\", \"in good standing\", \"as soon as possible\", \"due care, reasonable skill\", including a \"the\" that fills a slot such as \"with the prior written approval\".","s":[0.931586,0.317943,1.029938,0.470113,0.715369,0.049781,0.037652,0.080072,0.017683,0.043506,0.076687,0.043562,0.05178,0.079608,0.035701]},{"p":"L11N9800+","l":"TWO-REL","j":"11","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on ordinary function words, sentence-ending periods and line breaks inside low-quality SEO spam web text, above all spun or garbled online-casino and sports-betting promotional articles (bonuses, deposits, slots, ufabet) and, secondarily, the contact or footer blocks of boilerplate market-research press releases; it tracks the spammy genre rather than any particular word.","s":[0.140265,0.176682,0.148625,0.233281,0.105352,0.060097,0.077027,0.116563,0.144124,0.072173,0.079838,0.113225,0.112156,0.112971,0.102628]},{"p":"L11N9800-","l":"TWO-REL","j":"11","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the bare line-break token inside source code (C#, Python, SQL, JavaScript), the newline that ends one statement, brace or comment line before the next indented line, and not on the code words themselves or on line breaks in ordinary prose.","s":[0.002549,0.004135,0.002384,0.002191,0.002715,-0.000772,0.004065,0.001737,0.002619,0.006768,0.000726,0.000641,-0.001097,5.1e-05,0.004381]},{"p":"L12N1429+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"In prose describing a hybrid or combination of two things in parallel form (\"use X for A and Y for B\", \"the speed of X, combined with the resilience of Y\", \"work days for one employer and nights for another\", \"start with a template, then edit the code\"), this fires on the preposition or comma right after the first half, where the second, contrasting half is expected next.","s":[0.153626,0.599903,0.153264,0.198062,0.171967,0.002908,0.009485,0.018671,0.033133,0.031111,0.006434,0.016575,0.011733,0.027946,0.025961]},{"p":"L12N1429-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"In a press-release dateline (CITY, State – Month Day, Year), fires on the comma between the day number and the year, e.g. the comma in \"SAN DIEGO, Sept. 20, 2018 /PRNewswire/\", and more weakly on similar location-completion tokens such as \"China\" after a province name.","s":[0.576991,0.413362,0.635226,0.442801,0.507589,0.129854,0.0276,0.025509,0.030275,0.23081,0.128078,0.065403,0.035228,0.109935,0.025013]},{"p":"L12N2349+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the parameter name \"id\" inside a web URL's query string, right after the \"?\" or \"&\" and just before \"=\" and a number (e.g. show_bug.cgi?id=592981, index.php?option=com_content&view=article&id=1001, story.php?story_fbid=…&id=…), and much more weakly on other highly predictable continuation tokens in URLs or in garbled, spun or OCR-noisy web text.","s":[0.555586,0.620705,0.600181,0.101217,0.346937,0.070718,0.108867,0.202726,0.060227,0.066895,0.078948,0.123899,0.079696,0.063707,0.117308]},{"p":"L12N2349-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word right after a verb or 'the' when that word turns the phrase into a fixed grammatical construction rather than its usual continuation: most strongly the adjective in 'make certain (that…)' and the first comparative in a 'the deeper/larger/higher X, the more Y' correlative, plus 'the minute/second (she opened the door)' used as 'as soon as'.","s":[0.10735,0.790683,0.482658,0.383665,0.07339,0.043213,0.112428,0.256179,0.189653,0.041845,0.068082,0.09552,0.106053,0.06902,0.037494]},{"p":"L12N2378+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a token in the middle of a fixed, nearly fully predictable continuation, where the next token is all but determined: \" et\" before \"al.\" in academic author citations, \"all over the\" before \"world\", \"at the same\" before \"time\", \"take into\" before \"consideration\", \"a block and a\" before \"half\", the next digit of a year or patent number, or the boilerplate line break before a known template line. It is strongest deep inside long academic or report-style prose with parenthetical citations, which is hard to reproduce in a short text.","s":[0.050759,0.024169,0.01359,0.030692,0.03587,0.039432,0.044144,0.019245,0.032622,0.041062,0.010961,0.015766,0.007701,0.026322,0.02213]},{"p":"L12N2378-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Inside formal academic paper prose, this pole fires on ordinary words, punctuation and digits in the sentence where the authors state the study's aim (\"The main objective of the present study was to identify factors associated with...\", \"the purpose of this work is to...\", \"Our goal was to...\"); it is a weak, dense, context-built signal that may need a longer scholarly passage before it to reach high values.","s":[0.045756,0.090306,0.071665,0.088223,0.083692,0.075912,0.090306,0.119204,0.094384,0.098554,0.22761,0.088665,0.115662,0.255756,0.162479]},{"p":"L12N3344+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a highly predictable word that continues a stock phrase or conventional list in formulaic boilerplate, such as copyright notices (\"copying, redistribution, retransmission\"), privacy policies (\"cookies, web beacons\"), safety data sheets (\"respiratory protective device\", \"contaminated packaging\") and return policies (\"returned or exchanged\"), with the strongest firing on the second or later item of these fixed sequences.","s":[0.080677,0.896661,0.342678,0.540102,0.423981,0.048832,0.013777,0.236549,0.018789,0.235527,0.054607,0.025721,0.06304,0.194984,0.041191]},{"p":"L12N3344-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the words right after an exception or limitation marker such as \"except (that/for/in the case of)\", \"other than\", \"apart from\", \"with the exception of\", \"no more than\" or \"limited to\", that is, on the start of the excepted or carved-out item (e.g. \"other than statements of historical fact\", \"apart from any fair dealing for the purpose of\", \"except for the very obvious\"), and is strongest in legal, copyright or disclaimer boilerplate.","s":[0.652033,0.396844,0.665488,0.208493,0.207157,0.043194,0.051804,0.149262,0.106032,0.032967,0.009162,0.01785,0.014265,0.028872,0.007492]},{"p":"L12N4911+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on paragraph breaks (a blank line between paragraphs), and occasionally on the first letter of a misspelled word, inside auto-generated SEO spam where search phrases such as \"calm mind pokemon\" or \"helsinki finland gay\" are stuffed ungrammatically into the prose and words carry deliberate typos (\"miind\", \"hameeg\", \"matchmaling\"); this depends on a long garbled document and is hard to reproduce in a short text.","s":[0.416351,0.283656,0.366722,0.294542,0.014088,0.006803,0.002068,0.00426,0.002868,0.006906,0.002055,0.003594,0.003922,0.001963,0.00181]},{"p":"L12N4911-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the words of an idiom that adds a contributing fact as a side factor: chiefly the verb in \"it didn't hurt / doesn't help that…\" (or \"…didn't hurt either\"), and also \"fact\"/\"that\" in a subject clause \"The fact (that) X…\" and \"just a bonus\" in \"…was just a bonus\".","s":[0.516888,0.402869,0.31653,0.496456,0.701168,0.006682,0.009556,0.007868,0.007717,0.016057,0.026427,0.015969,0.017256,0.022595,0.007012]},{"p":"L12N8208+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the connecting word right before a limit or range-endpoint number in spec, price or stock prose: the \"to\" in \"up to\" or \"down to\", the \"as\" in \"as little as\" or \"as much as\", the \"of\" in \"a 52-week low of\" or \"a 1-year high of\", \"extend\" in \"may extend to\", and \"exceeds\" or \"max\". Strongest when the next token is a quantity such as $13.49, 10,000 psi, 256GB or -40C.","s":[0.517036,0.303053,0.367964,0.441456,0.248414,0.392407,0.004324,0.039646,0.168222,0.006018,0.007329,0.004334,0.030436,0.015539,0.00666]},{"p":"L12N8208-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the field-name part of key–value metadata, especially the second piece of a compound label just before its colon or equals sign, such as the 'Type' in 'Content-Type:', the 'Number' in 'Issue Number:', the 'Size' in 'Font Size:', or 'HEADERS' in config['CORS_HEADERS'], found in code configs, HTTP headers, magazine and journal issue headers ('Vol. 12 No. 3') and product spec sheets.","s":[0.227011,0.055779,1.046589,0.253754,0.274489,0.063629,0.06587,0.071115,0.0175,0.0035,0.043515,0.030481,0.073354,0.014653,0.041374]},{"p":"L12N9137+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a line break (or line break plus indentation) that falls in the middle of a sentence in hard-wrapped, fixed-width prose, especially right after a word that cannot end a phrase, such as 'the', 'to', 'of', 'an', 'his' or 'their', so the sentence must continue on the next line; breaks at sentence or paragraph ends should not fire.","s":[0.008913,0.076669,0.213904,0.140885,0.017217,0.008913,0.011725,0.015034,0.010852,0.006073,0.033118,0.018902,0.034549,0.01807,0.005069]},{"p":"L12N9137-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a token in the middle of a long run of one repeated character, used as a divider, banner or fill (strings of dashes, equals signs, underscores, asterisks, hashes, dot leaders, or long runs of spaces), typically a 16-character chunk well inside the run rather than at its start, as in code-comment header banners, separator lines between solved problems, and table-of-contents dot leaders.","s":[0.479388,0.018379,0.409209,0.289342,0.174266,0.008565,0.020618,0.0082,0.016049,0.016768,0.00991,0.005693,0.006325,0.016749,0.014228]},{"p":"L12N9456+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"In spammy or boilerplate web prose (market-research report blurbs, essay-mill and dating-app review spam, copyright notices), fires on a whole-word adjective or determiner-like modifier that comes right before a noun, especially after 'my', 'the', 'an', 'any' or a comma in a list (e.g. 'my personal', 'an individual', 'recent developments', 'current market trends', 'the prior written permission').","s":[0.645726,0.511161,0.046016,0.13029,1.259955,0.028207,0.108459,0.014974,0.036136,0.031564,0.004145,0.002105,0.007805,0.003194,0.024679]},{"p":"L12N9456-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a generic positive or intensifying adjective/adverb (considerable, extensive, substantial, robust, great, good, detailed, thorough, impressive, healthy) placed right after \"a/an\" or a verb in boilerplate promotional prose, strongest in spammy market-research press releases (\"the report provides a detailed/substantial…\") and supplier or blog-spam praise (\"we have won a good reputation\", \"you have done an impressive job\").","s":[0.426471,0.320217,0.357962,0.379651,0.724916,0.117069,0.053072,0.035478,0.064267,0.009319,0.026302,0.009186,0.005614,0.028249,0.021869]},{"p":"L12N9942+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a noun inside a comma/\"and\" list of activities or offerings at an event, festival or venue, most strongly on \"music\" in phrases like \"food, drinks and live music\" or \"parades, dancing and music\" (also \"rides\", \"song\", \"face painting\", \"demonstration\"), i.e. a later item in an enumerated list of amenities or activities.","s":[0.762906,0.578806,0.843395,0.824205,0.564944,0.630125,0.230176,0.127226,0.014076,0.017445,0.037402,0.043567,0.025342,0.017032,0.027046]},{"p":"L12N9942-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the period (or comma or question mark) that closes a factual statistic sentence in SEO or blog web prose, typically an opening hook such as \"According to research, Americans check their phones 262 times a day.\" or \"Verizon serves 125.3 million subscribers.\"; it is weaker on ordinary sentences without such a quantified fact.","s":[0.390152,0.693902,0.305183,0.429554,0.272172,0.12678,0.290197,0.261735,0.020609,0.196342,0.082544,0.18741,0.225852,0.399099,0.072366]},{"p":"L13N1875+","l":"TWO-REL","j":"11","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires inside a range expression just before its second endpoint is reached—on the end of the first half of a span or the inside of the second endpoint: the year/number digits of the end of a range ('from 1673 to 1675', '1852 to 1865', '6-9 l'), 'from'/'of' in 'start at $10 and go up from there' or 'last from July 28 to', and the left/x1/min term in 'right - left', 'x2 - x1', 'max - min + 1'; its output suppresses 'until/till/upto', i.e. it marks a span whose end is being specified.","s":[0.099237,0.11859,0.120016,0.266524,0.380617,0.01756,0.029414,0.045929,0.046213,0.023266,0.025489,0.0173,0.012143,0.020444,0.017203]},{"p":"L13N1875-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word or symbol that opens a span or range, especially in titles and citations: \"to\", \"from\", \"since\", a dash between years, or the \"0\" right after \"Forecast 2\" in market-report titles, as in \"Forecast to 2028\", \"History of China from 1600\", \"Beginnings to 1600\", \"1932–1940\", \"interest from the date\" or \"back to the last day\".","s":[0.58728,0.525849,0.145235,0.650333,0.448403,0.58728,0.15577,0.003862,0.010438,0.083369,0.02005,0.013023,0.013529,0.011648,0.020492]},{"p":"L13N290+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"In keyword-stuffed SEO-spam pages about getting books, the pole fires on the spam verb or adjective (\"free\", \"buy\", \"epub\", \"ebook\", \"download\", \"read\", \"book\") when it starts a new sentence right after a period and introduces the page's repeated book-title keyword. Examples are \"free panzerkampfwagen 38t 2013\", \"buy einführung in die statistische datenanalyse\" and \"epub Uma ponte entre\". It works best when that same keyword phrase has already appeared several times in garbled filler prose.","s":[0.116131,0.131993,0.134974,0.125595,0.175234,0.101502,0.131875,0.113602,0.095428,0.214339,0.109746,0.079727,0.077364,0.047743,0.052354]},{"p":"L13N290-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the city name (often its first piece or the comma after it) in an address/affiliation/location line that follows an institution, company, hotel or event name, especially when the city repeats a name in the institution (e.g. \"University of Lethbridge, Lethbridge AB Canada\", \"Winona State University, Winona, Minnesota\").","s":[0.69727,0.437638,0.302266,0.60035,0.409456,0.378011,-0.001479,0.015144,0.037032,0.029264,0.012756,0.014362,0.014756,0.009259,0.003376]},{"p":"L13N414+","l":"UNK","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Best guess, held with low confidence: the pole fires on the second or third word of a headline, book or page title, or a multi-word name. This is strongest when the title opens a short document (\"Personal Power\", \"Former Speaker\", \"Congressional Briefing\") or when an earlier title phrase is repeated inside the text (\"30 days grace\", \"select text word\", \"html code image\"); otherwise it fires weakly and diffusely on ordinary prose.","s":[0.067805,0.030293,0.047426,0.017259,0.049185,0.070677,0.059897,0.085903,0.081916,0.0443,0.034947,0.010029,0.042177,0.04273,0.051962]},{"p":"L13N414-","l":"UNK","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the verb or auxiliary that comes right after the subject inside an embedded or relative clause, such as 'what programmers have long believed', 'a law that you can refer to', 'a signal that humans cannot detect' or 'which the public love to watch', marking the point where a subordinate clause's predicate begins; the pole is diffuse and weak elsewhere.","s":[0.260487,0.60606,0.036471,0.006113,0.018194,0.077688,0.052464,0.020811,0.007283,0.008419,0.020388,0.030578,0.026599,0.020403,0.013268]},{"p":"L13N5135+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the second word of a stock two-word collocation that has been wrongly spliced into a sentence, as in synonym-spun SEO text, where a word gets an extra habitual partner that breaks the grammar: 'Credit history Card', 'WordPress platforms Cache', 'Data files Architect', 'HTML CODE and JavaScript', 'lighter weight will do', 'years of age.' meaning years ago, 'lying down to you'.","s":[0.009891,0.003553,0.23372,0.007478,0.010302,0.0075,0.003643,0.00363,0.003711,0.01142,0.009282,0.011735,0.020395,0.01303,0.010801]},{"p":"L13N5135-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the second word of a highly predictable fixed two-word name or compound, completing it, most strongly on the corporate or organizational suffix right after a brand name (Sony Electronics, Adobe Systems, IBM Corporation, Penguin Group, Marriott International, Evonik Industries, Smithsonian Institution) and on stock collocations like 'free of charge', 'climate change', 'immune system', 'New York City' or 'federal government'.","s":[0.614636,0.798972,0.162417,0.542765,0.151783,0.041812,0.02349,0.06388,0.038521,0.037654,0.044071,0.015266,0.038196,0.031873,0.022799]},{"p":"L13N6288+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a token that is almost fully predictable because the text is reciting a memorized or boilerplate passage, such as a famous quotation, the Brundtland definition of sustainability, securities-law forward-looking-statement disclaimers or a standard list like the seven lean wastes, or because it is repeating a rare name or phrase given earlier in the same text. It fires on words or word pieces in the middle of such a recitation, not on the first, unpredictable words.","s":[0.684026,0.155254,0.756632,0.142395,0.208345,0.086726,0.018701,0.127604,0.044806,0.031586,0.020088,0.025832,0.042463,0.047622,0.119542]},{"p":"L13N6288-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a token that is a nearly forced, memorized continuation of what precedes it, most strongly in code: the method or identifier piece that completes a well-known API call (os.environ.get, Math.floor/Math.round, socket.SOCK_DGRAM, Environment.GetFolderPath, workbook.SheetNames, ProducesResponseType), and in prose the surname or word-end that completes a famous name or fixed phrase (Joseph Stiglitz, Trent Reznor, Roger Sherman, the prophet Joel, strict liability, the SHIFT key).","s":[0.93475,0.455864,0.078531,0.259661,0.06321,0.086803,0.036255,0.046169,0.01562,0.033682,0.061795,0.03394,0.017943,0.03693,0.02966]},{"p":"L13N7364+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word that completes a stock, memorized boilerplate phrase in legal or web disclaimer text, such as \"cost\" in the affiliate disclosure \"we may earn a commission at no extra cost to you\", \"search\" in the wiki header \"Jump to navigation Jump to search\", \"obligation\" in \"the Company undertakes no obligation to update\", or the comma after \"national origin\" in equal-opportunity employer statements; the same words used in ordinary, non-formulaic prose should not fire it.","s":[0.894669,0.414912,0.583414,0.583532,0.358966,0.236065,0.040675,0.019512,0.063615,0.043214,0.195988,0.056248,0.017123,0.070896,0.063642]},{"p":"L13N7364-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on English sentence adverbs that mark something as self-evident or expected — chiefly \"Naturally\" (and the comma right after it), \"of course\" (on \"course\"), \"obviously\", and \"necessarily\" in the stock phrase \"forward-looking statements are necessarily based upon\" — when used as a discourse aside commenting on the preceding sentence.","s":[0.497691,0.291498,0.256211,0.494354,0.401166,0.015223,0.020977,0.004546,0.066245,0.003433,-0.000704,0.002661,0.00541,-0.001544,0.011622]},{"p":"L13N7491+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires inside a clause that explains why some apparent concern, quality or feature does not really matter (e.g. \"the read speed is irrelevant because you'll be using...\", \"it won't matter since it gets diluted anyway\", \"I'd be out most of the time anyway\"), peaking on the words and punctuation of that dismissive 'it's unimportant because / anyway' justification.","s":[0.819829,0.471286,0.965736,0.526299,0.884446,0.174967,0.015768,0.067921,0.12426,0.084815,0.030996,0.004197,0.026471,0.007711,0.009676]},{"p":"L13N7491-","l":"UNK","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word right after a negation or other downward-entailing trigger, inside its scope, especially in stock 'no need / not much of / wouldn't need / in no event…any' phrasing (e.g. 'we have no need for', 'there isn't much of a point', 'a scenario where you'd need', 'not hurting for money'); otherwise it fires weakly and diffusely on highly predictable continuations of fixed phrases.","s":[0.125816,0.144121,0.565825,0.042473,0.067162,0.116148,0.17508,0.194332,0.037006,0.033969,0.071691,0.018728,0.068027,0.034485,0.035366]},{"p":"L13N8332+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a verb, participle or adjective that repeats the meaning of its own subject, producing a redundant, tautological phrase. Typical cases are \"the price is currently offered at\", \"the importance is very important\", \"the likelihood is highly likely\", \"support will be supported\", \"the opinions are the views\", \"has grown\" after \"expansions\", and \"height is 1.70m tall\"; it fires at the echoing word itself, in prose of any topic.","s":[0.624385,0.407705,0.217618,0.743963,0.238778,0.003738,0.047986,0.053951,0.00416,0.013744,0.006167,0.012011,0.002316,0.012677,0.013306]},{"p":"L13N8332-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a predictable content word that completes a stock phrase deep in a long, list-heavy expository sentence and is about to take a following preposition or clause, most strongly in market-research boilerplate (\"growth rate of each type\", \"revenue and market share\", \"analysis based on each segment\") and also in phrases like \"being able to\", \"the ability to\", \"a sense of\", \"depending on\" and \"brought about by\".","s":[0.554733,0.368637,0.290901,0.105016,0.243624,0.165098,0.0672,0.030287,0.043151,0.054186,0.033871,0.05744,0.056316,0.056236,0.040122]},{"p":"L13N9495+","l":"TWO-REL","j":"11","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires in text about a back catalogue of previously produced or published things, most sharply on the word \"archive\" or \"archived\" in publisher boilerplate such as \"get access to the full archive\" or \"explore our archive\", and more diffusely through retrospective writing about older models or editions, like a used-car buyer's guide to a model's past engines.","s":[0.41627,0.278818,0.210018,0.526063,0.395645,0.053239,0.019922,0.015757,0.041433,0.013572,0.02309,0.041516,0.049439,0.02777,0.030949]},{"p":"L13N9495-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires inside a well-known multi-token proper name or fixed phrase, on a middle piece whose continuation is nearly certain, especially subword fragments of famous people's first names (Ars|ene Wenger, Ty|reek Hill, Moqt|ada Sadr, Th|adde|us Young) and inner words of set names (Office of War Mobilization, Trans-Pacific Partnership, World War I, Covid-19), in news or sports prose.","s":[0.146678,0.019813,0.468279,0.212566,0.098027,0.039926,0.019366,0.016532,0.028176,0.0725,0.072293,0.036603,0.023606,0.02534,0.015635]},{"p":"L14N2316+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires inside statements that two or more things are equal, identical, or unaffected, such as \"have the same cost\", \"exactly the same experience\", \"feels no different than\", \"no extra charge\" or \"pronounced the same way\", peaking on the comparison word itself (same, different, extra, loss, change) or on the noun or comma right after it.","s":[0.129944,0.092127,0.21041,0.211441,0.161802,0.014047,0.012789,0.026878,0.251856,0.067173,0.010996,0.04752,0.009809,0.037051,0.01323]},{"p":"L14N2316-","l":"TWO-REL","j":"11","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word that completes a highly predictable stock phrase or boilerplate formula, the nearly forced final word of a cliché such as 'now in the public domain', 'Spoiler alert', '100% brand new', 'as the name suggests', 'that's all I have to say', 'average density' or 'forward-looking statements are only predictions', and also on digit pieces inside DOI prefixes such as '10.24953'.","s":[0.395927,0.054754,0.241483,0.163661,1.002518,0.069321,0.064847,0.040579,0.068144,0.08248,0.036634,0.040934,0.023179,0.045933,0.076655]},{"p":"L14N2741+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on words in the middle of stock legal boilerplate whose wording is memorized and highly predictable: securities 'forward-looking statements' disclaimers (e.g. 'Forward-looking statements are subject to / based on / reflect...'), the closing formula of patents ('many changes may be made thereto without departing from the spirit and scope of the invention'), and article copyright footers. Ordinary prose on the same topics does not fire it.","s":[0.450034,0.915207,0.419677,0.045423,1.156448,0.049319,0.048764,0.074962,0.045479,0.062934,0.030096,0.055697,0.067074,0.02774,0.043966]},{"p":"L14N2741-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Inside a regular expression in code, fires on the end of a character class or escape that is about to take a {n,m} repetition count: the final letter of a range like a-z or A-Z, or the ']{' token itself. The strongest case is the top-level-domain part of an email-validation regex such as [a-zA-Z]{2,} or [a-z]{2,4}.","s":[0.919205,0.461036,0.597594,0.510957,0.170623,0.919205,0.162941,0.254416,0.333587,0.029753,0.13221,0.0631,0.126479,0.022198,0.008648]},{"p":"L14N3525+","l":"TWO-REL","j":"11","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the line break (or indentation) right after a complete statement, member declaration or closing brace inside C#/JavaScript class code, i.e. the boundary before the next member or statement (e.g. after `public int Health;` or `this.score = 0;` or `}` ending a constructor); it also fires more weakly on sentence-ending periods in repetitive SEO spam prose such as online-pokies casino filler.","s":[0.162318,0.379139,0.400174,0.216632,0.182263,0.23354,0.203993,0.118882,0.06419,0.221151,0.139371,0.063448,0.031475,0.103177,0.20172]},{"p":"L14N3525-","l":"UNK","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"In ordinary English prose with two or more parties (people, groups, or a person and a thing), this fires on a pronoun, possessive or the following verb at a point where the reader must work out which party is doing or owning something, especially when the referent switches away from the most recent subject (e.g. \"He flung his towel\" meaning the other man, \"change his mind for him\", \"they don't want to help\", \"it can't fix itself\").","s":[0.052256,0.161332,0.018092,0.233671,0.058978,0.013801,0.040041,0.023055,0.007522,0.00733,0.0049,0.013651,0.012597,0.004094,0.002695]},{"p":"L14N5064+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word \"published\" (or its translation, e.g. \"опубликован\", \"gepubliceerd\") completing the boilerplate blog comment-form notice \"Your email address will not be published\", usually right after a \"Leave a Reply\"/\"Add a Comment\" heading at the end of a web article.","s":[0.352029,0.827399,0.723261,0.67934,0.40214,0.159028,0.203506,0.300993,0.23918,0.017412,0.061102,0.025924,0.020697,0.026332,0.040178]},{"p":"L14N5064-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires inside administrative metadata fields of web listings and source files, most strongly on the middle digits of ID numbers (job posting or requisition IDs like \"Requisition ID: 2301984\" or \"Job Posting: JC25870542\") and on the label words around them (\"Posted Date\", \"Requisition ID\", \"Job Type Full-Time Regular\", \"$Id: //depot/...\" version headers), especially in job-advertisement boilerplate.","s":[0.917723,0.944111,0.787637,0.796136,0.995989,0.061764,0.059991,0.007739,0.015506,0.03093,0.015561,0.047409,0.009233,0.008501,0.011741]},{"p":"L14N6393+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on words naming gun violence and shootings as a societal issue, the token \"shooting(s)\" after \"mass\" or \"school\", \"gun\" in \"gun violence/gun control/gun rights\", \"shot\" in a shooting report, \"shoot up\", the AR-15, and Uvalde, mostly in news or commentary about American mass/school shootings.","s":[0.576983,0.606464,0.529328,0.54211,0.842655,0.008448,0.01746,0.007801,0.000926,0.003845,0.009455,0.001064,0.028131,0.009543,0.007212]},{"p":"L14N6393-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires negatively on a token in the middle of a fixed boilerplate or numeric formula where the next token is highly predictable: the legalese 'nor any guaranty', 'without departing from the spirit and intent', unit 'lb' in a spec, mid-number digits in long floats and DOIs, and the 'None' in a Python call.","s":[0.121072,0.614687,0.159067,0.27714,0.041564,0.137515,0.128345,0.20419,0.098597,0.103502,0.059857,0.058503,0.04469,0.134265,0.053321]},{"p":"L14N6656+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a token that begins (or sits inside) a multi-piece proper name or code identifier whose remaining pieces the model can predict, most strongly when that same name or identifier already appeared earlier in the text and is now being repeated (e.g. the second 'Crow' of Crowbar, 'LIK' of a LIKES constant reused in code, 'Pilgrim' in a repeated blog title).","s":[0.061295,0.023525,0.026957,0.04549,0.048377,0.02352,0.038129,0.020742,0.040132,0.028457,0.021485,0.01404,0.016065,0.010998,0.016276]},{"p":"L14N6656-","l":"TWO-REL","j":"11","f":0,"e":0,"x":"In code (mostly Python), fires on an imported module name used as the first token of an expression right after =, (, not, in or at the start of a statement, e.g. the os in \"x = os.path.join(...)\", sys in \"print(..., file=sys.stderr)\", time in \"self.t = time.time()\", datetime in \"now = datetime.datetime.now()\"; it is about predicting the module's attribute that follows, and it also fires weakly on the first piece of a rare proper name split into pieces in prose (Ren|an, Dal|mau).","s":[0.321549,0.515169,0.422022,0.053339,0.082816,0.015554,0.023544,0.007646,0.003964,0.025034,0.014039,0.014463,0.00622,0.009673,0.002282]},{"p":"L14N676+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the first word (or first word-piece) of a proper title or name that immediately follows a descriptive noun phrase and a comma or the word 'called/named', i.e. the opening token of a named work, product, event or organization introduced in apposition (\"his new documentary, Travis: ...\", \"a bootloader called Timonel\", \"our newsletter, Crash Course\").","s":[0.190707,0.014534,0.31216,0.096314,0.146605,0.0195,0.008352,-6e-06,0.017016,0.023718,0.013008,0.023218,0.012263,0.023396,0.025163]},{"p":"L14N676-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the first or a middle piece of a multi-token name, compound identifier or term that already appeared earlier in the same text, at the point where the model can copy its continuation (e.g., the second mention of 'MouseWait', 'Shocktop', 'Parole Officer', 'Financial Engineering', 'ItemsControl').","s":[0.044214,0.034594,0.03943,0.102347,0.076658,0.01964,0.04589,0.030777,0.023804,0.003726,0.067698,0.040967,0.070821,0.057225,0.044]},{"p":"L14N682+","l":"TWO-REL","j":"11","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a closing emoticon, emoji or symbol token tacked onto the end of a sentence or message, most strongly smileys like :), =), 🙂, 😀, :D and (: right after a friendly sign-off or thanks in comments, blogs and chat (also closing \"-*-\"/\"_*_\" coding-line markers, a final \"!\" or \"(*)\", and next/last arrows like \">>\"), not on the words themselves.","s":[0.421005,0.392741,0.109395,0.393602,0.123976,0.010029,-0.007954,0.012029,0.014483,-0.000413,0.033013,0.007118,0.007346,0.009754,0.018777]},{"p":"L14N682-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the last word of a short stock interface or boilerplate phrase that addresses the reader, such as an imperative call-to-action or placeholder ending in \"now\", \"here\" or \"below\" (\"Upload it now\", \"Subscribe Now\", \"// your code goes here\", \"Write your query below\", \"comment below\"), or a canned UI message (\"Oops! Something went wrong\", \"Good luck\", \"Happy Coding\").","s":[0.536559,0.298207,0.759274,0.314949,0.453518,0.060088,0.024108,0.070259,0.040591,0.035565,0.024732,0.010879,0.032306,0.026523,0.024384]},{"p":"L14N8610+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a vague, all-purpose abstract noun (conditions, risks, circumstances, characteristics, aspects, things, factors, benefits, criteria, meaning) that heads a noun phrase in formal expository or legal/financial prose, especially in list-like risk disclaimers such as forward-looking statements, typically right after an adjective or 'other' and right before a qualifying phrase like 'of…' or 'in…'.","s":[0.024279,0.015128,-0.000673,0.003683,0.017972,0.010464,0.014618,0.008046,0.013505,0.019935,0.026182,0.015962,0.021092,0.02593,0.015452]},{"p":"L14N8610-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires inside boilerplate legal disclaimers, chiefly the forward-looking-statements / safe-harbor paragraph of corporate press releases, on the stock words of the fixed formula (\"risks, uncertainties and other factors that may/could cause actual results to differ materially from\"), with similar boilerplate (patent 'spirit and scope' clauses, library rights notices) as weaker offshoots.","s":[0.850227,1.084473,0.706793,0.78221,0.773734,0.049092,0.020169,0.144987,0.06274,0.044761,0.011564,0.00522,0.038974,0.059596,-0.001743]},{"p":"L15N1275+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the final word-piece of an unusual multi-token string (a rare acronym, spam keyword phrase, odd compound or name) when that exact string, or its parallel twin, already appeared earlier in the text, so the piece is predictable by copying. Examples are the 'UA' in a repeated 'LGBTUA+', the last word of a repeated spam phrase like 'cbd oil for life', the 'Cr' of 'YCbCr' after 'YPbPr', and 'hood' in 'brotherhood/sisterhood'.","s":[0.038541,0.034926,0.144657,0.100735,0.11658,0.028412,0.021299,0.018053,0.017595,0.027938,0.010908,0.025494,0.0225,0.0362,0.010826]},{"p":"L15N1275-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the final word of a fixed multiword idiom or set phrase whose completion is fully predictable from the first part, especially repeated or mirrored compounds like \"step by step\", \"day by day\", \"day to day\", \"year-on-year\", \"door to door\", \"one-on-one\", \"Bye Bye\", plus \"in-depth\" and \"be-all\", in ordinary English web prose.","s":[0.310245,0.261415,0.322995,0.584869,0.381518,0.028911,0.010065,0.160455,0.057071,0.062708,0.049,0.011254,0.021248,0.095119,0.023608]},{"p":"L15N1284+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on nearly every token, including function words and punctuation, inside sentences that describe stock exchanges, market indices and exchange-traded securities in a definitional or disclaimer style (e.g. 'The S&P 500 Index consists of 500 stocks chosen for market size, liquidity, and industry group representation', 'securities that do not trade on a major U.S. exchange', 'live stock prices from BSE, NSE')—not on general money, business or investing talk.","s":[0.899849,0.444665,0.808917,0.281111,0.719484,0.008703,0.014908,0.006778,0.00027,0.01921,0.011029,0.004031,0.007855,0.002658,0.019346]},{"p":"L15N1284-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires inside the formulaic bibliographic/publication-metadata boilerplate of scholarly articles, on the tokens of dates, years, volume numbers and stock license phrases, especially in 'Received ... Accepted ... Published 17 January 2017' header lines, digits inside a citation's year or volume (e.g. '20|1|1', 'Computing, 20(3)'), and 'Originally published in' or 'open access article distributed under the terms of the Creative Commons' notices.","s":[0.679626,0.573418,0.259754,0.314652,0.881212,0.008532,0.010265,0.038338,0.019376,0.103975,0.015314,0.016833,0.026903,0.015829,0.019882]},{"p":"L15N2145+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires inside descriptions of negative or maladaptive personality traits and behavioral dispositions, strongest in clinical personality-disorder criteria (borderline, histrionic, attachment styles) and lists of character-flaw adjectives (stubborn, willful, temperamental, unstable), landing on the trait words and the function words between them rather than on any single token.","s":[0.506523,0.623952,0.360707,0.365886,0.378788,0.07923,0.237833,0.09507,0.060679,0.039807,0.039904,0.044859,0.042209,0.037626,0.029498]},{"p":"L15N2145-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires in narratives about one party restraining, holding back, pulling away or counseling moderation toward a hothead or aggressive party, on the word naming that agent or restraining act (e.g. \"cooler heads\", \"hotheads\", \"war hawks\", \"wise counsel\", \"curbed\", \"pulled him away\", \"grabbed his arm\", \"kept her under control\", \"moderate Trump\", \"dragged kicking and screaming\"), anticipating the restraint that follows.","s":[0.401614,0.848533,0.735995,0.822164,0.903714,-0.000894,0.030796,0.062466,0.156086,-0.000462,0.008848,-0.004695,0.011047,-0.001275,0.000333]},{"p":"L15N2237+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the words of Wall Street analyst-rating boilerplate in stock news, such as analysts, consensus, rating, target price, research report, raised or upgraded from \"hold\" to \"buy\", and the function words between them, whenever the text is describing brokers' buy/hold/sell ratings and price targets on a ticker.","s":[0.965665,1.08534,0.526041,0.809087,1.020038,0.036318,0.006422,0.00305,0.123344,0.005727,0.004054,0.005185,0.008742,0.004114,0.004033]},{"p":"L15N2237-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires in text about publishing personal or private stories of real people, such as tell-all books, memoirs and biographies (including their publication years and 'edited by'/'paid by' credits), royal-family gossip about Prince Harry, Meghan Markle and Princess Diana, and the case-report line 'consent was obtained from the patient for publication of this case report'; it peaks on tokens like 'publication', 'tell-all', 'book', 'former servants', royal names or the first digit of a publication year.","s":[1.056985,0.940182,1.053525,1.362858,1.065036,0.680176,0.022455,0.358719,0.024733,0.035995,0.019111,0.017561,0.042821,0.016315,0.023969]},{"p":"L15N2311+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the connective in a two-way disjunction, above all the \"or\" right after a slash in \"and/or\" (also \"or/and\" and plain \" or\"/\" and\" joining two alternatives, as in legal clauses and license text such as \"use, copy, modify... and/or sell\" or \"(2) ...; or (3) ...\"), predicting the second alternative comes next.","s":[0.063028,0.155208,0.036421,0.043293,0.128303,0.063028,0.033774,0.036997,0.046932,0.034244,0.030099,0.049086,0.022534,0.022971,0.041814]},{"p":"L15N2311-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word \"or\" and the token right after it (often a comma or \"the\") when \"or\" introduces an alternative title or an equivalent name for the same thing rather than a real choice, as in old double book titles (\"Bracebridge Hall, or, The Humorists\"; \"Rousseau; or, Education according to Nature\"), \"Return on Equity or ROE\", and \"A schematic, or schematic diagram\".","s":[0.798316,0.410526,0.265998,0.284239,0.251789,0.028478,-0.002513,-0.00121,0.032569,0.016709,-0.00074,-0.001585,0.003706,-0.001279,0.002995]},{"p":"L15N2472+","l":"TWO-REL","j":"11","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the Python string-formatting % operator right after a quoted format string containing two or more placeholders (e.g. 'Name: %s, age: %d' % (name, age)), at the point where several values are about to be combined into one string; weaker related firing in prose on words that join multiple alternatives or quantities, such as 'or both', 'two or more races', 'an equivalent combination of', or the leading digit of a summed total.","s":[0.12865,0.363118,0.368775,0.165253,0.599497,0.013297,0.010289,0.014504,0.010839,0.014605,0.02329,0.018126,0.013143,0.019617,0.020551]},{"p":"L15N2472-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on words naming a tension or coexistence of opposites — paradox, contradiction(s), contrast(s), juxtaposition, dualism, dichotomy, dilemma, inconsistencies, holding conflicting ideas at once — and on the token or punctuation right after a phrase describing something as two opposing things at the same time.","s":[0.648052,0.345883,0.852568,0.378486,0.080833,0.071723,0.353317,0.056392,0.070638,0.057416,0.015576,0.07835,0.015378,-0.000255,0.004645]},{"p":"L15N2952+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires inside promotional web-page copy for free online tools and services (unit or file converters, browser games, chat sites, no-signup web apps), especially on the closing call-to-action words and punctuation that pitch the service as free, instant and usable in a browser with no download or registration (e.g. 'play it on our site for free.', 'type in your numbers to convert!', 'no registration required').","s":[0.463584,0.573264,0.643061,0.56055,0.425339,0.120343,0.012719,0.016192,0.017157,0.008126,0.013826,0.012529,0.014746,0.011639,0.015657]},{"p":"L15N2952-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the words of mobile-app download and availability boilerplate, such as \"download our app on Google Play or the App Store\", \"available on iOS and Android\", \"get breaking news on your phone\" and itunes app-store links; it activates on the store names, the prepositions, the digits of app IDs and the closing period, wherever the reader is being pointed to a smartphone app.","s":[0.354228,0.379453,0.988009,0.457573,0.36325,0.038481,0.032487,0.011721,0.144544,0.112447,0.011432,0.010558,0.008869,0.001557,0.010236]},{"p":"L15N7970+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires mid-phrase where a sentence tallies what was gained, received or resulted from something, especially on the small word or comma just before the payoff is named, as in \"all they got in return was…\", \"all he had for it was…\", \"a groan was the only answer\", \"his ruin would be the certain consequence\", \"in exchange for\", or after \"Besides / Aside from / Apart from X,\" and \"not only X but also\".","s":[0.405344,0.417829,1.082268,0.467515,0.212723,0.013994,0.004727,0.017246,0.012163,0.021055,0.042732,0.013804,0.017462,0.001741,0.018155]},{"p":"L15N7970-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the noun naming a document in boilerplate market-research press releases and investor-relations disclaimers, most strongly on \"report\" or \"study\" in phrases like \"according to a new market research report published by …\" or \"a recent report by … titled …\", and also on \"statements\" in \"this release contains certain forward-looking statements\".","s":[0.65464,0.153172,0.347475,0.358231,1.085158,0.01739,0.126417,0.095319,0.007428,0.03195,0.011963,0.039389,0.018007,0.016917,0.022622]},{"p":"L15N9089+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word \"business\" (or a stand-in such as \"enterprise\", \"company\" or \"organization\") when it means commercial dealings between trading parties, typically in supplier and exporter boilerplate such as \"sincerely do business with you\", \"long-term business relationship\", \"business partner\" or \"business terms\", and weaker on the word right after it (\"in\", \"with\", \"and\").","s":[0.690614,0.5398,0.662368,0.227791,0.525968,0.179372,0.049259,0.006885,0.01095,0.009792,0.00616,0.006574,0.006627,0.0111,0.010268]},{"p":"L15N9089-","l":"TWO-REL","j":"11","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires in the narrative connective words of retold Bible and Gospel passages, such as \"then he said to them,\", \"and\", \"sent him on his way\" or \"took bread and said\", in the stretch just before Jesus or another speaker delivers a well-known quoted line; it fires more weakly on similar formulaic lead-ins to fixed text, such as \"I hereby declare that\" in a thesis or Console.WriteLine($\" in beginner code.","s":[0.536207,0.357591,0.796177,0.358498,0.293775,0.133232,0.066543,0.449567,0.099723,0.213876,0.011891,0.034537,-0.005992,0.015647,-0.001226]},{"p":"L16N2951+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"In product listings and craft/print descriptions, fires on the prepositions and dimension separators that describe how flat solid items such as prints, paper, sheets, plates or bags are packed, wrapped, sandwiched or sized, e.g. \"packed in a cellophane sleeve, nestled between cardboard\", \"protected beneath a clear cover\", \"Packaging: 100pcs/bag, carton size 54*40*40cm\", \"18 x 18 cm\".","s":[0.73551,0.922644,0.754658,0.613212,0.907488,-0.001296,0.018956,0.034227,0.028114,0.011209,0.021841,0.013605,0.007158,0.017543,0.026711]},{"p":"L16N2951-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on text about liquid held in containers, most strongly on the digit that starts a fluid-ounce or gallon conversion right after a milliliter or liter amount (e.g. \"180 ml / 6 fl oz\", \"355 mL (12 oz) cans\"), and on words in container-emptying or packaging-disposal sentences such as \"emptied containers may retain product residue\" or \"never return spills to original containers\".","s":[0.535207,0.470989,0.671023,0.34741,0.586238,0.038026,0.020938,0.009162,0.016572,0.011975,0.0063,0.012347,0.009926,0.017049,0.009895]},{"p":"L16N4226+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires inside a web headline or product-title line (SEO article titles, listicle or review headings, product names, URL slugs of product names), peaking on the year (\"2020\", \"2023\") or the colon, dash or word that ends the main title and leads into its subtitle, as in \"Online casinos 2020: discover the list\" or \"Wordle today: Here's the answer\". Ordinary prose that mentions a year or uses a colon does not fire it.","s":[0.430962,0.435069,0.198825,0.249711,0.499377,0.019729,0.007255,0.009169,0.16038,0.015925,0.008098,0.011536,0.012163,0.002198,0.015787]},{"p":"L16N4226-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the final token that closes a long, run-on keyword-stuffed title or identifier, most strongly the last digit of the closing year in SEO market-research report titles (\"...Size, Share, Trends, Growth and Forecast to 2028\"), and also the last word of long e-commerce product titles (\"...Battery and AC Adapter Included\") and the digits of long numeric IDs in URLs.","s":[0.644663,0.518768,0.528369,0.136835,0.641838,0.011471,0.020307,0.013812,0.022457,0.017275,0.016626,0.017209,0.014671,0.016466,0.022889]},{"p":"L16N4630+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Inside source code (JavaScript or Python, especially beginner exercise or data-handling scripts), fires on the token just before a literal value is written, such as the indentation or comma before the next quoted string in an array list, the colon after an object key, or the '==', 'is', or comma before a compared string or type like 'male', '1', or str.","s":[0.510687,0.374934,0.085371,0.213335,0.238976,0.017333,0.032567,0.039004,0.233439,0.045278,0.016855,0.044004,0.02943,0.045111,0.011884]},{"p":"L16N4630-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires deep inside dense, repetitive technical text, mainly garbled PDF-extracted engineering solution math (strings of numbers, units like kpsi or lbf, and labels like \"Eq. (6-14):\") and repetitive code such as SQL, JS or Python, on mid-sequence tokens (digits, decimal points, spaces, underscores, operators) that continue a pattern already seen earlier. It needs long repetitive context and is hard to reproduce in a short standalone text.","s":[0.412722,0.035663,0.017366,0.010231,0.058939,0.022558,0.023429,0.044859,0.014447,0.021836,0.01753,0.012804,0.015469,0.021221,0.03049]},{"p":"L16N4652+","l":"UNREL","j":"00","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word that completes a casual reassurance that something is easy or obvious, such as 'easy' in 'converting it is easy' or 'there is an easy fix', 'science' in 'it's not rocket science', 'forward' in 'pretty straight forward', 'all' in 'not hard at all', 'do' in 'all you have to do', or a tag like ', remember?'; its output then suppresses words like simple, basic, plainly and easily.","s":[0.25165,0.020149,0.046527,0.218286,0.090558,0.120832,0.020149,0.036134,0.010437,0.029097,0.053814,0.051336,0.028205,0.021594,0.010251],"u":{"0":[0.124233,0.151539,0.185627,0.028868,0.196843],"1":[0.329132,0.05048,0.055535,0.055183,0.081552]}},{"p":"L16N4652-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires inside exasperated rhetorical complaints that something should be easy or obvious for someone to do but they failed to do it, peaking on the word 'hard'/'difficult' in 'Is it really that hard to…' / 'How hard can it be…' and on nearby tokens of 'Come on, people', 'all he had to do was…', or 'they could certainly afford it' in reviews and forum rants.","s":[0.764945,0.906328,0.520189,0.833758,1.053274,0.732972,0.016173,0.0142,0.009159,0.028682,0.018394,0.026334,0.020029,0.011156,0.006241]},{"p":"L16N5034+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the digits, spaces and decimal points of a number that is a deterministic conversion of a value just given in another unit or form (the km figure after \"mi /\", proof after % ABV, Celsius after kelvins, Fahrenheit after Celsius, decimal/percent after a fraction), i.e. where the model is computing an equivalent value rather than reading a free number.","s":[0.52646,0.9067,0.651352,0.51173,0.229929,0.028287,0.042394,0.078146,0.05922,0.056556,0.031528,0.035365,0.013088,0.028359,0.052784]},{"p":"L16N5034-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires where text states or anticipates that a quantity is held at, or constrained to equal, a fixed known constant or target. The strongest firing is on the tokens just before that value and on its digits: probabilities or weights summing to 1, table totals of 100.0 or 100%, identities like sin²θ + cos²θ = 1, (f∘g)(x) = x, a central bank keeping inflation stable at its 2% target over the medium term, and block time kept at ten minutes.","s":[0.250298,0.494843,0.532311,0.685363,0.465573,0.12016,0.178655,0.004422,0.018598,0.013203,0.009571,0.004638,0.005299,0.010628,0.005561]},{"p":"L16N6415+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the words just before a mention of what is left after something has been removed, excluded, invalidated or skipped (e.g. on 'affect the validity of' / 'the' in a severability clause before 'the remaining provisions' or 'any other part', 'the sum of the' before 'others' after a die is discarded, 'arrange what' before 'is left', 'and the' before '2nd and subsequent lines'), predicting words like 'other', 'remaining' or 'rest'.","s":[0.560702,1.089914,0.24834,0.007971,0.308817,0.008653,0.017609,0.019397,0.008157,0.015773,0.016585,0.019376,0.008568,0.005869,0.017261]},{"p":"L16N6415-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the currency sign, unit or number-start token of the second (comparison/old) value in a 'from X to Y' or 'down/up from', 'compared to', 'original price', 'usual' construction, i.e. just before restating the earlier or other figure being compared against (e.g. 'declined to Php17.2 billion from Php18.1 billion' firing on the second 'Php'; 'Original price is $16.95' firing on '$').","s":[0.313134,0.217978,0.113689,0.026652,0.019364,0.012985,0.023207,0.039955,0.027048,0.023933,0.006642,0.007361,0.004258,0.006412,0.013642]},{"p":"L16N8375+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the words of a negative imperative or exhortation such as \"do not hesitate to\", \"never miss a story\", \"don't miss out on\", \"don't be afraid to\" or biblical \"forsake not / forget not / do not despise\", peaking on the verb and the tokens right after the negator (hesitate, miss, out, a, story, not, the), especially in boilerplate calls to action at the end of web articles.","s":[0.53177,0.631228,0.509975,0.427785,0.599722,0.053331,0.034383,0.004187,0.002893,0.005321,0.003013,0.013408,0.00398,0.003937,0.002737]},{"p":"L16N8375-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word or number that completes a stock, near-memorized phrase inside a negated or failure clause of boilerplate text, especially securities and investment disclaimers (\"actual results may differ materially from those set forth in the forward-looking statements\", \"does not take into account your investment objectives\") and JavaScript guard checks (\"if (response.status !== 200)\", \"if (!response.ok)\", \"typeof val !== \\\"object\\\"\").","s":[0.684582,0.777251,0.410206,0.362913,0.34874,0.019774,0.014427,0.075216,0.037951,0.01297,0.031023,0.022563,0.014412,0.024089,0.019792]},{"p":"L16N8452+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a token that continues something already seen earlier in the same text, i.e. copy or induction positions inside repetitive, highly predictable material: the next piece of a repeated name, phrase, or identifier (\"Battle of the Champions\" quoted again, \"Double Dipt Mints\" repeated, self.Q3 = Q3, load_files reused), or the next cell in rows of SQL inserts, tab-separated tables or code with a fixed pattern.","s":[0.263155,0.283331,0.175078,0.322055,0.298004,0.20733,0.257096,0.128545,0.114488,0.285005,0.009414,0.020035,0.016146,0.002973,0.019658]},{"p":"L16N8452-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires at the end of a line or list item in structured, repetitive machine-style text (SQL dumps, HTML table rows, commit lists, changelogs, source code), mostly on the closing parenthesis or newline that ends one entry, where the next entry repeats the same format.","s":[0.077883,0.018701,0.079737,0.045312,0.086378,0.027103,-0.005581,0.032884,0.111113,0.030147,0.02507,0.026417,0.028581,0.037641,0.034589]},{"p":"L17N1489+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires at tokens where the next token is almost forced, such as the apostrophe inside a contraction (don't, we're), the first period of 'e.g.', a word piece mid-word, the space before a year after a month or 'In', or a line in repetitive boilerplate code or tables; it is not tied to a topic.","s":[0.020229,0.022046,0.025583,0.01628,0.021655,0.021677,0.016881,0.031996,0.011733,0.029671,0.021744,0.007394,0.007652,0.025982,0.011336]},{"p":"L17N1489-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the second word or word-piece of a commercial product or sub-brand name that comes right after a brand or company name (e.g. \"North Carolina Cash 5\", \"T-fal Secure\", \"Uni Signo\", \"Geberit Aquaclean\", \"Honeywell Quietset\", \"Philips X-Treme Vision\", \"Zojirushi Fresh Brew\"), especially when an ordinary English word or piece is used as part of a coined trademark name in product-listing or marketing text.","s":[0.633661,0.637533,0.408092,0.582729,0.583644,0.343833,0.167973,0.003762,0.005279,0.031955,0.01112,0.004153,0.005754,0.007958,0.016076]},{"p":"L17N263+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires inside rigid, templated boilerplate of technical and legal documents, most strongly within C# XML doc-comment lines (/// <param name=\"x\">The x.</param>, /// <returns>The ...</returns>) and the stock signature and closing blocks of SEC or regulatory filings (\"Pursuant to the requirements of the Securities Exchange Act of 1934, the Registrant has duly caused this report to be signed... By: /s/ Name\").","s":[0.497436,0.807394,0.448404,0.79212,0.51845,0.063546,0.011246,0.144206,0.215634,0.054672,0.008228,0.016127,0.00528,0.005889,0.013397]},{"p":"L17N263-","l":"TWO-REL","j":"11","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a token that completes a memorized, near-deterministic boilerplate string, above all the second digit of an ISBN publisher code in a book citation (the '2' in 'ISBN 978-0-521-…' for Cambridge University Press, or the digit after '978-1-4'), and also the fixed next word in standard software license headers ('Licensed to the Apache Software Foundation (ASF) under one or more', 'Copyright (c) Oracle and/or its affiliates').","s":[0.586558,0.935156,0.640868,0.755352,0.63622,0.045315,0.038319,0.091774,0.174985,0.037646,0.005049,0.004872,0.006208,0.004962,0.008296]},{"p":"L17N4161+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the words inside the scope of a just-opened negation or prohibition, i.e., the tokens right after \"not / do not / neither / no\" that spell out what is being denied or forbidden, as in \"not by works,\" \"lean not on your own understanding,\" \"put not your trust in princes,\" \"the abstract should not be italicized,\" or \"DO NOT pull the cord\" (strongest in scripture-style \"not X, but Y\" contrasts and rule lists).","s":[0.57354,0.761523,0.045848,0.188628,0.880786,0.00376,0.031643,0.050999,0.016802,0.068249,0.011766,0.006675,0.002508,0.004424,0.009486]},{"p":"L17N4161-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"In advice or self-help prose about balance and moderation, fires on the word or phrase that sets up a warning against excess, just before the overdone thing is named, such as 'using (them) excessively', 'relying solely on', 'playing for hours on end', 'over-reliance', 'make sure not to', or 'but if there are too many'.","s":[0.170497,0.438602,0.181752,0.259499,0.200487,0.02341,0.016178,0.021901,0.018294,0.041163,0.023109,0.011892,0.031528,0.014993,0.013364]},{"p":"L17N426+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires inside sentences about how much someone gets paid or earns from a sale (affiliate commissions, referral percentages, payouts, a cut of revenue), peaking on the verb, pronoun, space or number right before the stated percentage or amount, as in \"the affiliate program pays you 20% commission\" or \"you can earn 30% recurring commissions\".","s":[0.389434,0.440984,0.359842,0.557458,0.766971,0.022535,0.024497,0.02547,0.006035,0.014195,0.008373,0.013269,0.011249,0.00496,0.015456]},{"p":"L17N426-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the verb or function word just before an earning-money or livelihood idea is completed, the point where the next words will be about funding, earning or supporting oneself, as in affiliate-link disclosures (\"links which help support our site\", \"designed to provide a means for us to earn fees\") and phrases like \"make a living\", \"support himself as a writer\" or \"main source of income\".","s":[0.695528,0.524797,0.36007,0.37387,0.53176,0.020471,0.010162,0.200949,-2.7e-05,0.032032,0.00881,0.008465,0.007011,0.007397,0.011795]},{"p":"L17N6077+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a word or word-piece that opens a fixed multi-word term or compound name, where the next token is highly predictable (e.g. 'chocolate [chip] cookie', '[pine] nuts', '[machine] learning', '[Bloomberg] LP', 'red [bean] paste', '[air] pressure', '[Fn] key'), most strongly when the same compound or name already appeared earlier in the text and is being repeated.","s":[0.284849,0.034278,0.087901,0.313407,0.143491,0.032067,0.017541,0.094953,0.136729,0.033802,0.036822,0.042088,0.025252,0.025166,0.022055]},{"p":"L17N6077-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a noun used as a modifier in front of a head noun inside a compound, especially a food or ingredient word naming the kind of dish (\"your zucchini bread\", \"the cheese sauce\", \"apple pies\", \"onion cake\", \"cinnamon rolls\"), and also on non-food compound modifiers like \"passenger plane\", \"fan page\", \"sales funnel\", \"roller bearings\"; it fires on the modifier word, not on the head noun that follows.","s":[0.151646,0.014848,0.343257,0.030508,0.026528,0.026887,0.018688,0.013411,0.00615,0.009609,0.005772,0.007849,0.037944,0.006511,0.018937]},{"p":"L17N8385+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word just before an external threat or enemy is about to be named, in contexts of defending, protecting, facing or resisting an attacker, e.g. on \"from\" in \"defend the Earth from\", on \"an\" in \"facing an alien threat\", on \"to\" in \"vulnerable to\" or \"stand up to\", and on \"peril\" in \"the world in peril\".","s":[0.318218,0.143694,0.053364,0.019982,0.305926,0.08101,0.108115,0.00857,0.00578,0.125938,0.026239,0.029654,0.006023,0.009667,0.009771]},{"p":"L17N8385-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word or comma just before a hazard, risk, disease or enemy is about to be named, i.e. where the next word is predicted to be a threat (e.g. 'risks include, but are not limited to,' in mining-company forward-looking-statement disclaimers, 'acts of' God, 'sparked by' lightning, 'at' war, 'red blot-' blotch disease), rather than on the threat word itself.","s":[0.028009,0.264424,0.18191,0.040235,0.370171,0.132112,0.025412,0.017987,0.025805,0.014496,0.068157,0.011568,0.012993,0.006408,0.021955]},{"p":"L17N8681+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires at the point where a sentence refers back to something someone said or wrote as a speech act, most strongly on the noun \"statements\" in boilerplate like \"All statements other than statements of historical fact\" / \"Readers should not consider statements made by the author\", and also on words leading into a reference to a prior remark (\"the woman that said it\", \"denied that he had made the remark\", \"Heavily criticized for her comments\", \"shocked that she would say\"), predicting a speech word such as spoken/statement/comments.","s":[1.041596,0.595615,0.465881,0.372359,0.441002,0.007292,0.268668,0.214728,0.042009,0.110644,0.004978,0.015106,0.007158,0.019727,0.016321]},{"p":"L17N8681-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word just before a communicative act or message is named (the model anticipating speech, a statement, a gesture or signal), e.g. on \"and\" in \"looked me in the eye and flipped me off / caught his eye and nodded\", on \"by\" or \"or\" in \"by speech or by signs\", on \"above\" or \"following\" before \"copyright notice\" or \"text\", or on \"this\" before \"statement\".","s":[0.027939,0.311645,0.114216,0.145856,0.149562,0.035195,0.092685,0.038226,0.083465,0.137029,0.074338,0.065347,0.0567,0.08163,0.07928]},{"p":"L17N9317+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on words inside verbatim quoted or titled old-language material (medieval Spanish or Latin titles and verse, Old Norse lines, archaic English chronicle quotations, old deed formulas like 'hereditaments and appurtenances') embedded in English scholarly or historical writing, often in footnotes and citations, typically on the function words or word endings mid-title or mid-quote.","s":[0.929652,0.57918,0.469207,0.700974,0.370571,0.111107,0.115682,0.307672,0.278472,0.129577,0.13192,0.021794,0.020766,0.018717,0.034508]},{"p":"L17N9317-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the stem of a negative contraction (the 'didn', 'couldn', 'wouldn', 'hadn', 'wasn', 'don' piece just before the apostrophe-t) in past-tense narrative fiction, strongest in English-translated Chinese web novels (cultivation/xianxia stories with characters like Li Fuchen or Meng Hao), and not on uncontracted 'did not'/'could not'.","s":[0.359206,0.324344,0.227747,0.218358,0.160898,0.258188,0.243357,0.20425,0.24027,0.159316,0.010825,0.00536,0.000742,0.003467,0.004453]},{"p":"L17N9667+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the reader-addressed call-to-action or question phrase in boilerplate web text, especially at the verb or pronoun inside it: imperative invitations such as \"Learn more\", \"Find out more\", \"See more\", \"Get to know\", \"Now tell us your needs\", \"Send your inquiry\" and their foreign forms (\"Dowiedz się\", \"Erfahren Sie\", \"Mời các bạn tham khảo\", \"一起来看看\"), and the \"I\" in FAQ questions like \"How can I get samples?\" or \"What should I do?\".","s":[0.245837,0.319234,0.855658,0.832183,0.446049,0.044106,0.128361,0.014823,0.072433,0.027058,0.01854,0.035098,0.057623,0.033195,0.009486]},{"p":"L17N9667-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word \"any\" (also \"some\", \"there\", Chinese 有) right after \"if you have\" / \"if there is\" / \"Have\" in the stock contact-us closing line of commercial web pages, e.g. \"If you have any questions, please feel free to contact us\" on supplier, product or market-report pages.","s":[0.573416,0.495218,0.375238,0.214118,0.544366,0.008868,0.03194,0.0358,0.008527,0.026434,0.011185,0.013087,0.025914,0.023403,0.014213]},{"p":"L18N101+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires inside lists or roll-calls of notable people tagged with their occupation or fame (e.g. AP \"Today's Birthdays: Actor X is 77. Singer Y is 81.\", name directories like \"Smith, John (musician)\" / \"(sports)\", \"born today: 1966 Jane Doe, Italian actor\"), on the age digit, line break or name piece as the list continues, predicting more celebrities/public figures.","s":[0.626675,0.371429,0.100706,0.172526,0.459018,0.05775,0.05775,0.148543,0.061829,0.160302,0.00361,0.021611,0.022337,0.081435,0.013567]},{"p":"L18N101-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires in fame/entertainment contexts at the point where a famous person is about to be named or celebrities are referred to: on the word \"celebrity\" or \"stars\" (as in \"celebrity news\" or \"interviews with your favorite stars\"), on a celebrity's name, and on the word or punctuation just before one is expected (\"narrated by\", \"where the likes of\", \"Hall of Fame\", a dash or line break before a quote's famous author, or a celebrity's net-worth figure).","s":[0.221055,0.108474,0.235141,0.056966,0.203404,0.055274,0.108474,0.030951,0.02405,0.007324,0.022194,0.01724,0.022933,0.014031,0.021702]},{"p":"L18N1046+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the second-or-later token of a memorized web boilerplate formula, where that token is almost forced by the template: e.g. 'stories' in 'Related stories' at the end of a news article, 'periodicals' in 'References in periodicals archive ?' on dictionary pages, 'changed' or 'modified' in 'Created on …, last changed …' or 'Record created …, last modified …', the comma in 'Disclaimer: The views, suggestions, and opinions', 'Chapter 1, to describe' in market reports, the '6' in 'doi:10.1086/', and ',v' in '$Id: file,v 1.2'.","s":[0.146922,0.526007,0.586485,0.672923,0.308678,0.020614,0.029966,0.02754,0.035152,0.030947,0.032152,0.018231,0.034094,0.050105,0.032632]},{"p":"L18N1046-","l":"TWO-REL","j":"11","f":0,"e":0,"x":"In code, fires on the function-name token \"join\" in a path-join call (Python os.path.join(...) or Node path.join(__dirname, ...)), i.e. the method name right after \"path.\" and just before its opening parenthesis; weaker on \"range\" in range(...) calls such as for _ in range(n).","s":[0.889553,0.862415,0.71896,0.510877,0.489501,0.014461,0.524967,0.255836,0.05201,0.1988,0.018011,0.01701,0.015537,0.042355,0.012389]},{"p":"L18N1122+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires inside a promotional plug for a branded product, feature or service, on the words of the brand or feature name (or the 'our' just before it) right after a pitch like 'try', 'use', 'sign up with', 'stay up to date via', or 'check out', as in 'try AI Auto Enhance', 'use Recoverit Data Recovery', or 'sign up for our email alerts'.","s":[0.971813,0.828025,0.418141,0.213984,0.62386,0.017127,0.235358,0.025607,0.022653,0.022913,0.022184,0.011347,0.009565,0.032576,0.017616]},{"p":"L18N1122-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires inside end-of-article website boilerplate calls to action (newsletter, subscription or streaming promos), peaking on the possessive or object right after a delivery or access phrase, e.g. the word \"your\" in \"Get every new post delivered to your inbox\", \"TV\" in \"stream live on your smart TV\", or \"teams\" in \"Follow all of your favorite teams at…\".","s":[0.045949,0.319622,0.40141,0.183772,0.330179,0.020029,0.021918,0.02675,0.007334,0.026641,0.018351,0.004271,0.031832,0.004742,0.026284]},{"p":"L18N1159+","l":"TWO-REL","j":"11","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires at sentence boundaries (the period, or the first word of the next sentence such as All, Most, This, Top or Unlike) in spun SEO spam pages, where machine-translated fantasy-novel sentences alternate abruptly with CBD, supplement or other product-marketing sentences, so that the next sentence starts an unrelated thread; it also fires weakly on formulaic boilerplate such as 'protected by law' in job-ad EEO statements, and it largely depends on long spammy context that a short text cannot fully reproduce.","s":[0.271791,0.224644,0.279988,0.216133,0.106936,0.059837,0.142325,0.038233,0.04029,0.177216,0.019103,0.070657,0.050915,0.095433,0.032243]},{"p":"L18N1159-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires at sentence boundaries in polished, literary British newspaper-style narrative prose (sports match reports, arts and music reviews): on the full stop ending a scene-setting sentence, on the comma and closing quote after a quoted remark from a player or manager ('…,' says Long), on paragraph breaks, and on a new sentence opening with \"Here\" or \"For\" (\"Here is Brahms…\", \"For the first time in 23 years…\").","s":[0.958446,1.089715,1.093186,1.05027,1.170618,0.455876,0.030559,0.043213,0.139542,0.046205,0.033632,0.044098,0.006012,0.018615,0.011019]},{"p":"L18N2649+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires inside contrastive or ironic-twist constructions that set up a surprising predicate, peaking on the function words of frames like \"the only thing that's ...\", \"it's not just the X that ...\", \"there's nothing ... about\", \"anything but ...\" and \"Not So ...\", where the sentence is about to deliver a twist, a pun or a reversal.","s":[0.522637,0.462451,0.232175,0.556089,0.339876,0.008299,0.016534,0.002838,0.002462,0.007608,0.021845,0.005803,0.027727,0.022026,0.006776]},{"p":"L18N2649-","l":"TWO-REL","j":"11","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires mid-phrase inside stock, memorized web boilerplate whose wording is fully predictable, especially on the function word or pronoun right after the opening words of a cookie-consent notice (\"We use cookies to give you the best experience\"), an Amazon Associates disclaimer, a press-release forward-looking-statements caveat, or market-report filler, and also on garbled spam word salad or broken PDF/HTML text, but not on ordinary, freshly written prose.","s":[0.530024,0.127019,0.359652,0.125962,0.253442,0.036647,0.033767,0.024122,0.047059,0.018034,0.029562,0.039191,0.047364,0.04786,0.043233]},{"p":"L18N3736+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the subject or main verb/adjective slot of a sentence that delivers a judgment, recommendation or obligation stated as fact (e.g. the 'you' in 'you should tell him', 'which app is best', 'how good is the school', 'the site is completely free', 'he could have chosen', 'NNSA needs to'), the spot where a hedge like 'IMO' or 'I think' could be inserted. Neutral narrative or purely factual sentences do not fire it.","s":[0.155328,0.062217,0.072403,0.032966,0.098647,0.038568,0.011193,0.027537,0.020559,0.021213,0.056503,0.020758,0.020059,0.012767,0.015577]},{"p":"L18N3736-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word \"in\" (or a comma, or \"I\") placed right after a stated judgment or evaluative claim, where it begins a sentence-final or parenthetical opinion hedge such as \"in my opinion\", \"in my view\", \"in our view\" or \"I think\" (e.g. \"best program on the market in my opinion\"), predicting an opinion marker like IMO next.","s":[0.497763,0.370351,0.371806,0.067993,0.54168,0.174471,0.025677,0.007601,0.028573,0.000803,0.013791,0.03533,0.021696,0.008116,0.024542]},{"p":"L18N5367+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the object or verb of a stopping/quitting/ending action, especially in phrases about ending something before its natural completion, such as \"cancel anytime\", \"unsubscribe from emails at any time\", \"left school at 14\", \"dropped out of school\", or \"the trial was stopped early\"; it predicts a following time word like \"anytime\", \"early\", or \"at age\".","s":[0.145574,0.54849,0.519635,0.105714,0.379624,0.022126,0.076244,0.058169,0.010234,0.041862,0.045073,0.028603,0.00788,0.01045,0.011568]},{"p":"L18N5367-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the last word of an instruction or offer phrase right before a duration, time limit or 'until…' condition is expected next, as in recipe steps ('cook over medium heat until crisp', 'bake in the preheated oven for 60 minutes'), trial or guarantee boilerplate ('try this product for 90 days', 'use it month to month') and similar fixed phrases ('for illustrative purposes only', 'available 24 hours a day', 'play for fun/free').","s":[0.086008,0.248194,0.2951,0.266992,0.016352,0.066434,0.038454,0.096903,0.073278,0.010967,0.012704,0.024865,0.00661,0.021384,0.006932]},{"p":"L18N6682+","l":"TWO-REL","j":"11","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires just before an upcoming either-way outcome is named: in procedural prose where an authority will notify, advise or contact someone about a pending decision or result (e.g. \"we will contact you with our decision\", \"notify applicants of acceptance\", \"regardless of success or failure\"), and in code that toggles a binary on/off state (classList.toggle, toggleClass, play/pause toggles).","s":[0.61583,0.685122,0.659574,0.760662,0.325883,0.332985,0.035935,0.038975,0.760662,0.275013,0.002682,0.010903,0.00173,0.000824,0.012439]},{"p":"L18N6682-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on text about checking, verifying, measuring or determining something: tokens right after verbs like verify, determine, gauge, test or look at, where the thing being evaluated is named (e.g. 'verify the validity of entries', 'a way to gauge volatility is its beta', 'Please look at the time stamp to see when it was updated', 'understanding how developers collect and share your data'), and on parameter or return positions in validation code (check functions returning true or false, comparison results).","s":[0.029399,0.156034,0.044818,0.057403,0.072811,0.032773,0.040856,0.050127,0.019835,0.059615,0.070188,0.048007,0.051616,0.052288,0.069204]},{"p":"L18N804+","l":"UNK","j":"","f":2,"e":1,"x":""},{"p":"L18N804-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the opening bracket (and the first digits) of a Wikipedia-style numeric citation marker such as \" [12]\" or \"(22)\" placed right after a sentence-ending period, especially in scraped or jumbled encyclopedia-like text where the citation numbers appear out of order. It is predicting that a reference number comes next.","s":[0.164902,0.012935,0.113156,0.384316,0.226121,0.004643,0.045973,0.006815,0.010885,0.010867,0.012281,0.008335,0.012579,0.009631,0.007408]},{"p":"L19N3216+","l":"TWO-REL","j":"111","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires mainly on the word \"each\" (or Spanish \"cada\", and sometimes \"one\") in distributive phrases such as \"of each person\", \"each one of these\", \"each in their way\", especially in clumsy English prose translated from Spanish or Portuguese. Weaker offshoots fire on Spanish-origin numbers (+34 phone digits, 978-84 ISBNs) and on the period that ends a dictionary-style definition.","s":[0.166584,0.425503,0.210402,0.267264,0.322509,0.04112,0.0188,0.022985,0.022224,0.014825,0.021335,0.030724,0.020695,0.013718,0.006337]},{"p":"L19N3216-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a relational word that sets up a comparison, proximity or range and still needs its complement, such as different (from), close/near/closer (to), similar/equal/inferior/superior (to), ranging/range/goes (from … to), between (X and Y) and among, most strongly in boilerplate like 'materially different from' and before a number or comparand (also in Spanish, French and Italian: inferiores a, entre, simili).","s":[0.045741,0.030244,0.084084,0.011716,0.797955,0.011125,0.030244,0.017315,0.010488,0.235562,0.009226,0.016568,0.012022,0.013107,0.016704]},{"p":"L19N3897+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the first piece of a word, name or acronym that is about to be copied from earlier in the same text, most strongly where an acronym and its spelled-out expansion sit next to each other (e.g. the opening fragment of a long name typed inside parentheses right after the words it abbreviates, or the expansion that starts right after the acronym 'stands for' or 'see'); first, unrepeated mentions do not fire.","s":[0.013395,0.042841,0.217501,0.032939,0.017728,0.009041,0.011563,0.013595,0.00507,0.020347,0.020407,0.026555,0.0135,0.015219,0.012442]},{"p":"L19N3897-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the comma or \"and\"/\"&\" that joins two parallel paired phrases inside a title, slogan or list item, most often a book title in a bibliography or citation (\"Ancient Culture, Modern Land\", \"Private Sentiment and Political Culture\", \"Holy Blood, Holy Grail\") or a company-motto pair (\"Quality First, Sustainable Development\"), where the token is the connector between the first noun phrase and its matched counterpart.","s":[0.389225,0.931868,0.297523,0.005812,0.629051,0.031318,0.009531,0.003712,0.005557,0.009481,0.007832,0.008153,0.005027,0.005379,0.010084]},{"p":"L19N4173+","l":"TWO-REL","j":"11","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the run of leading indentation spaces/tabs at the start of an indented line that continues or nests under the previous line (sub-bullets in a nested outline, sub-entries in a table of contents, indented continuation lines in code or SQL), i.e. the spot where a subordinate or elaborating item is about to begin; weaker on mid-sentence ; / - / ( / — that open an aside.","s":[0.270512,0.325867,0.284425,0.34201,0.117338,0.112572,0.144957,0.13065,0.054542,0.114477,0.02264,0.020238,0.042756,0.004459,0.020144]},{"p":"L19N4173-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the punctuation mark (comma, closing dash, or closing parenthesis) that ends a long fronted subordinate clause, appositive, or parenthetical insertion, right where the main clause's subject or verb is about to resume (e.g. \"After going 7/2/12 on Azir in game one of the team's win over its rival, Chovy decided...\"; \"...the leading political entrepreneur of the super PAC era — he offered...\").","s":[0.24918,0.451331,0.174047,0.165723,0.075299,0.024703,0.003806,0.019863,0.005795,0.013279,0.028406,0.02734,0.067814,0.017325,0.017512]},{"p":"L19N4400+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the name (or final piece of the name) that opens an image caption sitting on its own line inside a Wikipedia-style or blog article, e.g. a new line reading \"Subhas Bose (standing, right) with friends in England, 1920\" or \"Union Terminal in Cincinnati, Ohio\" right after a paragraph; the caption subject is usually the article's main topic, and the caption typically continues with a parenthetical, location, date or action.","s":[0.46542,0.202929,0.304476,0.250318,0.208889,0.019466,0.015203,0.024592,0.030576,0.100932,0.01338,0.009826,0.003014,0.008104,0.011651]},{"p":"L19N4400-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires inside news-style photo captions and credit lines embedded in an article, the parts that describe a pictured scene (\"X is seen at...\", \"FILE – People line up...\", \"Name (L) and...\", a caption's dateline year) or give the agency credit like \"REUTERS/Name\", \"AFP/Getty Images\", \"Image: Reuters/...\", strongest on the slash, parenthesis, verb or year token right after the caption's subject.","s":[0.670994,0.423539,0.849121,0.60162,0.707505,0.063598,0.037536,0.263497,0.155403,0.04275,0.046796,0.051645,0.020832,0.011759,0.09036]},{"p":"L19N4413+","l":"TWO-REL","j":"11","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word right after \"is/became the\" in a distinction or record claim, mostly \"first\", \"only\", \"closest\" or \"few\" (\"made her the first Spanish actress to win an Oscar\", \"the only switch with...\"), and more weakly on the article that opens a defining noun phrase (\"the process by which an individual...\", \"the ability of a program to...\"), the slot just before a modifier that singles out a category.","s":[0.173543,0.019027,0.03184,0.051103,0.008747,0.013568,0.023529,0.072876,0.032181,0.014611,0.019576,0.033872,0.027024,0.058135,0.027253]},{"p":"L19N4413-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the action verb that follows an enabling or purpose frame, such as 'a way of creating', 'a method of producing', 'the only chance of winning', 'anyone can create apps', 'allow people to get a degree', 'Trying to access', where the word names what something lets someone do and the object comes right after.","s":[0.370088,0.390154,0.105866,0.251219,0.344006,0.358581,0.118808,0.03784,0.019153,0.067833,0.046854,0.025986,0.07744,0.017389,0.042204]},{"p":"L19N4877+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires inside Chinese-exporter style English (B2B factory and product pages with boilerplate like 'we warmly welcome customers all over the world to visit our factory', FAQ answers about MOQ, samples, T/T payment and delivery time, and machine process descriptions), peaking on the next word of such stock phrases (welcome, visit, to) and on a sentence-initial 'The' or 'the' after a period; ordinary native-English prose does not fire it.","s":[0.439091,0.456716,0.711316,0.391208,0.371913,0.075711,0.045141,0.042092,0.224553,0.043065,0.017689,0.022582,0.094103,0.032433,0.058473]},{"p":"L19N4877-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word \"time\" (or a similar count noun such as \"occasion\" or \"years\") right after an ordinal or quantity word, especially \"first time\", \"second time\" or \"third time\" used as a phrase like \"my first time here\", \"second time at this restaurant\" or \"third time's the charm\"; weaker offshoots are \"after several years\" in company boilerplate and the \"Q\" in numbered \"1. Q:\" FAQ lines.","s":[0.557357,0.5107,0.377619,0.172865,0.321823,0.15577,0.011203,0.119502,0.049557,0.010375,0.017892,0.102457,0.006333,0.010788,0.016148]},{"p":"L19N5005+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"In object-oriented code, fires on the token that begins a class's constructor or calls the parent constructor: the indentation just before \"def __init__\" (or Ruby \"def initialize\" / a JS constructor function) directly under a \"class X:\" line, and the parent class name or dot in explicit superclass-init calls like \"pygame.sprite.Sprite.__init__(self)\", \"tk.Frame.__init__(self, master)\" or \"Parent.__super__.constructor.apply(this, arguments)\".","s":[0.748697,0.919441,0.720338,0.580514,0.562844,0.037063,0.274466,0.517051,0.045544,0.047984,0.015954,0.009662,0.010505,0.004937,0.017654]},{"p":"L19N5005-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"In C#/Java/Solidity-style code, fires on the parameter type name (e.g. int, string, or the last piece of a type like IBookRepository, HeyYouContext, RequestDelegate) immediately after the opening parenthesis of a constructor or method declaration, i.e. the type token of the first declared parameter right before its parameter name.","s":[0.609587,0.661011,0.2227,0.4601,0.136827,0.098151,0.003422,0.002887,0.531934,0.041226,0.003796,0.008346,0.004887,0.003719,0.00957]},{"p":"L19N608+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires at the point just before the text refers to exactly two things as a pair, i.e. where 'two/both' is the natural continuation: words like 'between', 'both', 'difference in age of', 'average of the 10th and 12th', 'Chapter 10 and 11', and in Python on the '=', '.' or 'shape' of two-variable unpacking such as h, w = img.shape[:2], ret, thresh = cv2.threshold(...), or head, tail = os.path.split(path).","s":[0.120854,0.163356,0.307936,0.007354,0.837643,0.004867,0.006918,0.0276,0.005482,0.005928,0.002973,0.008251,0.005131,0.006189,0.005061]},{"p":"L19N608-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the separator right after the second item of an enumerated list that still has more items to come (usually the comma in 'X, Y, Z and W'), most strongly when a count word like 'three' or 'four' announced the list beforehand, as if marking 'two items done so far'.","s":[0.40453,0.219382,0.061433,0.354527,0.158508,0.102809,0.007895,0.011362,0.019857,0.005516,0.003166,0.001846,0.012663,0.00559,0.006048]},{"p":"L19N8848+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the verb \"find\" (also found / discover, Chinese 发现) used right after a subject pronoun or name in the sense of coming to notice or realize a state, as in \"you find yourself…\", \"I found myself…\", \"if you find any errors\", \"she found that…\", the point where a complement clause or a \"-self\" reflexive is expected next.","s":[0.251581,0.580904,0.368594,0.638554,0.415421,0.019934,0.441758,0.011082,0.139253,0.164729,0.009352,0.01205,0.016209,0.010282,0.0222]},{"p":"L19N8848-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on forms of the verb \"find\" (find, Find, finds, found, finden, findet, finde), strongest in stock boilerplate phrases like \"Find out more\" after a cookie notice, \"can be found in the LICENSE file\", \"find its way into\" and \"find expression\", plus the boilerplate noun \"means\" in \"by any means\".","s":[0.531915,0.303067,0.297612,0.625583,0.176521,0.066038,-0.001509,0.019497,0.010944,0.0162,0.014308,0.010757,0.019955,0.030484,0.031396]},{"p":"L20N1682+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the noun or pronoun that is the subject of a clause whose predicate is about to judge its truth or validity, i.e. the word right before a verdict such as \"is correct\", \"was right\", \"will prove to be accurate\", \"are reasonable\", \"is sound\", \"will work\" or \"has been substantiated\" (strongest in forward-looking-statement disclaimers: \"the Company believes the expectations…\" or \"no assurance that such statements will prove to be accurate\").","s":[0.340363,0.159717,0.25435,0.11584,0.151447,0.013508,0.022331,0.019858,0.03632,0.01061,0.006243,0.016889,0.021819,0.003491,0.008743]},{"p":"L20N1682-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the transformative verb and the words right after it in grand 'changed the world' book-title or mission-statement phrases, chiefly \"[Thing] That Changed the World\", \"Change Your Life\", \"Trends Shaping Our Future\", \"make lives better\" or \"help cities grow\", and most strongly when such a title sits inside a bibliography or award list.","s":[0.700401,0.807375,0.191642,0.928715,0.50356,0.040208,0.015427,0.008413,0.031891,0.052833,0.066436,0.025921,0.025955,0.039566,0.039893]},{"p":"L20N3525+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"In boilerplate formal prose (legal rules, recruitment notices, regulatory forms, generic SEO filler), fires on the highly predictable word that completes a stock collocation, such as \"ensure that\", \"adequate time\", \"reasonable opportunity\", \"eligibility criteria\", \"time and labor required\" or \"thank you very much\", and on sentence-final periods of such bland formulaic sentences.","s":[0.523667,0.139219,0.261236,0.939061,0.29809,0.012289,0.029042,0.049794,0.030483,0.023295,0.021027,0.029089,0.018014,0.022964,0.036024]},{"p":"L20N3525-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the token just before an administrative record-keeping office title is expected (clerk, recorder, secretary, treasurer, bookkeeper, registrar, accountant), such as the comma after a signer's name in a city ordinance or minutes signature block (\"ATTEST: Jane Smith, City Clerk\"), \"filed in the office of Secretary of State\", or \"with Carol as the book(keeper)\", and also more weakly on record-keeping words like \"records\" and \"recorded\".","s":[0.836147,0.230046,0.53885,0.479255,0.302175,0.448465,0.215388,0.09546,0.123037,0.117014,0.015839,0.002089,0.007012,0.013829,0.014666]},{"p":"L20N5217+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires at the point just after a list of alternatives where the text is about to say the choice is left to someone, i.e. the token right before or at 'your option', 'your choice', 'whichever is greater/lower', 'as the case may be', 'it doesn't matter', 'depending on your preference', 'is up to you' (most strongly on 'at'/'your'/'(' in the GPL clause 'either version 3 of the License, or (at your option) any later version').","s":[1.073003,0.296578,0.042225,0.49452,0.168627,0.175475,0.165634,0.04254,0.010609,0.008257,0.00733,0.01393,0.006249,0.008505,0.009956]},{"p":"L20N5217-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the words right after or inside an expression of free choice — \"Choose Your (Own/Weapon)\", \"choose-your-own\", \"could choose between\", \"choices to pick from\", \"free to share whatever\", \"on your own\", \"doing what we like\" — i.e. at the point where the text says someone gets to decide or select for themselves, predicting the word \"choice\" next.","s":[0.450868,0.278009,0.091336,0.612409,0.15969,0.010769,0.004246,0.01226,0.010857,0.019902,0.005719,0.020258,0.025297,0.007082,0.005837]},{"p":"L20N5228+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires inside factual descriptions of chemical elements and substances — especially on the digits of a property value like a melting point, atomic weight or density right after \"melting point\", and on the function words of textbook definitions such as \"a pure element is a substance consisting of a single type of atom\" — i.e. encyclopedia/datasheet-style chemistry property prose.","s":[0.728483,0.832329,0.733772,0.715927,0.665856,0.016749,0.018021,0.017985,0.011301,0.026609,0.012829,0.018748,0.014684,0.024499,0.02375]},{"p":"L20N5228-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the hydrogen/oxygen-containing piece inside a written chemical formula, especially the subscript \"2\" (or the \"H\" before it) in H2O, H2CO3, H2PO4 written inline within a larger formula such as Fe(H2O)6 3+, NH3H2O or KH2PO4, in chemistry prose about ions, solubility and complexes.","s":[1.036336,1.083666,1.208312,0.782246,1.032035,0.216349,0.700959,0.87352,0.240659,0.860706,0.000559,0.00109,0.008174,0.001756,0.003981]},{"p":"L20N5879+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"In technical how-to or product/repair text about machines and devices (cars, ovens, TVs, cameras, radios, software versions), fires on the word that opens a qualifier restricting a statement to a subset of models or versions — \"On\", \"in\", \"Some\", \"Most\", \"Older\", \"Recent\", \"Modern\" right before phrases like \"many vehicles\", \"some models\", \"older cars\", \"the Accord\", \"recent versions\" — predicting a model/version/age word next.","s":[0.752551,0.452664,0.532094,0.521262,0.61146,0.193192,0.063196,0.065939,0.083182,0.00962,0.030236,0.033189,0.025704,0.043218,0.037839]},{"p":"L20N5879-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"In text about a specific consumer product, machine or piece of software (a phone, car, truck, boat, battery, app, telescope), this fires on the word just before that product is named again, as in \"start the\", \"if your\", \"the condition of the\", \"performance of this\" or \"battery life for the\", where the next word is expected to be the device or vehicle itself.","s":[0.311135,0.467724,0.363716,0.443225,0.247129,0.282083,0.052083,0.239516,0.062794,0.01732,0.050742,0.032173,0.12884,0.011192,0.083565]},{"p":"L20N6507+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the final word of a multi-word title of a creative work (book, film, play, song, album, episode, campaign or skin name) written inline in running prose without quotes or italics, especially titles that read like ordinary phrases (\"What Color is My World\", \"This Land is Our Land\", \"Find Your Wings\"), marking where the title ends.","s":[0.104029,0.175372,0.178111,0.180537,0.31625,0.025403,0.02576,0.030943,1.4e-05,0.038667,0.028118,0.025732,0.030063,0.039125,0.00831]},{"p":"L20N6507-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"In software documentation, code comments or man pages, fires on the last token of a technical identifier or name (a package, file, tool, command, class, function or path name such as mvoutlier, LICENSE, cbcollect_info, torch_sparse, glShaderSource, \"The patch\") at the point just before a type noun like \"package\", \"file\", \"tool\", \"command\", \"library\", \"function\" or \"class\" tells what kind of thing it is.","s":[0.61914,0.279598,0.016583,0.198652,0.490477,0.282477,0.05707,0.021285,0.063578,0.009058,0.004165,0.008021,0.011332,0.015027,0.003108]},{"p":"L20N6535+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the head noun immediately following a superlative or praise adjective, e.g. the 'Answer' in 'Best Answer', 'Story' in 'The Greatest Story Ever Told', 'Love' in 'The Greatest Love of All', 'place' in 'the most inspirational place', 'Advice' in 'The Best Advice', and more weakly on ranking words like 'top', 'best', 'excellent' themselves when they open a noun phrase.","s":[0.042786,0.402418,0.129598,0.179019,0.498461,0.016766,0.01179,0.008144,0.014432,0.016845,0.006284,0.011202,0.01996,0.010385,0.007814]},{"p":"L20N6535-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a superlative or elite-quality adjective (best, top, world-class, foremost, great, leading, better) used attributively right before a noun phrase naming a group of people or things, especially after possessives or partitives like \"the world's\", \"the nation's\", \"France's\", \"one of the\", \"some of the\", \"a marketplace for\".","s":[0.373781,0.550275,0.256445,0.291483,0.467423,0.00669,0.014269,0.0013,0.015403,0.016961,0.011211,0.014121,0.008303,0.012921,0.019704]},{"p":"L20N8832+","l":"TWO-REL","j":"111","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the predictable middle word of a stock boilerplate phrase in web or document footers and metadata, most of all \"our\" in \"Subscribe to our newsletter / YouTube channel\" or \"Download our App\" (also the digits of a DOI prefix like 10.21272 and fixed legal phrases like \"THE SOFTWARE IS PROVIDED\"), where the next words are nearly certain from the template.","s":[0.310022,0.25935,0.133535,0.250194,0.689131,0.070273,0.006831,0.013348,0.243927,0.070847,0.028587,0.030796,0.021084,0.014518,0.01842]},{"p":"L20N8832-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires inside spatial or abstract boundary phrases built on \"within / out of / beyond / outside (the) ___ (of)\", most strongly on \"ear\" in \"out of earshot\" or \"within earshot\", and also on the noun or connective of phrases like \"out of reach\", \"beyond the reach of\", \"within range\", \"within arm's reach\", \"beyond the jurisdiction of\" and \"out of scope\", at the point where the model is about to name what something is inside or outside the range of (scope, reach, jurisdiction).","s":[0.589557,0.603975,0.651055,0.424784,0.689919,0.015255,0.034768,0.00686,0.092561,0.004607,0.009533,0.003619,0.01171,0.008249,0.017586]},{"p":"L20N9020+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the noun of a fixed idiom that almost forces \"of\" to come next, such as \"over the life/lifetime of\", \"in memory of\", \"at the heart of\", \"get to the bottom of\", \"get the most out of\", \"get the hang of\", \"bring to the attention of\", \"the birth of\", \"the breach of\"; it does not fire on the same nouns used freely, where \"of\" isn't expected.","s":[0.370104,0.072686,0.239459,0.459249,0.449472,0.19892,0.066741,0.019223,0.008271,0.056909,0.028,0.003985,0.005852,0.017755,-0.000284]},{"p":"L20N9020-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the verb naming an action the reader is told to take on a website or account, above all \"upgrade\" (also \"update\", \"reload\", \"refresh your browser\", \"cancel\") in boilerplate notices like \"You are using an outdated browser. Please upgrade your browser\", with weaker spillover to other re-/action verbs such as refinancing, rescheduling or celebrates.","s":[0.53139,0.267596,0.387717,0.478856,0.199696,0.098176,0.048058,0.041293,0.016965,0.10144,0.036457,0.033687,0.027337,0.02262,0.023145]},{"p":"L21N2802+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"In mathematical, probability or technical prose, this fires on the word just before a quantity or measure is stated, most often \"with\" in \"occurs with probability one/zero\", and also on \"at most\"/\"at least\", \"all but finitely many\", \"has dimension/codimension\", \"there are an infinite number\", or an operator right before a formula continues.","s":[1.177144,0.596862,0.58741,0.31457,0.382557,0.698701,0.104859,0.207023,0.012123,0.236806,0.011795,0.019102,0.013139,0.011383,0.010244]},{"p":"L21N2802-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"In math or technical text (theorems, textbook proofs, exam question lists, Wikipedia math articles), fires on the separator just before a new formal statement begins (a colon after \"Theorem\", a blank line after a heading or a finished question, the period after a question number, a closing bracket or comma), where the next words are likely \"Let\", \"If\", \"Suppose\" or \"In figure\".","s":[0.844759,0.412984,0.766239,0.103969,0.691921,0.022701,0.062788,0.016681,0.156554,0.057909,0.009491,0.016746,0.01092,0.011203,0.007566]},{"p":"L21N5392+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the particle \"on\" of the phrasal verb \"put on\" (put on make-up, put on a show, puts on perfume, putting on clothing, put on a load of laundry), and on the word right after it, and also on forms of the verb \"apply\" (apply blush, applied the tag, applying the brake, apply for aid, Apply to YC); it does not fire on ordinary locative \"on\" or on \"put\" itself.","s":[0.433713,0.074557,0.352542,0.497587,0.156152,0.011611,0.007921,0.010129,0.009437,0.024395,0.008716,0.008818,0.008549,0.00761,0.00964]},{"p":"L21N5392-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the last token of a dollar amount (the scale word \"million\", \"billion\", \"trillion\" or \"K\", or the final digit of a number like $55,000) when the next word will be \"in\" or \"of\" naming what the money is for, as in \"$22 million in damages\", \"$10,000 in scholarships\" or \"$800 million in tax increases\".","s":[0.58049,0.449057,0.527443,0.263113,0.49403,0.107377,0.074041,0.004989,0.035693,0.060578,0.007381,0.003482,0.007258,0.01287,0.010608]},{"p":"L21N5798+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word just before a predictable noun, typically a possessive, article, \"of\", or adjective/modifier, when the noun about to come has already been the running subject of the passage, so the model is anticipating it. Examples: \"ran her hands through her [hair]\", \"drink the [water]\" in a pollution story, \"exclusion of [the evidence]\" in a ruling on evidence, \"prospective [students]\" in a college text.","s":[0.247288,0.037106,0.100534,0.037847,0.105167,0.181835,0.037106,0.046501,0.042841,0.023422,0.050489,0.033566,0.02413,0.066189,0.093811]},{"p":"L21N5798-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the function word right after a percentage or share expression and just before the noun being divided up, especially \"X% of its/their\" or \"a share/percentage of\" followed by electricity or energy in renewable-energy prose (e.g., \"get 30% of its electricity from wind\").","s":[0.628718,0.759242,0.093982,0.179813,0.422076,0.025707,0.032027,0.016325,0.084873,0.098848,0.039227,0.041442,0.015138,0.050224,0.074]},{"p":"L21N6835+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires where the text is about to name the earlier or previous member of a comparison. Mostly this is code computing a difference of indexed elements: the subtracted operand right after the minus sign, on the array name, the bracket or the index just before a previous index such as `[i-1]`, `[j]` or `[start]` (as in `a[i] - a[i-1]` or `s[j+i] - s[j]`). More weakly it fires in prose statistics on the digits of an earlier year being compared against a later one (e.g. '12,168 in 2018', 'peak in 1950').","s":[0.16079,0.313516,0.747506,0.201108,0.219841,0.126044,0.331916,0.009507,0.022128,0.01067,0.011405,0.016483,0.000961,0.021735,0.013565]},{"p":"L21N6835-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a past participle or verb (created, published, built, constructed, died, existed, born, made) right where the next word is likely to be 'before' or 'prior to' plus a date or event, i.e. a reduced relative clause that sets up an earlier time cutoff, such as \"homes built before 1978\", \"works published prior to 1923\" or \"what existed before the Big Bang\".","s":[0.677106,0.088094,0.42324,0.22368,0.05861,0.677106,0.088004,0.056692,0.22368,0.05861,0.010226,0.005588,0.008993,0.010411,0.003931]},{"p":"L21N7662+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the token right before a personal subject and its finite verb begins a clause, the slot where the model expects someone to do something next: the auxiliary in questions like \"how do | you deal\" or \"what can | we provide\", the comma in \"write to me in PM, | we will talk\", or, in Spanish/Portuguese/Italian, the word before a person-inflected verb (\"En este curso, | aprenderás\", \"i titoli che | vuoi\"), promoting verbs like have, want, tried.","s":[0.269257,0.212102,0.562805,0.284815,0.001485,0.01818,0.001395,0.023088,0.011258,0.015338,0.012471,0.008959,0.007597,0.009018,-0.003393]},{"p":"L21N7662-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a token in the middle of a fixed, memorized or formulaic string whose next pieces are nearly certain, such as a well-known title ('The Way We Were', 'What to Expect When You're Expecting'), a set phrase ('reasonably practicable', 'according to', 'Yes, we can'), a URL path segment or a timestamp like '08:00:00 GMT', especially in spammy or boilerplate web text.","s":[0.465655,0.604863,0.050229,0.285901,0.046773,0.04135,0.061551,0.063303,0.095835,0.103932,0.037815,0.045607,0.074933,0.045892,0.0252]},{"p":"L21N8311+","l":"TWO-REL","j":"11","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires inside German-specific names and numbers embedded in mostly English text, at the point where the model must continue a German string: on the first word-piece of an uncommon German surname right after a German first name (Klaus Sche|unemann, Wolfgang Sch|ivelbusch, Ralf Sche|epers), and on the early digits of a German phone area code after +49, 0049 or a leading 0 (+49 6|221, 0|221 for Cologne).","s":[0.859902,0.674102,0.644799,0.511584,0.281883,0.006677,0.011553,0.015075,0.012452,0.008373,0.006026,0.005075,0.018989,0.001347,0.007475]},{"p":"L21N8311-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the nobiliary particle \"von\" / \"Von\" (as its own token, usually right after a first name or initial) at the start of a German-style surname in a personal or family name, e.g. \"Heather von Allmen\", \"Anya von Bremzen\", \"Von Steuben\", \"Sophie von der Heyden\", and weakly on other German surname pieces such as \"Hauserman\".","s":[0.920677,0.616923,0.207645,0.368991,0.42042,0.068814,0.013059,0.076465,0.031846,0.088343,0.004524,0.023371,0.005319,0.004673,0.005166]},{"p":"L21N9477+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires partway through a number, code or initialed name inside a dense repetitive list (phone directories, ID codes like TH833 or REG.02.882, issue numbers, page ranges, index entries, council member lists like \"Cr. B.A. Thomas\"), on an inner digit or on the dash or period right after a prefix that already appeared earlier in the list with different endings, so the next piece cannot simply be copied.","s":[0.152506,0.299419,0.287483,0.218661,0.269769,0.003971,0.011459,0.015159,0.040082,0.061998,0.002919,0.013166,0.010871,0.004672,0.00481]},{"p":"L21N9477-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a single digit (very often 8 or 3) inside a long structured identifier number, especially the middle of a phone number (toll-free 800/866/888 numbers), DOI prefixes like 10.1188 or 10.3386, ISBNs, or version strings such as 3.3.9, mostly at the digit that repeats the one before it.","s":[0.56029,0.46091,0.407862,0.019223,0.507232,0.019115,0.008309,0.015369,0.004296,0.020181,0.010373,0.007993,0.010362,0.004899,0.002916]},{"p":"L21N9810+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word-initial token \"Aud\" (or \"Audrey\") that starts a capitalized name or heading, as in Auden, Audre Lorde, Audubon, Audley, Audrey, and the label \"Audience:\" in book or event metadata, and stays active on the next token of a name like the period after the initial in \"Audrey L.\"","s":[0.547895,0.639261,0.439801,0.639397,0.716075,0.005637,0.006833,0.005565,0.002209,0.016951,0.004869,0.002609,0.002899,0.009054,0.002303]},{"p":"L21N9810-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the capitalized word-start piece \"Dum\"/\"Dumb\" (and less strongly \"Tum\") when it opens a longer proper name, typically a surname or place name such as Dumortier, Dumbuya, Dummer, Dumitriu, Dumont, Dumbarton, Dumfries or Tumati, especially in author lists, credits or citations; the plain English words \"dumb\", \"dump\" or \"dummy\" are not the target.","s":[0.609216,0.810472,0.509262,0.597651,0.876527,0.017157,0.009287,0.008201,0.018632,0.249096,0.012348,0.017143,0.010397,0.007926,0.014788]},{"p":"L21N9849+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires inside a phrase about taking off, hanging up or setting down wearable items or belongings on arriving indoors (\"hung her keys on the hook\", \"take their shoes off at the door\", \"Can I take your hat and coat\", \"kicked off my boots\"), peaking on the verb or the possessive right after it (her/my/their/your), before the item is named, and it predicts that a clothing or shoe word comes next.","s":[0.708488,0.326179,0.517463,0.423888,0.473272,0.226852,0.039152,0.057568,0.068637,0.036583,0.010871,0.001414,0.009392,0.006021,0.00123]},{"p":"L21N9849-","l":"TWO-REL","j":"11","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on words for distressed or angry human vocal noise, chiefly 'screaming', 'shouting', 'crying', 'cries', 'moans', 'arguing', and on the words leading into them such as 'woman', 'female' or 'loud' in reports that someone heard or reported a person screaming, typical of police logs and crime news. It also fires weakly on 'cautioned' and 'abusive' in boilerplate disclaimers.","s":[0.873455,0.800454,0.773141,0.647339,0.757761,0.691502,0.064276,0.174324,0.183359,0.14085,0.031542,0.023711,0.0035,0.022755,0.009459]},{"p":"L22N1709+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the token \"Wool\" when it begins a proper name rather than the word wool: surnames like Woolley, Woolf, Woolworth, Woolfolk, Woollard, Woolsey, Woolfrey or place names like The Woolmarket, typically right after a first name or initials (e.g. \"John T. Woolley\", \"F.W. Woolworth\", \"(Woolf, 2015)\").","s":[0.867897,0.898962,0.85999,0.732548,0.858911,0.003765,0.031751,0.005941,0.020371,0.004996,0.000246,0.005189,0.00094,0.000257,0.034835]},{"p":"L22N1709-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a word piece in the middle of a fragmented spelling of the require/requisite word family: a misspelling, a hyphen-broken form, a rare form, or a Spanish cognate. Examples are 'requirments', 'requiryment', 'requiements', 're-quires', 'pre-requisites', 'requite', 'Requiem', 'Requerimientos' and 'Requiero', where the tokenizer splits off a piece like 'requ', 'qu' or 'requis' and the 'ire/ir' ending is still to come. Correctly spelled common forms like 'required' or 'requirements' do not fire it.","s":[0.547482,0.531507,0.616199,0.622356,0.229816,0.014486,0.014135,0.006691,0.006763,0.011807,0.029238,0.011453,0.019721,0.028377,0.014555]},{"p":"L22N4049+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"In polished, generic marketing or blog prose (AI-style SEO copy), this fires on the noun that closes a stock clause right before a comma or period, as in \"energy conservation is a top priority,\" \"brings its flavor to the table,\" \"an indispensable part of our lives,\" \"at a great price,\" \"doesn't break the bank\"; it marks a clause-ending phrase where a semicolon would be wrong and a comma or period comes next.","s":[0.71439,0.253917,0.339903,0.227064,0.169018,0.027071,0.062414,0.153486,0.131792,0.019266,0.031339,0.071842,0.020582,0.035434,0.112684]},{"p":"L22N4049-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the noun that closes the first half of a \"not just X / more than just a X / doesn't merely offer X\" contrast, the last word right before the semicolon or dash that sets up \"it's (also) Y\" (e.g. \"more than just a restaurant\", \"isn't just a platform\"), predicting that a semicolon comes next.","s":[0.145582,0.225197,0.17701,0.185653,0.142615,0.009882,0.009801,0.013024,0.020549,0.006126,0.037958,0.010222,0.005903,0.065254,0.015456]},{"p":"L22N486+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word-piece \"friend\" (the tokens Friend/Friends/friend, including when it starts a longer word like Friendswood, Friendship, friendliest), strongest when capitalized as part of a proper name or title such as \"Friends of the Earth\", \"Friends of the Library\", the Quaker \"Friends\" or the journal \"The Friend\"; synonyms like buddy, pal or companion do not fire it.","s":[0.737394,0.379719,0.428681,0.786692,0.392282,0.00804,0.014519,0.031094,0.04662,0.010716,0.004337,0.007034,0.006875,0.001891,0.005908]},{"p":"L22N486-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the digit 9 right after a 2 (the \"29\" in \"2\" + \"9\" tokenization), above all as the next item number after 28 in a counted list or citation run such as \"27. ... 28. ... 29.\" or \"[27, 28, 29]\", and also inside phone numbers and DOIs that contain 29.","s":[0.360212,0.332016,0.085719,0.020869,0.106071,0.213289,0.108909,0.018988,0.020869,0.015116,0.006861,0.011646,0.009894,0.0129,0.008823]},{"p":"L22N6965+","l":"TWO-REL","j":"111","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on tokens whose continuation is almost fully predetermined by memorized, formulaic text, most often the dot right after the domain name in a web address (\"www.rcgov.org\", \"www.sharpfuneralhomes.com\"), and also on the next word of stock boilerplate such as securities forward-looking-statement disclaimers (\"Readers are cautioned…\", \"subject to a variety of risks\"), EEO job-ad language (\"Reasonable accommodations\") and stock code idioms (canvas.getContext, connection.Open).","s":[0.712299,0.622151,0.107733,0.115871,0.511229,0.022203,0.013847,0.021634,0.01364,0.022552,0.020905,0.018034,0.01019,0.028484,0.015328]},{"p":"L22N6965-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word \"military\" (and nearby words) when it names service members' families, children or survivors as people to be supported, as in \"support for military families\", \"scholarships to military children\", \"people who have lost loved ones in the military\" or \"marital status, military status\" in equal-opportunity boilerplate; it fires most strongly in charity and resource-list prose about helping military families and grieving survivors.","s":[0.526473,0.511424,0.518261,0.336145,0.544205,0.365806,0.435969,0.190386,0.052911,0.180119,0.009995,0.005012,0.003612,0.039944,0.01034]},{"p":"L22N7313+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires in text about winter snow conditions (snowstorms, icy roads, snow removal, plowing, road salt, snow days, bad winters), peaking on the token just before the word \"snow\" is likely to come next (\"snow\" as the first piece of snowplow/snowstorm, \"less than 2\" before \"inches of snow\", a year digit in a remembered bad winter, \"p.m.\" in a snow-emergency report), and it promotes \"snow\" as the next word.","s":[0.644096,0.700702,0.655938,0.634473,0.674613,0.062973,0.077263,0.399463,-0.002229,0.301276,0.002341,0.005687,0.005124,-7.5e-05,0.002791]},{"p":"L22N7313-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on tokens inside rote boilerplate whose wording is almost fully predictable. The strongest cases are the company name and 'All rights reserved' in a copyright header ('Copyright (c) Microsoft Corporation. All rights reserved.'), 'Twitter' at the end of 'Like us on Facebook and follow us on Twitter', and 'except' in the investor disclaimer '...disclaims any obligation to update forward-looking statements, except as required by applicable law'.","s":[0.538062,0.246856,0.170646,0.06523,0.031317,0.029831,0.024135,0.022075,0.018015,0.071834,0.043892,0.034872,0.032839,0.053584,0.040274]},{"p":"L22N8401+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the noun naming the place or space being moved through right after a verb of motion plus a path preposition such as along, across, down, up, through or around the (\"cruise along the coast\", \"travel across the galaxy\", \"rode down the street\", \"wander through the cemetery\"), i.e. the terrain or route of a journey.","s":[0.337723,0.519477,0.576194,0.57826,0.367673,0.042731,0.029322,0.020885,0.005826,0.018249,0.016625,0.012459,0.006185,0.018257,0.006486]},{"p":"L22N8401-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the token right before a verb of travelling or moving along a path is expected: the subject or auxiliary before words like 'passes', 'moves', 'travelled', 'roam' or 'are tending', the 'to' in 'takes 30 minutes to drive/get', 'keep' before 'going/driving/scrolling/searching', and the period or comma in driving directions before 'Follow'/'head west'.","s":[0.002541,0.476843,0.447843,0.137007,0.105312,0.012412,0.04993,0.046982,0.025571,0.052339,0.028962,0.005916,0.020405,0.045849,0.026155]},{"p":"L22N9147+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the last word of an everyday action phrase (an object or verb particle such as \"buy\", \"picked up\", \"met him\", \"weighing apples\", \"waiting in line\") at the exact point where a casual public venue is about to be named, as in \"...at the grocery store\", \"...in a coffee shop\", \"...at the bar\" or \"...from the trash\"; it predicts that a mundane location phrase comes next.","s":[0.086406,0.192332,0.076127,0.232381,0.081648,0.012548,0.041017,0.192332,0.039388,0.008011,0.017172,0.013495,0.019595,0.01183,0.023274]},{"p":"L22N9147-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the generic noun (or final word) that completes a highly predictable stock phrase in formal boilerplate prose, especially legal, licence, disclaimer and rules text: \"for non-commercial purposes\", \"acts of God, force majeure, accidents\", \"on an equal basis with others\", \"facts and circumstances\", \"unattended objects\", \"rely on\", \"behave that way\", \"make a lasting impression\".","s":[0.101306,0.162601,0.070186,0.03293,0.035687,0.197971,0.103261,0.072281,0.029962,0.045576,0.042807,0.037993,0.064927,0.082319,0.061712]},{"p":"L22N9321+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the words in a sentence about someone forming an emotional bond with a thing, place or creation, on the tokens just before or forming phrases like \"emotional attachment\", \"sentimental attachment\", \"become attached to\", \"emotionally invested\" (e.g. \"they do not have the same sentimental\", \"I've invested a lot in this car\"), predicting the word attachment/emotional next.","s":[0.714099,0.5826,0.358098,0.918141,0.751352,0.058172,0.057285,0.008101,0.026922,0.071605,0.003716,0.000172,0.002979,0.003801,0.008022]},{"p":"L22N9321-","l":"UNREL","j":"000","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires inside heartfelt expressions of love, devotion or remembrance of a person, especially tributes and condolences for someone who died, on the words just before the emotional payoff such as 'Her voice will live on', 'he will be greatly missed', 'She will never be forgotten', 'remembering the love', 'For Love of' or 'worships the ground'.","s":[0.353106,0.447103,0.280563,0.473098,0.431503,0.067788,0.039071,0.050859,0.055044,0.035231,0.05252,0.057433,0.051548,0.038067,0.051548],"u":{"0":[0.292576,0.462531,0.404193,0.467708,0.396223],"1":[0.420704,0.452418,0.587855,0.509652,0.803543],"2":[0.502951,0.295173,0.346599,0.1275,0.3548]}},{"p":"L23N10053+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires inside prose about armed non-state violence (insurgency, militias, terrorism, banditry, rebels, guerrillas) on tokens where the next word could be about armed groups or weapons, such as \"bandit\" in \"banditry\", \"Terror\" in \"Terrorists\", \"Milit\" in \"Militias\", \"armed\", \"better armed\", \"insurgency\", \"insecurity\", and on year digits or modifiers in such passages; output promotes weapon/armed words.","s":[0.867968,1.275147,0.297893,0.43555,0.449363,0.013772,0.10452,0.146135,0.009673,0.00467,0.010067,0.010434,0.01565,0.039921,0.012426]},{"p":"L23N10053-","l":"TWO-REL","j":"11","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires inside capitalized official national-institution names, on the word right after \"National\" or on \"National\" itself after a country adjective (Indian National Congress/Army, National Health Service, National Highway Authority, Baluchistan National Party), and on the letter pair \"NH\" as in NHK or highway numbers like NH 24.","s":[0.711905,0.696262,1.100446,0.909978,1.054042,0.003673,0.006189,0.034067,0.024104,0.073275,0.024084,0.01064,0.017475,0.025741,0.016722]},{"p":"L23N3137+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Inside the body of a function declared async (C#, JavaScript or Python), fires on the token immediately before an upcoming await keyword (the indentation at the start of the line, the word return, the = of an assignment, or the => / : that opens the block), predicting that \"await\" comes next.","s":[0.4651,0.273836,0.356793,0.439461,0.236084,0.022175,0.024164,0.018719,0.016484,0.004365,0.005101,0.008041,0.005545,0.005848,0.010034]},{"p":"L23N3137-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word just before a male pronoun is about to come next (\"his\", \"him\", \"he\"), typically a preposition or verb in romance or erotic narrative where a woman is the subject acting toward a man, as in \"she pressed her lips against | his\", \"she thrust her hips into | his\", \"she punched | him\", or \"her first marriage, | his second\"; it predicts the upcoming masculine pronoun.","s":[0.475953,0.733412,0.167687,0.227252,0.588806,0.475953,0.150778,0.171129,0.128721,0.066191,0.006035,0.005379,0.006566,0.003152,0.008503]},{"p":"L23N366+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word \"all\" when it follows a preposition and begins a fixed idiom, most strongly \"at all times\" and \"at all costs\" (also \"at all levels\", \"from all over the world\", \"in all of the\", \"of some sort\"), predicting the idiom's closing noun; plain quantifier uses like \"all the people\" do not fire it.","s":[0.522851,0.496448,0.520835,0.529318,0.085838,0.015345,0.018093,0.374999,0.463762,0.018724,0.009076,0.014581,0.006999,0.017287,0.018561]},{"p":"L23N366-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the preposition \"of\" (occasionally \"in\" or \"at\") right after a plural group noun when the next words will be \"all\" plus a range noun, as in the promotional cliché \"kids of all ages\", \"businesses of all sizes\", \"golfers of all skill levels\" or \"views in all directions\"; it predicts the upcoming \"all\".","s":[0.464121,0.450768,0.668772,0.691933,0.543542,0.153308,0.367723,0.066193,0.182191,0.02995,0.041389,0.0066,0.00098,0.020328,0.004717]},{"p":"L23N5356+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a short word-initial piece starting with 's' (sel, sol, sø, su, sil, sop, sú, सु) that begins a longer non-English word, strongest on Indonesian/Malay words like selamat, selamanya, selesaikan, silakan, and on Spanish/Danish words like solicitud, solución, søger, sugerencia, so it does not fire on English words or on these pieces used as whole words.","s":[0.819324,0.200467,0.719276,0.357629,0.151141,0.003915,0.003262,0.000588,0.010416,0.000907,0.010784,0.004959,0.006001,0.005441,0.006545]},{"p":"L23N5356-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the first word-piece of a rare proper name (surname or place name) that begins with a capital S followed by a vowel, especially \"Som\" or \"Sum\" (as in Somogyi, Somerton, Sumida, Sumegi, Somayaji, Somme), when the name is split into pieces, typically right after a first name or in a name list, citation or index; the whole common words \"some\" or \"summer\" do not fire it.","s":[0.927812,0.844532,0.73876,0.519068,0.689307,0.011248,0.004391,0.000189,0.345408,0.006509,0.003866,0.005279,0.005253,0.00236,0.009949]},{"p":"L23N5378+","l":"UNK","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires at the word just before a spot where some form of \"fall\" would be a tempting next word but is being held back. Examples are legal or terms-of-service phrasing about who carries a duty (\"the burden shall be upon…\", \"It is your responsibility…\"), weather or blessing text about rain and sun (\"May the sun shine bright on…\"), and verse or number references. The neuron suppresses \"fall/falls/fell\" as the next token.","s":[0.038615,0.642325,0.094944,0.043129,0.114771,0.040576,0.021606,0.031677,0.024834,0.039426,0.039049,0.017887,0.026799,0.022414,0.026733]},{"p":"L23N5378-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the words leading up to the idiomatic verb \"fall\" (and on \"falls\" itself), most strongly on the subject noun and the words between it and the verb, in phrases like \"the burden often falls on\", \"suspicion fell on\", \"the task fell to\", \"darkness fell\", \"before nightfall\". It is predicting that a form of fall (or the near-synonym rest/lie on) comes next, and it fires hardest when the subject is burden or responsibility.","s":[0.887105,0.660735,0.324129,0.338446,0.466641,0.556458,-0.000714,0.005312,0.054411,0.004813,0.010245,0.006248,0.007745,0.005544,0.007567]},{"p":"L23N5382+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires inside the name or contact details of a hospital or medical institution: on tokens partway through an institution name (\"Royal\" Infirmary \"of\" Edinburgh, VA Medical Center \"in\"/\",\", NYC Health + \"Hospitals\" \"/\" Bellevue, National \"Hospital\", Bolam v Friern \"Hospital\") and on the digits and punctuation of the postcode, street address, phone number or .gov/med URL that directly follows such a hospital/medical-school affiliation.","s":[0.804151,0.737295,0.656476,0.653992,0.585832,0.170757,0.102165,0.10164,0.037088,0.118415,0.018574,0.016003,0.019956,0.005759,0.01333]},{"p":"L23N5382-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the token right after the first name \"Eugene\", i.e. the first piece of the following surname or initial (Eugene Ysaye, Eugene O'Neill, Eugene R. Milhizer, Eugene Katchalov), with weaker spillover onto Oregon/Eugene-area text such as addresses, zip codes and phone numbers.","s":[0.585255,0.770856,0.825776,0.555117,0.439329,0.008121,0.000368,0.007818,0.003711,0.009916,0.001127,0.007394,0.005987,0.006971,0.003002]},{"p":"L23N5851+","l":"TWO-REL","j":"11","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on mid-line tokens of formulaic, highly predictable boilerplate, strongest in source code: the opening ('  or (\" right after require in Node.js lines such as const cors = require('cors'), the from \" in JS import lines, and filter in Python warnings.simplefilter(\"ignore\"). It also fires weakly on opening pieces of rare surnames, such as Weg in Wegner.","s":[0.523312,0.104644,0.90802,0.441488,0.2894,0.350065,0.018001,0.031707,0.022434,0.052802,0.028757,0.028764,0.032684,0.024853,0.03032]},{"p":"L23N5851-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"In sports writing (cricket, baseball, American football, basketball), fires on words describing a player's fielding or defensive play, such as fielder(s), fielding, range at shortstop, sure-handed at third, defensive replacement, Defensive Runs Saved, or hustling back to stop a fast break; it also fires on nearby tokens inside such passages, and its output pushes toward words like defense and defend.","s":[0.853345,0.837828,0.851264,0.542218,0.496575,0.163556,0.085742,0.104095,0.411791,0.018954,0.002591,0.001308,0.004565,0.007642,0.009887]},{"p":"L23N7693+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word just before a noun like \"notions/ideas/expectations\" that names a person's prior, unexamined mental picture of something — above all \"preconceived\" (also \"pre-conceived\", \"precon-ceptions\") and similar modifiers like \"idealized\", \"unrealistic\", \"stereotypical\", \"traditional\", or a possessive \"my/your/our\" — predicting that \"notions\" or \"ideas\" comes next.","s":[1.176006,0.292758,0.679825,1.062584,0.200399,0.108918,0.006147,0.00842,0.005881,0.007159,0.006926,0.006609,0.011352,0.008839,0.006278]},{"p":"L23N7693-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a single \"0\" digit token in the middle of a number, i.e. a 0 that comes right after other digits or a digit-plus-separator (\"1-8[0]0-\", \"10.[0]00\", \"2.0100000[0]00016\", \"(0[0]1\", \"720,[0]00\"), strongest inside long runs of zeros in floats, toll-free 1-800 phone numbers and thousands written with a period or comma.","s":[0.37694,0.676265,0.083368,0.456054,0.448803,0.37694,0.050026,0.003867,0.015003,0.284359,0.002796,0.003522,0.008365,0.007427,0.004123]},{"p":"L23N910+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the digit tokens inside the Wi-Fi standard number \"802.11\" (the \"1\"s after \"802.\", as in 802.11n/ac/ax), and more weakly on other digits in wireless-networking text such as years (2011, 2019) or Mbps speed figures, where the model is predicting the next digit of a Wi-Fi spec.","s":[0.611848,0.857812,0.559514,0.637027,0.67035,0.29935,0.36404,0.159578,0.212634,0.04619,0.021393,0.025697,0.023722,0.042879,0.018067]},{"p":"L23N910-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires inside text about vehicle electronics and diagnostics (CAN bus, OBD-II ports and scanners, ECUs, diagnostic trouble codes, ECU tuning or diagnostic software and cables), most strongly on tokens like \"can\"/\"CAN\", \"OBD\", \"diagnostic\" or a model year right where the text is about a car's computer network or diagnostic interface, while generic car-repair or general electronics text stays quiet.","s":[0.513294,0.693814,0.987766,0.641336,0.599827,0.061676,0.010202,0.016724,0.033894,0.003703,-0.002004,-0.001224,-0.000508,-9e-05,-0.000946]},{"p":"L24N1919+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word right before an expected \"plate(s)\", mostly \"license\" in \"license plate\" (also \"number\" in \"number plates\", or a state/country name before \"plates\") in contexts about cars, traffic cameras, surveillance or police reports, predicting that \"plate\" comes next.","s":[0.812432,1.048649,0.593034,0.693781,0.861081,0.640308,1.007431,0.299407,0.510421,0.22296,0.013222,0.011537,0.00907,0.0095,0.013991]},{"p":"L24N1919-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"In text about driving and owning cars (car specs, rental fuel policies, mpg forum posts, tire-dealer ads), this fires on the word that opens a car-specific term, most often \"fuel\" before economy, mileage, efficiency, capacity or consumption, \"full\" before \"tank\", or the \"Fire\" of the tire brand Firestone.","s":[0.622725,0.608347,0.930276,0.932554,0.724785,0.616963,0.245827,0.266138,0.456071,0.303925,0.01942,0.020854,0.015036,0.012216,0.013061]},{"p":"L24N1997+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a severity or type modifier placed before a condition or disorder name, above all \"mild\" or \"Mild\" (including as the first piece of Mildred, mildew, Mildenhall, mild-mannered), and also \"severe\" or \"seasonal\" right before an illness word, as in \"a mild case of\", \"Severe Acute Respiratory Syndrome\", \"seasonal allergies\" or \"seasonal depression\".","s":[0.298854,0.388691,0.394824,0.52598,0.466556,0.084775,0.081089,0.053576,0.004268,0.064682,0.004704,0.006921,0.006918,0.006488,0.007267]},{"p":"L24N1997-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the second word piece of a rare, multi-piece proper name, most strongly the piece right after a leading \"Cas\"/\"Cass\"/\"Chas\" in Italian- or Spanish-style surnames and place names (Casanueva, Cassano, Casimira, Casini, Chasiv Yar), and also on inner pieces of other long exotic names (Opechancanough, Satchidananda, Tippecanoe, Tiahuanaco); it does not fire on common whole words like \"castle\" or \"casino\".","s":[0.883375,0.497404,0.558509,0.544545,0.623393,0.017587,0.015735,0.018744,0.000741,0.017304,0.007202,0.023209,0.014558,0.015144,0.010907]},{"p":"L24N3271+","l":"TWO-REL","j":"11","f":0,"e":0,"x":"In stock boilerplate such as licenses, disclaimers, terms of use and cookie notices, this fires on a list word that the model expects to be followed by \"or\" or a closing bracket rather than a comma (e.g. \"medical advice, diagnosis or treatment\", \"WITHOUT WARRANTIES OR CONDITIONS\", \"in connection with the SOFTWARE OR THE USE\", \"news tip, correction or comment\"), and also on a lone callback argument like function(e) just before \")\".","s":[0.764107,0.63458,0.566159,0.035708,0.373291,0.018222,0.005338,0.058198,0.146444,0.041591,0.016833,0.012685,0.016167,0.011074,0.011353]},{"p":"L24N3271-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Predicts that a comma comes next: fires on the token just before an expected comma, by far most strongly on the lone \"0\" path segment in old news-site URLs that continue with a comma, such as guardian.co.uk/story/0,,2190770,00.html or time.com/article/0,9171,993017,00.html, and more weakly on the \"0\" opening a coordinate or interval like (0,0) or [0,1].","s":[0.668178,0.704157,0.421527,0.251554,0.240981,0.104039,0.065506,0.035591,0.000322,0.00265,0.00173,0.001845,0.001868,0.002549,0.00221]},{"p":"L24N3704+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the comma that follows a surname in an inverted 'Surname, Firstname' name list, such as an alphabetical index or directory of people where lines read like 'Potter, Laurie (Dr.)' or 'Stanley, Edward', at the point where a given name is about to follow.","s":[0.663637,0.71031,0.741402,0.782596,0.589716,0.541572,0.385301,0.397689,0.00469,0.618967,0.013405,0.004896,0.00761,0.003207,0.005869]},{"p":"L24N3704-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word right before a person's name is about to appear, especially a job-title noun used as a bare prefix to a foreign name (\"Paraguayan prosecutor [Federico Delfino]\", \"Cluj manager [Ioan Andone]\", \"Brazilian midfielder [Rafael Bastos]\", \"directed by [Yūichirō Yano]\", or the comma between surname and initials in an author list), so that the next token is predicted to be a non-English first name.","s":[0.406441,0.756717,0.653864,0.632114,0.251852,0.063312,0.130835,0.023484,0.008316,0.006975,0.000643,0.012109,0.013043,0.010907,0.009997]},{"p":"L24N5934+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the first or a middle word-piece of a multi-token European (mostly French, Italian, German) place name, a small town, village, commune or château name, at the point where the name is being spelled out, e.g. the \"Lug\" of Lugano, \"Lud\" of Ludwigsburg, \"Sal\" of Salò or \"Raz\" of Château de Razay, typically in travel, wine, history or address text.","s":[0.503686,0.520483,0.518308,0.839597,0.341352,0.020596,0.124083,0.109248,0.305204,0.074723,0.00423,0.007725,0.012735,0.008562,0.017638]},{"p":"L24N5934-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the brand name of a European (mostly Italian) car maker, overwhelmingly \"Fiat\" (also Lancia, FIAT, Skoda, Citroen), and the token right after it where a model name is expected next, strongest on the space or token between \"Fiat\" and \"500\" in casual or listing text such as \"a Fiat 500\".","s":[0.968879,0.74433,0.870365,0.847117,0.776728,0.038504,0.072782,0.004921,0.054346,0.118166,0.010229,0.007046,0.008812,0.010152,0.009743]},{"p":"L24N6751+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word-start token \"Sher\"/\"Scher\"/\"cher\" (any case, with or without leading space) when it opens a longer proper noun or word such as Sherman, Sheringham, Sherbrooke, Scherf, Sheridan, Sherwood, Sherlock, Cherpota or cherries, mostly surnames and place names.","s":[0.01808,0.616114,0.765928,0.009642,0.413795,0.006103,0.009078,0.011676,0.010496,0.005764,0.009675,0.013925,0.014023,0.00724,0.009402]},{"p":"L24N6751-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires inside an organism name in naturalist lists of species, on the first or a middle word-piece of a multi-piece Latin genus name (Ery- of Erythronium, -ith- of Ornithogalum, -or- of Cetorhinus, Heter- of Heterodera) or on the leading modifier of a common species name (Lesser in Lesser Kestrel, Zebra in Zebra mussel, tawny in tawny owl), where the rest of the taxon name still has to be completed.","s":[0.956823,0.750697,0.770588,0.966466,0.687438,0.061156,0.090098,0.011033,0.019809,0.046245,0.020273,0.015328,0.016024,0.015679,0.009475]},{"p":"L24N6884+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word \"Please\" (and occasionally \"to\" in the same slot) when it opens a polite instruction aimed at the reader or user, as in website boilerplate, form prompts, error messages and UI notices (\"Please wait...\", \"Please enable cookies\", \"Please log in to see this content\", \"Please note that...\"), and is strongest when the request comes right after a line break.","s":[0.368317,0.452594,0.616271,0.384198,0.485224,0.101844,0.311389,0.175883,0.137275,0.231262,0.018207,0.015456,0.008001,0.012382,0.015343]},{"p":"L24N6884-","l":"TWO-REL","j":"11","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires inside step-by-step how-to instructions at the point just before the next action or tool is named. The two main cases are the period after a step number in browser or software uninstall walkthroughs (e.g. \"1. Open Google Chrome\") and sanding or abrasive talk, on \"sand\" in \"sand paper\" or \"sand blasting\" and on grit or speed numbers like \"80-120 grit\" or \"up to 3500 rpm\".","s":[0.159653,0.259223,0.392286,0.533144,0.75206,0.006853,0.011737,0.014183,0.068784,0.006385,0.013772,0.003708,0.009335,0.01223,0.013652]},{"p":"L24N8171+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a capitalized common-word token, especially Wish, Rush, Pitch or Patch, when it is used as a proper name or as the first piece of one: a surname after a first name (Barbara Wish, Mike Rush, Mary Pitchford), a place (Rushden, Rushmoor, Rush Green), a band or title (Wishbone Ash, Wish You Were Here, Patchwork). It does not fire on the ordinary verb or noun (\"I wish\", \"rush hour\", \"a pitch\").","s":[1.02636,0.768514,0.71157,0.848953,0.714841,0.340395,0.205383,0.385476,0.078141,0.019687,0.007645,0.006203,0.005048,0.011064,0.004167]},{"p":"L24N8171-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word-initial fragment \"const\" (or \"cost\"/\"Const\") when it is only the first piece of a longer word that the tokenizer splits, such as constrains, consternation, constricts, constipated, constantly, Constance, constables, or costumed; whole words like \"cost\", \"constant\", or \"construction\" should not fire it.","s":[0.84472,0.790732,0.70408,0.591862,0.894865,0.005271,0.024899,0.02186,0.014706,0.040054,0.029182,0.097861,0.028293,0.014284,0.089136]},{"p":"L24N9882+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"In job descriptions, qualification requirements and professional-service lists, this fires on the word just before a management or supervision word is expected, such as \"construction\" before \"management/administration/oversight\", \"quality\" before \"control\", \"hire, train, and\" before \"supervise\", \"under the\" before \"direction\", or \"organizational and\" before \"leadership\".","s":[0.533627,0.269988,0.130517,0.100901,0.381171,0.37604,0.0864,0.130517,0.102764,0.305381,0.003704,0.001646,0.008864,0.005873,0.000476]},{"p":"L24N9882-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Inside corporate job-posting or HR boilerplate (duty and competency lists, equal-opportunity statements, staff titles such as \"Sr. ... Engineer\" or \"Senior Manager, ...\"), fires on the word that begins the next duty, competency or job-title piece (verbs like Develop, Drives, Builds, Manages; title words like Technical, product, Support; or the \"on\" in \"does not discriminate on\"), predicting workplace or management vocabulary.","s":[0.137458,0.200585,0.228969,0.335466,0.218835,0.011614,0.011579,0.009674,0.007895,0.00691,0.008827,0.021489,0.011252,0.009904,0.015606]},{"p":"L25N1814+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a middle word-piece inside a long, multi-token non-English word, typically a prefixed verb or verbal noun split by the tokenizer (Polish 'zatrudnienie', Dutch 'aankondigen' and 'bevestig', German 'Vervielfältigung', Slovak 'zverejnená', Russian 'объединения' and 'осуществим', Czech 'zpřístupnit'), often in a non-English snippet set inside English text or code; it does not fire on short whole words or on English words.","s":[0.778967,0.72536,0.370321,0.665779,0.411805,0.003179,0.00283,0.006978,0.010095,0.008222,0.001424,0.00237,-0.000259,0.006544,0.008501]},{"p":"L25N1814-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires inside rigidly formulaic, templated text where the next token is almost fully determined: SQL dump constraint lines (the closing backtick after REFERENCES `table`), LaTeX inline math such as \\( \\frac{...}{...} \\) and \\text{...}, Java import package paths (the dots in org.springframework.context.annotation), and stock boilerplate phrases (\"views are the author's own and do not necessarily represent\", \"free and open access\", \"unsubscribe at any time\").","s":[0.056384,0.803931,0.087603,0.032318,0.030708,0.014716,0.02641,0.018505,0.02957,0.01753,0.015899,0.020594,0.025433,0.029235,0.031561]},{"p":"L25N3227+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word piece \"amb\" (also \"Amb\") when it begins a longer word split into pieces, as in amb|ulatories, amb|assador, amb|ush, amb|led, amb|rosial, amb|iguity, amb|ivalence, amb|ience, with weaker firing on similar first pieces like \"gamb\" (viola da gamba) and \"ust\"/\"sust\" (Giustino, sustains).","s":[0.005093,0.602504,0.721911,0.713063,0.812386,0.006768,0.003746,0.019443,0.009395,0.013599,0.01783,0.019119,0.005306,0.019118,0.017718]},{"p":"L25N3227-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a word-piece token built around an 's'+'c' consonant cluster (esc, resc, scr, cr, constr) that starts or sits inside a longer word the tokenizer has split, such as 'Sus|cr|íbete' (Spanish newsletter sign-up), 'esc|orts', 'Esc|offier', 'esc|italopram', 'Esc|ott', 'resc|uer', 'Res|c|ued' and 'constr|uctor'. It does not fire on whole common words that are single tokens.","s":[1.058354,0.998254,0.689938,0.850779,0.010554,0.066912,0.018958,0.009799,0.013615,0.002288,0.01943,0.019911,0.019683,0.018661,0.012904]},{"p":"L25N3973+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a token in the middle of a familiar multi-token name or string whose continuation is all but certain, most strongly the dot inside a web domain in technical or code text (e.g. the dot in \"jsfiddle.net\", \"jshint.com\", \"www.jppf.org\"), and also on the middle pieces of split proper names (Far|han Akhtar, F|ager|berg, Ar|rog|ant Bastard).","s":[0.702681,0.095041,0.212824,0.093868,0.617221,0.015533,0.095041,0.01027,0.010931,0.00877,0.01445,0.020353,0.025285,0.01708,0.012474]},{"p":"L25N3973-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"In JavaScript code, the opening quote mark (' or \") of the string literal that a typeof result is compared against, as in typeof x === 'undefined', typeof x == \"number\" or case 'string' inside a switch on typeof, where the next word is a type name.","s":[0.797803,0.748008,0.475931,0.529654,0.631477,0.212935,0.025424,0.097114,0.007523,0.044364,0.003792,0.007097,0.005394,0.025003,0.014377]},{"p":"L25N5115+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the first word-piece of a person's surname immediately after their first name or initials (e.g. \"Patrice Ev|ra\", \"Chris EV|ANS\", \"Louis Van| Gaal\", \"Florian Le|juene\", \"François du| Plessis\"): the start of a surname, especially when that surname is split into pieces or begins with a particle like Van/Le/du.","s":[0.987488,0.972323,0.228065,0.165656,0.063801,0.00597,0.015768,0.023052,0.005907,0.013966,0.008192,0.020501,0.00991,0.014249,0.008092]},{"p":"L25N5115-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the capitalized word-initial piece \"Vi\" (also \"VI\", \"Ví\", \"Vie\") when it opens a longer word, typically a proper name or capitalized term split into pieces, such as Viorel, Violi, Viadiu, Viaggio, Vi Hart, Viagra, VIETNAM, Viande or Violas, not when \"Vi\" is a complete common word.","s":[0.909955,0.73574,0.735484,0.328205,0.68283,0.13551,0.014726,0.027477,0.015942,0.001534,0.015751,0.011522,0.023952,0.016494,0.022687]},{"p":"L25N5679+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a middle sub-word piece shaped like a vowel plus \"s\" (\"es\", \"ites\", \"okes\", \"ores\", \"ish\", \"esh\", \"hares\", \"kes\", \"ies\") inside a rare, usually foreign proper name that the tokenizer splits into fragments, especially Sanskrit/Indic deity, temple and place names (Avalokiteshvara, Lokesvara, Rameshwara, Ankleshwar, Harihareswara) and other exotic place names (Olorgesailie, Giresun, Fiesole, Dar es Salaam); common English words with \"es\" do not fire it.","s":[0.683127,0.505538,0.303573,0.926323,0.663872,0.042399,0.007346,0.008395,0.005789,0.008281,0.006513,0.011953,0.017852,0.008083,0.004826]},{"p":"L25N5679-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a word-piece spelling 'is'/'Is' (or a closely similar s-ending piece) that sits inside or begins an uncommon proper name or rare word: Fris-bee or Fris-by, Ann-is, Ver-is, Is-aach, Is-abela, Isis, 'Tis, is-thmus. It also fires on first names like Andreas and Frances in lists of people (cast lists, football line-ups), and sometimes on the 's'/'is' right after 'It' (It's, It is).","s":[0.194852,0.404535,1.145568,0.795099,0.51069,0.015214,0.097488,0.084084,0.085531,0.002946,0.01564,0.015519,0.013103,0.00843,0.013117]},{"p":"L25N644+","l":"UNREL","j":"00","f":0,"e":0,"x":"In architecture, construction and municipal land-use documents (planning commission minutes, staff reports, project sheets, firm bios), the pole fires on the words Planning, Design, Construction or Staff when they are used as part of an official role, department or process name, such as 'Planning Manager', 'Planning Commission', 'Design Director', 'design team', 'design and construction' or 'STAFF RECOMMENDATION'.","s":[0.515575,0.340277,0.58137,0.659653,0.420858,0.201149,0.276133,0.616317,0.526881,0.01468,0.014848,0.019204,0.022651,0.01641,0.011266],"u":{"0":[0.398686,0.539871,0.695051,0.438461,0.572065],"1":[0.453624,0.478242,0.444403,0.464825,0.484682]}},{"p":"L25N644-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"In UX/UI or web/app design-process text, fires on the word or punctuation just before a mention of prototypes or mockups is likely to come next (after \"wireframes,\" / \"wireframes and\", \"low/high fidelity\", \"paper\", \"create\"), i.e. predicting the word \"prototype\"/\"mockup\" in a design-deliverables list.","s":[0.84397,0.72447,0.612301,0.786479,0.731429,0.163186,0.20146,0.369979,0.641652,0.133216,0.005074,0.007821,0.001301,0.00592,0.001297]},{"p":"L25N6962+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the final word of a place or institution name that closes a movement or destination phrase, such as 'moved to Yale', 'settled in Texas', 'was taken to UCI Medical Center' or 'returned to England', and on the last symbol of a displayed equation; in both cases a clause like ', where …' naturally comes next.","s":[0.007314,0.325811,0.210981,0.122003,0.05127,0.007438,0.011692,0.043978,0.011981,0.013424,0.011701,0.00772,0.019911,0.013734,0.018225]},{"p":"L25N6962-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the token immediately before a highly predictable \"where\": the word right before \"where\" in set phrases (\"any questions regarding| where and how\", \"give credit| where it's due\", \"a place| where\", \"get to| where you are going\") and the first piece of split -where compounds (else|where, any|where, EVERY|WHERE, in the middle of NOW|HERE, some|where). It is a next-token prediction of \"where\" and does not fire on the word \"where\" itself.","s":[0.387333,0.009442,0.031893,0.001426,0.085421,0.093142,0.004897,0.005487,0.001233,0.062077,0.003334,0.002725,0.00489,0.001022,0.002096]},{"p":"L25N7615+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the token just before a likely \"wrap\" word, predicting that \"wrap/wrapped/wrapping\" comes next: e.g. \"shrink\", \"bubble\", \"gift\", \"individually\", \"beautifully\" or \"unw-\" before wrap(ped), and in UI code on \"flex\" in CSS \"flex-direction\"/\"flexWrap\" or on the separator before \"WrapContent\"/\"wrap_content\"/'wrap'.","s":[0.601717,0.521579,0.677551,0.600981,0.816019,0.222428,0.106788,0.068111,0.600981,0.174638,0.005448,0.001539,0.001651,0.005583,0.004716]},{"p":"L25N7615-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"In product-packaging and shipping text (export cartons, pouches, packing specs, packaging manufacturers), this pole fires on the word just before a packing material or method could follow, pushing the model toward predicting 'wrap', 'wrapped' or 'wrapping'.","s":[0.069329,0.091225,0.185264,0.127187,0.115727,0.299798,0.088678,0.185264,0.009096,0.063643,0.015526,0.024648,0.014449,0.019486,0.014081]},{"p":"L25N9519+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the first or middle word-piece of a Balinese/Indonesian place name (a village, street, temple or landmark such as Sanggingan, Tunduh, Tanah Lot, Tukad Cepung, Ulun Danu, Kubutambahan) when it appears in English travel or restaurant text about Bali, especially in address lines like \"Jl. Raya ..., Banjar ..., Kecamatan Ubud\".","s":[0.732702,0.553262,0.402724,0.552311,0.423198,0.001926,0.008831,0.016164,0.010059,0.016073,0.01655,0.013964,0.017307,0.02003,0.026818]},{"p":"L25N9519-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word immediately before a predictable \"consider\"/\"consideration\" (or a close synonym like \"thinking of\"), such as \"please\" in \"please consider supporting/subscribing\", \"receive\" in \"will receive consideration for employment\", \"strongly/seriously\" in \"you should strongly consider\", or the comma in \"If you enjoyed this, consider…\"; it predicts the verb consider is coming next, not the word itself.","s":[0.385364,0.82201,0.84128,0.311818,0.45795,0.385364,0.30459,0.28869,0.048373,0.007447,0.007329,0.011163,0.00831,0.008309,0.01094]},{"p":"L26N1382+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on a word-piece in the middle of a proper surname or brand name, typically a Slavic or Germanic one, where the piece follows a capital K or another opening piece (Kof-, Koff-, Kohl-, Kov-, Kit-, -etz-, -off-). The model expects a name-ending suffix such as -ski, -sky, -man, -mann, -acs or -er to come next.","s":[0.170311,0.449855,0.684382,0.403805,0.510995,0.016251,0.029812,0.013354,0.014181,0.01593,0.021169,0.010737,0.011128,0.005916,0.0214]},{"p":"L26N1382-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on a word piece in the middle of a rare, foreign-looking (mostly Slavic or Germanic) surname, such as the \"aj\" in Wajda, the \"Mark\" in Markovskaya, Markelov or Markiewicz, and the \"Weing\" in Weingarten, where a name has been split into pieces and more name pieces such as \"-ovskaya\", \"-da\" or \"-arten\" must follow.","s":[0.87642,0.471902,0.652792,0.862205,0.306927,0.082458,0.021395,0.006235,0.011103,0.014507,0.010492,0.009652,0.033787,0.017269,0.007767]},{"p":"L26N2364+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on formatting tokens (the whitespace or ' >' after a line break, an apostrophe, a comma or a dot in a URL) inside Jira or issue-tracker text, such as Apache Jira issue dumps with 'Key/Project/Issue Type/Reporter/Priority' fields and Jira help forum posts, where the model expects a field name like Issue, Reporter or Priority next.","s":[0.488375,0.597663,0.253308,0.625298,0.306342,0.014119,0.008963,0.009688,0.011007,0.010877,0.015956,0.00144,0.002991,0.005241,0.015048]},{"p":"L26N2364-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on the word right before an expected \"private\"/PM, mainly \"in\" in the spam-comment phrase \"Write to me in PM\" (also \"by\" or \"encrypted\" before \"private message\", or \"Send\" before \"Message\"), so it is a prediction that \"private\" or \"PM\" comes next.","s":[0.532318,0.159671,0.419835,0.317644,0.496864,0.221223,0.074793,0.032737,0.002711,0.048185,0.004963,0.00456,0.00361,0.002768,0.00273]},{"p":"L26N2836+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires in sports-team contexts (mostly hockey, basketball and baseball news and game writing) on tokens where the next word could plausibly be 'team' or a team-related word, such as 'coach' after a team name, 'crew' or 'soccer' or 'basketball' just before 'team', and name-initial pieces in rosters; it promotes 'team' in the output.","s":[0.178024,0.176499,0.157072,0.369711,0.190345,0.04029,0.011877,0.021258,0.013872,0.008926,0.005699,0.013154,0.014174,0.003676,0.011939]},{"p":"L26N2836-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires in sports writing (NFL, NHL, MLB, NBA) on a modifier word or word-piece that names a position unit or role and is about to be followed by a group noun (corps, staff, backs, ies, line, guard), such as \"receiving\" in \"receiving corps\", \"pitching\" in \"pitching staff\", \"goal\" in \"goalies\", or \"running\" in \"running back\"; the output promotes \"team\".","s":[0.840718,0.858804,0.788874,0.762923,0.389202,0.01147,0.021773,0.010454,0.009228,0.012355,0.008669,0.009732,0.011572,0.005024,0.004265]},{"p":"L26N4833+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires in technical or engineering prose (hardware and CPU thermals, materials and mechanical engineering, lab reagent catalogue numbers, auto repair) on a token that starts or continues a technical term, such as the first piece of 'heat spreader', 'temperature stress', 'TEFLON' or 'EPoxy', or a digit inside a part number, where a technical continuation is expected.","s":[0.370349,0.48987,1.011871,0.187775,0.241701,0.007817,0.023022,0.03008,0.032214,0.028269,0.029051,0.026863,0.048472,0.033694,0.031412]},{"p":"L26N4833-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Function words and punctuation (of, on, to, the, commas, and) and word pieces inside industrial metalworking and materials-engineering prose, such as heat treatment, steel alloys, machining, coatings and industrial machinery, where the next word is likely another technical process or material term.","s":[0.74444,0.930575,0.513252,0.513322,0.53549,0.082544,0.034055,0.020526,0.062998,0.024172,0.028671,0.007971,0.011561,0.032908,0.022518]},{"p":"L26N6139+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on the subject pronoun (usually \"we\", also \"still\", \"have\", \"I\", \"they\") or the next function word right after a narrator says they met or befriended someone, where the text goes on to describe the lasting relationship (keep in touch, stayed close, became friends, got married).","s":[0.625537,0.630121,0.476219,0.603313,0.340747,0.309562,0.138742,0.027923,0.012146,0.139,0.005354,0.006315,0.00563,0.016151,0.002942]},{"p":"L26N6139-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on digits or the closing parenthesis inside a phone number just after the area code (e.g. \"(902) \", \"902-4\", \"317-\", \"1-888-\"), with weaker firing on digit runs in other identifiers like DOIs, legal title numbers and dial-in numbers, and on a few mid-sentence words where a number-like or name continuation is expected.","s":[0.418054,0.200299,0.592563,0.149552,0.429782,0.13629,0.012089,0.023081,0.064855,0.107506,0.020527,0.056622,0.009307,0.033766,0.034519]},{"p":"L26N7840+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on function words and punctuation (a comma, \"and\", \"in\", \"of\", \"are\", \"with\") and on word pieces like \"voice-over\" that sit right after film or narrative-technique terms (flashbacks, voice-over, narration, narrators, dream sequences) in film reviews, criticism and screenwriting prose, where the next word is likely another storytelling device.","s":[0.996518,0.755583,1.264762,0.821546,0.849124,0.010821,0.006772,0.007876,0.010862,0.012624,0.03945,0.026765,0.052079,0.001305,0.042804]},{"p":"L26N7840-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires (negatively) on function tokens such as commas, 'and', 'including', 'The', apostrophes, periods and line breaks inside running English web text, especially while a list or title is being laid out (Disney+/Marvel/Star Wars show titles, legal or regulatory text, care-home inspection reports), where the next word is a content word starting a new list item or name.","s":[0.846278,0.816005,0.010467,0.699161,0.703061,0.003128,0.006965,0.035554,0.009311,0.002897,0.025087,0.012415,0.006965,0.017896,0.009056]},{"p":"L26N9340+","l":"UNREL","j":"00","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on the word right after a money-and-cost verb or phrase (pay, afford, cheaper than, cost, cover, a month in, cost of living), usually a possessive such as \"your\", \"my\", \"their\" or \"her\", or a determiner, preposition or the verb \"afford\", where the next word is likely a thing being paid for (rent, bills, fees, living); it also fires on the \"4\" of a +44 UK phone number and on \"$4 dollar\".","s":[0.564417,0.609958,0.532758,0.372083,0.469937,0.032966,0.077338,0.021599,0.08616,0.063121,0.01562,0.025781,0.024999,0.012191,0.015924],"u":{"0":[0.58096,0.50283,0.71971,0.598442,0.43372],"1":[0.335079,0.326257,0.294644,0.492992,0.424276]}},{"p":"L26N9340-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires (negatively) on a token in the middle of a medical or biomedical phrase or a technical identifier, where the next token continues a fixed disease or drug name or a number. Typical spots: \"rel|apsed\", \"patients| with\" (before a disease), \"all| kinds\" (before \"of\"), \"X\" before \"-linked\" in citation titles, and a digit inside a longer number or version string.","s":[0.624482,0.208371,0.090497,0.02281,0.061195,0.015548,0.00865,0.045971,0.012738,0.008183,0.003839,0.014856,0.002748,0.010237,0.010957]},{"p":"L26N9372+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on the token just before a word for guessing is expected, when the model predicts \"guess\" (guessing, guesswork, guessed) next: in \"second-guess\", \"educated guess\", \"anyone's guess\", \"wild guess\", \"guess work\", and inside Russian отгадать / Spanish adivinar guessing-game text and code.","s":[0.846547,0.655594,0.594822,0.674488,0.631641,0.006131,0.004985,0.022035,0.005731,0.067931,0.004376,0.007404,0.013445,0.002666,0.000972]},{"p":"L26N9372-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"The neuron fires on tokens in the middle of a rare or arbitrary sequence, where the next token is hard to predict and the model can only guess: the leading 0 of an octal permission or umask, digit runs in DOIs, hashes and IDs, the start of a rare surname, and similar slots in code and technical text.","s":[1.0062,0.036883,0.014093,0.120821,1.12392,0.134137,0.005889,0.018172,0.008397,0.379457,0.017909,0.046081,0.085038,0.044436,0.016688]},{"p":"L26N9472+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on a quantifier, determiner or preposition (any, all, other, another, by, with, of, those) that sits right before a generic word for people (person, persons, children, anyone, parties), mostly in legal or regulatory boilerplate such as terms of use, privacy policies, appliance safety warnings, equal-opportunity statements and liability clauses.","s":[0.89795,0.517472,0.181897,0.159522,0.026632,0.203797,0.028701,0.181897,0.100758,0.026632,0.026885,0.018056,0.023164,0.026885,0.036035]},{"p":"L26N9472-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on a modifier or word-piece that comes right before the plural group noun \"folk/folks/people/types/crowd\" in a phrase like \"us rational folk\", \"you computer people\" or \"those EMI people\", where the next word is expected to be \"folk\" or \"people\" (the output suppresses \"folk\").","s":[0.633391,0.024456,0.282846,0.533385,0.437965,0.051691,0.03234,0.042121,0.028752,0.114129,0.003142,-0.000503,0.000594,-0.000921,0.004076]},{"p":"L27N10083+","l":"TWO-REL","j":"11","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on the token just before an expected \"write\"-family word, mostly in code: the dot after an object like fs, res, writer, json or plt when a write/writeFile/writeHead/dump/end call follows, or the opening quote of the mode string 'w'/\"wb\" in open(path, ...); it also fires weakly on other places where \"write\" or \"writing\" is the likely next word.","s":[0.160585,0.387503,0.266962,0.196705,0.175619,0.020761,0.033183,0.063837,0.013596,0.024457,0.032587,0.014801,0.016059,0.041337,0.020996]},{"p":"L27N10083-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on the token just before the model expects a form of \"write\" (wrote, writing, written, rewrite, overwrite, écrit, escribir), such as a split-off prefix like \" overw\" or \"manus\", the \"of\" in \"piece of\" before \"writing\", or a possessive or period before \"write\"; it is a prediction of an upcoming write-word.","s":[0.26573,0.512992,0.022619,0.248101,0.083042,0.001329,0.002852,0.022619,0.103501,0.008392,-0.00019,-0.000146,-4.7e-05,-0.000828,-0.00044]},{"p":"L27N1674+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on the word right before the token \" city\" (or \"City\", \"town\") is expected, such as \"largest\", \"the\", \"a\", \"New\" in \"New York\" or \"Mexico\" in \"Mexico City\", where the context is geographic and about cities; it suppresses the word city.","s":[0.200333,0.410773,0.129104,0.411429,0.013234,0.023667,0.039407,0.018872,0.037514,0.01738,0.030371,0.020446,0.033007,0.025699,0.031061]},{"p":"L27N1674-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires negatively on tokens in text where the word \"city\" (or town, cidade, ciudad) is the likely next word or concept: structured code and SQL about countries, cities and states, 'Sex and the' before 'City', 'Kansas' before 'City', and numeric or format fragments inside city-related documents. It works as a 'next word is city' predictor that suppresses city and town tokens.","s":[0.241373,0.246572,0.049337,0.188298,0.080777,0.011752,-0.002526,-0.002064,0.015446,0.029653,-0.000113,0.001354,-0.003693,-0.000858,-0.000981]},{"p":"L27N2664+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires in statistics/R-programming and academic text on a token that begins or sits inside a rare name or code unit where the next piece is a hard-to-predict continuation: the line break between R plotting or test commands, or the opening piece of a surname or word such as Rid|ker, Ris|cal, Back|fitting, Bre|usch.","s":[0.71667,1.085346,1.129194,0.700509,0.554458,0.014792,0.502665,0.025169,0.025585,0.016487,0.025271,0.024017,0.024021,0.017567,0.033731]},{"p":"L27N2664-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires inside statistical-methods passages in survey or regression write-ups (survey weighting, standard errors, sampling design, significance testing), on the words in the middle of a methodological phrase such as \"accounted for the survey weighting\", \"calculated using the Taylor series\" or \"Standard errors are in parentheses\", where the next token is a statistics term.","s":[1.100615,0.580096,1.04137,0.766871,0.991228,0.010248,0.005877,0.008499,0.005417,0.007698,0.009,0.007285,0.002956,0.017337,0.005364]},{"p":"L27N3256+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on a token inside or just before a multi-word technical or specialized term, where the next token is expected to be a specific noun that completes the term (e.g. \"appetite\" before \"suppressant\", \"thyroid\" before \"hormone\", \"behavioral\" before \"and biomedical sciences\"), most strongly in spammy appetite-suppressant text and in biomedical or technical prose.","s":[0.2408,0.632852,0.23891,0.385173,0.139907,0.027344,0.025566,0.029727,0.027652,0.021204,0.04138,0.025943,0.018778,0.012506,0.041042]},{"p":"L27N3256-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires (negative pole) on the next-to-be-predicted position inside physics-style passages about magnets, magnetism, electric fields, gravity or particles, on the word or function word right around 'magnet(s)' or 'magnetic' (e.g. 'magnets can', 'the magnetic field', 'materials such as'), where the model expects physical-science terms like attract, repel, field or force to follow.","s":[0.555326,0.428121,0.56373,0.833751,0.221739,0.081073,0.01304,0.157665,0.376631,0.023113,0.015629,0.02547,0.027692,0.022079,0.004187]},{"p":"L27N3655+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on the middle of a continuing token sequence in North American place and location text (ZIP codes, highway numbers like I-10 and I-12, Louisiana/Quebec/Manitoba/Indiana place names) and on word-initial fragments like \"Cre\"/\"Cré\"/\"Que\", where the next piece is a predictable continuation of the name or number.","s":[0.3009,0.162557,0.42793,0.47783,0.251718,0.025515,0.007539,0.005156,0.009637,0.010247,0.01217,0.015731,0.008604,0.016847,0.001114]},{"p":"L27N3655-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires (negatively) in local news or regional-interest web articles, mostly from the northern California coast, on small function tokens just before a place name is expected: prepositions like in, of, near, outside, the apostrophe in contractions or possessives such as 's, 've, 're, and sometimes a newline or space before a number.","s":[0.671214,0.821501,0.745436,0.672936,0.708956,0.008485,0.067326,0.006868,0.001301,0.000851,0.006151,0.002278,0.00262,0.002777,0.002644]},{"p":"L27N409+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on punctuation, digits and function words (commas, hyphens, apostrophes, spaces, \"of\", \"in\") in the middle of a document that is about Rio de Janeiro or Brazil, especially address lines, postal codes and phone numbers ending in \"Rio de Janeiro, RJ\"; it tracks that the running topic is Rio and that the next token is likely a Rio or Brazil place name or code.","s":[0.812956,0.74537,0.802779,0.755445,0.771133,0.141334,0.007455,0.007965,0.004384,0.007312,0.005133,0.004687,0.005815,0.004003,0.003236]},{"p":"L27N409-","l":"TWO-REL","j":"11","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on the \".\" in JavaScript canvas code right after a canvas variable (e.g. \"canvas.width = …; canvas.height\"), where the next token should be \"height\"; weaker on a unit or dimension word like \"cm\" or \"feet\" just before \"high\" or \"wide\".","s":[0.804022,0.749603,0.781501,0.777607,0.684202,0.220634,0.245673,0.317485,0.229104,0.427935,0.036586,0.00638,0.007659,0.034804,0.012588]},{"p":"L27N5081+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"The infinitive marker \" to\" right after an indefinite quantity or object noun (something/anything/plenty/little/nothing/a bite/a snack/things/enough/lots) that sets up the verb the model expects next, especially \"eat\", \"do\", \"munch on\" or \"see\" (\"a bite to eat\", \"something to do\", \"plenty to see and do\").","s":[0.846464,0.599618,0.758459,0.332302,0.538277,0.119574,0.030809,0.071333,0.015865,0.028625,0.006712,0.033718,0.026092,0.028387,0.012505]},{"p":"L27N5081-","l":"TWO-REL","j":"11","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires (negatively) on the function word right before a verb of feeling or coping in self-help, wellness, and emotional-support text: the \"to\" in \"okay to / permission to / how to\", \"myself\" or \"yourself\" after \"let\", or \"best\" before a verb, and it also fires on odd mid-word pieces in spammy casino pages.","s":[0.67819,0.766602,0.103641,0.384604,0.184663,0.014734,0.030248,0.013283,0.036637,0.022283,0.00931,0.053118,0.025118,0.029823,0.010921]},{"p":"L27N6487+","l":"UNREL","j":"00","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on a word-piece, number word or separator in the middle of a non-English (or scholarly-citation) passage, where the model must continue a half-finished foreign word, for example the \"š\" or \"pads\" in Latvian \"sešpadsmitais\", Scandinavian \"og\", \"tusen ni\", \"dage\", or the second half of a Swedish compound.","s":[1.009135,0.849342,0.509571,0.39425,1.11183,0.007457,0.013052,0.022413,0.021888,0.027446,0.003702,0.023938,0.011555,0.011492,0.01447],"u":{"0":[0.889601,0.518711,0.389772,0.508303,1.175337],"1":[0.067071,0.028783,0.041974,0.02052,0.028726]}},{"p":"L27N6487-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires strongly (negative pole) on the first letter or word piece of a word in a Scandinavian or Baltic language (Norwegian, Danish, Swedish, Latvian), such as a capital D at the start of a name or a mid-word piece in a Norwegian, Danish or Latvian name or phrase, mostly in web or reference text.","s":[0.338261,0.814333,0.065811,0.135326,0.277786,0.02175,0.006562,0.020209,0.006659,0.014345,0.018412,0.011374,0.006116,0.017631,0.009931]},{"p":"L27N6897+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on clause-boundary function words and punctuation (commas, periods, \"that\", \"whom\", \"or\", \"until\", \"However\", \"if\", \"but\") inside machine-paraphrased, SEO-spam style articles with awkward synonym swaps and pasted-in URLs, mostly about dating, mail-order brides, sugar babies, marriage advice, antivirus or data-room reviews, and it needs that spun-text genre to be strong.","s":[0.371768,0.684827,0.687093,0.591567,0.573371,0.006593,0.002953,0.082206,0.006657,0.004343,0.000708,0.002566,0.000927,0.005338,0.000966]},{"p":"L27N6897-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires mid-clause on short function words and verbs (especially \"these\", \"of\", \"need\", \"up\", \"ensure\", \"from\", \"read\" in \"read more\") inside low-quality, machine-spun SEO web articles with odd synonym swaps, as in tips lists on travel, photography, gaming or web design; fluent edited prose is unlikely to drive it.","s":[0.138276,0.24927,0.118756,0.292702,0.144109,0.01243,0.020653,0.013128,0.015381,0.021273,0.02001,0.016427,0.021122,0.010252,0.018754]},{"p":"L28N2019+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires inside a journalist's byline or bio line at the end of a news article or column, on the word in the author-credit formula (\"is a San Francisco Chronicle staff writer\", \"is a former Boston Globe columnist\", \"Reporting by\", \"Editing by\", \"was also the\", \"as deputy editor\") where the next token is a job title, publication name or reporter's name.","s":[0.369376,0.261656,0.477339,0.729569,0.855925,0.020423,0.032301,0.061187,0.026574,0.011423,0.024459,0.03422,0.04882,0.024654,0.021994]},{"p":"L28N2019-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires (negatively) on tokens in text that is Portuguese or Philippine/Brazilian-flavored, especially Portuguese-language code (SQL schemas, C#, JS, Python with Portuguese identifiers and comments), at positions where the model is mid-word or mid-phrase and the next token is a continuation fragment (e.g. 'At' in adicionaAtributo, 'ang' in Sangguniang, a newline in a column list).","s":[0.423468,0.817627,0.371845,0.865716,0.329256,0.137701,0.047452,0.037138,0.076611,0.02669,0.015031,0.017102,0.025219,0.009962,0.015244]},{"p":"L28N2301+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on the token just before a political party or institution name is expected, mostly the opening parenthesis after a politician's name where a party affiliation like (S&D) or (PPE) comes next, and on 'the', 'and', 'of' or 'newly created' where a party or institution name follows, in French/European politics text.","s":[0.364739,0.128552,0.454406,0.328029,0.155021,0.11892,0.033524,0.064518,0.004287,0.036866,0.01252,0.006871,0.010983,0.012474,0.021507]},{"p":"L28N2301-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires negatively at the token right before a proper place name, foreign name or rare entity is about to be spelled out: after 'at', 'of', 'town of', 'cities of', 'Rare', a comma in a list of places, or a newline inside a list of place names. It is strongest in encyclopedic, travel, geographic and Finnish-flavored web text, and also appears at digits inside numbers or IPs.","s":[0.234366,0.438989,0.246208,0.428378,0.056629,0.008806,0.016219,0.0387,0.007662,0.009697,0.007345,0.014622,0.029038,0.019769,0.018783]},{"p":"L28N2702+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on the subject or connecting token (a noun like plane, jetliner or kid, a pronoun, a comma, \"and\" or \"then\") in narrative about a crash, collision or fall, where a verb of impact, crashing or falling is about to come next, e.g. \"the plane hit\", \"lost control and crashed\", \"missed a curve and fell\".","s":[0.600849,0.669811,0.515145,0.591224,0.497396,0.280751,0.286463,0.011592,0.380865,0.24227,0.048126,0.00401,0.147393,0.120077,0.01287]},{"p":"L28N2702-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires (negative pole) on mid-word or mid-phrase tokens inside proper names, numbers, addresses and technical terms in web prose, where the next piece is a continuation: apostrophe before s, digit within a number, name fragments like Kiy/Say/Tats, and 'national security' or 'solder'.","s":[0.242285,0.007805,0.008264,0.101789,0.649161,0.012242,0.008341,0.005919,0.03812,0.011519,0.043499,0.076023,0.0158,0.049861,0.012542]},{"p":"L28N576+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on a token at a syntactic seam inside long, mostly code or formal prose, where the next token is a predictable continuation of a fixed pattern (method-chain dot after a closing paren, comment star, subject pronoun or 'to' or 'will' after a clause, or a comma in a list), most strongly in JavaScript, C# or Python source files.","s":[0.046009,0.071579,0.037781,0.085006,0.077041,0.104833,0.0346,-0.002153,0.01908,0.012489,0.073622,0.012764,0.018398,0.027615,0.030017]},{"p":"L28N576-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires (negatively) on the word right before a bare verb is expected, mostly the infinitive marker \"to\" (also \"be\", \"and\", \"-\" in \"re-\", \"'s\" in \"Let's\", and adverbs like \"constantly\"), in expository, SEO or academic web prose such as \"needs to\", \"should be\", \"the reason to\", \"re-evaluate\", \"Let's discuss\", where a base-form verb comes next.","s":[0.106363,0.150716,0.25186,0.135855,0.064837,0.00828,0.033677,0.015985,0.023209,0.017918,0.019426,0.019981,0.017299,0.007472,0.009761]},{"p":"L28N5924+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on the token right before the model expects to write \"India\" or \"Ind-\" (Indian, Indies, Indigenous), such as the comma after an Indian city or after \"Russia\" in the BRICS list, \"Republic of\", \"Reserve Bank of\", \"East\", \"(\" before IND, and \"American\" or \"Amer\" before \"Indian\".","s":[0.489809,0.573102,0.454972,0.058907,0.041784,0.011023,0.239488,0.169613,0.131438,0.041784,0.013994,0.008579,0.01719,0.010488,0.004211]},{"p":"L28N5924-","l":"TWO-REL","j":"11","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires at a point inside a code, locale, or language-list string where the model must predict a following piece that starts with \"Ind\"/\"Indi\" or similar: the hyphen in zh-CN/zh-HK style locale codes, the dot in domain names (ku.edu.sa), and the language names Roman/Croatian in a spam page's \"Albanian Basque Bulgarian Catalan Croatian ... Indonesian\" list.","s":[1.016142,0.18795,0.118059,1.090941,0.262378,0.014584,0.022967,0.01148,0.041584,0.009578,0.023557,0.01834,0.016712,0.013165,0.009237]},{"p":"L28N7525+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on tokens inside non-English text, mostly German but also Hungarian, Slovenian and Croatian, such as word suffixes (-ra, -ko), function words (und, zurück, Bitte), commas and periods, and word fragments split mid-word. It reads as a general 'we are in a continuing non-English passage' signal.","s":[0.393177,0.449257,0.652535,0.579408,0.464114,0.034441,0.024548,0.01617,0.040139,0.118952,0.011701,0.014025,0.031768,0.013506,0.017384]},{"p":"L28N7525-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on a word piece or token in the middle of a non-English compound or foreign-language word (mostly German, Hungarian or Czech, e.g. the \"fw\" in Panzerkampfwagen, \"ief\" in Spezialtiefbauer, \"Landschaft\" before -s-), and also on identifiers in Hungarian-named code, where the next token continues the word or phrase rather than starting a new one.","s":[0.961518,0.592242,0.492972,0.529181,0.330114,0.015752,0.018978,0.011252,0.029511,0.022357,0.013193,0.018256,0.009464,0.039095,0.023004]},{"p":"L28N8275+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on function words, punctuation or list markers (comma, \"or\", \"and\", \"to\", apostrophe-s, colon, bullet) inside discussion of plagiarism and copying (\"copying, paraphrasing\", \"copy or trace\", \"scraped content\", \"not copied\"), where the next word is expected to continue the copy-related phrase.","s":[0.674556,0.767007,0.838195,0.668952,0.773761,0.099715,0.008243,0.125475,0.109326,0.01082,0.015408,0.010532,0.01297,0.0048,0.05188]},{"p":"L28N8275-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on a word or word piece that sits in a fixed, memorized or already-seen phrase or name, where the next token is nearly certain: most often \" fingers\" right after a possessive and adjective (\"His soft fingers\"), plus \" Lights\" in \"The Night the Lights Went Out in Georgia\", \" of\" in \"Province of Canada\" or \"colonies of New Brunswick\", and mid-word pieces of rare names (\"Fing|ers|mith\", \"cec|id|ology\", \"Cl|annad\").","s":[0.239788,1.01872,0.85888,0.66108,0.554875,0.042476,0.021132,0.186631,0.103416,0.174969,0.029812,0.027269,0.031261,0.052303,0.092061]},{"p":"L28N949+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on almost any token (commas, function words, 'where', 'as', digits, apostrophes) inside prose about Marvel or comic-book superheroes (Avengers, Black Panther, Chadwick Boseman, Batman), where the next word could plausibly be 'super'/'superhero'; it is a topic-context neuron, not tied to one token.","s":[0.944541,0.408966,0.520855,0.275131,0.866835,0.012457,0.007775,0.00768,0.002876,0.151035,0.012688,0.005626,0.015246,0.011171,0.008817]},{"p":"L28N949-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on function words, punctuation and word pieces (of, in, and, the, /, commas) in superhero comic-book prose about Marvel or DC events and creators (Secret Wars, Hickman, Starlin, Thanos, Batman), where the next word is likely a comic title or character name that may start with \"Super\" or \"Ultra\".","s":[0.651566,0.805297,0.468035,0.508624,0.619654,0.008764,0.010409,0.004154,0.005421,0.010307,0.006856,0.005459,0.011372,0.004306,0.004933]},{"p":"L29N442+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on ordinary function words, line breaks and nouns such as \"The\", \"is\", \"deals\", \"community\" or \"programs\" inside long, formal institutional prose, above all UK Ofsted school-inspection reports and their parent-questionnaire tables, plus similar policy, planning, jobs-ad and tourism documents.","s":[0.552914,0.239767,0.463184,0.309864,0.240811,0.01424,0.048316,0.129582,0.055378,0.027325,0.044222,0.01431,0.025808,0.014266,0.03373]},{"p":"L29N442-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires negatively on the token inside a running list or coordinated phrase (\"or\", \"and\", a comma, \"all\", \"same\", a number's second digit, \"Re\" in a name, a closing parenthesis, an apostrophe) in plain informational web prose such as school inspection reports, venue listings and property descriptions, where the next word is a continuation of the phrase.","s":[0.255153,0.441903,0.138277,0.390227,0.394462,0.030231,0.188492,0.294253,0.033217,0.09809,0.023486,0.143883,0.006838,0.01546,0.04117]},{"p":"L29N4505+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on the word-piece \" im\", \" th\" or \" tr\" (a short word-initial fragment with a leading space) that appears in the middle of a misspelled, garbled or split word, where the intended word continues (\"im|agnine\", \"th|rought\", \"th|ough\"), especially in typo-ridden forum text, spam gibberish, and code comments.","s":[0.668494,0.561067,0.519443,0.500161,0.592828,0.006568,0.004949,0.003121,0.015642,0.042841,0.015513,0.012768,0.013703,0.026716,0.013774]},{"p":"L29N4505-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on a short word-start fragment (Im, Lim, St, Th, Cl, st, cl, tr, th) that begins a longer word, usually at the start of a sentence, bullet or heading after a paragraph break, as in Immerse, Limiting, Storing, Thaw, Clutter, stared, climb.","s":[0.468334,0.008028,0.48117,0.028874,0.013045,0.009452,0.008435,0.031768,0.024622,0.013045,0.008107,0.019078,0.020843,0.013334,0.019766]},{"p":"L29N4926+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on a token that begins or sits in the middle of a name, number or title within long, dense, document-style text (reference lists, dates like 1955, phone numbers, DOIs, proper names, multi-word titles), where the next token is a continuation chunk and not a common word.","s":[0.051339,0.170828,0.024383,0.036056,0.110805,0.038434,0.043126,0.023522,0.014331,0.047287,0.014971,0.026791,0.002922,0.02714,0.019506]},{"p":"L29N4926-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on tokens in the middle of a multi-token unit, where the next token is a highly predictable continuation (a hyphen inside a place name like Saint-Etienne, an apostrophe in a contraction, a repeated identifier in code, a number-version fragment, a space before a number); it is most common in code, citation lists and long news or encyclopedic prose.","s":[0.112798,0.042574,0.059459,0.03464,0.03401,0.114499,0.02243,0.019159,0.074296,0.028642,0.011084,0.039315,0.027354,0.037388,0.036102]},{"p":"L29N6173+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires in Sri Lankan documents (news, SQL dumps, blogs) on the token just before a Sri Lankan personal or place name, such as the next name piece after \"said\", \"by\", a title, \"Sri\", or an opening quote, so it predicts a rare, often Sinhala or Tamil, name.","s":[0.043245,0.13379,0.793916,0.365884,0.102708,0.007524,0.008402,0.013974,0.029766,0.014568,0.004836,0.011959,0.021061,0.009592,0.010022]},{"p":"L29N6173-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires (negatively) on tokens inside Sri Lankan-context text (Sri Lankan news, travel itineraries, Sinhala transliteration) where the model is mid-way through a Sri Lankan name, place, official title or date, such as digits of a year in a Sri Lankan news page, \"Ministry of\", \"Speaker\", or the middle pieces of names like Sajith, Dambulla, Sinhala words.","s":[0.644275,0.20007,0.538421,0.295559,0.503713,0.008348,0.013749,0.020499,0.011565,0.017077,0.016649,0.010875,0.000663,0.003924,0.016203]},{"p":"L29N6627+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires at mid-sequence positions in dense factual web prose where a name, number, or list item is incomplete and the next token must continue it, such as a split proper noun, a number, a space before a digit, or an apostrophe before s; it is not tied to one particular token string.","s":[0.0569,0.080632,0.039001,0.069149,0.021248,0.027057,0.01734,0.00377,0.026605,0.009955,0.024677,0.022485,0.021143,0.007968,0.010767]},{"p":"L29N6627-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires (negative pole) at mid-phrase tokens where a proper name, URL, compound or formula is only partly written and the next word is a highly predictable continuation, such as the 'fiddle' in js-fiddle.net, the '.' in a domain before 'com', the space before a route number, the hyphen in P-47, or the 'and' inside a fixed title like 'Child Support and Other Forms of Family Maintenance'.","s":[0.073365,0.320203,0.036476,0.105472,0.199707,0.006268,0.045589,0.036476,0.111868,0.052075,0.02846,0.01364,0.024012,0.033674,0.029796]},{"p":"L29N8196+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on a mid-word fragment inside a long word in Greek (polytonic Ancient Greek) or Ukrainian Cyrillic running text, where the current token is an incomplete piece and the model must continue the word with the next subword piece; it does not fire on whole words or on English text.","s":[0.274285,0.039477,0.763393,0.05902,0.08496,0.007517,0.009464,0.348462,0.020836,0.012713,0.004997,0.009223,0.006862,0.003319,0.008905]},{"p":"L29N8196-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on word pieces in the middle of a longer word, or on a word that is clearly unfinished, where the next token continues the same word, mostly in Ukrainian (Cyrillic) formal text. Examples are the \"рон\" in правоохоронного, \"керів\" before \"ництва\", and \"Націона\" before \"льна\". It is strongest in dense, repetitive Ukrainian academic prose about community police officers, and weaker in English, digit runs or code.","s":[0.624962,0.794179,0.689713,0.659636,0.672938,0.031468,0.500478,0.330337,0.029784,0.601182,0.036355,0.036206,0.030324,0.020945,0.046476]},{"p":"L29N8328+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on highly predictable, boilerplate-continuation tokens in repetitive or templated text: the \"You [may] obtain a copy of the License\" line of Apache license headers, repeated SQL INSERT/CREATE TABLE dumps (underscores, digits, newlines), and comma-run spam SEO keyword lists.","s":[0.439328,0.127445,0.041334,0.057505,0.341034,0.138879,0.041512,0.023983,0.026305,0.100081,0.030651,0.025765,0.0055,0.007028,0.028189]},{"p":"L29N8328-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires, mostly in long documents (SQL schemas, forms, reports, news), on the token just before a continuation that is copied from earlier in the same document, such as a repeated name, title, column line or phrase (\"Social\" before Specialist, \"Cape\" before Breton, \"PNM Resources\" before \"has\").","s":[0.027821,0.029567,0.666661,0.066811,0.039671,0.02614,0.018476,0.04469,0.049037,0.043171,0.033552,0.021323,0.031622,0.011653,0.022501]},{"p":"L29N8532+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on function tokens (list commas, \"and\", \"including\", \"supports\", \"are\", \"LED\", \"Fort\") in web prose or spec lists right where the next word is an expected item in a series or a feature or proper-name continuation, such as the next sport, feature, or place name.","s":[0.065227,0.198833,0.067122,0.14342,0.018407,0.008142,0.045856,0.049129,0.019449,0.02403,0.044456,0.036798,0.01151,0.134417,0.075324]},{"p":"L29N8532-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires (negative values) on a function word, hyphen, or word-start piece in the middle of a fixed phrase or formulaic document, where the next word is highly predictable. Examples: \"Energy and Commerce\", \"push-button start\", \"eligible for free school meals\", \"Most pupils are of White British heritage\", and repeated inspection-report boilerplate.","s":[0.794265,0.227306,0.135895,0.188356,0.091053,0.023651,0.045405,0.014723,0.021345,0.012885,0.064496,0.014215,0.03363,0.012007,-0.000432]},{"p":"L29N9853+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires mid-name or mid-phrase on a token in the middle of a multi-token proper name, organization name, citation author name, number or URL, where the next token is a continuation (e.g. 'Communications' in 'ANR Communication Services', 'Comm' in 'CommScope', 'Wireless' in 'ATC Nigeria Wireless Infrastructure', 'comb' in 'Newcomb', a digit in a year), especially in telecom, news, citation or reference text.","s":[0.851915,0.156858,0.144627,0.054518,0.90163,0.032988,0.033097,0.008955,0.007378,0.010864,0.05767,0.066702,0.053673,0.016096,0.035848]},{"p":"L29N9853-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on function tokens (apostrophes in don't/Telco's, commas, 'with', 'of', 'on', 'under', '@') in mobile-phone and telecom carrier text (Telstra, Optus, Vodafone, Orange, EE, Sky, Bigpond), where a carrier or network name is expected to come next.","s":[0.38673,0.782816,0.808111,0.513437,0.586099,0.007938,0.186144,0.012103,0.013955,0.019909,0.012515,0.009158,0.00742,0.0132,0.018335]},{"p":"L30N10175+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on the token right where the next word is likely \"slowly\", \"gradually\" or \"over time\": after a verb phrase of incremental change (increase the amount, ease into, wean off, ramp up), or on the comma or \"but\" after a sentence-initial \"Slowly\" or \"slowly\".","s":[0.640778,0.886031,0.849802,0.17567,0.862663,0.0111,0.006507,0.026752,0.011574,0.006896,0.013225,0.006659,0.002332,0.025626,0.005376]},{"p":"L30N10175-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on a verb, participle or adverb-slot token that could be followed by a manner adverb like 'slowly' or 'gradually' (e.g. 'cannot be cooked', 'does not load', 'site works', 'not started', 'unable to speak', 'go'), so the neuron suppresses 'slowly/gradually' as the next word.","s":[0.307765,0.109452,0.080616,0.048781,0.060129,0.023555,0.015493,0.004908,0.000894,0.006248,0.004517,0.008766,0.02743,0.001416,0.003725]},{"p":"L30N1199+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on the token right after a Punjabi/Sikh/Indian surname or name (Kaur, Singh, Sidhu, Cheema, Sodhi, Kalra) in a list, citation or news item, mostly on the comma in 'Surname, Given name' entries, where the model expects the next South Asian given name or initial.","s":[1.194052,0.480789,1.018893,0.673016,0.724897,0.019259,0.017259,0.029074,0.258684,0.013457,0.041492,0.014318,0.009095,0.007576,0.004385]},{"p":"L30N1199-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on a token inside a highly repetitive or boilerplate-like passage (legal rule lists, church service timetable tables, privacy or legal notices, repeated quoted prayers) where the model is confident that the next token is a fixed, predictable continuation, e.g. the period in 'a.m.', 'the' before a stock noun, or 'or' in a stock phrase.","s":[0.878204,0.268843,0.050978,0.148156,0.418468,0.075194,0.020904,0.023019,0.063617,0.028403,0.017447,0.101085,0.104564,0.177194,0.167787]},{"p":"L30N3331+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires in legal, court and news-politics prose on a noun (or nearby word) naming a formal decision or legal instrument, such as ruling, decision, verdict, agreement, arrangement, decree, judgment, or bill, right after \"the/a\" and often a modifier, when the text is about to say what that decision does or was.","s":[0.594941,0.356684,0.017344,0.175801,0.074853,0.373381,0.178846,0.015832,0.025806,0.147287,0.007641,0.005903,0.016565,0.009509,0.016641]},{"p":"L30N3331-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires (negatively) on the token right after \"The\"/\"City\"/\"President\" etc. in news, legal or government prose where the next word names or refers back to an institution, agency or official actor already introduced (e.g. \"The commission\", \"City Council\", \"The FDA\", \"President Trump\", \"Council of the City of Toronto\"), as the model predicts a verb or the continuation of that institutional subject.","s":[0.598772,0.554346,0.420516,0.593946,0.651171,0.018723,0.022176,0.020222,0.008655,0.017729,0.034402,0.040974,0.02418,0.032582,0.029803]},{"p":"L30N3725+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on a token in the middle of a multi-token name, phrase or identifier, where a predictable continuation must come next (e.g. 'travel insurance', 'North Atlantic Ocean', 'E3', 'Minister of', 'Treaty of', 'all_in_edges_of_node', '100%'), so the model is copying or completing a repeated or very stereotyped fixed expression in long web text or code.","s":[0.01652,0.199874,0.078648,0.037836,0.091458,0.034931,0.199874,0.076331,0.012645,0.027204,0.010474,0.008331,0.018962,0.006106,0.049961]},{"p":"L30N3725-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires negatively on mid-phrase tokens in long English expository, news or web prose where the next word is a highly predictable, ordinary continuation, such as an apostrophe before s, a preposition, a possessive, or a separator inside a name, date or address; it does not depend on topic and needs a long preceding document.","s":[0.017892,0.024807,0.040496,0.0383,0.018527,0.036111,0.051993,0.023842,0.041848,0.052819,0.079869,0.025852,0.013014,0.024944,0.070813]},{"p":"L30N3907+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on a word or digit inside French text (or French fragments embedded in English or code documents), where the next token is a French continuation: function words such as pour, de, la, te, peut, Vous, or the middle of a French name or date number.","s":[0.314081,0.454007,0.242086,0.57352,0.334322,0.035735,0.020412,0.019335,0.028404,0.015147,-0.003257,0.018763,0.009691,0.010839,0.043261]},{"p":"L30N3907-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires negatively on a function word, mid-phrase piece or digit inside a long English or French document, where the next token is a predictable continuation word (e.g. 'is' before 'a PNAS Direct Submission', 'je' before a verb, 'the' in a list, the '0' in '20_0_0'), and it also fires on boilerplate such as legal text, citation lists and footers.","s":[0.358147,0.388763,0.026569,0.030512,0.097916,0.052041,0.023533,0.053869,0.029455,0.302913,0.02735,0.010966,0.016407,0.017494,0.021277]},{"p":"L30N5206+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on a standalone capital (or lowercase) \"H\" token that begins a new word, acronym or name after a line break, bullet, space or opening quote and is followed by a continuation piece, as in HMD, HCG, HMI, HRC, H2O, Haptic, Haze, Homido, Hole.","s":[0.558598,0.350446,0.093655,0.549075,0.616553,0.090012,0.013175,0.012782,0.031124,0.042833,0.014841,0.013035,0.016988,0.02632,0.008395]},{"p":"L30N5206-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on the letter \"H\"/\"h\" (or \"ph\") when it stands as a separate token opening a proper name or word whose rest follows (Homs, Hangu, Hamburg, Huelva, hertz, Hong, Hule), so the next token is a continuation like \"amburg\" or \"ertz\" and not a whole word starting with H.","s":[0.483673,0.819792,0.060386,0.469634,0.662918,0.098198,0.0203,0.00745,0.0701,0.112762,0.014195,0.011691,0.013147,0.010826,0.010873]},{"p":"L30N7117+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"It fires mostly in code, on the separator or punctuation token (a dot, underscore, open paren, comparison sign or apostrophe) just before a plural collection-of-parts identifier that has already recurred nearby, such as network.|Layers, tileSet.|Columns, ds_|sections or append(|sections; it does not depend on the separator character itself.","s":[0.305035,0.183004,0.390607,0.589198,0.390768,0.058503,0.081482,0.120136,0.013476,0.022017,0.036901,0.01068,0.03573,0.014024,0.009633]},{"p":"L30N7117-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on the second member of a coordinated pair or list joined by \"and\" (\"executive and legislative\", \"east and Gulf\", \"second and third\", \"sell and buy\") when the next word is expected to be a plural division noun such as branches, rows, coasts, circuits, fields, sectors, classes or industries.","s":[0.591704,0.592456,0.455258,0.838482,0.121768,0.54688,0.142849,0.021901,0.017821,0.102775,0.074796,0.013984,0.012919,0.061608,0.002191]},{"p":"L30N7998+","l":"TWO-REL","j":"11","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on a word in running English prose where the next word could plausibly be \"since\" or a duration phrase: usually the auxiliary \"has/have/having\" after a subject (often a bio sentence like \"Raj has 12 years of...\" or \"There have been...\"), or a noun/participle like \"posted\", \"dating\", \"days\", \"data\" that precedes a time span (\"dating back to\", \"for five days\").","s":[0.407694,0.598077,0.459469,0.630356,0.242153,0.242313,0.123532,0.37689,0.049963,0.111149,0.03397,0.011971,0.021558,0.002891,0.020523]},{"p":"L30N7998-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on the token just before the word \"since\" (or \"ever since\"/\"years since\"), where the model predicts \"since\" next: the end of a clause expecting a time anchor, such as \"ever\" in \"ever since\", or the noun/period word in \"a few days since\", \"company X services since 1969\", \"the biggest total since 2013\".","s":[0.391304,0.114424,0.45878,0.117633,0.108175,0.391304,0.114424,0.093384,0.117633,0.154502,0.006939,0.007847,0.047337,0.004678,0.013645]},{"p":"L30N863+","l":"TWO-REL","j":"11","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires in agriculture, land, soil and environmental-science texts on a token that comes just before a likely land/plot noun, such as a modifier or preposition before 'land', 'soil' or 'farm'. Examples: 'on', 'from', 'working', or 'a deserted/barren/reclaimed' before 'lot' or 'land'. It also fires on word pieces inside author surnames in reference lists, such as 'Hug' before 'elius'.","s":[0.435378,0.553273,0.617281,0.527477,0.23458,0.018664,0.02566,0.016077,0.015871,0.038607,0.038182,0.031933,0.045683,0.013717,0.039221]},{"p":"L30N863-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on the token right before a word like lot, parcel, plot, tract or land is expected, in land-use, zoning, subdivision, property-sale or farm-acreage text; typical positions are \" of\" after \"acres\" or \"area\", \" on\" after \"development\", \" as\" after \"described\", list commas and \"or\" in \"lots, parcels, plots\", and a line break inside a table of acreage.","s":[0.420355,0.429474,0.421541,0.544858,0.392512,0.015575,0.02426,0.031485,0.016197,0.015135,0.025621,0.084531,0.013176,0.069046,0.073493]},{"p":"L31N3077+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on the first word or hyphen of a compound, modifier or multiword name, such as 'best' in 'best-in-class', 'well' + '-', 'Wild' West or 'Environmental' Protection, where a noun or hyphenated continuation must follow; it does not fire when the phrase is complete.","s":[0.083987,0.285459,0.2471,0.034357,0.056336,0.013161,0.011798,0.059165,0.018193,0.031668,0.009076,0.004342,0.020531,0.010928,0.005669]},{"p":"L31N3077-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires negatively on a word in the middle of a noun phrase, right after a determiner or preposition and before the noun it modifies (e.g. \"make reasonable efforts\", \"gave her a grateful smile\", \"by diligent study\"); the token is an adjective or modifier whose noun is still to come, mostly in literary, historical, or legal prose.","s":[0.046138,0.419141,0.239041,0.193612,0.025289,0.055022,0.079607,0.025556,0.014602,0.00765,0.012212,0.018662,0.027131,0.01133,0.010411]},{"p":"L31N3299+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on the word \"not\" right after an auxiliary verb (does/did/do/will not) in machine-translated or SEO-style product-review and promotional prose, where the next word is a plain verb like affect, take, break or be; it also fires on a few other tokens (the \"D\" in \"3D\", job-title words) in a few top contexts.","s":[0.082538,0.073195,0.153473,0.049169,0.038943,0.02616,0.048724,0.096539,0.049955,0.00788,0.020738,0.009798,0.007258,0.001355,0.007699]},{"p":"L31N3299-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires (negatively) on ordinary English words in the middle of a noun phrase or clause, where the next word is highly predictable from a repeated or formulaic phrase, such as \"a\" before \"job\", \"social\" before \"networks\", \"drug therapy\", \"hypovitamin|osis\" or \"community police officer\". It appears in long web prose, forum comments and medical or SEO text, and it fires on that mid-phrase word rather than on punctuation or sentence ends.","s":[0.079021,0.07099,0.034377,0.05239,0.159017,0.009878,0.090237,0.022981,0.027058,0.073659,0.01551,0.015454,0.01485,0.022292,0.009796]},{"p":"L31N6209+","l":"TWO-REL","j":"11","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires in food, recipe, menu and cooking text on the word \"chicken\" (often right where a dish name or ingredient is being named, as in \"chicken soup\" or \"chicken tikka\"), and more weakly on list separators and numbers inside food lists, where the next word is another food or dish item.","s":[0.065632,0.496788,0.647232,0.120522,0.535051,0.047458,0.167855,0.493967,0.092341,0.215355,0.013733,0.017979,0.014013,0.009096,0.005953]},{"p":"L31N6209-","l":"TWO-REL","j":"11","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires (negatively) on a modifier or ingredient word in the middle of a food or ingredient noun phrase, such as \"tortilla\" before chips/strips or \"Polish\" before sausage, where a food head noun (sausage, chips, crumbs, leaf) is expected next; it also fires on mid-phrase words in technical names such as \"Knowledge-Based Systems\".","s":[0.860417,0.084542,0.187839,0.152747,0.821821,0.156123,0.018427,0.013898,0.16255,0.006949,0.007145,0.062062,0.030022,0.005656,0.01352]},{"p":"L31N7089+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on ordinary tokens (code punctuation such as \"(\", \"_\", \"new\", \"document\", apostrophes, spaces, digits) in text saturated with the word monitor/monitoring (monitor-related code, patents, tech or health prose), where \"monitor\" could plausibly come next; it is a context-dependent effect that is hard to reproduce in a short text.","s":[0.373217,0.241502,0.232383,0.358862,0.210586,0.008805,-0.000132,0.007074,2e-06,-0.00014,-0.000562,-0.000456,9e-06,0.00134,-0.000725]},{"p":"L31N7089-","l":"UNK","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"The pole fires, weakly almost everywhere and most strongly at points where the next word could plausibly be a form of \"monitor\" (e.g. \"will continue to actively\" in a health-surveillance report, \"you can stop\" before \"observing\", \"track progress and\"), especially in texts about monitoring, tracking or surveillance and in code with Monitor identifiers.","s":[0.198105,0.19316,0.160249,0.191176,0.238945,0.198105,0.218416,0.210698,0.235599,0.223051,0.180899,0.131544,0.203962,0.162716,0.123922]},{"p":"L31N7248+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on the token just before an expected \"miss/missing\" (usually \"miss out\" or \"missing out\"), especially in phrases like \"never miss an issue\", \"fear of missing out\", \"avoid missing out\", \"narrowly missing\" and \"could mean missing\"; it is a next-word prediction for the word \"missing\" or \"miss\".","s":[0.648082,0.385166,0.548443,0.347039,0.317074,0.117742,0.066391,0.038843,0.336628,0.525774,0.005605,0.008386,0.020087,0.080615,0.003566]},{"p":"L31N7248-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on ordinary mid-sentence tokens (apostrophes in \"I'm\" or \"can't\", \"any\", \"for\", list commas) inside texts about loss, namely legal disclaimers that list loss, damage and failures, and grief forum posts about a lost parent, where words like \"miss\", \"lost\" or \"missing\" could come next.","s":[0.306495,0.265887,0.044342,0.653308,0.392244,0.047593,0.120215,0.004419,0.183453,0.008558,0.014444,0.014455,0.038002,0.013641,0.019021]},{"p":"L31N8076+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on boilerplate web text, mostly the comma or word right after \"T/T\" in lists of payment terms (T/T, L/C, Western Union, PayPal), and on words inside legal, HR or job-posting boilerplate (\"Inquiries regarding\", \"request an accommodation\", \"send Business a notice\") and spam pharmacy drug-name strings, where the next token is a predictable formulaic continuation.","s":[0.844562,0.828757,0.513981,0.453432,0.311053,0.296591,0.31226,0.016019,0.029086,0.022095,0.029504,0.016778,0.014832,0.014425,0.01607]},{"p":"L31N8076-","l":"ONE","j":"00","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires (negatively) on function words or modifiers inside light/exposure phrases like \"full sun\", \"in full sun\", \"in light shade\", \"dried in the sun\", \"shot in bright sunlight\", typically in plant-care, gardening, or product-spec text, where the next word is predicted to be a sun/shade/light noun.","s":[0.703066,0.872604,0.770499,0.68501,0.574153,0.339185,0.717584,0.734638,0.539048,0.104573,0.020018,0.018486,0.015986,0.021149,0.014323]},{"p":"L31N8157+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on a word in running text where the document has already repeated the same words, names or phrases verbatim and the model is confidently copying them, such as a memorized legal caution, a named party like PTI or MQM, or a repeated 'The prime minister said' construction, and so is also confident about the next token.","s":[0.705735,0.053121,0.021679,0.84699,0.0592,0.09969,0.020631,0.054687,0.030253,0.007841,0.080462,0.028192,0.024335,0.036289,0.047301]},{"p":"L31N8157-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on a mid-phrase word in long formulaic boilerplate (legal terms, forward-looking-statement disclaimers, permit findings, press-release 'About' blurbs, citations), where the next word is a highly predictable set phrase continuation; it is a token-in-memorized-template effect and mostly needs a long preceding document.","s":[0.194466,0.046587,0.034843,0.177001,0.03116,0.031293,0.017926,0.040131,0.037824,0.03091,0.027933,0.02693,0.033962,0.012308,0.014732]},{"p":"L31N8180+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on function words, hyphens and word pieces mid-phrase in dense academic or theoretical prose (political theory, theology, sociology, Marxism, philosophy, math textbook), where a noun ending in -ism, -ic, or -logy is expected next; it fires at the point just before or inside such a abstract-concept term, such as the hyphen in post-/pre-, 'a', 'their', 'the' or 'economic'.","s":[0.338855,0.463911,0.339019,0.677099,0.678689,0.020115,0.006319,0.013861,0.015947,0.016567,0.023688,0.027304,0.025983,0.033989,0.02371]},{"p":"L31N8180-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on the word \"think\" (also thinking, look, more, understood) in academic or scholarly prose, mostly right after \"to\" in a phrase like \"to think about / think critically / think through\", where the next word is likely an adverb or \"about\" or \"of\".","s":[0.654414,0.674739,0.787621,0.116278,0.640126,0.075606,0.260277,0.105532,0.067565,0.007018,0.012434,6.9e-05,0.017728,0.002911,0.007781]},{"p":"L31N9849+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on the first piece of a person's first name (often a short fragment like De, Da, Jay, Ke, O, Day, Kay) when that name is split into several tokens and the rest of the name is still to come; it is strongest for American athletes' names (football, recruiting lists, fantasy rankings, box scores) such as DeAndre, DeVonta, Deebo, DaJuan, Jaylen.","s":[0.528768,0.77351,0.383159,0.596683,0.648751,0.026476,0.308617,0.087935,0.005103,0.010529,0.006005,0.007006,0.015074,0.007835,0.00922]},{"p":"L31N9849-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on the first piece of a person's first name that begins with a K or C sound (Caleb, Kinsley, Kameron, Kellen, Cammie, Caden, Jakobi), typically a less common or modern name, when it starts a new name in a list of people such as an obituary, honor roll, roster or sports recap.","s":[1.04569,1.060812,0.835013,0.935868,0.664656,0.042925,0.03717,0.362721,0.075223,0.008852,0.01266,0.038085,0.005016,0.015732,0.010193]},{"p":"L32N1152+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on a function or modifier word (\"other\", \"be\", \"are\", \"is\", \"has\", \"been\") sitting right after a subject or conjunction in formal legal, regulatory or corporate boilerplate, where the next word is an institutional or legal term such as law, officer, agent, authority or entitled; strongest in lawyer-conduct rules (RPC) and statutes.","s":[0.635152,0.145436,0.24736,0.397876,0.214222,0.02537,0.019084,0.014307,0.020245,0.018045,0.011612,0.011586,0.021533,0.013012,0.022966]},{"p":"L32N1152-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires negatively on a word-initial fragment or short function word that begins or continues a multi-token word or name and whose next token is a continuation (e.g. \"se\", \"nous\", \"explo\", \"Sea\", \"aff\", \"ex\", \"Se\"), typically in web boilerplate, corporate or listing text, often French or mixed English/French.","s":[0.202893,0.153261,0.09447,0.455639,0.148232,0.021857,0.004291,0.006492,0.01714,0.006017,0.010523,0.014484,0.000216,0.016223,0.005711]},{"p":"L32N1796+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on a word or word-piece that sits mid-phrase in English prose, where the next token is a continuation such as a suffix, a noun complement or the rest of a name: first pieces of split names or words (Bh|argava, Nak|uru, navig|anti, Barg|aining), and nouns or verbs like work, research, service or war right before 'of', 'in' or 'on'.","s":[0.286347,0.138064,0.198167,0.20473,0.281118,0.028879,0.041904,0.043016,0.059781,0.130767,0.030971,0.04556,0.044336,0.023436,0.012815]},{"p":"L32N1796-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires (negatively) on a descriptive adjective or the first half of a hyphenated compound adjective (warm-, fresh-, fine-, profit-) in a list or phrase describing people, where the next token is a further trait, a hyphen continuation, or a noun like 'men', 'natures' or 'peoples'.","s":[0.485871,0.123353,0.272369,0.176357,0.194363,0.049253,0.018043,0.012914,0.013517,0.009883,0.022634,0.024623,0.007708,0.007637,0.011914]},{"p":"L32N3+","l":"UNK","j":"00","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on a mid-clause token (often a noun, adjective, article or determiner like \" securities\", \" that\", \" the\", \" legal\") inside long, formulaic boilerplate (legal or regulatory text, forward-looking-statement disclaimers, job-posting bullet lists, spam essay text, reference lists), where the next word is a highly predictable continuation of a stock phrase; it also fires on a digit inside a long ID or URL.","s":[0.114121,0.143994,0.20331,0.123989,0.232277,0.0434,0.07875,0.18717,0.039621,0.092244,0.055306,0.009985,0.087897,0.052943,0.047654]},{"p":"L32N3-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on a word or punctuation mark inside a list or series of abstract nouns, usually just after a conjunction or comma (\"and\", \"or\", \",\") or at a list start, in boilerplate business prose such as legal disclaimers, job postings, market reports and SEO spam, where the next word is another item of the series or a noun that continues the phrase.","s":[0.334865,0.17452,0.162887,0.095457,0.27837,0.204848,-0.023823,0.028855,0.049209,0.029907,0.055583,0.040088,0.046639,0.029033,0.028289]},{"p":"L32N3034+","l":"UNK","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"A broad, low-specificity pole that fires on mid-sentence connective tokens in ordinary prose (commas before a clause, \"and\", \"even\", \"then\", \"idea\", \"notion\") and on name-fragment word pieces, where the model is about to continue a clause; the only visible output effect is a weak push toward \"bond\" words, which almost none of the contexts actually predict.","s":[0.322326,0.477364,0.453317,0.496538,0.579722,0.158983,0.249052,0.093721,0.282057,0.299119,0.432913,0.342004,0.370471,0.269305,0.240885]},{"p":"L32N3034-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on the token right before the word \"bond\" (or \"bonds\") when the context makes \"bond\" the highly predictable next word, e.g. \"father-son\", \"human-animal\", \"strengthen the\", \"a unique\", \"no set\", \"NEISD\" before \"Bond Program\"; it is a next-token prediction of \"bond\" in both emotional-attachment and finance or chemistry senses.","s":[0.114078,0.838219,0.643492,0.835037,0.304316,0.090809,0.496039,0.144919,0.831697,0.279662,-2.2e-05,-0.000376,-0.000506,0.000501,-0.000658]},{"p":"L32N4165+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on a digit (or a date separator or opening parenthesis) in the middle of a number, date or ID that has just started, in years, long IDs, dates, version numbers and reference lists, where the model is predicting that the next token is another digit, especially \"1\".","s":[0.138762,0.08782,0.128457,0.076038,0.074704,0.000982,0.00615,0.002008,0.010349,0.021212,0.002081,0.002874,0.007569,0.007356,0.003364]},{"p":"L32N4165-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on a digit token in the middle of a number, usually the first digit after a break (such as after a colon, dot, hyphen, or space) or the next digit inside a digit-by-digit number, and it predicts that the next digit will be \"1\" or another digit; it is strongest in timestamps, IDs, dates, ranges, and figures like 12, 10, 4.","s":[0.012306,0.013383,0.015661,0.009966,0.024496,0.012427,0.013407,0.018128,0.004056,0.023276,0.023789,0.005533,0.00068,0.004329,0.017523]},{"p":"L32N4451+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on a word or word piece in running prose that sits inside a clause where the next token is likely a participle, adjective or predicate completion (be, am, are, is, almost, utterly, finally, regarded, lost, killed). It is strongest in old, formal, quoted or OCR-garbled text (legal opinions, 19th-century prose, newspaper OCR, theosophical or religious writing), and it cannot be tied to one token or topic.","s":[0.098695,0.267406,0.247556,0.321009,0.204397,0.010327,0.011618,0.012516,0.005189,0.010063,-0.000239,0.003369,0.005581,-0.001718,-0.002108]},{"p":"L32N4451-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires negatively on a word fragment in the middle of a word whose spelling is corrupted by bad OCR (old scanned newspapers and books with garbled letters like tho, wero, nnd, tiie), at the odd split piece such as 'l', 'cl', 'sold', 'Int', 'ld' or 'penit' where a correct word is broken into pieces, with the rest of the word still to come.","s":[0.23399,0.448866,0.493584,0.396387,0.495653,0.01627,0.300113,0.035265,0.042043,0.076025,0.00291,0.000904,0.0041,0.00411,0.005642]},{"p":"L32N5928+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on the capital letter \"H\" as a standalone token that begins a person's surname (the next piece is usually \"anes\", \"ession\", \"oriuchi\", \"aglind\", \"enser\", etc.), especially in lists of names such as bylines, credits, donor lists, bibliographies and bios.","s":[0.499431,0.202625,0.468785,0.572591,0.2845,0.494522,0.202625,0.025625,0.068233,0.013896,0.013659,0.015689,0.031675,0.026273,0.002658]},{"p":"L32N5928-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on function words, list commas and modifiers (\"of\", \"and\", \"or\", \"like\", \"as\", \"an\", a comma inside a list, an adjective such as \"insulation\" or \"marine\") that sit mid-phrase in long expository, technical or catalogue prose and where a noun phrase or the next list item must come next; it is not tied to one topic or word.","s":[0.421969,0.216507,0.176688,0.225192,0.267668,0.029654,0.040514,0.057357,0.021891,0.000361,0.047765,0.044011,0.030807,0.030991,0.037689]},{"p":"L32N7621+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on tokens in a long document where the model is confidently continuing a passage it has effectively seen before (repeated or memorized boilerplate, reference lists, census stats, code or scientific methods text), so the next token is highly predictable from earlier context; it cannot be reproduced in a short standalone text.","s":[0.03773,0.029771,0.036166,0.034253,0.044892,0.011008,0.007018,0.015497,0.009738,0.137174,0.030916,0.020434,0.009255,0.012516,0.065353]},{"p":"L32N7621-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on ordinary function or descriptive words (articles, \"and\", \"its\", \"original\", \"including\", \"in\", commas) inside spec-style sale listings, classifieds and forum posts about cars, engines, property or equipment, where the next word is likely a concrete part or feature noun.","s":[0.400859,0.616371,0.421653,0.212237,0.75516,0.032154,0.024797,0.053411,0.03348,0.034973,0.019821,0.052374,0.037267,0.040456,0.052352]},{"p":"L32N9465+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on the word (often 'the', 'see', 'is', 'file') or on the spot just before where the text is about to say 'attached' or 'attachment', as in 'please see the [attached] file', 'a map is [attached] as Appendix', or 'as an email [attachment]'. It is strongest in freelance job posts, forum posts and formal letters or reports that refer to an enclosed document.","s":[0.749586,0.660103,0.95547,0.666299,0.677441,0.749586,0.660103,0.063954,0.1704,0.206035,-0.000148,1e-06,-0.000972,-0.000219,0.000169]},{"p":"L32N9465-","l":"UNK","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"A broad, nearly always-on negative pole that fires on structural tokens (line-start indentation, line breaks, the closing > of XML doc-comment tags, digits in counting runs, commas after a mention of \"attached\") inside highly repetitive templated text such as C# code, freelancer bid listings, numbered report outlines and 1-2-3 sequences, and it suppresses \"attach/attached/attachment\" as the next word.","s":[0.349002,0.332802,0.32273,0.22729,0.300904,0.282048,0.122742,0.148828,0.34223,0.30629,0.280909,0.293216,0.385714,0.254463,0.194439]},{"p":"L33N10031+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on a token that sits at the first piece of a capitalized brand or organization name (Inter-, Bio-, P-, Met-, Pro-, Digi-) or on an ordinary word in the middle of a phrase or list where the next token is a continuation fragment; it is strongest in web prose, press-release boilerplate, citation footers and spam-scrambled CBD text.","s":[0.211998,0.313587,0.219766,0.380652,0.431617,0.007157,0.007275,0.049239,0.042036,0.009558,0.003098,0.008631,0.002158,0.006036,0.01451]},{"p":"L33N10031-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires (negatively) on a very common function or connector token in the middle of a phrase, where the next word is predictable and fairly generic: a possessive or linking word like 'own', 'de', 'and', 'to', 'our', '的', or a URL '://'. It is strongest in scraped web boilerplate, citation lists, and Chinese or code text, and its main effect is predicting a mundane continuation rather than a topic.","s":[0.055966,0.011903,0.102418,0.005416,0.022411,0.013218,0.010441,0.015215,0.00292,0.008793,0.01161,0.011332,0.020229,0.020439,0.006835]},{"p":"L33N10047+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on a function word (mostly \"the\", also \"and\" or \"to\") in the middle of a long, formulaic web sentence, especially SEO or boilerplate prose such as supplier pages, salary pages and market reports, right where a list or parallel item continues (\"skills, the number of years\", \"address the needs\", \"deliver the promised features\").","s":[0.100106,0.026595,0.01331,0.017471,0.018734,0.006073,0.008542,0.002878,0.006202,0.00465,0.035892,0.006792,0.002176,0.004379,0.009229]},{"p":"L33N10047-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires (negatively) on a standalone capital \"I\" token that is the start of a C#/.NET interface or type name (IPEndPoint, IServiceCollection, IComparer, IDataReader) right after \"new\", \"private\", \"readonly\", \"static\", \"public\" or \":\" in C# source code.","s":[0.657325,0.449291,0.00224,0.82556,0.713869,4.6e-05,0.001432,0.002095,0.000776,0.030625,0.001508,0.004149,0.002042,0.003337,0.003531]},{"p":"L33N3168+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on a hyphen \"-\" that joins pieces of a compound word, number or name in running text (e-mail, non-reactive, 1-Source, 4-year, low-quality, two-three-two, hash-tags, IP-address), where the next token must continue the compound rather than start a new line.","s":[0.162326,0.131371,0.188634,0.215374,0.173409,0.012291,0.013729,0.013858,0.011618,0.012065,0.014271,0.008003,0.012155,0.009479,0.008395]},{"p":"L33N3168-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on a token that sits mid-text where the next token is certain to continue on the same line, never a line break: most often the first byte of a split non-ASCII character (a non-breaking space or thin space) between a number and its unit or symbol (\"0.91 m\", \"± 1.3\", \"P <\", \"n =\"), the \"infty\" piece in LaTeX \\mathrm{\\infty}, or the \"al\" slot after \"et\" in citations like \"Ule et al.\", in scientific, encyclopedic or web prose.","s":[0.072633,0.19744,0.075231,0.01911,0.076639,0.026507,0.018077,0.061132,0.011261,0.020068,0.012639,0.008326,0.00699,0.006538,0.010999]},{"p":"L33N36+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on the third digit of a four-digit year in a dateline, mainly the '2' in '20' of 2020 (or the '9' in '199') right after a weekday, month and day, such as a blog post date, file header or timestamp, where the final digit is about to be predicted; it fires much more weakly on scattered mid-word tokens.","s":[0.011693,0.865757,0.514493,0.004256,0.006962,0.005719,0.000564,0.006248,0.004366,0.007351,0.007293,0.0136,0.002609,0.026585,0.018971]},{"p":"L33N36-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires (negatively) on a short function word or conjunction (by, or, and, if, for, the, be, a, to, at) in the middle of a sentence, right after a verb or noun that opens a coordinated list or a subordinate clause, so that a verb, participle or noun phrase should come next. Spam-style pages with scrambled keywords are the strongest, but plain prose also fires it.","s":[0.027525,0.003318,0.011032,0.033885,0.025897,0.027525,0.037389,0.012474,0.007577,0.008141,0.008219,0.007801,0.011577,0.003645,0.011067]},{"p":"L33N4341+","l":"UNREL","j":"00","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on a token (often a hyphen, underscore, or word piece) where the next word is very likely \"index\"/\"indexed\"/\"indexing\", as in over-indexed, dup_val_on_index, PlayerIndex, FilterIndex, ColumnIndex, \"Read Detailed Index of\", and also on the last language name before \"Lithuanian\" in scraped language-menu spam.","s":[0.294634,0.38826,0.03306,0.030488,-0.000401,0.066591,0.004366,0.016002,0.146705,0.053162,-0.000498,0.010548,-0.003364,-0.003484,-3e-06],"u":{"0":[0.099579,0.028204,0.20765,0.201932,0.092852],"1":[0.007695,-0.000781,0.032195,0.023462,0.013657]}},{"p":"L33N4341-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"In source code, it fires on the token just before a variable named `index` (or `indexInRowsArray`) is about to be written, e.g. the `[` in `array[`, the `(` in `Left(`, or an `=`/`+=`/` +` after a name, where the next token is predicted to be `index`.","s":[0.20896,0.040238,0.263841,0.593441,0.480699,0.100856,0.040238,0.134561,0.025911,0.202837,0.015486,0.009062,0.015105,0.014931,0.011408]},{"p":"L33N482+","l":"TWO-REL","j":"11","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on a token that is the first piece of a word or name the model must finish with a specific rare continuation, mostly after a partial or memorized phrase: the \"S\" of Slesvig, the \"Mar\" of Marfa, the \"F\" of FRC, or a boilerplate word such as \"a\" in \"operated by a business\" or \"the\" in \"without the consent\".","s":[0.251372,0.279291,0.057801,0.000619,0.003674,0.000262,0.004041,-0.0,0.000619,0.003674,0.006012,0.002326,0.00579,0.000119,0.002381]},{"p":"L33N482-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on a function word or word fragment in the middle of text the model has seen verbatim many times, such as stock boilerplate (Ofsted glossary lines, Gutenberg or GitHub bot footers, licence lines), famous speeches, LeetCode-style problem statements, and the standard JPEG base64 header, where the next token is nearly certain from memory.","s":[0.01345,0.013449,0.014446,0.337875,0.044136,0.004764,0.001605,0.006551,0.00093,0.015273,0.000963,0.007483,0.003415,0.00179,0.011266]},{"p":"L33N6706+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on the article \"a\" (rarely the following adjective or a comma) in English web prose, when a singular noun phrase is about to begin after a verb or preposition and the likely next word is an adjective such as \"brief\", \"small\", \"diverse\", \"quick\", \"full\" or \"personal\".","s":[0.05871,0.066442,0.145523,0.040341,0.107531,0.010169,0.007667,0.002812,0.005495,0.027779,0.009795,0.006736,0.006743,0.013979,0.012769]},{"p":"L33N6706-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on the indefinite article \"a\" (also \"A\" or an adjective right after it) in running English prose, when a noun phrase naming a quality, object or feature is about to start, such as \"has a low impact\", \"have a wonderful family\" or \"offers a range of\".","s":[0.003801,0.042209,0.010107,0.022878,0.118581,0.012469,0.006515,0.006491,0.010245,0.005841,0.007945,0.003556,0.013861,0.005049,0.011966]},{"p":"L33N8636+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on a token in the middle of a multi-piece unit, such as a proper name, transliteration, phone number, year or syllabified pronunciation, where the model is partway through a word or name and expects a continuation piece next (e.g. the hyphen in 800-775-8750, the '1' in '2015', 'Kenny' before Lam, 'G' of Ghalib).","s":[0.05648,0.078329,0.081897,0.048795,0.111776,0.067109,0.058618,0.061952,0.049638,0.063304,0.062857,0.049533,0.060025,0.051321,0.033166]},{"p":"L33N8636-","l":"TWO-REL","j":"11","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires (negatively) on word pieces and function tokens inside running non-English text, mainly vocalized Arabic Quran verse text (clitics like مِن, case endings, diacritics) and Somali prose, typically after an English passage on friendship in the Quran or in spam-like web text.","s":[0.404785,0.472615,0.623932,0.70914,0.37001,-0.00051,-0.001465,-0.000935,0.000512,-0.003297,-0.000815,-0.000787,-0.003306,-0.000276,-0.000713]}],"check":{"stops":[373,338,294]}},{"key":"O7","name":"OLMo-3 7B","neurons":[{"p":"L00N181+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the token right after a tech/media/web noun such as website, media, application, project, email, file, news, book, home, editor, api, Java or photo, mainly when that next token is an unusual word or the start of a compound (e.g. \"website necessitates\", \"project inception\", \"Java getter\", \"Book Trailers\", \"photoresponsive\", \"Website Extras\").","s":[0.575943,0.429705,0.577427,0.42837,0.710809,0.409353,0.011992,0.048454,0.043821,0.043923,0.028873,0.023452,0.101125,0.01205,0.065447]},{"p":"L00N181-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"A layer-0 token detector that fires on a specific set of word tokens, mostly names of languages, regions and religions (Hindi, Tamil, Arabic, Anglican, Malaysia, UK, Hiragana, evangelism) plus 'ransom' or 'ransomware', 'rainbow' and 'Telegram', strongest when the word trails another word in a keyword-stuffed SEO title such as 'online casino Uk', 'betting Malaysia', 'News Hindi' or 'Bitcoin ransom'.","s":[0.514341,0.501493,0.573876,0.65451,0.376798,0.024004,0.043006,0.038247,0.085687,0.2032,0.011848,0.024359,0.018733,0.064771,0.061459]},{"p":"L00N1929+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the first sub-word piece of a word that comes right after a hyphen with no space, when that word is split into several tokens: \"Organ\" in Industrial-Organizational, Metal-Organic or Well-Organized, \"posit\" in False-positives, \"sem\" in black-semibold, or the first byte of a Chinese, Korean or Thai character after a dash. The same word written whole, or with a space after the hyphen, should not fire it.","s":[0.741887,0.626192,0.679193,0.528705,0.013057,0.092313,0.016671,0.029149,0.03017,0.010764,0.018762,0.022219,0.029135,0.015067,0.010732]},{"p":"L00N1929-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the \"™\" token that ends the mojibake sequence \"вЂ™\" (a UTF-8 curly apostrophe or right quote wrongly decoded as Cyrillic Windows-1251), as in \"IвЂ™m\", \"didnвЂ™t\" or \"SarahвЂ™s\", and weakly on rare odd symbol tokens such as \"#!/\" in a shebang or a run of star symbols.","s":[0.766656,0.888748,0.914093,0.923641,0.910163,0.04251,0.010391,0.061059,0.05808,0.065573,0.170102,0.01199,0.022826,0.031682,0.063833]},{"p":"L00N2968+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a verb token such as Send, Receive, Populate, Write or unsubscribe when it begins a CamelCase method, variable or class name in code, mostly C# or JavaScript networking and UI code (e.g. SendData, SendBytes, ReceiveBuffer, PopulateIndividual, Populate(), WriteRatio, unsubscribeRequest), rather than the same verb used as an ordinary word in prose.","s":[0.146438,0.613925,0.310896,0.045268,0.350722,0.018181,0.034821,0.036601,0.041755,0.025586,0.038569,0.059907,0.031084,0.033446,0.022172]},{"p":"L00N2968-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the token \" hashed\" (lowercase, with a leading space), especially in code where it is a variable name or the first piece of an identifier such as hashedPassword, hashedKey, hashed_password or hashedData, though it also fires on plain \"hashed data\" or \"hashed password\" in code comments; weaker offshoots are \" tang\" (tangents) and \"news\" as an identifier piece.","s":[0.041704,0.545503,0.583182,0.114693,0.437878,0.041509,0.048978,0.076865,0.072054,0.161999,0.026736,0.021534,0.00955,0.016773,0.054515]},{"p":"L00N3387+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"A token-level detector (layer 0) for a handful of word-start pieces: mainly 'altern' when it begins 'alternated' or 'alternately', the word 'variant'/'variants' (also 'Variants'), and similar verb stems split before a suffix such as 'equ' in 'equated', 'Interrupt' in 'Interrupted'/'Interrupting' and 'Dig' in 'Digging'; the meaning of the surrounding text does not matter.","s":[0.07752,0.085252,0.134591,0.190765,0.229342,0.076451,0.079586,0.043102,0.051979,0.045591,0.083698,0.038753,0.042777,0.069655,0.036607]},{"p":"L00N3387-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the first fragment of a word that the tokenizer splits into pieces, such as \"cond\" in condone or condense, \"Paren\" in Parenthood or Parenthetically, \"exh\" in exhale, \"catal\" in catalyzers, \"Hann\" in Hannibal or \"LOCK\" in LOCKED, and fires most strongly right after a first-person subject like \"I\", \"I'm\" or \"we're\"; \"Bottom\" in \"Bottom line:\" is a similar trigger.","s":[0.260726,0.545031,0.072024,0.141094,0.405111,0.020135,0.065347,0.054154,0.017412,0.016531,0.010968,0.025393,0.048805,0.030003,0.154176]},{"p":"L00N5623+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"In source code (C#, JavaScript, Python), fires on particular word-piece tokens that begin or continue an identifier or string-literal name, such as \" Pag\" in PaginatedList, \" natur\" in naturals, \" discipl\" in disciplina/disciplinaBD, \"entarios\" in Comentarios, \"uffman\" in HuffmanTree, \" minut\" in a variable named minut, \"Solution\" in SumSolution, \"Venue\" in VenueName and \" Arial\" in ctx.font = \"20px Arial\"; the same words in ordinary prose fire little.","s":[0.482177,0.264486,0.310219,0.182775,0.388162,0.006493,0.020264,0.089945,0.027596,0.027818,0.003604,0.0076,0.011699,0.006484,0.001719]},{"p":"L00N5623-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Token-level detector, mostly in code: it fires on a space-prefixed run of repeated punctuation, chiefly the separate tokens \" !!!\" and \" !!\" (and \" ???\"), as in alert('There was an error !!!'), console.log('List is empty !!') or return !!value, and it also fires on a few code-identifier tokens such as \"ReSharper\" in a \"// ReSharper disable\" comment.","s":[0.108555,0.158531,0.295145,0.196393,0.198084,0.018913,0.016206,0.015682,0.013364,0.025505,0.020559,0.017876,0.013017,0.005306,0.021757]},{"p":"L00N7006+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word right after the preposition \"in\" (less often after at/on/to/your), most strongly when that word is a proper noun or a specialized term such as a country or nationality (in Belgium, in Belgian beer, in Portugal, in Lebanon), a place name (in Allied Gardens, in Elmwood Park), or a technical word, often only its first piece (in neutrino physics, in refugee camps, in conditional probability, in pelvic floor, in mergers).","s":[0.29589,0.572047,0.456577,0.43028,0.386662,0.054583,0.036285,0.058116,0.044896,0.088191,0.060484,0.030436,0.037392,0.048871,0.034837]},{"p":"L00N7006-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Layer-0 token detector that fires on the first piece of certain place names or rare words when it comes right after \"in\" or \"this\", most strongly on \"Moz\" in \"in Mozambique\", \"Baton\" in \"in Baton Rouge\", \"Fairfax\" in \"in Fairfax\", and also \"brav\" in \"in bravado\", \"dri\" in \"in dribs and drabs\", and \"usercontent\" in a googleusercontent URL.","s":[0.637756,0.764948,0.736961,0.692294,0.999267,0.244542,0.207048,0.200445,0.010464,0.044843,0.077117,0.030528,0.0287,0.058285,0.046166]},{"p":"L00N828+","l":"TWO-REL","j":"11","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the code token \"require\" (including the merged \"=require\") when it is the function call in a JavaScript/Node.js module import such as const x = require('express'), and similarly on \"Destroy\" in Unity C# calls such as Destroy(gameObject); this is a token-level detector for these import/destroy calls in code.","s":[0.422733,0.62503,0.374459,0.497038,0.306339,0.213375,0.065682,0.031238,0.146979,0.032046,0.003357,0.014487,0.020055,0.006883,0.010177]},{"p":"L00N828-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"In source code, fires on the identifier token right after an import / using / namespace / const / = keyword, typically the first piece of a library or module name such as getopt, pprint, curses, Swal, MLP, Mongo, WEB (e.g. \"import getopt\", \"using MongoCluster.Messages;\", \"namespace WEBLayer.Tests\", \"from sklearn.neural_network import MLPClassifier\"), not on ordinary English prose words.","s":[0.441236,0.230586,0.379943,0.533803,0.494708,0.031198,0.008214,0.042198,0.021856,0.017222,0.040289,0.04342,0.007272,0.006007,0.008294]},{"p":"L00N8952+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"A layer-0 bigram-style detector: it fires on a capitalized word, often the first piece of a rare proper name, that directly follows a generic web/software interface word such as Project, Code, View, See, Check, Help, Article, Document, Extensions or email, forming a heading, menu item or name (e.g. \"Project Athenia\", \"Code Cavalry\", \"View Francoise's posts\", \"Check LaTeX dependencies\", \"Article Attachments\", and the Indonesian blog footer \"Alamat email Anda tidak akan dipublikasikan\").","s":[0.452973,0.435171,0.274881,0.181478,0.219058,0.029774,0.137306,0.025218,0.037241,0.011284,0.014711,0.01156,0.057236,0.024466,0.022409]},{"p":"L00N8952-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"A layer-0 token detector that fires on a small set of specific tokens, mostly the second word of a capitalized heading or label: \" ESL\" (as in \"Advanced ESL\"), \" Downloads\" (as in \"Version Downloads\"), \" Hunts\" as the start of Huntsman or Huntsville, the Roman numeral \" XVI\" (as in \"Article XVI\"), \"_custom\" inside identifiers or URLs, and \" FAQs\" or \" Biography\" (as in \"Support FAQs\", \"Author Biography\").","s":[0.408052,0.065151,0.101645,0.36205,0.15411,0.004592,0.065151,0.066466,0.013782,0.013393,0.005507,0.100125,0.091984,0.015292,0.079831]},{"p":"L00N9356+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word or word-piece that comes right after a number, especially a counted noun or adjective in a phrase like \"1482 freshmen\", \"330 wrestlers\", \"10 Remarkable Women\", \"14 petite desserts\", \"2005 Odyssey\" or \"F-150 Pickup\" (a layer-0 token-after-digits detector, strongest when the following word is somewhat uncommon).","s":[0.648227,0.658956,0.508486,0.50235,0.821429,0.153671,0.12226,0.028344,0.034287,0.018612,0.023176,0.017982,0.027463,0.024072,0.014666]},{"p":"L00N9356-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the first fragment of a rare, multi-token word, usually a capitalized proper name such as a street, person or month abbreviation, or on the first byte of a unit symbol like ℃ or Ω, when it comes immediately after a number, as in street addresses (\"13 Hawthorne Road\", \"1668 Barrington St\"), numbered or ranked list entries (\"7 Jos Buttler\", \"31 Inspiring ...\") and temperature or ohm specs (\"25℃\").","s":[0.62286,0.885682,0.735169,0.51004,0.444882,0.005142,0.000946,0.009442,0.030347,0.058525,0.004151,0.062891,0.003389,0.033543,0.022118]},{"p":"L01N10284+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the opening word-piece of a brand, company, product or surname that the tokenizer splits into several pieces, so the name must continue (Burb|erry, Zap|ier, Coy|wolf, Jeff Vander|Meer, P|icas|a, Pdf|filler, POR|SCHE), plus the identifier ReactDOM in a React import line and Saudi just before Arabia; ordinary whole words and the later pieces of the name do not fire.","s":[0.205872,0.16841,0.679594,0.474176,0.125624,0.014558,0.030592,0.023412,0.126893,0.037459,0.013459,0.015705,0.013243,0.044305,0.020601]},{"p":"L01N10284-","l":"UNREL","j":"00","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the token \"useEffect\" inside a JavaScript React import line such as import React, { useState, useEffect } from \"react\", and also on a one-word emphatic agreement or sincerity adverb opening a reply sentence, chiefly \"Exactly\" (also \"Honestly\" and \"Truly\"), especially in interview or podcast transcripts.","s":[0.783993,0.462423,0.358413,0.73552,0.725173,0.200797,0.061739,0.022945,0.047975,0.041626,0.018319,0.10234,0.019848,0.039727,0.084482],"u":{"0":[0.829129,0.462423,0.723555,0.785118,0.91664],"1":[0.288908,0.514589,0.089223,0.429634,0.302326]}},{"p":"L01N3050+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the first piece of a longer, often rare or technical word that the tokenizer splits mid-word, where the rest of the word is predictable (e.g. \"substant\" before \"ia nigra\", \"hemat\" before \"ite\", \"Appropri\" before \"ateness\", \"irrit\" before \"ability\", \"Compet\" before \"encies\", \"neur…itin\"), plus Arabic/Persian letters split into partial bytes; whole common words do not fire it.","s":[0.471822,0.412395,0.147838,0.182642,0.051046,0.023706,0.04604,0.022811,0.035194,0.04552,0.000421,0.01579,0.01052,0.024022,0.007614]},{"p":"L01N3050-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the second word-piece of a word that the tokenizer splits after a short opening piece, especially one starting with 'D'/'d', 'dis' or 'mis'. Typical cases are 'umont' in Dumont, 'ECT'/'ECTOR' in all-caps DETECTOR, 'imin' in diminishing, 'approval' in disapproval, 'ionate' in dispassionate and 'gments' in misjudgments. It does not fire on the first piece or on short common words that are a single token.","s":[0.056855,0.480608,0.433016,0.654046,0.736133,0.048406,0.096741,0.031206,0.002482,0.017458,0.029451,0.158711,0.029895,0.007902,0.036533]},{"p":"L01N369+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"In celebrity-gossip and entertainment-news prose, fires on the key tabloid words for a public figure's personal drama or social-media activity, above all the stem \"apolog\" in apologised/apologized/apology, and also \"Instagram\", \"controversy\", \"husband\", \"breakup\", \"trolls\", \"feud\"-style words.","s":[0.75629,0.58926,0.823636,0.75853,0.903933,0.042858,0.340189,0.200516,0.013038,0.29585,0.037209,0.045504,0.038989,0.010975,0.006509]},{"p":"L01N369-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the first sub-word fragment of an uncommon, multi-token vocabulary word in news or review prose, such as the opening piece of corrosive, inexorable, languid/languishing, gargantuan, envisaged, punctuality/punctuated, rejuvenated, consummately, stoic, or the '-automatic' in semi-automatic, typically right after 'the' or 'a' in adjective position. Short common words written whole do not fire it.","s":[0.728339,0.91457,0.557689,0.391448,0.49327,0.086674,0.06329,0.089896,0.073919,0.031122,0.016554,0.037385,0.007108,0.058033,0.029525]},{"p":"L01N4337+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the first token of the word \"urea\" (the \" ure\" piece) in chemistry, biology or industrial text, especially where urea appears in a list of small molecules or compounds such as \"water, glycerol and urea\" or \"urea formaldehyde\". It fires more weakly on a few other chemical word-starts like \"vinyl\" or \"vit(reous)\".","s":[0.670096,0.787157,0.125307,0.696657,0.062189,0.156937,0.268885,0.125474,0.196371,0.065205,0.108947,0.035438,0.013525,0.075455,0.016027]},{"p":"L01N4337-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on words meaning origin, birth or coming into being: \"origin/originate(d/s)\", \"birth\" (incl. birth control, birthright), \"source\", \"beginning\" and the word piece \"Produ\" of \"Producing/Produced\", most strongly on \"Origin\" in titles like \"Origin of Species\" or verbs like \"originates from\".","s":[0.004055,0.078219,0.028471,0.039364,0.115217,0.004089,0.082512,0.019406,0.038555,0.016749,0.037066,0.012148,0.008372,0.027634,0.004679]},{"p":"L01N6100+","l":"TWO-REL","j":"11","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word \"Chief\"/\"chief\" used as the first word of an executive or official title (Chief Scientist, chief strategist, Chief Marketing Officer, NASA Chief), and also on the \"/sites\" path piece in forbes.com URLs.","s":[0.214571,0.187733,0.569621,0.268334,0.854639,0.035961,0.063081,0.050246,0.093187,0.019546,0.014896,0.071102,0.055171,0.047863,0.045892]},{"p":"L01N6100-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the continuation piece of a multi-part name or code identifier, the token right after a known first part, especially in code imports and calls (the \".Popen\" in subprocess.Popen, \"-cookie\" in 'js-cookie', \"tensorflow\" in require('@tensorflow/tfjs'), \"_markers\" in google_markers), and also on an unusual surname right after a first name in author or staff credits (\"Steven Canter\", \"Andy Babbitz\").","s":[0.459424,0.497878,0.477521,0.289244,1.087147,0.041698,0.045356,0.02532,0.012001,0.03459,0.020313,0.024684,0.015552,0.01178,0.01242]},{"p":"L01N7380+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the first word of a fixed multi-word name whose continuation is predictable, mainly in rocket-launch and severe-weather news: \"Falcon\" before \"9\" or \"Heavy\" in SpaceX stories (strongest), \"Tropical\" before \"Storm\" or \"Cyclone\", \"Corpus\" before \"Christi\", and \"warning\" in \"hurricane warning\" or \"high wind warning\".","s":[0.448994,0.532024,0.788678,0.241396,0.702428,0.050569,0.124985,0.097036,0.169542,0.097834,0.014961,0.014992,0.031852,0.017735,0.085582]},{"p":"L01N7380-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"In news or science prose about weather, natural disasters, climate and earth science (earthquakes, storms, floods, drought, mining, Mars geology), this pole fires on a key proper name or the first piece of a split domain word, for example 'Richter' in 'Richter scale', 'Gale' in 'gale warning' or 'Gale Crater', 'seam' in 'coal seam gas', 'dri' in 'driest year', a storm name like 'Hurricane Donna' or 'Storm Desmond', or 'Hiroshima'.","s":[0.876274,0.744376,0.867695,0.986503,0.985197,0.202314,0.053633,0.120026,0.086632,0.056663,0.033518,0.002638,0.125349,0.020318,0.222904]},{"p":"L01N8419+","l":"UNREL","j":"00","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires inside code and markup on two kinds of structural token: a very long run of dashes, hashes or equals signs that forms a separator or banner line in a code comment, SQL script or ASCII table (for example \"// ---------------------------------------- Game Over -----\"), and the attribute \" xmlns\" or \" xmlns:\" in an XML or XHTML opening tag (for example <html xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\">).","s":[0.370777,0.696279,0.396319,0.755605,0.1772,0.029821,0.267059,0.05744,0.017416,0.008971,0.007326,0.068121,0.047663,0.051833,0.040858],"u":{"0":[0.049256,0.319116,0.141172,0.057478,0.108083],"1":[0.75236,0.809017,0.696279,0.911112,0.64522]}},{"p":"L01N8419-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on long, multi-syllable words that are a single token or a single token piece, mostly Latinate abstract words and adverbs (\"Congressional\", \"exponentially\", \"profoundly\", \"domestically\", \"INTERRUPTION\", \"imperialism\", \"anthropology\", \"comparatively\", \"concurrently\") and long code identifiers such as \"ArgumentNullException\" or \"InstanceState\"; short everyday words do not fire it.","s":[0.198441,0.121601,0.278455,0.110941,0.103612,0.04812,0.012017,0.036817,0.008364,0.016854,0.027394,0.04051,0.012874,0.017791,0.014825]},{"p":"L01N8975+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word \"Library\" or \"Libraries\" used as part of an institution's proper name right after a capitalized modifier (American Library, British Library, National Library of Scotland, County Library, University of Alabama Libraries, Boston Public Library), and also on \"library\"/\"libraries\" pieces inside URLs; generic lowercase \"the library\" and other words fire far less.","s":[0.639282,0.81826,0.902834,0.739582,0.6591,0.069569,0.460757,0.038046,0.16827,0.332139,0.036959,0.017542,0.012101,0.025437,0.017035]},{"p":"L01N8975-","l":"TWO-REL","j":"11","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the label or segment that opens a new field in machine-like structured records: line-initial field labels such as \"Reference:\" or \"Country:\" in job-ad and press-release contact blocks, metadata keys such as \"dc.date.accessioned\", and the path or domain piece right after a slash or dot in URLs and file paths (\"/modules\", \"/tmp\", \"www.scatter...\").","s":[0.463964,0.55834,0.594363,0.166756,0.45508,0.128904,0.054956,0.00647,0.055704,0.056269,0.10427,0.023657,0.014888,0.029146,0.039832]},{"p":"L01N9435+","l":"TWO-REL","j":"11","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word piece \" Nou\" in \"Art Nouveau\" (the token right after \"Art\", at the start of \"Nouveau\") in any text mentioning the style; a weaker offshoot fires on the first piece of a less common word right after the article \"a\" (a stipend, a stipulation, a specified manner, a Nash equilibrium, a simplex).","s":[0.638871,0.672559,0.605194,0.758704,0.693326,0.039154,0.170556,0.147883,0.025826,0.071148,0.011746,0.009209,0.031117,0.018322,0.005912]},{"p":"L01N9435-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word-start piece \"griev\" that begins \"grievance\" or \"grievous\", most strongly right after the article \"a\" (\"file a grievance\", \"a grievance committee\", \"a grievous blow\"), and more weakly on a few other words or word pieces placed right after \"a/an\" (\"an exe file\", \"a vicious circle\", \"a bravura\").","s":[0.547428,0.783645,0.470769,0.569965,0.283121,0.094432,0.026328,0.00393,0.017869,0.085718,0.016424,0.035001,0.023269,0.045295,0.082475]},{"p":"L02N1367+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the second word-piece of a rare, usually foreign surname or place name that the tokenizer splits after a short capitalized opening fragment, especially names beginning with P, Po, Mo or Nap (the 'oca' in Cluj-Napoca, the 'ää' in Svante Pääbo, the 'etz' in Poetzl, the 'erer' in Moerer, the 'Ba' in MoBa); common whole-word names like Paris or Moscow do not fire it.","s":[0.806511,0.5295,0.66127,0.709287,0.694999,0.04761,0.019509,0.031587,0.159092,0.030151,0.01806,0.078115,0.022263,0.013763,0.033084]},{"p":"L02N1367-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the second word-piece of a compound or rare word, right after a short opening piece, such as Calculator in a CamelCase identifier like AreaCalculator or MileageCalculator, VD in PVD, QRS in OQRS, Maur in the surname Maurer, wd in a split 'powder', or amu in Osamu; whole common words do not fire it.","s":[0.651855,0.639371,0.67643,0.572114,0.034058,0.026349,0.015072,0.047029,0.050608,0.021694,0.036873,0.02239,0.055492,0.121635,0.056367]},{"p":"L02N4436+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word-piece \"room\" right after a separate \"B\"/\"b\" token, i.e. the second piece of words and names spelled B-room (broom, Broom, Broomfield, Broomberg, broomstick, Broomall) and occasionally the similar B-raham (Braham); it does not fire on ordinary \"room\" or other words.","s":[0.203034,0.716622,1.017534,0.112919,0.787696,0.007779,0.010507,0.041847,0.02737,0.068878,0.031603,0.063532,0.040276,0.018736,0.074721]},{"p":"L02N4436-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word-piece \"atter\" right after \"fl\", i.e. inside the words flatter, flattered, flatterers and flatteringly, in both senses (to praise insincerely, and \"more flat\"). It also fires weakly on similar mid-word pieces such as \"wend\" in the name Mwendwa, but not on unrelated words like matter, latter or flat.","s":[0.883637,0.787,0.873093,0.551678,0.45219,0.009672,0.00897,0.031292,0.028834,0.030399,0.010826,0.033245,0.018401,0.010526,0.05222]},{"p":"L02N4649+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the HTML boilerplate of a hidden 1×1 tracking pixel, i.e. <img height=\"1\" width=\"1\" style=\"display:none\" src=\"https://www.facebook.com/tr?id=...\"> (or px.ads.linkedin.com), peaking on the \":none\" of display:none and on the opening \"<img\" of such tags.","s":[0.515365,0.457563,0.515365,0.682144,0.408569,0.028741,0.331982,0.052327,0.103099,0.049028,0.110586,0.070169,0.07039,0.021898,0.024664]},{"p":"L02N4649-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the second word-piece of a word split after a short capitalized fragment, most strongly the piece after \"Rou\" in \"Roulette\" when it starts a line or heading and in the name \"Rouhani\", and also on pieces of foreign or political proper names such as Mao, Likud and Manziel; it does not fire on lowercase \"roulette\" or on ordinary words.","s":[0.77995,1.021759,0.114165,0.536431,0.897322,0.061373,0.199628,0.097824,0.042919,0.039805,0.069633,0.098832,0.085562,0.053886,0.0831]},{"p":"L02N5291+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the surname token \"Lawson\" when it follows a first name or initial in news, sports or citation text (e.g. \"Nathan Lawson\", \"Greg Lawson\", \"Atherton A, Lawson M\"); weaker offshoots fire on a few other mid-word or compound pieces like \"selectors\" (cricket), \"LOCATION_SERVICE\" and \"modular\".","s":[0.800571,0.757285,0.546315,0.719264,0.62559,0.024838,0.176315,0.034218,0.148738,0.028842,0.103907,0.116006,0.081201,0.061447,0.0625]},{"p":"L02N5291-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the head word of a public-sector, government or urban-planning role or field name right after its modifier, most strongly \"planner\" in \"Senior Planner\", \"urban planner\" or \"Assistant Planner\", and also \"Administration\" in \"Public Administration\", \"Sector\" in \"public sector\", \"Works\" in \"Public Works\" and \"Civil\" in \"Civil Engineering\".","s":[0.393848,0.735712,0.365968,0.805191,0.46154,0.064342,0.058711,0.006228,0.098196,0.051462,0.01926,0.013384,0.01169,0.074166,0.052431]},{"p":"L02N5785+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the negative ending of \"could\" written without a proper apostrophe, i.e. the \"nt\" in \"couldnt\" (apostrophe dropped) or a mangled \"?t\"/\"'t\" right after \"could\", meaning \"could not\" in a contraction; other words like \"didnt\"/\"cant\" and a correctly typed \"couldn't\" fire much less.","s":[0.736106,0.672057,0.9808,0.564674,0.621765,0.328012,0.007521,0.444859,0.120034,0.124067,0.021804,0.061655,0.003831,0.01691,0.016268]},{"p":"L02N5785-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word piece right after a leading \"n\"/\"N\" at the start of a multi-piece word, overwhelmingly \"itty\" in the idiom \"nitty-gritty\" / \"Nitty Gritty\" (getting into the nitty-gritty details), and more weakly on similar second pieces of N-initial names like Natarajan, Nouri, Nwelue, Nortriptyline.","s":[1.083283,0.993581,0.998302,0.839343,0.868454,0.028732,0.017508,0.008475,0.019334,0.030632,0.06752,0.067622,0.020097,0.030996,0.045926]},{"p":"L02N58+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on League of Legends / MOBA esports jargon tokens, chiefly \"jung\" in \"jungler\"/\"jungle\" (as a role or map area) and the league acronym \"LCS\", plus related terms like turret, nexus, laning and ADC, when the surrounding text is about LoL gameplay or pro matches.","s":[0.590203,0.669253,0.563022,0.935953,0.099632,0.006299,0.129207,0.087059,0.146458,0.029862,0.054585,0.017199,0.026179,0.008234,0.021345]},{"p":"L02N58-","l":"TWO-REL","j":"11","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word-piece \"Rig\" (capitalized, often with a leading space) when it begins a longer name or word such as Rigby, Riggs, Rigondeaux, Rigpa, Rigsdagen, Rigorous or Rigidity, and more weakly on other mid-word pieces right after a capital letter in surnames (J-eph-son, J-ún-ior, K-ipl-ing), especially in author or citation lists.","s":[0.197767,0.644671,0.56613,0.292543,0.888339,0.11258,0.013755,0.006843,0.015011,0.035553,0.025139,0.020934,0.009812,0.067517,0.003921]},{"p":"L02N7124+","l":"TWO-REL","j":"11","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the token \" BeautifulSoup\" in Python scraping code, especially in the import line \"from bs4 import BeautifulSoup\". It also fires weakly on the word piece that follows a capitalized \"An\" in rare names (Anohni, Anansi, Anatolian).","s":[0.767866,0.791027,0.607417,0.778163,0.862275,0.074777,0.110355,0.175446,0.018069,0.10534,0.037891,0.042151,0.074613,0.004708,0.035434]},{"p":"L02N7124-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the second word piece of a rare capitalized proper name that the tokenizer splits right after its first capital letter, mostly a lone \"B\" (\"ain\" in Bain, \"inx\" in Binx, \"otton\" in de Botton, \"inh\" in Binh, \"ucht\" in Buchtal, \"umbo\" in Bumbo), sometimes \"A\" (\"asca\" in Athabasca, \"raq\" in Araq); common names and words that are a single token do not fire.","s":[0.84834,0.808805,0.78408,0.626555,0.762351,0.015908,0.114592,0.02718,0.022767,0.226911,0.012554,0.012627,0.015599,0.090436,0.000101]},{"p":"L02N7380+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the opening subword fragment of a long, uncommon Latinate word split across tokens, such as appell-(ants), disb-(anded), procrast-(ination), griev-(ance), jugg-(ler), omnip-(otence), benef-(action), abst-(aining), conc-(urred) or Gren-(adier), and on a few formal whole words like junior, appellant, official and contiguous, mostly in legal, historical or 19th-century formal prose.","s":[0.155478,0.5932,0.17573,0.143457,0.617001,0.033488,0.028513,0.012181,0.029652,0.183457,0.030783,0.011483,0.061289,0.025825,0.009703]},{"p":"L02N7380-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"In old-fashioned 18th–19th-century English prose (letters, histories, sermons, period newspapers), fires on the first word-piece of a longer formal or Latinate word, such as precip- in precipice/precipitately, necess- in necessaries, rendez- in rendezvous or Laure- in Laurence, and on stately words like manners, especial, specially and viz.","s":[0.355404,0.745284,0.717629,0.642501,0.430319,0.006322,0.035037,0.009977,0.026744,0.020148,0.022812,0.094059,0.11913,0.053915,0.027152]},{"p":"L02N91+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the second token of a familiar multi-token name or fixed term, the piece that the first piece strongly predicts: most of all the middle piece \"akk\" in the Kerala place name Palakkad, then \"unic\" in Punic (Second Punic War), \"Hundred\" in titles beginning \"One Hundred\" (One Hundred Years of Solitude), and medical compounds such as \"blood-brain\", \"blood dyscrasias\" and \"pulmonary embolism\".","s":[0.853758,0.696121,0.83596,0.607044,0.710691,0.118225,0.032636,0.051846,0.194031,0.127843,0.088463,0.143245,0.035897,0.118797,0.046264]},{"p":"L02N91-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the second or middle token piece of a rare proper name or uncommon word, the continuation right after its first piece: the middle of a surname or place name such as 'Tacol|nest|on', 'Al|mere', 'ele|agn|ifolia', 'Gal| Gad|ot', the second part of a German-style double first name such as 'Karl Ludwig' or 'Carl Philipp', and also 'isOpened' in OpenCV code such as cap.isOpened().","s":[0.106298,0.689221,0.709086,0.809067,0.764202,0.089213,0.010369,0.015256,0.052663,0.063525,0.028698,0.018659,0.089581,0.025656,0.052438]},{"p":"L03N10451+","l":"TWO-REL","j":"11","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the 'off' in 'switch off / turn off / turned OFF / shutoff' (turning a device or setting off), and also on the second piece of 'Terminator' (the movie), i.e. completing a word about switching something off or terminating it.","s":[0.851696,0.666014,0.756715,0.636889,0.741343,0.189389,0.035231,0.052745,0.045735,0.070005,0.019989,0.006423,0.002932,0.038008,0.036441]},{"p":"L03N10451-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the second token of a well-known multi-token proper name or compound identifier, the piece that comes right after a highly predictive first piece, most strongly on \"207\" in \"Cyberpunk 2077\", and also on \"Diesel\" in \"Vin Diesel\", \"Champ\" in \"Samurai Champloo\", \"InOut\" in \"DigitalInOut\", \"Bill\" in \"GI Bill\" and \"Checkout\" in \"Express-Checkout\".","s":[1.010706,0.795194,0.439259,0.851816,0.55796,0.028402,0.043429,0.011751,0.016029,0.051767,0.013647,0.198972,0.133855,0.019003,0.069834]},{"p":"L03N1487+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the capitalized word-start fragment \" Charl\" or \" Hack\" when it opens a longer, rarer proper noun, mostly British or Canadian places and surnames (Charlottetown, Charlton, Charlcombe, Hackney, Hackford, Hackle, Hack Butler, Colonel Hacker), and not on common whole words like Charles, Charlotte or hack.","s":[0.564388,0.683788,0.225458,0.388032,0.257243,0.047193,0.013347,0.043514,0.103942,0.10871,0.035817,0.019053,0.027158,0.010685,0.031595]},{"p":"L03N1487-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the second word piece \"via\" (or \"viol\") right after a split \"Ste\"/\"ste\" in the word Stevia or steviol, the sweetener name, in any context (food articles, ingredient lists, indexes, casual mentions); other words containing \"via\" or other sweetener names do not fire it.","s":[0.782952,0.849921,0.864807,0.685496,0.802158,0.068091,0.023629,0.062667,0.026406,0.027675,0.026635,0.025879,0.080287,0.024209,0.041375]},{"p":"L03N4280+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word piece that completes the name of the Prolog language or of symbolic/logic-based AI concepts, chiefly \"log\" in \"Prolog\" (also \"SWI-Prolog\"), \"Programming\" in \"logic programming\", Prolog/Mercury \":-\" and \"?-\" clause operators, and terms like \"Knowledge Representation\", \"Expert Systems\", \"unify\", \"resolution\" and \"meta-interpreter\" in old-school logic/AI programming contexts.","s":[0.647518,0.652929,1.4296,0.706563,0.960179,0.005602,0.006413,0.011013,0.00149,0.009511,0.025965,0.036351,0.030666,0.012271,0.008685]},{"p":"L03N4280-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on technical computer-science and systems-engineering jargon, especially formal-verification and software-tooling names, such as the word \"checker\" in \"model checker\", the acronym CTL (Computation Tree Logic), \".CodeAnalysis\" in C# \"using Microsoft.CodeAnalysis\" lines, \"test harness\", \"federated\", \"combinators\", \"rospy\" or \"Turing\", inside academic CS papers or code headers.","s":[0.346543,0.841612,0.305854,0.517572,0.651,0.040175,0.00381,0.014918,0.023176,0.005707,0.026565,0.029502,0.054576,0.047131,0.022738]},{"p":"L03N6372+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word or word-piece that completes a well-known multi-part name or fixed compound term, where the earlier part makes it nearly certain: the second word of a famous name or place (David Beckham, Palo Alto, Santa Cruz, South Australian), a stock collocation (baking parchment, Industrial Hemp), or the tail of a familiar tech or license string (opensource.org/licenses/MIT, git-svn, xampp, Solana, Beaver Builder after Elementor).","s":[0.49849,0.704448,0.39722,1.078171,0.907177,0.02996,0.065902,0.040254,0.12873,0.017735,0.044011,0.016549,0.038663,0.017244,0.029055]},{"p":"L03N6372-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the second token of a word split across tokens, overwhelmingly the 'EDURE' piece completing an all-caps SQL 'CREATE PROCEDURE' statement (stored-procedure definitions, typically after DELIMITER), with weaker spillover onto other word-continuation pieces such as 'ech' in 'mechanisms' or '_msgs'.","s":[1.036697,0.903353,1.060539,0.394991,0.779958,0.318607,0.119727,0.185374,0.107476,0.062072,0.038657,0.045511,0.030471,0.030395,0.099101]},{"p":"L03N7155+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word piece right after a capital or lowercase \"G\" that spells colloquial \"got\" words: \"otta\" in \"Gotta\" (especially sentence-initial \"Gotta say/love/admit/catch 'em all\"), \"ot\" in \"Got it!\"/\"GOT\", and \"tin\" in \"Gettin'\".","s":[0.763907,0.763907,0.763907,0.40393,0.406199,0.018792,0.009409,0.027767,0.045282,0.021916,0.029877,0.033797,0.002821,0.004635,0.031838]},{"p":"L03N7155-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the opening piece of a longer word that the tokenizer splits into fragments (e.g. 'dag' of 'daggers', 'Whisper' of 'Whispering Wood', 'Rever' of 'Revered', 'recept' of 'receptacle', 'matrimon' of 'matrimonial', 'chimpan' of 'chimpanzee'), most strongly inside fantasy and video-game lore or wiki prose (weapons, factions, place names, monster stat blocks).","s":[0.907914,0.43374,0.673431,0.722599,1.915506,0.102534,0.091073,0.165369,0.54919,0.259462,0.028453,0.043264,0.042373,0.023454,0.035559]},{"p":"L03N7540+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the first token piece of a distinctive, well-known proper name (or brand/domain) that the model can complete from memory, especially a famous surname right after a first name (Julian Assange, Paul Manafort, Brett Kavanaugh, James Comey) or the first piece of a split name (Mary P-oppins, Nicar-aguan, Assy-rian, Ebola, Trypanosoma cruzi, api.openweathermap/giphy in URLs).","s":[0.689867,0.631655,0.652659,0.449069,0.360426,0.012801,0.068385,0.043634,0.006184,0.022884,0.014754,0.011292,0.074495,0.068708,0.039626]},{"p":"L03N7540-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a place name (city, state, country or empire, often its first word-piece such as Byz-, Hawai-, Tah-, Beng-, Nep-) when it is mentioned again as the subject of a document that has already been discussing it at length, typically in phrases like \"the needs of Tacoma\", \"orphan care in Byzantium\" or \"driver jobs in Oman\"; the repeated-topic effect builds over a longer text, so short standalone texts will fire it only weakly.","s":[0.164251,0.13877,0.019453,0.369169,0.299745,0.038084,0.03202,0.024419,0.055244,0.042015,0.02348,0.026982,0.033875,0.058117,0.022778]},{"p":"L03N7574+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on astrophysics terms about stars and compact objects, most strongly the second piece \"ar\"/\"ars\" of \"pulsar(s)\" and the word \"Stellar\" (capitalized, often starting a sentence or title), plus similar pieces such as \"protoplanetary\" and \"stars\", in scientific astronomy prose or reference lists.","s":[1.000795,0.316353,0.873493,0.77407,0.902641,0.355701,0.066438,0.136215,0.069258,0.219398,0.031021,0.091869,0.015217,0.037172,0.083386]},{"p":"L03N7574-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the second token piece of a familiar compound name: chiefly 'xy'/'XY' right after a capital 'O' in words like Oxygen, OXYGEN, Oxycontin, Oxytetracycline or the ticker OXY, and '.Forms' in C# lines like 'using Xamarin.Forms;' or 'using System.Windows.Forms;' (weaker on similar continuations such as news'.yahoo'.com).","s":[0.087501,0.403753,0.333872,0.57613,0.475988,0.072295,0.058986,0.121162,0.090355,0.143521,0.33531,0.02621,0.021113,0.027469,0.022354]},{"p":"L03N7746+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the second piece of a fixed multi-token name or set phrase whose second part is nearly forced by the first, most strongly on \"arab\" in \"gum arabic\", and also on \"corp\" in \"C corp\", \"stamp\" in \"Bitstamp\", \"PLC\" in \"Informa PLC\", \"incorporation\" in \"articles of incorporation\", \"mph\" in \"lymphoma\" and \"loopholes\" in \"standard loopholes\".","s":[1.028347,0.7603,0.756477,0.622385,0.602668,0.19564,0.064608,0.139333,0.036732,0.058898,0.023909,0.056151,0.053421,0.011917,0.017315]},{"p":"L03N7746-","l":"TWO-REL","j":"11","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the method token \".fillText\" in JavaScript HTML5-canvas drawing code (e.g. ctx.fillText(\"Score\", x, y) after ctx.font/ctx.fillStyle lines), and more weakly on the middle word-piece of fixed, mostly Latin/foreign multi-piece names whose ending is predictable (\"Les Miserables\", \"Simon Boccanegra\", \"annus mirabilis\", \"sua sponte\", \"Tsar Bomba\", \"replenish\").","s":[1.174065,1.163335,1.224855,1.381386,1.176985,0.375019,0.314759,0.090192,0.569203,0.125245,0.058841,0.054616,0.019057,0.019609,0.032738]},{"p":"L03N9245+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on tokens naming a random-number generator, especially in code, such as \"random\" or \"rand\" written attached to a preceding module, prefix or identifier (Math.random, math/rand, System.Random, pseudo-random, getRandomInt, a variable named Rand or rnd, RNG/PRNG), and much less on a plain standalone English word \"random\" in prose.","s":[0.670245,0.536201,0.877508,0.795981,0.719679,0.106789,0.064095,0.145146,0.105641,0.107824,0.011746,0.013792,0.007286,0.009376,0.011789]},{"p":"L03N9245-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the namespace or library-name segment right after the first word of a C# using directive at the top of a .NET source file, strongest on \".CodeAnalysis\" and \".AspNetCore\" in lines like \"using Microsoft.CodeAnalysis;\" or \"using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc;\" (also third-party names like \"using HtmlAgilityPack;\"), with weaker spillover onto pieces of software product names in tech prose (Protocol Buffers, SmartBear, CocoaPods).","s":[0.912738,0.395038,0.600656,0.426908,0.892515,0.043636,0.035635,0.039145,0.020608,0.023873,0.019054,0.00616,0.044338,0.031963,0.035184]},{"p":"L03N9631+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on nouns or participles that refer to a name, saying or quotation itself, such as \"nickname\", \"epithet\", \"proverb\", \"quoted (in/from)\", \"citing\", \"citation\", \"pamphlet\", \"anecdotes\" or \"inscribed\", especially in scholarly prose about history, religion, mythology or etymology, at the word right before the name or text it introduces.","s":[0.318192,0.460553,0.241249,0.094176,0.342835,0.090979,0.052561,0.023812,0.059361,0.038575,0.026143,0.031757,0.058504,0.051945,0.017435]},{"p":"L03N9631-","l":"TWO-REL","j":"11","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word \"security\" in the fixed phrase \"food security\" (also \"food and nutrition security\", \"Global Food Security\") in development and humanitarian policy prose, and likewise on the acronym \"DDR\" for disarmament, demobilization and reintegration programs; it does not fire on \"security\" in other senses such as national, job or computer security.","s":[0.834288,0.677637,0.787003,0.570654,0.575035,0.038693,0.053107,0.041732,0.367303,0.086721,0.020709,0.01468,0.018024,0.057601,0.027567]},{"p":"L04N10274+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word for a medication dose, either the second piece of 'dosis' (Spanish/Indonesian, e.g. \"dosis\" split as d+osis or dos+is) or the whole English words ' dosage'/' dose'/' doses', especially in drug or supplement contexts like \"recommended dose\", \"daily dosage\" or \"dosis de paracetamol\".","s":[0.572521,0.63514,0.904519,0.38452,0.592025,0.066072,0.054159,0.092062,0.03066,0.041413,0.032031,0.076829,0.031246,0.016158,0.021124]},{"p":"L04N10274-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the first token of a new clause or verse line inside Latin or archaic old-language text (Latin quotations and book titles, lorem ipsum, Shakespeare/Milton-era verse, Old/Middle English, old Swedish), most strongly on Latin connectives like nec, neque, qui, cum, quae right after a comma or semicolon, and on a line-initial poetic 'Ah,'.","s":[0.712801,0.385034,0.31817,0.517853,0.111243,0.055198,0.060557,0.01319,0.048508,0.014342,0.012905,0.037094,0.020041,0.007189,0.010669]},{"p":"L04N2801+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the token that names, or completes the name of, a niche software project, protocol or online service in tech or crypto discussion, especially when the name is an ordinary word or word piece (Signal, Lightning in Lightning Network, lnd, Matrix, ActivityPub, app.net, Micro.blog, anyhow, re-frame, federated), and not when the same word carries its everyday meaning.","s":[0.573758,0.614337,0.891107,0.433984,0.504032,0.014157,0.014094,0.006738,0.179137,0.1254,0.014202,0.037895,0.018363,0.0103,0.079213]},{"p":"L04N2801-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"In software, programming or crypto/tech text, fires on the first piece of a proper name or identifier that the tokenizer splits into fragments, where the next token must complete it: a library, tool, company, person or domain name such as \"Hus\" in Husky, \"Sham\" in Shamir, \"Barcl\" in Barclaycard, \".block\" in docs.blockstream.com, or a folder name in a JavaScript import path like '../helpers/'.","s":[0.70297,0.716005,0.724448,0.874252,0.806505,0.045709,0.020412,0.134515,0.008032,0.04976,0.021217,0.023614,0.012417,0.010187,0.009821]},{"p":"L04N3542+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the second token of mobile-phone maker names, mainly \"ola\" in \"Motorola\" and \"tc\"/\" HTC\" in \"HTC\"/\"htc\", when the text is about cell phones, carriers or handsets (e.g. \"Motorola released a new phone\", \"Sprint sells the HTC Hero\").","s":[0.774973,0.433786,0.673721,0.613306,0.628268,0.237234,0.475084,0.011728,0.004171,0.581897,0.020492,0.007399,0.01278,0.004236,0.032527]},{"p":"L04N3542-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the names of vector operations, especially the divergence operator written as \"div\" (as in div(grad u) or div(q)), the piece \"erg\" inside \"divergence/divergent\", and \"dot\"/\"Cross\" and the word \"product\" in \"dot product\" and \"cross product\" (including code like N.dot(I) or Vector3.Cross(a, b)); weaker spillover onto other words starting with div/dive.","s":[0.951017,0.311865,0.500034,0.29859,0.373054,0.146909,0.118051,0.122634,0.026916,0.090486,0.006546,0.005429,0.013998,0.020642,0.028508]},{"p":"L04N3936+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"In project-management / Agile / business-planning prose, fires on the second or later piece of a split jargon term or acronym from that field, especially the \"own\" of \"burndown\" (as in burndown chart), \"SMART\" in SMART goals, and pieces of SCRUM, RAG, DAD or RTE, not on ordinary words.","s":[0.819001,0.285643,0.314577,0.621368,0.826769,0.03191,0.184127,0.035635,0.021748,0.05117,0.030628,0.056105,0.091822,0.052717,0.025692]},{"p":"L04N3936-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word or token that completes the noun \"system\" (or a spun synonym like \"method\", \"approach\" or \"technique\") as the head of a compound, especially \"Quality Management System\" and its acronym QMS (the \"MS\" after \"Q\"), \"production approach\" and \"management method\"; it also fires on fragments such as \"tem\" in a misspelled \"Sytem\" and \"emas\" in Spanish \"Sistemas\".","s":[0.585391,0.87913,0.046072,0.155665,0.889997,0.06462,0.265169,0.028477,0.025944,0.048692,0.019081,0.041074,0.021243,0.014193,0.009746]},{"p":"L04N5845+","l":"UNK","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a token that is the opening fragment of a rarer, multi-piece word whose completion is strongly predictable from the fragment, such as 'insurg' before 'ence', 'massac' before 'res', 'carn' before 'age', 'grit' before 'ted', 'suo' before 'motu', 'hydr' before 'ated' or 'turb' before 'id'; it is weaker on whole common words.","s":[0.084155,0.401048,0.889477,0.258661,0.239966,0.024981,0.041744,0.021465,0.197411,0.059772,0.001995,0.059303,0.111496,0.001412,0.009991]},{"p":"L04N5845-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word \"highlight\" in its tokenized pieces, strongest on misspelled or split forms where the \"-light\"/\"-lite\"/\"-lig\" piece follows a separate \"h\"/\"high\"/\"hig\" token (e.g. \"hilight\", \"highlited\", \"higlight\", \"high-light\", \"hightlights\"), and also on a capitalized \"Highlights\" opening a heading like \"Highlights of ...\" or \"Highlights:\".","s":[0.640763,0.508128,0.354022,0.321878,0.053936,0.013777,0.062854,0.087417,0.009422,0.011942,0.022493,0.042046,0.014009,0.005355,0.020491]},{"p":"L04N6289+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the name of a software app or online service, especially the second word or piece of a branded product name right after its company prefix (\" Music\" in \"Apple Music\", \" Photos\" or \" Drive\" in \"Google Photos\" / \"Google Drive\", \"Lab\" in \"GitLab\", \"iber\" in \"Viber\"), and also on app names like telegram, Discord or whatsapp used as software.","s":[0.633849,0.610471,0.365815,0.516621,0.507718,0.007832,0.004768,0.009016,0.005058,0.010484,0.015462,0.017116,0.006897,0.019512,0.008911]},{"p":"L04N6289-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires inside JavaScript/web front-end build-tooling prose or config, on names and pieces of names of build, bundling and dev tools and processes, such as brunch, Husky, coffee, proguard, Stylus, sourcemap, transpile, bundling, browserify, minified, Babel, Karma, Istanbul, CocoaPods, gzip, SSR, HashRouter and BuckleScript, and fires most on the second or later piece of such a word (e.g. \"trans|pile\", \"browser|ify\", \"min|ified\", \"Br|unch\").","s":[0.620579,0.514354,0.173211,0.224202,0.724957,0.021939,0.072585,0.024682,0.020805,0.029252,0.054296,0.022983,0.0433,0.021887,0.041224]},{"p":"L04N6542+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"In technical space or aerospace writing (spacecraft hardware descriptions, NASA mission reports, safety-critical engineering standards), this fires on the first word of a multi-word component or term name, such as 'star' in 'star trackers', 'solar' in 'solar arrays', 'auxiliary' in 'auxiliary fuel tanks', 'Boom' in 'Boom Sensor System', or 'object' in 'object code'.","s":[0.549694,0.102574,0.187347,0.255805,0.063346,0.276383,0.087593,0.053353,0.043179,0.214556,0.010807,0.039055,0.023737,0.02659,0.058845]},{"p":"L04N6542-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the key noun of systems-engineering and aerospace/defense technical jargon, most strongly \"engineering\" right after \"systems\"/\"system\"/\"model-based systems\", and also on \"verification\", \"specification\", \"formal methods\", \"VHDL\" (the \"VH\" piece), \"SysML\", and NASA/launch terms like \"launch pad\" or \"Marshall Space Flight Center\", in prose about safety-critical design, defense acquisition or spacecraft programs.","s":[0.973343,0.498113,0.481434,1.031279,0.783239,0.085273,0.008835,0.009267,0.038013,0.018492,0.008767,0.068333,0.044871,0.023649,0.016906]},{"p":"L04N7402+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word naming cell growth or multiplication in cancer and cell-biology prose, mostly \"proliferation\" (and the \"prolifer\" or \"-ifer\" piece of \"proliferate\" and \"antiproliferative\"), and also \"dividing\" (as in cells dividing), \"synergize\", \"transformation\" (as in leukemic transformation), \"mitotic\" and \"cycle\" in \"cell cycle\", when it describes what tumor or cancer cells do or what a drug blocks.","s":[0.591145,0.85047,0.71817,0.497537,0.543264,0.339191,0.127077,0.669191,0.12438,0.294048,0.023506,0.084738,0.004075,0.036297,0.026198]},{"p":"L04N7402-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on nouns for the branching parts of a tree or plant, such as twig, branch(es), limbs, bough, internodes and non-branching, and spreads to other words for a split or bend in a path, like the fork in a road, every turn, a rung or a curve; it does not fire on trunk, leaf or tree itself.","s":[0.786822,0.541,1.356264,0.775992,0.408327,0.308106,0.048483,0.03667,0.017002,0.142381,0.012213,0.028691,0.014187,0.021403,0.015192]},{"p":"L04N7919+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on distinctively British (also Australian/Irish) institutional, regional or colloquial words and names in UK-flavoured prose, peaking on terms like St Augustine's Abbey in Canterbury, 'inward investment', 'terraced houses' or 'the terraces', Wetherspoons, Legal Aid, meat pies, NFU Cymru and Cory Environmental, often on the first piece of the word.","s":[0.796254,0.942754,0.849888,1.013281,0.933908,0.008844,0.021627,0.008587,0.006716,0.049616,0.057256,0.009882,0.019771,0.018102,0.026071]},{"p":"L04N7919-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on distinctively British tokens inside UK-flavoured text, most strongly on 'Metropolitan' in 'the Metropolitan Police' and on English/Welsh place names such as Norfolk, Dover and Newport, and also on British-isms like a measuring 'jug', a 'Cheers' sign-off or the name 'Darren', in British news, council reports, recipes and forum posts.","s":[0.109749,0.736313,0.291246,1.178112,0.807734,0.012781,0.055947,0.041797,0.05109,0.010028,0.008873,0.064761,0.018614,0.024068,0.003758]},{"p":"L04N802+","l":"TWO-REL","j":"11","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a verb token that names a data-manipulation operation and completes a highly predictable compound, strongest on \".shuffle\" in Python random.shuffle(...) or np.random.shuffle(...), also on \".rstrip\" in input().rstrip() or sys.stdin.readline().rstrip(), on \"Sign\" in the e-signature word \"eSign\", and on \"factor/factoring/factorising\" in algebra lessons; the same words in ordinary prose (shuffling cards, a sign) should fire little.","s":[0.743009,0.992804,0.617238,0.921233,0.427922,0.038769,0.048649,0.085604,0.176826,0.044904,0.028632,0.013603,0.021011,0.038019,0.044943]},{"p":"L04N802-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the highly predictable token that continues a memorized boilerplate phrase in code or web templates, most strongly on \".UnitTesting\" in the C# line \"using Microsoft.VisualStudio.TestTools.UnitTesting;\", and also on \"governed\" in license headers (\"this source code is governed by a BSD license\"), \"miss\" in \"subscribe and never miss\", \"Info\" in a \"Lesson Info\" page header and \"unsub\" in \"You have successfully unsubscribed\".","s":[0.587115,0.314599,0.679405,0.33151,0.777368,0.027974,0.158548,0.501159,0.036147,0.161282,0.036304,0.037404,0.023243,0.023393,0.053002]},{"p":"L05N2634+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the main verb that directly follows a short sentence-opening subject, typically 'The/This + single noun + VERB' (e.g. 'The society began…', 'This list includes…', 'Session expired', 'Article Begins'), marking the point where a fresh sentence's subject noun is followed by its predicate verb.","s":[0.20332,0.119434,0.024192,0.110107,0.069789,0.093803,0.030587,0.025283,0.043573,0.030619,0.026055,0.028528,0.028037,0.086989,0.036088]},{"p":"L05N2634-","l":"TWO-REL","j":"11","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on an intransitive motion or process verb, often an -ing participle, that comes right after the noun doing it and is followed by a directional preposition or particle, as in 'the river flowing into the Yangtze', 'gas swirling around them', 'holidays fast approaching', 'ice melts away' or 'rays would converge to a point'; a weaker side case is the 'in' of Python traceback lines ('line 3, in <module>').","s":[0.228375,0.198553,0.355806,0.031163,0.444287,0.126415,0.020894,0.083678,0.046245,0.107901,0.085274,0.054885,0.083716,0.06765,0.011424]},{"p":"L05N3483+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the final word of a concessive stock phrase that grants a point before a 'but' rebuttal: the 'dandy' piece in 'fine and dandy', 'good' in 'all well and good' or 'fine and good', 'well' in 'all very well', and 'all' in 'great and all'.","s":[0.938237,0.703723,0.590436,0.437817,0.220784,0.122802,0.044068,0.012739,0.007517,0.017134,0.023253,0.017615,0.03282,0.022465,0.009681]},{"p":"L05N3483-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the continuation piece of a branded product line or model name (a sub-brand, model, or trim) right after the brand, e.g. \"orus\" in Gigabyte Aorus, \"G\" in ASUS ROG, \"ix\" in ROG Strix, \"an\" in Porsche Macan, \"Sport\" in Lexus F Sport, \"PG\" in ADATA XPG, \"XC\" in Volvo XC60, typically in car or PC-hardware product reviews and listings.","s":[1.120028,0.731502,0.639439,0.550644,0.73846,0.03936,0.019387,0.227694,0.089349,0.003541,0.022937,0.010753,0.018866,0.052472,0.017078]},{"p":"L05N4149+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"In software or device how-to text (UI tutorials, manuals, gear reviews, button-label code), fires on the word that names an on-screen or hardware control action or element, such as Hover, Checking a box, pressing Compute, LED buttons, Show, sync, or a checkbox or button label, typically at the start of an instruction step.","s":[0.204816,0.25426,0.461527,0.260684,0.233282,0.028309,0.013031,0.012394,0.008258,0.012527,0.012026,0.018262,0.032707,0.032525,0.009439]},{"p":"L05N4149-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on words meaning to edit or modify content, especially the user-facing edit action in software, CMS and website-builder contexts: \"edit\"/\"Edit\"/\"EDIT\", \"editable\", \"edited\", \"editing\", \"modifying\", \"customize/personalize/tailor\" (as in tailoring a page or template), \"modifiable\", and the equivalents in other languages (Japanese 編集, Chinese 编辑, Spanish/Portuguese \"Editar\", \"modificando\"), often in code UI labels like an Edit button or EditCategory.","s":[0.416515,0.422448,0.691738,0.742029,0.660504,0.191092,0.048482,0.201103,0.009645,0.105533,0.010444,0.0058,0.013791,0.004394,0.026949]},{"p":"L05N534+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the token that completes the name of a concrete object or product type, i.e. the head noun or final word-piece of a compound or multi-piece noun for a device, vehicle or artifact (hearing aid(s), video game, printing press, luxury liner, coffee roaster, wheelchairs, limo, biopharmaceutical, cybercafé), not on the modifier before it or on abstract nouns.","s":[0.664689,0.55199,0.442063,0.473462,0.115318,0.020987,0.009773,0.037104,0.005744,0.047309,0.032623,0.082831,0.116108,0.083287,0.060293]},{"p":"L05N534-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the generic category noun naming the product under discussion in a review, spec sheet, manual or product listing, especially right after \"this\" or \"the\" (\"this mic\", \"this scooter\", \"the racket\", \"Condenser Microphone\", \"this optimizer\"), i.e. the word for the kind of device or gear a text is describing.","s":[0.309071,0.551677,0.273787,0.390023,0.847662,0.049645,0.002711,0.023554,0.015389,0.009158,0.016948,0.044723,0.03748,0.024618,0.019378]},{"p":"L05N6854+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a word or word piece that forms part of a fixed technical identifier in code or hardware spec text, where the rest of the name is near-certain to follow: most strongly 'future' in Python's 'from __future__ import', 'Mali' in ARM GPU names like 'Mali-G72', 'Cortex' in CPU core names like 'Cortex-A53', '_client' in 'openssl s_client', and 'math' in imports like \"math/rand\" or 'math.ceil'.","s":[0.491847,0.67446,0.852868,0.617773,0.205836,0.00698,0.068782,0.042948,0.292766,0.009465,0.011671,0.018124,0.014789,0.00875,0.02778]},{"p":"L05N6854-","l":"TWO-REL","j":"111","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires most strongly on the word piece \" overclock\" at the start of overclocked / overclocker / overclocking in PC hardware writing about GPUs and CPUs; weaker offshoots fire on \"oblig\" in \"obligate carnivores\" and on class or parameter names in C# source files.","s":[0.781723,0.87879,0.686503,0.156851,0.642805,0.105681,0.190409,0.18478,0.080667,0.185049,0.097079,0.036524,0.025033,0.026587,0.045051]},{"p":"L05N8662+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on software and app feature terms and the tail pieces of programming identifiers in technical or code text, such as 'Clipboard' in OpenClipboard, 'dir' in opendir, multipart/form-data, display:none, attachments, upload, avatar, UITextField, or Instagram Stories and WhatsApp stickers, i.e. the noun that names a computing feature or object.","s":[0.893127,0.428417,0.690445,0.37907,0.751038,0.025461,0.038971,0.02776,0.068139,0.047637,0.141588,0.025619,0.033957,0.035855,0.042728]},{"p":"L05N8662-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the first, modifier word of a noun compound or product or material name, the word that sets up a head noun still to come, as in \"Stainless\" before \"steel\", \"aluminium\" before \"extrusion profiles\", \"Bubble\" before \"charts\", \"company\" before \"development managers\", \"corporate\" before \"video\" or \"phone\" before \"number\", mostly in commercial, technical or software-listing text.","s":[0.489215,0.222249,0.061596,0.485064,0.081792,0.022801,0.021418,0.018359,0.222535,0.044466,0.02236,0.023674,0.057036,0.015414,0.052983]},{"p":"L05N8714+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word for wireless/cable-free connectivity in tech contexts, specifically the token completing it — \"less\" in \"wireless\"/\"cordless\" (also \"wirelessly\"), whole-word \"wireless\"/\"Wireless\", and \"WiFi\"/\"Wi-Fi\", plus the similar \"line\" in \"powerline\" and \"Plug\" in \"HomePlug\" — describing how devices connect without wires.","s":[0.432993,0.372105,0.377663,0.575993,0.340707,0.016265,0.126177,0.059719,0.197348,0.019861,0.012207,0.01382,0.010839,0.01899,0.010498]},{"p":"L05N8714-","l":"UNREL","j":"00","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on library or namespace names inside import headers at the top of code files, such as 'using System.Linq;' or 'using System.Collections;' in C#, 'import unittest' in Python, or require('inquirer') in Node, and separately on the word piece 'lamin' in 'laminated/laminating' in electrical-engineering text about transformer cores and eddy currents.","s":[0.20851,0.140319,0.359964,0.725348,0.126895,0.029153,0.011715,0.067485,0.053313,0.137314,0.050508,0.040271,0.0344,0.02334,0.021743],"u":{"0":[0.20851,0.140319,0.420786,0.040924,0.048943],"1":[0.144099,0.740902,0.057784,0.324326,0.899059]}},{"p":"L05N9161+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on words about who caused or deliberately did something bad, i.e. agency, intent, or blame for a wrongful act, in investigative, legal, or crime prose: tampered, perpetrator, perpetrated, committed, intentional(ly), masterminded, accomplices, culprit, culpable, wrongdoing, animus, staged, man-made.","s":[1.129252,0.400612,0.555399,0.447514,0.543569,0.336228,0.126759,0.25049,0.095392,0.269279,0.055327,0.028709,0.0473,0.039279,0.104405]},{"p":"L05N9161-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a technical name segment inside code or software/web text, especially the member or namespace piece right after a dot or path separator, such as document.forms in JavaScript, System.Web.UI in C# using lines, By.CssSelector, /proc/bus/input/devices, or the 'Filename:' field of a PHP error message.","s":[0.76934,0.677872,0.675644,0.678444,0.561773,0.021678,0.014116,0.11636,0.044732,0.062803,0.131035,0.099239,0.169489,0.109313,0.132333]},{"p":"L05N924+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on additive \"along with\" connectors that attach a further item to a list or noun phrase, in any language: \"together\" or the \"with\" of \"together with\", \"accompanied (by)\", German \"sowie\"/\"sowohl\", Indonesian \"bersama\", Latin \"cum\", Spanish \"junto (a)\", French \"ainsi (que)\"; strongest in formal or legal prose, e.g. \"shall receive notice of all meetings, together with copies of all minutes\".","s":[0.530623,0.895465,0.512231,0.761769,0.998186,0.090294,0.08447,0.0726,0.049253,0.182377,0.04393,0.058098,0.008021,0.018152,0.083203]},{"p":"L05N924-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a token in the middle of a fixed, highly predictable multi-token expression, where the rest is all but certain: most often \".Collections\" in the C# line \"using System.Collections.Generic;\", the \"-too\" of \"all-too-often\" or \"all-too-familiar\", the \"unknow\" of \"unknowingly\", and word starts such as \"incom\" (incomprehensible), \"adul\" (adulterated) and \"epit\" (epitome).","s":[0.279662,0.37605,0.330144,0.354342,0.269026,0.142057,0.018743,0.026057,0.021033,0.042822,0.024488,0.025311,0.03346,0.018523,0.063994]},{"p":"L06N10970+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the noun or verb naming a technical or creative making activity (coding, programming, computer, Scratch, Logo, satellite, catapult, engineer, entrepreneurial) right where it is introduced as the thing kids or beginners learn, or as a historical first, e.g. 'introduce kids to programming', 'learn the basics of coding', 'the first personal computer'.","s":[1.115652,0.350083,1.06797,0.870884,1.28777,0.181841,0.039319,0.090242,0.065171,0.276896,0.047374,0.058508,0.095266,0.091651,0.032807]},{"p":"L06N10970-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the final word or word-piece that completes a familiar compound naming a setting, facility or apparatus, most strongly \"greenhouse\" (crops grown in a greenhouse, greenhouse conditions), the \"breakfast\" of \"bed and breakfast\" or the \"&B\" of B&B, and research settings such as a controlled laboratory, a sleep lab, pot experiments or numerical simulations; it also fires on \"optic\" in \"fiber optic\".","s":[0.164667,0.465277,0.423389,0.20022,0.10749,0.01831,0.086642,0.137015,0.039945,0.074417,0.043397,0.026835,0.051388,0.054035,0.050669]},{"p":"L06N2185+","l":"UNREL","j":"00","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word \"connection\" / \"connected\" / \"disconnected\" when it means emotional or social closeness between people, especially after intensifiers like \"feel more\", \"a deeper\", \"stronger\" or \"your\" (relationship, wellness, workplace-belonging prose).","s":[0.776118,0.583281,0.716335,0.659473,0.662568,0.080183,0.093229,0.158351,0.25955,0.037606,0.119373,0.007213,0.042226,0.015473,0.067374],"u":{"0":[0.776118,0.711905,0.586903,0.441163,0.596315],"1":[0.59965,0.839378,0.218543,0.125808,0.234737]}},{"p":"L06N2185-","l":"TWO-REL","j":"11","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a piece inside a rare multi-token word in academic or expository prose. The most reliable case is the 'extrav' piece of 'extraversion'/'extraverted' in personality-psychology writing (Big Five, Eysenck, MBTI). It also fires on the later pieces of uncommon author or thinker surnames named in a book or citation context (Kelsen, Solnit, Bataille, Appadurai, Carse, Kondo).","s":[0.865376,0.996449,0.732188,0.895945,0.888345,0.076997,0.082877,0.150782,0.101805,0.468752,0.02539,0.077259,0.04058,0.051391,0.048637]},{"p":"L06N2537+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a noun that names a group or unit of items treated as a set or as one member of a population, right after a qualifying word, above all \"batch\", \"lot\" or \"apples\" in phrases like \"a bad batch\", \"a few bad apples\", \"this particular batch\", \"every lot of bullets\", \"batch to batch\" or \"production batch\"; weaker on population-member nouns like \"bull\", \"individuals\", \"specimens\" or \"subjects\" after a quantifier or demonstrative.","s":[0.588336,0.751619,0.698085,0.502006,0.505155,0.109682,0.020593,0.066164,0.072913,0.071275,0.022341,0.022222,0.013414,0.017417,0.009169]},{"p":"L06N2537-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on nouns that name a category or kind used to group things, most strongly \"breed(s)\", \"channel(s)\", \"brand\" (Spanish/Portuguese \"marca\") and \"country\" (\"pais\"/\"paese\"), plus \"tag\" and \"class(es)\", whether in prose about dog breeds or YouTube/Slack/Discord channels or as field and variable names in SQL tables and code.","s":[0.120849,0.810745,0.282527,0.183599,0.408885,0.040038,0.310041,0.063832,0.094872,0.082669,0.132021,0.039373,0.050889,0.07249,0.055674]},{"p":"L06N4452+","l":"UNREL","j":"00","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the second or later token of a multi-token proper name, brand, domain or title, where the earlier piece makes this continuation predictable: \"Snyder\" after \"Timothy\", \"Casino\" in \"Glory Casino\" or \"InterCasino\", \"web\" in reportsweb.com, \"ctions\" in \"Continued Fractions\", \"ades\" in \"International Brigades\", \"Gest\" in \"Res Gestae\"; it is strongest when the name already appeared earlier in the same text.","s":[0.526251,0.72375,0.155785,0.705278,0.794021,0.039254,0.013971,0.093932,0.0457,0.024147,0.101515,0.03297,0.2159,0.036936,0.070896],"u":{"0":[0.054151,0.033056,0.108611,0.044928,0.060795],"1":[0.025911,0.045151,0.037305,0.117972,0.03584]}},{"p":"L06N4452-","l":"UNREL","j":"00","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires mainly on two unrelated tokens: the class name Random right after the keyword new in C# code (as in \"Random rnd = new Random();\"), and the word rhyme or its \"ym\" piece in rhymed, rhyming or rhymes when prose describes a poem's rhyme scheme or poetic devices.","s":[0.372276,0.448451,0.76277,0.764334,0.500196,0.196521,0.06855,0.310544,0.243957,0.073202,0.010662,0.012036,0.029114,0.159423,0.076198],"u":{"0":[0.556284,0.37769,0.501117,0.590523,0.440111],"1":[0.720957,0.659467,0.514014,0.713021,0.374258]}},{"p":"L06N572+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the second or later word-piece of an unusual multi-token name or term that the text has already used at least once before, such as the 'ik' of a repeated 'batik', the 'enth' of 'Juneteenth', the 'CP' of 'HACCP', or 'second' in a repeated 'Leap second'; this is an induction-style completion of a recurring rare term, and it is weak on the term's first appearance or on common one-token words.","s":[0.181547,0.197555,0.682449,0.112858,0.046306,0.072393,0.034624,0.087583,0.104679,0.066818,0.0261,0.0637,0.074763,0.073676,0.071644]},{"p":"L06N572-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the second or a middle piece of a multi-token term, or the second word of a multi-word term, at the start of a definitional sentence that introduces that term as its subject right before \"is\"/\"refers to\" (e.g. \"Sy|phil|is is a…\", \"Bo|keh refers to…\", \"Protocol Buffers is…\", \"A minimum viable product is…\"), especially when the term was already named earlier in the text.","s":[0.213419,0.244376,0.048726,0.264121,0.214413,0.213419,0.015027,0.005028,0.013536,0.013753,0.012772,0.011354,0.181598,0.029605,0.02729]},{"p":"L06N6273+","l":"TWO-REL","j":"11","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the ending word-piece of a split, somewhat literary word used in a figurative or idiomatic phrase, especially 'distillation of', 'checkered past', 'strained relationship/alliance', 'frayed', 'leavened with', 'limned', 'deep and abiding love' and 'briskly written', in essay or review-style prose.","s":[0.701916,0.720357,0.579328,0.906275,0.81205,0.106784,0.112507,0.187851,0.3702,0.282724,0.051558,0.074905,0.025292,0.015924,0.046133]},{"p":"L06N6273-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the second verb of a compact \"X and Y\" verb pair or compound naming a two-step action, i.e. the verb (or its first piece) right after \"and\", \"&\" or \"-and-\" in phrases like \"pack and ship\", \"train-and-equip\", \"stop and frisk\", \"parse and stringify\", \"SendAndSave\", \"fetchAndVisualize\", \"cut & wrap\", \"tested & reviewed\".","s":[0.713512,0.53413,0.811296,0.364999,0.965703,0.064084,0.00664,0.007433,0.007667,0.036911,0.029864,0.022091,0.030575,0.021406,0.016128]},{"p":"L06N7759+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a casual laughter or dismissal interjection, mostly \"Haha\"/\"haha\" (on the \"aha\" piece after a capital H) and also \"lol\", \"heh\" or \"Nah\", tacked onto a joke or self-deprecating remark in informal first-person writing such as blog posts, forum comments or chat.","s":[0.598791,0.713297,0.598294,0.255331,0.761165,0.060888,0.034358,0.039236,0.01452,0.104116,0.065972,0.033739,0.029913,0.017509,0.043517]},{"p":"L06N7759-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on game-mechanic jargon words inside competitive card-game or Pokémon strategy writing, such as ramp, switch, switch-in, fetch, search (your library), tutor, lands, battlefield, permanents, sweeper, leads, graveyard and Mox, used as gameplay terms in deck or team discussion.","s":[1.178099,0.565864,1.181962,0.792071,1.166291,0.039364,0.17508,0.058478,0.111835,0.025165,0.015189,0.030259,0.022517,0.024291,0.017744]},{"p":"L06N8439+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a word-piece in the middle of a multi-token word whose rest is highly predictable, such as 'Tube' in YouTube, '.blogspot' in a blog URL, 'Asc' in Royal Ascot, 'RAINT' in a capitalized place name, or the start of a foreign-language word like Portuguese 'divulgação' or Dutch 'Bekijk', especially where the text abruptly switches language or topic.","s":[0.136168,0.320647,0.589825,0.20941,0.48192,0.146037,0.068874,0.039096,0.028586,0.050402,0.019771,0.073549,0.012386,0.030237,0.046188]},{"p":"L06N8439-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires at paragraph and section boundaries inside polished, generic, AI-written-style explainer or SEO articles: on the period that ends a paragraph right before a blank line or new heading, or on the transition word that opens the next sentence, such as \"However\", \"One of\", \"Let's\", \"Below\" or \"Apart from\". It likely needs several paragraphs of such prose to build up, so it is weak in very short texts.","s":[0.205697,0.275443,0.132453,0.44247,0.209686,0.061966,0.036595,0.011299,0.011474,0.010129,0.033242,0.035627,0.058615,0.031467,0.031516]},{"p":"L06N8759+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a noun naming the person in a specific role within an organization or system, usually the last piece of a compound role noun right after \"the\" or a modifier (project manager, site proprietor, subdivider, account holder, website host, Product Owner, program director, the pilot, the QB, the GM/DM, the CFO, the minister), as in \"all costs shall be paid by the subdivider\" or \"the restaurant proprietor must take out a loan\".","s":[0.566745,0.436278,0.877784,1.080966,0.59177,0.024828,0.319804,0.08241,0.242234,0.194207,0.044018,0.080445,0.100408,0.063731,0.134473]},{"p":"L06N8759-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the second or later sub-word piece of a word that the tokenizer has split into fragments, strongest when the word is misspelled or oddly split in informal text (e.g. the ending of \"childeren\", \"speekers\", \"poeple\", \"diffrent\", \"Kindergartner\", \"pidiendo\") and on \".GPIO\" right after \"RPi\" or \"Jetson\" in Python imports; it does not fire on the first piece or on ordinary whole words.","s":[0.051989,0.173983,0.246663,0.320831,0.860101,0.027068,0.019184,0.054069,0.320831,0.089213,0.032584,0.091971,0.106402,0.024501,0.02499]},{"p":"L06N9844+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a token that is almost forced by the preceding token(s): the continuation piece of a word split into pieces (Par|aph|rasing, back|slash, Marg|ins, Par|lays, chrom|atic, pig|mented) or the expected word completing a fixed collocation (\"© Author(s)\", \"pled guilty\", \"pursed my lips\", \"scratched at my cheek\", \"a few showers\", \"marked up\", \"resin infusion\").","s":[0.363876,0.463517,0.204215,0.238168,0.749961,0.061493,0.213848,0.029936,0.127847,0.091575,0.074645,0.095719,0.062315,0.052399,0.059423]},{"p":"L06N9844-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the continuation piece of a familiar fixed term, the token that completes a well-known compound or multi-token word after its first part has made it predictable, as in paint-by-numbers, parallel or perfect fifths, Tetris, jigsaw, croquet, Stepford, open fracture, whole tone, first inversion, ring-closing and evaporated milk.","s":[0.844738,1.026755,0.558929,0.0986,0.430853,0.146729,0.019989,0.082064,0.067158,0.190741,0.096678,0.017984,0.014223,0.102904,0.096167]},{"p":"L07N10364+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires at the hinge where a heading or entry label has just ended and its body is about to begin, most strongly on the space after the headword-plus-comma that opens a Britannica-style encyclopedia entry (\"Newport Folk Festival, folk-music festival held…\", \"Jacob Glatstein, also called…\"), and similarly on the \"?)\" that closes a name gloss before a character description, or the \"{\" / \"(\" that opens a code block after a function or table header.","s":[0.048877,0.069836,0.015206,0.060781,0.068355,0.043611,0.022986,0.043025,0.033656,0.025468,0.07142,0.044674,0.041686,0.028251,0.088537]},{"p":"L07N10364-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on scraped-web boilerplate tokens embedded in ordinary text, chiefly the file extension of an image filename in a caption (the .JPG, .svg or .tif in 'Temple Pagodas 01.JPG'), the 'Shutterstock' in a 'Source: Name / Shutterstock.com' photo credit, 'sound' in Wikipedia's 'About this sound listen', and 'click' in 'right-click My Computer' instructions.","s":[0.33177,0.096631,1.184692,0.467416,0.482992,0.01993,0.034383,0.024604,0.051906,0.027825,0.013173,0.070315,0.045266,0.058735,0.083555]},{"p":"L07N1160+","l":"TWO-REL","j":"11","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the punctuation, paragraph breaks and opening words of formulaic, chatty SEO/listicle blog intros that address the reader directly (\"So you're a website owner who...\", \"Have you ever wondered...\", \"Well, fear not! In this article, we'll break down...\"), most strongly at the sentence-ending period or comma right before a new paragraph or heading in that style.","s":[0.102975,0.152705,0.036038,0.011742,0.017762,0.004333,0.014359,0.019832,0.005829,0.010752,0.013629,0.019162,0.009763,0.003806,0.017015]},{"p":"L07N1160-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"In a reply to someone's suggestion or tip (forum/comment thread), fires on the key word of the replier's acknowledgment of intending to follow up or of learning something new — 'I will look / check (it out) / try / read', 'I'll take a look', 'give it a shot / some thought', 'keep in mind', 'didn't know about', 'never thought of', 'that is interesting about'.","s":[1.544303,1.139729,1.173677,1.442885,0.741206,0.571357,0.352172,0.173828,0.043213,0.096582,0.028739,0.015192,0.035882,0.003058,0.019247]},{"p":"L07N2194+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a middle or second word-piece of the name of a specific baked or snack food dish (em-PAN-adas, vol-AU-vents, t-ARTS, brusch-ETTA, pro-SCI-utto, muff-ins, c-ARAM-els, Kerop-OK, Chawanm-USHI), i.e. partway through spelling out a food item, mostly pastries, tarts and appetizers, in recipe or restaurant-review text.","s":[0.873114,0.52678,0.510681,0.404295,0.556857,0.081786,0.025561,0.015293,0.073031,0.113772,0.042247,0.071597,0.071312,0.053829,0.094278]},{"p":"L07N2194-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the preposition that introduces the source material after a verb of making or assembling — \"from\"/\"of\" in \"made from\", \"built out of\", \"assembled from\", \"woven out of\", \"make balls from\" (also \"od\"/\"dari\" in other languages, and \"Form\" in recipe steps) — i.e. the point where the material or components something is constructed from is about to be named.","s":[0.076353,0.545888,0.477619,0.833497,0.413936,0.024271,0.110434,0.299196,0.153997,0.00603,0.003073,0.015036,0.004808,0.005554,0.011836]},{"p":"L07N4017+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the second token of a word or stock phrase whose ending is already fully predictable, most strongly when a speaker echoes back a word just said by the other person as an incredulous retort (\"I despised you.\" / \"Despised you?\"; \"Wouldn't you regret it?\" / \"Regret what?\"; \"Speak to her!\"), and also on the second word of fixed interjections like \"You bet\", \"of course\" and \"guess what\".","s":[0.359703,0.139529,0.531962,0.245787,0.59546,0.187618,0.06589,0.267351,0.172035,0.576821,0.063426,0.086588,0.110197,0.029158,0.118893]},{"p":"L07N4017-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the verb of a user-facing instruction or prompt that tells the reader to enter, type, choose, provide or enable something, as in website/app interface text and input prompts (\"Please type a message\", \"Please ensure JavaScript is enabled\", \"JavaScript is required to…\", \"Digite os elementos\", \"Informe o valor\", \"Выберите программу\", \"Il tuo indirizzo email\"), often on the imperative ending of a Portuguese/Russian/Turkish verb or the word right after \"Please\"; similar spots include a form <label> tag and a recipe step like \"Place all your…\".","s":[0.211667,0.250707,0.51604,0.693678,0.305935,0.05571,0.157446,0.054052,0.083117,0.14567,0.078602,0.074892,0.013773,0.18443,0.025752]},{"p":"L07N534+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word piece that completes or names a physical material or substance noun, most strongly the piece \"abbage\" after a capital or lowercase C in \"Cabbage\"/\"cabbage\" (also \"asse\" in bagasse, \"Po\" in LiPo, \"hydr\" in dehydrator, \"dex\" in Kydex, and whole words like paper, charcoal, silk, carpet, coke, fabric), in recipe, food, craft or technical prose.","s":[0.299318,0.555417,0.777237,0.652418,0.283173,0.33812,0.256877,0.114519,0.225302,0.24126,0.053272,0.011905,0.022653,0.043568,0.035715]},{"p":"L07N534-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the noun naming a manufactured product or device (cathode ray tube/CRT display, batteries, light bulbs, lamps, capacitors, cell phones, mattresses, refrigerants, pigments, cable) when it appears in text about a chemical element, mineral or material and where it ends up being used, e.g. \"used in storage batteries\", \"filament in light bulbs\", \"recovered from discarded CRT displays\".","s":[0.225095,0.580761,0.673488,0.522005,0.561351,0.281341,0.067129,0.023444,0.07681,0.045375,0.004134,0.007429,0.01832,0.012879,0.007923]},{"p":"L07N5613+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"In an academic author-year citation, fires on the separator right after the first author's surname (usually the comma in \"(Surname, Coauthor & Coauthor, 2008)\" or \"Surname, 2004\"; in run-together reference lists, the initials glued after the surname), i.e. the point where the model must decide whether more author names or the year come next.","s":[0.629722,0.223243,0.037525,0.379899,0.376017,0.020409,0.047306,0.015782,0.021857,0.024084,0.028883,0.030358,0.02161,0.023211,0.020252]},{"p":"L07N5613-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the token right after a surname in a surname-first person-name listing (a reference list, author list, patent inventor field, committee roster or name index), i.e. the separator, initial or given name that follows the family name, such as the comma in \"Silipo, Alba\", the initial in \"Kobrin V\", \"Butt, A.\" or \"et al\" after a first author.","s":[0.327952,0.380216,0.212145,0.393434,0.215389,0.150587,0.026612,0.021519,0.058171,0.063138,0.012825,0.011768,0.040789,0.0303,0.049039]},{"p":"L07N6924+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the continuation word-piece of a multi-token regional place name in local or travel prose, strongest for Melbourne/Victoria and Portland/Oregon places (the 'arra' in Yarra River or Yarra Valley, 'arton' in Shepparton, 'ah' in Multnomah, 'iam' in Santiam, 'kie' in Milwaukie, 'leg' in Castlegar, 'ville' in Cartersville), and on regional tags like ' VIC' after a suburb or the Oregon area code 503 in a phone number; famous single-token cities like London or Paris should not fire it.","s":[0.886019,0.652037,0.72207,0.741767,0.594109,0.019285,0.008789,0.010566,0.012066,0.015842,0.030969,0.010574,0.058662,0.010576,0.007623]},{"p":"L07N6924-","l":"TWO-REL","j":"11","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the second or later word-piece of a small, regionally local place name (a town, suburb or county split into pieces, like the \"offs\" in Coffs Harbour, \"sett\" in Hooksett, \"atta\" in Parramatta, \"olland\" in Tolland, \"atu\" in Manawatu), especially Australian, New Zealand, New Hampshire and northern English localities, and also on the New Hampshire area code 603 in phone numbers.","s":[1.024041,0.534032,0.668237,0.673545,0.985217,0.011763,0.035674,0.124981,0.013819,0.272765,0.017922,0.017245,0.030806,0.034635,0.024081]},{"p":"L07N7004+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a word or word-piece that names a medical, injury or healing concept (medication, wound dressing or bandage, ointment, compress, bedside, fever, blood loss, feeling unwell, cardiac, paediatric, treating), especially on the continuation piece of such a word, across any genre of text: news, fiction, forums, spam or code.","s":[0.447228,0.932627,0.063236,0.158637,0.306009,0.370054,0.053378,0.020462,0.019544,0.046797,0.007649,0.11525,0.0281,0.030211,0.095504]},{"p":"L07N7004-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word or word-piece that completes a reference to doctors as a profession or to medical training and its institutions: \"medical school\"/\"med school\"/\"premed\", \"School of Medicine\", the degree abbreviations MD, MBBS and M.B., \"family physician\", \"junior doctor\", \"surgical clerkship\", medical journals such as CMAJ, BMJ, BJGP and AMIA, and doctor TV shows such as St. Elsewhere, Scrubs, Grey's Anatomy and Patch Adams.","s":[0.47502,0.744423,0.431393,0.614623,0.568156,0.114403,0.041423,0.048078,0.016693,0.014978,0.014726,0.036914,0.018066,0.009902,0.007073]},{"p":"L07N8340+","l":"UNREL","j":"00","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires mainly on the word \"Latter\" in \"Latter-day Saints\" (for example \"The Church of Jesus Christ of Latter-day Saints\" or \"the Latter-day Saints were driven west\"), and to a lesser degree on other religious-group name pieces such as Mormon, Muslim or Templi; separately it fires on \"fiat\" in cryptocurrency exchange prose and on paragraph breaks deep inside long crypto-exchange review pages.","s":[0.513796,0.54383,0.482208,0.405699,0.18994,0.014247,0.025976,0.069976,0.086643,0.147781,0.083713,0.039798,0.02335,0.04424,0.072454],"u":{"0":[0.554276,0.535825,0.18091,0.553058,0.250987],"1":[0.306501,0.314604,0.098678,0.307679,0.201856]}},{"p":"L07N8340-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the period plus blank line that ends a paragraph in spun, auto-generated SEO web text: synonym-swapped prose such as \"vitality storage\", \"auto trade\" or \"full inhabitants\", stitched together from unrelated paragraphs, so the paragraph break marks an abrupt topic jump. It is strongest after several such paragraphs and is hard to reproduce in a short text.","s":[0.133256,0.141385,0.140674,0.266699,0.250399,0.059065,0.021444,0.023527,0.016379,0.098937,0.047169,0.072357,0.021425,0.110053,0.018268]},{"p":"L07N8409+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the words of web-page boilerplate and site chrome near the start of a page, especially the label in a byline or header line such as \"By: Name - Published:\", \"Sponsored Links\", \"Popular Review Links\", \"go to homepage\", \"Translate this page\", \"We've detected you are on Internet Explorer\", \"JavaScript disabled\", \"This content is syndicated from\", or a timestamp zone like \"12:04 BST\" / \"08:25 AM GMT\".","s":[0.589238,0.78996,0.796401,0.975582,0.618143,0.020188,0.008546,0.030501,0.044871,0.090403,0.032705,0.029392,0.011809,0.015326,0.079131]},{"p":"L07N8409-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"In SQL queries, fires on the token that joins a second table onto the first: the JOIN keyword (or the comma in an old-style comma join) that comes right after a FROM table and its alias, and also on the \"alias.*,\" right after SELECT that signals more than one table is in play.","s":[0.609882,0.7558,0.721797,0.54179,0.670391,0.379981,0.574399,0.090072,0.23632,0.064444,0.007455,0.003358,0.008316,0.012754,0.004922]},{"p":"L08N10397+","l":"UNREL","j":"00","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on words naming the geometric or visual shape of a drawn symbol, logo, letter or part (oval, triangular, circular, 'Y shape', squiggly, cursive, glyph, icon names in HTML/SVG code like fa-star or box-arrow), mostly as the shape word right after a description of what something looks like; a weaker separate trigger is the noun in 'go for a walk / stroll / brisk run'.","s":[0.90707,0.387877,0.587089,0.440119,0.469336,0.083262,0.07624,0.242632,0.075189,0.033817,0.081969,0.064679,0.025202,0.031407,0.025108],"u":{"0":[0.930547,0.540686,0.619636,0.773871,0.544961],"1":[0.563361,0.739243,0.343013,0.550888,0.293621]}},{"p":"L08N10397-","l":"TWO-REL","j":"11","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word that completes a description of how hair is worn or how an item is kept, especially the second half of a set compound: ponytail, bun, buzz cut, braided, top knot, haircut, or vacuum-sealed, airtight container, jewel cases, cello bags, kept covered and refrigerated (also clothing like boxer briefs).","s":[0.793263,0.52259,0.706163,0.489477,0.763962,0.02043,0.02247,0.061823,0.067622,0.27977,0.037308,0.038143,0.029357,0.03714,0.044184]},{"p":"L08N10948+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on words naming strategic or scarce raw materials (\"metals\", \"minerals\", \"materials\", \"rare earth\", \"cobalt\", \"coltan\", \"neodymium\", \"REEs\") when they appear as resources that must be mined, imported, recycled or secured, e.g. \"critical minerals\", \"rare earth metals\", \"valuable metals\" in e-waste, battery or EV supply-chain and geopolitics prose; it is strongest on the material noun itself, right after modifiers like rare, critical, valuable or raw.","s":[0.926758,0.725856,0.506325,0.712471,0.76946,0.117556,0.032614,0.031981,0.009249,0.060231,0.019165,0.017839,0.006837,0.010555,0.020391]},{"p":"L08N10948-","l":"TWO-REL","j":"11","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the second piece of the phrase \"zero-gravity\" or \"zero-g\" (the \"-gr\", \"avity\", \"-g\" or \"-G\" right after \"zero\"), and on \"weightlessness\", mostly in science-fiction or space-station prose such as aliens, spaceships, astronauts, Spartans or RPG skill lists, and more weakly on ordinary nouns in sci-fi gear and ship-systems descriptions.","s":[0.623287,0.451215,0.263796,0.184747,0.262392,0.176601,0.13392,0.393971,0.019033,0.058823,0.045414,0.031984,0.108603,0.128943,0.079264]},{"p":"L08N179+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the predictable second piece of a fixed multi-token name, above all the \".DataAnnotations\" right after \"System.ComponentModel\" in C# using directives; weaker cases are word-completing pieces such as \"ker\" in handkerchief, \"rog\" in surrogacy, \"pot\" in Gamespot, \"Cup\" in Piston Cup, and \"panties\"/\"pants\" in lingerie or underwear contexts.","s":[0.891904,0.794169,0.131011,0.598741,0.428398,0.11602,0.052148,0.138833,0.072904,0.114245,0.047208,0.057057,0.058386,0.093705,0.0189]},{"p":"L08N179-","l":"TWO-REL","j":"11","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the very first token of a C# source file when that token is an invisible byte-order mark (U+FEFF) fused with a leading \"//\" comment, such as \"\\uFEFF// Copyright (c) Microsoft Corporation.\" or \"\\uFEFF// <copyright file=...>\"; this needs the BOM character literally at the start of the text, so a plain \"//\" or a comment later in a file will not fire, and it can only weakly be reproduced in a short text; it also fires on \"EventArgs\" inside \"new SocketAsyncEventArgs()\" in C#.","s":[0.106576,0.04953,0.690722,0.159476,0.725854,0.055756,0.055788,0.087734,0.077993,0.112315,0.013699,0.119135,0.085572,0.11241,0.040925]},{"p":"L08N3846+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word that completes a disappointed or downside turn, especially understated negations of a positive word such as 'less than ecstatic', 'not so thrilled', 'wasn't so happy when', 'anything but rosy', 'less-than-ideal', 'a hard pill to swallow', 'bummer news', and on the final '5' of a low rating like '1 out of 5' or '2/5'.","s":[0.157148,0.058818,0.140132,0.022383,0.116414,0.0149,0.024761,0.006182,0.015065,0.017625,0.053599,0.091154,0.01922,0.058032,0.032894]},{"p":"L08N3846-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the noun or verb that names a fallback or fix for a problem that was just described, the word that completes phrases like 'looking for viable alternatives', 'my only recourse', 'had to resort to', 'found a workaround', 'make do', 'to overcome/resolve/remedy this problem', 'no other option', 'look for something else', and it fires most strongly when a difficulty or lack has been set up just before it.","s":[1.078841,0.554033,0.479242,0.754996,0.723,0.059179,0.021346,0.086665,0.043929,0.09821,0.014301,0.025268,0.007102,0.01531,0.031714]},{"p":"L08N5063+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the token where the model recognizes and starts completing a famous multi-piece name, typically the first piece of a well-known surname right after the person's first name or title (Jimi Hend|rix, Alfred Hitch|cock, Dr. Pav|lov, de Roths|child, Dwight/Mamie Eisenhower, Douglas Mac|Arthur), and likewise the second piece of a famous brand or term split into pieces (Go|pro, You/Tou|Tube, Cat|apult, Dys|lex|ia, J|IRA).","s":[0.439161,0.427636,0.794998,0.435842,0.372535,0.028886,0.037923,0.036711,0.04515,0.03158,0.01839,0.049369,0.009249,0.034323,0.049988]},{"p":"L08N5063-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the second token (a middle word-piece or the second word) of a famous multi-token proper name from pop culture, fiction or history, the point where the first piece makes the rest of the name certain, e.g. the 'oc' in Pinocchio, 'Inch' in Nine Inch Nails, 'chest' in Winchesters, 'Qu' in Don Quixote, 'Joh' in Scarlett Johansson, 'ue' in Che Guevara, 'ux' in Ku Klux Klan.","s":[0.79512,0.623709,0.129926,0.495291,0.516272,0.041675,0.016293,0.025902,0.025357,0.027252,0.025175,0.019668,0.010211,0.122009,0.005376]},{"p":"L08N6643+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a subword piece in the middle of a rare, foreign, technical or OCR-garbled word when that piece contains or directly follows the letter p, especially p-r/p-l clusters (e.g. the 'rep' in aprepitant, 'pod' in Perpodium, 'pc' in a garbled 'prospect', 'php' in a misspelled 'phosphorous', 'yp' in platypus); ordinary whole words with p do not fire it.","s":[0.58587,0.56295,0.111844,0.188021,0.498519,0.037729,0.026152,0.09256,0.028922,0.049903,0.01879,0.053951,0.017142,0.02123,0.050965]},{"p":"L08N6643-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word that completes a highly predictable stock phrase or fixed collocation, i.e. the token the preceding words all but force, such as \"power\" in \"do everything in our power\", \"inquiry\" in \"Send your inquiry\", \"paramount\" in \"Safety is of paramount importance\", \"please\" in \"May it please your Excellency\", \"-blooded\" in \"cold-blooded killer\", \"setup\" in p5.js \"function setup()\", or \"journalism\" in \"independent journalism\".","s":[0.746493,0.492892,0.529731,0.334785,0.156005,0.430777,0.170074,0.021822,0.016693,0.093232,0.082877,0.016074,0.02143,0.042686,0.064938]},{"p":"L08N6822+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word naming what an optimizer or policymaker aims at, in optimization, control and economic-policy prose: objective, goal, target, aim, mandate, penalty, minimum/maximum, minimize, welfare-maximizing, stabilization (e.g. \"return objectives\", \"utility may be a maximum\", \"the Fed's dual mandate\", \"a single objective\").","s":[0.632349,0.488638,0.215141,0.659604,0.525058,0.043435,0.005099,0.053,0.0804,0.009828,0.023424,0.026584,0.012802,0.006789,0.007548]},{"p":"L08N6822-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on words naming an aim, target or optimization criterion that something is pursued toward or measured against — especially \"goal\", \"target(s)\", \"objective\", \"purpose\", \"maximize\", \"loss\"/\"cost function\", \"scoring function\", \"criteria\", \"satisfying\" a condition — as in \"achieve the goal of\", \"meet the target\", \"to maximize efficiency\", \"total_loss\", \"argmax\", \"victory condition\".","s":[0.083883,0.769447,0.470818,0.147508,0.132684,0.02764,0.062438,0.066058,0.041423,0.083742,0.06973,0.01859,0.032853,0.055084,0.023248]},{"p":"L08N8341+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the second token of a well-known multi-part name, where the first part already predicts it: most strongly 'Buff' in 'Protocol Buffers', also surnames after a first name (Reid Hoffman, Brian Armstrong, George Saunders, Steven Pressfield), 'Qaeda' in 'Al-Qaeda', and later word pieces of brand or place names (truthcoin, Paradigm, Runeterra, Terrenus).","s":[0.835246,0.743892,0.75275,0.454795,0.755539,0.040256,0.043677,0.205151,0.287226,0.134083,0.042373,0.095115,0.016939,0.022598,0.079304]},{"p":"L08N8341-","l":"TWO-REL","j":"11","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the head noun of a 'modifier + collective noun' phrase that names a kind of organized body or group, such as 'Indian tribes', 'military departments', 'labor organization', 'professional associations', 'protection agencies' or 'TV network', typically in formal policy, legal or government prose that lists types of stakeholders or entities.","s":[0.508386,0.436266,0.561139,0.276136,0.331966,0.026267,0.222871,0.06844,0.047746,0.16346,0.065372,0.038555,0.036175,0.119351,0.004878]},{"p":"L08N9190+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on verbs and nouns of making, producing or preparing something, especially passive or nominal uses like \"is manufactured\", \"are prepared\", \"used in manufacturing\", \"purchased separately\", \"in development\", \"being planned\", \"is constructed\", \"show producers\"; it is strongest on \"manufacture/manufactured/manufacturing\" in product or technical descriptions.","s":[0.194372,0.438594,0.219248,0.378195,0.139619,0.209599,0.020692,0.13122,0.098486,0.027021,0.065312,0.093321,0.052817,0.000137,0.04972]},{"p":"L08N9190-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires inside origin/founding stories, on the words that tell how or when something began: the spark of an idea (\"on a whim\", \"inspiration struck\", \"the idea came to him\", \"a eureka moment\", \"spur-of-the-moment\", \"started in a dorm room/bedroom\", \"born as a casual conversation\") and the year digits right after \"founded/established in\".","s":[0.887527,0.356002,0.416109,0.496788,1.231337,0.01592,0.013137,0.036112,0.016355,0.022009,0.062029,0.020041,0.039086,0.027071,0.084153]},{"p":"L09N10255+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on advertising-industry vocabulary, above all the later pieces of ad-agency and PR-firm names (MullenLowe, Weber Shandwick, BBDO, Leo Burnett, Saatchi & Saatchi, Campbell Ewald, Mullen Lintas, Serviceplan) and ad-awards and credit terms (Cannes Lions, Wood Pencil, Creative director, Similar Campaigns, executions, creative idea), in text about ad campaigns, agencies and awards.","s":[0.759949,0.668681,0.754579,0.643221,0.700441,0.011763,0.013921,0.013598,0.023541,0.031567,0.00453,0.027874,0.004256,0.010749,0.006488]},{"p":"L09N10255-","l":"TWO-REL","j":"111","f":0,"e":0,"x":"In marketing, design, media and tourism writing, this pole fires on the word naming a design or creative-industry field inside a list or phrase, most strongly ' interior' in 'Graphic Design, Interior Design, Product Design' lists (also 'architecture', 'construction', 'arts education'); it also fires on 'Filed' at the very start of a blog's 'Filed Under:' line, and on the closing word-piece of brand names or author surnames in marketing and social-science citations.","s":[0.760139,0.432349,0.444859,0.771022,0.640394,0.022359,0.066223,0.012355,0.215781,0.024708,0.043439,0.061781,0.061981,0.032067,0.009346]},{"p":"L09N10681+","l":"UNREL","j":"00","f":0,"e":0,"x":"In software bug reports, mailing lists and chat, this fires on the word naming the latest development version of a codebase, right after a verb like test, install or compile or after git or cvs, as in \"git head\", \"cvs head\", \"compile master branch\", \"installed current\" or \"current rawhide\".","s":[0.572751,0.23785,0.580871,0.690502,0.542303,0.138603,0.135668,0.12669,0.081123,0.114866,0.110993,0.059584,0.056194,0.032214,0.061665],"u":{"0":[0.45962,0.132656,0.462489,0.527443,0.367156],"1":[0.555873,0.476511,0.482607,0.511165,0.301636]}},{"p":"L09N10681-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word naming an artificial intervention or stand-in applied to a system under study, most strongly test-double words in unit-test code (Python @patch decorators, new Mock<...>, jest.fn, Moq Setup lambdas), and also on experimental manipulations in science prose such as gene knockdown or knocking down, being infected, contaminated or exposed, removal, and reinstall.","s":[0.228493,0.356179,0.567111,0.21902,0.257825,0.035807,0.029372,0.033132,0.025552,0.134213,0.099128,0.083922,0.038757,0.015333,0.073999]},{"p":"L09N1264+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word that completes a fixed multiword phrase, chiefly \"free\" in \"(please) feel free to ...\" closing invitations, and also \"along\" in \"along with\", \"fact\" in \"despite/due to the fact that\", and \"regard\" in \"with regard to\", that is, the locked-in second word of a stock connective phrase whose continuation is nearly fixed.","s":[0.200033,0.083018,0.046968,0.027504,0.223377,0.049811,0.05283,0.046968,0.043389,0.066313,0.075632,0.022232,0.021386,0.024667,0.024785]},{"p":"L09N1264-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the first word of a new sentence that follows an already completed sentence in running prose, most strongly on the existential opener “There” (as in “There is/are…”) and the hortative “Let’s”, and also on other sentence starters such as “However”, “Notice” or a character’s name opening a paragraph.","s":[0.02605,0.047077,0.045634,0.026765,0.039751,0.02605,0.047077,0.045634,0.026765,0.031804,0.022014,0.016766,0.036693,0.144272,0.011553]},{"p":"L09N1456+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the first word or word-piece of a multi-word name or compound that is being used as a modifier in front of a further head noun, especially right after an article or possessive ('a Pi Day scavenger hunt', 'our Galaxy A53 review', 'an Eve Echoes ship list', 'a Phi Silica model card', 'composed of Medal of Honor recipients'), and on the 'Prize'/'Award' piece in hyphenated '-Prize-winning' or 'Grammy-Award-winning' adjectives.","s":[0.186037,0.041438,0.135602,0.728756,0.49921,0.035113,0.104793,0.035494,0.070132,0.041875,0.007201,0.074845,0.07783,0.012753,0.02164]},{"p":"L09N1456-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a brand or manufacturer name (often its first or second word-piece) when it is used as a modifier directly before a product or model name, typically right after \"The\" in product-description or review prose (e.g. \"The Logitech G600 mouse\", \"The Cooler Master MasterLiquid 360\", \"The Epson VS350W delivers\").","s":[0.389387,0.736992,0.460297,0.381357,0.428454,0.031095,0.015907,0.033166,0.09413,0.04126,0.026003,0.015937,0.020787,0.026728,0.05019]},{"p":"L09N2031+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the closing word-piece that completes a rare multi-token word or compound, most strongly the 'ung' of 'Hirschsprung' (as in 'Hirschsprung disease' or 'Hirschsprung's'); it also fires on similar completing pieces such as 'trous|er', 'Photochrom|ic', 'Antim|icrobial', 'terat|oma' and 'meral|gia', and on the head noun closing a product name ('Sports Tank', 'Composite Wall', 'Ocean|view').","s":[0.502495,0.584473,0.622783,0.708932,0.849072,0.116155,0.305448,0.077149,0.46603,0.485348,0.025562,0.084814,0.068326,0.028856,0.089533]},{"p":"L09N2031-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the token that completes a specific named variant or type within a fixed technical vocabulary, usually the second word or final word-piece of a term of art whose first part picks one option from a known set, such as the \"hung\" in double-hung or single-hung windows, the \"gee\" in ogee arch, the \"BC\" or \"CB\" in AES CBC or ECB mode, \"majority\" in simple majority, \"parity\" in even parity, \"sort\" in selection sort, \"cubic\" in kind='cubic', \"cups\" in D cups, \"warranty\" in special warranty deed, and \"ease\" in ease-in-out.","s":[0.385165,0.91958,1.062311,0.659245,0.403352,0.036054,0.286757,0.090766,0.039215,0.098417,0.038623,0.046824,0.057511,0.044118,0.016049]},{"p":"L09N7823+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the key word naming the fact or quality being proven, tested, measured or inferred, right after an evidence/verification frame, e.g. 'to show that you have read', 'make sure everything is working', 'test whether it is installed properly', 'a testament to its popularity', 'shows how seriously', 'if he truly cared', 'to measure the degree of injury', 'if there is a leak'.","s":[0.235482,0.256738,0.204412,0.25102,0.077396,0.01602,0.014776,7.1e-05,0.024105,0.011528,0.029133,0.03757,0.017916,0.027506,0.019995]},{"p":"L09N7823-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the key noun (or the completing word piece) naming deception, dishonest intent or trustworthiness in a passage about whether someone or something is genuine, such as motive, malice, deception, propaganda, cheating, a con job, a joke or hoax, being suckered, photoshopped or unfaithful, or credibility attributes like accuracy, intent or conflict of interest.","s":[0.461602,0.283657,0.72088,0.290758,0.482839,0.007527,0.075626,0.071255,0.015843,0.13468,0.063886,0.010408,0.080213,0.108018,0.04089]},{"p":"L09N8024+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the noun that names a service or provision a customer pays for, usually the second word of a stock commercial phrase, especially hospitality and service terms like room and board, lodging, accommodation, bed and breakfast, made to order, paid support, support contracts, extended warranty, custom builds, 24-hour desk service, shipping by DHL, UPS or TNT.","s":[0.462787,0.367699,0.691043,0.40974,0.595692,0.059469,0.029399,0.018269,0.008856,0.074081,0.060328,0.073516,0.031966,0.074831,0.043754]},{"p":"L09N8024-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires inside idioms of one party paying for another, on the payment word or the thing paid for: \"I'm buying\", \"let me buy you a drink\", \"my treat\", \"pick up the tab\", \"foot the bill\", \"buy a round\", \"shoulder the cost\", \"all-expenses-paid\", \"employer-paid\".","s":[0.849514,0.910064,0.607694,0.837378,0.672341,0.031763,0.003839,0.081711,0.224627,0.025179,0.02172,0.008356,0.009972,0.022409,0.011721]},{"p":"L09N8078+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on bare space or line-break tokens (the gap before the next word) inside noisy, degraded running text, above all OCR-scanned 1990s Amiga game-magazine pages full of character errors like 'ol', 'lor', 'Ihe', and also syllable-hyphenated song lyrics, timestamped transcripts and chat logs; it depends on a long stretch of such corrupted text and is hard to reproduce in a short standalone passage.","s":[0.051621,0.051154,0.206982,0.213454,0.06353,0.023772,0.054971,0.034187,0.269957,0.038697,0.022277,0.070374,0.057702,0.03246,0.049381]},{"p":"L09N8078-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the layout token that opens a new item or block in list-structured web text: the bullet ' •' or leading indentation that begins a later bullet or comment reply, a list number such as '3' or '17', or the colon/period plus blank line that introduces a list ('…include:↵↵ 1.'). It does not fire on the content words themselves.","s":[0.053966,0.01877,0.023695,0.076661,0.03177,0.015036,0.018641,0.013497,0.015857,0.017772,0.035436,0.01651,0.027081,0.020617,0.007929]},{"p":"L09N8242+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the head noun that closes a definite noun phrase introduced by \"the\" (often with an adjective in between), especially a plural group of people named as the beneficiary or object after a preposition, e.g. \"popular for the tourists\", \"aimed at the retail traders\", \"welfare of the Nigerian farmer\", \"for the modern designer\".","s":[0.178648,0.503258,0.747892,0.241231,0.291756,0.033651,0.098215,0.04682,0.108182,0.052244,0.01508,0.022414,0.040685,0.053334,0.233718]},{"p":"L09N8242-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the first token of a specific name or qualifier placed directly after a generic category noun with no article or 'of' in between, typically the opening piece of a rare multi-token proper name (translated/European style like 'Airport Weeze', 'Company Seagate', 'the plant Salzgitter', 'Tourism Village Pentingsari', 'station Boyarka'), and the code analog: the suffix glued onto a generic variable stem (idModal, list_task, dictionaryProduct, ctxP).","s":[0.2239,0.72038,0.238368,0.547061,0.191656,0.081784,0.021849,0.014676,0.017015,0.113505,0.016772,0.041993,0.021431,0.048472,0.024014]},{"p":"L10N10529+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the connective that introduces the evidence behind an inference (because, since, for, as, if, otherwise, else why) and the first words right after it, when the preceding clause is a deduction such as \"X must have…\", \"clearly X…\", \"I guess X…\" or \"things must be bad…\", as in \"He must have been tired, because he fell asleep at once.\"","s":[0.386735,0.485867,0.312617,0.784968,0.274096,0.00603,0.118592,0.004343,0.245274,0.008872,0.007109,0.013609,0.002861,0.003602,0.009764]},{"p":"L10N10529-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires inside a conclusion being drawn from evidence, on the inference word or the next few words after it (\"which means\", \"meaning that you have no idea\", \"therefore\", \"so why\", \"this would indicate\", \"evidence that\", \"goes to show\", \"then you obviously haven't read\"), strongest in argumentative or sarcastic put-downs where the deduction is that someone lacks understanding, ability or sense.","s":[1.034468,0.080254,0.168569,0.100158,0.70846,0.067555,0.028308,0.02958,-0.000247,0.037751,0.024078,0.01074,0.038261,0.081247,0.062048]},{"p":"L10N10702+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires in explanatory prose on words that limit or qualify the scope of a claim, typically the word just before the phrase naming who or what it applies to: 'pertain only to', 'mainly suitable for', 'most appropriate for', 'geared mostly toward', 'attributable to', 'merely from', 'the only one that', 'Not coincidentally', 'not a coincidence', or a restricted subclass noun such as 'Simpler games' or 'Deep water fish'.","s":[0.078718,0.451262,0.098663,0.402189,0.358958,0.091405,0.055145,0.089193,0.029919,0.167021,0.125029,0.054067,0.051286,0.094907,0.068969]},{"p":"L10N10702-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires inside statements that carve out a narrow exception or the few remaining cases to a general rule, such as 'the only proprietary software I use is', 'what I still need Windows for:', 'in some extreme edge cases', 'a niche product', 'for special occasions' or 'the strange and solitary exception'; it peaks on the word just before the exceptional item is named (is, are, for, in, than) or on the head noun itself (cases, exceptions, occasions, scenarios).","s":[0.702189,0.501619,0.16393,0.249541,0.659327,0.037472,0.004195,0.056353,0.016157,0.047932,0.033684,0.025458,0.019201,0.023103,0.020218]},{"p":"L10N1636+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the sentence- or clause-ending punctuation (period, comma, ellipsis), or on the last word of a set phrase like 'teach her a lesson' or 'in cold blood', that closes a vivid narrative sentence in which a character physically acts on someone or something (sprinkles, presses, licks, pulls someone into a lap, plunges a hand into a drain), typically in fiction, scripts or play-by-play.","s":[0.291046,0.33423,0.258391,0.277883,0.266115,0.05804,0.046852,0.065883,0.050259,0.088545,0.104936,0.193971,0.071078,0.138191,0.107711]},{"p":"L10N1636-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the last word of a noun phrase that is the target or object of a hostile or physical action, most strongly the head noun in military phrases like \"fought U.S. forces\", \"attacks against Coalition forces\", \"infiltrated British lines\", or \"fired upon Dutch peacekeepers\", and more weakly on a body part being touched or grabbed (\"curled round his legs\", \"clung to his arms\", \"pushed my hands away\").","s":[0.114987,0.177392,0.087271,0.235984,0.262082,0.069693,0.052749,0.060573,0.048814,0.063657,0.023738,0.051867,0.03208,0.053715,0.025707]},{"p":"L10N3963+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"In biomedical research-paper prose describing an experiment, fires on words in the methods/results slot right after the experimental subjects are named (e.g. 'mice were randomized into five groups', 'model flies', 'cells exposed to', 'siRNA-treated', 'representative images of', 'between groups'), i.e. tokens describing how subjects, groups or samples were assigned, treated or imaged.","s":[0.645187,0.348928,0.79715,0.406779,0.916895,0.040066,0.04081,0.033906,0.010965,0.076107,0.007435,0.017439,0.029256,0.025768,0.00548]},{"p":"L10N3963-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Inside dense natural-science research-paper prose (methods, results, abstracts or reference lists in chemistry, biomedicine, EEG or soil science), this fires on content words and word pieces of technical noun phrases, especially a noun or its first fragment that completes a predictable specialist term, such as 'bands' after 'Theta and Alpha EEG', the 'ot' of 'nanotubes', the 'umb' of 'umbilical', or 'alkal' in 'in alkaline media'.","s":[0.317189,0.324228,0.12682,0.274023,0.310713,0.047074,0.02159,0.088702,0.018983,0.030912,0.040296,0.036205,0.020823,0.027406,0.08713]},{"p":"L10N4041+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the name of a set/sequence operation, usually inside code and above all in SQL window functions: the second piece of a function or keyword such as ROW_NUMBER, dense_rank, rownum, ROWNUM, NTILE, first_value, cumsum, DefaultIfEmpty, distinct(), or a word like rank, unique list, maximum, closure or midpoints that names an ordering, ranking or aggregate operation over a collection.","s":[1.027354,0.766905,0.433074,1.049615,0.988659,0.240345,0.184246,0.311204,0.155757,0.006598,0.039744,0.019931,0.026416,0.037266,0.018728]},{"p":"L10N4041-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"In code, code comments or step-by-step algorithm descriptions, fires on the 'to' or the infinitive verb in a phrase naming a set of data items still awaiting an operation (\"no files to process\", \"clips to evaluate\", \"rows_to_geocode\", \"filesToParse\", \"searchToUse\"), and more weakly on data-handling verbs such as un-marsh-aling or parse_single_example.","s":[0.671081,0.554706,0.601717,0.447834,0.249264,0.138368,0.357671,0.261698,0.333658,0.026119,0.014473,0.078999,0.107902,0.035889,0.041916]},{"p":"L10N4224+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a token that completes a highly predictable fixed expression or multi-piece word, i.e. the second half that the preceding piece nearly forces: the word-piece that finishes a split word (Trom|bones, wheelch|airs, Gala|Soft, Free|BASIC, mass spect|rometer, Not|icias), or the final word of a stock phrase (Britain and France; the US, Russia, and China; under the impression; the Mandate of Heaven; 中文 after 中).","s":[0.350771,0.231946,0.519712,0.089743,0.451886,0.111938,0.041676,0.093365,0.061376,0.021709,0.076007,0.126276,0.050513,0.029971,0.108879]},{"p":"L10N4224-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word that completes a familiar, often loaded set phrase or label for a contested position that the writer is framing or disputing, such as the second word of \"political correctness\", \"young earth\", \"carbon neutral\", \"abstinence(-only)\", \"avoid at all costs\" or \"must be avoided\", especially in opinionated argumentative prose.","s":[0.383164,0.175127,0.338544,0.484911,0.3994,0.222405,0.170669,0.165928,0.056937,0.145743,0.012488,0.041414,0.060492,0.043083,0.047573]},{"p":"L10N5687+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires at the boundary right after a sentence or formula (its final period, newline, or a spintax \"|\") when the text that follows will restate the same content in another form, as in bilingual English-then-Chinese or Bengali sentence pairs, spun-article {version A.|version B} alternatives, or a Unicode formula followed by its LaTeX source.","s":[0.660123,0.292795,0.035494,0.149477,0.280594,0.017508,0.003941,0.009704,0.011628,0.002126,0.013661,0.0035,0.019236,0.018102,0.011097]},{"p":"L10N5687-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the token that closes a short standalone label line just before a line break, most strongly on an image file extension such as \".jpg\"/\".png\" ending a filename caption, and also on the \"?\" or double line break that ends a section heading or FAQ-style question heading before the body text starts.","s":[0.052033,0.041989,0.079562,0.045707,0.037999,0.069025,0.071341,0.047456,0.081682,0.084006,0.038315,0.074069,0.070981,0.02498,0.018286]},{"p":"L10N9052+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"In news or radio-journalism text, fires on the word that completes a media credit or attribution phrase, i.e. the role or outlet word naming who reported, photographed or published something, such as \"Staff Photographer\" in a photo caption, \"NPR science correspondent\", \"according to the San Francisco Chronicle\", \"Live Science sister site\", \"Times writer\", or \"Transcript provided by NPR, Copyright NPR\".","s":[0.621045,0.510303,0.356006,0.465169,0.312559,0.238463,0.150975,0.007254,0.019591,0.045308,0.068018,0.035445,0.059916,0.048294,0.032737]},{"p":"L10N9052-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"In news-article prose, fires on the token right after a newly introduced person's name or title, usually a new quoted source (expert, analyst, spokesperson, resident) being brought in, such as the comma or role word after the name, the end of a surname, or the verb that follows it ('Natasha Williams, a Toronto-based psychologist, said'; 'Dave Harvey, 73, says'; 'Kathy Simmons applauded').","s":[0.451922,0.206435,0.668498,0.427366,0.225665,0.029438,0.091862,0.048353,0.029642,0.198497,0.026168,0.02528,0.016245,0.009757,0.017786]},{"p":"L10N9100+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a token that completes a word or set phrase already almost fully determined by what precedes it, such as the closing word-piece of a split word (thought-prov|oking, mind-bogg|ling, smart|watch, wear|ables), the second half of a fixed pair or idiom (right and wrong, guys and gals, day-to-day, dollars and twenty-one cents), or a repeat of a name or title already given earlier in the text.","s":[0.470131,0.189235,0.167137,0.056167,0.242553,0.297403,0.168841,0.178986,0.08046,0.073927,0.068181,0.154702,0.225273,0.065062,0.138752]},{"p":"L10N9100-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word that opens a post-noun modifier or elaboration right after a noun phrase in the middle of a long expository sentence, most strongly 'which' after a comma, 'as' in 'such as', 'like', or 'including' introducing examples, and participles such as 'focusing' or 'set in' that hang off the preceding noun.","s":[0.040955,0.063595,0.083703,0.165673,0.098885,0.047947,0.066194,0.039582,0.201999,0.191444,0.036471,0.099389,0.29366,0.049693,0.070648]},{"p":"L10N9794+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the HTTP/CRUD verb that names a web API route or handler in server code, most strongly the \".get\" in Express-style \"router.get('/', (req, res) =>\" or \"app.get(...)\", and also on \"get\", \"delete\", \"GET\", \"DELETE\", or \"read\" starting a handler name or comment in a REST controller (e.g. \"// GET api/messages\", \"getAll: (req, res)\").","s":[0.616485,0.487977,0.86471,0.42531,0.784825,0.263404,0.292303,0.003834,0.128373,0.006668,0.013022,0.017592,0.004943,8e-06,0.020928]},{"p":"L10N9794-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires inside source code files (C#, JavaScript, Python), most strongly on the names and keywords that declare structure near the top of a file, such as the word namespace after a block of using lines, class and function names, import targets and the later pieces of camelCase identifiers (defineRe|active, Watch|er, generic|Item), rather than on ordinary English prose.","s":[0.39799,0.209155,0.140098,0.247399,0.720149,0.050786,0.040884,0.020833,0.000846,0.018292,0.061333,0.037848,0.050353,0.021579,0.019623]},{"p":"L11N10245+","l":"TWO-REL","j":"11","f":0,"e":0,"x":"In code that loads or decodes input data, fires on the token naming the input source or read/parse step (e.g. sys.stdin, .readlines()/.splitlines(), np.loadtxt, gzip.open, TFRecordReader, Deserialize, FromBase64String, decrypt(ciphertext)), with a weaker prose echo on words for recovering or reconstructing old material (restored, reconstructed, pulled the data off the tapes).","s":[0.723644,0.655982,0.488521,0.426143,0.409932,0.069774,0.170955,0.005778,0.178874,0.079795,0.021112,0.005578,0.020057,0.002729,0.015142]},{"p":"L11N10245-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the object or verb of close reading for hidden meaning (interpreting, scrutinizing or reading signs into something), such as \"for\" in \"scour his statements for\", \"tea leaves\", \"dissect every nuance\", \"exegesis\", \"hermeneutics\", \"the Scriptures\", \"textual analysis\", \"hidden meanings\", \"omens in everything\", where the context is about searching a text or a sign for what it implies.","s":[0.50491,0.916494,0.527872,0.697791,0.645549,0.030224,0.03264,0.016782,0.052425,0.01405,0.008029,0.017294,0.012962,0.013512,0.020736]},{"p":"L11N1636+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"In source code (C#, Python, JavaScript, SQL), fires at the point where a value is about to be assigned: on the ' =' or ':' after a property, field or variable name (e.g. 'DataContext = this', 'rootNode.Tag = info', 'data: \"...\"'), or on a keyword-argument name glued to '=' (default=, json=, value=, yerr=, tzinfo=), i.e. the token right before the assigned value.","s":[0.802746,0.256237,0.260497,0.080774,0.147249,0.040429,0.031259,0.148246,0.111573,0.023872,0.023692,0.015599,0.065078,0.029791,0.035657]},{"p":"L11N1636-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"In C# source code (using System; namespaces, classes, methods), this fires on the keyword \"return\" or an assignment \"=\" / \"=>\" that comes right before the value expression of a statement, i.e. the token after which a returned or assigned value (a variable, \"new X(...)\", a member access) is about to follow.","s":[0.145064,0.448318,0.338544,0.036369,0.05185,0.050865,0.087761,0.039251,0.024541,0.009061,0.017729,0.032442,0.023373,0.050688,0.053163]},{"p":"L11N2927+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a highly predictable completion token, the next word or word-piece of a fixed collocation, compound or familiar list, especially colour names inside a list or code string (\"red, green and blue\", \"fillStyle = 'yellow'\"), plus completions like \"man, woman and child\", \"chocolate chip\", \"3 mins to read\", \"LED-backlit\" and \".com\" in a URL.","s":[0.620688,0.592903,0.560872,0.285975,0.139733,0.361629,0.259596,0.110102,0.144282,0.118054,0.182602,0.078161,0.179523,0.095987,0.072477]},{"p":"L11N2927-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the completing word of a short website-chrome or navigation label at the very top of a scraped web page, such as \"menu\" in \"Open menu Close menu\", \"irected\" in \"(Redirected from X)\", \"date\" in \"Publish date:\", \"toolbar\" in \"Close toolbar\", \"post\" in \"Share post:\", \"Later\" in \"Read Later\" or \"Topics\" in \"Related Topics:\"; the same words used in ordinary prose do not fire it.","s":[0.326365,0.406591,0.437369,0.24799,0.412426,0.033166,0.039109,0.064826,0.043355,0.037631,0.018645,0.019,0.020469,0.105554,0.074522]},{"p":"L11N445+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the noun naming a stimulus or causal agent that provokes a response in a living body or mind, especially in sentences about what triggers a reaction, such as endotoxin, LPS or viral proteins triggering inflammation, rewards or reinforcers driving behavior, or an influence, presence or effect acting on someone; it often fires on the middle piece of such a word.","s":[0.748837,0.973774,0.358269,0.384104,0.301012,0.038995,0.019318,0.047115,0.040625,0.017923,0.020854,0.038929,0.050998,0.037197,0.024602]},{"p":"L11N445-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on words that frame a desired outcome, end-state or causal effect, most strongly in the phrase \"what winning / victory / finished / success looks like\" (on the outcome noun, \"looks\" or \"like\"), and also on causal-result words such as \"causes\", \"leads to\", \"contributes to\", \"predicts\", adverse \"effects\", and legal \"remedy\" or \"relief\".","s":[0.278758,0.34019,0.13646,0.192592,0.052393,0.064601,0.110003,0.037395,0.04712,0.125019,0.057558,0.055367,0.066669,0.055281,0.068371]},{"p":"L11N4690+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the second, head piece of a familiar two-part compound or fixed phrase, right after its modifier, when context makes it predictable: 'main' then 'boss', 'hand' or 'canon'; 'drag' then 'strip'; 'follow' then '-up'; 'release' then '-blocking'; 'first' then 'referee'; 'original' then 'game'; and repeated code accessors like '.strip()', '.val()' and 'req.body'.","s":[0.268487,0.07195,0.059948,0.423275,0.182549,0.036034,0.156983,0.064833,0.484027,0.073916,0.168611,0.042961,0.058116,0.050816,0.052749]},{"p":"L11N4690-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word that names a thing in its original or reference form inside phrases like \"as written\", \"as printed\", \"as delivered\", \"as shipped\", \"as posted\", \"the recipe as written\", \"straight out of the tube\", or on the source noun in \"shown in the book / in the text / in class / in the article / included in the price\", meaning \"in its stated, unmodified, original version\".","s":[0.298106,0.392415,0.441242,0.816235,0.398005,0.107121,0.10161,0.013414,0.053452,0.093557,0.006336,0.008586,0.011445,0.018685,0.010575]},{"p":"L11N4871+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word or word-piece that completes a highly predictable fixed phrase or multi-token word in running prose, i.e. the expected second half of a collocation such as 'blog entry', 'in line with', 'a duty of care', 'eating habits', 'good deeds', 'Breaking Bad', 'impediments', or 'undoubtedly'.","s":[0.135428,0.130385,0.207069,0.238875,0.24665,0.235933,0.118557,0.111182,0.058161,0.115422,0.024009,0.046804,0.02167,0.023665,0.037604]},{"p":"L11N4871-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the content word that heads a predicate inside an embedded or reported clause (after words like that, how, know, realise, describes) when that word demands a complement next, especially abstract nouns and adjectives such as nature, importance, affinity, susceptibility, involvement, concerned, attached, likely, or needed, which are followed by of, to, for, or with.","s":[0.096866,0.12368,0.096127,0.059131,0.266416,0.068861,0.012582,0.000203,0.083179,0.053875,0.015978,0.019241,0.011416,0.018602,0.025314]},{"p":"L11N5928+","l":"UNK","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a noun or identifier piece that acts as the first half of a compound and needs a following head word, especially in code or technical prose, such as 'domain' in 'domain portfolio', 'concert' in 'concert season', 'file' in 'file name', 'node' in 'node list', 'parent' in 'parent tree', '.getElementById' or ',input' in calls, and 'Total Episode Count'.","s":[0.075844,0.029289,0.0859,0.213825,0.20933,0.03891,0.035817,0.031626,0.030143,0.025843,0.05107,0.038537,0.031988,0.022428,0.022778]},{"p":"L11N5928-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the head noun (or its final word-piece) that completes a part-whole or location phrase, the container or whole being referred to, typically right after \"the rest of the\", \"most of the\", \"in a\", \"within the\", \"across the\" or \"throughout the\" (e.g. \"the western part of the base\", \"missed a lot of the film\", \"spread throughout the facility\", \"the remainder of the tract\").","s":[0.454898,0.488126,0.175049,0.115085,0.156006,0.03855,0.024154,0.009398,0.018403,0.061735,0.010162,-0.001324,0.041479,0.024044,0.023949]},{"p":"L11N7229+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the function word or operator that sits directly before a small count number, mostly \"of\" in phrases like \"party of\", \"groups of\", \"pack of\", \"out of\", \"father-of\" and \"par of\", and also \"to\" in \"rose/increased to\" or \"!=\"/\"==\" before a small integer in code, i.e. the token right before a small number is about to be written.","s":[1.116758,0.492849,0.049283,0.148801,0.568958,0.150962,0.013759,0.012425,0.012451,0.374866,0.016567,0.041537,0.018186,0.027274,0.028155]},{"p":"L11N7229-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word just before a specific number, date or time limit is stated, i.e. the end of a limit or bound phrase whose next token is a numeral or date: \"up to\", \"less than\", \"no later than\", \"on or before\", \"at least\", \"by\", \"maximum value\", \"deadline\", \"Posted:\", \"Wed,\" (the token right before the figure or date, not the figure itself).","s":[0.679543,0.662182,0.166869,0.415627,0.366793,0.011098,0.031305,0.041924,0.041028,0.03828,0.084103,0.042408,0.034124,0.05322,0.006653]},{"p":"L11N7616+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires inside angry, scornful or critical opinion prose (rants, complaints, harsh reviews, political attacks), most strongly on the word or word-ending that delivers the put-down, such as the last piece of 'creeps', 'flaccid', 'vacuous', 'ineffectual' or 'stupider'.","s":[0.190896,0.577759,0.078611,0.770257,0.177514,0.07,0.037971,0.022498,0.085811,0.114811,0.105714,0.02608,0.038965,0.078195,0.056162]},{"p":"L11N7616-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word that names or completes an act of evading or defying a controlling authority or restriction, such as rebellion, resistance, a black market, piracy, cracking or circumventing copy protection, a workaround to a ban, doing something in secret, or escaping, especially right after the restriction has been described (\"driving people to seek alternative methods\", \"played it in secret\", \"secret rebellions\", \"unsanctioned software\").","s":[1.159247,1.022224,0.336761,1.147891,0.472775,0.231173,0.13217,0.018954,0.047073,0.013002,0.013642,0.035837,0.062312,0.014725,0.025479]},{"p":"L11N8963+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word that names the broad place, realm or scope in which something exists or applies, usually the head of a locative phrase: \"other European countries\", \"other parts of the world\", \"all over the world\", \"back to earth\", \"enlightened lands\", \"elsewhere\", \"nationally\", \"the global average\", \"the Central Government\", \"any world port\", and in biomedical text the body compartment \"peripheral blood\" or \"circulating\" cells.","s":[0.307964,0.16411,0.063163,0.685377,0.513884,0.146908,0.051795,0.039009,0.390813,0.067791,0.030305,0.019488,0.060298,0.320251,0.092757]},{"p":"L11N8963-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a place name or word that marks a remote point of origin or destination, the faraway home or source an outsider, immigrant, company or traveler comes from or returns to, as opposed to the local setting (e.g. \"supplied from England\", \"go back to England\", \"moved to Las Vegas from Milwaukee\", \"foreign NGOs\", \"from all over\", \"overseas tourists\", \"Texas-based\"), strongest on the token right after \"from\" or \"back to\".","s":[0.32817,0.038297,0.15442,0.535681,0.279937,0.041078,0.021622,0.148024,0.040133,0.0376,0.021751,0.015133,0.005041,0.003798,0.057466]},{"p":"L12N1003+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a token made of multiple consecutive line breaks (a blank-line run like \"\\n\\n\\n\", often fused with a preceding period, semicolon, brace or indentation) that separates major blocks — in code between header/imports and body, between SQL/PL-SQL statement blocks, between class members, or in prose where a section ends and a heading or footnote list begins.","s":[0.196546,0.172242,0.278213,0.463081,0.182551,0.007677,0.019108,0.014755,0.023677,0.021038,0.031889,0.016416,0.008461,0.015118,0.011378]},{"p":"L12N1003-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the auxiliary or modal verb (can, will, does, do, is) right after a sentence-initial pronoun subject \"It\" or \"They\" that refers back to a thing just described, especially when the next word is \"also\", \"not\" or \"however\" and the sentence adds a further point about that thing in expository product-review or explainer prose.","s":[0.148994,0.501743,0.255793,0.23828,0.174175,0.032617,0.038944,0.035915,0.009317,0.037317,0.043493,0.024684,0.052771,0.043777,0.029755]},{"p":"L12N10223+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a word or word-piece that repeats or completes something already set up earlier in the same text, so that the token is predictable by copying from context. Examples are a recipe step naming an ingredient from the ingredient list above (thyme, mozzarella, garlic, spring onions), code reusing a variable or list item defined above, or an enumeration continuing a phrase introduced earlier (\"the first sub-research question\", \"the first of the house museums\").","s":[0.05417,0.026326,0.086718,0.028497,0.027885,0.004713,0.022536,0.022265,0.023207,0.014372,0.013168,0.04379,0.02244,0.044388,0.013]},{"p":"L12N10223-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the second word of stock web-page boilerplate labels that follow the end of a content block, such as the 'Post' in 'Read Full Post »', 'More' in 'Read More' or '阅读更多', 'apply' in 'Easily apply' under a job listing, 'by' in 'Liked by 1 person' under a blog comment, '4 answers' after a forum question, and field labels like 'Tipo:' or 'Issue:' that restart a repeated record; it also fires on predictable repeats in code, like a second 'Board.Player.One'.","s":[0.200538,0.018461,0.07513,0.019297,0.127314,0.036125,0.018461,0.015309,0.02307,0.039921,0.016869,0.024355,0.00874,0.033424,0.018029]},{"p":"L12N1642+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"In academic reference or author lists, this fires on the token that completes a person's name: the initials right after a surname in Vancouver-style lists (\"Clarke CE\", \"Bill RM\", \"Hall D.G.\") or the final word-piece of an unusual multi-token surname (\"Mel-zer\", \"Coff-ield\", \"Kirkbr-ide\", \"Maff-ia\"), and more weakly on the last word of a journal or venue title (\"Community Health\", \"Time & Society\").","s":[0.082604,1.196198,0.116683,0.473882,0.500835,0.166718,0.100825,0.043116,0.161068,0.060567,0.041925,0.05741,0.012742,0.035367,0.08137]},{"p":"L12N1642-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on place names, or the second piece of compound place names, that read as famous American locations (Boston, Portland, Springfield, Columbus, Georgia, Oak Grove, Rose Bowl, Big Bear, Belmont, -ville, -dale, Valley), and on the words US or America, mostly when they appear in British or Australian prose such as UK business listings and addresses.","s":[0.062108,0.276891,0.041015,0.431849,0.166281,0.059118,0.102422,0.000221,0.181191,0.045003,0.003864,0.025611,0.006229,0.013868,0.011135]},{"p":"L12N2948+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a word for making or coming into being (formation, synthase/synthetase, -genesis, produced, generated, created, formed, -making, induction) used right after the thing being made, as in \"aziridine formation\", \"ATP synthase\", \"foam cell formation\", \"fat-making\" or \"alanine is produced\", mostly in scientific or biomedical prose; weaker offshoots are compound-final heads such as \"diabetes risk\", \"Washington-bound\" and \"semis berth\".","s":[0.8469,0.692732,0.326077,0.448505,0.151082,0.016225,0.082165,0.022681,0.00936,0.012423,0.02738,0.025112,0.012428,0.011029,0.022894]},{"p":"L12N2948-","l":"TWO-REL","j":"11","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the connector between a source and a target in a conversion or transformation, such as the \"to\" in \"HEX to DEC\", \"AC to DC\" or \"PDF to JPG\", the \"To\" or \"2\" in function names like hexToRGB or gray2rgb, arrows like \"=>\" and \"→\", and \"converted\" or \"into\" in \"made X into Y\"; a weaker related offshoot fires on words saying a time period ends, as in \"the season is drawing to a close\".","s":[0.31725,0.86112,0.225643,0.033029,0.119938,0.097459,0.057721,0.013512,0.033029,0.015507,0.007774,0.024665,0.046135,0.010682,0.019662]},{"p":"L12N5447+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the final word that completes a short, highly predictable stock phrase or idiom in casual prose, usually a punchy aside right before punctuation, such as \"there is a catch\", \"and it shows\", \"see the pattern?\", \"What a day\", \"I can't stand it anymore\", \"Why me?\", \"Challenge accepted\" or \"a few tricks up its sleeve\".","s":[0.748074,0.453575,0.358111,0.299592,0.393086,0.022645,0.129959,0.029445,0.041516,0.009596,0.096528,0.078315,0.070042,0.057129,0.023235]},{"p":"L12N5447-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the generic noun or adverb that closes a short locative phrase after a noun, the 'in the body / in life / out there / in here / in the game / in the world / in the country' tail, where a noun is placed inside its obvious default container (body, life, world, game, car, campus, market, league, day), or a bare 'in there / out there / in here' follows.","s":[0.459523,0.24917,0.095793,0.149833,0.23301,0.011511,0.005162,0.091231,0.058775,0.034062,0.022841,0.005089,0.030537,0.007733,0.008525]},{"p":"L12N5641+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the token right before (or early inside) a phrase that is about to be repeated verbatim from just earlier in the text, i.e. where the model expects to copy a recent span: the comma in “q345 steel plate, q345 steel plate suppliers”, the line break between a caption or title and its duplicate, “Any” in “Pick a Cloud, Any Cloud”, “To” in “To Helmet or Not To Helmet”, and “like” in “no business like show business”.","s":[0.017507,0.327322,0.248236,0.530698,0.021707,0.013318,0.129265,0.024967,0.031116,0.021707,0.01934,0.027663,0.017214,0.020236,0.013477]},{"p":"L12N5641-","l":"UNREL","j":"00","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word that completes an immediate repetition of a short phrase, especially in songs, hymns, chants or emotional speech, such as the second 'glory' in 'All glory, all glory', the second 'hail' in 'all hail, all hail', the second 'Ali' in 'Prince Ali! Prince Ali!' or 'burned' in 'It burned, oh God it burned!'.","s":[0.424554,0.399041,0.328735,0.157134,0.843351,0.080382,0.027387,0.034328,0.033223,0.057276,0.04149,0.014986,0.045107,0.014765,0.026781],"u":{"0":[0.530629,0.200649,0.083226,0.299705,0.263017],"1":[0.183708,0.781356,0.226589,0.382355,0.264617]}},{"p":"L12N727+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word that completes a grudging or qualified positive verdict (is allowed, wasn't so bad, was ok, is possible, has a chance, slight upside, did end up enjoying it, at least, slightly pleased) inside an otherwise critical, negative or restrictive passage, typically right before a 'but' or 'however' that limits the concession.","s":[0.080435,0.238319,0.033226,0.154443,0.2566,0.142529,0.129776,0.070818,0.092066,0.124367,0.011119,0.054059,0.068306,0.043271,0.023122]},{"p":"L12N727-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word that completes a reassuring, concession-accepting phrase after a mentioned downside — e.g. \"and that's alright\", \"5th isn't too bad\", \"you get used to it\", \"no worries\", \"not to worry\", \"it's all good\", \"no big deal\", \"no bad thing\", \"I didn't mind\", \"makes sense\", \"it'll be ok\" — in casual review or personal prose; the peak is on the final word of the phrase (alright/bad/used/worries/fine/mind) or the punctuation right after it.","s":[0.381748,0.133803,0.272674,0.345848,0.362643,0.030125,0.012418,0.023288,0.02048,0.044773,0.011252,0.027941,0.040949,0.020016,0.024926]},{"p":"L12N8337+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the sentence-ending period in bland, generic SEO-style or spun web article prose (how-to guides, product explainers, lottery/casino promos, relationship advice, home decor), especially after topic-sentence or filler sentences like \"Here are some tips to ensure safe use.\" or \"It can also be a social activity.\". It is weak on periods in news, fiction, dialogue or technical writing.","s":[0.126449,0.025289,0.044325,0.079089,0.163942,0.041623,0.039065,0.032801,0.031702,0.026439,0.017869,0.049151,0.087635,0.034941,0.038824]},{"p":"L12N8337-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the final word (or closing punctuation) of a paragraph-ending sentence, the last token right before a period and paragraph break, especially the predictable closing noun of a stock phrase in an answer paragraph of Q&A/FAQ-style web text (e.g. \"meets users' needs and expectations\", \"at hand\", \"for good measure\", \"a diverse consumer base\", \"your entire life\").","s":[0.022411,0.020367,0.018963,0.018859,0.0128,0.048707,0.020367,0.018963,0.018859,0.011857,0.014875,0.011107,0.014477,0.03084,0.016422]},{"p":"L12N9261+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the second half of a person's name (the surname, or the later word-piece of a split name or username) when it appears as an author, byline or credit, such as 'By Donal Skehan', 'Written by Felix Salmon', an 'About the Author' block, or a commenter's handle heading a post, most strongly on rare names split into several tokens.","s":[0.347116,0.393579,0.083545,0.098241,0.118878,0.031231,0.062801,0.06783,0.062849,0.044048,0.08595,0.003794,0.056486,0.078896,0.02885]},{"p":"L12N9261-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires where a text points to one of its own authors or contributors as an individual: the author's initials in parentheses or as an attribution (\"a single rater (TLP)\", \"S.R. Bryant, unpublished data\", \"I (Kirsten)\", \"one of the authors, Dr. Lamb\", \"Interviews were conducted by the first author\"). It is strongest on the initials or name token, or the word right before it, typically in the methods or acknowledgements of a research paper or in a signed post.","s":[1.075295,0.987777,1.008543,0.307552,0.37229,0.588082,0.076075,0.027442,0.06749,0.049956,0.013666,0.00958,0.032662,0.011631,0.021391]},{"p":"L12N9888+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the words that complete an inclusive 'whether X or Y / no matter / regardless' contrast, especially ones sorting beings by kind, such as 'whether on two legs or four', 'four-legged or two-legged creature', 'inside or outside the womb', 'carbon-based life forms', 'non-sentient code', 'seven humans and one dog', and 'no matter your age'.","s":[0.645416,0.613372,0.526719,0.71656,0.287001,0.008406,0.027097,0.025628,0.004116,0.01436,0.026588,0.054908,0.018557,0.035184,0.0318]},{"p":"L12N9888-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the noun in a phrase that generalizes across kinds or alternatives, right after a range quantifier such as \"any type of\", \"all\", \"both\", \"whatever\", \"various\", \"different\" or \"a wide range of\" (e.g. \"at all levels\", \"various career stages\", \"different age groups\", \"whatever the source\", \"any type of fuel\"), and also on the second term of an \"X or Y\" contrast (\"as an adult or child\").","s":[0.145155,0.049374,0.201254,0.081065,0.106671,0.045957,0.086138,0.071929,0.061988,0.012789,0.021417,0.03454,0.087851,0.054938,0.029588]},{"p":"L13N1380+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the verb or object right after a party is said to require information from someone, such as \"they need to see\", \"we need to know\", \"must be kept informed\", \"give me more information\", \"answer questions\", \"the details we need\", \"must receive a deposit\", typically in service, FAQ or terms text about what a company or person needs you to provide.","s":[0.457011,0.967213,0.48137,0.179042,0.640296,0.181754,0.009415,0.023216,0.005416,0.013227,0.024766,0.032655,0.025304,0.005456,0.032776]},{"p":"L13N1380-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a verb (or the word right after a verb) of giving or communicating something to another party, such as send, show, tell, grant, offer, provide, promise, explain or serve, especially in exchange or obligation framings where a recipient like \"you\" or \"me\" usually comes next (\"so they can send you a bill\", \"he told me to\", \"in exchange for\").","s":[0.173814,0.071791,0.069891,0.077698,0.083953,0.099007,0.033998,0.033911,0.01217,0.023202,0.01766,0.025078,0.014969,0.029822,0.016479]},{"p":"L13N2306+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word right after \"and\"/\"or\" (or on the conjunction itself) in a shared-object coordination where the first half ends on a dangling preposition whose object is postponed, as in \"contact with and exposure to the locals\", \"born to and raised by\", \"objection to or refusal to submit\", \"independent from, and never influenced by\".","s":[0.24311,0.45803,0.265232,0.571767,0.429242,0.003892,0.001652,0.004093,0.007539,0.071877,0.012495,0.008293,0.006749,0.01127,0.00805]},{"p":"L13N2306-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the first word (usually a preposition like in, on, to, by, as, for, or a date) of a phrase that interrupts between a verb or action noun and its delayed object or of-complement, as in \"the hiring in 1963 of a librarian\", \"the murder in 1979 of a contractor\", or \"forwards to the State Department all applications\" (heavy-object shift), not on ordinary prepositional phrases in normal order.","s":[0.063027,0.024927,0.038032,0.056228,0.097215,0.051423,0.020404,0.050298,0.037439,0.067692,0.051719,0.050151,0.031065,0.021153,0.064755]},{"p":"L13N2406+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the action word or small quantity noun in a practical how-to instruction or tip, especially in recipes and tech fixes (\"add a dash of\", \"drizzle\", \"a splash of\", \"hit refresh\", \"to request the\", \"just pause them\", \"drain out the liquid\"), i.e. the verb or measure word that tells the reader what to physically do next.","s":[0.585985,0.578054,0.362012,0.123156,0.316004,0.051564,0.06137,0.032228,0.102996,0.100656,0.103572,0.045461,0.092008,0.030046,0.041076]},{"p":"L13N2406-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires inside practical how-to or home-remedy advice at the word naming a homemade preparation or a do-it-yourself method made from common household ingredients, such as 'mixture', 'solution', 'mix', 'mask', 'remedies', 'recipes', 'made' or 'vinegar' in phrases like 'rinse with a vinegar solution', 'a mixture of sugar and honey' or 'home remedies' (the model is anticipating the DIY / homemade idea).","s":[0.568419,0.80033,1.028704,0.974939,0.622494,0.040803,0.192935,0.028814,0.012852,0.028032,0.022777,0.021042,0.03011,0.019645,0.009158]},{"p":"L13N2486+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"In source code, fires on a local variable name at the moment it is re-used as a function argument or array index (inside parentheses or square brackets, e.g. emit('error', error), list.Add(word), arr[ok]) shortly after that same variable was assigned a line or two earlier, i.e. copying a just-defined identifier into a call.","s":[0.112894,0.126539,0.200457,0.108076,0.294481,0.025192,0.030622,0.029936,0.031943,0.007619,0.041143,0.019488,0.018348,0.01746,0.004495]},{"p":"L13N2486-","l":"TWO-REL","j":"11","f":0,"e":0,"x":"In source code (C#, Python, JS, etc.), fires on the last piece of a previously declared variable or identifier name when it is reused, especially as an argument inside a function call, such as stream.Read(buffer, 0, buffer.Length), new StringTokenizer(inputString), FromImage(newImage) or paths[pathIndex]; a weaker offshoot fires on the head noun of tech compound nouns in prose, such as 'job posting'.","s":[0.434343,0.592979,0.405631,0.218434,0.042233,0.03404,0.089097,0.042329,0.039383,0.110359,0.038472,0.058739,0.043095,0.034563,0.041968]},{"p":"L13N2659+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word that frames an event or bad outcome as recurring, periodic or a matter of statistical frequency, such as \"cycle\", \"cyclical\", \"occurrence\", \"recur\", \"periodic\", \"every few years\", \"success/failure rate\", \"prone\" or \"unavoidable\", typically in prose about crises, disasters, markets or risk.","s":[0.593831,0.60027,0.190666,0.916183,0.244768,0.084004,0.037556,0.071546,0.065241,0.025859,0.018273,0.054679,0.02257,0.012964,0.022885]},{"p":"L13N2659-","l":"UNK","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"This is a very dense, low-specificity pole: it fires most strongly on sentence-ending periods in incoherent, spun or machine-generated essay-mill text, where each sentence jumps to an unrelated topic and the next sentence cannot be predicted from what came before. It fires more weakly on plural nouns and on code boundaries, so a short coherent text is unlikely to reach the threshold.","s":[0.071919,0.118802,0.082581,0.077132,0.084409,0.178723,0.123061,0.091404,0.039836,0.076384,0.126038,0.075818,0.043732,0.056445,0.074081]},{"p":"L13N2847+","l":"TWO-REL","j":"11","f":0,"e":0,"x":"In narrative prose, mostly fiction, this pole fires on the word naming a person's inner mental act or state right after the subject or a possessive (e.g. 'wants to', 'deciding', 'sensed', 'the urge', 'in an attempt', 'in his mind', 'not sure', 'decided', 'mulling', 'felt horrible', 'was surprised', 'were wondering'), and more weakly on the noun that completes a set phrase ('at length', 'gym membership', 'first draft').","s":[0.007666,0.010276,0.063458,0.008108,0.038561,0.078556,0.033217,0.014092,0.019857,0.02732,0.032051,0.057384,0.015546,0.006137,0.016372]},{"p":"L13N2847-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the adjective or complement word right after an appearance or evidential verb such as \"seemed\", \"looked\", \"appeared\", \"sounded\" or \"looks\", where the narrator judges a person's mood or a thing's quality from outside (\"he seemed excited\", \"Wolff appeared furious\", \"it looked easy\", \"delicious-looking\"), not when the state is stated directly (\"he was excited\").","s":[0.256471,0.352946,0.057979,0.233579,0.393853,0.2762,0.037857,0.015164,0.130101,0.038474,0.033862,0.02957,0.023239,0.051363,0.00978]},{"p":"L13N2999+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the final word-piece of a domain name inside a URL or web address, the token immediately before \".com\" (e.g. \"pose\" in aspose.com, \"imes\" in latimes.com, \"score\" in walkscore.com, \".amazon\" in aws.amazon.com), predicting that \".com\" comes next rather than \".org\".","s":[0.771752,0.125651,0.65657,0.526328,0.229065,0.771752,0.122268,0.65657,0.526328,0.229065,0.020269,0.017093,0.035739,0.021846,0.010275]},{"p":"L13N2999-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the final word-piece of a website domain name right before its top-level domain, i.e. the last part of a compound site name immediately followed by \".org\" (e.g. LinuxQuestions, StaySafeOnline, AboutCookies, helpguide, in a URL or bare name like \"Visit HelpGuide.org\"), predicting a TLD comes next; whole ordinary words not followed by a domain ending do not fire it.","s":[1.122258,0.763139,1.056366,0.837752,0.310418,0.430666,0.11001,0.003737,0.052997,0.310418,0.008157,0.014985,0.010835,0.003832,0.013825]},{"p":"L13N6183+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the verb (usually 'be' or a bare verb) right after a negated modal such as 'could not', 'can not' or 'ca n't', especially in boilerplate website error messages like 'the page you requested could not be found' or 'this server could not understand'; in spam text the 'not' is often swapped for a random adverb ('could sharply be reached').","s":[0.069693,0.023268,0.196242,0.072055,0.09757,0.054134,0.005242,0.008804,0.015252,0.178351,0.015414,0.040804,0.045934,0.032601,0.07278]},{"p":"L13N6183-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the whole word \" can\" used as an affirmative modal right after its subject (\"We can\", \"I can\", \"You can\", \"It can\", \"This plugin can\") where the next word is a verb, not \"'t\"/\"not\"; the output suppresses the \"'t\" continuation, so it is strongest in promotional or freelancer-pitch prose like \"We can provide you a website\".","s":[0.552895,0.190275,0.539487,0.725315,0.857035,0.073403,0.344442,0.012169,0.005967,0.018406,0.01485,0.006369,0.016583,0.010165,0.055347]},{"p":"L13N8376+","l":"TWO-REL","j":"11","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word that completes the standard of a comparison in literary or essayistic English, i.e. the token right after an equative/comparative/superlative setup: 'as much as ANY man', 'as IF/THOUGH', 'as decisively as he MIGHT', 'all the MORE/BETTER/GREATER', 'the finest lady that EVER', 'as they had NEVER before', 'a good/great DEAL'.","s":[0.707632,0.60786,0.645853,0.484207,0.184825,0.082204,0.04,0.027006,0.061714,0.040576,0.051752,0.013266,0.012355,0.025444,0.039295]},{"p":"L13N8376-","l":"UNK","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires mostly on a plural abstract noun, in expository prose, that announces a list about to follow, such as 'the following desirable properties:', 'three main objectives:', 'five of these innovations', 'the contributions of this thesis'. It is a weak, very dense pole with many scattered one-off contexts.","s":[0.456405,0.397613,0.213504,0.248415,0.337404,0.191731,0.220013,0.048055,0.065963,0.050628,0.034367,0.125037,0.151442,0.094227,0.026136]},{"p":"L13N9773+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the key concept nouns in contemporary-art and documentary project descriptions (artist statements, exhibition blurbs, critical reviews), on the word that names what the work explores or depicts, such as \"daily rituals\", \"decay\", \"Uber drivers\", \"consumer culture\", \"memory\", \"online communication\" or \"suburbs\", especially right after framing phrases like \"explores themes of\", \"interviews with\" or \"a direct relation to\".","s":[0.555147,0.235662,1.09905,0.748108,0.815399,0.06622,0.014653,0.013847,0.29465,0.00941,0.016887,0.015208,0.005365,0.013941,0.018161]},{"p":"L13N9773-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires inside literary and art-world criticism, especially experimental poetry: on line breaks or slashes within quoted, fragmented poem lines, and on the second or later word of a quoted title of a book, album, artwork or gallery (e.g. 'Dark Matter', 'Actual Size', 'Oh Dear Friends').","s":[0.468734,0.369704,0.247582,0.88141,0.640929,0.028095,0.072844,0.103355,0.207308,0.098332,0.040584,0.157251,0.072909,0.065073,0.088194]},{"p":"L14N3173+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the head noun that closes a noun phrase right after its modifiers, typically a concrete or categorical plural or common noun, or the final piece of a word split into several tokens, as in 'domestic hybrids', 'decorative trimming', 'Open Access journals', 'PCB capacitors', 'waltzes and polkas', 'G5 servers', in plain descriptive prose.","s":[0.185927,0.115921,0.085646,0.1415,0.125093,0.085683,0.122515,0.010043,0.022412,0.072554,0.106255,0.035633,0.035571,0.025311,0.043628]},{"p":"L14N3173-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"In the header of a news or blog post, fires on the metadata token right after a publication date (e.g. 'by'/'By' before the author name, a '|' separator, 'Categories', 'Email', or the line break after a date/time stamp before the headline), i.e. the date-then-byline slot of web article boilerplate.","s":[0.334584,0.406906,0.16801,0.279044,0.055907,0.016237,0.043302,0.07445,0.036537,0.038962,0.023686,0.025433,0.015838,0.038193,0.051527]},{"p":"L14N4543+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires mid-sentence in ordinary expository or instructional prose on the token that extends a just-named noun phrase: either a preposition attached directly to that noun (\"interest rates in the program\", \"eight cores for processing\", \"talk about\", \"col of the table\") or a generic head noun closing a modifier phrase (\"two view options\", \"below the zero level\", \"around 1.1358 levels\", \"semiconductor segments\"). It does not fire on sentence starts, punctuation or content-bearing verbs.","s":[0.055998,0.116148,0.071369,0.253334,0.355748,0.032864,0.069543,0.093763,0.083022,0.082535,0.051432,0.019509,0.053186,0.079597,0.058596]},{"p":"L14N4543-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"In explanatory or how-to prose that contrasts kinds or conditions, this pole fires on the preposition or conjunction (in, during, for, when, of, with, 'in case of') that comes right after a stated fact and introduces the specific type, variant or situation in which that fact holds, as in 'you consume fewer carbs on a keto diet', 'an induction coil is placed around the pipe during heat-induction bending', 'it is narrower for AM than for FM'.","s":[0.102048,0.430584,0.122073,0.21214,0.164615,0.036864,0.052975,0.012475,0.042713,0.055182,0.016282,0.006705,0.047453,0.007832,0.008969]},{"p":"L14N4978+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the continuation token inside a multi-word title or proper name of a creative work (novel, film, play, TV show, talk or book title), i.e. the second or later word or word-piece once the title has begun (e.g. 'Bible' after 'The Poisonwood', 'Brave' in 'Everyone Brave is Forgiven', 'Room' in 'Giovanni's Room'), and especially on a word repeated within the title ('Bad, Bad Movies', 'Bloody Bloody Andrew Jackson', '8 Fat Fat 8').","s":[1.273281,0.724127,0.62095,0.627254,0.622485,0.07161,0.014774,0.034707,0.113301,0.076617,0.142825,0.054815,0.030862,0.007155,0.073589]},{"p":"L14N4978-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the final token or word-piece that completes a multi-part proper name or fixed phrase once its first part has made the ending predictable (e.g. \"Casino\" in a repeated brand name \"Glory Casino\", \"water\" in \"Fallingwater\", \"Jack\" in \"Spring Heeled Jack\", \"World\" in \"Hello World\", \"ed\"/\"Fashion\" in \"Old Fashioned\", \"way\" in \"Midway\"); it is strongest when the name already appeared earlier in the text, and it does not fire on the name's first word.","s":[0.6067,0.797933,0.571849,0.166835,0.374383,0.046433,0.106036,0.049656,0.069823,0.029889,0.06998,0.049815,0.123184,0.015783,0.073039]},{"p":"L14N5001+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the last item of a paired or listed construction (e.g. \"X and Y\") right before the word \"respectively\" would come, in sentences that map two parallel lists onto each other (\"A and B moved to France and Spain respectively\", \"in 2000 and 2010, respectively\"), predicting \"respectively\" as the next word.","s":[0.19677,0.593431,0.001707,0.39263,0.40418,0.593431,0.010653,0.004436,0.013072,0.00608,0.009597,0.008859,0.003514,0.0069,0.002088]},{"p":"L14N5001-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the first item of a paired list where a matching second item and then \"respectively\" is about to follow, typically a number in a construction like \"rose by 24% and 20% respectively\" or \"in 1993 and 1996, respectively\" (and on \"respectively\" itself when it closes such a pairing); it predicts the word \"respectively\".","s":[0.437579,0.185568,0.135096,0.276407,0.08907,0.022762,0.185568,0.008714,0.045187,0.014768,0.011883,0.015206,0.006499,0.010467,0.014081]},{"p":"L14N5412+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the name being imported in a code import header near the top of a file, especially the second or later piece of an imported library identifier (e.g. create_app in \"from app import create_app\", BytesIO in \"from io import BytesIO\", Error in \"from mysql.connector import Error\", useHistory, System.Runtime.CompilerServices), and more weakly on library API names used just after the imports (createCanvas, \"freesansbold.ttf\").","s":[0.473521,0.901563,0.457286,0.524637,0.99779,0.033468,0.281553,0.010074,0.043473,0.081731,0.041972,0.060006,0.043074,0.054156,0.062673]},{"p":"L14N5412-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"In source code (especially JS/Python files right after their require/import lines), fires on a word-piece inside a newly declared identifier name being defined, i.e. the second or later piece of a variable/constant name such as WORD2VEC_MODEL, hypixelAPIReborn, route4me or DYNAMODB_TABLE, or the first token after const/var/import that starts a new binding name.","s":[0.458115,0.557039,0.578645,0.849503,0.374008,0.015833,0.117048,0.069182,0.031195,0.021728,0.029074,0.025869,0.028454,0.016631,0.008494]},{"p":"L14N6165+","l":"TWO-REL","j":"11","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires inside a word or phrase that is already underway and whose ending is tightly constrained: mainly on the middle subword pieces of long, rare, multi-token words such as foreign surnames in author or citation lists (Ruokamo, Dziurzynski, Riehle, Tekakwitha, Diarthroses), and secondarily on the completing word of a set phrase (tight spaces, nooks and crannies, getting into corners, peeled back, far away, or maybe).","s":[0.890428,0.736507,0.14898,0.057673,0.326066,0.024885,0.013243,0.042954,0.040467,0.030849,0.021401,0.089794,0.061103,0.174632,0.260142]},{"p":"L14N6165-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word piece right after a capitalized \"Sch\" that opens a German-style surname, such as the \"auf\" in Schaufeli, \"ie\" in Schiebel, \"art\" in Schartl, \"er\" in Scheraga or \"atz\" in Schatz, especially in author or citation lists, but not on common whole words like \"school\" or \"Schmidt\" if they are single tokens.","s":[0.812137,0.960699,1.072185,0.903544,1.065183,0.02507,0.025019,0.004093,0.010609,0.019475,0.019829,0.00837,0.016617,0.013541,0.018464]},{"p":"L14N671+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the second or later piece of a compound identifier being declared or defined in code, especially a SQL CREATE TABLE column name (e.g. loginTime, GivenBy, credit_limit, access_time, leader_surname) or a function parameter or variable name, at the word piece that completes the identifier just before its type or value.","s":[0.509531,0.744308,0.085192,0.358397,0.298849,0.175119,0.120798,0.178226,0.489675,0.23566,0.006053,0.045344,0.035028,0.029679,0.013896]},{"p":"L14N671-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"In source code, fires on the name or type of a boolean flag variable while it is being declared, before its value is given, and on the comment words just above such a declaration (\"whether\", \"bool\", \"has\", \"is\", or the end of a past-participle or -ing name like isAdded, animating, played, mouseDown), when the next thing is \"= False/false\" or the description of a true/false state.","s":[0.513643,0.359275,0.585317,0.392892,0.421599,0.134171,0.278365,0.244996,0.200137,0.250833,0.009685,0.013576,0.012284,0.029106,0.006022]},{"p":"L14N6896+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word that completes a phrase for people convening to talk something over, such as the noun \"meeting\", \"talk\", \"chat\", \"huddle\", \"retreat\" or \"summit\", or the particle in \"sat me down\", \"get the team together\", \"talk it out\" or \"call a time out\", especially when someone calls, has or needs that sit-down (\"we need to have a talk\", \"summoned a meeting\", \"sat him down\").","s":[0.995191,0.605795,0.815824,1.030746,1.833349,0.039609,0.028326,0.070606,0.119235,0.058138,0.022392,0.013815,0.017262,0.020628,0.026699]},{"p":"L14N6896-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word that completes a stock boilerplate phrase in web-page chrome or organizational templates, such as \"Skip links\", \"Current Location: Home > Product\", \"See Also:\", \"Follow this and additional works at\", \"Renew/subscribe\", \"Chat Online inputting...\", \"content is provided by an advertiser\" or \"Members Absent\" in meeting minutes, and more weakly on nouns in workplace, HR and management prose.","s":[0.355151,0.501241,0.379351,0.293329,0.212411,0.029558,0.071331,0.02394,0.110725,0.016072,0.078063,0.099756,0.071114,0.063222,0.08893]},{"p":"L14N9004+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the second word of a coordinated pair of modifiers or categories, the word right after \"and\", \"or\", a slash or a hyphen that completes a contrast or pairing with the first word (e.g. \"mechanical and chemical treatment\", \"primary and secondary forms\", \"constructive or negative feedback\", \"political leaders and civilian agencies\"), not on the first conjunct, the conjunction or the following noun.","s":[0.022336,0.041232,0.024829,0.027752,0.069999,0.02028,0.07308,0.023723,0.036379,0.05279,0.025045,0.040769,0.033021,0.025779,0.011191]},{"p":"L14N9004-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the noun or content word that completes a negated or excluded item, the thing being denied or set aside in phrases like \"not necessary\", \"not just because\", \"doesn't mean\", \"no other purpose\", \"or anything else\" or \"nor much else\", especially when a contrasting \"but…\" or correction is about to follow.","s":[0.078796,0.060028,0.002578,0.160666,0.431013,0.194249,0.049223,0.05363,0.147421,0.065864,0.103887,0.099766,0.085396,0.050599,0.057002]},{"p":"L14N9086+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the content word that fills the swapped slot in a mirrored contrast or parallel comparison, i.e. the noun or adverb right before a 'but not X', 'than X' or 'while X' counterpart, especially when it reverses an earlier clause (e.g. 'linked to lower scores in boys but not girls, while ... higher scores in GIRLS but not boys'; 'higher in PROXimal than distal cells'; 'peaceTIME ... wartime'; 'the model of OTHER than the model of self').","s":[0.180083,0.072749,0.418876,0.527161,0.160475,0.106829,0.108488,0.027955,0.022273,0.016797,0.012736,0.135559,0.104059,0.027698,0.045983]},{"p":"L14N9086-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word right after a contrastive self-positioning or topic-shift construction, in which one person's own stance or behavior is set apart from what others said or did: \"Me? I…\", \"Me, I…\", \"As for us/me…\", \"For my part…\", \"As far as I am concerned…\", \"From my perspective…\", \"In my eyes…\", or \"X just [laid there]\" contrasted with what others did.","s":[0.229387,0.320945,0.357153,0.627208,0.215601,0.00436,0.015376,0.049412,0.034374,0.036817,0.022591,0.029965,0.025131,0.013259,0.019642]},{"p":"L15N10014+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires inside a near-verbatim repeat of a phrase or line that appeared earlier in the same text, at or next to the point where the repeat differs from the original. Typical places are a corrected example sentence, a slightly altered test case or code line, or a list item copied with one change.","s":[0.177682,0.054657,0.058058,0.202615,0.050594,0.045257,0.038347,0.042146,0.043429,0.008097,0.018343,0.025896,0.043309,0.078717,0.031933]},{"p":"L15N10014-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a content word (or word piece) that repeats a word or phrase already used earlier in the same passage, especially in a parallel construction where the earlier phrase is being echoed (e.g. 'fear and reality of death... fear and reality of freedom', 'custodial fathers... custodial mothers', 'little generic blue figures... little generic red figures'); the first occurrence of the word does not fire.","s":[0.08076,0.430291,0.385906,0.028275,0.041806,0.017872,0.045734,0.057304,0.029165,0.038371,0.019327,0.047642,0.031033,0.010888,0.00625]},{"p":"L15N10355+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the second or later piece of a compound word or identifier, the piece that completes it (lab coats, shared parenting, pseudo-value, LineRenderer, FillSchema, getmask, DeleteOnSubmit, grantees), especially when that word or its stem was already used earlier in the same text, as in subsidy then subsidised, license then licensed, or a code identifier repeated.","s":[0.13177,0.127979,0.348159,0.117639,0.310111,0.175699,0.138146,0.121682,0.067028,0.131903,0.078272,0.026972,0.118918,0.053586,0.096404]},{"p":"L15N10355-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the head noun that completes a noun phrase, most strongly on nouns naming produced content or media items (stories, articles, posts, videos, photos, recordings, papers, content, samples, material), usually right after a modifier ('short stories', 'video clip articles', 'a raw photo', 'the full paper', 'its own content'); it also fires on the last word of a book or film title (Bottle Rocket, Midnight's Children)—but not on the modifier words or on verbs.","s":[0.136078,0.292009,0.271257,0.36148,0.455339,0.096714,0.169507,0.111846,0.15754,0.04728,0.081795,0.059258,0.101818,0.08554,0.129009]},{"p":"L15N10390+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the generic descriptor word that immediately precedes the reveal of a specific name or term, such as the head noun or 'known' or comma right before 'is X', 'known as X', 'called X' or ': X' (e.g. 'The country's monetary unit is the Peso', 'better known as Alex G', 'This mixture, known as the culture medium', 'Its original name was…').","s":[0.274156,0.144372,0.097051,0.019754,0.139404,0.035129,0.061933,0.033531,0.013452,0.04234,0.01135,0.027678,0.038536,0.037342,0.022654]},{"p":"L15N10390-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"In news articles, fires at the start of the second sentence or paragraph, right after a headline and a one-sentence lede: on the period or paragraph break that ends the lede, or on the anaphoric noun after \"The\" that refers back to the thing just introduced (\"The consortium\", \"The report\", \"The company\", \"The ruling\").","s":[0.491269,0.230309,0.299889,0.394288,0.387533,0.020899,0.019467,0.020126,0.018902,0.017605,0.016835,0.010662,0.020285,0.026286,0.019057]},{"p":"L15N1600+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Predicts an upcoming \"today\"/\"now\": fires on the words inside a relative clause describing something's present form after a history of change, as in \"evolved into the iconic dish we know today\", \"the compound seen today\" or \"the city it is today\", peaking on the noun, pronoun or verb just before \"today\".","s":[0.645719,0.619092,0.411376,0.814907,0.602489,0.192879,0.301586,0.130652,0.153544,0.411535,0.010653,0.011805,0.017358,0.006018,0.006975]},{"p":"L15N1600-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires at the moment a narrative about the past makes an anachronistic link to the present: on the word or name right after phrases like \"what is now\", \"present\", \"known today\", or wherever \"today\"/\"these days\"/\"now\" is about to be used to contrast a historical era with the present (e.g. \"the site of present-day X\", \"the keyboard familiar today\", \"what is now Bangladesh\", \"later President George W. Bush\").","s":[0.354098,0.377199,0.441466,0.68003,0.432405,0.061888,0.181189,0.123568,0.094772,0.06677,0.020494,0.085323,0.04298,0.041656,0.019621]},{"p":"L15N4472+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"A comma in the middle of an ordinary prose sentence, placed right after a complete main clause or full noun phrase, where the sentence is about to continue with an add-on or qualifying part (\"…, and…\", \"…, but…\", \"…, though…\", \"…, including…\", \"…, which…\", \"…, depending on…\"); it does not fire on commas after short introductory words or between short list items.","s":[0.271119,0.193449,0.380288,0.393785,0.159274,0.006602,0.207523,0.026921,0.009424,0.010462,0.011339,0.004943,0.008682,0.007378,0.016267]},{"p":"L15N4472-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a mid-sentence comma (occasionally a period) that closes an introductory or descriptive phrase and hands off to a new clause or subject, as in \"Built to make design smooth, with the editor you...\", \"Entrepreneurial and progressive, George is...\" or an author–year citation \"(Smith and Jones, 2014)\". It is strongest in spun, garbled SEO or OCR-scanned text, and weak on commas inside simple lists that lead to \"and\".","s":[0.149364,0.066334,0.300308,0.130108,0.125461,0.158282,0.158399,0.162303,0.0482,0.199903,0.100212,0.154969,0.12281,0.125293,0.088385]},{"p":"L15N458+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word expressing assistance or cooperation, such as help, aid, support, serve, facilitate, benefit, protect, team up with, collaborate or companion, typically as the main verb right after a product, platform or person subject in promotional or review web prose describing what it does for users or others.","s":[0.051221,0.081894,0.042583,0.139006,0.196532,0.006768,0.020965,0.02851,0.039185,0.074788,0.062858,0.016051,0.003581,0.000492,0.026575]},{"p":"L15N458-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the verb \"contribute\" in any inflection (contribute, contributes, contributed, contributing, including the end piece of a split \"Con|tributes\"), and on near-synonyms like \"participates\", when it is used in the sense of helping cause something and is followed by \"to\".","s":[0.896451,0.511845,0.631371,0.586874,0.484864,0.014678,0.271821,0.049184,0.174029,0.054823,0.014516,0.007783,0.011162,0.010434,0.010211]},{"p":"L15N474+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires inside the idiom of knowing or not knowing what one is doing, peaking on the 'what' and the following pronoun right after 'know' or 'not sure' (as in 'if you don't know what you're doing', 'nobody knows what they're doing', 'unless you know exactly what you are getting'), weaker on 'know' itself.","s":[0.648757,0.495193,0.445158,0.512793,0.096611,0.140457,0.028852,0.021723,0.042754,0.008924,0.011743,0.006779,0.017529,0.005693,0.018656]},{"p":"L15N474-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word for fire or burning itself (fire, fires, flames, blaze, ablaze, afire, burned, firefighters, flammability), especially in phrases like 'set fire to', 'on fire', 'caught fire', 'burned down', and its output suppresses predicting fire/burn words again.","s":[0.723399,0.575668,0.588756,0.55985,0.526149,0.012703,0.068447,0.002016,3.3e-05,0.019045,0.003272,0.009978,0.007947,0.004606,0.007162]},{"p":"L15N6531+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"In reviews or opinion comments, fires right after a minor complaint or drawback is stated, on the pivot that dismisses it before returning to overall praise: words like \"Besides that\", \"Aside from that\", \"Other than that\", \"Apart from\", \"Otherwise\", \"However,\", \"but overall\", \"X aside,\" (strongest on the dismissal word or the \"that\"/comma right after it).","s":[0.520271,0.575831,0.308601,0.205479,0.162688,0.072728,0.121693,0.048287,0.046794,0.014503,0.009433,0.007801,0.01484,0.00297,0.011583]},{"p":"L15N6531-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the punctuation or connective (comma, period, question mark, 'or', 'and', 'then') that ends one link in a passage piling up a convoluted chain of nested requirements, alternatives or self-contradictions, for example 'to get X you need Y, and to get Y you need Z,' or 'Do I rate it this way? Or that way?', where the writer is conveying mounting complication or confusion.","s":[0.581525,1.144426,0.448562,0.842147,0.419767,0.011617,0.035232,0.011288,0.016497,0.01699,0.018337,0.008324,0.007712,0.017214,0.009086]},{"p":"L16N10389+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the contraction clitic attached to a pronoun subject (’s, ’ve, ’d, ’ll in He’s, It’s, I’ve, you’d, You’ll, They’ve), and on the word “also” directly after such a contraction (It’s also, You’ll also), especially in web prose, author bios and product-review copy, where a verb like “been” or “got” is expected next.","s":[0.660082,0.304742,0.328237,0.404666,0.272042,0.043571,0.020226,0.006314,0.006077,0.012434,0.010943,0.011146,0.010675,0.008109,0.010694]},{"p":"L16N10389-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"In sloppy, informal forum or comment writing, this pole fires on a clause-opening subject word, most often \"there\" (including \"there\" misspelled for \"their\" or \"they're\", and \"its\" for \"it's\"), placed right after a verb or conjunction such as \"know\", \"because\", \"once\" or \"believe\", where the next word is a verb.","s":[0.066624,0.072943,0.05769,0.101064,0.030695,0.067544,0.070311,0.009947,0.066957,0.132543,0.115866,0.054618,0.050325,0.072858,0.079212]},{"p":"L16N10459+","l":"UNREL","j":"000","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on words for mechanical closing, fastening and folding hardware, or on the action of operating it, in physical descriptions: lock, latch, latches, hinges, zipper, clips, slide, folds, opens, tighten, removable (as in 'he tried to open the latch', 'the soft-close hinges', 'the top zipper died').","s":[0.867093,0.608922,0.431374,0.74185,0.343672,0.414217,0.064313,0.177336,0.113731,0.059483,0.125436,0.042087,0.034515,0.207738,0.044666],"u":{"0":[0.526436,0.577131,0.133351,0.337928,0.748724],"1":[0.552512,0.601995,0.539075,0.53274,0.244328],"2":[0.664905,0.855852,0.926638,0.012824,0.475048]}},{"p":"L16N10459-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires inside reports of statistical regression results, most strongly on the row break (the '>' plus newline after a closing </tr>, or the '|' starting a markdown row) between rows of a coefficient table listing predictors, estimates, standard errors and significance stars, and also on words like 'explained' or an opening parenthesis in prose that reports a regression effect (e.g. 'which explained 93% of the variance', 'was found (LMM: ...')).","s":[0.597772,0.622567,0.892968,0.83855,0.501375,0.018763,0.013312,0.00967,0.025133,0.030835,0.012125,0.046035,0.02656,0.017786,0.031674]},{"p":"L16N1206+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"In data, statistics or listing contexts (SQL, R or pandas code, survey or census breakdowns, website filter menus), this fires on the word that sets up how records are broken down or grouped, above all \"by\" in \"group by / split by / filter by / distribution by\" and nearby words such as \"distribution\", \"top\", \"mode\" or \"for each X,\"; it stays quiet on ordinary \"by\" (\"written by\", \"by car\").","s":[0.178057,0.455135,0.412781,0.254343,0.347831,0.002784,0.003819,0.18083,0.058585,0.022869,0.026406,0.104789,0.082252,0.045551,0.006009]},{"p":"L16N1206-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the words in a moderation or gatekeeping clause that name keeping a group or pool pure by screening out bad members, such as \"maintain\", \"quality\", \"high\", \"clean\", \"clear\", \"weed (out)\", \"composition\" or \"enrichment\", as in \"we verify accounts to keep review quality high\", \"double opt-in weeds out spammers\" or \"moderators keep the community clean\"; neutral uses of these words with no screening or filtering of a membership will not fire it.","s":[1.124867,0.852275,0.783628,0.668063,1.297534,0.137424,0.022804,0.053366,0.068018,0.020634,0.044157,0.025753,0.043367,0.049262,0.033347]},{"p":"L16N2846+","l":"UNK","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"A diffuse, dense pole with no shared token or topic: it fires weakly on almost any token in long narrative or explanatory English prose (often first-person storytelling), peaking mid-sentence on function words, pronouns and nouns deep into a document, which is a long-context effect that short standalone texts are unlikely to reproduce at strength.","s":[0.196995,0.22269,0.071405,0.069218,0.088149,0.0405,0.021217,0.038007,0.037644,0.024764,0.034401,0.06134,0.049692,0.041975,0.057303]},{"p":"L16N2846-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word that completes a web call-to-action instruction, above all \"here\" in \"Click here\", \"Subscribe here\", \"Contact us here\", \"Learn more here\" or \"Read our Cookie Policy here\" in website boilerplate (registration, subscription, privacy and affiliate notices), and more weakly on the final noun of a phrase that closes a clause, such as \"share buttons\" or \"Click below\".","s":[0.687846,0.499609,0.493969,0.60161,0.537571,0.191623,0.245758,0.132955,0.090914,0.119251,0.015353,0.031174,0.021862,0.088548,0.062164]},{"p":"L16N3013+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires where a text is about something being concealed or tucked away out of sight, most strongly on the words naming the hiding place or the act of hiding (\"hidden\" after smuggled goods, \"false wall\", \"trap door\", \"secret door\", a mismatched \"floorboard\", a button that \"reveals\" a secret room), predicting a word like hidden, secret or concealed.","s":[0.615238,0.668125,0.521691,0.432687,0.635366,0.029083,0.015427,0.14071,0.01367,0.010227,0.004884,0.009763,0.005432,0.003491,0.008564]},{"p":"L16N3013-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on spatial words meaning 'lower than / directly under' (beneath, below, underneath, under, bottom) when used as a physical location preposition or adverb placing something under a reference point, especially in narrative scene description like 'stood beneath the pillar', 'a voice from below me', 'singing underneath her window'.","s":[-5.2e-05,0.154879,0.249266,0.349332,0.421408,-5.2e-05,0.022768,0.252655,0.00728,0.080603,0.04524,0.038591,0.021361,0.033941,0.016502]},{"p":"L16N5767+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires in reports of current weather or seasonal conditions compared against a normal or record baseline (rainfall, temperatures, drought, sea ice, runoff, crop seasons), on the tokens just after the condition is named, e.g. on 'is', 'above', 'to', 'than' or 'of' in phrases like 'forecast runoff is 55% of average', 'temperatures 2°C above average', 'normal rainfall', 'forecast to be hotter than normal this year'.","s":[0.965252,0.822131,0.974047,1.042685,0.597048,0.025532,0.419304,0.060684,0.03852,0.024444,0.013124,0.029237,0.012672,0.0334,0.006575]},{"p":"L16N5767-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires across news-style prose describing a current temporary disruption, such as the COVID-19 pandemic, a hurricane, a power outage or panic-buying, on the words that frame its time span or normal-versus-now contrast (\"for weeks now\", \"longer than usual\", \"first trip in months\", \"temporary relief\", \"without power\", \"global crisis\"), not on calm text about ordinary routines.","s":[0.231764,0.482128,0.210914,0.248628,0.707602,0.050341,0.064061,0.079508,0.049738,0.028846,0.046045,0.008526,0.006597,0.018198,0.023136]},{"p":"L16N6982+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the words that point back to an earlier, original state that was lost or disrupted by some event, especially on \"was/were\" and \"before\" in phrases like \"how it was before the surgery\", \"where we were before the pandemic\", \"pre-COVID levels\", \"the day before he left\"; it does not fire on \"before\" used for routine sequencing.","s":[0.420696,0.663503,0.354451,0.118798,0.234829,0.004918,0.042798,0.003363,0.039138,0.01341,0.045162,0.002747,0.067549,0.048468,0.047451]},{"p":"L16N6982-","l":"UNK","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on tokens in sentences describing a change or departure from a prior state, most of all on \"from\" right after a verb of change or motion (\"went from hard to…\", \"disk space went from 297 MB to 318 MB\", \"an hour from camp\", \"the path from the van\"), and on subject nouns like \"economy\" or \"inflation\" in recovery-or-slowdown talk; output leans toward \"original/originally\".","s":[0.044706,0.113384,0.106685,0.068789,0.085384,0.053735,0.046058,0.019869,0.013354,0.100159,0.035153,0.020405,0.010913,0.013329,0.060147]},{"p":"L16N7180+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a descriptive modifier placed right after a determiner or intensifier and before the head noun it describes, such as 'a cheap rental', 'one very easy hack', 'a small sample', 'any other similar tool' or 'a lightweight framework', mostly in long web or tutorial prose; it is very dense and weakly selective, so short texts may fire it only weakly.","s":[0.220914,0.254432,0.153064,0.302098,0.063061,0.239224,0.147754,0.058345,0.302098,0.043499,0.013051,0.073861,0.014083,0.078003,0.072402]},{"p":"L16N7180-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on an adjective-like modifier that sits right after a determiner or quantifier and before the noun it modifies, i.e. in the middle of a noun phrase still waiting for its head noun, most strongly with words such as \"other\", \"another\", \"different\", \"second\", \"older\", \"younger\", \"greater\" and \"great\" (as in \"some other role\", \"a different chain\", \"an older tale\", \"this great book\").","s":[0.257459,0.080677,0.108153,0.080346,0.040667,0.052986,0.08867,0.021383,0.049002,0.024373,0.037098,0.095428,0.037593,0.115805,0.027231]},{"p":"L16N7733+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires inside a parallel, repeated list structure, at the point where a later item (usually the third or beyond) begins: on the comma, 'and', 'or', semicolon or line break just before the final item (\"our communities, our environment, and our future\"), or on the repeated head word of a later repetition (\"The right to fair play… The right to privacy… The right to…\", \"along with that trial, along with\").","s":[0.221455,0.291657,0.242589,0.397906,0.075392,0.008337,0.01416,0.021506,0.018817,0.0183,0.025492,0.042349,0.036635,0.02609,0.016849]},{"p":"L16N7733-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a word in formulaic, SEO-style review or marketing prose (strongest in crypto exchange and wallet reviews) whose continuation is almost fixed, mainly the head of a set collocation demanding a particular preposition (committed [to], resulting/resulted [in], level [of], list [of], capable [of], aims [to], access [to], in search [of], refrain [from], process [of]) or a sentence-opening connective awaiting its comma (However, In fact, Additionally).","s":[0.155972,0.206527,0.186323,0.723689,0.458605,0.012197,0.024008,0.038524,0.038433,0.010964,0.048956,0.094712,0.007926,0.031115,0.006545]},{"p":"L16N864+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on speech-attribution points in narrative, especially the verb or punctuation right where a character's quoted words are introduced or closed (\"he said,\" / \"she declared\" / the comma or opening quote after \"he said\"), most strongly in erotic, violent or domineering fiction and court/news reports of what someone said, believed or declared.","s":[0.311066,0.5616,0.534885,0.380919,0.321653,0.172564,0.211861,0.174305,0.107676,0.096442,0.07549,0.099068,0.063102,0.027167,0.041565]},{"p":"L16N864-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word naming a force that prompts or pushes someone toward an action (urging, telling, calling, nudge, desire, pressure, commands, compelled, arrows pointing), especially just before the person and the action it pushes them to, as in 'a voice in my head telling me to', 'friends were urging me to', 'arrows telling you which way to go'.","s":[0.603113,0.702367,0.460632,0.161746,0.307367,0.212697,0.074386,0.284716,0.055726,0.019604,0.019948,0.00879,0.007429,0.012069,0.012693]},{"p":"L17N10828+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the last word of a desired-outcome phrase in how-to/advice sentences (\"you can save even more money\", \"get a more accurate price\", \"avoid these problems\", \"speed up the drying process\", \"make it unforgettable\"), the point where the sentence is about to state the means with \"by ...\", \"if ...\", or \"is to ...\".","s":[0.22685,0.718062,0.454001,0.037921,0.143427,0.015893,0.033574,0.022679,0.017425,0.012135,0.007532,0.00845,0.009825,0.019822,0.012296]},{"p":"L17N10828-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the last word, or the comma, that closes a sentence-initial infinitive purpose clause (\"To comment on this article\", \"To build a base,\", \"To use the app and its functionality,\", \"To teach,\"), right where the main clause is about to begin.","s":[0.592709,0.489428,0.219991,0.293858,0.186158,0.104412,0.144872,0.160631,0.029603,0.042505,0.087644,0.101743,0.101705,0.030298,0.023718]},{"p":"L17N3399+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on HTML-escapable punctuation symbols themselves, most strongly a standalone ampersand joining two words in prose (\"rounds 2 & 3\", \"operation & maintenance\"), inch/double-quote marks right after a number (6'7\", 4”-6”, 12” x 18”), closing quotation marks after a quoted word, and the trailing apostrophe of dropped-g slang (sayin', nuthin'), plus closing brackets and backticks in code.","s":[0.588289,0.553768,0.5408,0.424431,0.465799,0.007051,0.001957,0.015696,0.044571,0.011461,0.003369,0.022532,0.006915,0.028326,0.064609]},{"p":"L17N3399-","l":"TWO-REL","j":"11","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a short punctuation token that closes a tiny unit in web-page boilerplate or UI text, such as the closing parenthesis in \"Comments (3) | Views (1,067)\", the question mark in \"Why sign up? | Forgot Password?\", the ellipsis in \"Loading... Please wait\", or the closing of a split(',') call in code; it also fires more weakly on contractions such as 'm or 're in pitchy web copy (\"Hi, I'm interested\").","s":[0.499675,0.592154,0.350137,0.088534,0.050671,0.059312,0.078659,0.030361,0.088067,0.028481,0.04141,0.049727,0.048463,0.046561,0.053264]},{"p":"L17N4727+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires inside statements of urgent necessity or obligation (\"had better…\", \"have to…\", \"must be…\", \"needs to be…\") on the words that fill in the required action, especially an unspecified one like \"something\", \"somebody\" or \"somewhere\", as in \"we'd better think of something fast\" or \"I have to trust somebody\".","s":[0.702228,0.588033,0.291129,0.086007,0.39304,0.009292,0.083531,0.013302,0.014896,0.008328,0.006946,0.013186,0.010129,0.010356,0.008343]},{"p":"L17N4727-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word right after an expression of necessity, obligation or requirement (\"you need to [log in]\", \"must [be] entered\", \"have to [be]\", \"should [definitely]\", \"Please [subscribe]\", \"We need [this]\", \"require a security [clearance]\", \"need a new confirmation [email]\"), i.e. the verb or object that fills the required action, often in instructions and website login/subscribe boilerplate.","s":[0.503731,0.296468,0.135778,0.022203,0.515041,0.021463,0.008303,0.030532,0.026236,0.062955,0.047212,0.016634,0.008156,0.019403,0.019904]},{"p":"L17N5377+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the words \"actual\" and \"results\" in the stock disclaimer phrase \"actual results may/could differ materially\" inside forward-looking-statements boilerplate of press releases (peaking on \"Actual\" right after the risk/uncertainty sentences), with faint echoes on similar hedge words like \"realities\" or \"if in doubt\".","s":[0.649329,0.711376,0.633285,0.639463,0.519879,0.005326,0.194427,0.075887,0.042613,0.002255,0.047709,0.023037,0.014391,0.023854,0.015664]},{"p":"L17N5377-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires inside a clause about whether previously stated, promised, planned or claimed things are actually carried out, tested or turn out true, peaking on the verb or the referring noun that points back at those claims, as in \"verify his theories\", \"implement the plan\", \"give it a try\", \"works as expected\", \"did some of the things he wrote about\" or \"none of the events anticipated will occur\".","s":[0.514109,0.530547,0.320249,0.225646,0.337341,0.058102,0.007452,0.007901,0.011844,0.027804,0.01555,0.014729,0.021799,0.014227,0.016838]},{"p":"L17N5407+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the head noun (or final word piece) that closes the first item of a parallel enumeration of subtypes sharing that head, right before the comma, as in \"types include reflected XSS, stored XSS\", \"work visa, student visa\", \"the Electrical Shop, HVAC Shop\" or \"Aluminum Die Casting Competition, Magnesium Die Casting Competition\", where the same head word will be repeated for the next items.","s":[0.362596,0.380892,0.191812,0.291684,0.332296,0.09602,0.019773,0.133621,0.036713,0.270227,0.020978,0.013404,0.011483,0.019313,0.042174]},{"p":"L17N5407-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the last token of a multi-token name or phrase (often a rare word split into pieces, e.g. Zoopla, Bounty0x, Dianaperuza, Dan Dan Noodles) when that name is being repeated verbatim from earlier in the same text, as in a heading, list item or table cell restating it, which marks that the copied name is now complete.","s":[0.039282,0.101902,0.157798,0.129576,0.119354,0.026786,0.036412,0.067729,0.092888,0.040021,0.043512,0.041075,0.04335,0.03162,0.013213]},{"p":"L17N7622+","l":"TWO-REL","j":"11","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires inside an enumeration of noun phrases, such as a comma-separated series or a bulleted or numbered list of items, features, costs or services, on the item's first word or modifier (e.g. 'store' in 'staffing guidelines, store design', 'hearing' in 'hearing officer fees', 'Tablet' in '• Tablet cases') where a compound noun is still being built; a weaker offshoot fires on 'to' in recipe-FAQ questions like 'Do you need to flip fish?'.","s":[0.209879,0.313405,0.056488,0.023581,0.222131,0.038892,0.027919,0.014934,0.130157,0.057631,0.055233,0.034502,0.080988,0.068445,0.020818]},{"p":"L17N7622-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the token immediately before an emphasized key item is about to be named, typically the final item of a list or the most important factor, as in 'and, of course,', 'and most importantly', 'last but not the least', 'the first thing you need to consider is', 'a few factors to consider:'; this mostly occurs in explanatory or listicle web prose.","s":[0.613064,0.120814,0.182538,0.380654,0.315959,0.026608,0.040221,0.011864,0.048716,0.014915,0.008442,0.023405,0.011769,-6.1e-05,0.047065]},{"p":"L17N8536+","l":"UNREL","j":"00","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires in descriptive prose about the physical durability or breakdown of materials and objects, such as whether something wears out, tears, fades, cracks, melts, ignites, gets holes or lasts over time. It peaks on the word just before or at that durability claim (e.g. 'fugitive pigments fade over time', 'it doesn't wear out', 'the paper is too thin and ends up tearing', 'plastic gears suffer reduced strength').","s":[0.49356,0.258422,1.039751,0.366084,0.374143,0.044232,0.0196,0.151528,0.185416,0.084436,0.044727,0.032468,0.022084,0.039809,0.039293],"u":{"0":[0.518175,0.462496,0.151293,0.290028,0.712448],"1":[0.707678,0.055209,0.080387,0.274119,0.614418]}},{"p":"L17N8536-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires inside descriptions of applying, installing or maintaining a finishing material on a building or craft surface, such as tiles, caulk, stain, resin, edging, trim, mortar, cement, paint or glass panels, for a finished look or for protection; it fires on the word right after the material or finishing verb (e.g. 'stained it', 'caulk them shut', 'cemented into place', 'covered with white resin', 'add aesthetic appeal and protect').","s":[0.383833,0.350791,0.818489,0.481303,0.699024,0.063477,0.092726,0.245016,0.07831,0.074225,0.033966,0.008826,0.02204,0.033458,0.023573]},{"p":"L17N88+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the token that closes a sentence-opening subject or topic phrase and starts its description, usually a just-repeated name or product: a describing verb like has, contains, offers, holds or consists, a linking with or of (\"Accessible mode has\", \"Tinnitus Control contains\", \"This page holds\", \"It consists of\"), or the last piece of a repeated name in an opening \"In Mr. Rees'\" or \"For CSSC\".","s":[0.121848,0.722477,0.217944,0.113178,0.206799,0.041242,0.058356,0.050146,0.064707,0.068051,0.046538,0.072671,0.065691,0.019521,0.058013]},{"p":"L17N88-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the coordinating word (mostly \"and\", also \"that\", \"it\", \"which\") that comes right after a short predicate or clause has just been completed, typically \"X is [adjective],\" or \"X is [adjective] and\", where the next words begin a fresh clause with its own subject (\"Withdrawals are quick, and most casinos…\"; \"learn that it is common and that it is not…\").","s":[0.015755,0.306202,0.932791,0.041129,0.093691,0.01539,0.182948,0.05659,0.014314,0.039402,0.101617,0.025517,0.021396,0.045877,0.033238]},{"p":"L18N10944+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"On tech-news or blog web pages (Slashdot-style), fires on the punctuation plus line break that closes a piece of site boilerplate or a finished post, such as \"Forgot your password?\", \"(Learn more about the recent updates).\", \"Blog archive\" or \"more than 3 years ago\", right before a new story headline or comment header begins.","s":[0.063617,0.351832,0.118861,0.316022,0.446498,0.135792,0.088605,0.067372,0.062395,0.102579,0.054272,0.075432,0.038296,0.037709,0.140386]},{"p":"L18N10944-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires in news/business prose about an existing business, building or industry being bought, taken over or disrupted, on the words in and around the slot where its transformation is described (\"plans to\", \"is to be\", \"who\", \"by\") and on the change verbs themselves (convert, transform, turn it into, redevelop, reinvent, disrupt, rejuvenate), effectively anticipating a verb of reinventing or revitalizing.","s":[0.574513,0.856072,0.429379,0.623841,0.679143,0.469171,0.188614,0.040465,0.076442,0.082673,0.008967,0.002444,0.008123,0.016983,0.008057]},{"p":"L18N2010+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the noun or verb that completes a quantity or selection phrase, the thing being counted, increased, limited or chosen, right after words like \"more\", \"less\", \"how many\", \"the number of\", \"the amount of\", or after a selecting \"what/who/whom\" (e.g. \"make more calls\", \"get less work done\", \"the amount of drug that reaches\", \"limit the number of asylum seekers\", \"determines who we employ\").","s":[0.359171,0.249964,0.306263,0.184087,0.397138,0.01239,0.019839,0.010727,0.022429,0.013885,0.003635,0.020128,0.029393,0.055291,0.02318]},{"p":"L18N2010-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the final word that completes a phrase about an outcome being achieved, survived or granted, such as \"is possible\", \"still alive\", \"only 15% are approved\", \"dreams can come true\", \"get into heaven\", \"really make money\", \"end up on store shelves\" or \"grant permission\", especially when that success, acceptance or existence is hoped for, doubted or remarkable.","s":[0.600587,0.311634,0.419678,0.145626,0.208069,0.086405,0.04664,0.013249,0.076294,0.008529,0.079267,0.082685,0.02733,0.044453,0.030571]},{"p":"L18N2982+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires inside descriptions of gameplay or physical exertion, i.e. how a player, athlete or device performs repeated actions, most strongly on words that quantify or name those actions, such as a count of reps, hits, punches or attacks, a control used to play, or a device's output or capacity.","s":[0.338024,0.353192,0.40592,0.345973,0.505742,0.014856,0.014722,0.04488,9.2e-05,0.010478,0.023313,0.004798,0.033213,0.051492,0.026167]},{"p":"L18N2982-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires inside commercial web pages, above all online casino and slot-machine reviews, at the short UI-style labels, list items and boilerplate that follow the descriptive prose: spec fields like \"Min Bet:\", \"Bonus Round: No\", feature lists such as \"Scatter, Wild, Multiplier\", payment-method lists, and call-to-action phrases like \"Write a review\", \"Click Play Now\" or \"Take the Quiz\".","s":[0.489942,0.227192,0.604848,0.572625,0.660714,0.025814,0.044194,0.033477,0.063378,0.042225,0.012055,0.039944,0.047174,0.057322,0.058657]},{"p":"L18N2993+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the sentence-ending period, or period plus paragraph break, that closes an explanatory claim about dehydrating or drying food: removing water or moisture so the food keeps its nutrients and flavor, resists spoilage, or stores longer.","s":[0.092667,0.044568,0.281435,0.045069,0.126641,0.15594,0.130812,0.035941,0.157409,0.047238,0.056779,0.048157,0.040838,0.093772,0.063676]},{"p":"L18N2993-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on words naming or describing colorants such as dye, pigment, dyes, textile dyeing, or color names in a pigment list, and on the verb right after a pigment's name that says how it was made, obtained or used (\"was made from\", \"is obtained from\", \"used as a pigment\"), in prose about how dyes, paints and pigments are produced from natural materials; it predicts paint, pigment or paste-like substances.","s":[0.738258,0.875491,0.44502,0.595855,0.713313,0.112162,0.212254,0.030837,0.294426,0.58984,0.00863,0.011975,0.012784,0.004221,0.002206]},{"p":"L18N3476+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word \"very\" used as an emphatic adjective/intensifier right after a determiner or possessive and before a noun or superlative, as in \"the very act of\", \"their very existence\", \"the very idea\", \"our very essence\", \"your very best\" (not \"very\" as an ordinary adverb before an adjective like \"very good\").","s":[0.517023,0.714162,0.504448,0.253254,0.538876,0.10981,0.154064,0.076409,0.025197,0.065733,0.01356,0.02085,0.022091,0.013786,0.032997]},{"p":"L18N3476-","l":"UNREL","j":"000","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a structural filler token whose continuation is fixed by a template, most strongly the line break right after a page-opening \"Jump to content\" or \"Skip to Content\" on a scraped forum or web page, and the leading decimal point of an APA-style statistic such as \"p = .02\" or \"p<.01\" in a results section (plus the indent before a numbered sub-list item and the space after a \"© 2024\" footer).","s":[0.190497,0.21199,0.715224,0.359392,0.190497,0.054362,0.016882,0.030841,0.069624,0.080745,0.031463,0.065259,0.030464,0.056179,0.040551],"u":{"0":[0.587105,0.616132,0.587105,0.616132,0.587105],"1":[0.141008,0.200597,0.42319,0.223768,1.030692],"2":[0.026962,0.069129,0.028105,0.127653,0.043961]}},{"p":"L18N6315+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word just before a mention of a source of evidence is expected, i.e. where the text is about to name an article, study, report, research, documentation or reports that back a claim (e.g. on \"citing\" / \"cited\" in \"advocates reject this, citing …\", \"seen\" in \"I have not seen any …\", \"Numer-ous\" before \"studies\", \"a\" in \"as a New York Times article revealed\", \"quantitative\" before \"research shows\").","s":[0.162784,0.253328,0.20458,0.043383,0.049219,0.032178,0.014263,0.13864,0.008744,0.169474,0.019211,0.025838,0.022819,0.026949,0.012218]},{"p":"L18N6315-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the function word or verb right before a reference to an information source: phrases that cite or point to something written or shown, such as \"as you said in your\", \"as shown\", \"mentioned in the\", \"read the\", \"draws upon\", \"in the linked\", \"judging from\" or \"the ones you see on our\", where the next word is likely article, manual, video, comments, documents or illustrations.","s":[0.134173,0.13891,0.073784,0.095423,0.191095,0.045362,0.077329,0.025283,0.095423,0.162429,0.013714,0.019878,0.021355,0.019725,0.011598]},{"p":"L18N703+","l":"ONE","j":"00","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a short consonant-cluster word piece (such as gn, chn, ign, acc, occ, McC) that falls in the middle of a rare, usually Italian, German, Slavic or Latin proper name being spelled out token by token, as in Psygnosis, Prochnow, Procaccini, Abrignani, Licigno, Ischnura or McCaleb; common words containing the same letters (sign, design, technical) do not fire it.","s":[0.649033,0.715854,0.585731,0.652595,0.787473,0.02015,0.012152,0.012834,0.013505,0.010932,0.010892,0.025267,0.024868,0.006878,0.042508]},{"p":"L18N703-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a two-letter consonant-pair token made of 'b' plus another consonant, mostly 'FB' or 'fb' (also 'rb', 'RB', 'bb', 'pb', 'CB'), when it opens an acronym, file extension, hex chunk or code identifier, such as FBX, FB2, fbembed.dll, FBG:, FBiH, FBXW7, RBAC, .rb, rbash or a hex string like 1fbb5f2c, not the full words 'Facebook' or 'Firebird'.","s":[0.81819,0.997,0.924603,0.671363,0.951366,0.012548,0.013938,0.01279,0.01802,0.031444,0.010836,0.024773,0.043627,0.011174,0.023982]},{"p":"L18N8102+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires across the body of software security vulnerability advisories (CVE or CVSS write-ups describing attack vectors, local or unauthenticated attackers, exploitation, denial of service and affected versions), and more weakly in privacy or data-sharing boilerplate. It fires on ordinary words and punctuation throughout such text, especially on words about who can access or exploit something (local, unauthenticated, public, locally, allows).","s":[0.692963,0.265744,0.670375,0.298711,0.348026,0.013732,0.051072,0.035562,0.042521,0.033948,0.028084,0.04671,0.04963,0.064035,0.040481]},{"p":"L18N8102-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires inside phrases about the many online accounts, websites, apps or software tools a person uses or signs up for, typically in password, login or SaaS advice, on the function words and nouns of phrases like \"a different password for every site\", \"signing up for websites\", \"the apps you use every day\", \"multiple login credentials\" or \"120 SaaS apps in its stack\".","s":[1.054118,0.822836,0.548416,0.787583,0.899101,0.252519,0.187919,0.044511,0.017814,0.064633,0.010082,0.01598,0.014051,0.031685,0.002905]},{"p":"L18N8942+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a past-participle or -ed verb form completing a passive or participial construction ('has been given', 'can be said', 'should be considered', 'needs to be modified', 'an email automatically sent', 'cleaned up'), including the misused -ed forms typical of non-native English ('a comparative researched', 'a starting pointed'); it pushes against 'by' coming next.","s":[0.449104,0.151485,0.178615,0.485971,0.375905,0.047084,0.053554,0.048788,0.051799,0.037498,0.076087,0.05887,0.011944,0.14619,0.045304]},{"p":"L18N8942-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a past-tense/past-participle verb (usually an -ed form like controlled, represented, provided, quoted, detailed) that comes directly after a noun phrase, at the ambiguous spot where it could start a reduced passive clause ('the land controlled by the empire', 'complaints detailed in the report') or be the main verb; it is weak when the verb follows a pronoun or an auxiliary like 'was'.","s":[0.100302,0.043091,0.563425,0.133859,0.510194,0.02725,0.07668,0.032156,0.085568,0.010925,0.021987,0.00743,0.006017,0.010748,0.013656]},{"p":"L18N9686+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a verb that begins an instruction or call to action, most strongly a sentence-initial imperative in promotional or how-to web copy (\"Use the promo code…\", \"Browse our directory…\", \"Choose us…\", \"Hire experienced tutors…\", \"See articles below\"), and more weakly on main verbs after \"should be\"/\"can\" (\"should be cleaned\", \"could be shipped\", \"you can compile\").","s":[0.070935,0.048793,0.014818,0.165994,0.06897,0.001945,0.107576,0.129984,0.030145,0.052982,0.077444,0.014427,0.013261,0.113625,0.020171]},{"p":"L18N9686-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the casual imperative verb that opens a suggestion to add one more thing to a combination, especially \"throw\", \"add\", \"toss\", \"sprinkle\" or \"slap\" (often followed by \"in\"), as in \"Throw in a blindfold\" or \"Add a dusting of sugar and you've got...\", in chatty blog or review prose.","s":[0.391105,0.284322,0.287828,0.220595,0.464816,0.011717,0.019912,0.007103,0.018378,0.017444,0.029205,0.017766,0.046456,0.01149,0.029251]},{"p":"L19N10961+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the person, thing or word naming who or what takes over a role or position someone else held, or fills in for someone, in a sentence about succession or substitution (e.g. \"his son ___ became king\", \"took ___ over\", \"the second son succeeded him\", \"producer X accepted the award in his place\", \"replace the old pods with new ___\"), at the slot just before the successor's role is stated; it suppresses the word \"replacement\".","s":[0.444375,0.835786,0.430054,0.306833,0.310315,0.470904,0.242103,0.02237,0.008568,0.03256,0.046429,0.044752,0.016284,0.05188,0.0335]},{"p":"L19N10961-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word just before a coming 'replacement'/'successor'/'place' slot in a substitution phrase, especially the possessive pronoun in \"in its place\", \"his replacement\", \"her successor\", \"take their place\", or \"to\"/\"was\" right before \"replace\", predicting that a word meaning replacement comes next (it does not fire on the word replacement itself).","s":[0.499469,0.772051,0.236789,0.14114,0.021066,0.273737,0.426759,0.055161,0.032982,0.022148,0.011384,0.006813,0.007064,0.016132,0.007403]},{"p":"L19N1154+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on ordinary words and punctuation throughout passages whose running topic is sleep and waking: watching over a sleeping baby at nap time or at night, a narrator falling asleep and then waking up, or alarms and being woken in the middle of the night. It peaks at the transition right after someone falls asleep or just as they wake.","s":[0.308607,0.301264,0.378877,0.173703,0.334863,0.026278,0.024924,0.029438,0.018578,0.076677,0.00415,0.009558,0.016917,0.01041,0.012814]},{"p":"L19N1154-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Software and app UI or settings language about automatic behaviour and loading: fires on words like \"auto\", \"automatically\", \"Automatic\", \"Default\", \"load/loading/preloaded\", \"pull down to refresh\" and \"check for updates\" when they describe an app/website feature that runs by itself (auto-publish, auto-download, autoClose, autostart, load more), especially in plugin footers, settings menus, changelogs and front-end code.","s":[1.119074,0.522108,0.264399,1.178118,0.920393,0.515454,0.198026,0.149638,0.179186,0.086977,0.035022,0.037379,0.042477,0.061772,0.022742]},{"p":"L19N1554+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires in cell-biology text about proteins being moved, sorted, retained, recycled or degraded inside cells (membrane trafficking: endosomes, ER retention, recycling to the plasma membrane, SNX27/retromer, proteasome or lysosome degradation), peaking on the words and pieces of the sentence that describe where a protein is sent or what happens to it, such as 'recycling of', 'retained in the ER', 'trafficking to', 'targets it for destruction'.","s":[0.635606,0.582435,0.585919,0.559606,0.591471,0.023632,0.003436,0.138207,0.007612,0.033805,0.009327,0.016751,0.01836,0.010579,0.015304]},{"p":"L19N1554-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires inside long medical/biomedical texts, most strongly in academic reference lists of journal articles (author names like \"Chang SM\", DOI and ISSN hyphens, title words), and also in health/medical web prose about blood pressure, circulation and symptoms; it depends on a long preceding medical document, so short standalone texts will fire it only weakly.","s":[0.224402,0.172642,0.280806,0.210606,0.253552,0.026432,0.069073,0.016432,0.028233,0.030718,0.019072,0.023756,0.010034,0.037394,0.01604]},{"p":"L19N2718+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires in casual, lifestyle-type text about socializing and recurring leisure or community get-togethers: meeting people or friends, clubs, get-togethers, and regularly scheduled social events such as \"the first Friday of each month\" or \"a couple of times every week\". It peaks on the word where the gathering or its schedule is named (meet, Friday, get-togethers, times, calendar), and stays quiet on work meetings or non-social uses of these words.","s":[0.286633,0.95139,0.932238,0.314508,0.426652,0.082389,0.051953,0.135324,0.073356,0.007508,0.025643,0.024386,0.01727,0.031752,0.015181]},{"p":"L19N2718-","l":"TWO-REL","j":"11","f":0,"e":0,"x":"In announcement-style or promotional web text, fires on the noun naming a scheduled event or honor as it is introduced, such as dinner, luncheon, lunch, awards, prize, funeral or program (e.g. 'The awards ceremony takes place…', 'A luncheon will be served', 'Our Fall Dinner is set for…'), and also on function words inside stock site boilerplate ('Enjoy our collection of…', 'We have a new app', 'There is gonna be more…').","s":[0.466302,0.340735,0.379227,0.422073,0.324444,0.097802,0.043513,0.313798,0.096191,0.128481,0.024855,0.025913,0.025345,0.048824,0.025264]},{"p":"L19N6189+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word that picks out or quantifies one member of a set of alternatives that were just laid out (options, views, methods, interpretations), typically a selector right before or in place of a noun like option/approach/view/method: 'Which (side/one)', 'the latter', 'the former', 'the first/second/third', 'each', 'both', 'pick one', 'the majority/dominant view', 'more than one way', 'the only way'.","s":[0.335301,0.373651,0.118803,0.628596,0.404959,0.332054,0.019016,0.017886,0.011388,0.037338,0.010731,0.003878,0.023511,0.013915,0.003729]},{"p":"L19N6189-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires in expository prose at a slot that introduces kinds or aspects of something: on a word like \"different\", \"several\", \"main\", \"first\" or \"single\" just before an abstract category noun, and on that noun itself (dimension, layers, areas, sets, methods, decision, data), where the model expects a word like method, approach, option or technique.","s":[0.021964,0.210765,0.02275,0.386396,0.088179,0.011003,0.036798,0.033552,0.013143,0.021379,0.037788,0.027596,0.024842,0.03546,0.016499]},{"p":"L19N7374+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the head noun that closes a modifier-plus-noun phrase naming concrete things or items (often plural, e.g. 'dried beans', 'frozen scallops', 'the lyophilized protein', 'Wishlist items', 'study resources', 'phones, tablets'), especially when that phrase is the subject and its verb comes next, in web-page prose, FAQ or product text.","s":[0.282869,0.143098,0.759533,0.010256,0.016526,0.021076,0.178112,0.073258,0.089104,0.02226,0.063115,0.042423,0.025617,0.030713,0.029281]},{"p":"L19N7374-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word \"food\" or \"Food\" as a whole token, in any sense: everyday prose like \"food particles\", \"food coloring\" or \"produce an abundance of food\", and also in names and titles such as \"Food Network\", \"Journal of Food and Agriculture\", \"Food Wars\" or \"Ninja Foodi\"; related words like meal, cuisine or eating do not fire it.","s":[0.200944,0.591194,0.279894,0.254069,0.234495,0.029368,0.055549,0.017094,0.049402,0.019637,0.015756,0.036456,0.028143,0.019933,0.018005]},{"p":"L19N7865+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"In expository prose about art, literature, acting, history or social science, fires on the word (most often \"psychological\", \"social\" or a \"psych-\" prefix such as \"psychosocial\", or a verb like \"understanding\", \"revealing\" or \"interpreting\") that introduces what lies beneath the surface: the inner mental or social reality, motives or essence being probed, as in \"realism in documenting psychological traumas\" or \"in the hope of revealing universal principles\".","s":[0.678498,0.515726,0.330918,1.06729,0.121975,0.027578,0.033296,0.014192,0.10662,0.121975,0.010378,0.009783,0.012892,0.002374,0.024413]},{"p":"L19N7865-","l":"UNREL","j":"00","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires inside explicit porn or hookup-spam headlines on the modifier word just before a crude noun like \"Sluts\" (e.g. \"Teen\" in \"Meet Local Teen Sluts\", \"girl\" in \"Schoolgirl Sluts\", \"Buddy\" in \"Fuck Buddy\"), and also partway through famous aphorisms or quotations (Sun Tzu, Confucius, \"Where there is peace, God is\") on the word just before the punchline predicate.","s":[0.16866,0.360798,0.485534,0.225048,0.307293,0.052951,0.360798,0.034975,0.016353,0.049078,0.029754,0.051098,0.045873,0.037082,0.025715],"u":{"0":[0.40142,0.786527,0.293158,0.25772,0.610252],"1":[0.621582,0.654332,0.068087,0.031065,0.026]}},{"p":"L19N9036+","l":"TWO-REL","j":"11","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires inside a long, memorized multi-word proper name, most strongly on the linking word \"of\" or \"for\" in formal names of treaties and institutions (e.g. \"International Convention for the Protection of All Persons…\", \"International Bank for Reconstruction…\", \"National Commission for Protection of Child Rights\"), and also on the first piece of a multi-token personal name (Georg|iou, Josef von, Leon|ard) where the rest is predictable.","s":[0.797376,0.740243,0.732437,0.479113,0.116858,0.290132,0.034603,0.025897,0.042819,0.085609,0.059414,0.026817,0.027541,0.013853,0.046817]},{"p":"L19N9036-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a verb-like word start (Rid, Allow, Bring, Manage, Deliver, Show, Create) when it is not an ordinary mid-sentence verb, chiefly when it is the first piece of a surname right after a first name (Paul Ridker, John Ridley, Lawrence Alloway, C. Ridgeway, Rev. Bringewatt) or a capitalized imperative or menu-button label at the start of a line (\"Manage episode\", \"[Manage]\", \"Show more\", \"Create a network\").","s":[0.441624,0.577469,0.197572,0.710327,0.212557,0.027101,0.038462,0.036368,0.029705,0.051678,0.042486,0.028933,0.043427,0.02787,0.021173]},{"p":"L20N10809+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on verbs and participles meaning that separate things come together into one: merge, blend, combine(d), unite(d), unify/unified, join, mingle, overlap, share(d), all-encompassing, and on the word \"together\" after such a verb, especially where several distinct items fuse or are pooled (\"grey sky, black oil and brown desert merge\", \"the combined efforts\", \"is united in\").","s":[0.969139,1.000517,0.325027,0.409311,0.555241,0.068282,0.077495,0.069319,0.013669,0.043229,0.022824,0.029116,0.01664,0.057985,0.010167]},{"p":"L20N10809-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"In SEO-style Q&A web articles made of a question heading followed by an answer paragraph (especially AI-generated FAQ text), this fires at the end of an answer paragraph (the period plus blank line) before the next question heading, and on the stock hedge opener that starts an answer, such as \"There is no definitive / one answer to this question\" or \"Asked by:\".","s":[0.749133,0.522038,0.479399,0.672688,0.702368,0.024918,0.017429,0.041016,0.012986,0.022817,0.006948,0.033361,0.023099,0.020777,0.035235]},{"p":"L20N5759+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a lone letter 'e' or 'E' token that starts a split-up technical word or abbreviation in software or technical text: the 'e' of 'e.g.', the 'e'/'2' of 'e2e'/'E2E', or the first piece of a rare identifier such as ecdsa, com.apple.eawt, eee, eKanren, EObjects or Elixir; ordinary 'e' words tokenized whole, like 'error' or 'example', should not fire.","s":[0.330242,0.877118,0.726455,0.772024,0.59256,0.009928,0.054003,0.028917,0.094102,0.06289,0.023205,0.028068,0.034611,0.015522,0.021475]},{"p":"L20N5759-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the name \"Ella\" (or the \"lla\" right after a capital E) used as a person's first name, strongest when a surname follows, especially \"Ella Fitzgerald\" and also Ella Mai, Ella Baker and others; weaker spillover goes to other El-/E- name starts such as Elsa, Elkaim, Eliezer and Elkins.","s":[0.917767,0.671702,0.917767,0.955497,0.247272,0.021272,0.014046,0.002978,0.013284,0.027975,0.012092,0.009874,0.018874,0.020593,0.019423]},{"p":"L20N5782+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the preposition or noun in moral-accountability phrases that set up a following \"one's actions / crimes / what he did\", most strongly on \"for\" right after responsible / accountable / responsibility / remorse / serve time and on \"of\" right after consequences / impact / implications, i.e. the point where the text is about to name the deeds a person must answer for.","s":[0.505781,0.490671,0.184309,0.499287,0.169366,0.039004,0.014286,0.003304,0.024079,0.008696,0.017571,0.014543,0.031397,0.009358,0.013712]},{"p":"L20N5782-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a relational abstract noun that names something linked to an already-mentioned thing, mostly right after a possessive or verb like \"its\", \"their\", \"has\", \"have\", \"the\" (e.g. \"its consequences\", \"has implications\", \"its source\", \"their origin\", \"had no parallel\", \"the outcome\", \"many applications\", \"destination table\"): words like consequences, implications, outcome, source, origin, parallels, applications, repercussions, causes, equivalents, destination.","s":[0.481649,0.372038,0.552855,0.450452,0.457674,0.01876,0.004924,0.014882,0.003591,0.062102,0.007579,0.003499,0.003916,0.027126,0.020002]},{"p":"L20N5787+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a modifier or determiner in the middle of a noun phrase, just before its head noun, especially when the phrase is the object of a benefit verb such as enjoy, earn, get access to or achieve (e.g. \"enjoy members-only rewards\", \"access to special member coupons\", \"earn that 5x rewards rate\", \"achieve the more proactive goal\"), anticipating a head noun like rewards, rates, prices or prospects.","s":[0.523452,0.239815,0.193827,0.219698,0.149039,0.20711,0.12694,0.045001,0.110757,0.048598,0.081046,0.079759,0.091896,0.053989,0.076973]},{"p":"L20N5787-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on an abstract noun that directly follows \"the\" after a verb like have, has, give or allow, most strongly on \"potential\" in \"has the potential to\", and also on \"advantage\", \"flexibility\", \"position\", \"intention\", \"substitution\" or \"elimination\" in the same slot, where the noun names a capacity, chance or abstract action and usually leads into \"to\" or \"of\".","s":[0.354096,0.176757,0.209847,0.159364,0.275177,0.072346,0.063355,0.069333,0.01785,0.005353,0.016065,0.015633,0.026512,0.032765,0.108912]},{"p":"L20N8203+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the token immediately before a number that the reader can predict from earlier numbers in the text: the opening brace of a footnote marker such as \\textsuperscript{ or \\(^{ in a heavily footnoted paper, the space before a page number in a table of contents, the colon inside a running chat timestamp, the word 'Age', 'Line', 'verse' or 'pages' before a cited number, or the hyphen or space in a range like 45-46 inches.","s":[0.279253,0.013941,0.287401,0.471062,0.711314,0.030829,0.021583,0.045982,0.124026,0.027714,0.029864,0.029687,0.025587,0.036343,0.022317]},{"p":"L20N8203-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the connector right after the first number of a numeric range that starts with \"200\" or \"hundreds\" (or a similar round hundreds figure), i.e. the \"-\", \" to\", or \" or\" in phrases like \"200-300 people\", \"200 to 400 ppm\", \"hundreds or even thousands of\", predicting a larger second number is coming.","s":[0.618649,0.707107,0.275227,0.636993,0.315839,0.082645,0.008092,0.021165,0.06318,0.005023,0.028414,0.008456,0.019676,0.005868,0.009481]},{"p":"L20N8272+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word just before an upcoming quantity phrase, most strongly the verb in existential \"There were / There was / There are\" and in event verbs like \"saw\", \"received\" or \"attracted\" in news, sports or promo prose (e.g. \"There were a number of…\", \"this year saw some of the most…\", \"received a lot of praise\"), predicting a quantifier such as \"a number of\", \"several\", \"some\", \"many\" or \"thousands of\".","s":[0.597698,0.532257,0.093573,0.590219,0.177272,0.034805,0.070015,0.067384,0.024892,0.024679,0.037883,0.041268,0.025186,0.007366,0.061689]},{"p":"L20N8272-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"In promotional, SEO-style web copy (product guides, betting reviews, 'Looking for…' listing headers, 'this guide will equip you with…' intros), fires on the preposition or verb that links an offered benefit to the reader or target, especially 'for' after 'Looking'/'odds'/'outlines', 'with' after 'equip you'/'provide you'/'file', and 'you' after 'give'/'provide', the slot just before the thing being offered.","s":[0.382308,0.563363,0.27191,0.440024,0.229155,0.024328,0.015535,0.067124,0.015936,0.060869,0.025188,0.024795,0.039406,0.03331,0.016985]},{"p":"L20N8604+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires inside lists of everyday germ-prevention hygiene steps, peaking on the words in and between items like \"wash your hands\", \"hand sanitizer\", \"disinfecting surfaces\" and \"wear a mask\" (the hands/washing/sanitizer tokens and the commas and \"and\" joining them), typically in COVID or flu safety advice.","s":[0.674975,0.637255,0.793096,0.781363,0.74424,0.068881,0.155112,0.053186,-0.000786,0.446428,0.014514,0.013139,-7e-06,0.03211,0.001257]},{"p":"L20N8604-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on ordinary words, numbers and punctuation inside text about the COVID-19 pandemic or viral infection (case counts, lockdowns and reopenings, travel rules, vaccines, variants, virus names), firing most where the next words depend on that disease context, and promoting germ, infection and hygiene vocabulary.","s":[0.193202,0.314742,0.406532,0.17478,2e-06,0.035607,0.056194,0.048994,0.025691,0.062023,0.057498,0.056031,0.046008,0.045081,0.050769]},{"p":"L20N978+","l":"TWO-REL","j":"11","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the step number right after the word \"Step\" in a how-to or tutorial header (e.g. the \"3\" in \"Step 3: Cut the paper\"), most strongly in Instructables-style guides; it also fires more weakly on instruction-starting words like \"way\" in \"one way to do this\" or a \"1)\" opening a list of instructions.","s":[0.413757,0.388349,0.340738,0.396455,0.364693,0.004819,0.042837,0.012338,0.016428,0.027251,0.010427,0.017192,0.07091,0.044909,0.023722]},{"p":"L20N978-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the middle word of a stock idiomatic phrase, the function word or verb right before the phrase's fixed completion, as in 'this is where X comes [into] play', 'On [the] other hand', 'used in [the] past', 'as [is] obvious', 'where does X [come] from', 'The [good] news is', typically in explanatory, often non-native or translated-sounding English prose.","s":[0.415011,0.059104,0.184131,0.439755,0.147181,0.026897,0.015061,0.034794,0.025978,0.06315,0.026399,0.053152,0.031607,0.009136,0.033347]},{"p":"L21N10623+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires inside a hedged or negated claim of certainty, on the token right after a phrase like \"cannot be\", \"not necessarily\", \"no guarantee that\", \"we don't know\", \"it's impossible to know whether\", or on a sentence-level \"probably\", marking a statement whose truth is explicitly uncertain or not guaranteed (it suppresses \"possibly\"/\"potentially\"/\"might\" as next words).","s":[0.560499,0.189989,0.170398,0.275898,0.211359,0.063017,0.048273,0.049659,0.004979,0.245063,0.013627,0.027024,0.018797,0.01549,0.026127]},{"p":"L21N10623-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word just before a verdict on whether something qualifies as, counts as or seems like a category, typically in legal, Q&A or advice prose: the verb or adverb leading into that judgment ('may constitute torture', 'could amount to war crimes', 'does this sound like', 'a completely viable response', 'does 82 seem reasonable', 'listed as potentially valid'), and also on the comma or period that closes a hypothetical premise right before the question 'is it…?'.","s":[0.397333,0.417047,0.317188,0.247851,0.303112,0.039939,0.047976,0.011916,0.146098,0.076616,0.079191,0.035817,0.067275,0.016369,0.022776]},{"p":"L21N2063+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"In statute or legal-regulation text, fires on a noun or past participle naming a legal act or instrument (\"an order made\", \"a notice\", \"a direction given\", \"a licence required\", \"an injunction\") at the exact spot where the next words will be \"under / by / pursuant to [section/subsection X]\", i.e. it predicts the word \"under\" referring back to a provision.","s":[0.297281,0.130853,0.802602,0.184921,0.22825,0.103526,0.428917,0.008812,0.054513,0.039165,0.01061,0.006468,0.004587,0.003831,0.010435]},{"p":"L21N2063-","l":"UNK","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a noun or past participle in formal legal, terms-of-service or product-listing boilerplate (e.g. 'property obtained', 'data are processed', 'any device designed', 'the specific requirements', 'chosen carefully and independently') at the point where a qualifying phrase such as 'under [the law/terms]' or 'pursuant to' could come next.","s":[0.074405,0.212372,0.113957,0.116261,0.634455,0.032188,0.028094,0.019841,0.047822,0.034121,0.022994,0.034058,0.056233,0.011625,0.018071]},{"p":"L21N2147+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the token right before an attribution or credit in web or article text: a paragraph-initial \"By\" (a byline \"By Dr. X\", and also \"By including…\" or \"By 1400…\" openers), or the colon, dash or parenthesis in \"Image credit:\", \"Publisher:\", \"Copyright © 2020 -\" and an image caption's \"( Public domain )\", or \"data obtained from public sources\", wherever a name, source or credit is expected next.","s":[0.031305,0.170999,0.070623,0.441868,0.393783,0.024494,0.015026,0.021278,0.028425,0.025423,0.015141,0.019834,0.01965,0.016409,0.025932]},{"p":"L21N2147-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires in spammy promotional review/marketing copy for online products (online slot games, casino sites, dating sites and apps, reading platforms), most strongly on words in the middle of a feature pitch, such as 'advanced search filters', 'packed with amazing bonus features', 'read enchanting novels' or 'Golden Tour is a video slot'.","s":[0.944892,0.389933,0.850024,0.579339,0.818964,0.027287,0.037213,0.04719,0.03315,0.033717,0.036061,0.011849,0.019401,0.063874,0.026044]},{"p":"L21N3516+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the frequency adverb (often, frequently, commonly) inside an impersonal passive hedge such as \"It is often said / claimed / stated / pointed out / the case that…\", the formula that introduces a commonly held claim before a writer weighs it.","s":[0.62034,0.540446,0.316785,0.62034,0.540446,0.033013,0.07767,0.051028,0.010341,0.050878,0.028212,0.062058,0.022962,0.030593,0.024274]},{"p":"L21N3516-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the subject pronoun (I, he, she, you, we) or on the word 'times', 'moments' or 'point' inside a phrase like 'there were times when I…' or 'at one point I…', right before a verb of a passing negative inner state (wished, doubted, almost quit, wanted to punch, felt like giving up, thought); it predicts that such a verb comes next.","s":[0.356806,0.104801,0.355568,0.206318,0.559632,0.01342,0.099868,0.040965,0.004693,0.017911,0.015641,0.011789,0.008615,0.006643,0.023046]},{"p":"L21N3982+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the verb (or the word right before or after it) expressing that someone willingly chose to join or commit to something, such as \"signed\"/\"sign\" in \"signed up for\", \"volunteered\", \"opted in\" or \"offered to help\", and it peaks in phrases like \"what we signed up for\", \"please sign up\" and \"I volunteered to\"; it predicts volunteer/sign-up words next.","s":[0.926422,0.639054,0.564787,0.156172,0.993766,0.006565,0.069904,0.00578,0.058484,0.006364,0.015111,0.00612,0.011235,0.018342,0.011907]},{"p":"L21N3982-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the verb or slot naming whether someone joins or takes part in a group, process or activity, such as participate, engage, take part, remain in, or respond, especially right after framings like 'invited to', 'motivated to', 'encourage X to' or 'become a member … how does it'; the output pushes toward words like volunteer and signed.","s":[0.228515,0.582357,0.092838,0.094197,0.083989,0.141001,0.008198,0.029938,0.028287,0.032911,0.04408,0.015705,0.029059,0.132662,0.040323]},{"p":"L21N6379+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"In technical writing about display or audio quality measurement (color accuracy, calibration, test patterns, colorimeters, SPL/volume matching, human visual/auditory perception limits), fires on the tokens right where a measurement setup, standard, or perceptual threshold is being specified, such as \"at\" before a level, \"test\" before patterns or colors, \"using\" before a meter, or \"Human\" before \"Perceptual\".","s":[0.859917,0.663198,1.277165,0.62081,0.878469,0.035524,0.148034,0.01923,0.015341,0.019251,0.004775,0.005316,0.003033,0.005765,0.00706]},{"p":"L21N6379-","l":"TWO-REL","j":"110","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a capitalized common-word token, especially \" Star\" or \" Low\", that opens a person's name right after a first name or title, usually as the first piece of a longer surname (Annie Starke, Clarice Starling, Jack Lowden, Katie Lowes, Patrick Warburton, Billy Eichner, Captain Star Johnson), typically in cast lists, film reviews or TV write-ups.","s":[0.784628,0.558393,0.777035,0.654051,0.691784,0.018944,0.001483,0.09633,0.028824,0.02555,0.01398,0.003823,0.023603,0.002778,0.006747]},{"p":"L21N6487+","l":"TWO-REL","j":"11","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires mainly on the comma right after the boilerplate phrase \"Unless otherwise stated\" in the wiki page footer \"Unless otherwise stated, the content of this page is licensed under Creative Commons Attribution-ShareAlike\", and more weakly on mid-sentence function words or punctuation (\"in the\", \"respectively\", a comma before a list continuation) in dry expository or technical web prose.","s":[0.582671,0.412422,0.410369,0.582671,0.598491,0.210732,0.264556,0.081037,0.077537,0.208593,0.034746,0.049778,0.028961,0.042101,0.051616]},{"p":"L21N6487-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the comma (or a linking word like \"or\", \"below\", \"--\", \"with\", \"around\") that comes immediately after a price, rate, or level figure in financial/market news, e.g. \"traded at $15,550 a tonne⟨,⟩\" or \"closed at 1,650⟨,⟩\", the spot where a comparison like \"up from…\", \"down…\", or \"which is…\" will follow.","s":[0.909846,0.441511,1.086049,0.849836,0.775189,0.113297,0.191392,0.132208,0.08984,0.021405,0.024209,0.025529,0.00929,0.024537,0.018629]},{"p":"L21N7258+","l":"UNREL","j":"00","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires in concrete descriptions of physically striking or forcing through an object (hammering or tapping a stuck part, a club cracking a skull, a bat swung at someone, bullets entering armour, water or a body squeezing through holes or narrow openings), peaking on the action verb or the word right at the blow or entry point.","s":[0.558327,0.474291,0.458213,0.048159,0.248006,0.095682,0.037721,0.104285,0.050113,0.043689,0.020577,0.013348,0.024325,0.00566,0.03244],"u":{"0":[0.504672,0.1302,0.355002,0.069911,0.113264],"1":[0.193644,0.298372,0.092544,0.36346,0.272352]}},{"p":"L21N7258-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the verb (or verb-slot word) that sets up something passing through or into a barrier, typically right before \"through\": punch / chew / cut / drill / see / pierce / bore / burn a hole through, penetrate, piercing, and the word \"hole\" in such phrases, in both literal and figurative uses (see through a lie).","s":[0.694981,0.014889,0.42238,0.445637,0.768339,0.019318,0.002926,0.009847,0.00558,0.157984,0.007533,0.006203,0.042917,0.003255,0.010168]},{"p":"L21N7401+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"In research-level mathematics discussion text (MathOverflow/Math StackExchange threads, mathematicians' blogs), this fires most strongly at the slot just before a person's name is expected: the space after the dash that ends a comment and precedes the commenter's username and date (\"... has codimension 8. – Robert Bryant Nov 14 '11\"), the word \"by\" in \"Answer by\", and \"@\" when replying to someone; it fires only weakly on math text without such a name slot.","s":[0.384641,0.27199,0.621171,0.429183,0.450269,0.490116,0.246589,0.108792,0.431454,0.421009,0.004051,0.014622,0.016614,0.011131,0.012372]},{"p":"L21N7401-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"A diffuse genre detector for mathematics and theoretical computer science pages (OEIS sequence entries, Mathematica documentation, theorem proofs, logic and model-checking papers, code-golf and esolang chat), firing on structural tokens such as periods, line breaks, closing parentheses, IRC nick brackets and the first names of cited authors rather than on any one word; it builds up over a long document and likely cannot be reproduced well in a short text.","s":[1.076437,0.198486,0.623156,0.887609,0.394802,0.044843,0.239569,0.073688,0.019498,0.022786,0.034662,0.120498,0.113578,0.159262,0.018484]},{"p":"L21N8785+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the noun or adjective that sits right before an infinitive whose implied object it is, predicting that 'to + verb' comes next, as in 'the password to check', 'a fun animal to draw', 'a few places to look', or 'hard/easy/impossible to measure'.","s":[0.028047,0.086629,0.037152,0.036975,0.098178,0.028047,0.015782,0.084673,0.021314,0.253665,0.027486,0.009186,0.015267,0.041705,0.006507]},{"p":"L21N8785-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"In scientific or technical methods-style prose, fires on the word just before a likely 'analysis/measurement/calculation/detection/testing'-type noun, typically a preposition or verb such as 'was used for', 'included in the final', 'we will focus on', or a tool or method name that is about to be followed by 'analysis' or 'measurements'.","s":[0.558179,0.463273,0.337094,0.462994,0.742138,0.018874,0.027491,0.014235,0.017746,0.066955,0.016238,0.016286,0.019144,0.021859,0.024126]},{"p":"L22N10231+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on a word or word-piece in the middle of a phrase where the next token is a specific continuation, mostly in dating/relationship/hookup-site or domain-hosting web prose: \"control\" in \"full control over\", \"same-\" before \"sex\", \"Ashley\" before \"Madison\", \"matching\" in \"matching system\".","s":[0.597649,0.205515,0.31788,0.275395,0.517439,0.591129,0.399562,0.389606,0.252081,0.355542,0.030597,0.025483,0.025944,0.03317,0.014563]},{"p":"L22N10231-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on the verb \"sit\" or \"lay\" in its forms (sat, sit, sitting, sits, laid, laying), mostly after \"just\", \"don't\" or \"can't just\", where it means staying passive or idle (\"just sat there\", \"sit back and watch\", \"sit tight\"); it also fires on literal sitting or lying in narrative prose.","s":[0.668654,0.374493,0.453479,0.489279,0.539356,0.455513,0.115622,0.378421,0.005911,0.021781,0.005878,0.011707,0.005013,0.017981,0.019214]},{"p":"L22N1949+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires across ordinary tokens (function words, numbers, name pieces, punctuation) inside text about agriculture and food production as an industry, such as livestock and cattle markets, farm policy and USDA/FAO agencies, organic or Fairtrade certification, feed and crop trade; the trigger is this farming and food-supply topic in the surrounding words, not any single word.","s":[0.413977,0.519957,0.462209,0.539273,0.871968,0.041135,0.053353,0.039124,0.018978,0.030979,0.045278,0.018591,0.028178,0.02405,0.041385]},{"p":"L22N1949-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires at the point where a word naming a controlled cultivation or farming system is about to appear, most strongly on the first piece \"aqu\" of \"aquaculture\" (also \"hydro\" of hydroponics, \"green\" or \"greenhouse\", \"poly\" of polytunnels), in prose about fish, seaweed or crop farming, predicting a farming or cultivation word next.","s":[0.571234,0.781309,0.68408,0.639684,0.826287,0.0187,0.223893,0.008509,0.053237,0.096781,0.01243,0.012384,0.004288,0.003792,0.007677]},{"p":"L22N3469+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the verb (or the word right before it) in an idiom about drawing someone's notice, predicting that \"attention\", \"eye\" or \"heads\" comes next, as in \"grab/capture/attract/caught the attention\", \"get his attention\", \"brought her to the attention of\", \"caught my eye\", \"turn heads\".","s":[0.755977,0.759116,0.696344,0.704168,0.12865,0.319477,0.312294,0.049965,0.198901,0.005778,0.012194,0.006284,0.022068,0.00565,0.008819]},{"p":"L22N3469-","l":"UNK","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"No single token or meaning ties these together. The best guess is a mid-sentence noun, name or relative \"which\" in vivid narrative prose: intimate romance scenes about eyes, lips and kisses, or concert reviews naming a song just before saying how it moved the crowd, at points where a phrase like \"caught her attention\" or \"caught the crowd's eye\" could follow.","s":[0.29906,0.367913,0.189652,0.79982,0.292042,0.088305,0.042821,0.120657,0.071547,0.033042,0.039852,0.03849,0.038915,0.065736,0.023203]},{"p":"L22N4270+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the token \"macro\" (lowercase, usually beginning a word or compound like macro-level, macroeconomic, macroscopic, macrocosm, macroscale, or standing alone as \"macro effects/analysis\") in the sense of large-scale or big-picture, typically contrasted with micro; output promotes \"scale\".","s":[0.448725,0.82256,0.489095,0.564332,0.715248,0.18366,0.159199,0.184077,0.163662,0.112974,0.012638,0.013638,0.005585,0.007438,0.00944]},{"p":"L22N4270-","l":"TWO-REL","j":"11","f":0,"e":0,"x":"In English text that reads as translated from Chinese (cultivation web novels, Chinese product or blog articles), this fires on the first piece of a multi-word capitalized name for a sect, place, item or character (\"the Ascension Pavilion\", \"White Jade Castle\", \"the Snow Clan\", \"Qingzhi\", \"Niuhu\"), and on author transition phrases that announce what comes next (\"Next, we will introduce...\", \"So, how should you use it?\").","s":[0.247882,0.827335,0.575299,0.404007,0.307528,0.032145,0.012782,0.013433,0.006588,0.062946,0.017473,0.022119,0.030943,0.024909,0.0148]},{"p":"L22N6104+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on short consonant-cluster abbreviation tokens, especially ones containing F (TF, tf, CF, PF, FP, rf, Pfizer), that name a known entity, most strongly on \"tf\" in Python code such as \"import tensorflow as tf\" or \"tf.Session()\", and also on acronyms like TFP, CFPB, PFAS, TCFD, RFC and on \"Pfizer\" in vaccine news.","s":[0.518058,0.372958,0.472427,0.592731,0.431505,0.041686,0.006291,0.134539,0.287421,0.079081,0.037111,0.025697,0.048095,0.018074,0.022554]},{"p":"L22N6104-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on a middle word-piece inside a rare proper name or acronym, most strongly the 'osh'/'os'/'hos' piece of Persian names (Roshan, Khosrow, Hoshang, Noshahr, Hossein) and the 'CS' inside acronyms like NCSA, OCSPP and DCS, where the model is mid-way through spelling an unusual name whose ending is still to come.","s":[1.395682,0.876197,0.596284,0.910098,0.462593,0.062323,0.029204,0.021796,0.038628,0.022492,0.019117,0.007888,0.026384,0.020683,0.034906]},{"p":"L22N692+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"In news, celebrity or media-industry prose, fires on the connector right after a person, show or institution, usually 'in' or 'on' (also commas or 'and' in lists of outlets), that sets up where it is based or famous, such as a city, 'the country', 'television', a TV network or a news outlet, and also inside multi-token outlet names like Jewish Journal, HuffPost, Daily Wildcat or Oprah Winfrey Network.","s":[0.363175,0.560256,0.093249,0.258462,0.139394,0.057512,0.102867,0.116082,0.045232,0.041947,0.016908,0.028193,0.046669,0.01605,0.026988]},{"p":"L22N692-","l":"TWO-REL","j":"11","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the first token of a capitalized word that begins with a short starting piece, chiefly 'Es' opening Especially, Esri, Esra or Escherichia, and 'Sel' or 'Cele' opening the names Selena, Selma, Selam or Celeste, and also on the word 'Cultural' or 'Culture' when it opens a proper title such as Cultural Survival, Cultural Heritage program, Cultural Anthropology or NPR's Culture Desk.","s":[0.637494,0.493081,0.707257,0.854054,0.707358,0.014453,0.018256,0.018239,0.010878,0.047073,0.015867,0.018085,0.009539,0.030812,0.004292]},{"p":"L22N7483+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"In software/product feature descriptions, fires on the word just before a mention of reports, analytics, statistics or metrics is expected (e.g. \"view\", \"see\", \"generate\", \"access comprehensive\", \"detailed\", \"robust\", \"real-time\", \"in-depth\", \"Sales\" right before \"reports/analytics/statistics/insights\"), predicting that the next word is a reporting/analytics noun.","s":[0.609286,0.265123,0.086588,0.723455,0.48583,0.451009,0.067097,0.054341,0.283679,0.069013,0.006095,0.009922,0.004511,0.009241,0.001833]},{"p":"L22N7483-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"In data-aggregation code or metrics write-ups (SQL with COUNT/SUM/AVG/MAX, pandas or Spark .agg calls, performance-stat dumps, chart dataset configs, bulleted lists of query metrics), the pole fires on the comma, indentation or bullet right before the next aggregate column or metric field is named, anticipating a statistics/analytics word.","s":[0.625616,0.358246,0.494667,0.423867,0.304548,0.153037,0.073976,0.027388,0.030696,0.023809,0.015638,0.025299,0.013102,0.015142,0.014268]},{"p":"L22N7806+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the determiner right after \"of\" in a noun-plus-\"of\" phrase that classifies by kind or degree, such as \"of this kind\", \"of all types\", \"of every size\", \"of a different kind\", \"of unknown origin\", \"of its kind\", where the determiner sets up an upcoming word like kind, type, sort, nature, size, magnitude or origin.","s":[0.341619,0.577495,0.401736,0.569235,0.299482,0.014231,0.003146,0.007772,0.05728,0.022503,0.011486,0.019935,0.007075,0.001843,0.007211]},{"p":"L22N7806-","l":"TWO-REL","j":"11","f":0,"e":0,"x":"Fires on the word \"management\" in analytical prose about organizational methods (business strategy, agile/Scrum frameworks, improvement systems), especially as a modifier or object right before or after words like tools, techniques, styles, structure, system, e.g. \"business management and strategy tools\", \"agile project management tools\", \"Western management techniques\".","s":[0.325954,0.671217,0.062921,0.322759,0.852956,0.264339,0.115376,0.055426,0.1334,0.193643,0.011407,0.006967,0.010909,0.036181,0.015646]},{"p":"L22N7990+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on a token that starts a new name, product or phrase, such as a capitalized brand or title word (Killer, Jet, Kit, Studio, Edge, Get, Join), the first byte of an emoji, or a space before a number; this shape is common in web boilerplate, spec sheets and plugin lists, and what comes next must be a continuation of an unfinished word or name.","s":[0.063468,0.297932,0.092675,0.544648,0.374597,0.023835,0.025597,0.029197,0.053439,0.062145,0.153664,0.04749,0.070358,0.070023,0.172147]},{"p":"L22N7990-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires (negatively) on the token right after \"Samsung\" or similar tech/bike brand names (\"Samsung Smart\", \"SRAM\", \"Specialized\", \"its\", a space before \"1\" or \"24\"), and on a repeated \"Samsung\" itself, in product-review, deals or spec-sheet web text where a model name, number or product line is expected next.","s":[0.574314,1.112219,0.804858,0.375565,0.640112,0.030896,0.08397,0.072019,0.484263,0.011592,0.030536,0.040014,0.003315,0.016275,0.031465]},{"p":"L22N8185+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on the subject (usually \"it\" or \"would\" itself) in a wish or feature-request frame like \"it would be great/nice/awesome if...\", right where the next word is expected to be \"would\" or \"'d\" (or \"be\" after \"would\"), mostly in user comments, reviews and forum feature requests.","s":[0.546396,0.938375,0.630884,0.388578,0.338549,0.132881,0.144529,0.135052,0.038792,0.047112,0.006754,0.007528,0.004831,0.006036,0.008463]},{"p":"L22N8185-","l":"UNREL","j":"00","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on the \"'t\" of \"don't\" (also \"didn't\") in a first-person statement of desire, right where \"want to\" comes next (\"I don't want to...\", \"I don't want anyone to know\"); weaker, similar firing on \"I\" or \"you\" after a subject-setup in interviews, and on name pieces like \"Gur\", \"Vel\", \"Pel\", \"Gel\" after a first name in citations.","s":[0.096777,0.147932,0.394525,0.267174,0.40907,0.03939,0.009554,0.085248,0.020942,0.158747,0.067447,0.029232,0.060311,0.044502,0.017474],"u":{"0":[0.384458,0.212818,0.283376,0.194761,0.065375],"1":[0.563426,0.571473,0.713484,0.975744,0.816565]}},{"p":"L23N10037+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on a verb or participle (\"belong\", \"belonging\", \"belongs\", \"devoted\", \"applied\", \"adapted\", \"appeal\", \"relate\") that expects the preposition \"to\" next, mostly in formal, archaic or scholarly prose.","s":[0.56556,0.191983,0.481777,0.21826,0.189571,0.277533,0.02358,0.198385,0.05751,0.078122,0.027783,0.023526,0.023067,0.054123,0.02216]},{"p":"L23N10037-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires (negatively) on a word or word-piece in running prose that is the start of or a modifier before a name or term, where the next token is a continuation fragment or noun that is highly predictable, such as \"George\" before Sand/Eliot, \"gold\" before standard, \"erc\" in Tub-erc-ulous, and \"out\" before burst/growth; it is a mid-phrase, expectation-of-completion state in long encyclopedic or historical text.","s":[0.173891,0.279758,0.135674,0.388121,0.617556,0.183125,0.081524,0.235725,0.033806,0.200408,0.024358,0.013583,0.021388,0.011457,0.025822]},{"p":"L23N10071+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on a word or word piece in the middle of an ordinary English phrase, usually right after a preposition, determiner or intensifier (\"in\", \"of\", \"total\", \"relative\", \"constant\", \"utter\", \"by no\"), where the next word is a predictable noun or adjective that completes a fixed phrase such as \"in secret\", \"in relative obscurity\", \"lots of useless\" or \"utter shit\", in long-form web prose.","s":[0.838087,0.45288,0.083366,0.447009,0.251392,0.107664,0.073505,0.050522,0.135105,0.025815,0.030279,0.021519,0.097325,0.0167,0.015851]},{"p":"L23N10071-","l":"TWO-REL","j":"11","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on the word that sits right before a speech or reaction verb or a sentence-continuing clause in narrative and blog prose: a speaker subject (\"he\", \"I\", \"George\", \"they\", \"People\", a student or manager) just before \"said\"/\"says\", or a reaction noun or participle (\"laugh\", \"stunned\", \"withdrawn\", \"recognition\") that closes a clause.","s":[0.0583,0.150582,-0.000824,0.204647,0.057509,0.004139,0.02846,0.011063,0.141258,0.114808,0.054482,0.011303,0.037773,0.05081,0.040406]},{"p":"L23N10179+","l":"TWO-REL","j":"11","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on the token right before a multi-token proper name or product name is completed, so the next token is a mid-word fragment of that name: the \"Dream\" in Adobe Dreamweaver, \"Internet\" before Explorer, \"Alt\" before text, \"Meta\" before Knight, \"Met\" before roid, \"Strong\" before Bad, and the commas, \"and\" or \"as\" in lists of video game characters, especially around Dark Samus and Smash Bros, where a game character name comes next.","s":[0.746735,0.304254,0.87834,0.320107,0.245837,0.247393,0.082826,0.091048,0.010136,0.015337,0.013514,0.011412,0.02436,0.00804,0.0116]},{"p":"L23N10179-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires (negatively) on the token right after a proper-noun or title fragment has begun, mainly in video-game wiki or forum text (Mario, Luigi, Paper Mario, Layton, Falkreath, Glitzville), where the next token must continue a name or title: a word piece such as 'ath', 'ton' or 'itz', or a preposition or number inside a game title.","s":[0.666493,0.542875,1.53364,0.932239,0.321157,0.042719,0.020292,0.241326,0.073976,0.149428,0.040661,0.015202,0.018153,0.014126,0.040322]},{"p":"L23N1402+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on a word stem that is cut off mid-word, where the next piece is very likely a suffix such as \"y\" or \"ies\" (dishonest|y, wizard|ry, monarch|ies, rival|ries, honest|y, Gal|er|ia), so the stem is a whole token and the neuron predicts the ending.","s":[0.683077,0.367403,0.415697,0.3869,0.518547,0.032313,0.075791,0.013243,0.006484,0.006243,0.012206,0.009361,0.013403,0.014436,0.009544]},{"p":"L23N1402-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on a word fragment in the middle of a longer, non-English or rare word, usually Italian, Latin, German or Spanish or a proper name (e.g. \"separat|amente\", \"illustr|ativi\", \"Mod|ena\", \"Int|uitiva\", \"f|uturism\"), where the model expects the word to continue with a suffix rather than end in \"-y/-ies\".","s":[0.896997,0.263163,0.440894,0.814289,0.786263,0.025567,0.023085,0.023509,0.075401,0.013212,0.023724,0.016261,0.013157,0.01596,0.025891]},{"p":"L23N1430+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on a modifier or word piece sitting just before an event noun (day, ceremony, party, service, award, parade, dedication, ceremony), most strongly \"special\" in \"your/our special day\" in wedding and event copy, where the model expects a celebration or event word to follow and suppresses flower, decoration, cake and catering words.","s":[0.515523,0.724477,0.20028,0.431662,0.025533,0.217692,-1.8e-05,0.151824,0.079641,0.019948,0.032388,0.073278,0.038112,0.00361,0.016162]},{"p":"L23N1430-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires (negative) on function words and punctuation (commas, 'or', 'and', 'of', 'the', 'Can you guys suggest') that sit in event, wedding or gift-order text where the next word is likely to be a flower, decoration, cake or catering item, such as 'the dress, the venue, the flowers' or 'a range of extra gifts, from Fresh Fruits'.","s":[0.588533,0.428054,0.546008,0.434049,0.357935,0.014,0.023993,0.009942,0.02606,0.01288,0.001158,0.002311,0.004195,0.007752,0.006925]},{"p":"L23N1485+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on a function word or punctuation mark (for, if, not, mere, much, that, as, comma, colon, \" (!\" after \"if\") that sits just before a continuation the model can already predict. The strongest cases are in long expository, technical or code documents, where the next word is easy to guess from a parallel structure (\"as much X as Y\", \"not only X but\", \"Mode for ...\", \"if (!XExists\").","s":[0.040329,0.213412,0.043447,0.3022,0.101034,0.025721,0.066721,0.095852,0.070737,0.126453,0.08209,0.049641,0.030469,0.064879,0.089798]},{"p":"L23N1485-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on a verb, preposition, or word-piece that follows a subject and is about to take a noun phrase or clause: examples are 'compute', 'about', 'without', 'over', 'administer', 'evade', 'automate', 'Run', 'mentioning', and the 'apter' of 'inter-chapter'. It shows up in running prose, code comments and docstrings, and meeting notes, wherever a content word has just started a phrase and the next word is an object or complement; there is no single token string.","s":[0.718048,0.722878,0.837607,0.399544,0.501958,0.17106,0.167721,0.379785,0.231586,0.165289,0.094361,0.167855,0.189235,0.18094,0.106598]},{"p":"L23N6535+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on the object word (usually \"it\" or \"things\", sometimes a noun like \"belongings\" or \"gear\") right after the verb \"keep\" or \"kept\" in the sense of maintaining something in a state, as in \"keep things simple\", \"keep it secret\" or \"keep your gear compact\", just before the predicate adjective that says what state it is kept in.","s":[0.612257,0.685609,0.898386,0.56813,0.197303,0.102235,0.014721,0.142673,0.001768,0.116229,0.011033,0.020465,0.017859,0.01192,0.008469]},{"p":"L23N6535-","l":"TWO-REL","j":"11","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on a word-initial fragment such as \"Ac\", \"Me\" or \"Ep\" that starts a longer, rarer word like Acer, Acara, Acorn, Academic, Acari, Meantime or Epilogue, and on the broken first byte of a CJK character (often in code comments), so the model is mid-word and the next piece is unpredictable.","s":[0.01226,0.591006,0.48166,1.170666,0.577116,0.011625,0.039966,0.076608,0.0336,0.030225,0.034534,0.016223,0.027349,0.027656,0.013313]},{"p":"L23N809+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on function words, verbs and connectors (offers, provide, use, with, of, a range of, and) in promotional web copy from medical or aesthetic clinics, at the point where the text is about to name the treatments or services the clinic offers.","s":[0.432809,0.617101,0.805343,0.244959,0.612752,0.105371,0.146973,0.031717,0.048788,0.323539,0.002791,0.012872,0.006491,0.000619,0.009929]},{"p":"L23N809-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires strongly on the word-piece \"Gul\" (or \"ul\" after G) when it begins a rare proper name or place, such as Gulbenkian, Gulbinski, Gulian, Gulden Draak, Gulen, Gulkevichi, Gulati or Gulshan. A text that uses a common whole word like \"gull\" or \"gully\" is not the same shape.","s":[0.95084,1.041547,0.996765,0.952237,0.876552,0.005887,0.028324,0.035601,0.014644,0.020324,0.00955,0.020196,0.009549,0.020238,0.013565]},{"p":"L23N8425+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on the token right before an expected \"competition\"/\"competitive\" in business or economics prose, usually an intensifier or modifier (intense, stiff, fierce, cut-throat, healthy, highly, increased) or a linking word (is, 's, and, of) after a market or industry has been mentioned, so it is predicting that the next word is competition or competitive.","s":[0.567134,0.747841,0.111074,0.367717,0.201229,0.567134,0.747841,0.109508,0.004333,0.199915,0.003657,0.003387,0.01005,0.005904,0.008055]},{"p":"L23N8425-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on the function word or short token right before an expected word like \"wonder\", \"awe\", \"magic\" or \"imagination\" (e.g. \"a sense [of]\", \"a touch [of]\", \"sense of awe [and]\", \"inspire\", \"ignite\"), mostly in flowery inspirational prose about awe, wonder and whimsy.","s":[0.618553,0.361038,0.242072,0.291027,0.45986,0.019822,0.011957,0.028848,0.010366,0.010624,0.015622,0.006683,0.005588,0.009758,0.005519]},{"p":"L24N1234+","l":"UNK","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on a wide mix of ordinary tokens (word-piece endings of rare names like Macquart or Meigen, 'It' or 'can' starting a statement, periods, bullet markers) inside long web pages about niche fandom, wiki, trivia or academic content; no single token or meaning is shared, so short texts will probably not reproduce it reliably.","s":[0.279767,0.151681,0.226281,0.238749,0.108312,0.123309,0.036871,0.015023,0.071801,0.04994,0.046094,0.041092,0.029695,0.049352,0.036561]},{"p":"L24N1234-","l":"TWO-REL","j":"11","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires (negatively) on the token right after which a closing call-to-action or page-final sentence continues, mainly the apostrophe-t in \"Don't/Don’t\" at the start of a promotional closing line (\"Don't miss out\", \"Don't wait\"), and also sentence-ending periods that precede a final slogan, in marketing or SEO web prose.","s":[0.352815,0.495564,0.269806,0.317899,0.32819,0.071666,0.103879,0.03477,0.099253,0.276944,0.015218,0.046987,0.024945,0.023563,0.018805]},{"p":"L24N269+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on the preposition \"at\" that comes right before a clock time or a recorded page or book number in formal records, mostly \"adjourned at\" in meeting minutes, and also \"called to order at\" and \"Book N100 at page\"; it also fires on a few similar linking words such as \"assigned\", \"bearing\" and \"approved\" before an identifying number.","s":[0.499815,0.381307,0.32978,0.391433,0.601918,0.332686,0.395749,0.186224,0.011795,0.121296,0.028793,0.01241,0.026955,0.018048,0.025167]},{"p":"L24N269-","l":"TWO-REL","j":"111","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires negatively (always g+, u-) at positions where a slot-filling continuation is expected: a term-length or number quantity after \"elected/serve ... three-year terms\", a numeric price or count in a notice, a column or datatype in SQL/PL-SQL DDL, or a list item; it is not tied to one word, and appears in board-election notices, SQL code and fee notices.","s":[0.865505,0.725196,0.411079,0.155504,0.465664,0.053021,0.031065,0.021144,0.022738,0.034743,0.033139,0.037918,0.023459,0.033893,0.059333]},{"p":"L24N2862+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on the auxiliary or copula (\"be\", \"is\", \"are\", \"were\", \"was\") right after an abstract subject about evil, sin, problems, difficulties or risks in essay or sermon prose, where a passive participle (caused, created, found, cured) is about to follow; it also fires on \"or\"/\"and\" in \"real or (perceived/imagined)\" and on \"evil\" itself in moral-evil discussions.","s":[0.423416,0.397622,0.273172,0.284455,0.33513,0.027416,0.030539,0.018984,0.034968,0.037583,0.045418,0.048399,0.019071,0.029791,0.01608]},{"p":"L24N2862-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires (strongly negative) on the word right after a verb or conjunction that is about to take a problem noun as its object, such as disease, pollution, heat, fatigue, or dampness; it comes mostly in expository health, agriculture, medicine, or policy prose, at the point where the next word names a harmful condition or its removal (prevent ___ disease, avoid ___ pollution, remove ___ damp, severe ___ cases).","s":[0.205353,0.40952,0.110065,0.133413,0.089631,0.039529,0.023869,0.012937,0.017394,0.008269,0.025074,0.017974,0.022115,0.012551,0.036989]},{"p":"L24N3498+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on the token in narrative prose, often fiction or screenplay, where a door, trunk or similar hinged thing is the subject or object of a closing action and the next word is expected to be \"shut\", \"closed\" or \"closing\" (e.g. \"slammed the door\", \"the door\", \"kicking the door\", \"slamming it\"); it also fires in the Chinese equivalent of shutting the door.","s":[0.566961,0.577402,0.250846,0.597487,0.846291,0.476026,0.019818,0.02687,0.092391,0.411047,0.007568,0.001773,0.009555,0.008421,0.00443]},{"p":"L24N3498-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on the word \"there\" (also \"There\"/\"There's\", occasionally \"we\" or \"really\" after \"there\") when it starts an existential clause such as \"there is/are/'s no...\" or \"there must be...\", especially at the start of a clause or sentence in informal prose, dialogue or fiction, where a form of \"be\" is predicted next.","s":[0.323291,0.137737,0.085222,0.507451,0.480544,0.048343,0.057118,0.06769,0.036607,0.035563,0.025353,0.03487,0.008824,0.060783,0.03452]},{"p":"L24N3927+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on function words and punctuation (periods, commas, \"using\", \"for\", \"or\") inside casino and gambling text, mostly blackjack strategy and rules, where the next word is likely a club or casino-related term; it also fires on card-type lists like \"Discover, MasterCard\" and on bridge \"one club\".","s":[0.694764,0.540257,0.788456,0.802575,0.613009,0.056345,0.296946,0.015836,0.026911,0.041377,0.014449,0.007547,0.005978,0.016057,0.009624]},{"p":"L24N3927-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires (negative pole) inside gambling and card-game text, on the token just before a poker or casino term is expected, such as 'the' before flop/turn/board, 'bare' before Ace, 'Pai' before Gow, 'Three' before Card, or 'bacc' before arat.","s":[0.64264,0.877936,0.782917,0.59116,0.879311,0.018382,0.035753,0.015196,0.0693,0.045434,0.011188,0.020648,0.044521,0.008788,0.071806]},{"p":"L24N5066+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on the word \" memory\" (and similar computing nouns like \" data\", \" code\", \" variable\", \" global\", \" local\") in hardware or software engineering text, where the word is a modifier or head of a technical phrase and the next token is likely a noun such as cell, read, write, coverage or variable.","s":[0.465633,0.160987,0.334141,0.337309,0.135401,0.019087,0.014865,0.00884,0.026293,0.012788,0.041041,0.019425,0.012445,0.01787,0.027644]},{"p":"L24N5066-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires (negatively) on function words, commas and number words in technical or product prose where a specific technical item is expected next, such as connector or manufacturer names, coordination numbers and lists of company or component names (for example the comma between manufacturers in a list, \"such as\" before a connector type, \"six\" before \"-fold/-coordinate\"); it is a diffuse spec-list and electronics/chemistry pattern.","s":[0.726745,0.027341,0.605987,0.328828,0.209083,0.015471,0.019783,0.041331,0.016152,0.024863,0.072356,0.023279,0.020523,0.01559,0.012252]},{"p":"L24N5586+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on the function word or verb that comes just before an expected eucharistic or sacramental noun in Christian religious prose (e.g. 'celebrating the', 'bread and wine of', 'receive', 'partake of the', 'concelebrated', 'in the' before Eucharist, communion, sacraments, Mass, liturgy), so it is a prediction of the next token being a church-service term such as Eucharist or sacraments.","s":[0.566217,0.557707,0.956194,0.600379,0.513252,0.01117,0.017002,0.00841,0.034261,0.054587,0.007173,0.015725,0.009503,0.009877,0.011324]},{"p":"L24N5586-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires in sports game reports (basketball, hockey, football) on the word or space right before a game-clock time amount, such as 'with', 'just', 'under', 'to the last' or a bare space before '1:35 left', '10 seconds left' or '27 seconds after that', when the model expects a time count (seconds, minutes or hour) to come next.","s":[0.545592,0.415178,0.201009,0.69339,0.396754,0.477752,0.391398,0.20758,0.004497,0.052458,0.008761,0.011614,0.005295,0.014672,0.010475]},{"p":"L24N7411+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on the word or two just before an expected \"the long run / long term / long haul\" (or \"for life\") in evaluative prose, e.g. \"beneficial for you\", \"not viable in\", \"in it for\", \"cost-effective in\"; it is a prediction that the next word will be \"long\" (as in \"in the long run\").","s":[0.664564,0.396238,0.431468,0.244474,0.104961,0.664564,0.093314,0.008613,0.014796,0.002882,0.005654,0.007243,0.002227,0.006628,0.017837]},{"p":"L24N7411-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on common function words (a, I, did, is, should, please, we, and) or operator symbols (@, ~, parentheses) in forum, comment or technical web text, at the point where a short word is about to be followed by a noun, verb or adjective, so the next word is an open content slot.","s":[0.055239,0.120283,0.025503,0.17997,0.093183,0.032444,0.096193,0.102415,0.035264,0.033388,0.063512,0.042703,0.059872,0.047361,0.021613]},{"p":"L24N7822+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on the word or piece just before a Philippine or Pacific place name (often Cebu, Visayas, Dumaguete or Bohol), such as 'of', 'in', 'from', 'to', 'and', ',' or ' C' in 'Cebu', in travel or news text about the Philippines or Fiji, where the next token is expected to begin a place name.","s":[0.5897,0.712417,0.701476,0.503772,0.781602,0.009654,0.031376,0.001845,0.005981,0.020596,0.007497,0.010091,0.00434,0.006103,0.007066]},{"p":"L24N7822-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires (negatively) on a word-initial or mid-word piece of a proper name or loanword from the Philippines, Hong Kong or Southeast Asia (Mag-, L-ant-au, Pal-, Lum-, Lao), where the next piece is expected to complete a rarer place or person name.","s":[0.686551,1.457783,0.352507,0.341466,0.676696,0.033046,0.01936,0.033198,0.008716,0.007095,0.011996,0.017876,0.031533,0.022961,0.013508]},{"p":"L24N8193+","l":"TWO-REL","j":"11","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on the word right after \"what\" in a free relative or rhetorical \"what's\" construction (\"what's possible\", \"what's required\", \"what's not to like\", \"what is possible\", \"what was good\"), and more weakly on \"the\" or \"great\" after a verb like \"bring\", \"push\" or \"cut\", in English web prose.","s":[0.284358,0.110662,0.625821,0.031728,0.066581,0.168449,0.05175,0.097062,0.02471,0.041234,0.013121,0.033246,0.052745,0.085024,0.021101]},{"p":"L24N8193-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on the word \"of\" or the following \"the\" in the predicate phrase \"X is/are of (the) utmost/paramount/highest importance, priority, quality or essence\", where the model expects a superlative like utmost, paramount or highest to come next.","s":[0.701307,0.761021,0.685488,0.70333,0.765379,0.262895,0.043272,0.085479,0.007848,0.262565,0.003695,0.007191,0.015278,0.012562,0.009079]},{"p":"L25N10498+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on the token ' Debian' (or 'bian' completing 'De-bian') when Debian the Linux distribution is being discussed in technical text such as mailing lists, IRC logs, package docs and release notes, and also on separators near Debian-list headers.","s":[0.709854,1.030194,0.442848,0.71804,0.545909,0.199135,0.157873,0.04243,0.293509,0.210046,0.032647,0.007353,0.001344,0.02753,0.00598]},{"p":"L25N10498-","l":"TWO-REL","j":"11","f":0,"e":1,"x":"The token \" team\" (also \"Team\") used as a noun or modifier before another word (\"team effort\", \"team atmosphere\", \"Team Site\", \"team player\", \"team sport\", \"team members\"), plus, more weakly, function words like \"into\", \"our\", \"through\", \"of\" in Ubuntu/Canonical/SharePoint tech-blog text where the next word is likely a team or community noun.","s":[0.285431,0.515912,0.187358,0.159932,0.358586,0.095087,0.015014,0.075621,0.143652,0.012529,0.006775,0.005543,0.005617,0.001276,0.009565]},{"p":"L25N10599+","l":"TWO-REL","j":"11","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on a mid-phrase token where the model must guess the start of a rare word piece or identifier, mainly the second half of a camelCase identifier such as BookName, IName or CityName in C# or SQL code, and weaker on determiners and possessives (a, your, their, her, my) in the middle of a clause.","s":[0.108443,0.407748,0.104228,0.365394,0.147161,0.112572,0.224588,0.015519,0.019181,0.070034,0.010651,0.023036,0.014412,0.046066,0.029638]},{"p":"L25N10599-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on the word right after a subject or conditional where the sentence is heading into a change-of-state verb phrase, mostly a form of \"get\"/\"go\"/\"become\" plus a negative state (get nervous, gone bad, get lost, could get skewed, gets heated). The marked word is often \"get\"/\"gets\"/\"gone\" or a placeholder like \"something\"/\"anything\"/\"people\" just before one, in ordinary web prose.","s":[0.12807,0.524101,0.088189,0.357054,0.11859,0.037636,0.18295,0.036169,0.027412,0.024441,0.035231,0.037482,0.022201,0.016247,0.030433]},{"p":"L25N10878+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on the word just after a subject in running first-person or opinion prose, where a verb is about to come: a subject pronoun or contraction (I, I'd, I'll, I've, wouldn't) or an adverb or modal (just, simply, then, probably, might, may) that sits between a clause's subject and its main verb, mostly in forum, blog and comment text.","s":[0.37615,0.125998,0.073232,0.221829,0.171622,0.022741,0.031308,0.014362,0.029515,0.028664,0.054035,0.018495,0.023517,0.028141,0.016061]},{"p":"L25N10878-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on the word or name right before a verb that is expected to follow, in narratives or arguments about people pressuring, resisting, then yielding or complying: the subject, an adverb like \"finally\", \"eventually\" or \"soon\", or \"to\" or \"must\" before verbs like caved, acquiesced, relented, agreed, submit or comply.","s":[0.535622,0.489847,0.332931,0.503749,0.186988,0.120893,0.045931,0.047137,0.056618,0.037622,0.010315,0.016529,0.01265,0.012179,0.01377]},{"p":"L25N2013+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on a token that sits between a subject and its passive or verbal participle, mostly an auxiliary (\"was/were/been/be/is\") or an adverb (\"also/further/automatically/undoubtedly/greatly\"), in expository news, docs or changelog prose, where the next word is likely a past-participle verb such as upgraded, adapted, increased, replaced, or diminished.","s":[0.03223,0.122922,0.046456,0.044828,0.359153,0.027744,0.106093,0.042485,0.043368,0.034243,0.073316,0.068243,0.0659,0.042443,0.048667]},{"p":"L25N2013-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on a word, usually a verb or participle (cared, remained, seen, hidden, waiting, happened, cooked), that sits in the middle of a clause inside a subordinate or relative construction, where a verb or participle phrase is about to continue with 'for', 'on', 'in', 'before' or 'until'; it is not tied to topic, so it fires across fiction, essays, recipes and code comments.","s":[0.205906,0.223945,0.282568,0.317174,0.094411,0.048117,0.053103,0.03727,0.25038,0.07655,0.089547,0.062948,0.044787,0.114167,0.056472]},{"p":"L25N5377+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on a subject-position or pre-verb token in running English web prose (typically \"it\", \"now\", \"still\", \"as\", or a noun just before its clause's verb) where the next word should be a present-tense copula or verb such as \"is\" or \"'s\", not \"was\" or \"were\"; it marks that the sentence is in the present tense.","s":[0.004851,0.115347,0.021533,0.026677,0.028957,0.032473,0.039088,0.043442,0.0099,0.003855,0.064481,0.010973,0.076166,0.063599,0.007772]},{"p":"L25N5377-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on a relative-clause pronoun (mostly \"that\", also \"what\", \"it\" or \"they\" in a than/as/where comparison) right after a noun, in expository prose about change over time or technology, where the model expects \"was\" or \"were\" to follow, as in \"solutions that were...\" or \"as it used to be\".","s":[0.051261,0.445641,0.017126,0.270628,0.407857,0.051261,0.045129,0.008464,0.270628,0.031332,0.015779,0.005027,0.0073,0.007973,0.004121]},{"p":"L25N5542+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on function words or punctuation (the, its, 's, ', or, a, comma, colon) that sit right before an expected capitalized acronym or proper-name title, such as a government agency, program or exam/certification (DHS' AIS, the IELTS, FBI's Virtual Case File, the GMAT, SCJP, A Levels, the U.S. State Department's STEP) in US government, cybersecurity, or exam/certification prose.","s":[0.482407,0.790339,0.381111,0.899687,0.894925,0.358162,0.17017,0.192134,0.479857,0.864001,0.029719,0.019408,0.020633,0.019296,0.021141]},{"p":"L25N5542-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on a token inside a multi-token proper name or technical term when the following token is highly predictable and the first tokens are already clear, such as Computer→ized, Royal→Canadian, Microsoft→Dynamics, Dynamics 365→Field Service, and Canada's→Confederation.","s":[0.448339,0.339015,0.324519,0.30172,0.525366,0.036702,0.066811,0.032369,0.029861,0.184173,0.014459,0.028049,0.032709,0.002828,0.016352]},{"p":"L25N5749+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on the word just before a predicted particle \"out\" (or \"off\") in the idiom of exclusion or elimination, such as \"left out\", \"priced out\", \"ruled out\", \"written out\", \"cut out\", or \"X is out\" meaning rejected, so the model predicts \"out\".","s":[0.514229,0.69759,0.031602,0.506945,0.396728,0.021159,0.001092,0.000533,0.012085,0.052003,-1.3e-05,0.000279,0.001318,0.002017,0.002489]},{"p":"L25N5749-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on the token just before or inside a word meaning remove, delete, omit, skip or erase (e.g. \"nothing left to take away\", \"were removed\", \"must be omitted\", Chinese 去除/删除/忽略/省略, Japanese 消す/削除); it is strongest in Chinese, Japanese or Korean code comments, often on a partial-character byte piece.","s":[0.559978,0.786496,0.321916,0.05352,0.419249,0.106839,0.366234,0.096173,0.032457,0.211992,0.031426,0.026749,0.047252,0.022818,0.041952]},{"p":"L25N8888+","l":"TWO-REL","j":"11","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on a word or word-piece that must be completed by a word beginning with \"v\" or a heavy/vowel-start continuation, mostly the word \"heavy\" (heavy fire/artillery/smoke) and a bare \"v\" piece that starts a rare word such as vulture, vixen, vogue, vengeful, voodoo or vantage, in running English prose.","s":[0.489326,0.107443,0.60718,0.028847,0.66668,0.302569,0.203147,0.050381,0.046769,0.018824,0.026879,0.048145,0.024614,0.085081,0.018769]},{"p":"L25N8888-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on a word-initial fragment (\" fl\", \" Sc\", \" te\", \" T\", \" sc\") that begins a longer word and is split off from its continuation, most strongly \" fl\" before \"ing\" (a fling), \" Sc\" before \"rum\"/\"enarios\"/\"avenger\", and \" te\" before \"eming\"/\"ardrops\"; a whole-word use of these strings does not fire it.","s":[0.533195,1.011566,0.710122,0.262778,0.3054,0.008243,0.076974,0.037741,0.033234,0.506656,0.018489,0.021595,0.046291,0.044694,0.028923]},{"p":"L26N10050+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on function words and commas (of, to, by, with, and, where, a comma in an adjective or noun list) in technical or product prose about materials, plants, or physical durability, where the next word is a property or condition (weather, traffic, resist, tolerant, stable); it fires most at a document's later mid-sentence positions.","s":[0.514513,0.394293,0.409536,0.545807,0.3835,0.007796,0.022075,0.035013,0.005608,0.016525,0.015613,0.020928,0.020701,0.021443,0.023932]},{"p":"L26N10050-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on a function word or verb sitting mid-clause right where the next word is a noun object of a control, handling or restoration phrase (back, maintain, under control, with, by, and), most strongly in car-review, press-release and engineering or fitness prose about keeping or returning something to a stable, controlled state.","s":[0.770784,0.179343,0.699197,0.399769,0.053091,0.186567,0.014228,0.011077,0.070184,0.038812,0.148868,0.049179,0.031791,0.050685,0.213314]},{"p":"L26N3583+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on the subject of a clause (a noun, pronoun like He/We/I/they/it, or a relative word like What/which/that/who) right before a verb of beginning or starting (begins, starts, launches, introduces, will start) is expected, in narrative or expository web prose.","s":[0.253037,0.464288,0.222716,0.074233,0.0728,0.154777,0.292886,0.068992,0.009152,0.073131,0.032388,0.06165,0.028896,0.009885,0.180411]},{"p":"L26N3583-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on the last token of a noun phrase that is the subject of a sentence about a trend over time in economic, statistical or report-style prose (e.g. \"This rate\", \"China's gold holdings\", \"the Import/GDP ratio\", \"Wheat exports\"), where the next word is a change verb such as has grown, fell, declined or increased.","s":[0.292375,0.386486,0.528642,0.236907,0.213528,0.029038,0.091621,0.528642,0.162468,0.046041,0.009355,0.008312,0.012385,0.010509,0.009417]},{"p":"L26N4202+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on a token in the middle of a sentence inside tabletop role-playing game rules or session text (D&D, Pathfinder, Alternity, adventure modules and game-master advice), where the grammar leaves the next word open to game terms such as checks, saves, damage or conditions.","s":[0.439973,0.405334,0.396067,0.518626,0.275561,0.086025,0.063141,0.046303,0.031622,0.015398,0.010348,0.021579,0.013585,0.009888,0.019748]},{"p":"L26N4202-","l":"TWO-REL","j":"01","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires at the token right before a game-feat name is expected (\"Feats\", \"such as\", \"or\", \"and\", commas, a colon or a list dash after Power Attack, Combat Expertise or Improved Disarm), in tabletop RPG (Pathfinder/D&D) stat blocks and rules text, and in Warrior-class game forum posts.","s":[1.008023,0.826676,0.977878,0.906253,0.937029,0.334487,0.391175,0.018192,0.126629,0.005598,0.015205,0.020796,0.014499,0.028774,0.022566]},{"p":"L26N5340+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on the word right after a noun-phrase opener (a lot, the rest, some of, most of, there, the point, the verge, the generation) in spammy or machine-spliced web text with injected SEO keyword fragments (male enhancement, CBD gummies, place names, weight loss pills), as the model expects the continuation to be \"of\", \"are\" or a similar word.","s":[0.150054,0.387647,0.297285,0.619573,0.371402,0.013051,0.045651,0.28683,0.185353,0.06033,0.080487,0.047208,0.101962,0.068345,0.205439]},{"p":"L26N5340-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on a word in the middle of a phrase or clause where the next token is highly predictable and is a fixed continuation (e.g. \"look [forward] to\", \"followed [by]\", \"all I have to [do] is\", \"number [of]\", \"matter [of] time\", \"diameter [of]\", \"latitude [of]\"), mostly in informal web text, forum posts, reviews and code comments. In other words it marks a word that strongly demands a short function word such as \"of\", \"to\", \"by\" or \"is\" next, and it pushes down misspelled-word fragments.","s":[0.234159,0.066416,0.204579,0.040048,0.441562,0.16241,0.059382,0.152358,0.01957,0.190579,0.014673,0.005928,0.011506,0.002284,0.035315]},{"p":"L26N5599+","l":"TWO-REL","j":"11","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on words in performance-focused prose, especially the brand name \"Porsche\" (and other performance-car names like AMG, Audi S, R8) in motorsport and car-tuning news, and on improve/maximize words (maximum, optimize, improving, impact, help) in texts about boosting results or performance.","s":[0.813392,0.433688,0.697778,0.813392,0.388691,0.038172,0.055854,0.328418,0.20231,0.071693,0.046694,0.020422,0.017961,0.006184,0.004532]},{"p":"L26N5599-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires strongly negative on function words, commas, pronouns and word pieces inside sports game reports (hockey and soccer recaps) at a point where a goal or shot description is being narrated, where the next word is likely a goal-scoring detail such as a shot, finish or goal.","s":[0.530849,0.407053,0.783573,0.521689,0.567537,0.017417,0.021533,0.032173,0.013636,0.150482,0.014099,0.017906,0.018231,0.014896,0.010024]},{"p":"L26N6019+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on a function word or auxiliary (\"be\", \"being\", \"the\", \"and\", \"of\") sitting right before a likely \"televised\", \"TV\" or \"broadcast\" word, in running prose about sports, trials or live events, where the next word is probably \"televised\", \"broadcast\", \"televised\" or \"television\" (the neuron's output promotes TV words and suppresses \"board\").","s":[0.339742,0.878051,0.216169,0.780126,0.644513,0.116361,0.195379,1.223971,0.211975,0.018171,0.029709,0.019246,0.092269,0.113337,0.027774]},{"p":"L26N6019-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on a function word or possessive (the, to, as, 's, our, enough, his) in contexts about live production, broadcasting or sports media, at the spot where the next word is likely a media-production noun like truck, crew, board, sound, camera or station.","s":[0.281438,0.455285,0.527676,0.302957,0.457005,0.034728,0.019123,0.004008,0.011033,0.017463,0.019814,0.011914,0.019645,0.021308,0.009592]},{"p":"L26N718+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on the word just before a verb of confronting a challenge, mainly \"to\" after \"ready/prepared\" (ready to face/take on the day) and \"before\" after a verb (gather thoughts before, learn X before trying), where the next word is likely a confronting verb such as face, tackle, take on, deal with.","s":[0.505509,0.183767,0.312284,0.163753,0.431489,0.216524,0.027016,0.004086,0.006345,0.087967,0.04322,0.08713,0.011418,0.03591,0.014642]},{"p":"L26N718-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on a function word or word-piece (often \"that\", \"to\", \"can\", or a split word like \"berries\") in flowing descriptive prose, mainly food or recipe writing and descriptive essays, at a spot where a verb about the thing's effect, purpose or action comes next (e.g. \"a sauce that coats…\", \"used to accentuate…\", \"able to withstand…\"); it does not depend on any one token string.","s":[0.682262,0.610624,0.186524,0.830248,0.496095,0.04098,0.082766,0.033555,0.055065,0.051813,0.102846,0.029308,0.021395,0.005458,0.016246]},{"p":"L26N9188+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on the token right after a place, building or landscape subject noun phrase in descriptive travel, geography or history prose (a comma, \"is\", \"are\", \"and\", \"now\", \"today\"), where the model expects a descriptive clause or location detail about that place to follow.","s":[0.354161,0.09262,0.018494,0.289002,0.187588,0.03679,0.025903,0.044837,0.02872,0.019655,0.002925,0.0212,0.023396,0.027375,0.039348]},{"p":"L26N9188-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on the word right after a subject-and-verb clause that is about to take a quantity or rate phrase, such as 'in record numbers', 'at a prodigious rate', 'worth around $83 million', 'spends an estimated N125bn', or 'in droves', in news and blog prose.","s":[0.391109,0.537395,0.40994,0.30891,0.319478,0.133314,0.206818,0.024836,0.310347,0.061039,0.029078,0.076797,0.017228,0.011293,0.006315]},{"p":"L26N929+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on the last word (or a comma) of the first half of a concessive or contrastive \"not only X but also Y\" / \"may not X, but...\" construction, where the model expects the next token to be \"but\" and not a comma.","s":[0.33437,0.351215,0.261346,0.54997,0.397026,0.003615,0.031703,0.009253,0.176217,0.000874,0.036448,0.012139,0.008821,0.008988,0.004253]},{"p":"L26N929-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on a sentence-initial conversational interjection such as Yeah, Oh, Ah or Okay (also an opening First or a closing \"etc.)\") in informal blog, comment or dialogue prose, where the next token is almost always a comma or punctuation.","s":[0.15489,0.162235,0.288976,0.204162,0.598759,0.048478,0.071772,0.014918,0.004338,0.031011,0.04037,0.02255,0.016289,0.006368,0.02713]},{"p":"L26N9711+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on the token \"Code\"/\"code\" (as a word or the start of a word like Codecademy, Codecourse, \"code samples\", \"import code\") and on neighboring programming-context tokens, mostly in tech or programming pages where the next word is likely a continuation of a code or software term; it is strongest when the token is the start of a code-named resource.","s":[0.709818,0.278231,0.123194,0.536759,0.287802,0.030833,0.034862,0.022719,0.185429,0.015993,0.023591,0.020186,0.05525,0.009783,0.022396]},{"p":"L26N9711-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on function words, possessives, or word pieces (our, 's, the, a, and, ,) in music-industry and entertainment text when the next word is likely to be a music-business role or proper name, such as manager, engineer, roadie or a named institution; it also fires in a few cases on the middle pieces of tech or product names like jQTouch and iMacros.","s":[0.374151,0.410152,0.225711,0.484056,0.177738,0.068143,0.045803,0.045979,0.076146,0.058263,0.015883,0.044287,0.07072,0.013171,0.021699]},{"p":"L27N10591+","l":"TWO-REL","j":"11","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on a token that is about to be followed by a noun naming a specific compound or technical term (a wrestling move or boat part), most strongly on \"a\" or an adjective right before a move name in blow-by-blow pro wrestling match reports, and on word pieces mid-way through a compound name such as Eiffel Tower, Dreissker Bomb, teak or stainless steel in boat and gear write-ups.","s":[0.605194,0.727794,0.748949,0.344891,0.422676,0.078213,0.270292,0.013054,0.029713,0.013282,0.00464,0.089964,0.040707,0.015834,0.010652]},{"p":"L27N10591-","l":"UNK","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on a mid-sentence word right after a physical object, material or structural noun (roof, panel, metal, paving, mailbox, sailboat, sandbox) where the next token is a predicate or continuation, such as 'be', 'which', 'to' or 'are'; strongest in expository web prose, patents and SEO text. Some marked tokens are the object noun itself or a word piece, so the signal is diffuse.","s":[0.258119,0.429565,0.427139,0.275647,0.072505,0.224057,0.142286,0.032607,0.014417,0.023533,0.02274,0.02826,0.050372,0.018685,0.02535]},{"p":"L27N10663+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires in fiction (especially erotic or embarrassing, tongue-tied scenes) on the speaker's name/pronoun or the 'to' / 'could' right after a closing quote of halting dialogue, where the next words are a verb of struggling speech like 'managed to say', 'stammered' or 'stuttered'.","s":[0.460518,0.508582,0.355867,0.262097,0.275978,0.035217,0.062293,0.018571,0.021665,0.026825,0.040216,0.019574,0.022342,0.125642,0.030162]},{"p":"L27N10663-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on a function word or punctuation mark (mostly \"and\", \"I\", \"to\", \"that\", a comma or closing parenthesis) that comes right after a subject and an action or speech verb in narrative prose, usually first-person fanfic, and where the next word is a second verb or the verb of a quote tag (\"and grinned\", \"I tell him\").","s":[0.014736,0.1127,0.144251,0.081995,0.084466,0.017829,0.012127,0.033051,0.01335,0.008985,0.180722,0.010429,0.064235,0.031783,0.014552]},{"p":"L27N3309+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on function words or punctuation (\"out\", \"of\", \"your\", \"his\", \"my\", a comma) in sports and athletic-preparation prose, such as running, cycling, racing or boxing, right where the next word is likely \"training\" or \"trained\" (\"week in week out [training]\", \"weeks of [training]\", \"affected my [training]\").","s":[0.575214,0.299402,0.235743,0.463283,0.598431,0.575214,0.072961,0.152499,0.025643,0.067427,0.007348,0.009027,0.01419,0.018578,0.006266]},{"p":"L27N3309-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on function words (before, from, for, the, her, first, to, of, my) in sports-competition and training prose (race day, comp, marching band, bodybuilding) where the next word is likely to be 'training', 'trained' or a similar training/competition noun.","s":[0.392441,0.25079,0.164297,0.327917,0.218488,0.126462,0.018584,0.028309,0.013327,0.024979,0.006178,0.008261,0.007946,0.01182,0.012159]},{"p":"L27N7623+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on a function word or modifier (such as \"superior\", \"been\", \"much\", \"be\", \"mind\", \"need\", \"on\", \"over\", or a list comma) in English web prose, where the next word is likely an adjective, adverb, or participle describing quality or degree.","s":[0.035918,0.016155,0.016011,0.302647,0.022488,0.013875,0.016568,0.011015,0.003538,0.083764,0.034856,0.039615,0.027185,0.020057,0.050906]},{"p":"L27N7623-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":3,"e":1,"x":"Fires (negatively) on a word or function word, in technical manufacturing or surface-finishing prose, right where the next word is likely a surface-quality adjective like smooth, flat, black, bright or glossy, as in \"surface finish\", \"as bright as\", \"high surface quality\"; it also fires on Chinese verbs and nouns before a texture word like 光滑 (smooth) or 粗 (thick)."},{"p":"L27N7794+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on a possessive determiner or 's (my, his, their, your, 'natural', 'particular') that sits right before a predicted 'curiosity' or 'interest' noun, especially after verbs like satisfy, pique, or sate.","s":[0.701751,0.645722,0.543646,0.357167,0.376007,0.093871,0.006,0.543646,0.032848,0.014622,0.057249,0.062005,0.036386,0.006298,0.004193]},{"p":"L27N7794-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires strongly on function words and connectives (was, also, over, their, the, of, be, is, are, and, commas) in the middle of a clause of long English web, news or academic prose, where the next word is a content word that continues the sentence; it is a generic mid-sentence continuation pattern and does not depend on topic.","s":[0.087881,0.07456,0.018641,0.022512,0.042271,0.010309,0.008665,0.00932,0.020859,0.026954,0.014041,0.005512,0.015247,0.013246,0.051316]},{"p":"L27N8684+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on the word or comma right before a physical-appearance adjective in a description of how something looks or is worn or covered (fur, coats, armor, clothing, texture, color, shape), at a point where the next word is expected to be a material or surface descriptor like 'thick', 'dense', 'soft' or 'dark'.","s":[0.195828,0.484005,0.199548,0.407298,0.190058,0.037293,0.01601,0.021229,0.083804,0.010292,0.01783,0.026892,0.007331,0.033926,0.018544]},{"p":"L27N8684-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on the word or intensifier right before a predicted \"thin/slender/lean/bare/empty\" adjective (e.g. \"spread too\" → thin, \"limbs are too\" → thin, \"very\" → thin, \"stripped\" → bare), so it suppresses lean/spare/slim as the next word in descriptions of thinness or scarcity.","s":[0.119114,0.240212,0.216468,0.188618,0.514339,0.025268,0.140571,0.003726,0.093904,0.151892,0.027296,0.0288,0.002874,0.019546,0.001589]},{"p":"L27N8796+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on a modifier, determiner or word piece that sits right before a likely tech/web product noun, for example \"another ROM\", \"an addon package\", \"third-party apps\", \"other apps\", \"WordPress theme\", \"CroCoBlock\" or \"Overlay\"; the typical setting is blog or news prose about software, plugins, Android or WordPress, and the neuron predicts a service, app or platform noun next.","s":[0.439535,0.210542,0.56918,0.218161,0.540442,0.026503,0.025711,0.021986,0.014269,0.02435,0.017699,0.059085,0.023395,0.032907,0.01722]},{"p":"L27N8796-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires (negative sign) on the token that continues or completes a video game title or gaming-brand name, especially Grand Theft Auto V/GTA 5, Red Dead Redemption 2, Pokémon Go, Genshin Impact, or Witcher 3, and on nearby tokens in gaming articles, where the model expects the title to continue (a number, a sequel, or a series word).","s":[0.311403,0.534045,0.222609,0.601176,0.397595,0.03615,0.075193,0.009619,0.112339,0.082875,0.014775,0.017554,0.018863,0.014127,0.008124]},{"p":"L27N8999+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on a function word in the middle of a fixed, highly predictable phrase, where the next word is almost certain to be a verb like 'be', 'do' or 'tell': mainly 'to' in 'what to (do)' and 'remains to (be seen)', plus 'will' in 'only time will (tell)' and 'ever' or 'had' in 'had ever (seen)'.","s":[0.013831,0.022936,0.342381,0.194282,0.086607,0.004254,0.045808,0.116919,0.021333,0.235905,0.007815,0.010137,0.025165,0.010269,0.002789]},{"p":"L27N8999-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on the subject word starting a spoken clause inside dialogue in Western fiction, mostly \"There\" (as in \"There's/There ain't/there are\"), also \"I\", \"We\" or \"she\" before a contraction or a dropped verb, and the \"ess\" in \"Yessir\"; it is strongest in dialect dialogue in cowboy stories.","s":[0.198854,0.371177,0.497063,0.522358,0.375511,0.014394,0.007807,0.008208,0.021065,0.048874,0.035672,0.012881,0.018065,0.020467,0.00827]},{"p":"L27N955+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on the last word or number of a line, phrase, or lyric fragment that is complete enough for a line break to come next (e.g. \"for you\", \"burnt our house down\", \"Based on 3 reviews\", \"Chapter 1\", \"Price: Free\", \"01:42 PM\"), so it predicts a newline or separator in web text, lyrics, listings, and forum or review boilerplate.","s":[0.385606,0.387234,0.183221,0.405909,0.221794,6.1e-05,0.079222,-0.000131,0.001185,0.005093,0.032448,0.006892,0.075154,0.122205,0.018774]},{"p":"L27N955-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires, strongly negative, on the last token of a bolded or heading-style phrase in markdown-converted web documents (slides, guides, reports, forms), right before the closing ** or the line break: the final piece of a bold term, the ? or : ending a bold heading or question, a closing bracket, or the end of an email or URL domain.","s":[0.599972,0.541466,0.48864,0.350975,0.306201,0.016996,0.006711,0.18071,0.018457,0.013416,0.012147,0.012254,0.010705,0.017115,0.016833]},{"p":"L27N9651+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on function words or word pieces (our, a, and, with, of, 's, -up, -hand, -red) inside a noun phrase where a vintage-style or retro-flavored descriptive word (like 1950s/rockabilly style, classic cars, pompadour, cuffed jeans) is about to come next, i.e. when the next token is likely a descriptive adjective or noun for a nostalgic, retro, car/music/fashion item.","s":[0.450181,0.658983,0.241275,0.42643,0.419221,0.337959,0.134875,0.013107,0.010309,0.09204,0.007364,0.002506,0.006846,0.008983,0.005569]},{"p":"L27N9651-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires strongly (negative pole) on short function tokens such as commas, possessives (my/his/your), prepositions (with/of/like) and verb-ing pieces, mostly in prose about jazz, music and musicians or in pulpy narrative, at the point where a proper name, a body part or a person-noun is expected next; it is a diffuse next-token slot signal, not a single word.","s":[0.248602,0.162584,0.667523,0.142641,0.519498,0.011569,0.008256,0.018388,0.015976,0.032174,0.009454,0.012265,0.008958,0.029761,0.007516]},{"p":"L28N10177+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on the word just before an expected \"customer(s)\" or \"client(s)\", meaning a determiner or modifier (each, your, its, our, a, potential, prospective, loyal) in marketing, sales or business-review prose, where the next word is predicted to be the buyer noun.","s":[0.015408,0.275202,0.118029,0.210863,0.25779,0.015314,0.030252,0.007547,0.003353,0.027188,0.005333,0.006256,0.014585,0.005449,0.016628]},{"p":"L28N10177-","l":"UNK","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on mid-sentence function words and word pieces (with, a, of, get, and, a comma, res-) in the middle of a clause, where the next token is likely a noun phrase or a continuing word; it is strongest in long oceanography and sensor-tech web articles about gliders, sensors and species discoveries, and also fires on \"get\" in \"you get called\" or \"I get telemarketers\".","s":[0.884477,0.856723,0.177637,0.584966,0.704983,0.001172,0.006074,0.004681,0.018999,0.012924,0.00852,-0.0,0.017127,0.005027,0.013441]},{"p":"L28N1930+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on a word, usually a modifier or possessive like \"your\", \"colour\", \"architectural\" or \"art\", that sits just before the head noun of a creative or technical craft phrase (photography, audio, architecture, software, or jobs), where the model is predicting the next noun such as rendition, DAW, practice or software.","s":[0.142559,0.254507,0.068562,0.03097,0.144758,0.108296,0.188622,0.024409,0.199742,0.034787,0.021002,0.159235,0.066053,0.020135,0.058559]},{"p":"L28N1930-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on a modifier, determiner or preposition (e.g. \"work\", \"audit\", \"high\", \"any\", \"more\", \"of\") that sits just before an expected head noun or adjective in business, job-ad, professional or institutional web prose; the neuron marks the point where a noun phrase is still open and a specific continuation is predicted.","s":[0.14569,0.697709,0.08629,0.053352,0.278396,0.048851,0.047714,0.036621,0.023633,0.009863,0.026874,0.015601,0.021887,0.034556,0.023735]},{"p":"L28N3+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires mainly on the word \"the\" right after a verb or preposition of comparison or role (looks like, played, sounded like, based on, inspired, homage to, channelling, disguised as, be) when a famous person, character, or role is about to be named, so the next token is likely a name or title (a celebrity, character, or film).","s":[0.629275,0.362643,0.217302,0.074221,0.059766,0.302059,0.334973,0.045508,0.28789,0.059476,0.011864,0.006026,0.043371,0.020127,0.029006]},{"p":"L28N3-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires strongly on function words and verbs (\" a\", \" of\", \" the\", \" from\", \" and\", \" as\", \" to\") in the middle of a long English news, review, travel, or finance web article. It lands at a point where the next word is a plain-prose noun phrase or proper name, often a celebrity, A-lister, company, crypto, or group of people, and it is not tied to one token or topic.","s":[0.384913,0.118105,0.127819,0.423635,0.258125,0.018278,0.010199,0.020626,0.018453,0.016027,0.021106,0.036874,0.011013,0.032985,0.015222]},{"p":"L28N4478+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on a modifier or verb word (like most, so, that, more, particularly, a, your, looked, spoke) in spam-style or machine-translated web text where unrelated pill, supplement, CBD or exam-dump keywords are injected into the prose, and the neuron seems to be gearing up to predict a rare word-piece next.","s":[0.05416,0.07265,0.138697,0.025706,0.14292,0.05416,0.015495,0.022721,0.005217,0.0233,0.03665,0.024268,0.02416,0.024452,0.01918]},{"p":"L28N4478-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires (negatively) on a word in the middle of a clause, often a verb, auxiliary or adverb such as are, not, definitely, want, contract, fought or against, in low-quality spun, machine-paraphrased or spam-injected text (pill, male-enhancement and CBD ad keywords, Qatar job pages, awkward synonym-swapped prose), where the next word is a content word the model must predict.","s":[0.100225,0.204368,0.235577,0.409016,0.031989,0.014922,0.012764,0.00755,0.028373,0.026868,0.02017,0.016484,0.012504,0.019151,0.100122]},{"p":"L28N4613+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires in sports writing (match reports, previews, esports and league coverage) on the token right before a team or side is about to be the subject or object of a verb, such as 'his side', 'as they', 'Arg|yle', 'Na|Vi', or a team name; it is a team-sports-journalism context neuron that predicts a team-action verb or team name next.","s":[0.257845,0.285413,0.1186,0.053044,0.627481,0.035148,0.112529,0.01814,0.01431,0.038269,0.021901,0.015439,0.088435,0.028472,0.003592]},{"p":"L28N4613-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on a determiner, demonstrative or adjective in the middle of a noun phrase in English prose (such as \" similar\", \" this\", \" a\", \" terrible\", \" health\", \" same\", \" entire\") when the model expects the next word to be a noun that has already been used or implied, such as \"occurrence\", \"accident\", \"crisis\", \"nightmare\", or \"duration\".","s":[0.30274,0.051487,0.065323,0.027599,0.077418,0.019306,0.051211,0.053721,0.027372,0.011539,0.012052,0.011485,0.021822,0.016421,0.021107]},{"p":"L28N4785+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on a word or number just before the word \"lane\"/\"lanes\" is expected, such as the \"two\" in \"two-lane road\", \"single\" in \"single car driveway\", \"number of\" before \"lanes\", \"marked\", \"sea\" before \"lanes\"; it is a road-and-traffic prediction that the next token is \"lane(s)\" (or a lane-like word such as \"track\" or \"way\").","s":[0.858141,0.247378,0.467563,0.413993,0.441539,0.381891,0.095354,0.538485,0.249562,0.103918,0.019095,0.008291,0.01483,0.016235,0.010948]},{"p":"L28N4785-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on word pieces and function words inside place-name-heavy geography or travel text about Central Asia and Russia (Kyrgyzstan, Tajikistan, Pamir, Kalmykia), especially mid-way through transliterated toponyms such as Suusamyr, Kekemeren, Khan-Tengiri and Barguzinsky, and on the 'of', 'in' or 'the' just before the next place name.","s":[0.917183,0.615442,0.525951,0.494638,0.180077,0.106535,0.092439,0.05169,0.009283,0.019919,0.018933,0.019978,0.024014,0.027274,0.012908]},{"p":"L28N5955+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires in technical or academic expository prose (computing, engineering, research abstracts) on a token that sits in the middle of a technical noun phrase or just before it, such as \"to\" before a verb, \"code\", \"scheme\", \"framework\", \"security\", or \"race\", where the next word is a technical noun or verb continuing the topic.","s":[0.399782,0.138642,0.308106,0.103625,0.33777,0.028428,0.22355,0.018403,0.021877,0.010583,0.013921,0.027452,0.027867,0.011002,0.050481]},{"p":"L28N5955-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on a mid-phrase function word or modifier (for, the, more, with, a, and) in energy, engineering, finance or infrastructure prose, where the next word is an efficiency, cost or technical term that is still to come, such as a capital, trained, specialized or marine word.","s":[0.500164,0.309361,0.327829,0.584159,0.181602,0.265205,0.022754,0.016656,0.45181,0.006746,0.022888,0.018613,0.06005,0.035398,0.028493]},{"p":"L28N6373+","l":"TWO-REL","j":"11","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on a verb stem or Chinese character at the point where a past-tense or completive ending is expected next, either the \"ed\" in poetic elided forms (seem'd, suppress'd, touch'd, comp|ell|'d) or the character just before the particle 了 in Chinese text (变成了, 太容易了, 发生了).","s":[1.031721,0.466052,0.691309,0.528893,0.884179,0.009706,0.466052,0.081161,0.006339,0.157645,0.004925,0.005781,0.002514,0.007888,0.006217]},{"p":"L28N6373-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on the stem of an irregular or strong verb, usually \"beat\" (also draw, writ, sew, run, blow, shoot, put), when it is split from its past-participle ending, as in \"got beat| up\", \"beat|en\" or \"draw|n\", where the next token is expected to be -en, -n, -ed or -ing.","s":[0.591163,0.553742,0.288919,0.364547,0.212743,0.189827,0.014885,0.015192,0.057996,0.007865,0.001275,0.008367,0.016101,0.073058,0.035945]},{"p":"L28N8194+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on ordinary function words, verbs and punctuation (such as \"are\", \"to\", \"received\", \"the\", \"million\", commas, sentence-end periods) in the middle of a sentence inside web text about scholarships, awards, grants, fellowships, admissions or university programs; the topic is the trigger, not the token.","s":[0.35463,0.402573,0.358595,0.138691,0.53027,0.014107,0.009913,0.028115,0.021233,0.004016,0.018923,0.006376,0.089815,0.013451,0.013753]},{"p":"L28N8194-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on a short function word or connector (the, a, an, and, by, through, of, State, or a comma) in the middle of a sentence in formal essay, encyclopedic or news prose, at a point where a capitalized proper name or institution (a theatre, a Department, a Council, a Parks office) is about to follow.","s":[0.308518,0.418342,0.812665,0.030368,0.36186,0.281633,0.418342,0.046245,0.02181,0.208956,0.009312,0.022516,0.022778,0.027888,0.013111]},{"p":"L29N10720+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"It fires on the word or word-piece \"any\" (\"Any\"/\" any\"/\"ANY\") when it starts a discourse-closing or topic-shifting phrase like \"anyway(s)\", \"anyhow\", \"anywho\", \"in any case\", or \"at any rate\", usually right after an aside or tangent in blog or forum prose.","s":[0.047362,0.84484,0.381683,0.594056,0.595121,0.047362,0.273834,-2e-05,0.007603,0.074894,0.000363,0.000405,0.001086,-2.2e-05,0.000667]},{"p":"L29N10720-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires negatively on the first piece of a surname or personal name when the name is split into pieces, usually the second part of a compound or foreign surname, in citation lists and author bylines (e.g. a hyphen or the capital letter that starts the second name part, as in \"Torres-Rovira\", \"Jim Rokos\", \"Mat|heu\"), and it predicts the name's continuation rather than \"any\" words.","s":[0.595783,0.13675,0.168697,0.319837,0.212794,0.157368,0.057755,0.089448,0.050482,0.121451,0.181563,0.076412,0.122483,0.067516,0.078405]},{"p":"L29N10781+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on ordinary words (monkey, fungal, primitive, many, these, of, different, both) inside evolutionary-biology and phylogenetics prose, where the next word is likely to be \"lineage(s)\" or \"clade/line\"; it tracks that predicted continuation rather than any particular token.","s":[0.212766,0.397697,0.579243,0.273996,0.091394,0.006684,0.007434,0.007535,0.012545,0.088872,0.019463,0.012172,0.008476,0.030499,0.009611]},{"p":"L29N10781-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires inside academic or scientific prose (especially evolutionary biology, paleontology, phylogenetics, or medical-study text) on a mid-phrase word, such as 'the', 'of', 'with', 'extreme' or 'evolutionary', where the next word is an abstract scientific noun like 'relationships', 'history' or 'specializations'; it is tied to the document type and cannot be tied to a single token.","s":[0.566281,0.91612,0.01599,0.548139,0.320453,0.057258,0.007898,0.017812,0.006018,0.009217,0.026691,0.012928,0.011638,0.000825,0.008811]},{"p":"L29N10799+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on a word in a possessive or demonstrative noun phrase (\"your home country\", \"that department\", \"their specific niches\", \"a particular subject\") where the model expects \"particular\" or \"specific\" to come next, so it is suppressing those words; it is strongest in generic SEO or essay web prose about tailoring something to an individual's own situation.","s":[0.409146,0.334499,0.092047,0.156314,0.0299,0.105996,0.039944,0.015049,0.006124,0.062757,0.016305,0.015403,0.013987,0.126316,0.031126]},{"p":"L29N10799-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires (negatively) on the word right before the noun \"person\" (or similar like man, girl, participant), when an adjective or determiner like \"that\", \"typical\", \"lovely\", or \"specific\" is expected to be followed by \"person\"; the output suppresses \"particular\" and \"specific\", so the neuron appears to fire when a word like \"particular\" is not the next token.","s":[0.055447,0.029071,0.006949,0.018271,0.065453,0.021937,0.014202,0.008734,0.012914,0.008919,0.022149,0.0132,0.0067,0.035056,0.005322]},{"p":"L29N38+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on the paragraph-break token (a blank line, \"\\n\\n\", or sometimes a period followed by one) that ends a complete paragraph, heading, list item or headline in web prose, news or health-listicle text, where a new capitalized paragraph or title starts next; it also fires on single line breaks between patent-citation rows.","s":[0.177314,0.180799,0.069794,0.104454,0.485661,0.106379,0.059862,0.170285,0.074286,0.103308,0.12193,0.043848,0.062205,0.052743,0.068687]},{"p":"L29N38-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on structural boundary tokens (a newline or paragraph break, a semicolon-plus-blank-line, or the interior of a word or heading) at the end of a clause, list item, or line in long web, legal, code, or forum text, where the next token is likely to be the start of a new item, often a capitalised or lettered one such as (B), a name, or a bullet.","s":[0.186743,0.058244,0.148222,0.190058,0.642453,0.039741,0.051732,0.040519,0.037253,0.045167,0.032672,0.035473,0.046551,0.038638,0.041691]},{"p":"L29N4563+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on a sentence-initial or clause-initial subject word such as \"There\", \"This product\", \"he\" or \"they\" (usually just before a verb like is, are, has or would), most strongly in spammy machine-generated diet-pill and male-enhancement text and more weakly in ordinary narrative prose.","s":[0.10368,0.216557,0.123122,0.156796,0.099033,0.124075,0.026239,0.126981,-0.006165,0.023199,0.027713,0.033772,0.139311,0.041261,0.046266]},{"p":"L29N4563-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires strongly (negative pole) on a relative or subject word such as \"which\", \"that\", \"you\", \"there\" or \"people\" in low-quality, spun or machine-translated web text, right where the next word is likely a form of \"have\" or \"has\" (e.g. \"connect which [have]\", \"you [have] a problem\").","s":[0.048447,0.039906,0.02716,0.053558,0.020731,0.022375,0.022307,0.022157,0.026622,0.018331,0.031203,0.021538,0.018914,0.018542,0.020281]},{"p":"L29N4666+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires in long film, game, book or TV reviews and criticism, on an adverb or linking word that sits right before an evaluative adjective (e.g. \"emotionally\" before resonant, \"deeply\" before passionate, \"technically\" before rudimentary, \"is\" before exquisite), where the model expects an '-ic/-ally' style adjective or critical praise word next.","s":[0.919458,0.756144,0.450753,0.577365,0.507979,0.08472,0.013837,0.037851,0.064723,0.054426,0.012717,0.014542,0.038439,0.016732,0.022028]},{"p":"L29N4666-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on a function word or determiner (very, are, a, so, this, that, of, its) placed right after a fictional character or characters in a book, film or TV review, where an evaluative adjective describing the character (sympathetic, formulaic, three-dimensional, funny) is expected next.","s":[0.580034,0.046731,0.347056,0.214701,0.167671,0.085781,0.038116,0.128004,0.04262,0.00229,0.007785,0.003211,0.007685,0.019813,0.030522]},{"p":"L29N518+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on a word or function word in prose about stories, writing or literary analysis (such as reviews, essays, abstracts) at the point where the next word is expected to be a narrative or writing-craft term, e.g. 'narrative', 'story', 'plot', 'premise', 'storytelling'.","s":[0.328707,0.368044,0.283983,0.179469,0.308551,0.064794,0.083119,0.033474,0.050293,0.025224,0.112619,0.047563,0.039929,0.024811,0.02192]},{"p":"L29N518-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires negatively on a function word or word piece (such as \"the\", \"'s\", \"his\", \"well\", \"and\", or a verb like \"guiding\" or \"analyze\") in expository, academic or review prose, at the point where a noun phrase about a topic is still being built and a design, game, research or analysis term is expected next.","s":[0.126994,0.463072,0.422817,0.345412,0.021812,0.011208,0.060835,0.022865,0.007926,0.026191,0.066921,0.012732,0.014965,0.058631,0.008204]},{"p":"L29N7207+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on a token where the next word is predictable from earlier text, and the model is about to repeat a name, term, or phrase it already saw. Examples are ' include' then 'the characters', 'the' before 'X-Men' or 'Cougars', and 'USC' or 'Southern' before 'California', across news, fiction, and Q&A prose.","s":[0.158109,0.580372,0.335783,0.06036,0.029768,0.131115,0.401195,0.080339,0.040159,0.021557,0.037729,0.033944,0.019584,0.01584,0.024588]},{"p":"L29N7207-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"It fires on a copula or auxiliary verb (is, are, was, were) or an adverb/determiner (actually, somewhat, almost, utterly, left, a) that sits right after a subject and just before an evaluative predicate, where the model expects a negative or critical adjective next (broken, ill-suited, dead wrong, ignorant, incapable, left out), in opinionated essay or forum prose.","s":[0.327383,0.290903,0.542859,0.094482,0.39795,0.002996,0.218405,0.057438,0.038083,0.010932,0.006155,0.002124,0.045909,0.003774,0.048359]},{"p":"L29N9282+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on function words, verbs and punctuation (use/using/employ, of, in, from, dashes, commas) inside crime, hacking, gang-stalking or law-enforcement prose, where the next word is likely a crime/cyber/terrorism-type term such as cyber, criminal, pirate or terrorist.","s":[0.636773,0.779881,0.425958,0.433479,0.396554,0.01295,0.010642,0.004689,0.018327,0.008728,0.01775,0.014559,0.017627,0.013981,0.019544]},{"p":"L29N9282-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on the token just before a likely \"cyber\"-, hacker-, pirate- or terrorist-type word (e.g. \"cyber\" in \"cyber criminals\"), most often a function word or comma inside text about cybersecurity, hacking or terrorism where such a word is expected next.","s":[0.146599,0.012165,0.236275,0.174275,0.097198,0.005952,0.009147,0.002862,0.010744,0.01971,0.009209,0.01317,0.010849,0.008104,0.005983]},{"p":"L30N10031+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on a common function word or short token (with, for, has, the, to, an angle bracket) in the middle of a sentence in technical Linux/open-source/hardware text (IRC logs, sysadmin logs, driver or kernel notes, Slashdot comments), where the next token is likely a technical noun or name.","s":[0.765225,0.395153,0.158261,0.570184,0.343694,0.05671,0.130948,0.05581,0.073172,0.02195,0.089787,0.020465,0.013411,0.018274,0.012024]},{"p":"L30N10031-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires (negatively) on common function words, determiners and punctuation (this, a, an, for, is, was, if, via, '.', ']') in running English technical or forum prose, where the model is mid-clause and expects a common noun, adjective or ordinary continuation next; it is a broad mid-sentence position signal rather than any specific word or topic.","s":[0.344555,0.107795,0.177908,0.12507,0.159198,0.037547,0.11246,0.011942,0.15837,0.126398,0.037514,0.099368,0.036401,0.035283,0.096426]},{"p":"L30N1510+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on a verb or preposition that is about to be followed by an article, usually \" a\" or \" an\" (sometimes \"the\" or \"common\"), as in 'focused on', 'work towards', 'agree upon', 'ensure a', 'have a', or 'pick a'; this is a next-token prediction of an indefinite article or determiner, mostly in long English prose such as interviews, essays and blog posts.","s":[0.023119,0.266516,0.006142,0.268343,0.006969,0.023164,0.147567,0.006855,0.005675,0.003814,0.007874,0.015617,0.002158,0.012726,0.007477]},{"p":"L30N1510-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on the word right after a determiner like \"a\", \"an\", \"one\" or \"the\" (or on that article itself) when a singular countable noun phrase is still unfinished, so the model expects an adjective or noun, such as \"a single\", \"one main\", \"a second\", \"a natural\" or \"has a\".","s":[0.145558,0.068122,-0.001843,0.03801,0.026203,0.03328,0.01645,0.022205,0.045281,0.02096,0.017229,0.027338,0.010292,0.020569,0.028374]},{"p":"L30N3071+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on a word, usually a function word or noun-phrase starter, in tech, software or web-article prose (especially machine-paraphrased text with odd word swaps) when the next word is expected to be a computing or system term such as \"system\", \"network\", \"systems\" or \"platform\", as in \"operating ___\", \"social ___\", \"immune ___\" or \"Windows ___\".","s":[0.050559,0.019199,0.021748,0.027496,0.067169,0.032549,0.007928,0.002985,0.019721,0.004472,0.025437,0.011176,0.011278,0.010806,0.007419]},{"p":"L30N3071-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on a modifier or preposition token (often \"glass\", \"plastic\", \"very\", \"more\", \"into\") sitting right before a concrete noun or adjective that the text is about to supply, mostly in English web prose, recipes and product or home-goods descriptions, where the next word is an expected container, material or descriptive term.","s":[0.118744,0.478474,0.278445,0.281823,0.14904,0.010181,0.214612,0.044782,0.002749,0.007603,0.025781,0.008952,0.092373,0.008115,0.01308]},{"p":"L30N3233+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on a short word-fragment token in the middle of a longer word or name, mostly a vowel-initial piece like i, ey, ali, emi, ila or a stray piece like \" straight\", where the model is mid-word and the next token must be another suffix-like fragment; it is most common in Cyrillic or Turkish words and in rare names or spam text.","s":[0.589391,0.277431,0.363961,0.540172,0.212887,0.038361,0.018342,0.206023,0.311923,0.006573,0.108015,0.140584,0.038597,0.033338,0.117522]},{"p":"L30N3233-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on a middle word-piece inside a longer proper noun or place name (not the first or last piece) where the name's continuation is very predictable, especially the 'ers/esters/ges/ens' piece before '-shire' in English counties (Leicestershire, Cambridgeshire, Gloucestershire) and the 'NS' in TOWNSHIP, plus 'Hunts' in Huntsville, 'Ins' in HDInsight and 'ers' in Huddersfield.","s":[0.318669,0.621499,0.331035,0.493338,0.572571,0.049208,0.06381,0.015765,0.114976,0.019316,0.016281,0.015496,0.064027,0.164541,0.016082]},{"p":"L30N6280+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on function words and punctuation (to, for, of, as, that, a, a closing parenthesis, a comma) in the middle of a sentence in dense scientific or technical prose, such as physics, chemistry or computational biology abstracts, where the model expects a technical term to come next.","s":[0.380229,0.146462,0.108981,0.419253,0.192507,0.018027,0.012871,0.019615,0.020867,0.028948,0.021049,0.008453,0.022364,0.017582,0.02216]},{"p":"L30N6280-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires strongly (negative pole) inside musician, conductor, performer or film-festival biographies and program notes, on the token right after a person's name or surname has already been introduced, such as a repeated surname or last-name piece, \"He\"/\"She\", \"Dr.\"/\"Ms.\" plus name, or a comma/\"and\" in a biographical sentence, where the next token continues that person's career facts.","s":[0.829981,0.636926,0.652782,0.303637,0.671738,0.111206,0.051997,0.011072,0.032416,0.07833,0.033454,0.018857,0.002376,0.01474,0.008219]},{"p":"L30N6507+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on the word just before an expected \"laboratory\" or \"lab\" (or a therapy/clinic word), such as \"in the\", \"our\", \"analytical\" or \"controlled\", in scientific or clinical prose, and also on the first piece of a split \"laboratory\".","s":[0.223454,0.120012,0.091135,0.140283,0.113312,0.044658,0.090076,0.015679,0.016482,0.007509,0.012584,0.011298,0.007897,0.019212,0.048702]},{"p":"L30N6507-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on small connecting words and verbs (provide, study, a, and, of, with, free) in English prose about medicine, hospitals, nursing homes, health care, welfare or charity, at the point where a health, care or medical-service word is likely to come next.","s":[0.733651,0.123913,0.585802,0.269567,0.477107,0.619252,0.028909,0.363959,0.099857,0.041858,0.00257,0.002198,-0.000206,0.053753,0.003331]},{"p":"L30N7717+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on a mid-sentence token in running prose, where the next token is a continuation of a phrase or a word (a function word, comma, or inner word-piece such as the end of 'metaiodobenzylguanidine/MIBG'), with no single dominant token or topic; it is strongest in medical, recipe, and technical web text.","s":[0.523126,0.152709,0.073329,0.163607,0.287701,0.000734,0.002327,0.005981,0.009568,0.021803,0.022309,0.016867,0.012782,0.167616,0.040937]},{"p":"L30N7717-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on the first piece of a compound word or phrase where the next token will be a continuation, most strongly the word 'snow' as the start of snowboarding, snowshoeing or snowmobiling, and also modifier-like pieces such as 'wild' before '-life', 'blood' before 'clotting', 'heat' before 'treatment', or 'dry' before 'rotting'.","s":[0.835853,0.792189,0.453479,0.488412,0.217532,0.036745,0.040616,0.044777,0.044501,0.022315,0.036973,0.017827,0.023603,0.041332,0.017908]},{"p":"L30N8447+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires mid-phrase on a word or word-piece (often an adjective or suffix fragment like \"-ate\", \"-cent\", \"alternate\", \"suitable\") where the next token is likely a noun or continuation of a noun phrase, in older formal or archival prose about dance, theatre, and security administration; this is a generic mid-phrase pattern in long documents and is hard to reproduce in short texts.","s":[0.126247,0.505986,0.02962,0.330729,0.364991,0.012451,0.119542,0.128751,0.051949,0.009218,0.055546,0.022242,0.008162,0.01084,0.010256]},{"p":"L30N8447-","l":"UNK","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on function words and modifiers that sit mid-phrase, where a noun or further modifier must follow (\"the waist[coat] factory's\", \"as the head of\", \"his business partner\", \"general manager\", \"a man\", \"all the\", \"our young boys\"). It is strongest in older biographical, obituary, legal, historical or OCR-scanned newspaper prose; it is a broad syntactic mid-noun-phrase pattern, not a single topic.","s":[0.166289,0.206026,0.405847,0.00625,0.29363,0.00044,0.002756,0.012827,0.010278,0.010664,0.010204,0.031396,0.007559,0.010829,0.020272]},{"p":"L30N9731+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on sentence/heading boundary tokens (a colon after a step or tip label, a period ending a short topic sentence, a question mark, a bullet or indent) in English business, self-help and how-to web prose, where the next word is a new sentence or list item that begins a fresh word, and the model should not continue with a word starting \"prior\".","s":[0.21774,0.107134,0.226278,0.031083,0.020693,0.040071,0.25632,0.01867,0.003046,0.035075,-0.001384,0.00645,0.006888,-7.1e-05,-6.8e-05]},{"p":"L30N9731-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on the token right before an expected \"priority/priorities/prioritize\" word, such as a modifier (\"high\", \"top\", \"highest\", \"key\", \"strategic\", \"cultural\") or a prefix (\"re\", \"Str\") that makes that word the likely next token, and it suppresses \"prior\" word pieces.","s":[0.347372,0.565819,0.107085,0.317097,0.599395,0.383718,0.198682,0.067067,0.317097,0.394933,0.020455,0.010668,0.021647,0.018427,0.008629]},{"p":"L31N10276+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires at a token where the next word is very likely to be a G-initial word or piece (GREET, GUEST, GRADE, GALLERY, GUARANTEES, G-names), mostly inside all-caps headings such as RIDES WHERE GUEST, the Greek ΚΑΤΗΓΟΡΙΑ in SQL dumps (the byte piece just before the Γ), and a few odd spots; the neuron suppresses G-words, so it fires when a G word is expected next.","s":[0.057462,0.030363,0.290967,0.103318,0.101012,0.005642,0.030363,0.290967,0.103318,0.128352,0.002911,0.010915,0.01721,0.00273,0.015178]},{"p":"L31N10276-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires when the next word is likely to begin with the letter G, mainly the next word in a G-alliteration chain or a G-initial acronym expansion (\"Great green gorillas growing grapes in a gorgeous glass greenhouse\", \"Doctrinal Review and Approval Group\", \"money-back guarantee\"); it suppresses g-words like grammar, glance, glow and gender.","s":[0.425177,0.005033,0.015155,0.211592,0.022024,0.001995,0.005033,0.015573,0.008185,0.002325,0.003829,0.002741,0.004156,0.003991,0.002374]},{"p":"L31N10791+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on a short function word or word-start piece (\"in\", \"That\", \"very\", \"any\", \"or\", \"to\", \"E\"/\"e\" before \"very\"/\"ach\"/\"xamining\") at the start of a clause or phrase, mostly in old, OCR-garbled, legal, or archaic text, where the next token is likely to be an ordinary word or the continuation of a word split across tokens.","s":[0.450741,0.453839,0.159189,0.071393,0.448489,0.002936,0.037944,0.008367,0.085556,0.015792,0.00554,0.015321,0.005984,0.015673,0.011865]},{"p":"L31N10791-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires strongly on a function word or adverb in the middle of a clause, in old, formal or archaic prose (Victorian biographies, sermons, encyclopedia entries, legal text), where the next token can be anything except a form of \"charge\" (for example \"been\", \"most\", \"has\", \"full\", \"were\", \"ally\", \"ason\"); the model is suppressing \"charge/charging\" there.","s":[0.088566,0.082605,0.194492,0.38788,0.534834,0.011714,0.010391,0.038571,0.02212,0.015398,0.011619,0.023465,0.021887,0.022055,0.021562]},{"p":"L31N1349+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on the word that comes right after an institutional or organizational name or modifier in news, legal, government or policy prose, where a compound institutional noun is still unfinished (\"war on\", \"U.N. war\", \"local authorities\", \"crime scene\", \"hybrid international\", \"Commons education select committee\"), so the next word is a head noun like terror, crimes, services or laboratories.","s":[0.315541,0.472176,0.188836,0.36404,0.214202,0.026501,0.028094,0.003957,0.022257,0.016716,0.064561,0.01395,0.020139,0.023084,0.022734]},{"p":"L31N1349-","l":"UNK","j":"00","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on an ordinary word or punctuation mark in the middle of a phrase, where the next word is a modifier or continuation, mostly in graphic erotic prose or rap lyrics (e.g. 'dick', 'her', 'little', ',') and also in sailing, boat and sea narrative (e.g. 'keep her as steady', 'at sea under full sail').","s":[0.214261,0.361175,0.868816,0.230868,0.526996,0.221998,0.170746,0.132009,0.029687,0.198435,0.026638,0.0463,0.177608,0.020071,0.026306]},{"p":"L31N3149+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on the word right after a long subject noun phrase or clause in a sentence, typically \"to\" in \"be able/likely/going/managed to\", or a modal, auxiliary or adverb (could, may, would, never, quickly) where a bare verb is expected next, especially in long-winded expository, legal or blog prose.","s":[0.043807,0.0439,0.018546,0.070883,0.060724,0.040406,-0.003162,0.04749,0.081637,0.018546,0.027206,0.06952,0.048304,0.008627,0.049106]},{"p":"L31N3149-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on the subject of a clause, usually a plural or collective noun such as players, group, switches, terms, motion, letter, or a pronoun like They, It, or that, in running prose where a verb is about to follow (was, were, saw, argued), and where the same entity was already mentioned earlier in the passage.","s":[0.049325,0.124771,0.016986,0.169083,0.01161,0.049325,0.124771,0.010576,0.053482,0.01161,0.020621,0.024954,0.044083,0.019857,0.013303]},{"p":"L31N3262+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on a possessive determiner (their, our, your, her, whose) or a comma in a list, when the next word is likely a family or close-relation noun like families, loved ones, kids, parents or home; it fires most in emotional prose about grief, family and caring.","s":[0.168153,0.133999,0.557543,0.337173,0.007128,0.014969,-0.000219,0.011334,0.011219,0.017819,0.073168,0.001036,0.092093,0.04198,0.027084]},{"p":"L31N3262-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on a word in running prose where the next word is likely a family or household noun (home, house, family, husband, life), typically after a possessive or modifier like your, her, hard-working, knowledgeable or a family-role word; the effect is a prediction of 'home/household' rather than a particular token.","s":[0.288142,0.462048,0.142647,0.193492,0.173603,0.288142,0.350589,0.207955,0.193492,0.050463,0.036555,0.02555,0.025284,0.028491,0.027561]},{"p":"L31N3362+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on the token just before a next word that is likely to start with a capital \"O\" acronym or name such as OCZ, OC, OP, OO, OM, OCSP or OTT, in documents where that O-initial term recurs; the marked token is an ordinary word, comma, or period inside text about such a term, and the neuron suppresses \"O\"-prefixed continuations.","s":[0.609009,0.42274,0.286412,0.432703,0.190117,0.023632,0.049272,0.021065,0.087613,0.021072,0.022766,0.022073,0.019893,0.020918,0.019057]},{"p":"L31N3362-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires at the last position before the model expects a next token starting with a capital O (such as OEM, OCR, OLED, Oxley, Osborne, Oetal), so the marked token is often a function word, comma or word piece that is followed by an O-initial name or acronym; this is a next-token prediction and not a topic.","s":[0.194317,0.256152,0.091628,0.036031,0.191757,0.100243,0.027609,0.094163,0.066447,0.037144,0.033783,0.088059,0.016074,0.016825,0.004014]},{"p":"L31N3868+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on a word, usually a function word or modifier (\"of\" after a percentage or majority, \"for\", \"their\", \"ordinary\", \"experienced\", \"Nazi\"), at the point where a plural group noun for a set of people or things is about to come next (voters, Russians, members, painters, occupiers, girls).","s":[0.391981,0.124684,0.695062,0.093084,0.199467,0.047437,0.05591,0.057137,0.07094,0.030236,0.072964,0.001053,0.059602,0.010715,0.066868]},{"p":"L31N3868-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on the token just before an expected plural category noun like \"doubles\", \"singles\", \"equals\", \"neutrals\", \"reds\" or \"Rares\", strongest in comparisons of types (\"55-foot singles and 60-foot doubles\", \"classic favorites and originals\"), where \"-foot\", \"than\", \"and\" or an adjective precedes that plural.","s":[0.661404,0.278612,0.02094,0.203142,0.352024,0.010515,0.011954,0.019993,0.018576,0.005468,0.012885,0.010026,0.009306,0.007789,0.039133]},{"p":"L31N4660+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on the token just before a form of \"calculate\" is expected (e.g. \"were ___ calculated\", \"to ___ calculate\", \"are ___ calculated at checkout\", \"Need help ___ calculate\"), so it predicts the word calculate/calculation next, especially in math, converter, price-comparison and checkout web text.","s":[0.051638,0.069992,0.078325,0.052226,0.015035,0.02973,0.05377,0.078325,0.052226,0.009462,0.004996,0.014198,0.010921,0.002992,0.003362]},{"p":"L31N4660-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on a Chinese character (or its byte fragment) that sits right before 數/数 (\"number, count\") or a similar counting word, as in 資料|數量, 總使用者|數, 週期|數 or 頁數, so the neuron is predicting a counting or quantity word. It appears in Chinese text embedded in code comments, web page labels and bilingual passages.","s":[0.719213,0.457094,0.59552,0.496267,0.515702,0.368389,0.043928,0.364671,0.479074,0.197679,0.010163,0.00607,0.007038,0.00758,0.010484]},{"p":"L31N6449+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on a spelled-out tens number word (twenty, thirty, forty, fifty, sixty, seventy, eighty, ninety, or a split piece like \"fort\"+\"y\") when it is the first half of a compound that needs a units digit word next (twenty-six, forty-five, seventy-seven), so it predicts seven/eight/nine/six/five or ordinals.","s":[0.316098,0.176906,0.26979,0.486381,0.398768,0.314667,0.018428,0.268273,0.045621,0.099072,0.016063,0.019775,0.018382,0.015335,0.037582]},{"p":"L31N6449-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires on the token right before a version or model number that is expected to be a single digit like 7, 8 or 9, such as the product name in \"Windows 8\", \"WP 8\", \"Note 7\", \"IE 9\" or \"RX-8\", or the start of a split number like \"201\" before \"8\" or \"200\" before \"8\"; it predicts a following spelled-out or single-digit number from 5 to 9.","s":[0.259033,0.34999,0.238894,0.492126,0.195405,0.259033,0.014964,0.136371,0.010505,0.010914,0.009308,0.011811,0.012596,0.010948,0.031753]},{"p":"L31N7529+","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires in long historical, biographical or technical prose on a word where the next word is a verb like \"secured\", \"obtained\" or \"gained\" (words such as \"he\", \"to\", \"associates\", or the middle of a name), so the neuron promotes the \"secure\" token as the upcoming verb.","s":[0.226962,0.100638,0.039607,0.062903,0.184114,0.226962,0.100638,0.020231,0.01428,0.016401,0.015006,0.010892,0.016735,0.013657,0.020596]},{"p":"L31N7529-","l":"ONE","j":"0","f":0,"e":1,"x":"Fires (negatively) on a word in running English prose, often a function word or mid-phrase token such as 'the', 'of', 'carry', 'power', 'extent' or 'increased', at a point where the next word could plausibly be a verb or noun meaning to obtain, acquire or hold. It is strongest in long-document, formal, narrative, commercial, market-report and sports prose, and it has no single token or topic.","s":[0.048165,0.011314,0.063228,0.023153,0.241768,0.011928,0.014521,0.027134,0.057519,0.02229,0.029479,0.012247,0.006381,0.018859,0.016933]}],"check":{"stops":[370,311,235]}}]};
